// Curriculum data. Each day: { phase, title, videos, cc?, drills?, materials, exercise, quiz }.
// Phases: 1 = Days 1-28, 2 = Days 29-56, 3 = Days 57-84, 4 = Days 85-112, 5 = Days 113-140, 6 = Days 141-168, 7 = Days 169-195.
// Review days: every 7th day.
Object.assign(KITS,{"1":{"objectives":["Frame a synthetic channel-launch decision using incremental contribution, a no-launch baseline, a cash constraint and evidence that could reverse the recommendation."],"videos":[{"id":"e5r7-KT6mQ0","title":"Ecommerce Contribution Margin Explained","by":"LedgerGurus: Ecommerce Accounting Experts","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"LedgerGurus: ecommerce contribution margin","url":"https://ledgergurus.com/resources/video/ecommerce-contribution-margin/","note":"Read the published topic guidance; supplied toy figures are independently computed, not source-company facts. Definitions and exceptions matter; no current Indian legal/accounting certification."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA: relevant costs","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f5/technical-articles/relevant-costs.html","note":"Read the published topic guidance; supplied toy figures are independently computed, not source-company facts. Definitions and exceptions matter; no current Indian legal/accounting certification."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Bain: case-interview preparation","url":"https://www.bain.com/careers/hiring-process/case-interview/","note":"Read the published practical tips on clarifying objectives, showing reasoning and adapting. Linked practice cases and commercial resources are separate work, not assigned as already completed."}],"exercise":"This is a synthetic learning case, not a named company, a platform offer, an investment recommendation or permission to launch. No Indian tax/contract rate is asserted. Use one stated currency, time period and alternative baseline. Separate contribution before fixed costs, incremental operating surplus after avoidable fixed costs, and cash after working-capital timing. Do not copy a source example without checking its arithmetic. Concept lab: a snack brand can run a one-month, reversible new-channel pilot or keep its existing channels only. Management wants positive incremental operating surplus and no more than Rs 1 lakh additional operating funding need during the month. These are case constraints, not an external authority grant. Define the memo's decision owner as a fictional role and set a review after the final settlement reconciliation, before any scale-up.\nStart with this teaching example, distinct from D 2's numerical lab. Net sales are Rs 10 lakh, variable product/fulfilment/platform costs Rs 6 lakh and avoidable pilot-only fixed costs Rs 1 lakh. Contribution is Rs 4 lakh; incremental operating surplus is Rs 3 lakh only if there are no displaced existing sales or other changing costs. Do not call the after-fixed-cost result contribution margin. If the pilot displaces Rs 0.8 lakh of old-channel contribution, business contribution is Rs 3.2 lakh and operating surplus Rs 2.2 lakh. An allocated head-office expense that is identical with and without the pilot is not a differential cost, although it still needs funding at company level. A new coordinator hired only for the pilot is a relevant fixed cost even if spare factory capacity exists. Already paid non-refundable research is sunk; a resale value or alternative use of an asset is not automatically zero just because its purchase cost is sunk.\nDefine net sales, commission base, refund responsibility and settlement dates explicitly. A hypothetical 20% commission is not evidence of any current platform's fee. Contribution does not equal cash: reconcile outstanding settlements, inventory, supplier credit, capital spending, tax and financing separately where present. Do not double-count commission already netted from a settlement. If operating surplus is Rs 2.2 lakh and the increase in operating receivables plus inventory less payables is Rs 2.7 lakh, operating cash is minus Rs 0.5 lakh under the stated no-other-items assumption. Compare timing with opening cash and the funding constraint, not just month-total profit.\nDistinguish gross new-channel orders from genuinely incremental business orders. Identify displaced old-channel volume using a defensible counterfactual; a higher observed total after launch alone does not isolate a causal effect when price, festivals or assortment changed. Describe a comparison design, measurement gaps and possible spillovers without claiming randomization or matching guarantees causality. Test a feasible downside with lower net unit contribution and more displaced old sales within the baseline volume. A pilot has information value only if its measurement can change a later decision and its cost/delay/reversibility are accounted for; do not add a separate learning value to a payoff already defined to include it.\nSubmit a one-page memo with alternatives, unit definitions, the 3-step contribution/surplus/cash bridge, assumptions, a falsifier, the fictional owner's dated gate and an explicit condition for pause/restart. State uncertainty and the next evidence needed. Do not contact a vendor, spend, promise a launch or treat a classroom memo as company approval.","quiz":[{"q":"A synthetic channel pilot must earn positive incremental operating surplus within a one-month cash-funding cap. What should be fixed before forecasting?","o":["The highest plausible order volume and a revenue chart for that volume.","The alternatives, baseline, horizon, metric definitions and decision constraints.","The historical company margin, used as the target for every new channel.","The market growth rate, used to choose the channel before costs are checked."],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic month: sales 10 lakh, variable costs 6 lakh and avoidable fixed costs 1 lakh; no other changes. Which labels are correct?","o":["Contribution 3 lakh; operating surplus after these fixed costs 3 lakh.","Contribution 3 lakh; operating surplus after these fixed costs 4 lakh.","Contribution 4 lakh; operating surplus after these fixed costs 4 lakh.","Contribution 4 lakh; operating surplus after these fixed costs 3 lakh."],"a":3},{"q":"A pilot earns 4 lakh new-channel contribution but displaces 0.8 lakh old-channel contribution. Avoidable fixed costs are 1 lakh. What is incremental operating surplus?","o":["4.8 lakh.","3.0 lakh.","3.2 lakh.","2.2 lakh."],"a":3},{"q":"A production line has spare capacity. A new coordinator costing 0.5 lakh is hired only if the pilot runs. How should the cost enter?","o":["Exclude it because spare capacity makes every fixed cost irrelevant.","Include it as an avoidable fixed cost of the pilot.","Allocate it to existing sales only, leaving pilot economics unchanged.","Treat it as sunk because a staff budget was discussed last month."],"a":1},{"q":"Which observation most directly challenges a forecast of mostly incremental new-channel sales?","o":["The market category is growing faster than the company historical sales trend.","New-channel gross orders exceed the prior-month target without old-channel measurement.","The new channel has positive unit contribution before its fixed launch costs.","Reconciled customer/order evidence shows most new-channel orders replace old-channel baseline orders."],"a":3},{"q":"Two settlement files use gross order value and net sales after refunds respectively. What is the first useful reconciliation?","o":["Use the larger reported amount as the common revenue definition.","Align period, population, refunds and fee base before comparing the results.","Average the reported amounts and retain the two different labels.","Choose the figure closest to the forecast and use its percentage everywhere."],"a":1},{"q":"Operating surplus is 2.2 lakh; operating receivables plus inventory less payables rise 2.7 lakh. No other cash/accrual items occur. What is operating cash?","o":["Minus 0.5 lakh.","Plus 0.5 lakh.","Plus 2.2 lakh.","Plus 4.9 lakh."],"a":0},{"q":"Warehouse rent is unchanged up to 10000 monthly orders. A pilot forecast reaches 12000 orders. Which cost assumption needs testing?","o":["Whether rent is sunk forever because it was fixed at lower volume.","Whether exceeding the stated relevant range adds rental/capacity cost.","Whether doubling every cost category is required at the first extra order.","Whether variable costs can be replaced with the prior fixed-cost allocation."],"a":1},{"q":"Last month research cost 2 lakh and cannot be refunded. An unused asset can now be sold for 0.3 lakh but the pilot would consume it. Which treatment fits?","o":["Exclude both because all historical spending is irrelevant to any future alternative.","Include sunk research; exclude the asset because its original purchase was in the past.","Exclude sunk research; include the 0.3 lakh foregone sale as an opportunity cost.","Include both historical research and the full original asset cost regardless of resale value."],"a":2},{"q":"A festival and a price cut coincide with a 15% post-launch sales rise. What can the result alone establish?","o":["The launch caused no rise because other factors changed at the same time.","The launch caused the entire 15% rise because it preceded the measurement.","Sales rose in that period, but the isolated launch effect remains unresolved.","The launch effect is exactly the rise less the percentage discount without another model."],"a":2}]},"2":{"objectives":["Calculate and stress a synthetic launch case from net unit contribution through displaced sales, avoidable costs and operating cash; apply a stated decision gate."],"videos":[{"id":"e5r7-KT6mQ0","title":"Ecommerce Contribution Margin Explained","by":"LedgerGurus: Ecommerce Accounting Experts","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"LedgerGurus: ecommerce contribution margin","url":"https://ledgergurus.com/resources/video/ecommerce-contribution-margin/","note":"Read the published topic guidance; supplied toy figures are independently computed, not source-company facts. Definitions and exceptions matter; no current Indian legal/accounting certification."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA: relevant costs","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f5/technical-articles/relevant-costs.html","note":"Read the published topic guidance; supplied toy figures are independently computed, not source-company facts. Definitions and exceptions matter; no current Indian legal/accounting certification."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran: cash-flow construction","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/dcfcf.pdf","note":"Read Measuring Cash Flows/Measuring Cash Flow to the Firm and Working Capital Investments. The full deck and historical company figures are broader than this lab."}],"exercise":"This is a synthetic learning case, not a named company, a platform offer, an investment recommendation or permission to launch. No Indian tax/contract rate is asserted. Use one stated currency, time period and alternative baseline. Separate contribution before fixed costs, incremental operating surplus after avoidable fixed costs, and cash after working-capital timing. Do not copy a source example without checking its arithmetic. Numerical lab: one month has 10000 new-channel orders. Net sales after discounts are Rs 200 per order; product, packaging and delivery cost Rs 120 per order. Commission is 20% of net sales, additional to the 120. Ignore taxes, returns, capital spending, financing and all other unspecified changes. There are at least 3000 no-launch old-channel orders that would otherwise have earned Rs 55 each, and 3000 of the new-channel orders replace them. Remaining new-channel orders are incremental. Avoidable pilot fixed costs are Rs 1 lakh. Receivables rise Rs 0.8 lakh, inventory Rs 0.7 lakh and payables Rs 0.3 lakh. State the same-month definitions before using them.\nCompute commission 40/order, unit contribution 40/order, new-channel contribution 4 lakh, displaced contribution 1.65 lakh, incremental business contribution 2.35 lakh, operating surplus 1.35 lakh, additional non-cash operating working capital 1.2 lakh and operating cash 0.15 lakh. A positive month-total cash number does not prove that every within-month payment can be funded. Supply an invented dated collection/payment schedule and inspect its peak funding need separately; label it as a new scenario rather than an observed fact.\nTest commission sensitivity at 25% of the unchanged net-sales base: unit contribution 30, new-channel contribution 3 lakh, operating surplus 0.35 lakh and operating cash minus 0.85 lakh if all other assumptions remain fixed. In a separate break-even scenario with 4 lakh new-channel contribution,1 lakh avoidable fixed cost and 50 lost contribution per displaced order, zero surplus occurs at 6000 displaced orders. This threshold is feasible only if at least 6000 old-channel baseline orders exist and displacement does not exceed new-channel or eligible old-channel volume. It is not the threshold for every version of the case.\nThe stated pilot rule is positive incremental operating surplus and no more than 1 lakh additional operating funding need, subject to no binding safety/compliance issue. Make a conditional recommendation and state what settlement, attribution or timing evidence would change it. The 10 quiz cases each specify their own inputs; do not silently carry the 55 old-channel contribution into a question that says 50. Submit workings, a baseline/downside comparison, the dated cash schedule and a one-page memo. No actual contract, platform rate or launch approval follows.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic month-one launch: net sales after discounts are Rs 200 per order. Product, packaging and delivery cost Rs 120 per order; the platform fee is 20% of net sales and is not included in Rs 120. Ignore taxes and returns. What is contribution margin per order before fixed costs?","o":["Rs 80","Rs 160","Rs 40","Rs 200"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic launch: 10,000 new-channel orders earn Rs 40 contribution each. Of these, 3,000 replace old-channel orders that would have earned Rs 55 contribution each. Volume and prices elsewhere do not change. Before launch fixed costs, what is the change in total business contribution versus no launch?","o":["Rs 4.00 lakh","Rs 1.20 lakh","Rs 5.65 lakh","Rs 2.35 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic launch: incremental business contribution after variable costs and displaced sales is Rs 2.35 lakh. Launch-only, avoidable fixed costs are Rs 1 lakh for the month. What is the incremental operating surplus after these fixed costs, ignoring tax and financing?","o":["Rs 2.35 lakh","Rs 1.35 lakh","Rs 3.35 lakh","Rs 1.00 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic launch: contribution and avoidable fixed costs produce Rs 1.35 lakh incremental operating surplus. Receivables rise Rs 0.8 lakh, inventory rises Rs 0.7 lakh and payables rise Rs 0.3 lakh. Assume all other cash/accrual items are zero. What is incremental operating cash for the month?","o":["Rs 2.55 lakh","Minus Rs 0.15 lakh","Rs 1.35 lakh","Rs 0.15 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic pilot: new-channel contribution is Rs 4 lakh and avoidable launch fixed cost is Rs 1 lakh. Every displaced old-channel order loses Rs 50 contribution. What is the maximum number of displaced orders at which incremental operating surplus is zero? Assume at least 6000 eligible old-channel baseline orders and sufficient new-channel volume.","o":["6,000","3,000","2,000","8,000"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic launch decision: Rs 2 lakh was spent last month on non-refundable research. A new launch-only Rs 0.5 lakh fee is payable next month only if the team proceeds. No resale or alternative use exists. How should these enter the incremental go/no-go model?","o":["Subtract both as future avoidable costs","Exclude both because they are labeled launch costs","Exclude the sunk research; include the avoidable future fee","Include the research but exclude the new fee"],"a":2},{"q":"A synthetic pilot shows sales rose 15% after launch, but the same month included a festival and a price cut. Which statement is supported by this result alone?","o":["The effect equals 15% because the launch and measurement use the same month.","The observed rise is not yet an isolated causal launch effect; examine the counterfactual and concurrent price/festival changes.","The effect equals 15% minus the discount rate, without an additional demand model.","The effect is zero because a festival and a price cut occurred at the same time."],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic commission audit: the contract charges 20% of net sales after discounts. Monthly gross sales are Rs 12 lakh, discounts Rs 2 lakh, and no other adjustments apply. What commission belongs in the launch model?","o":["Rs 2.4 lakh","Rs 10 lakh","Rs 0.4 lakh","Rs 2.0 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic memo: the base case remains profitable, but a plausible combined fall in order contribution and rise in displaced sales makes cash negative before the next collection date. What should the recommendation add?","o":["Only the month-total surplus, because interim settlement timing does not change profitability.","A weighted average that merges the base and downside funding needs into one unlabeled number.","A dated cash-funding bridge and an explicit pause/stop gate tied to the downside assumptions.","The same cash balance as the base case, because higher sales always finance their own inventory."],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic pilot review: finance reconciles platform settlements and finds commissions were understated. Correcting the fee turns incremental operating surplus negative under the approved hurdle. What is the sound next step?","o":["Recompute with the reconciled fee, record old/new assumptions and apply the pre-agreed gate.","Compare only gross sales because the original goal referred to launching a sales channel.","Apply the previous go decision because reviewing a pilot cannot alter an accepted hurdle.","Keep the old fee for the decision and show the reconciled number only in an appendix."],"a":0}]},"3":{"objectives":["Explain why a synthetic dealer-credit expansion can raise profit while lowering ROIC or straining cash; specify denominators, timing and evidence."],"videos":[{"id":"qfssMonQ8Ek","title":"Data Update 5 for 2023: Profitability, Returns and Value","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA: working capital management","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f9/technical-articles/wcm.html","note":"Read liquidity, ratio assumptions and cash operating cycle sections. ACCA notes 360-day exam assumptions and average/closing-balance approximations, not a universal day-count convention. No current tax/credit law asserted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran: ROIC measurement and implications","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/adamodar/pdfiles/papers/returnmeasures.pdf","note":"Read the ROIC definition, operating-income/tax/invested-capital sections and timing discussion. Beginning invested capital is the author's main convention; average capital is also discussed. The lab explicitly uses average capital, not an undeclared universal convention."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran: estimating cash flows","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/dcfcf.pdf","note":"Read cash-flow construction and Working Capital Investments. Distinguish operating non-cash working capital from current assets less all current liabilities. Historical tax/company inputs are not current Indian facts."}],"exercise":"This is a synthetic learning case, not a named company, a platform offer, an investment recommendation or permission to launch. No Indian tax/contract rate is asserted. Use one stated currency, time period and alternative baseline. Separate contribution before fixed costs, incremental operating surplus after avoidable fixed costs, and cash after working-capital timing. Do not copy a source example without checking its arithmetic. Concept lab: an appliance maker considers longer dealer credit. Trace credit policy to credit sales, invoice ageing, collections, average receivables, invested capital and cash. Longer promised credit does not guarantee extra demand, ultimate collection or higher economic value. Profit and cash are different measurements; a positive receivable is not cash and an overdue invoice is not automatically a 100% realized loss.\nDefine CCC=DIO+DSO-DPO for comparable periods and consistent denominators. DSO uses average trade receivables/credit sales x days; DIO usually uses average inventory/COGS x days; a DPO convention may use purchases rather than COGS if appropriate and disclosed. Do not mix credit-sales and total-sales denominators silently. ACCA discusses 360-day exam assumptions; D4 explicitly uses360 days. Balance-sheet-date ratios can conceal seasonal or quarter-end patterns; use better time averages/ageing where material. A shorter CCC is not automatically better if stockouts, rejected creditworthy buyers or overdue supplier payments cause harm.\nFor this lab, define ROIC as annual after-tax operating profit divided by average operating invested capital. Operating return belongs to debt and equity capital, so net income after interest divided by capital is not the same measure. State treatment of cash, operating/non-operating assets, leases, impairment and other accounting differences before comparing firms. The 2007 Damodaran paper uses beginning capital for its main formula and discusses average capital; neither is silently substituted for the toy's stated average. A supplied 25% tax rate is an assumption, not current Indian law. Do not subtract interest tax benefits twice by combining inconsistent numerator and cost-of-capital definitions.\nA before/after steady-state example has annual after-tax operating profit 6 crore on 40 crore average invested capital, then 7.2 crore on 60 crore. Profit rises 20%, but ROIC falls 15% to 12%. This before/after ratio does not by itself establish the marginal project's return or causality; account for changes in existing operations and the timing of investment. For a separately defined project with incremental annual after-tax operating profit 1.2 crore on incremental average capital 20 crore, the simple incremental return is 6%; a hypothetical 10% comparison hurdle would not be met under those assumptions. This is not a forecast or full NPV, and investment timing/risk/horizon still matter.\nBridge annual after-tax operating profit to unlevered cash by adding depreciation, subtracting capital spending and subtracting increases in non-cash operating working capital. Treat this separately from reported statutory CFO and cash to equity; do not subtract interest again without changing the cash-flow basis. A one-time receivables build is not an annual recurring expense or a permanent profit loss, but it still needs funding now. Recoverability, timing, provisions and write-offs need separate evidence; this lesson does not assert an accounting-rule conclusion.\nSubmit a causal diagram, definition table, one worked profit/ROIC/cash bridge, two plausible downside mechanisms and a falsifier. Compare no expansion, a bounded pilot and wider credit only as classroom alternatives. Use fictitious dealers and invoices. Name a fictional approval owner, cash/ageing limits and a dated review without granting real credit or changing a payment term.","quiz":[{"q":"A dealer-credit pilot increases reported profit but delays collections. Which measurement most directly tests liquidity?","o":["A dated operating-cash bridge and funding need, alongside receivables ageing.","The revenue growth rate alone, using the month with the strongest sales.","The gross margin percentage alone, excluding changes in payment timing.","The final invoice count alone, treating all unpaid balances as current cash."],"a":0},{"q":"DIO 50, DSO 40 and DPO 30 days use the same period. Which CCC formula and result are correct?","o":["50+40-30=60 days.","50+40+30=120 days.","50-40+30=40 days.","50-40-30=minus 20 days."],"a":0},{"q":"Receivables are unchanged, but the analyst switches from credit sales to larger total sales in DSO. What happens mechanically?","o":["Calculated DSO falls even without better collection, so the definition change must be reconciled.","Calculated DSO rises, proving a deteriorating credit collection policy.","Calculated DSO stays unchanged because the receivables balance did not change.","Calculated DSO becomes a cash-flow measure and no longer depends on the sales period."],"a":0},{"q":"Under a stated after-tax operating profit/average invested capital convention, profit rises 6 to 7.2 crore and capital 40 to 60 crore. What follows?","o":["ROIC rises 15% to 18%; the old denominator can be retained after expansion.","ROIC falls 15% to 12%; higher profit alone does not show a higher return.","ROIC remains 15%; profit and invested capital both increased in rupee terms.","ROIC falls 20% to 12%; pre-tax and after-tax returns are the same starting measure."],"a":1},{"q":"Why is net income after interest not directly interchangeable with operating profit in an all-capital ROIC numerator?","o":["Net income excludes every tax charge, so an operating tax rate would be counted once.","Net income is always larger than EBIT, so it would overstate every firm return.","Net income includes financing effects, while the denominator represents debt and equity operating capital.","Net income measures cash collections directly, making any capital denominator unnecessary."],"a":2},{"q":"In a 360-day toy, average receivables 4.5 crore and annual credit sales 36 crore imply what DSO?","o":["45 days.","8 days.","36 days.","60 days."],"a":0},{"q":"What best distinguishes the financial-statement current-assets-minus-current-liabilities measure from the cash-flow toy operating working capital?","o":["The latter includes only cash and bank debt because inventories are already expensed.","The latter excludes cash and financing liabilities and focuses on changing operating balances.","The latter is the same balance whenever the company has positive revenue growth.","The latter subtracts operating receivables because customers owe rather than hold the cash."],"a":1},{"q":"A firm reduces CCC by paying suppliers 15 days after agreed due dates. What interpretation fits?","o":["The unpaid amount is a new operating revenue until the supplier demands payment.","The shorter cycle proves an economic gain of 15 percentage points of margin.","The arithmetic can improve while penalties, supply continuity and relationship risks worsen.","The change automatically leaves supplier credit terms and the firm risk unchanged."],"a":2},{"q":"For one defined incremental project, annual after-tax operating profit 1.2 crore uses average capital 20 crore. Under a toy 10% hurdle, what does the simple return show?","o":["12%, above the hurdle; the 20 crore denominator should be reduced by half.","6%, below the stated hurdle; timing/risk and full cash-flow analysis still matter.","10%, exactly at the hurdle because a supplied benchmark fixes project return.","60%, above the hurdle; crore units cancel by moving one decimal place only."],"a":1},{"q":"Two firms use beginning versus average capital and different lease adjustments for ROIC. What should precede a performance ranking?","o":["Replace both capital figures with market capitalization while keeping operating income unchanged.","Rank the higher published percentage because all ROIC labels imply identical inputs.","Average the two denominators and retain each firm original profit measure without review.","Reconcile definitions and periods, then compare returns under a consistent basis."],"a":3}]},"4":{"objectives":["Calculate a defined 360-day cash cycle, average-capital ROIC and unlevered cash bridge; test a synthetic dealer-credit funding cap."],"videos":[{"id":"qfssMonQ8Ek","title":"Data Update 5 for 2023: Profitability, Returns and Value","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA: working capital management","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f9/technical-articles/wcm.html","note":"Read liquidity, ratio assumptions and cash operating cycle sections. ACCA notes 360-day exam assumptions and average/closing-balance approximations, not a universal day-count convention. No current tax/credit law asserted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran: ROIC measurement and implications","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/adamodar/pdfiles/papers/returnmeasures.pdf","note":"Read the ROIC definition, operating-income/tax/invested-capital sections and timing discussion. Beginning invested capital is the author's main convention; average capital is also discussed. The lab explicitly uses average capital, not an undeclared universal convention."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran: estimating cash flows","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/dcfcf.pdf","note":"Read cash-flow construction and Working Capital Investments. Distinguish operating non-cash working capital from current assets less all current liabilities. Historical tax/company inputs are not current Indian facts."}],"exercise":"This is a synthetic learning case, not a named company, a platform offer, an investment recommendation or permission to launch. No Indian tax/contract rate is asserted. Use one stated currency, time period and alternative baseline. Separate contribution before fixed costs, incremental operating surplus after avoidable fixed costs, and cash after working-capital timing. Do not copy a source example without checking its arithmetic. Numerical lab, all figures synthetic. Use a 360-day analytical year, annual credit sales 36 crore, average trade receivables 4.5 crore, DIO 50 days and DPO 30 days. DSO=45 days and CCC=65 days for this dataset. The separate quiz case with DSO 40 has CCC 60; do not merge the two. State average versus closing balances and annual versus daily units.\nA separately defined steady-state credit extension keeps daily credit sales at 10 lakh but raises collection time 30 to 45 days. Additional receivables=10 lakh x 15=150 lakh=1.5 crore. This is a funding-stock increase, not an annual cost equal to 1.5 crore. With a 1.2 crore cap on extra receivables, at most 12 additional days fit, so total collection time must be at most 42 days under the stated unchanged-sales assumptions. An average DSO bound does not alone guarantee the absence of overdue individual invoices; include a fictional invoice-ageing table and explicit dispute/bad-debt flags. No real dealer records are needed.\nFor the defined ROIC case, EBIT 8 crore and an assumed operating tax rate 25% give after-tax operating profit 6 crore; average operating invested capital 40 crore gives 15%. Treat 25% as a classroom input, not a current legal tax rate. For the before/after comparison 6/40=15% and 7.2/60=12%, higher profit does not imply higher ROIC. Beginning and average capital are different conventions; use the one stated, with consistent operating/non-operating scope.\nFor a separate annual unlevered cash bridge, after-tax operating profit 6 crore, depreciation 1, receivables increase 2, inventory increase 1, operating payables increase 0.5 and capital spending 2 give 6+1-(2+1-0.5)-2=2.5 crore. Financing and all other items are excluded. It is cash to the firm after capital spending under this toy, not net income or statutory CFO. Do not combine this case's 2-crore receivables increase with the separate 1.5-crore credit-extension scenario.\nSubmit four clearly separated workings, a period/denominator table, the fictitious ageing table and a conditional decision memo. Stress slower collections, a higher uncollectible fraction and supplier-term changes separately before combining a coherent downside. Do not model all receivables as cash or all overdue invoices as 100% loss. Apply the stated funding cap and say what verified evidence would permit restart. No actual credit approval, collection action, legal/accounting-rule conclusion or customer disclosure follows.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic appliance maker: DIO is 50 days, DSO 40 days and DPO 30 days for the same period. What is the cash conversion cycle?","o":["60 days","20 days","120 days","80 days"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic annual data, using a 360-day analytical year: credit sales are Rs 36 crore, average trade receivables Rs 4.5 crore. All sales are on credit. What is DSO?","o":["36 days","80 days","8 days","45 days"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic steady-state dealer expansion: daily credit sales stay Rs 10 lakh. Collection time rises from 30 to 45 days; no other assumption changes. Approximately how much extra receivables funding is tied up?","o":["Rs 4.5 crore","Rs 3 crore","Rs 1.5 crore","Rs 15 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic annual ROIC exercise: EBIT is Rs 8 crore, the operating tax rate is 25%, and average invested capital is Rs 40 crore. Define ROIC here as EBIT x (1 - tax rate) / average invested capital. What is ROIC?","o":["20%","25%","15%","5%"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic steady-state comparison: before dealer expansion, annual after-tax operating profit is Rs 6 crore on Rs 40 crore average invested capital. After expansion, these are Rs 7.2 crore and Rs 60 crore. Using after-tax operating profit / average invested capital, what changed?","o":["ROIC fell from 20% to 12%","ROIC rose from 15% to 18%","ROIC stayed at 15% because profit rose","ROIC fell from 15% to 12% despite higher profit"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic period bridge: after-tax operating profit is Rs 6 crore and depreciation Rs 1 crore. Operating receivables rise Rs 2 crore, inventory rises Rs 1 crore and operating payables rise Rs 0.5 crore. Capital spending is Rs 2 crore. Assume no other adjustments. What is cash after this working-capital change and capital spending?","o":["Rs 2.5 crore","Rs 7 crore","Rs 3 crore","Rs 5 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic CCC review: DIO and DSO are unchanged. DPO rises from 30 to 45 days because the company is paying suppliers after their agreed due dates. What follows?","o":["The 15-day shorter CCC establishes the same improvement in cash return without further evidence.","ROIC rises 15 percentage points because each saved cash-cycle day adds one point of return.","CCC falls 15 days, but overdue-payment penalties, supply and relationship risks still need review.","Operating profit rises by the unpaid supplier balance because delayed cash payment creates revenue."],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic receivables comparison: month-end receivables fell, but quarter-average receivables and overdue balances rose. Which analysis best tests the dealer-credit decision?","o":["Use the month-end balance alone because it is the most recent reporting date.","Average the DSO percentages while retaining their different sales periods and definitions.","Treat the rise in overdue balances as an equal realized cash loss without recoverability evidence.","Use comparable-period average balances, sales definitions and ageing, then reconcile the month-end change."],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic launch policy: the team has a Rs 1.2 crore cap on extra receivables funding. Daily credit sales remain Rs 10 lakh and baseline collection time is 30 days. Using extra receivables = daily credit sales x extra collection days, what is the maximum collection time within the cap?","o":["42 days","45 days","32 days","120 days"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic dealer pilot: sales and reported profit rise, but collections exceed the pre-agreed cash-funding limit. What recommendation respects both the economics and the control?","o":["Scale on the reported profit result and postpone the collections review until the annual accounts.","Reconcile incremental profit, invested capital, ageing and cash timing; apply the agreed pause gate and restart evidence.","Exclude unpaid but recorded invoices from both receivables and the earnings bridge until collection.","Raise the funding limit inside the analysis so the existing expansion fits the original recommendation."],"a":1}]},"5":{"objectives":["Define and check a bottom-up synthetic delivery estimate using unique reachable households, active share, order frequency, capacity and explicit uncertainty."],"videos":[{"id":"HYGIoe-QXhU","title":"McKinsey Case Interview: Market Sizing Walkthrough","by":"Management Consulted","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"SBA: market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/plan-your-business/","note":"Read only the market-research/competitive-analysis section, through the data-reference table. US business-registration/finance guidance elsewhere on this long page is not Indian law or part of this lesson."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"US Census: sampling-error definitions","url":"https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/methodology/sample-size-and-data-quality/sample-size-definitions.html","note":"Read sampling-error and reliability definitions as measurement context. US survey sample counts and 90% conventions are not data about an Indian city or universal survey standards."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT capacity lecture: process flow, demand and capacity","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760b-introduction-to-operations-management-spring-2004/16dde953a29f89a4d2018361900ce886_lec3_capacity.pdf","note":"Read opening process-flow/demand-capacity/throughput/congestion sections."}],"exercise":"Synthetic concept lab, not an estimate of any actual tier-2 city, a market forecast or approval to launch. Specify a fictional service area, a calendar month, households as the buying unit and orders as the output. Residents, households, registered accounts, active paying households and fulfilled orders are different counts. Define active as at least one paid qualifying order in the month. Count each household once across overlapping service zones; do not add zone totals without subtracting overlap.\nModel monthly requested orders as unique reachable households x active share among those households x orders per active household per month. The activity share is a period-specific assumption, not a cumulative account-registration rate. Frequency must use active households as its denominator if multiplied by active share; multiplying by an all-household frequency would count inactivity twice. For a toy 50000 reachable households,20% active and 4 orders/active/month yield 40000 requested orders/month. The all-reachable-household average is 0.8 orders/month, not 4. An average is not a promise that each household buys 0.8 orders.\nFor two zones 30000 and 25000 with 5000 common households, the union is 50000. The overlap is not a negative-customer category; subtract it once. For more zones, pairwise overlap counts alone may be insufficient if triple overlap exists. Use a clear unique-household base and fictitious IDs or aggregate tables, not real personal addresses.\nA survey only of existing customers can estimate stated intentions within its respondents, not automatically adoption across the whole service area. A larger sample does not remove a systematic selection bias. Record recruitment, nonresponse, question wording, season, year, geography and uncertainty. Distinguish intention, observed ordering and causal response to a launch. A general public statistics source is context, not proof of customer demand for this particular service. A matched comparison is useful only with an explicit credible design; do not use it as a universal remedy for duplicate records.\nSeparate market demand from fulfillment capacity and the company's captured volume. Under a toy no-backlog/no-outsourcing rule, demand 60000 and capacity 45000 cap fulfilled orders at 45000; do not add demand and capacity. Explain unmet demand without claiming it is realized revenue. If the business acts as an agent, basket value may be transaction value rather than its recognized revenue; specify the toy business model before converting orders to sales. Even full-company order growth may include channel displacement rather than genuinely new demand.\nBuild a low/base/high scenario from mechanisms, not an automatic statistical confidence interval. Frequency and adoption may be linked, so do not present independent corner combinations as equally plausible forecasts. Label all input assumptions, give one sensitivity to reachable-household duplication and one to activity/frequency. Submit a unit-checked model, overlap reconciliation, scenario table and a short recommendation stating the next evidence that could change the decision. No real field survey, customer outreach, spending or location decision follows.","quiz":[{"q":"Which base best fits a monthly household-delivery model for a stated service radius?","o":["Unique eligible households in that radius and period.","All residents in the whole city regardless of delivery coverage.","Cumulative registered accounts across every operating region.","Orders recorded across all time without household identifiers."],"a":0},{"q":"50000 reachable households x 20% active x 4 orders per active household per month gives what?","o":["40000 requested orders/month.","10000 requested orders/month.","200000 requested orders/month.","40000 households/order."],"a":0},{"q":"With 20% active and 4 orders per active household, what is the average across all reachable households?","o":["4 orders per reachable household per month.","0.8 orders per reachable household per month.","20 orders per reachable household per month.","0.05 orders per reachable household per month."],"a":1},{"q":"Zones contain 30000 and 25000 households;5000 are in both. How many unique households are reachable?","o":["55000.","50000.","60000.","5000."],"a":1},{"q":"A 200-person survey recruits only existing service customers;80% intend to reorder. What does this establish?","o":["A representative estimate because 200 responses remove recruitment bias.","An 80% active share for every household in the delivery area.","A causal 80% sales rise if the firm opens another service zone.","Respondent intention within that selected group, not all-household adoption."],"a":3},{"q":"Frequency is already measured as 0.8 orders per month across every reachable household. Which volume model is consistent?","o":["Active households x 0.8, while describing 0.8 as an active-only frequency.","Reachable households x 0.8 xactive share, to count inactivity twice.","Reachable households x 0.8, without another active-share multiplier.","Reachable households divided by 0.8, to turn orders into buying households."],"a":2},{"q":"Demand 60000, monthly service-level capacity 45000, no backlog or outsourcing. Which fulfilled volume is feasible?","o":["105000 orders because capacity is added to demand.","At most 45000 orders under these rules.","60000 orders because demand defines the fulfilled market.","15000 orders because only demand above capacity is served."],"a":1},{"q":"What can a low/base/high sensitivity table establish by itself?","o":["A 95% confidence interval whenever three rows are presented.","Results under labeled assumptions, not statistical confidence coverage.","A guaranteed minimum and maximum for every possible future month.","A causal demand estimate independent of how households were selected."],"a":1},{"q":"A model increases customer registrations but keeps paid qualifying orders unchanged. What follows?","o":["Order frequency must rise by the same percentage as registrations.","Every extra registration must be an extra active paying household.","Registrations alone do not establish a higher active-household rate.","Reachable households necessarily expand when existing users register again."],"a":2},{"q":"New-zone orders are 10000, including 2000 displaced from the company existing zone. No other volume changes. What is company order growth?","o":["12000 orders.","10000 orders.","8000 orders.","2000 orders."],"a":2}]},"6":{"objectives":["Compute synthetic delivery demand, overlap, annual sales, capacity and break-even without mixing units, populations or scenarios."],"videos":[{"id":"HYGIoe-QXhU","title":"McKinsey Case Interview: Market Sizing Walkthrough","by":"Management Consulted","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"SBA: market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/plan-your-business/","note":"Read only the market-research/competitive-analysis section, through the data-reference table. US business-registration/finance guidance elsewhere on this long page is not Indian law or part of this lesson."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"US Census: sampling-error definitions","url":"https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/methodology/sample-size-and-data-quality/sample-size-definitions.html","note":"Read sampling-error and reliability definitions as measurement context. US survey sample counts and 90% conventions are not data about an Indian city or universal survey standards."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT capacity lecture: process flow, demand and capacity","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760b-introduction-to-operations-management-spring-2004/16dde953a29f89a4d2018361900ce886_lec3_capacity.pdf","note":"Read opening process-flow/demand-capacity/throughput/congestion sections."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"LedgerGurus: contribution margin publisher transcript","url":"https://ledgergurus.com/resources/video/ecommerce-contribution-margin/","note":"Read the complete publisher transcript, including fixed-cost steps and variable-cost assumptions. Absolute profitability claims are limited to its operating model, not every business or strategy."}],"exercise":"Synthetic numerical lab, not actual city/customer data. Work each case separately, label units and assumptions, then reconcile the final recommendation. CaseA:80000 unique reachable households,25% active in the month,3 orders per active household/month. Requested volume 60000/month;20000 active households and 0.75 orders per all-reachable-household/month are different units. CaseB is a separate geography: zones 30000/25000 with 5000 overlapping households give 50000 unique. Applying 20% active and 4 orders gives 40000/month. Do not use the 80000 base in CaseB or multiply the union by two.\nFor CaseB, unchanged 40000 monthly fulfilled orders for 12 months and 250 rupees net sales/order produce 120000000 rupees=12 crore annual net sales, but only under a stated principal-selling model and no returns/tax/refund adjustments. A delivery intermediary may instead earn a fee on basket value; the toy is not an accounting-rule determination. Monthly versus annual and lakh versus crore conversions must be visible. A seasonal/ramping business needs a monthly schedule rather than multiplying its best month by 12.\nCaseC: requested 60000/month but service-level capacity 45000, with no outsourcing/backlog. Maximum fulfillment 45000. At 30 rupees contribution/fulfilled order before avoidable operating fixed costs 9 lakh/month, operating break-even volume is 30000/month;45000 fulfilled orders give 4.5 lakh monthly operating surplus under no-other-cost assumptions. Demand beyond capacity is not earned margin. In a separate downside,50000 reachable x 16% active x 2.5 frequency=20000 orders, leaving minus 3 lakh operating surplus at the same 30/9 lakh cost structure if fulfillment equals demand. Separate these scenarios rather than mixing the best demand with a different fee/cost base.\nCaseD:10000 new-zone orders include 2000 orders displaced from the existing zone, with no other change; company volume rises 8000. If contribution/order differs by zone, multiply each volume by its own contribution before computing money. Existing-customer survey results do not directly supply the all-household active share. Create an input provenance table with unit/year/geography/denominator and a feasible coherent sensitivity case. Record one arithmetic error and one evidence gap, and show what data would resolve each. No real survey, outreach, forecast, booking or launch permission.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic city delivery estimate: 80,000 households are inside the service area. Assume 25% are active customers and each active household places 3 orders per month. What is expected monthly order volume?","o":["60,000 orders","240,000 orders","20,000 orders","960,000 orders"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic service map: zone A reaches 30,000 households and zone B reaches 25,000. Exactly 5,000 households are in both zones. How many unique households are reachable across A or B?","o":["55,000","50,000","5,000","60,000"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic survey: 200 respondents were recruited only from people who already ordered from the delivery service. Eighty percent say they would reorder. Can 80% be used as the active-customer share of all city households?","o":["No; any imperfect sample proves the true all-household adoption share must be zero.","Yes;200 respondents yield enough precision to remove existing-customer recruitment bias.","Yes; stated reorder intention measures the same population as all-household current adoption.","No; existing-customer respondents do not represent all households, so validate reach and adoption with a suitable sample."],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic unit check: the model multiplies 50,000 reachable households by 20% active share and 4 orders per active household per month. What unit does the result have?","o":["Households per order","Orders per person per year","Rupees per year","Orders per month"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic steady monthly volume is 40,000 orders, with the same volume each month for 12 months. Average net sales are Rs 250 per order, excluding taxes and refunds. What is annual net sales under these assumptions?","o":["Rs 1 crore","Rs 12 crore","Rs 10 crore","Rs 100 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic monthly delivery plan: forecast volume is 60,000 orders, but the team can fulfill only 45,000 orders at the stated service level. No outsourcing or backlog is allowed. What is the maximum fulfilled volume for this month?","o":["60,000 orders","45,000 orders","105,000 orders","15,000 orders"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic downside: reachable households remain 50,000. Active share falls to 16% and frequency to 2.5 orders per active household per month. What is downside monthly volume?","o":["20,000 orders","40,000 orders","125,000 orders","8,000 orders"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic unit economics: each fulfilled order contributes Rs 30 after all variable costs. Monthly avoidable operating fixed costs are Rs 9 lakh. What monthly fulfilled volume covers these fixed costs, ignoring tax and financing?","o":["30,000 orders","27,000 orders","3,000 orders","300,000 orders"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic no-launch comparison: a new delivery zone records 10,000 monthly orders, but 2,000 replace orders the same company would have served in its existing zone. No other orders change. What is the increase in total company order volume?","o":["12,000 orders","10,000 orders","2,000 orders","8,000 orders"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic estimate review: two household datasets differ because one counts city residents and the other counts households inside the delivery radius, from different years. What should happen before combining them?","o":["Average residents and households without converting units, retaining both original years.","Reconcile unit, year, geography and coverage; use the reachable-household base and show uncertainty.","Multiply both population counts because the sources describe complementary markets.","Use the larger geography count and the smaller-area adoption rate without reconciling coverage."],"a":1}]},"7":{"objectives":["Use worked answers to identify two provisional learning needs, correct the reasoning and test fresh transfer without treating day-level scores as question-level diagnosis."],"videos":[{"id":"HYGIoe-QXhU","title":"McKinsey Case Interview: Market Sizing Walkthrough","by":"Management Consulted","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"CMU: learning principles","url":"https://www.cmu.edu/teaching/principles/learning.html","note":"Read mastery, targeted practice/feedback and self-directed learning principles; underlying bibliography is not a complete literature review."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"CMU: retrieval practice","url":"https://www.cmu.edu/teaching/resources/instructionalstrategies/activelearningstrategies/retrievalpractice/index.html","note":"Read whole guidance including task-type differences, open-book benefits, feedback and delayed retention. No universal superiority or guaranteed individual result is implied."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"SBA: market research questions","url":"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/plan-your-business/","note":"Revisit only market research/competitive analysis as context for the fresh scenario, not US business-registration or financing law."}],"exercise":"Review lab, not a measured diagnosis of the learner or a new platform score. Existing day-level quiz scores do not reveal per-question skill weaknesses. Do not claim the app has identified the weakest two measured skills. Instead, create a manual log from actual worked responses: question, your reasoning, result, error category, corrected calculation, evidence/source limitation and next test. Suggested categories are definition/units, arithmetic, overlapping populations, capacity, source inference and decision communication. One mistake can have several causes; a low total score alone does not identify them.\nFirst solve this fresh synthetic variant without reading its worked solution: zones A/B reach 24000/18000 households with 4000 overlap;20% active and 3 orders per active household/month. Capacity is 20000 orders/month, no backlog/outsourcing. Contribution is 35 rupees per fulfilled order, avoidable fixed costs 5.6 lakh/month. Define the requested/fulfilled volumes and operating surplus. Then reconcile the solution:38000 unique households,22800 requested orders,20000 fulfilled,7 lakh contribution,1.4 lakh operating surplus before all excluded items. The implied operating break-even is 16000 fulfilled orders. A lower-demand scenario at 15% active and 2.5 frequency yields 14250 orders and minus 61250 rupees surplus under the same costs. Treat all figures as fictional; no current service/price/local fact.\nSelect two provisional learning needs only after inspecting your written work. Explain one error mechanism for each and distinguish an input-knowledge gap from an arithmetic slip. If there are fewer than two evidenced gaps, choose one maintenance/transfer target instead of inventing a deficit. Set a specific next task with answer criteria, then solve an unseen variant after a delay and compare workings. Retrying the identical option order may reflect recognition, so vary the inputs/structure without assuming a harder-looking problem proves transfer.\nCMU guidance supports goal-directed practice with useful feedback and monitoring. It distinguishes short-answer, free-recall and multiple-choice tasks; it does not say one form is best for every goal or that open-book retrieval has no benefit. Start closed-note where feasible to expose what you can generate, then use sources/feedback to correct reasoning. Do not turn mistakes into a character judgment or record a fabricated time-to-mastery. A correct answer with wrong reasoning still needs review, while a wrong answer does not prove a stable inability.\nSubmit the error log, corrected workings, a brief explanation of the two selected targets and a next-week fresh test with a key kept separate until after attempting it. Share only fictional/business-classroom data; no private health/customer/employee records. These practice criteria do not change platform unlock rules or claim question-level analytics. No clinical, legal, credit, launch or spending authority follows.","quiz":[{"q":"The app shows only day-level totals. What evidence supports choosing two provisional learning targets?","o":["The longest reading page, treated as the only possible weak topic.","The lowest day total, automatically mapped to two exact question-level skills.","Inspection of the learner actual worked steps and error patterns.","The highest score, used to infer identical mastery of every underlying skill."],"a":2},{"q":"Zones 24000/18000 overlap 4000.20% active and 3 orders/active/month. What requested volume follows?","o":["20000 orders/month.","25200 orders/month.","38000 orders/month.","22800 orders/month."],"a":3},{"q":"Requested 22800, capacity 20000, no backlog/outsourcing,35 contribution/order and 560000 fixed costs. What is operating surplus?","o":["140000 rupees.","238000 rupees.","700000 rupees.","78400 rupees."],"a":0},{"q":"A learner obtains the right number by canceling incompatible units. What should the review record?","o":["The answer matched, but the reasoning/units need correction and a fresh check.","No error, because only the final numerical choice measures understanding.","A permanent inability to learn quantitative reasoning from one attempt.","A new mastery percentage inferred from this single correct answer."],"a":0},{"q":"Which task gives more direct evidence of transfer than repeating identical choices?","o":["Read only the correct option and declare the error category permanently solved.","Retry the same option positions until the response becomes familiar.","Copy the worked solution and count copying speed as independent recall.","Solve a fresh scenario with changed inputs and explain the method before seeing feedback."],"a":3},{"q":"Only one error mechanism is supported by the work. What should the two-target review do?","o":["Replace the recorded error with two broad personality traits for easier tracking.","Invent a second measured deficit because a two-target template requires two failures.","Infer all other skills are mastered because only one mistake was observed.","Use one evidenced repair plus a stated maintenance/transfer target, without inventing a second weakness."],"a":3},{"q":"Which statement fits the CMU retrieval-practice guidance?","o":["One closed-note attempt guarantees long-term mastery for all learners.","Multiple-choice is always best for every kind of transfer and retention.","Open-book retrieval never helps because no memory is involved in any such task.","Task type should match the learning goal; feedback and repeated recall can help."],"a":3},{"q":"A manual error log says \"I am bad at business.\" What revision makes it actionable?","o":["State the specific step, its cause hypothesis, corrected method and next test.","Replace it with a confident score without showing the attempted work.","Keep the label and remove the example so it applies to every future task.","Attribute every mistake to motivation without checking units or source definitions."],"a":0},{"q":"A fresh downside uses 38000 households,15% active,2.5 orders/month,35 unit contribution and 560000 fixed costs. What surplus follows if all demand is fulfilled?","o":["Plus 140000 rupees.","Plus 61250 rupees.","Minus 61250 rupees.","Minus 14250 rupees."],"a":2},{"q":"What does a next-week fresh test result establish?","o":["A causal proof that the entire course is complete because practice occurred.","A permanent ranking of all business capabilities from one item.","Performance on that specified task, with limits on generalization and need for further practice.","A clinical explanation for any wrong answer without further assessment."],"a":2}]},"8":{"objectives":["Structure a fictional outpatient service-capacity decision using a process map, consistent flow units and testable constraints; distinguish a toy upper bound from a clinical forecast."],"videos":[{"id":"bhOhCpBYpDk","title":"What Is MECE Framework? (And How To Use It In Your Case Interviews!)","by":"My Consulting Offer","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT: actual capacity lecture","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760b-introduction-to-operations-management-spring-2004/16dde953a29f89a4d2018361900ce886_lec3_capacity.pdf","note":"Read process-flow, demand/capacity, min throughput and congestion inputs near the start. 2004 teaching examples are not current hospital planning standards."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT Lean Academy glossary","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-660j-introduction-to-lean-six-sigma-methods-january-iap-2012/cb7b7689f877c88831caea046d125a59_MIT16_660JIAP12_Glossary.pdf","note":"Read capacity/activity-time/bottleneck entries only; glossary wording is not a complete queueing model or clinical staffing standard."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Bain: clarify objectives and explain reasoning","url":"https://www.bain.com/careers/hiring-process/case-interview/","note":"Use the practical tips as communication guidance, not a substitute for service-capacity evidence."}],"exercise":"Fictional service-operations lab set in an outpatient facility considering a Jaipur site, not a current hospital-market study, clinical decision or staffing/triage recommendation. No real patients, identifiable records or safety/clinical threshold. Treat each toy visit as requiring registration, clinician and checkout once in a serial process with aligned sessions, no rework, shared-resource conflict or skill-mix difference. All rates must use visits/day on the same operating-day basis.\nBuild a three-level issue tree from the decision rather than pasting a generic acronym. Top branches may be demand/reach, feasible service flow, economic/cash feasibility and safety/compliance/readiness. This is an organizing proposal, not proof every real issue is covered or causally independent. Define which costs and constraints belong where and show cross-links rather than double count them. MECE means checking overlap and missing scope relative to the decision; labels alone do not prove a valid model. Demand and capacity are separate constraints, not additive sources of visits.\nToy requested visits 180/day; registration 200/day, clinicians 120/day and checkout 150/day. Feasible steady daily throughput is at mostmin(180,200,120,150)=120 under stated assumptions. Improving registration to 240 does not raise this bound; raising clinicians to 160 moves the bound to 150 at checkout. If demand instead falls to 90/day with original resources, throughput is at most 90; the clinician stage is no longer a binding actual-demand constraint even though it remains the lowest-capacity stage. In a balanced system, several constraints can bind; do not insist every process always has one unique bottleneck.\nA capacity rate is not an observed throughput, quality guarantee or wait-time forecast. Real arrival/service variability, appointment timing, clinician skills, case mix, emergencies, rooms/equipment, routing, buffers and safety rules require evidence. Monthly average demand cannot settle peak-hour congestion. More doctors do not automatically create interchangeable capacity or safely shorter visits. Do not solve a planning exercise by prescribing reduced care, overtime or clinical prioritization.\nFor a separate non-clinical financial toy with 120 fulfilled visits/day,20 operating days/month,600 rupees contribution/visit before avoidable fixed costs 10 lakh/month, contribution is 14.4 lakh and surplus 4.4 lakh before tax/financing/other excluded items. Hypothetical break-even is 1666.67 visits/month, so 1667 whole visits if costs truly stay fixed over this range; this is not evidence the clinic should open or an actual fee. No patient-price or treatment decision follows.\nSubmit the issue tree, serial process table, unit-checked throughput limits before/after two changes, economic toy and an evidence plan. For each branch specify a question, a falsifying observation, data needed and a fictional owner. Do not claim an attractive spreadsheet establishes a license, safe staffing or authorization. Cite source depth and record what would require a competent clinical/regulatory reviewer before any real action.","quiz":[{"q":"For a serial toy with demand 180, registration 200, clinicians 120 and checkout 150 visits/day, what throughput upper bound follows?","o":["120 visits/day.","650 visits/day.","180 visits/day.","150 visits/day."],"a":0},{"q":"Only registration rises 200 to 240; other toy limits remain 180/120/150. What happens to the upper bound?","o":["It rises 240 visits/day because the improved step sets every downstream rate.","It rises 160 visits/day because registration gained 40.","It remains 120 visits/day.","It rises 180 visits/day because all demand becomes automatically served."],"a":2},{"q":"Clinicians rise 120 to 160 with demand 180, registration 200 and checkout 150. What new bound follows?","o":["180 visits/day, because demand is the target.","160 visits/day, because only the changed stage matters.","150 visits/day, now constrained by checkout.","510 visits/day, by summing all stage capacities."],"a":2},{"q":"Original stage capacities are 200/120/150 but demand falls 90/day. What follows under the toy assumptions?","o":["Throughput stays 120/day because a bottleneck must always be fully utilized.","Throughput is at most 90/day; the lowest-capacity stage need not be demand-binding.","Throughput rises 210/day by adding demand to clinician capacity.","Throughput falls 30/day by subtracting demand from clinician capacity."],"a":1},{"q":"A clinic averages fewer requested visits than daily capacity. What additional evidence is needed before predicting wait times?","o":["Only the annual revenue target, converted into visits at an assumed fee.","Only the difference between the two daily averages, without a time pattern.","Arrival/service timing and variability, routing and resource constraints.","Only the slowest stage name, without its capacity or demand loading."],"a":2},{"q":"Which process-table comparison is directly usable without another conversion?","o":["Doctor headcount alone and annual neighborhood population without schedules.","Demand in patients/month and doctor capacity in hours/week without visit times.","Registration in households/day and checkout in rupees/month without mappings.","Demand and all stage rates in visits per the same operating day."],"a":3},{"q":"What makes an issue tree useful for this fictional service decision?","o":["Separate profit and revenue branches that count the same revenue gain twice.","A familiar acronym whose branch names are treated as proof of completeness.","Decision-linked branches with defined scope, overlap checks, constraints and evidence tests.","Independent branch scores averaged without checking hard safety constraints."],"a":2},{"q":"A toy has 120 fulfilled visits/day,20 days/month and 600 rupees contribution/visit before 10 lakh fixed costs. What is monthly surplus?","o":["4.4 lakh rupees.","14.4 lakh rupees.","7.2 lakh rupees.","Minus 8.56 lakh rupees."],"a":0},{"q":"What can the simple rate-bound model establish for a real clinic?","o":["A clinical triage rule because the model uses visits rather than orders.","A conditional capacity upper bound, not safe staffing, clinical quality or actual waiting time.","A license to shorten consultations until every doctor reaches the upper bound.","A guarantee all visits have the same clinical needs and resource consumption."],"a":1},{"q":"New observation shows many visits require a second clinician step and shared rooms. What should change?","o":["Treat rework as zero time because a visit was already counted on arrival.","Keep the original bound because changing structure would reduce comparability.","Add all clinician and room counts directly to demand without time conversions.","Revise routing/resource requirements and recompute before relying on the one-pass serial bound."],"a":3}]},"9":{"objectives":["Build a serial outpatient capacity tree with consistent visit/day units; separate usable staffing, demand and flow constraints.","Distinguish an operating break-even screen from a safe clinical or full investment decision."],"videos":[{"id":"bhOhCpBYpDk","title":"What Is MECE Framework? (And How To Use It In Your Case Interviews!)","by":"My Consulting Offer","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT: capacity and process flow, opening lecture section","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760b-introduction-to-operations-management-spring-2004/16dde953a29f89a4d2018361900ce886_lec3_capacity.pdf","note":"Read demand, process capacity and bottleneck logic; this exercise is original and omits clinical complexity."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA: relevant costs","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f5/technical-articles/relevant-costs.html","note":"Distinguish future incremental costs from irrecoverable historical spending."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic planning case, not clinical guidance or a company forecast. A proposed Jaipur outpatient site has 200 requested visits/day and serial usable capacities of registration 180, consultation 120 and checkout 150 visits/day. All visits require all stages. Compute the throughput upper bound; test registration 240 alone, then consultation 160 alone. Separately convert 3 doctors x 6 usable hours x 60 / 20 minutes into 54 consultations/day: these are alternative staffing inputs, not an extra capacity to add to the 120 case. With Rs 500 contribution/visit and Rs 30,000 avoidable operating fixed cost/day, calculate operating break-even. Draw a three-level tree: local demand -> catchment/referrals; usable supply -> staffing/session time; delivery -> serial bottleneck. Record units, source date, quality/safety constraints missing from this toy model, uncertainty and a test that would reject opening. Do not authorize a clinical launch from this arithmetic.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic outpatient planning exercise, not clinical advice: every visit needs registration, consultation and checkout. Their usable capacities are 180, 120 and 150 visits/day. Demand is 160/day. With no other constraints, what is the upper bound on completed visits/day?","o":["450","150","160","120"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic staffing arithmetic: 3 doctors each have 6 usable consultation hours/day. Every visit uses 20 doctor-minutes; one doctor sees one patient at a time. Ignoring all other constraints, how many consultations/day can this staffing support?","o":["54","360","18","90"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic clinic: the roster offers 80 consultation slots/day before an explicitly specified 25% reduction for leave and unavailable sessions. No other reductions apply. What is usable consultation capacity?","o":["60 slots/day","100 slots/day","20 slots/day","80 slots/day"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic pilot: 70 visits/day are requested and usable capacity is 90/day. Assume all requested visits can be served and no backlog or other constraints. What is expected completed volume?","o":["160 visits/day","70 visits/day","20 visits/day","90 visits/day"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic sequential clinic: registration can process 180 visits/day, consultations 120/day and checkout 150/day. Demand is 200/day. Registration is expanded to 240/day while all other capacities stay fixed. What is the new upper bound on completed visits?","o":["150/day","180/day","120/day","240/day"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic same clinic: registration capacity is 180/day, consultation capacity improves from 120 to 160/day, checkout is 150/day and demand is 200/day. What is the new process capacity?","o":["160/day","150/day","200/day","120/day"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic operating-cost screen: each completed visit contributes Rs 500 after all variable costs. Avoidable fixed operating costs are Rs 30,000/day. What volume covers these costs, excluding launch investment, tax and financing?","o":["15 visits/day","6 visits/day","150 visits/day","60 visits/day"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic pilot choice: a completed feasibility study cost Rs 4 lakh, cannot be recovered, and is identical under both future options. Which treatment is correct in the incremental comparison?","o":["Include it as a future cash outflow because accounting still records it","Exclude it from future-option differences; retain it in historical spend","Allocate half to each option and use it to rank the options","Include it only in the option with the larger future budget"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic evidence check: a chain uses its existing metro clinic visit count as the demand forecast for a proposed Jaipur site without testing geography, case mix or referral access. What is the sound next step?","o":["Use the metro count unchanged until the first Jaipur quarter closes","Replace local demand research with the usable doctor-capacity figure","Scale the metro count by population and treat the result as observed demand","Validate local catchment/referrals and show a supported uncertainty range"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic scheduling pilot: visits completed increase from 100/day before a change to 110/day afterward. Demand and staffing also changed, and there is no comparison group. What can this establish?","o":["A causal gain of 10 visits/day because before and after use the same site","A transferable 10-visit/day gain after adjusting only for the site name","An observed 10-visit/day gain without identified causal attribution","A zero effect because an uncontrolled observation contains no information"],"a":2}]},"10":{"objectives":["Audit a paid-contract renewal claim using eligibility, cohort dates and source reconciliation.","Use interviews to explain outcomes without treating current-customer testimony as a renewal census."],"videos":[{"id":"NZp5j5hvn9I","title":"50 Founders Share How They Got Their First Customers","by":"Y Combinator","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ChartMogul: Net MRR Retention report","url":"https://help.chartmogul.com/article/143-chart-net-mrr-retention","note":"Vendor-specific settings and reactivation treatment matter; NRR differs from contract renewal."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK: finding research participants","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/find-user-research-participants","note":"Read recruitment bias, former/likely users, permission and privacy. This is research guidance, not an Indian legal compliance opinion."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic evidence audit: quarter-end ledger lists 120 organizations: 100 paying annual contracts due this quarter, of which 80 renewed and 20 did not; 20 unconverted free pilots have no paid renewal due. Ten interviews with current users include nine positive opinions. Calculate paid-contract renewal and explain why nine positive interviews do not verify 90% renewal. Build an evidence log with organization IDs, contract dates, due status, outcome, source date, duplicates and unresolved records; use only the minimum personal data needed. Recruit retained and non-renewing customers through permitted, voluntary routes, including relevant access needs, and record nonresponse/selection bias. Label reasons as reported explanations, not proven causes. Reconcile any claimed NRR separately with opening recurring revenue, churn/contraction/expansion and reactivation policy. State what remains unknown, who verifies the ledger and what evidence would change the claim. No real outreach is authorized by this exercise.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic ledger: 100 paying contracts are due this quarter; 80 renew. Twenty free pilots have no paid renewal due. Under the stated paid-contract definition, which result is correct?","o":["80/120 = 66.7%","100/100 = 100%","100/120 = 83.3%","80/100 = 80%"],"a":3},{"q":"Nine of ten interviewed current payroll users express satisfaction. What does this directly measure?","o":["90% NRR after removing customer expansion","90% renewal of all contracts due during the quarter","90% paid retention after excluding unconverted pilots","90% satisfaction among respondents, not cohort renewal."],"a":3},{"q":"The renewal ledger says 80/100; a slide says 90% with no denominator. What is the best audit action?","o":["Average 80% and 90% and show 85% as the central estimate","Prefer 90% because it is the most recent presentation","Reconcile source records, cohort dates, eligibility and duplicates first.","Keep 80% but omit the conflict to avoid distracting readers"],"a":2},{"q":"Which recruitment plan best addresses current-customer selection bias?","o":["Interview the ten most active users again using a longer questionnaire","Ask the sales team to select its strongest reference customers","Recruit eligible renewers and non-renewers with voluntary permitted contact; record nonresponse.","Increase the number of current users until the sample reaches 100"],"a":2},{"q":"A salesperson proposes excluding all 20 non-renewals because they were low-engagement users, although they were paid contracts due. What should happen?","o":["Exclude them because engagement predicts product fit","Keep eligible non-renewals in the cohort; segment engagement separately.","Move them into the free-pilot group to keep renewal definitions stable","Count them as renewals until an interview confirms the reason for leaving"],"a":1},{"q":"A former customer reports switching because support was slow. Which conclusion is supported by this interview alone?","o":["Slow support caused every non-renewal in the cohort","The company should reverse all pricing changes immediately","No useful evidence exists until a randomized experiment is run","One reported reason to check against tickets, dates and other evidence."],"a":3},{"q":"Opening-cohort recurring revenue rises, but overdue invoices also rise. What should an evidence note say?","o":["Higher NRR confirms every invoice in the cohort was collected","NRR can rise while collections weaken; inspect aging and cash separately","Overdue invoices should be subtracted from the customer count","Recurring revenue growth proves cash runway improved"],"a":1},{"q":"Two NRR exports differ because one excludes reactivations and the other includes them. What should the analyst do?","o":["Record the settings and compare like definitions before interpreting change","Average both NRR values to remove vendor-setting effects","Treat the larger export as more complete and discard the other","Call the difference new customer revenue and add it to both exports"],"a":0},{"q":"For research recruitment, which data-handling plan best matches the cited guidance?","o":["Store every contact field indefinitely for later research convenience","Publish identifiable non-renewal reasons so the team can verify them","Minimize details, restrict access and delete when participation management no longer needs them.","Share the full participant list with all interviewees to improve response"],"a":2},{"q":"After reconciliation, two of the 80 recorded renewals are duplicates; all 100 eligible contract IDs remain valid. What is the corrected renewal rate?","o":["80%, because the original slide was already circulated","76%, because every duplicate removes a numerator and denominator entry","79.6%, using 78/98 after changing eligibility","78%, using 78 verified renewals / 100 eligible contracts"],"a":3}]},"11":{"objectives":["Calculate contract renewal, logo retention, NRR and GRR with fixed eligibility and period definitions.","Explain how expansion can mask customer loss and why recurring revenue is not collected cash."],"videos":[{"id":"NZp5j5hvn9I","title":"50 Founders Share How They Got Their First Customers","by":"Y Combinator","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ChartMogul: NRR calculation and settings","url":"https://help.chartmogul.com/article/143-chart-net-mrr-retention","note":"Read the full report conditions; toy questions explicitly fix cohort membership and do not add reactivations."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ChartMogul: gross versus net retention","url":"https://chartmogul.com/blog/gross-vs-net-retention/","note":"Read definitions and cohort segmentation. Historical vendor benchmarks are not universal targets."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic retention lab. Part A: 100 paid annual contracts are due, 80 renew, and 20 unconverted free pilots are outside paid eligibility. Part B is a separate revenue dataset: fixed opening cohort MRR Rs 10 lakh; churn Rs 1 lakh, contraction Rs 0.5 lakh, expansion Rs 2 lakh; no reactivations. New-customer MRR Rs 3 lakh is outside that cohort. Calculate paid renewal 80%, NRR 105% and GRR 85%; do not mix the separate denominators. Verify customer-level GRR by capping each ending customer MRR at its opening MRR. Explain how 15/20 surviving logos can coexist with Rs 2.2 lakh / Rs 2 lakh = 110% NRR. Build a reconciliation with source date, cohort boundary and settings; report cash aging separately. If using real records, anonymize responsibly and do not claim these synthetic figures describe a company.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic payroll startup: exactly 100 paying customers have annual contracts due for renewal this quarter. Eighty renew. Twenty unconverted free pilots also exist, but have no paid contract to renew. Define paid-contract logo renewal as renewing eligible paying customers divided by eligible paying customers. What is it?","o":["80%","66.7%","100%","20%"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic renewal review: of 200 paying customers, 50 have a contract due to renew this month and 45 of those renew. The other 150 are not yet due. Using renewing eligible contracts / contracts due, what is this month's renewal rate?","o":["22.5%","90%","97.5%","75%"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic fixed opening cohort: beginning monthly recurring revenue is Rs 10 lakh. During the period, that cohort loses Rs 1 lakh to cancellations and Rs 0.5 lakh to downgrades, and gains Rs 2 lakh from expansions. New customers contribute Rs 3 lakh but are outside this cohort. What is NRR?","o":["105%","145%","115%","85%"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic opening cohort: beginning MRR is Rs 10 lakh; churn loses Rs 1 lakh and downgrades lose Rs 0.5 lakh. Expansion adds Rs 2 lakh. Define GRR to exclude all expansion. What is GRR?","o":["105%","95%","100%","85%"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic three-customer cohort: starting MRR is A Rs 100, B Rs 100 and C Rs 100. Ending MRR is A Rs 200, B Rs 50 and C Rs 0. Define GRR using each customer's ending MRR capped at its starting MRR. What is GRR?","o":["50%","83.3%","66.7%","100%"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic opening cohort: 20 paying customers begin the year; 15 remain at year end. Their combined MRR rises from Rs 2 lakh at the start to Rs 2.2 lakh at the end. What pair correctly describes logo retention and NRR?","o":["75% logo retention and 110% NRR","75% logo retention and 75% NRR","110% logo retention and 75% NRR","110% for both"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic comparison: cohort A has 10 contracts due and 9 renewals; cohort B has 90 contracts due and 63 renewals. What is the combined eligible-contract renewal rate?","o":["80%","90%","72%","70%"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic definition check: opening MRR is Rs 5 lakh; the same opening customers end at Rs 4 lakh. New customers add Rs 2 lakh. What is fixed-cohort NRR?","o":["120%","80%","40%","100%"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic research: a founder claims 90% renewal based only on interviews with ten currently active customers. No renewal ledger or former-customer evidence was checked. What should the analyst do?","o":["Recruit more active users and use their satisfaction share as renewal","Accept 90% provisionally as a renewal estimate because interviews were direct","Reconcile eligible outcomes and include non-renewing as well as retained perspectives","Weight active interviews by MRR and use the result as contract renewal"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic payroll dashboard: cohort NRR is 110%, but the report contains no cash collections or overdue invoice data. What conclusion is justified?","o":["The cohort retained every opening logo and added new customers","Expansion collections exceeded cancellation losses by exactly 10%","Recurring cohort value grew; collections and overdue balances need separate evidence","Net profit grew 10% because recurring revenue grew 10%"],"a":2}]},"12":{"objectives":["Write an answer-first channel recommendation supported by a consistent return, cash and capital comparison.","Expose constraints and reversal conditions; do not treat presentation quality as economic proof."],"videos":[{"id":"elMPIxLYtoc","title":"Consultant Explains the Pyramid Principle","by":"Analyst Academy","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"McKinsey: Barbara Minto on structured thinking","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/alumni/news-and-events/global-news/alumni-news/barbara-minto-mece-i-invented-it-so-i-get-to-say-how-to-pronounce-it","note":"Read the profile for logical grouping; this is not the full paid book or a financial decision rule."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Aswath Damodaran: return measures","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/adamodar/pdfiles/papers/returnmeasures.pdf","note":"Focus on after-tax operating profit, invested capital and timing conventions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Aswath Damodaran: cash flow definitions","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/dcfcf.pdf","note":"Separate operating profit from reinvestment and cash flow."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic one-year comparison, Rs lakh. Both options have the same risk and timing for this limited screen; no debt, capex, depreciation, disposal or growth beyond year 1. Constant capital is fully invested at start and unchanged through year end. A has incremental NOPAT 24, invested capital/initial cash 120; B has 18 and 60. Compare NOPAT/capital (20%,30%) and absolute NOPAT (24,18). Budget is 80, options are indivisible and financing is unavailable: only B fits. Do not call this ratio an NPV or lifetime investment verdict. Separately, if B needs 10 additional working capital at year end, year-1 operating free cash flow is 18-10=8, ignoring the initial 60 outlay; show initial and end-year cash separately and revisit return-capital timing rather than silently keeping a constant-capital assumption. Write one answer-first page: recommend B under the stated budget; give supporting math, unresolved demand evidence, source dates and a reversal condition. Include one chart with capital on x, annual NOPAT on y, units and the 80 budget boundary. A presentation is a proposal, not board approval or permission to spend.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic one-year channel screen: A NOPAT 24 on constant invested capital 120; B 18 on 60, all Rs lakh. What are the return ratios?","o":["A 20%; B 30%","A 5%; B 3.33%","A 30%; B 20%","A 24%; B 18%"],"a":0},{"q":"Using that case alone, what tradeoff is supported?","o":["A has higher absolute NOPAT; B has higher NOPAT per unit of capital","B has both greater absolute NOPAT and a lower capital requirement","A dominates because a larger profit proves a larger NPV","B dominates because a higher ratio proves a longer economic life"],"a":0},{"q":"Budget is Rs 80 lakh, options A=120 and B=60 are indivisible, and financing is unavailable. What follows?","o":["Choose A because its annual profit is Rs 6 lakh larger","Split A to fit the budget although the option is indivisible","Choose both because their combined return exceeds 20%","B fits; A does not fit the stated capital constraint"],"a":3},{"q":"Separate B cash variant: NOPAT is 18 and additional year-end working capital is 10, with no depreciation or capex. What is year-1 operating free cash flow before the initial investment?","o":["28 lakh","8 lakh","60 lakh","18 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"Which opening best reflects answer-first reasoning for the constrained synthetic case?","o":["The market is changing quickly and our research process had several stages","Both channels are attractive, so the detailed appendix contains our decision","Recommend B within the Rs 80 lakh budget; validate demand before commitment","Choose A for profit and omit the budget until the financing team reviews it"],"a":2},{"q":"Which chart best supports the supplied capital/profit tradeoff without inventing a lifetime value?","o":["An unlabeled bar chart titled Best Channel with only return percentages","Capital (Rs lakh) on x, annual NOPAT (Rs lakh) on y, with A/B and budget boundary labeled","A chart of total company valuation copied from a listed competitor","A cumulative cash chart that omits initial investment for both channels"],"a":1},{"q":"A peer reviewer finds that A capital is measured at year-end but B uses a full-year average. What should happen?","o":["Keep the higher ratio because both denominators use rupees","Average the two ratios to remove measurement timing effects","Replace capital with revenue for both to avoid the mismatch","Align and disclose numerator/denominator timing before comparing"],"a":3},{"q":"Sales forecasts for B disagree by source and could make its NOPAT negative. Which answer-first note is strongest?","o":["Keep the budget conclusion distinct, show the source conflict and a demand threshold that changes the profit case","Remove the lower forecast because it would confuse the board","Use the average without naming the source conflict","Choose B with certainty because the capital ratio was higher in the base case"],"a":0},{"q":"The board says the page is clear but has not approved spending. What is accurate?","o":["The analyst may spend up to the budget because it was shown on the chart","Clear communication establishes approval for the lowest-cost option","The analysis is understood; investment authorization remains unconfirmed","The strongest base-case ratio replaces the need for an owner decision"],"a":2},{"q":"Verified evidence adds working capital to B and changes its capital timing. What should the analyst revise?","o":["Only the chart color because the recommendation is answer-first","The cash timeline and capital convention, then the ratios and conditional recommendation","The profit numerator only, leaving all capital amounts unchanged","The budget limit so B still fits without changing the recommendation"],"a":1}]},"13":{"objectives":["Calculate incremental EBIT, NOPAT and a defined constant-capital return; distinguish scale, scarce-resource allocation and cannibalization.","Write a conditional recommendation that keeps evidence and funding gates visible."],"videos":[{"id":"elMPIxLYtoc","title":"Consultant Explains the Pyramid Principle","by":"Analyst Academy","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran: return measures","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/adamodar/pdfiles/papers/returnmeasures.pdf","note":"Read operating profit, tax, invested capital and timing conventions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA: relevant costs","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f5/technical-articles/relevant-costs.html","note":"Use the future incremental difference, including opportunity costs."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"McKinsey: structured problem solving","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/how-to-master-the-seven-step-problem-solving-process","note":"Use defined decisions, priority tests and uncertainty, not a rigid universal framework."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic channel lab, not company data. Annual 20,000 incremental orders have net sales Rs 500/order and all variable costs Rs 350/order. Avoidable annual fixed operating costs Rs 10 lakh include all relevant depreciation; no displacement. Compute EBIT Rs 20 lakh, NOPAT Rs 15 lakh at 25% operating tax with no adjustments, and defined ROIC 25% on Rs 60 lakh constant invested capital. Compare a separate B with NOPAT Rs 18 lakh on Rs 120 lakh: more absolute profit but 15% ROIC. In a separate cannibalization variant, new sales 10,000 units include 3,000 displaced existing units; new contribution Rs 120/unit, lost existing contribution Rs 150/unit, new fixed costs Rs 2 lakh/year. Compute incremental EBIT Rs 5.5 lakh. Do not combine these separate datasets. Stress a downside 15,000 orders x Rs 100 contribution less Rs 10 lakh fixed = Rs 5 lakh EBIT. Explain why passing a 20% ratio screen on assumptions is insufficient without credible demand and a funded cash plan. Show units, sources, unknowns, owner and a decision gate; no real spending is authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic annual channel plan: 20,000 orders have net sales of Rs 500/order and variable costs of Rs 350/order. Additional avoidable annual fixed operating costs are Rs 10 lakh, including all relevant depreciation. No orders displace existing sales. What is incremental EBIT?","o":["Rs 30 lakh","Rs 10 lakh","Rs 100 lakh","Rs 20 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic channel: incremental annual EBIT is Rs 20 lakh, the operating tax rate is 25%, and no tax adjustments apply. What is incremental after-tax operating profit?","o":["Rs 15 lakh","Rs 5 lakh","Rs 20 lakh","Rs 25 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic return screen: annual incremental after-tax operating profit is Rs 15 lakh. Incremental invested capital is Rs 60 lakh at the start and stays unchanged all year. Define incremental ROIC as that profit divided by that capital. What is ROIC?","o":["25%","40%","20%","4%"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic channel comparison: A adds annual after-tax operating profit of Rs 15 lakh on Rs 60 lakh capital. B adds Rs 18 lakh on Rs 120 lakh. Which statement is correct?","o":["B has 15% ROIC and higher profit, so it must create greater lifetime value","A and B have equal ROIC after adjusting capital for the profit difference","A has 25% ROIC, B 15%; B adds more profit, and the ratios alone do not settle the choice","A has higher ROIC and higher absolute annual operating profit"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic no-launch comparison: a new channel sells 10,000 units/year. Of these, 3,000 would otherwise have sold through the existing channel. New-channel contribution is Rs 120/unit; displaced existing-channel contribution is Rs 150/unit. New annual avoidable fixed costs are Rs 2 lakh. What is incremental operating profit before tax?","o":["Rs 10 lakh","Rs 3.5 lakh","Rs 12 lakh","Rs 5.5 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic constrained packing line: channel A contributes Rs 120/order and uses 2 packing minutes/order; B contributes Rs 90/order and uses 1 minute/order. All other constraints and incremental fixed costs are equal, and demand for both is sufficient. Which first allocation maximizes contribution per scarce minute?","o":["A, because Rs 120/order exceeds Rs 90/order","B, with Rs 90/minute versus A's Rs 60/minute","Either, because contribution per minute is equal","A, with Rs 240/minute"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic channel downside: annual volume falls to 15,000 orders, contribution falls to Rs 100/order, and avoidable fixed operating costs remain Rs 10 lakh/year. What is incremental annual EBIT?","o":["Rs 15 lakh","Rs 10 lakh","Rs 5 lakh","Rs 25 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic operating threshold: contribution is Rs 150/order and avoidable fixed operating costs are Rs 12 lakh/year. There is no cannibalization or other cost. What annual volume covers those costs?","o":["800 orders","80,000 orders","8,000 orders","1,800 orders"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic pilot: new-channel sales rise after launch, but pricing, seasonality and existing-channel promotions also change. What evidence would most help estimate incremental company sales?","o":["New-channel gross sales with a pre-launch company total as the denominator","The fastest post-launch week compared with the slowest earlier week","Current-customer satisfaction weighted by new-channel order value","A credible no-launch comparison of company-wide sales including displaced existing sales"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic board page: the approved screen requires at least 20% annual incremental ROIC, credible demand evidence and a funded cash plan before rollout. The model shows 25% ROIC, but demand evidence and cash funding remain unresolved. What is the sound recommendation?","o":["Approve rollout because the ratio gate is sufficient once a model exists","State the ratio screen passes on assumptions; defer rollout for the named evidence/funding gates","Call the ratio provisional but start rollout while evidence is collected","Treat the missing funding as a sunk cost and preserve the launch date"],"a":1}]},"14":{"objectives":["Use worked-answer evidence to choose provisional learning targets and test transfer on a fresh variant.","Update a business recommendation when contradictory inputs change contribution or feasibility; keep learning diagnosis separate from business metrics."],"videos":[{"id":"elMPIxLYtoc","title":"Consultant Explains the Pyramid Principle","by":"Analyst Academy","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"CMU: principles of learning","url":"https://www.cmu.edu/teaching/principles/learning.html","note":"Use prior knowledge, goal-directed practice and feedback; do not infer measured subskills from a total day score."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"CMU: retrieval practice","url":"https://www.cmu.edu/teaching/resources/instructionalstrategies/activelearningstrategies/retrievalpractice/index.html","note":"Attempt from memory, check feedback, then revisit with a fresh task."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"McKinsey: structured problem solving","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/how-to-master-the-seven-step-problem-solving-process","note":"Revise the model when context changes and keep uncertainty explicit."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic weekly review. Without notes solve: requested orders 24,000/month, usable capacity 18,000/month, all orders serviceable, net contribution Rs 40/order and avoidable fixed cost Rs 5.4 lakh/month. Fulfilled 18,000; operating surplus Rs 1.8 lakh; break-even 13,500. Then new verified cost evidence lowers contribution to Rs 25/order with demand/capacity unchanged. Recompute surplus minus Rs 90,000 and break-even 21,600, above capacity. A third, separate sensitivity gives usable capacity 22,000 and demand 24,000 at Rs 25/order: surplus Rs 10,000, before any additional capacity cost; this is not approval to expand. Record each attempted answer, reasoning, error type, corrected calculation, evidence and a next test. Pick at most two provisional learning targets from actual work, not a total day score. Reattempt a different variant after feedback. Separate what the new inputs show about the business from what your mistakes suggest about practice needs.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic review: demand 24,000 orders/month, usable capacity 18,000; all requested orders are otherwise serviceable. What fulfilled volume enters the cost screen?","o":["18,000","42,000","24,000","6,000"],"a":0},{"q":"At 18,000 orders/month, contribution Rs 40/order and avoidable fixed cost Rs 5.4 lakh/month, what is operating surplus?","o":["Rs 7.2 lakh","Rs 1.8 lakh","Rs 12.6 lakh","Rs 0.54 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"New verified evidence reduces contribution to Rs 25/order; volume remains 18,000 and fixed cost Rs 5.4 lakh. What is revised monthly surplus?","o":["Rs 90,000","Rs 4.5 lakh","Minus Rs 90,000","Rs 1.35 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"Under Rs 25 contribution/order and Rs 5.4 lakh fixed cost, break-even is 21,600 orders/month but capacity is 18,000. What follows?","o":["The model breaks even because demand 24,000 exceeds the threshold","The current operating-cost screen cannot break even at that capacity under those assumptions","Capacity plus demand exceeds break-even, so the screen passes","Use the old Rs 40 contribution to calculate a blended threshold"],"a":1},{"q":"A total day score is 6/10 with no item-level reasoning. What learning diagnosis is justified?","o":["Inspect actual worked responses before choosing provisional learning targets","The lowest business metric is necessarily the weakest reasoning skill","Exactly two weakest subskills can be ranked from the total","Repeat the same questions until the total rises and certify transfer"],"a":0},{"q":"Which manual error-log entry is most useful?","o":["Wrong; need more confidence; repeat tomorrow","Used demand 24,000 instead of capacity 18,000; correct min rule; next task changes the bottleneck","Score 6/10; therefore retention modeling is the weakest skill","Answer did not match; copy the reference result without the workings"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the strongest next test after feedback on a familiar calculation?","o":["Solve a new no-notes variant with changed inputs, then compare reasoning and errors","Read the worked solution while explaining why it looks reasonable","Change only answer-option order while keeping the worked solution visible","Repeat the same figures immediately and call a correct recall mastery"],"a":0},{"q":"Separate sensitivity: demand 24,000, usable capacity 22,000, contribution Rs 25/order, fixed cost Rs 5.4 lakh. No added capacity cost is included. What is modeled surplus?","o":["Minus Rs 90,000","Rs 60,000","Rs 10,000","Rs 5.5 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"The reference answer assumes Rs 40 contribution but your verified source now supports Rs 25. What should a review do?","o":["Mark every deviation wrong because a reference answer is fixed","Hide the new source until the next version of the case","Document the changed input, recompute and distinguish error from a justified updated answer","Average both contribution figures to keep the old conclusion stable"],"a":2},{"q":"Which distinction keeps the review honest?","o":["Learning-skill improvement is an operating cash inflow in the business model","Business surplus follows stated inputs; practice needs follow observed reasoning errors","A higher quiz score proves the business expansion has positive NPV","Two weak skills can be converted directly into a working-capital requirement"],"a":1}]},"15":{"objectives":["Clarify profit definition, scope, deadline and constraints before choosing a case structure.","Use a testable structure and conditional synthesis; distinguish hypothesis from a proven driver or interviewer motive."],"videos":[{"id":"sRQXkSR4GNI","title":"Every Section of a Case Interview Explained","by":"Leland","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"McKinsey: seven-step problem solving, publisher transcript","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/how-to-master-the-seven-step-problem-solving-process","note":"Read defining, disaggregating, prioritizing, work planning, analysis, synthesis and uncertainty."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"McKinsey: interviewing and integrity guidance","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/careers/interviewing","note":"Preparation differs from live assistance; interview details vary by role/recruiter. No real-time AI answers during interviews or assessments unless specifically permitted."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic practice, not a real interview or company claim: a Gurugram cloud-kitchen chain with 14 kitchens says it wants to double profit in 18 months. Before solving, ask about profit measure/baseline, included kitchens, endpoint versus cumulative profit, allowed expansion and capital/service constraints. Do not invent missing answers. For the next drill explicitly assume the owner clarifies annualized operating profit from Rs 40 crore to Rs 80 crore by month 18 across the existing 14 kitchens only, subject to a funded capex plan and service standards. Build a profit identity with net revenue minus relevant operating costs; use volume/price/cost drivers as subbranches and competitors as evidence affecting drivers, not an independent additive profit term. Prioritize one uncertain, material branch and state a falsifier. Give a 30-second conditional synthesis: objective, current hypothesis, required evidence and next test. If interrupted to examine one kitchen, confirm the narrowed scope and preserve what remains untested. Use AI for preparation only under the recruiting process rules, not unauthorized live answers.","quiz":[{"q":"A synthetic case says profits are down and asks what to do. Which first response avoids structuring the wrong problem?","o":["Benchmark two competitors before asking what decision is needed","Start with market size and defer the profit definition","Assume EBITDA and use the latest quarter as the baseline","Confirm profit measure, period, scope and constraints"],"a":3},{"q":"The owner clarifies: double annualized operating profit from Rs 40 crore by month 18, existing 14 kitchens only. Which restatement matches?","o":["Raise cumulative 18-month profit to Rs 80 crore including new kitchens","Raise annualized operating profit to Rs 80 crore by month 18 across existing kitchens","Raise operating margin to 80% across the chain by month 18","Raise annual revenue to Rs 80 crore by opening 14 more kitchens"],"a":1},{"q":"A proposed additive profit tree lists revenue, costs and competitors. What is the best repair?","o":["Delete competitor analysis because it cannot affect profit","Add market size as a fourth independent profit component","Use revenue minus costs as the identity and map competitor effects into its drivers","Keep all branches and subtract a competitor-cost percentage at the end"],"a":2},{"q":"What does a MECE check chiefly ask about a chosen decomposition?","o":["Whether the defined pieces avoid overlap and cover the stated whole","Whether the tree exactly matches a memorized case framework","Whether every branch can be analyzed with the same dataset","Whether all branches have equal financial importance"],"a":0},{"q":"The interviewer narrows discussion to one kitchen. What is a sound response without guessing why?","o":["Infer the kitchen is the root cause and recommend closure immediately","Finish the chain-level framework because changing scope reduces consistency","Treat the interruption as proof the prior structure was incorrect","Confirm the new scope, adapt the tree and state what remains untested"],"a":3},{"q":"The objective and financial scope are already fixed. Which next question best tests feasibility rather than repeating the prompt?","o":["Which profit measure should we use, despite the confirmed definition?","What capital and service-level constraints limit the available options?","Can we replace the deadline with a more convenient planning horizon?","Can we assume every kitchen has identical demand and costs?"],"a":1},{"q":"A candidate suspects order cancellations explain the decline. What makes this a useful hypothesis?","o":["Calling it the final answer before receiving operating data","Searching only kitchens with unusually high cancellations","Stating expected evidence, seeking contrary data and revising if it fails","Ignoring competing price/mix explanations until the hypothesis is accepted"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic fixed operating cost is Rs 6 lakh/month and contribution Rs 80/order, with no other costs/displacement. What is operating break-even?","o":["750 orders/month","75,000 orders/month","7,500 orders/month","7,500 orders/year"],"a":2},{"q":"After structuring but before testing any branch, what can a synthesis honestly state?","o":["A conditional hypothesis, why it matters and the next evidence test","A proven root cause because the tree is logically complete","A guaranteed profit doubling because the endpoint is specified","An implementation approval because the interviewer accepted the structure"],"a":0},{"q":"The recruiting page permits AI practice but forbids real-time interview answers and assessment AI unless specifically permitted. Which use fits?","o":["Generate live answers silently because the questions resemble practice","Use an AI note-taker during the live interview without checking restrictions","Practice a separate mock case before the interview and follow actual assessment rules","Use AI during an assessment because preparation was encouraged"],"a":2}]},"16":{"objectives":["Lead an original timed mock case with explicit comparable category definitions, unit economics and a prioritized next test.","Separate a stripped contribution calculation from actual tax/channel economics, causal inference and a full rollout decision."],"videos":[{"id":"M0-c7rXzhwk","title":"BCG Case Interview Example - CookieCo","by":"Management Consulted","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"BCG: case interview preparation","url":"https://careers.bcg.com/global/en/case-interview-preparation","note":"Read the main guidance on structure, questions, calculations and reasoning. Image-only tips and linked simulations are not required."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"McKinsey: structured problem solving","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/how-to-master-the-seven-step-problem-solving-process","note":"Use prioritization, uncertainty and conditional synthesis."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"LedgerGurus: contribution margin, publisher transcript","url":"https://ledgergurus.com/resources/video/ecommerce-contribution-margin/","note":"Contribution uses the specified net-sales and variable-cost basis, not a complete company profit estimate."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic packaged-cookies mock, not a reproduced CookieCo case or actual Parle economics. Use a 25-minute practice plan: 2 minutes clarify, 5 structure, 12 calculate/test a branch, 3 synthesize, 3 debrief. Assume the same defined category, geography, channels and pack-volume unit: category volume grows 9% over one year; brand volume is flat. If initial share is 10%, ending share is 10%/1.09 = 9.17%, not a nine-percentage-point loss. For a deliberately stripped arithmetic model only, treat Rs 10 as a reference amount with a retailer deduction of exactly 20% of that amount, no taxes, distributor margins, discounts or returns; company realization is Rs 8/pack. All variable costs are exactly Rs 5.5/pack; fixed costs are excluded. On 120 crore packs/year calculate contribution Rs 300 crore. A 5% volume increase is 6 crore packs and Rs 15 crore incremental contribution only if unit economics, capacity, displacement and fixed costs stay unchanged. Show how evidence on any missing real-world deduction/cost could change this. Prioritize one branch and state a falsifier; finish with a conditional recommendation and a next test. Debrief against worked reasoning rather than an unverified video transcript; an alternative sound tree need not match a publisher example. Timing is a practice plan, not a measured completion claim.","quiz":[{"q":"In a candidate-led mock, what should the learner practice while still following interviewer directions?","o":["Preserve the first structure even when the prompt changes","Avoid clarifying questions to show independent leadership","Analyze every branch equally before offering a view","Choose and justify the next useful branch rather than only waiting for prompts"],"a":3},{"q":"Same category/geography/channels and volume unit: category grows 9% in one year while brand volume is flat. What follows?","o":["Brand profit must have declined 9%","Brand share falls by exactly nine percentage points","Brand volume share is unchanged because brand volume is unchanged","Brand volume share falls; the cause and profit effect remain unproven"],"a":3},{"q":"Stripped synthetic model only: reference Rs 10/pack less retailer deduction 20% of Rs 10; no taxes, other deductions or returns. What company realization is implied?","o":["Rs 8","Rs 2","Rs 10","Rs 8.33"],"a":0},{"q":"All variable costs are exactly Rs 5.5/pack, realization Rs 8/pack and annual volume 120 crore packs. What is contribution before fixed costs?","o":["Rs 30 crore","Rs 300 crore","Rs 660 crore","Rs 960 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"On that basis, volume rises 5% from 120 crore packs; all unit economics, capacity, displacement and fixed costs stay unchanged. What extra contribution results?","o":["Rs 1.5 crore","Rs 15 crore","Rs 150 crore","Rs 60 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Time is short and four branches remain. What prioritization is most useful?","o":["Cover every branch equally to preserve MECE balance","Treat the first branch as proven because it received the most time","Choose the branch with the cleanest chart regardless of decision impact","Choose a material, uncertain and testable branch; explain the choice and park the rest"],"a":3},{"q":"Initial brand volume share is 10%; brand volume stays flat and category volume rises 9% on the same basis. What is ending share?","o":["1%","10.9%","About 9.1 percentage points lower","About 9.17%"],"a":3},{"q":"A real channel dataset adds variable freight Rs 0.50/pack not present in the stripped model. All other inputs stay fixed. What happens to contribution?","o":["It falls to Rs 240 crore before fixed costs","It becomes Rs 360 crore because freight is added to sales","It falls Rs 6 crore because the unit cost is small","It stays Rs 300 crore because COGS was unchanged"],"a":0},{"q":"The model shows Rs 15 crore extra contribution if volume recovers 5%. What is the best next statement?","o":["Test whether demand, capacity and unchanged economics support that case before rollout","Approve rollout because contribution is equivalent to free cash flow","Report 5% volume recovery as observed because it was modeled","Infer the cause of flat sales was insufficient advertising"],"a":0},{"q":"Which debrief best tests the quality of a mock-case solution?","o":["Check only whether the final recommendation matched the example","Mark every different issue-tree branch wrong even if the reasoning is sound","Award mastery after a familiar framework is repeated without errors","Check definitions, structure, arithmetic, evidence, uncertainty and the next decision test"],"a":3}]},"17":{"objectives":["Reconcile a synthetic EBITDA margin bridge with an exact cost schedule and an explicitly rounded headline.","Separate identities and hypotheses from causal findings; use relevant cost behavior over a stated horizon."],"videos":[{"id":"fN1IEfPd60c","title":"The Profitability Framework","by":"Management Consulted","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Bain: FashionCo case","url":"https://www.bain.com/careers/hiring-process/interviewing/fashion-case-study/","note":"Use diagnosis, tradeoffs and next tests; figures below are original, not reproduced case data."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"LedgerGurus: contribution margin transcript","url":"https://ledgergurus.com/resources/video/ecommerce-contribution-margin/","note":"Use defined cost behavior; fixed costs can change in steps."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"McKinsey: structured problem solving","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/how-to-master-the-seven-step-problem-solving-process","note":"Keep uncertainty and source reconciliation visible."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic grocery-chain case, not DMart or kirana company evidence. Same 40 stores and scope across two annual periods: revenue index 100 -> 112. Baseline cost indices: COGS 68, rent 6, labour 9, logistics 3, energy 2, other 5, leaving EBITDA 7. Apply absolute cost growth respectively 16%, 18%, 15%, 22%, 5%, 12%. New costs sum to 107.67; EBITDA is 4.33 index units, or 3.8661% of revenue. The stated headline 4% is rounded, not an exact accounting identity. Show cost-share movements: COGS +2.4286 percentage points, rent +0.3214, labour +0.2411, logistics +0.2679, energy -0.125, other 0. Total margin decline 3.1339 points; COGS and rent are the two largest adverse movements. With footfalls/store +4%, basket flat and all revenue captured by footfalls x conversion x basket, implied conversion growth is 1.12/1.04-1=7.6923%. Distinguish cost behavior over a defined horizon: purchase cost varies with units; rent is fixed only within the lease/capacity assumptions, staffing and logistics can be mixed or step-fixed. Propose a logistics hypothesis and evidence that could reject it; a supportive data cut is not automatically causal proof. A 2-point COGS-share reduction alone lifts modeled margin from 3.8661% to 5.8661%, before implementation costs or volume effects. Submit a bridge, rounding reconciliation and conditional recommendation, not a guaranteed saving.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic same-scope retailer: baseline revenue 100 and EBITDA 7; later revenue 112 and costs 107.67. What is later EBITDA margin, rounded to three decimal places?","o":["7.000%","4.330%","3.866%","4.000%"],"a":2},{"q":"A bridge derived from exact inputs gives a 3.1339-point decline, while a rounded headline says 7% to 4%. What should the analyst do?","o":["Add an unexplained cost adjustment so the decline is exactly three points","Show the exact bridge and label the rounded-headline difference","Delete the smallest adverse movement to make totals agree","Average exact and rounded margins and replace the source inputs"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic COGS starts at 68 on revenue 100, rises 16%, while revenue rises 12%. What is its new share, rounded to two decimal places?","o":["70.43%","76.00%","60.71%","68.00%"],"a":0},{"q":"Cost-share increases are COGS 2.4286 points, rent 0.3214, labour 0.2411, logistics 0.2679. Which pair contributes the largest adverse movement?","o":["COGS and logistics","COGS and rent","Rent and logistics","Labour and logistics"],"a":1},{"q":"Revenue/store rises 12%, footfalls/store rise 4% and basket is flat. Under revenue/store = footfalls x conversion x basket, how much does conversion rise?","o":["About 7.69%","12%","8% exactly","About 16.48%"],"a":0},{"q":"Separate variant: revenue/store is flat, footfalls rise 4%, and no separate basket value is supplied. What follows?","o":["Conversion alone fell exactly 4%","Basket must be flat because revenue is flat","Conversion x basket fell by about 3.85%; split them with more evidence","Store capacity increased by about 4%"],"a":2},{"q":"Which cost classification is defensible over a defined short-run store plan?","o":["Every labour and logistics cost is proportional to units","Rent is variable because its cost per sale changes","Purchase cost generally varies with units; rent may be fixed and labour/logistics mixed or step-fixed","All COGS is fixed once last year accounts are closed"],"a":2},{"q":"Logistics cost rises 22%. Which next test is most useful without claiming causality prematurely?","o":["Conclude it is efficient if sales also rose","Assign the full increase to fuel because logistics uses transport","Use the 22% increase as proof the logistics manager underperformed","Decompose delivery count, cost/delivery, distance and service mix, then test a specific hypothesis"],"a":3},{"q":"New margin is 3.8661%. Only COGS share falls two percentage points, with revenue and all other costs unchanged. What is modeled margin?","o":["5.8661%","4.0661%","1.8661%","Exactly 6%"],"a":0},{"q":"Separate synthetic comparison: revenue is Rs 800 crore in both years; EBITDA margin falls 7% to 4%. What is the EBITDA decrease?","o":["Rs 24 crore","Rs 3 crore","Rs 56 crore","Rs 32 crore"],"a":0}]},"18":{"objectives":["Separate procurement waste, foregone full-unit contribution and incremental recovery benefit under explicit cost-stage assumptions.","Distinguish fixed-cost absorption from cash savings; apply capacity and flow identities with consistent units and boundaries."],"videos":[{"id":"cGpNcNLWXrc","title":"Operations Case Interview with a Real Partner | Partners in Performanc","by":"Fless","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT: capacity and process flow","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-760b-introduction-to-operations-management-spring-2004/16dde953a29f89a4d2018361900ce886_lec3_capacity.pdf","note":"Read process capacity, equilibrium Little law and variability. No clinical/food-safety operating limits inferred."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA: relevant costs","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f5/technical-articles/relevant-costs.html","note":"Use future avoidable cash differences; historical paid input can be sunk in a recovery choice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"LedgerGurus: contribution margin transcript","url":"https://ledgergurus.com/resources/video/ecommerce-contribution-margin/","note":"Define variable costs and distinguish contribution from fixed-cost allocation."}],"exercise":"Original synthetic dairy arithmetic, not food-safety or plant operating advice. Separate scenarios, all per day unless stated. A full saleable litre earns Rs 58 and requires variable procurement 42 plus conversion 9, giving full-unit contribution 7. A batch contains 200,000 already-paid litres; 2% (4,000) becomes unsaleable with zero salvage. Procurement value in lost input is 168,000 rupees; 4,000 x full-unit contribution = 28,000 is a distinct foregone margin screen, not the incremental value of recovering already-paid input. In a separate feasible recovery variant, all 4,000 litres can safely be recovered and sold, no other sales are displaced, demand exists, the only future variable cost is 9/litre and no extra investment/fixed cost is needed: incremental recovery benefit is 4,000 x (58-9)=196,000, not 28,000 plus 168,000 as separate independent benefits. Real feasibility/quality rules and costs remain unverified. Separate utilization screen: input processed is 200,000 litres/day at 70% of a fixed practical capacity. Capacity is 285,714.29; at 85% output on the same processed-litre basis is 242,857.14. Unchanged fixed cost Rs 12 lakh/day allocates from Rs 6 to Rs 4.9412/litre, down 17.6471%, but total fixed cash does not fall. Additional volume creates value only with saleable yield, demand and incremental contribution/cost evidence. Original equilibrium flow variant: throughput 1,000 pouches/hour and mean time 0.5 hour implies mean WIP 500 pouches for the same boundary. Submit separate calculations, a conditional lever, risk and evidence gate; do not change product-safety requirements.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic full-unit economics: saleable realization 58/litre, variable procurement 42 and variable conversion 9. What is contribution/litre before fixed costs?","o":["Rs 16","Rs 7","Rs 9","Rs 49"],"a":1},{"q":"Two percent of 200,000 already-procured litres is lost, zero salvage, procurement 42/litre. What procurement value is in the lost input?","o":["Rs 28,000","Rs 168,000","Rs 196,000","Rs 232,000"],"a":1},{"q":"Separate recovery variant: 4,000 already-paid litres can be safely recovered/sold at 58; only future cost is conversion 9/litre, with no displacement/other cost. What incremental benefit results?","o":["Rs 28,000","Rs 364,000","Rs 168,000","Rs 196,000"],"a":3},{"q":"Separate fixed-cost allocation screen: utilization rises 70% to 85% on the same practical-capacity basis, fixed cost unchanged. What is the percentage fall in fixed cost per processed litre, rounded to two decimals?","o":["21.43%","15.00%","2.14%","17.65%"],"a":3},{"q":"Total fixed cost remains Rs 12 lakh/day while more litres are processed. Which statement is correct?","o":["The lower cost/litre saves exactly Rs 12 lakh cash/day","Allocated fixed-cost savings can be added to contribution from every extra litre","Fixed cash is unchanged; assess extra saleable volume and incremental costs separately","Total fixed cash automatically falls by 17.65%"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic stable system: throughput 1,000 pouches/hour and mean flow time 0.5 hour, same boundary. Little law gives mean WIP of:","o":["0.0005 hour","2,000 pouches","500 pouches/hour","500 pouches"],"a":3},{"q":"Which serial-process statement is accurate under comparable units and no other constraints?","o":["The lowest usable stage capacity caps process capacity; actual throughput may be lower if demand limits it","The costliest machine is necessarily the bottleneck","The fastest stage sets process throughput if work is scheduled well","Adding usable stage capacities gives the process throughput"],"a":0},{"q":"A monthly benefit model assumes a Rs 0.50 saving per saleable litre. What is the right scaling approach?","o":["Use relevant affected saleable volume over the same period and subtract implementation/incremental costs","Multiply by revenue because both quantities concern value","Multiply by plant nameplate litres, regardless of actual sales","Multiply by the number of shifts even if volume already covers all shifts"],"a":0},{"q":"A proposed alternate-day milk delivery saves logistics cost. What belongs in the decision gate?","o":["Accept if truck utilization rises, without checking product effects","Infer the longer route is safe from the unit-cost model","Treat spoilage as fixed because it happened historically","Verify applicable safety/temperature/service constraints plus stockouts, spoilage and full economics"],"a":3},{"q":"Which use of the lost-input and recovery calculations is correct?","o":["Use 28,000 as recovery benefit even when procurement is sunk","Add all three values to report total economic loss","Choose the largest figure as proof of a guaranteed cash saving","Label the 168,000 input value, 28,000 full-unit margin and 196,000 recovery difference as distinct views, not additive losses"],"a":3}]},"19":{"objectives":["Separate synthetic total category, addressable segment and attainable channel revenue; reconcile definitions rather than force numerical agreement.","Calculate a bounded entry burn screen and cash-plan omissions without presenting assumed charges as operative import law."],"videos":[{"id":"zmTYuqIQshQ","title":"Market Entry Cases EXPLAINED: The Only Framework You Need (Part 1) | C","by":"PREPnip Society","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"SBA: market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/plan-your-business/","note":"Read demand, size, reach, pricing and competition; U.S. data links are not UAE market evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA: working capital","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f9/technical-articles/wcm.html","note":"Consider inventory/receivable/payable timing and liquidity risks; not local import law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran: cash flow definitions","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/dcfcf.pdf","note":"Separate contribution, fixed burn, reinvestment and cash timing."}],"exercise":"Original fictional tea-entry classroom model, not current UAE/Gulf market facts, tariff advice, legal setup guidance or an actual recommendation. No real market size or diaspora counts are asserted. Lens A: annual total tea-category retail spend 1,000 crore; relevant premium segment 20% gives 200 crore addressable segment spend; assumed brand capture 2% of that gives 4 crore target annual retail sales, not total market size. Lens B: 100 reachable stores x 2 SKUs/store x 5 packs/SKU/store/week x 52 weeks x 250 rupees retail/pack = 1.3 crore annual retail sales. Lens C: 200,000 target households x 10% purchasing annually x 1,000 rupees annual brand retail spend/purchasing household = 2 crore. These distinct hypotheses may overlap and share biased assumptions; do not sum them or force agreement within 2 x. Reconcile time, category, customer/household unit, SKU rate and retail versus brand-net basis, then test the cause of disagreement. Separate unit-economics toy: reference price 2,000/kg, contractual distributor deduction exactly 30% of reference price, logistics 180/kg, COGS 700/kg, and a fictional charge exactly 5% of reference price (100/kg). This 5% is NOT a verified customs duty or tax. With no other deductions/variable costs, contribution is 420/kg. At constant 800 kg/month for all 18 months (40% of 2,000 plan), fixed operating cash burn 25 lakh/month and contemporaneous collections/payments, operating deficit is 21.64 lakh/month or 389.52 lakh over 18 months. This excludes setup, inventory/receivable financing, deposits, tax/FX changes, termination and contingency; not complete launch funding. Name evidence needed for a distributor choice rather than claim universal superiority. Propose an unexecuted research pilot with a hypothetical all-in budget cap below 10 lakh and pre-agreed measurable stop/continue rule, plus safety/legal/cash gates. An attractive result is not permission to spend.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic annual category spend is 1,000 crore; relevant premium segment 20%; assumed brand capture 2% of premium. What is 4 crore?","o":["Total tea-category market size","Target brand retail sales under assumed segment capture","The entire premium segment market","Verified company net revenue"],"a":1},{"q":"Bottom-up synthetic target: 100 stores x 2 SKUs/store x 5 packs/SKU/store/week x 52 weeks x Rs 250/pack. What annual retail sales result?","o":["Rs 13 crore","Rs 0.13 crore","Rs 1.3 crore","Rs 2.6 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Three sizing lenses give 4, 1.3 and 2 crore retail sales. What is sound?","o":["Average them and call the result verified TAM","Reconcile definitions, overlap and shared assumptions, then investigate material disagreement","Add all three because they use different formulas","Increase the smallest estimate until all lie within 2 x"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic anchor: 200,000 target households x 10% annual purchasing x Rs 1,000 brand spend/purchasing household/year. What is target annual retail spend?","o":["Rs 0.2 crore","Rs 20 crore","Rs 2 crore","Rs 200 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Which distributor-choice statement is most defensible?","o":["Compare actual contract margin, control, reach, service, capital and local compliance requirements","A distributor always needs no working capital and guarantees faster entry","Own entity always dominates once market size exceeds the pilot budget","A higher store count removes the need to test payment and inventory terms"],"a":0},{"q":"Toy price 2,000/kg less distributor deduction 600, logistics 180, fictional charge 100 and COGS 700. What contribution results?","o":["Rs 420/kg","Rs 1,020/kg","Rs 520/kg","Rs 300/kg"],"a":0},{"q":"At constant 800 kg/month, contribution Rs 420/kg and fixed operating cash burn Rs 25 lakh/month, with simultaneous cash timing, what is the 18-month operating deficit before setup, working-capital and other omitted cash items?","o":["Rs 4,500 lakh","Rs 389.52 lakh","Rs 2,164 lakh","Rs 3.36 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"An entry budget uses only that operating deficit. What is missing before treating it as complete funding?","o":["Only the total market size divided by target share","Nothing if the model covers at least 18 months","Setup, deposits, inventory/receivable/payable timing, FX/tax and exit/contingency costs as applicable","The prior research spend added once for every future month"],"a":2},{"q":"Which proposed pilot gate is measurable without committing funds?","o":["Continue if the distributor says interest feels strong","Treat store listings alone as proof repeat demand exists","Run the pilot immediately because the exercise says below 10 lakh","Before running an authorized pilot, define sales/returns/collections thresholds, duration, budget cap and stop rule"],"a":3},{"q":"After six months,100 stores are listed but sell-through, returns and collections are unverified. What conclusion is supported?","o":["Entry is proven because reach is the main objective in every market","Cash runway improved because listings precede all sales","Distribution reach is observed; customer demand, unit economics and cash results still need evidence","Repeat demand is established if the distributor renewed the agreement"],"a":2}]},"20":{"objectives":["Drive a full market-entry case end to end under time pressure","Defend an entry recommendation with sized economics and explicit risks"],"videos":[{"id":"LTkKeR2hQ9A","title":"Consulting Case Interview: A Market Entry Case Study with BCG Consulta","by":"Prepmatter","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Consultant Case Practice Video - Management Consulting","url":"https://www.bain.com/careers/hiring-process/interviewing/consultant-practice-case-interview/","note":"Bain's practice case - watch how a real candidate paces an entry case."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Tips for Case Interviewing: What BCG Looks For","url":"https://careers.bcg.com/global/en/blogarticle/tips-for-case-interviewing-what-bcg-looks-for","note":"The scoring rubric to self-grade against."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Free with a Google account - build the entry model here, one assumption per row."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical: a value-fashion chain is considering entry into Bangladesh. All numbers below are case assumptions, not current market or company facts. Complete the case in 30 minutes with pen and sheet. (a) 4 min: clarify the objective and constraints. (b) 8 min: structure demand, competition, sourcing/import eligibility, locations/partners, economics and risks. (c) 12 min: assume population 17 crore, target segment 8%, annual spend Rs 3,000 per target customer, and a year-5 share goal of 3% of segment spend. Calculate segment spend and target annual revenue separately. Investment is Rs 150 crore; opening capex plus working capital is Rs 1.2 crore per store. Calculate stores funded. For a simplified one-year investment-recovery benchmark, assume all funded stores operate for a full year at the target annual revenue, with contribution of 18% after variable costs. Ignore ramp-up, fixed overheads, tax, financing and discounting; no working-capital release. Calculate annual contribution at target revenue, revenue required to recover the investment in one year, required revenue per funded store, and implied share of segment spend. Also calculate simple payback if target revenue and contribution remain constant each year. Distinguish failure of the one-year benchmark from a general entry verdict; propose a change to horizon, share, margin or capital intensity and name evidence needed to justify it. (d) 6 min: give an answer-first recommendation with a staged test, two risks and one mitigation each. Self-grade using the linked case-interview guidance.","quiz":[{"q":"In the classroom case, 17 crore people x 8% target segment x Rs 3,000 annual spend x 3% share gives target annual revenue closest to:","o":["Rs 122 crore","Rs 410 crore","Rs 1,224 crore","Rs 12 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"At Rs 1.2 crore capex plus working capital per store, the Rs 150 crore investment funds roughly:","o":["1,250 stores","125 stores","417 stores","21 stores"],"a":1},{"q":"Assume annual revenue stays at Rs 122.4 Cr with 18% contribution, no ramp-up, fixed overheads, tax, financing, discounting or working-capital release. Simple recovery time for Rs 150 Cr is closest to:","o":["1 year","6.8 years","68 years","0.7 years"],"a":1},{"q":"When assessing a cross-border retailer's import-duty assumptions, checking product classification, the applicable agreement and rules of origin helps establish:","o":["That any shipment from a treaty-country factory receives the lowest tariff, irrespective of product rules or origin tests.","That a supplier quotation settles duty eligibility, so agreement conditions and product classification do not require independent checking.","Whether the planned goods and sourcing qualify for preferential duty; do not assume eligibility from the factory location alone","That the retailer can use its home-country tariff classification and rates without checking the importing jurisdiction or agreement."],"a":2},{"q":"A local-partner (franchise/JV) entry primarily buys:","o":["Demand certainty from the partners existing relationships, so shoppers willingness to pay and the proposed store economics need not be tested.","A substitute for product-market evidence: the partners presence establishes that the same offer and economics will transfer successfully.","A lower investment requirement without sharing economics or losing operational control, because local knowledge removes every ownership trade-off.","Local capability - real estate access, regulatory navigation, labour - at the cost of margin share and brand control"],"a":3},{"q":"The interviewer challenges the assumed value-fashion demand in a new country. The strongest next step is:","o":["Use the home-market sales rate as confirmed evidence because the brand sells the same product category in both markets.","Treat a large target population as sufficient demand validation, then use management confidence to settle willingness to pay.","Test whether target shoppers already buy the category at comparable prices, then validate willingness to pay and unit economics before scaling","Increase the store target first so scale economies can establish demand before testing local price points or shopping behavior."],"a":2},{"q":"A hypothetical Rs 150 Cr funds 125 stores at Rs 1.2 Cr each. At 18% contribution, one-year recovery needs Rs 833.3 Cr annual revenue (Rs 6.67 Cr/store), or 20.4% of Rs 4,080 Cr segment spend. The year-5 target is 3% share, Rs 122.4 Cr revenue. Ignoring ramp-up, fixed overheads, tax and financing, the sound conclusion is:","o":["At the target share the project meets a one-year recovery benchmark because total segment spending exceeds the investment and the retailer only needs a small part of that market.","At the 3% target, annual contribution cannot recover Rs 150 Cr in one year; consider a longer horizon or evidence-backed changes to share, margin or capex, rather than an automatic do-not-enter","The number of funded stores establishes the target sales rate independently of the market-share assumption; contribution margin can therefore be applied to the revenue needed for recovery as if it were forecast revenue.","The computed mismatch proves that market entry cannot work under any horizon or business model, so collecting evidence on costs, demand or a staged launch cannot change the decision."],"a":1},{"q":"A recommendation says \"enter via 50 company-owned stores in year 1.\" The sharpest risk to probe:","o":["A single simultaneous launch guarantees rapid learning across locations, so sales uncertainty can be ignored until stores have operated for a full year.","The stated fifty-store count is sufficient risk control on its own, without relating investment to sales evidence or feasible exit options.","Full ownership establishes control over demand as well as operations, so a smaller staged test cannot materially improve the entry decision.","Capital concentration before rate-of-sale is proven - stage the entry (pilot cluster, then scale on evidence)"],"a":3},{"q":"When your computed market size feels absurdly large, the first check is:","o":["Keep the headline total if the arithmetic is correct, without checking whether annual spending, customer counts and market share use compatible bases.","Increase the target segment until its revenue can support the planned store count, treating the investment objective as evidence of market size.","Unit consistency - crores vs lakhs vs millions, and whether the segment share was applied to the right base","Replace the result with an industry average before checking the multiplication, because a plausible-looking answer is more useful than a reconciled one."],"a":2},{"q":"The best closing synthesis for an entry case contains:","o":["A market-size estimate and ambitious growth target, leaving the entry mode, staging and evidence thresholds for implementation to decide later.","Enter/do not enter, the mode, the staged path, and the two numbers that would change the answer","A detailed store-opening schedule and list of partners, without a recommendation or the economic assumptions that could reverse it.","A preferred entry mode justified by competitor behavior alone, without linking demand, investment and contribution to a decision threshold."],"a":1}]},"21":{"objectives":["Re-run the dairy profitability case with changed evidence, using one input/output P&L and separating fixed-cost allocation from incremental cash.","Test profitability and entry concepts from days 15-20 with fresh numbers and state what evidence could reverse the recommendation."],"videos":[{"id":"vTvJFAVwG4w","title":"Profitability Case Walkthrough- BCG Style","by":"Management Consulted","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Video","title":"Inside a McKinsey Profitability Case Interview (Greek Cheese Manufacturer)","url":"https://managementconsulted.com/mckinsey-profitability-case-greek-cheese-manufacturer/","note":"Embedded coached case interview; compare the reasoning after working the case yourself. This page is not a written worked solution."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Bain case-interview guide","url":"https://www.bain.com/careers/hiring-process/case-interview/","note":"Candidate-facing case format and evaluation criteria."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets (free with Google account)","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Free tool - keep it open while you work the exercise."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical: the dairy case now has procurement inflation and worse yield. Define all cost and volume bases before choosing a lever. At baseline the plant takes 200,000 INPUT litres/day, equal to 70% of installed input capacity. Yield loss is 4.5% of input, with no salvage; the remainder is saleable milk. All saleable output sells at Rs 59 per SALEABLE litre. Procurement Rs 45 and variable conversion Rs 9 per INPUT litre are incurred before the loss. Fixed conversion cost is a separate Rs 12 lakh/day and stays unchanged in every scenario. Ignore tax, financing and other costs. (a) Calculate input capacity, saleable volume, revenue, procurement, variable conversion and operating surplus in one daily P&L. Show procurement embodied in lost milk as a memo item only: it is already in total procurement and must not be subtracted twice. (b) Compare two SEPARATE alternatives against baseline: procurement falls Rs 1.50 per input litre at unchanged volume/yield; or utilisation rises to 85% at unchanged unit economics, yield and fixed cost. For the latter, explicitly assume all extra saleable output sells at Rs 59 without displacing other sales. Calculate each daily cash improvement. (c) Compute fixed cost per INPUT litre at 70% and 85%, then explain why a lower allocation is not itself a cash-cost reduction. (d) Choose the first lever, state whether the operation is profitable after that lever, and name the supplier or demand evidence that could change the choice. Checks: 191,000 saleable litres; baseline operating surplus -Rs 7.31 lakh/day; procurement alternative adds Rs 3 lakh/day; utilisation alternative adds Rs 1.005 lakh/day; fixed allocation falls from Rs 6 to Rs 4.9412 per input litre, while total fixed cash stays Rs 12 lakh/day. These are classroom assumptions, not facts about a real processor.","quiz":[{"q":"A clarified case objective must contain a number, a deadline and:","o":["A unit and scope - what counts and what does not","A project budget rather than defining the target measure and its exclusions.","An optimistic growth target without specifying what the counted measure includes.","A preferred solution before agreeing which operating boundary the target measures."],"a":0},{"q":"First-level split of profitability:","o":["Fixed assets/working capital","Customer segments/marketing channels","Revenue / costs","Product features/organization design"],"a":2},{"q":"Revenue grows 12%, profit falls. The diagnosis must involve:","o":["Revenue growth alone proves operating improvement, so the lower profit can be ignored until the next reporting period.","Reconcile the revenue increase against a larger increase in total costs, then test price, volume, mix and expense drivers rather than infer one cause","A price cut is the only possible explanation whenever revenue rises but profit falls, regardless of cost or volume evidence.","A lower gross margin explains every profit decline, without reconciling other expenses or checking the mix and volume changes."],"a":1},{"q":"A rupee profit bridge attributes the change in profit across:","o":["Revenue growth percentages alone, without reconciling the rupee change in expenses.","The number of customers and employees only, excluding prices and unit costs.","The target profit and planned budget, without separating actual operating drivers.","Price, volume, mix and each cost line"],"a":3},{"q":"Contribution per unit = price minus:","o":["Allocated fixed cost per unit","Variable cost per unit","Initial investment per acquired customer","Total revenue per active customer"],"a":1},{"q":"EBITDA margin falls from 7% to 4%, with revenue held at Rs 800 crore in BOTH periods. The EBITDA decline from this margin change is:","o":["Rs 24 cr","Rs 3 cr","Rs 56 cr","Rs 32 cr"],"a":0},{"q":"The bottleneck is the step that:","o":["Processes the most units despite not restricting total system output.","Caps total throughput with the lowest effective capacity","Reports the highest utilization even if its capacity exceeds demand.","Has the largest unused capacity within the process boundary."],"a":1},{"q":"Utilisation 70% to 85% with unchanged fixed cost cuts fixed cost per unit by about:","o":["2%","25%","8%","18%"],"a":3},{"q":"Little's Law: WIP equals throughput times:","o":["Customer acquisition time outside the defined production process.","Total calendar time since the business first launched.","Average flow time within the same stable process boundary","The smallest recorded task time on the fastest unit."],"a":2},{"q":"Which mix shift can reduce profit despite higher total volume, depending on the contribution amounts?","o":["Volume moves toward higher-margin products.","Volume moved toward lower-margin products","Any new product reduces total profit.","Higher volume lowers total fixed cash cost."],"a":1},{"q":"In candidate-led cases, after structuring you must:","o":["Analyze every branch equally before stating which one affects the objective most.","Choose the easiest available calculation regardless of its relevance to the objective.","Pick the highest-impact branch yourself and say why","Wait for the interviewer to select the branch before explaining your own priority."],"a":2},{"q":"Flat volume in a growing category means the client is:","o":["Gaining category share because its absolute volume has not fallen.","Holding category share because its own sales volume is unchanged.","Improving category economics because market growth lowers its costs.","Losing share - a competitive-position problem"],"a":3},{"q":"Assume MRP Rs 10, the retailer keeps 20% of MRP, and there are no taxes or other channel deductions. Company net realization is:","o":["Rs 8","Rs 4.5","Rs 2.5","Rs 10"],"a":0},{"q":"After every piece of case math you should state:","o":["A restatement of every input, without drawing an implication or next test.","The so-what - what it means for the recommendation and what to test next","The arithmetic is complete, so assumptions no longer affect the case conclusion.","The most precise decimal answer, without explaining its decision relevance."],"a":1},{"q":"The first question of a market-entry case is:","o":["Which geographic market has the highest population regardless of client fit?","Which entry mode is fashionable among the strongest competing firms?","How many stores can be announced before testing local customer demand?","Does entry fit this client's objective, economics and capabilities?"],"a":3},{"q":"With consistent boundaries and units, a bottom-up estimate of product units sold per period can multiply:","o":["Rate of sale x acquisition cost x financing rate, without store or SKU coverage","Stores x SKUs per store x units sold per SKU per period; monetary revenue also requires prices","Store count x fixed expenses per store x annual rent, without unit sales data","SKU count x factory headcount x invested capital per employee, without customer purchases"],"a":1},{"q":"Distributor-led entry may trade some margin and control for which potential benefit?","o":["Complete control over every customer interaction without owning distribution or sharing any economics","Guaranteed customer demand and no need for local sell-through evidence or partner oversight","Faster channel access and lower owned-distribution investment, subject to partner terms and actual execution","Permanent exclusivity and full retention of channel margin, independent of distributor contract terms"],"a":2},{"q":"A kill criterion must be defined:","o":["Before the experiment runs, as a measurable threshold","When the final presentation is written, based on which outcome is easiest to explain.","Only after scaling begins, because early stopping rules necessarily prevent useful learning.","After results arrive, selecting the threshold that supports the preferred conclusion."],"a":0},{"q":"Distribution without rate of sale is:","o":["Proof of contribution growth because broader availability necessarily offsets trade discounts, returns and the cost of carrying unsold inventory.","A sufficient sign of demand because retailer listings establish end-customer willingness to buy, independent of sell-through or replenishment.","Not enough evidence of demand: measure rate of sale, replenishment and net contribution before scaling listings","Evidence of repeat demand as soon as inventory reaches stores, even if no customer transaction or reorder has been observed."],"a":2},{"q":"A classroom dairy takes 200,000 input litres/day; 4.5% is lost with no salvage. Saleable output is:","o":["9,000 litres/day","195,500 litres/day","200,000 litres/day","191,000 litres/day"],"a":3},{"q":"Saleable yield is 95.5%; realization is Rs 59 per SALEABLE litre. Procurement Rs 45 and variable conversion Rs 9 are per INPUT litre, incurred before loss. Contribution before fixed cost per input litre is:","o":["Rs 2.345","Rs 5.00","Rs 59.00","Rs 14.00"],"a":0},{"q":"The P&L already charges Rs 90 lakh for all 200,000 input litres at Rs 45. Lost input contains Rs 4.05 lakh of procurement cost, with no salvage. How should that waste memo enter the same P&L?","o":["Remove it from procurement to restore the margin, without changing the amount paid or recovering any value from the lost input.","Count it as additional revenue because the loss memo identifies litres that would have sold in an alternative no-loss scenario.","It is already included in procurement; show it as a diagnostic memo, not a second expense","Add it again below procurement because any identified waste must become a separate P&L charge even if the purchased input was expensed."],"a":2},{"q":"Baseline input throughput is 200,000 litres/day at 70% utilisation. At 85% it is 242,857.14. Each extra input litre adds Rs 2.345 contribution; all extra saleable output sells and fixed cost is unchanged. Incremental daily operating surplus is about:","o":["Zero because fixed allocation fell","Rs 1.005 lakh","Rs 4.05 lakh","Rs 12 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"With input volume fixed at 200,000 litres/day, procurement falls Rs 1.50 per INPUT litre. No other cost, yield or price changes. Daily operating surplus improves by:","o":["Rs 3 lakh","Rs 30 lakh","Rs 0.30 lakh","Rs 1.005 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Fixed cash conversion cost remains Rs 12 lakh/day while input rises from 200,000 to 242,857.14 litres. What is the sound interpretation of fixed allocation falling from Rs 6 to Rs 4.94 per input litre?","o":["The fixed-cost total falls by the same percentage as its per-litre allocation, so extra sales need not be checked when calculating the profit change.","Unit allocation falls, but fixed cash does not; extra profit depends on saleable output sold and its incremental costs","The lower allocation is itself an additional cash saving to add to the contribution on extra sales, despite the unchanged Rs12lakh total.","The allocation decrease proves all additional output is profitable even if it cannot be sold or its variable and selling costs exceed realized revenue."],"a":1}]},"22":{"objectives":["Master the three pricing lenses - cost-based, competition-based, value-based - and when each governs","Structure go-to-market choices: channel, motion and price-pack architecture"],"videos":[{"id":"TMsx5SSyFyg","title":"Pricing Case Interview Framework | MBB Case Prep | Consulting Prep Ser","by":"Dolly Bajaj","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Pricing: The next frontier of value creation in private equity","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/growth-marketing-and-sales/our-insights/pricing-the-next-frontier-of-value-creation-in-private-equity","note":"McKinsey on pricing as the highest-leverage profit driver."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Unified Theory of Strategic Pricing","url":"https://www.bcg.com/publications/2023/the-unified-theory-of-pricing","note":"BCG's framework linking price to value creation and capture."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Free with a Google account - build the entry model here, one assumption per row."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical: price a D2C electrolyte drink entering modern trade. All prices below are assumed examples, not current verified retail prices. Ignore tax, discounting, returns and all costs beyond those specified. (a) COGS is Rs 22/can; distributor and retailer each deduct 18% and 25% of MRP respectively, both on the SAME MRP base, not sequential markups. Find the minimum MRP for company contribution of at least Rs 10/can. State the exact threshold, then round UP to the next paise and to a whole rupee. (b) Compare assumed buyer alternatives: ORS sachet Rs 22/use, energy drink Rs 125, coconut water Rs 40-60. Do not assume functional equivalence; state which alternative a particular buyer would actually substitute and what research would test that. (c) Suppose a target user currently spends Rs 60 on a post-gym drink. Explain why that is not proof of willingness to pay Rs 55 or Rs 75 for this product; propose an actual-purchase test. (d) Design a single can, four-pack and subscription for distinct occasions; compute contribution at any bundle discount before recommending it. (e) Give a conditional price recommendation and the demand/cost evidence that would change it. Check: contribution = 0.57 x MRP - 22; exact threshold Rs 56.1403509, minimum paise price Rs 56.15 or whole-rupee MRP Rs 57.","quiz":[{"q":"The three classic pricing lenses are:","o":["Value-based, schedule-based, headcount-based","Cost-based, volume-based, organization-based","Cost-based, competition-based, value-based","Competition-based, inventory-based, ownership-based"],"a":2},{"q":"Cost-plus pricing's core weakness is that it:","o":["Ignores what the customer would actually pay - it can leave money on the table or price you out entirely","Prevents checking contribution margins, so the business must ignore its own costs whenever it investigates willingness to pay and competitive prices.","Requires a premium price by definition, even when customers compare the product with a lower-priced alternative and the cost base is modest.","Uses too little information about production costs, so the remedy is a more detailed allocation of overhead without studying alternatives or demand."],"a":0},{"q":"Classroom case: COGS Rs 22/can; retailer 25% and distributor 18%, both deducted from the SAME MRP. No tax, discounts, returns or other costs. For contribution of at least Rs 10/can, the minimum MRP rounded UP to a paise is:","o":["Rs 45.00","Rs 55.00","Rs 52.64","Rs 56.15"],"a":3},{"q":"Value-based pricing sets price against:","o":["The category leaders listed price, adjusted only for pack size rather than the customers actual comparison or value received.","A target revenue number set by management, with demand assumed to rise until it supports the resulting recommended price.","The accounting cost of the product plus a standard margin, without investigating which alternative the customer would otherwise choose.","The customer's next-best alternative and the measurable value your product adds over it"],"a":3},{"q":"In a hypothetical buyer test, a shopper compares your Rs 55 can to an assumed Rs 40 coconut-water option rather than an assumed Rs 125 energy drink. What should guide your pricing research?","o":["Investigate the shopper's actual substitute and the value of changing that comparison; a preferred category label does not establish willingness to pay","The cheapest production-cost estimate, assuming customer substitutes cannot change the value captured by the product.","The company's intended energy-drink category, treating the shopper's different comparison as irrelevant to willingness to pay.","The most expensive category benchmark, using its higher price as proof the shopper should value the new can similarly."],"a":0},{"q":"Price-pack architecture exists because:","o":["Channel pricing can be determined from retailer preferences alone, without examining how packs affect customer value, volume and unit economics.","Different channels and occasions have different willingness-to-pay - pack size and format let one product capture several","Larger packs necessarily improve margins at every price, regardless of discounts, costs, storage constraints or consumer purchasing patterns.","One standard pack removes differences between occasions and channels, so willingness to pay can be inferred from a single store price."],"a":1},{"q":"A 4-pack priced below 4x the single-can MRP mainly serves to:","o":["It creates higher willingness to pay per can by definition, even though the multipack is explicitly priced lower per unit.","It may encourage larger-basket trial or repeat behavior through a lower unit price; test contribution and cannibalization rather than assuming habit","It removes cannibalization because buying several units in one pack cannot replace purchases that would otherwise occur separately.","It proves total contribution increases because a larger basket offsets any discount, without checking repeat behavior or unit margin."],"a":1},{"q":"Penetration pricing (enter low) is most dangerous when:","o":["A low launch price can anchor expectations; later increases need value and demand evidence, not an assumption that lost margin can always be recovered","A low initial price proves unit economics are sustainable once volume grows, so subsequent price increases need no demand evidence.","Initial discounts establish the permanent maximum price even if the offer or customer value changes, making all later tests pointless.","Reference prices matter only for competitors, so the launch price has no effect on the customer's response to a later increase."],"a":0},{"q":"A pricing proposal cites willingness to pay but says incremental costs are irrelevant. For extra sales, the sound response is:","o":["Use average gross revenue as incremental profit, then evaluate capacity only after a positive revenue forecast has been approved.","Check contribution after incremental costs and displaced sales, then capacity and fixed investment; willingness to pay alone does not establish profitability","Choose the highest tested willingness to pay because it proves every additional sale increases operating contribution regardless of displaced products.","Treat variable production cost as the only relevant test, excluding channel charges and cannibalization because these are not factory costs."],"a":1},{"q":"At unchanged volume and unit variable cost, with no extra selling costs or channel deductions, why can a price increase have a strong profit effect?","o":["The gain equals the price change divided by total revenue, regardless of unit volumes, cost definitions or deductions in the model.","A price rise guarantees higher total profit because volume response cannot matter once unchanged-volume contribution has been calculated.","A higher price reduces variable cost automatically, so the gain exceeds the added revenue even when unit cost is explicitly unchanged.","The additional realized price adds directly to unit contribution; real decisions must still test volume response and other costs"],"a":3}]},"23":{"objectives":["Work a full hypothetical pricing case under time pressure and defend the number.","Combine demand-response and cannibalization logic with explicitly stated classroom cost assumptions."],"videos":[{"id":"4fKxOwrMpVg","title":"Pricing Case Interviews: the Park ‘n Shop Case","by":"My Consulting Offer","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Pricing: lecture notes","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-818-pricing-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/","note":"MIT OCW - elasticity, price discrimination and willingness-to-pay measurement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Companies Must Learn to Achieve the Price Advantage or Pay the Price","url":"https://knowledge.wharton.upenn.edu/article/companies-must-learn-to-achieve-the-price-advantage-or-pay-the-price/","note":"Wharton on building pricing as a capability, not an event."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Free with a Google account - build the entry model here, one assumption per row."}],"exercise":"Classroom pricing case (25 minutes): a hypothetical 60-store coffee chain has 90,000 shopper visits/day. Without bottled brew, 18% of visits buy one Rs 90 cold-coffee cup, giving Rs 26 contribution per cup. At Rs 120, assume 10% of shopper visits buy one bottle. At Rs 99, assume bottle conversion doubles to 20%; visits do not change. At BOTH prices, 60% of bottle sales displace a cup the shopper would otherwise buy; the other 40% would otherwise buy NOTHING from the chain. Check displaced cups against the 16,200-cup baseline. Bottle variable cost is Rs 86 at either price, so contribution is Rs 34 at Rs 120 and Rs 13 at Rs 99. Ignore taxes, returns, capacity constraints, fixed-cost changes and all other products; no shopper buys more than one bottle. (a) State the customer alternative and governing pricing lens. (b) For each price compute bottles sold, displaced cups, incremental bottle contribution minus foregone cup contribution, and remaining cup sales. (c) Recommend a price only under these assumptions, then state what actual evidence could reverse it. (d) Design a two-week pilot in six stores as an initial proposal, with comparable control stores or randomized store-periods where feasible. Explain how you would check sample size, seasonality, stockouts and cannibalization before calling the result conclusive. Checks: Rs 120 gives 9,000 bottles, 5,400 displaced cups and Rs 165,600/day incremental contribution; Rs 99 gives 18,000 bottles, 10,800 displaced cups and -Rs 46,800/day. Higher bottle volume alone is not the decision criterion.","quiz":[{"q":"Cannibalization in pricing means:","o":["A reduction in factory unit cost as the new product grows, independently of what existing buyers would otherwise purchase.","New-product sales to customers who would otherwise buy nothing from the firm, even when no existing sale is displaced.","A competitor losing sales to your product, without any substitution away from your own previously sold products.","New-product revenue that simply replaces your own existing-product revenue - only the increment counts"],"a":3},{"q":"Classroom case: 90,000 shopper visits/day; baseline cups are 18% of visits. Bottles convert 10% of visits at Rs 120; 60% of bottle sales displace a baseline cup. Displaced cups/day are:","o":["5,400","10,800","9,000","16,200"],"a":0},{"q":"Incremental contribution per cannibalized unit (bottled Rs 34 vs cup Rs 26) is:","o":["Rs 34","Rs 60","Rs 26","Rs 8"],"a":3},{"q":"In a hypothetical case, 40% of bottle buyers would otherwise buy NOTHING from the chain, and no extra capacity or fixed costs apply. Why is their bottle contribution fully incremental?","o":["Their purchase is genuinely incremental demand - no existing sale was displaced","The40%share establishes launch profitability after all chain costs, without checking any omitted investment.","Higher bottle revenue makes every sale incremental profit, regardless of costs or displaced cups.","Every bottle contribution is incremental, including displaced cups, because the new product format differs."],"a":0},{"q":"Elasticity in a pricing case is best estimated by:","o":["A controlled price test across comparable stores or periods, measuring percentage quantity response to percentage price change and checking confounders","Compare one stores price and sales with another stores on a different holiday, attributing all differences to the observed price difference.","Divide current quantity by current price, without changing price or checking how quantities would differ under a comparable alternative.","Ask buyers whether they prefer a discount, treating stated preference as a measured percentage demand response at the proposed prices."],"a":0},{"q":"A price cut from Rs 120 to Rs 99 doubles bottle conversion in a hypothetical case. The cut improves incremental operating contribution only if:","o":["Bottle revenue exceeds baseline cup revenue, so costs and the same no-bottle comparison are unnecessary for deciding which price contributes more.","After variable costs and all displaced cup contribution, the total increment exceeds the corresponding increment at Rs 120","The increase in conversion alone proves an improvement, because twice as many bottles necessarily offsets any decline in unit contribution.","The lower shelf price matches a competitor, so displaced cup contribution can be ignored when comparing the two scenarios."],"a":1},{"q":"Why run the price test in 6 stores for 2 weeks rather than chain-wide?","o":["A small pilot removes every launch cost and future risk, so decisions made after the trial can treat the full rollout as costlessly reversible.","The pilot proves the treatment works if its sales rise, even without comparing baseline, control conditions, customer mix or other concurrent changes.","It bounds initial downside and allows a comparison, but adequate sample size, controls and duration must be checked before declaring a result","Six stores and two weeks guarantee statistical adequacy for any effect size, so the result can be generalized without checking variation or demand timing."],"a":2},{"q":"Own-store distribution changes pricing power mainly because:","o":["An own-store sale has the same deductions as an external distributor arrangement, so the channel economics need not be specified.","There is no external retailer margin, but tax, own selling costs and customer alternatives still matter; MRP is not automatically net proceeds","Owning the stores removes price sensitivity, so higher bottle prices can be selected without testing customer choice or displaced cup sales.","Without an external retailer, the entire MRP becomes contribution because the chain does not incur selling costs or face alternative products."],"a":1},{"q":"A competitor matches your price within a month. The pricing-strategy response is to:","o":["Hold the existing price without testing because differentiation necessarily protects volume whenever the company describes its product as better.","Match the competitor immediately because an observed competitor price establishes the demand-maximizing and contribution-maximizing price for your own offer.","Increase marketing spending before checking the lost volume or contribution, assuming promotion replaces the need to understand customers' alternatives.","Test whether relevant differentiation supports willingness to pay and contribution; a matching competitor price does not establish the right response"],"a":3},{"q":"The strongest pricing-case synthesis states:","o":["The highest possible price and a volume target, with the governing customer comparison and contribution economics left for implementation.","The price, the governing lens, the math that supports it, and the experiment that would refine it","A competitor benchmark and product features, without stating the recommended price, displaced sales or evidence that could change it.","A full revenue forecast presented as profit, with no experiment or explicit assumptions about demand, variable costs and channel terms."],"a":1}]},"24":{"objectives":["Use DICE to diagnose duration, team capability, senior/local commitment and extra workload, treating the score as a screening aid rather than a guarantee.","Design change actions using the current Kotter accelerators and distinguish them from the original linear eight-step formulation."],"videos":[{"id":"22coAwMD9M0","title":"Kotter's 8-Step Change Model Explained","by":"EPM","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"DICE: a tool for executional certainty","url":"https://www.brightline.org/resources/dice-a-tool-for-executional-certainty/","note":"Public BCG-authored explanation of DICE, its factors and the weighted formula."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"A way to assess and prioritize your change efforts","url":"https://www.brightline.org/resources/a-way-to-assess-and-prioritize-your-change-efforts/","note":"BCG-authored public guide to scoring, comparing initiatives and reassessing them over time."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Kotter's 8-Step Change Model","url":"https://www.kotterinc.com/methodology/8-steps/","note":"Kotter describes how the original linear eight steps evolved into the later eight accelerators; use the headings on this page for this exercise."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Blank roadmap on paper or in your spreadsheet","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"No third-party template is required. Use paper, your existing spreadsheet, or Google Sheets (Google account required) with columns for action, owner, date, evidence and stop condition."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical: a 3,000-employee lender proposes moving loan approvals from branch files to a central digital system over 12 months; some branch managers fear losing authority. (a) Diagnose DICE: define meaningful review milestones, execution-team capability, senior commitment C1, local commitment C2 and extra workload. For a short initiative D can reflect total duration; for this longer program use the interval between structured milestone reviews, not total length alone. (b) Use supplied illustrative scores, not inferred facts: D=1, I=3, C1=2, C2=3, E=3 on a 1-to-4 scale where lower is more favorable. Calculate D + 2I + 2C1 + C2 + E. Compare a proposed resourcing/support plan with D=1, I=2, C1=1, C2=2, E=2. Explain why the lower score is a screening signal, not proof the plan will succeed. (c) Name the capabilities and protected staff time required, propose work to remove during cutover, and write three truthful sentences of manager communication without promising jobs or outcomes that leadership has not decided. (d) Map actions to the eight current headings on Kotter's page: Create A Sense of Urgency; Build A Guiding Coalition; Form A Strategic Vision; Enlist A Volunteer Army; Enable Action By Removing Barriers; Generate Short-Term Wins; Sustain Acceleration; Institute Change. Identify a likely skipped action from the supplied case evidence, not a national or ownership stereotype. Checks: illustrative scores are 17 and 11; name actual evidence needed before replacing the supplied scores with a real assessment.","quiz":[{"q":"DICE stands for:","o":["Duration, Integrity, Commitment, Effort - four hard factors used to assess change-execution risk","Duration, Incentives, Communication, Execution - a qualitative list of stakeholder preferences.","Demand, Integrity, Cost, Efficiency - the four inputs in a market-entry calculation.","Direction, Information, Capability, Engagement - the official four-factor DICE scoring formula."],"a":0},{"q":"In DICE, Duration can measure total length for a short initiative; for a long program, which measure is especially useful?","o":["Time between structured milestone reviews, so drift can be detected and acted on","Time from initial proposal to budget approval, ignoring progress checks after the implementation team begins work.","Time until the announced launch date, excluding review intervals because milestones do not affect drift during long programs.","Length of each meeting, treating shorter meetings as proof that the program can execute faster under any review cadence."],"a":0},{"q":"Kotter's first step is:","o":["Form the full coalition first and postpone the reason to act until after implementation roles have been assigned.","Announce completion of the first milestone before explaining what opportunity or challenge requires collective action.","Institutionalize new routines before articulating a change vision, because urgency follows automatically from redesigned incentives.","Create a sense of urgency around a clear opportunity and reason to act"],"a":3},{"q":"A transformation announces a 3-year roadmap with annual reviews. The DICE critique:","o":["A long roadmap guarantees enough planning detail to replace interim progress reviews, provided the annual presentation includes a full budget.","Annual gaps can let drift compound; choose structured milestone reviews frequent enough to detect and act on meaningful slippage","Annual review intervals are adequate for every project because a three-year horizon makes month-to-month evidence too noisy to act on.","The main problem is the total duration alone; shortening the announced roadmap removes the need to check milestones or respond to drift."],"a":1},{"q":"\"Integrity of the team\" refers to:","o":["Every department nominates a volunteer, so broad representation proves execution capacity even when no one has dedicated time or relevant skills.","The team signs the formal plan and supports the sponsor's intent, regardless of its skills, credibility or available implementation capacity.","Whether the change team has the skills, stature and dedicated time to actually execute - not a part-time committee of volunteers","The implementation team agrees to report progress, making its capability independent of authority, experience or the operational demands of the change."],"a":2},{"q":"The branch managers see digitization as a threat. The correct first move:","o":["Treat resistance as a communications problem only and announce new targets without investigating how digitization changes managers actual work or incentives.","Promise that no role will ever change before checking the operating plan, because reassurance takes priority over the truth of employment implications.","Delay involving managers until all roles are fixed, assuming implementation concerns cannot improve the design once the technical direction is chosen.","Understand the concern, involve managers in designing workable roles and support, and communicate decisions truthfully without inventing job guarantees"],"a":3},{"q":"Quick wins matter in Kotter's sequence because they:","o":["They establish permanent success after one positive result, removing the need to adapt routines, incentives or later implementation work.","They prove the initial strategy was correct across every location, so rollout gates can be removed once the first team reports improvement.","Produce visible proof that converts skeptics and funds political capital for the harder stages","They make visible activity counts sufficient evidence of benefits, even when the outcome baseline and causal assumptions have not been checked."],"a":2},{"q":"Kotter's current framework calls for instituting change. Which failure would undermine that?","o":["Revising implementation details as evidence arrives, while keeping the intended opportunity and the desired operating change explicit to participants.","Assigning responsibility for benefits and tracking actual behavior after launch, rather than ending governance at the installation milestone.","Updating incentives and operating routines after evidence of improved outcomes, while continuing to check whether the new behavior is sustained.","Declaring success at go-live while rewards, management practices and daily routines still reinforce the old behavior"],"a":3},{"q":"Effort (the E in DICE) warns that change fails when:","o":["People work hard on the new process while managers explicitly reprioritize old tasks and resource the additional implementation demand.","Extra change work without reprioritized day jobs can overload capacity and threaten execution; workload and support need testing","The organization reduces parallel workload and dedicates capacity, but still verifies whether new behavior and expected business benefits are emerging.","The team spends fewer hours in meetings after replacing some old duties, even while it continues tracking outcomes and correcting implementation problems."],"a":1},{"q":"For illustrative DICE scores D=1, I=3, C1=2, C2=3, E=3, use D + 2I + 2C1 + C2 + E. The screening score is:","o":["28","10","11","17"],"a":3}]},"25":{"objectives":["Build a staged classroom implementation plan with owners, operational evidence and stop conditions.","Separate gross revenue, incremental contribution, recurring benefits and one-off investment on a consistent annual basis."],"videos":[{"id":"As_EZt3ht98","title":"Comparison of a Successful vs. Failed Digital Transformation [Two Digi","by":"Digital Transformation with Eric Kimberling","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"How the best acquirers excel at integration","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/how-the-best-acquirers-excel-at-integration","note":"McKinsey on integration discipline - the closest cousin of transformation execution."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Gathering and using evidence","url":"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/test-learn-and-grow-playbook/gathering-and-using-evidence","note":"Public guide to assumptions, mixed evidence, triangulation and a living theory of change."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Blank implementation roadmap","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Use paper or your existing spreadsheet; no paid template is required. Google Sheets requires a Google account."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical: a 200-store general retailer is considering an inventory system and paid product-setup appointments over nine months. No clinical services are involved. All figures are assumptions, not observed company facts. Annual purchases are Rs 200 Cr; expiry waste is measured at purchase cost and is assumed to fall from 3.0% to 2.0% of unchanged purchases. Appointments increase from 0 to 4 completed appointments/store/day over 300 operating days/year. The retailer retains Rs 100 per completed appointment after partner fees and pays Rs 40 additional variable servicing cost; other store sales are unchanged. Recurring system/support cost is Rs 0.60 Cr/year; one-off rollout investment Rs 1.20 Cr. Ignore taxes, financing, discounting and working-capital release. (a) Build a full-year STEADY-STATE bridge: expiry saving, gross retained appointment revenue, variable servicing cost, net recurring operating benefit. Distinguish it from launch-year cash: no rollout ramp schedule is supplied, so launch-year cash cannot be computed. (b) Test a proposed Rs 18 Cr/year net-benefit claim against this bridge. (c) Design 4-6 workstreams with named owner ROLES, one outcome metric each, a proposed pilot and a measurable scale/stop gate. Pilot size and duration are hypotheses to justify using risk, variation, capacity and sufficient evidence, not a universal store count. (d) Draft a monthly baseline-to-actual view with consistent denominators and a risk register including staff adoption, data migration and customer experience. Check: expiry saving Rs 2 Cr/year; retained appointment revenue Rs 2.4 Cr; variable servicing cost Rs 0.96 Cr; net recurring benefit Rs 2.84 Cr before one-off investment. Do not subtract one-off investment as if it recurred every year or claim first-year benefit without a ramp schedule.","quiz":[{"q":"An outcome metric (vs activity metric) for an inventory-system rollout is:","o":["Every store received the licenses planned for the first wave.","\"Expiry waste fell from 3.1% to 2.2% of purchases\"","Forty training sessions were completed by the rollout team.","Inventory software was installed in fifty stores this quarter."],"a":1},{"q":"The right pilot design for a 200-store chain:","o":["Choose a fixed store count from another retailer and reuse its timeline without checking the downside, variation or evidence requirements.","Roll out to every store together because a single launch date removes uncertainty about how results differ across stores.","Use one flagship store and scale as soon as its manager reports satisfaction, without comparing process or store variation.","A proposed representative pilot sized for store variation, downside risk and adequate data, with a measurable scale/stop gate"],"a":3},{"q":"A benefits baseline exists to:","o":["Make benefit claims falsifiable - without the pre-change number, every \"saving\" is a story","Set a desired result after launch and treat the gap to that target as measured savings.","Record only the post-change outcome because a better result than forecast establishes the intervention effect.","Use the strongest pilot store as the baseline for all stores without adjusting for different starting conditions."],"a":0},{"q":"Which operational measure can provide an early signal of staff adoption problems in a classroom appointment-system rollout?","o":["Measure downloads by all employees and treat installation as evidence that the intended daily workflow is being completed.","Share of eligible staff completing the intended workflow accurately, checked with task quality and customer outcomes","Track aggregate satisfaction alone and infer that unchanged scores prove every store is using the system correctly.","Count total appointments and attribute any revenue increase to adoption without checking the staff workflow or the customers served."],"a":1},{"q":"A workstream owner should be:","o":["A vendor contact responsible for installation, even when business-process changes and staff capacity sit outside that contact's authority.","A committee sharing responsibility, without one person accountable for decisions or access to the required operational levers.","One named person with authority over the levers the workstream needs","A senior sponsor with no assigned operational time or decision route for the workstream's day-to-day constraints."],"a":2},{"q":"Wave-based scaling beats big-bang primarily because:","o":["A small pilot makes the entire program reversible at no cost, including licenses, staff time and commitments already made before the pilot.","Rolling out in waves is always cheaper because learning automatically offsets every delay, duplicated system and additional coordination cost.","Each wave can incorporate evidence and a failed gate can halt further expansion; already-incurred costs and later-wave risks still exist","Each successful wave proves later stores will behave the same way, so subsequent scale gates can be removed without reviewing new evidence."],"a":2},{"q":"Classroom steady-state bridge: expiry saving Rs 2 Cr/year; retained appointment revenue Rs 2.4 Cr; additional variable servicing Rs 0.96 Cr; recurring system cost Rs 0.60 Cr. Net recurring operating benefit before one-off investment is:","o":["Rs 2.84 Cr","Rs 4.40 Cr","Rs 3.44 Cr","Rs 18 Cr"],"a":0},{"q":"\"Change fatigue\" in month 6 of a rollout is best countered by:","o":["Pause all activity immediately based only on the fatigue label, without checking operational outcomes or identifying which tasks create strain.","Add more compulsory update meetings while retaining every parallel initiative, assuming fatigue is only a shortage of project information.","Investigate workload and causes, show credible progress, and remove or sequence conflicting initiatives when evidence supports it","Keep deadlines and workload unchanged and offer recognition, without investigating whether staff capacity or conflicting priorities cause the difficulty."],"a":2},{"q":"Which risk-register entry best supports active risk management?","o":["A detailed risk description and an owner, but no signal of deterioration or practical mitigation resources.","A risk description, a severity color and a review date, with no accountable owner or funded response.","A list of mitigations and budget totals, without linking them to specific risks, indicators or responsible roles.","Risk, leading indicator, owner, and a mitigation that is actually resourced"],"a":3},{"q":"Which post-go-live governance question most directly tests the benefits case without replacing stability, budget and training checks?","o":["Did every employee attend training, regardless of whether the intended workflow and business outcomes improved after launch?","Did the vendor close its support tickets, regardless of whether the benefits baseline and actual store results can be compared?","Were all planned features delivered on schedule, regardless of whether the operating measures changed or someone owns the benefit gaps?","\"Are the business metrics moving as the case promised - and who acts when they are not?\""],"a":3}]},"26":{"objectives":["Use a governing thought and structured supporting arguments, choosing a direct or context-first introduction for audience and purpose","Run client interactions - interviews, readouts, pushback - with a clear objective per meeting"],"videos":[{"id":"ppokRtCeuyI","title":"TALK LIKE A CONSULTANT - Top down communication explained (management ","by":"Firm Learning","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Pyramid Principle - Book Summary (Part 1)","url":"https://strategyu.co/pyramid-principle-partone/","note":"For direct presentations, use a governing thought with grouped support; the source also allows context-first introductions when audience and purpose call for them."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Interviewing at McKinsey","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/careers/interviewing","note":"The communication bar consultants are selected against."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Docs","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Free with a Google account - draft the client memo here."}],"exercise":"Classroom communication exercise, not a real client message. Raw fictional notes: field visits completed in Jaipur and Indore; 14 distributors met; margins described as an issue without amounts; ERP reportedly three weeks late; some Indore distributors dislike credit terms changed in March; vendor says data migration is blocking rollout; Jaipur described only as okay; team morale reported as fine. (a) Separate observations, attributed reports, hypotheses and missing evidence. Do not infer that credit changes caused margin problems or that the vendor explanation is verified. (b) Write a one-sentence governing thought and supporting groups that stay within the evidence; choose direct or context-first introduction for an explicitly named audience and purpose. (c) Propose owner ROLES and relative deadlines for verifying margin data, credit/collection effects and migration milestones. Label them proposals, not commitments. (d) Write a 20-second spoken version with a clear decision or information request. Check that each claim traces to a supplied note or is marked as a hypothesis. Produce a draft only; do not send to any actual client.","quiz":[{"q":"In a direct, answer-first Pyramid Principle presentation, which structure follows the governing thought?","o":["Available data tables first, followed by conclusions and grouped arguments.","Organizational team updates, without grouping under the governing thought.","Grouped supporting arguments, with evidence beneath each","Research chronology, with the recommendation deferred until the end."],"a":2},{"q":"A stakeholder says \"I only have 60 seconds.\" The consultant's response should be:","o":["Use the minute to describe the full project chronology, postponing both the conclusion and the decision request.","The recommendation, the two strongest reasons, and the ask - the full pyramid is for people with time","Present every calculation at greater speed, leaving the stakeholder to infer the recommendation and the requested action.","Read the methodology and offer to send the recommendation later, even though the stakeholder needs it now."],"a":1},{"q":"Arguments in a pyramid must be MECE because:","o":["Using equal-length headings eliminates the need to check overlap between arguments or gaps beneath the conclusion.","A fixed number of groups proves the recommendation even when the same evidence appears under several groups.","Overlapping arguments double-count evidence and gappy arguments leave the conclusion unsupported","Matching the client's organizational chart makes an argument complete even when some required evidence is missing."],"a":2},{"q":"In a client interview, the primary reason to state the purpose upfront is:","o":["It authorizes using the answers with any later audience, regardless of consent, privacy restrictions or the stated purpose of the interview.","It explains the inquiry and use of answers, helping informed participation and trust without guaranteeing honest or complete disclosure","It guarantees that every answer will be accurate, because an informed participant has no reason to forget or withhold information.","It establishes the preferred conclusion in advance, so interview questions can focus only on obtaining examples that support the team."],"a":1},{"q":"A client pushes back hard on your recommendation mid-readout. The skilled move:","o":["Accept the objection as conclusive and abandon the analysis immediately, without identifying its factual basis or the decision concern involved.","Treat the objection as an implementation preference and negotiate a compromise before checking whether the underlying factual claim is wrong.","Fairly restate the objection, separate factual disagreement from interests, verify disputed facts and ask what would make the recommendation workable","Repeat the recommendation with stronger confidence and postpone all disputed evidence until after the stakeholder has approved the decision."],"a":2},{"q":"\"So what\" testing a slide means:","o":["Each exhibit should answer a relevant question, including evidence that challenges or limits the recommendation; remove irrelevant material","Keep only exhibits supporting the preferred recommendation and move contradictory evidence out of the audience's view to avoid weakening conviction.","Make every exhibit imply certainty, so the audience sees one consistent story without needing to consider limitations or alternative explanations.","Include every completed analysis because the amount of work demonstrates diligence, even if some exhibits answer no relevant decision question."],"a":0},{"q":"Horizontal flow in a pyramid means:","o":["Each team supplies a descriptive topic heading, and the audience must work out how the separate topics support the recommendation.","Each level reads as a complete, grouped argument - the headers alone should tell the story as full sentences","Headers describe the order in which analysis happened, even if the reader cannot infer the argument without the underlying charts.","Every slide repeats the conclusion verbatim, replacing the supporting logic with visual consistency and avoiding differences between evidence groups."],"a":1},{"q":"For a CEO expecting a brief recommendation and already aware of context, which opener most risks burying the requested answer?","o":["As part of our ongoing engagement, we have conducted extensive analysis over the past weeks, beginning with market research, field visits and interviews before preparing this discussion.","Our evidence supports a limited rollout rather than all stores; the unresolved constraint is staff capacity, and we need a decision on resources.","We recommend the limited rollout, supported by lower waste and stable customer service; the decision is whether to fund the next wave.","The decision today is whether to fund the next wave; our recommendation is a limited rollout, with one material capacity constraint."],"a":0},{"q":"When you do not know an answer in front of a client:","o":["Say so, state how you will find out and by when - credibility compounds; bluffs get audited","Describe a nearby fact you do know, without acknowledging that it does not answer the question asked.","State that another team owns the question, without arranging a verification route or a realistic time for returning an answer.","Offer the most plausible answer as confirmed, then verify it privately after the audience has already relied on it."],"a":0},{"q":"Meeting hygiene that separates professionals: a meeting invite should carry:","o":["A sequence of discussion topics, without an objective, requested decision or relevant pre-read to prepare participants for that decision.","All background documents collected so far, without naming the decision or distinguishing required preparation from optional reference material.","Objective, the decision to be made, and pre-reads - so the meeting produces a decision, not another meeting","A detailed title and attendee list, leaving the objective and decision request for participants to discover during the meeting."],"a":2}]},"27":{"objectives":["Practice a classroom client-interview simulation and convert attributed claims into a provisional analysis and verification plan","Handle the full loop: hypothesis, question, probe, note, synthesis"],"videos":[{"id":"ggJaQ4I2h9w","title":"Talk like a Consultant - How to conduct Interviews with Clients","by":"Firm Learning","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Using in-depth interviews","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/using-in-depth-interviews","note":"Public practical guide: plan, obtain informed consent, ask neutral questions, follow examples and store notes safely."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Gathering and using evidence","url":"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/test-learn-and-grow-playbook/gathering-and-using-evidence","note":"Distinguishes assumptions from evidence and explains triangulation and mixed methods."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Docs","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Free with a Google account - draft the client memo here."}],"exercise":"Classroom interview simulation: a fictional 25-city logistics company reports on-time delivery falling from 94% to 87% between two quarters. No shipment counts, route mix or definition of on-time are supplied. (a) State the seven-percentage-point change, then list the denominator, measurement, mix and process data needed before attributing it to operational deterioration. (b) Build three provisional explanations and ten neutral interview questions, including at least three open probes and questions that could reveal explanations outside your initial tree. Begin with purpose, consent and the proposed use of notes; do not invent confidentiality promises. (c) For each explanation write two explicitly SYNTHETIC role-play answers pointing in different directions, plus the operational records that would test each. These invented answers are practice material, never observed evidence and never proof of causation. (d) Draft four lines labeled simulation: provisional observations, what they might mean, next verification test, and requested information. Mark each hypothesis supported, challenged or unresolved with its current evidence limits. Do not send the draft or contact a real person. A single interview claim cannot by itself establish the cause of the delivery-rate change.","quiz":[{"q":"Why write a hypothesis tree BEFORE a client interview?","o":["Provisional explanations focus discriminating questions, while open probes can reveal mechanisms outside the initial tree","A fixed explanation determines every question, and answers outside it are excluded to avoid changing the study halfway through.","A full interview script should be followed in the same order even when early answers reveal a different mechanism.","The initial tree proves which causes matter, so questions need only collect quotations to illustrate each selected branch."],"a":0},{"q":"After explaining purpose, obtaining informed consent and agreeing how notes will be used, which substantive probe is most concrete and neutral?","o":["\"Walk me through what happens to a package that misses its connection in Nagpur - start to finish\"","Would you agree that poor hub management caused the recent connection failures in Nagpur rather than any issue elsewhere?","Which team should take responsibility for the missed packages if the decline is as serious as the reported figures suggest?","Why are the hubs performing badly now, and is the main reason staffing shortages or failures in the tracking software?"],"a":0},{"q":"Open probes beat yes/no questions early in an interview because they:","o":["They establish population prevalence more accurately than a structured survey, even when only a few participants are interviewed.","Surface information you did not know to ask about - yes/no questions only test what you already suspect","They confirm the interviewer's initial causal tree as long as the participant is allowed to answer in their own words.","They remove the need for follow-up because an unrestricted answer always contains every relevant circumstance and alternative."],"a":1},{"q":"An interviewee gives a vague answer (\"the hubs are just slower lately\"). The next move:","o":["Record the statement as a verified trend and build a recommendation before asking which periods, hubs or measures the participant meant.","Replace the vague statement with the team's preferred numerical estimate, because a precise number will make the analysis easier to present.","Ask the participant to agree that understaffing explains the slowdown, rather than first investigating a specific occurrence and its baseline.","Drill with a concrete instance: \"which hub, which day, how much slower, versus which baseline?\""],"a":3},{"q":"You realize mid-interview your lead hypothesis is dying. Correct response:","o":["Finish the same branch before considering contrary evidence, because changing questions would make the interview's original hypothesis invalid.","Explain why the participant must have misunderstood the process, then repeat questions until the original explanation receives support.","Explicitly pivot - \"this is pointing away from X; let me test Y\" - the interview serves the truth, not your plan","Treat the new answer as proving the opposite causal explanation, without testing alternatives or checking operational records independently."],"a":2},{"q":"Numbers from interviews should be treated as:","o":["Claims to triangulate against system data - recall is biased, especially about performance","Reliable evidence of causation whenever the recalled numbers appear consistent with the initial hypothesis tree.","Verified measurements once the interviewee has enough seniority or experience with the process being discussed.","Representative estimates of every hub once two participants independently recall the same approximate performance change."],"a":0},{"q":"When agreed with the participant and authorized for the audience, a prompt written interview summary can:","o":["Establish every claim as confirmed fact simply because the participant has not responded to the summary immediately.","Replace independent verification of the operational records because a written account is more precise than spoken answers.","Test understanding while memory is fresh and give participants an agreed route to correct errors before further analysis","Expand disclosure to any additional stakeholder whose role might be relevant, without checking the agreed purpose or audience."],"a":2},{"q":"A defensive interviewee (worried the study blames their team) is best handled by:","o":["Promise complete anonymity before checking whether recording, reporting or the small participant group makes that promise possible to honor.","Framing questions at the process, not the person (\"how does the system decide X?\" not \"why did YOU do X?\")","Use individual-blame questions to obtain accountability first, assuming defensiveness will fall once the interviewer asserts sufficient authority.","Stop probing any process problem mentioned by the participant, because asking for concrete examples would necessarily create personal blame."],"a":1},{"q":"Which pair of closing questions can help identify missing issues and alternative perspectives?","o":["Which feature would you like us to add? and Would you rate the proposed solution highly in a survey?","Who approved the current process? and Can you confirm that the initial hypothesis is the correct explanation?","What should I have asked that I did not? and Who else sees this differently?","Would you support our preferred recommendation? and Can we quote you as agreeing before further evidence is checked?"],"a":2},{"q":"Converting interview notes into analysis means:","o":["Tag claims to provisional explanations, mark support/challenge/uncertainty, and name independent evidence needed before causal conclusions","Select quotations that fit the initial explanation and exclude contrary accounts to produce one internally consistent story.","Count how many interviewees mention a cause and treat the largest count as causal proof without independent process evidence.","Convert every attributed statement into an established finding once its wording has been checked against the interview notes."],"a":0}]},"28":{"objectives":["Re-judge the days 22-27 cold-brew pricing case after a competitor undercuts the category at Rs 99, scored on value-based pricing logic and response sequencing; do not let pattern-matching the original recommendation pass as evidence."],"videos":[{"id":"TMsx5SSyFyg","title":"Pricing Case Interview Framework | MBB Case Prep","by":"Dolly Bajaj","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Pricing case interview example (mbbprep)","url":"https://mbbprep.com/blog/pricing-case-interview-example","note":"Pricing framework and interviewer-style prompts; numerical inputs are incomplete for a full independent worked price calculation."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Case interview types: pricing","url":"https://www.case-prep.com/course/case-interview-types-pricing","note":"Pricing case formats: new product, reaction to competition, bundling."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets (free with Google account)","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Free tool - keep it open while you work the exercise."}],"exercise":"Classroom review: rerun the Day23 cold-brew case after a fictional comparable rival launches at Rs99. Keep the SAME denominator and baseline:90000 eligible shoppers/day,18% baseline cup purchases=16200 cups/day; cups contribute Rs26 each. Bottle variable cost is Rs86 at both prices and receipts equal the listed bottle price in this classroom model; ignore tax, channel deductions, fixed-cost changes, marketing cost and working capital. Updated demand assumptions: at YOUR Rs120, bottle conversion is12% of eligible shoppers; at YOUR Rs99,20%. At either price60% of bottles replace one otherwise-purchased cup; remaining bottles are incremental, and there are no other effects on cup demand. All figures are hypotheses, not observed demand. (a) Calculate bottles, displaced cups, remaining cups and daily incremental contribution versus the SAME no-bottle baseline at each price. Check feasibility against16200 baseline cups. (b) Find the minimum Rs120 conversion rate needed to match the Rs99 scenario, assuming its economics and60% displacement stay fixed; interpret negative implied break-even rather than invent negative sales. (c) Recommend which tested price has higher incremental contribution and name the demand/cost assumption that most threatens this limited conclusion. (d) Propose a response sequence using channel constraints, time-to-impact, reversibility and evidence needed for placement, differentiation or price changes; there is no universal mandatory order. Check: Rs120 gives10800 bottles,6480 displaced cups,9720 remaining cups and +Rs198720/day; Rs99 gives18000 bottles,10800 displaced cups,5400 remaining cups and -Rs46800/day. Effective contribution per bottle after displacement is Rs18.4 vs-Rs2.6. Matching a negative scenario needs no positive Rs120 conversion: zero earns0 incremental contribution, already better than-Rs46800. This does not prove zero-demand launch is desirable, or include omitted fixed/marketing costs.","quiz":[{"q":"In the review case, Rs120 bottle conversion12% of90000 eligible shoppers produces:","o":["6480 bottles/day","16200 bottles/day","18000 bottles/day","10800 bottles/day"],"a":3},{"q":"At Rs120 with60% displacement, how many of the16200 baseline cups remain?","o":["10800","16200","6480","9720"],"a":3},{"q":"At Rs120, daily incremental contribution versus the no-bottle baseline is:","o":["-Rs46800","Rs168480","Rs198720","Rs367200"],"a":2},{"q":"At Rs99, bottle contribution13 and60% displacement of cups contributing26 imply net incremental contribution per bottle of:","o":["Rs13","Rs2.6","Rs28.6","-Rs2.6"],"a":3},{"q":"Which conclusion follows within the supplied two-price classroom model?","o":["Rs99 has higher contribution because its additional bottles offset every displaced cup at the supplied unit economics.","The two tested prices are equivalent because the same60% displacement assumption applies to both demand scenarios.","The model establishes a profitable launch at either price after all fixed and marketing costs, without more data.","Rs120 has higher incremental contribution; no-bottle0 also beats the Rs99 negative scenario, and omitted launch costs still matter"],"a":3},{"q":"If Rs120 contribution and60% displacement stay fixed, what nonnegative conversion is required merely to equal or beat the negative Rs99 scenario?","o":["A negative conversion rate should be treated as an achievable target because the algebra returns a negative number.","The conversion rate must equal20% because matching the rival requires matching its volume as well as its contribution.","Zero already beats it; positive conversion adds contribution before omitted costs","The conversion rate must exceed100% because a positive per-bottle contribution cannot be compared with a negative scenario."],"a":2},{"q":"Wave-based rollout beats big-bang because:","o":["Rolling out in waves is always cheaper because learning automatically offsets every delay, duplicated system and additional coordination cost.","Each wave can incorporate evidence and failed gates can halt expansion; this does not erase incurred costs or guarantee a pilot-sized loss cap","A small pilot makes the entire program reversible at no cost, including licenses, staff time and commitments already made before the pilot.","Each successful wave proves later stores will behave the same way, so subsequent scale gates can be removed without reviewing new evidence."],"a":1},{"q":"A complete risk-register entry contains:","o":["Risk, leading indicator, owner, resourced mitigation","Mitigation budget, without risk or accountable owner","Risk, severity color, review date only","Risk and owner, without indicator or mitigation"],"a":0},{"q":"Which measure provides a useful early signal of adoption problems, to be checked with task quality?","o":["Measure downloads by all employees and treat installation as evidence that the intended daily workflow is being completed.","Count total appointments and attribute any revenue increase to adoption without checking the staff workflow or the customers served.","Track aggregate satisfaction alone and infer that unchanged scores prove every store is using the system correctly.","Share of intended staff completing the new workflow correctly per site"],"a":3},{"q":"For a direct, answer-first executive readout, Pyramid Principle order is:","o":["Chronology first, recommendation only at the end","Separate team updates, without one governing thought","Answer first, then grouped MECE arguments","Every available exhibit, without a clear conclusion"],"a":2},{"q":"Horizontal flow means the slide headers alone:","o":["Tell the full story as complete sentences","Describe research tasks in their original chronological order","Repeat the recommendation without providing supporting reasons","List separate topics without showing the argument"],"a":0},{"q":"CEO joins for the final minute. You deliver:","o":["Project chronology, methods, and findings later","Recommendation, two strongest reasons, the ask","Every calculation, delivered at greater speed","Background first, conclusion if time remains"],"a":1},{"q":"Client pushback mid-readout: first separate:","o":["Treat the objection as an implementation preference and negotiate a compromise before checking whether the underlying factual claim is wrong.","Factual disagreements from interests, verifying disputed evidence and understanding what decision or concern is at stake","Accept the objection as conclusive and abandon the analysis immediately, without identifying its factual basis or the decision concern involved.","Repeat the recommendation with stronger confidence and postpone all disputed evidence until after the stakeholder has approved the decision."],"a":1},{"q":"Every exhibit must pass which test:","o":["Make every exhibit imply certainty, so the audience sees one consistent story without needing to consider limitations or alternative explanations.","Keep only exhibits supporting the preferred recommendation and move contradictory evidence out of the audience's view to avoid weakening conviction.","Include every completed analysis because the amount of work demonstrates diligence, even if some exhibits answer no relevant decision question.","So what: it answers a relevant question, including contrary evidence or limits on the recommendation"],"a":3},{"q":"When you do not know an answer in front of a client:","o":["Say so, state how and by when you will find out","Give a plausible estimate as confirmed, then verify it privately later","Answer a nearby question without acknowledging the missing information","Name another team without arranging verification or a return time"],"a":0},{"q":"A meeting invite should carry:","o":["Title and attendees, objective discovered during discussion","All documents, without a defined decision request","Objective, decision to be made, pre-reads","Discussion topics, without decision or relevant preparation"],"a":2},{"q":"Questions in a client interview map to:","o":["A fixed explanation determines every question, and answers outside it are excluded to avoid changing the study halfway through.","Provisional explanations they may support or challenge, while open probes can reveal alternatives","The initial tree proves which causes matter, so questions need only collect quotations to illustrate each selected branch.","A full interview script should be followed in the same order even when early answers reveal a different mechanism."],"a":1},{"q":"Open probes early in an interview:","o":["Remove any need for subsequent concrete follow-up questions","Prove the initial causal explanation from unstructured answers","Surface what you did not know to ask","Estimate population frequency from a small interview sample"],"a":2},{"q":"A vague answer (\"hubs are slower lately\") gets:","o":["Insert the teams own numerical estimate to make the statement precise","A concrete-instance drill: which hub, which day, how much, vs what baseline","Treat the vague trend as verified and proceed to recommendations","Suggest understaffing as the cause before asking for a concrete occurrence"],"a":1},{"q":"Interview numbers are:","o":["Causal evidence whenever they fit the hypothesis","Claims to triangulate against system data","Verified measurements once confirmed by senior interviewees","Representative estimates of every operating location"],"a":1},{"q":"Mid-interview your lead hypothesis is dying. You:","o":["Pivot explicitly and test the next hypothesis","Accept the opposite explanation without independent verification","Finish the original branch despite contrary evidence","Challenge the participant until the original theory wins"],"a":0},{"q":"Defensive interviewee: frame questions at:","o":["Stop probing any process problem mentioned by the participant, because asking for concrete examples would necessarily create personal blame.","Use individual-blame questions to obtain accountability first, assuming defensiveness will fall once the interviewer asserts sufficient authority.","The process - \"how does the system decide\" not \"why did you\"","Promise complete anonymity before checking whether recording, reporting or the small participant group makes that promise possible to honor."],"a":2},{"q":"Which closing questions can reveal gaps and alternative perspectives?","o":["Who approved this process? Can you confirm our explanation?","\"What should I have asked?\" + \"who sees this differently?\"","Which feature do you want? Would you rate it highly?","Would you endorse our preferred recommendation? Can we quote agreement?"],"a":1},{"q":"When agreed with the participant and authorized for the purpose, audience and confidentiality limits, a prompt written interview summary can:","o":["Expand the audience without checking agreed purpose or confidentiality","Establish every claim as confirmed if no correction arrives immediately","Replace independent verification with a more polished written account","Test understanding and catch misreadings while memory is fresh"],"a":3},{"q":"Review-week synthesis: the consulting loop in order is:","o":["Choose the recommendation first, gather supporting examples, and treat objections as implementation concerns.","Analyze the available numbers first, select a conclusion, and then define the question to match it.","Clarify, structure, analyze, synthesize - then so-what into the next test","Structure around the first explanation, exclude inconsistent evidence, and present the result without a next test."],"a":2}]},"29":{"objectives":["Write a Rumelt kernel for a fictional edtech case: diagnosis, guiding policy and coherent actions linked to a cash constraint, without hindsight or invented real-company quotes."],"videos":[{"id":"UZrTl16hZdk","title":"Prof. Richard Rumelt — Good Strategy/Bad Strategy: the difference and why it matters (LSE Events)","by":"LSE","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Perils of Bad Strategy — Richard Rumelt, McKinsey","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/the-perils-of-bad-strategy","note":""},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Rumelt lecture context - LSE","url":"https://www.lse.ac.uk/lse-player/good-strategy-bad-strategy-the-difference-and-why-it-matters","note":"Publisher event overview only, not a full lecture transcript. Use the authored McKinsey article for the kernel definitions."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"One-page kernel worksheet","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Use paper or an existing editor: evidence and uncertainties; diagnosis; guiding policy and exclusions; coordinated actions; tests. No paid template required."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical, not BYJUS or any real company: LearnArc has Rs18Cr unrestricted cash and burns Rs6Cr/month at the current run rate. Paid acquisition cost per new learner rose from Rs4000 to Rs8000, while expected lifetime contribution BEFORE acquisition cost is Rs6000. These are comparable cohorts with the same contribution definition; estimates remain uncertain. Two acquired course businesses use separate learning platforms; integration consumes the same team needed to improve paid conversion. No financing is committed. Fictional draft from leadership: Become the countrys largest learning company through growth, innovation and customer delight. (a) Calculate acquisition contribution and current simple runway; list missing evidence and estimate risk. (b) Write a provisional diagnosis of the immediate obstacle, one guiding policy with an explicit exclusion and three mutually reinforcing actions. Different diagnoses can be defended with supplied evidence and stated uncertainty; do not force acquisitions as the sole cause. (c) Choose a proximate objective WITHIN the three-month liquidity horizon, and specify what must be verified in the first two weeks. Illustrative objective, not a forecast: reduce monthly cash burn to at most Rs3Cr within eight weeks while protecting existing learner service and minimum test cohorts. Account for cash spent before this target: an eight-week goal alone is no financing plan. (d) Critique the supplied fictional statement using the four bad-strategy hallmarks; do not attribute it to a real leader. Check: estimated lifetime contribution after paid acquisition is-Rs2000 per new learner versus+Rs2000 before the CAC rise; simple unchanged-burn runway is3months, not a guaranteed survival date. No outside borrowing, fundraising or client commitment is authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"A D2C skincare brand's strategy deck reads: \"Become India's #1 beauty brand by FY27 through relentless innovation and customer obsession.\" What is the core defect under Rumelt's kernel?","o":["The statement fails because innovation and customer focus can never be combined within a coherent competitive strategy.","It is a goal masquerading as strategy — there is no diagnosis of the obstacle and no guiding policy","The only missing element is a budget; adding a spend total would establish the diagnosis and guiding policy.","The statement is already a full strategy because the intended market position and deadline specify how the company will compete."],"a":1},{"q":"In a fictional classroom case, which statement is a testable diagnostic hypothesis rather than a goal or proposed action?","o":["We will grow revenue40% this year and become the best-known brand in our target market by building an ambitious sales organization and investing in innovation.","We must become digital-first, exceed every customer expectation, and establish leadership across all major segments through rapid expansion and relentless commitment to the companys values.","Our CAC may have tripled because70% of sales depend on one ad auction and a funded rival is bidding up keywords; verify cohort and auction evidence before claiming this cause","We should expand into two additional cities and recruit a larger regional sales team this quarter, while upgrading the sales platform and launching a national advertising campaign."],"a":2},{"q":"A new CEO announces 47 \"strategic priorities\" for the year. Which bad-strategy hallmark is this?","o":["Bad strategic objectives — a failure to choose","Failure to face the challenge: no obstacle acknowledged","Blue-sky objectives: goals beyond the firms current reach","Fluff: unclear language hiding the absence of thought"],"a":0},{"q":"A fictional snack company, after diagnosing a channel-access problem, adopts a policy of serving one defined segment through specialist stores and declining mass-market expansion. This sentence is its:","o":["Individual action","Outcome target","Causal diagnosis","Guiding policy"],"a":3},{"q":"A company defines its premium policy as no entry SKU or marketplace discounting, then launches an entry SKU with40% marketplace discounts without revising that policy. The problem is:","o":["Entry products necessarily undermine every premium brand, regardless of whether a revised policy makes them coherent.","The actions are incoherent with the stated policy — the parts fight each other","The discount is a pricing detail and cannot conflict with a policy that expressly excludes it.","Premium positioning is inherently unworkable whenever lower-priced competitors enter the same category."],"a":1},{"q":"A fictional quick-commerce company declines electronics and fashion to focus scarce fulfillment capacity on the urgent grocery job identified in its diagnosis. This restraint is valuable because:","o":["The restraint proves category expansion is always worse than remaining within a company's original product range.","A narrow assortment is a strategy by itself even without a diagnosis or an explanation of customer advantage.","Strategy is as much about what not to do — the refusal concentrates scarce resources against the diagnosis","The refusal establishes that electronics and fashion are unattractive industries for any well-funded competitor."],"a":2},{"q":"A strategy statement says only Be agile, be digital, be customer-first. Which follow-up best tests whether it contains an actual choice?","o":["Ask whether the board approved the wording, since formal approval establishes that a meaningful choice was made.","Ask which obstacle, customer segment and trade-off these words select, and which plausible alternative is excluded","Check whether the statement includes a numerical target, since targets alone establish the chosen way to win.","Count the initiatives following the statement; more actions necessarily make the strategic choice clearer."],"a":1},{"q":"A fictional short-haul airline chooses a common aircraft family, no included meals and fast turnarounds against a diagnosed unit-cost problem. Its strategy is stronger when:","o":["It offers the lowest fare in every market, even when the cost base cannot support those fares.","Its choices reinforce one another against a diagnosed cost problem, forming a coherent system","Its aircraft order is larger than rivals' orders, irrespective of the resulting operating economics.","It copied visible practices from the market leader without checking whether they fit its own obstacle."],"a":1},{"q":"A startup has 3 months of runway. Which is the best *proximate objective* (Rumelt's term)?","o":["Reduce cash burn within eight weeks using verified cost changes and monitor weekly liquidity, within the current three-month horizon","Pursue category leadership through expansion now, assuming additional funding will become available.","Reach a margin target in two quarters without addressing the current three-month liquidity constraint.","Target an IPO in several years, without testing whether cash lasts through the next quarter."],"a":0},{"q":"Why must diagnosis precede guiding policy in the kernel?","o":["A quantified goal replaces diagnosis because numerical targets identify the mechanism causing the performance gap.","Diagnosis is only a presentation convention; any policy works if the team commits strongly enough to implementing it.","A favored policy should determine the diagnosis, so evidence can be organized to justify the decision already made.","A policy chosen before diagnosis treats symptoms; the crux stays untouched and resources scatter"],"a":3}]},"30":{"objectives":["Distinguish activities, outcome targets and an integrated competitive strategy in a fictional mobility case, then state what must be true and how to test it."],"videos":[{"id":"iuYlGRnC7J8","title":"A Plan Is Not a Strategy — Harvard Business Review","by":"Harvard Business Review","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"A Plan Is Not a Strategy - optional HBR overview","url":"https://hbr.org/2025/05/a-plan-is-not-a-strategy","note":"Summary/opening available; full masterclass access not assumed. The complete assigned conceptual reading is the publisher transcript below."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Difference Between a Plan and a Strategy — HBR podcast transcript","url":"https://hbr.org/podcast/2023/05/the-difference-between-a-plan-and-a-strategy","note":""},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Plan, target, strategy worksheet","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Use paper or your existing editor. Columns: controllable action, desired outcome, competitive theory, assumptions and evidence tests. No paid canvas required."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical, not Ola Electric or any real company: MetroVolt has a draft list: open40 service sites; hire120 technicians; launch two products; reach EBITDA breakeven; win urban delivery riders who lose earnings when scooters are unavailable, by prioritizing service uptime over feature variety in three dense cities, and delay leisure/sport models. (a) Sort each entry into controllable activity, desired outcome target, or candidate integrated strategy. Breakeven is a financial outcome, not an action the company can simply order and not by itself a competitive strategy. (b) Explain how the candidate strategy connects a chosen customer, job, advantage, capabilities and exclusion; separate plausible theory from demonstrated advantage. (c) State what must be true about rider downtime, willingness to pay, service capacity, cost and competitor response, with one test per assumption. (d) Propose two actions consistent with the theory and one tempting action it excludes. No supplied evidence proves the theory will work; identify what could falsify it. Optional extension: examine a real company using a named, dated primary document and restrict any absence finding to that document, not all its communications.","quiz":[{"q":"\"Our strategy for FY28: invest ₹500 crore in marketing, open 200 stores, hire 400 engineers.\" Roger Martin would call this:","o":["A complete business model, because stores, marketing and engineers supply all required revenue and cost assumptions.","A guiding policy, because assigning a spend total identifies the obstacle and excludes alternative customer segments.","An integrated competitive strategy, because committing substantial resources establishes how customers will choose the firm.","A plan — a list of controllable resource allocations with no theory of how the firm wins"],"a":3},{"q":"Why do organizations default to planning instead of strategy, per Martin?","o":["Planning guarantees returns once spending is approved, whereas strategy relies mainly on managers' intuition.","Planning controls costs and resources — things the organization controls — while strategy bets on customer behavior it cannot control, which feels scarier","Planning is always mandatory under company law, while competitive strategy is optional for most organizations.","Planning removes resource trade-offs, whereas strategy requires choosing every initiative before implementation begins."],"a":1},{"q":"Which of these is a strategy rather than a plan?","o":["We will upgrade the booking system and open ten properties according to the approved investment schedule, with delivery milestones for each operating team.","We will target time-sensitive mid-market hotel guests with reliable ten-minute check-in, prioritizing service reliability over luxury amenities and testing whether customers choose us for that difference","We will reduce logistics spending by12% through a procurement program across all operating sites, assigning a budget owner and monthly savings targets.","We will hire fifty additional sales representatives in the southern region during the next financial year, with a monthly review of recruiting progress."],"a":1},{"q":"A strategy that cannot specify what customers will do differently is best described as:","o":["A conservative strategy, since avoiding customer-choice assumptions reduces uncertainty without affecting the competitive outcome.","A diversified strategy, since serving several segments removes the need to explain why any customer would switch.","An agile strategy, since frequent activity updates substitute for a theory about the customer response.","A hope, not a strategy — strategy requires a theory of cause and effect about customer choice"],"a":3},{"q":"The \"playing to win\" test for a real strategy is that it:","o":["Maximizes every stakeholder outcome simultaneously without requiring exclusions or competing priorities.","Makes an integrated set of choices about where to play and how to win — choices competitors could dispute","Lists dates, owners and resource commitments even if the company cannot explain why customers will choose it.","Guarantees a profitable outcome before implementation by choosing only actions fully controlled by management."],"a":1},{"q":"A founder says \"our strategy is to be the default UPI app for kirana stores\" but can't say why kiranas would switch from PhonePe. The missing piece is:","o":["A larger implementation budget, because greater spending automatically creates a defensible advantage over the incumbent.","A more detailed dashboard, because measuring adoption replaces the need to explain the proposed customer advantage.","A stronger aspiration, because naming the desired market position establishes the mechanism that will make customers switch.","The \"how to win\" — the advantage that makes the theory plausible"],"a":3},{"q":"Which symptom most reliably signals that a leadership team has confused planning with strategy?","o":["Every initiative in the \"strategy\" deck is an internally controllable activity; no slide names a competitor, a trade-off, or a customer choice","The implementation timeline is detailed, even though its actions are linked to customer choice and an explicit trade-off.","The document assigns dates and owners to activities, even though it also states a clear competitive theory.","The finance team attends the strategy meeting and helps estimate the costs of supporting chosen capabilities."],"a":0},{"q":"Martin argues a good strategy makes you *uncomfortable* because:","o":["It must cost more than a plan because strategic work always requires more assets and people.","It avoids uncertainty by refusing to name assumptions about customers or competitors before results arrive.","It is normally incorrect, so managers should expect discomfort whenever a strategy is described in precise terms.","It is a bet — it rules out options and can't be proven right in advance"],"a":3},{"q":"A resource-constrained company proposes every segment and every product without a shared advantage or explicit priorities. The strategy problem is:","o":["The problem is the number of initiatives; exactly three choices can never form an integrated strategic system.","Strategy is choice under scarcity; refusing to choose means the market will choose for you","The company needs a more ambitious vision, even if no segment choice or shared advantage is identified.","The proposal would become a strategy by adding a delivery schedule without resolving competing resource priorities."],"a":1},{"q":"An airline's five-year \"strategy\" is entirely a capacity and capex schedule. The single most useful question to ask its authors:","o":["\"What must be true about passenger choice and competitor response for this schedule to produce returns?\"","Does the plan include enough aircraft types, regardless of whether they support the proposed cost or service advantage?","Can the schedule be accelerated to create more capacity, without checking customer willingness to choose the airline?","Which executive approved the aircraft order, regardless of passenger demand or the economics of the selected routes?"],"a":0}]},"31":{"objectives":["Analyze five forces in a supplied fictional food-delivery market, distinguishing evidence from assumptions and industry structure from firm position."],"videos":[{"id":"mYF2_FBCvXw","title":"The Five Competitive Forces That Shape Strategy - Harvard Business Review (Michael Porter)","by":"Harvard Business Review","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Five Forces - Harvard Institute for Strategy and Competitiveness","url":"https://www.isc.hbs.edu/strategy/business-strategy/Pages/the-five-forces.aspx","note":"Accessible primary explanation of the five forces, dynamic industry structure and firm position. The 1979 HBR article is not assumed fully accessible."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Porter's Five Forces Explained - Investopedia","url":"https://www.investopedia.com/terms/p/porter.asp","note":""},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Screener.in","url":"https://www.screener.in/","note":"Optional discovery tool for Indian listed companies. Use named, dated primary filings for any real-company financial claim, with consistent segment definitions; no company metrics are supplied by this homepage."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical, not observed Zomato/Swiggy facts. Define the focal industry as urban restaurant-order aggregation and delivery in fictional CityX for calendarYear1. Two incumbent platforms each handle40% of delivered restaurant orders and smaller platforms handle20%; shares alone do not establish take rates, margins or rivalry intensity. Diners can install/use multiple apps without a contractual penalty, compare displayed total prices, and are price-sensitive in a supplied small survey whose sample quality is unknown; churn is not supplied. Five restaurant chains account for35% of orders; remaining restaurants are fragmented. Some popular chains have loyal customers and their own ordering channels. A new open protocol can connect catalogs, payment and delivery participants, but entrants still need reliable supply, customer trust and viable delivery density. Other ways to obtain dinner include cooking at home and tiffin subscriptions. (a) Write one assessment for each force, separating supplied facts, implications and uncertainty; give an alternative interpretation and evidence that would change it. (b) Explain why low contractual switching costs and individually small buyers can pull buyer-power assessment in different directions; do not infer low churn. (c) Assess whether branded chains and undifferentiated kitchens necessarily have the same supplier power, including own-channel options. (d) Name comparable return-on-capital, take-rate/fee, order-density and customer-retention evidence needed to assess the profit pool. Do not manufacture unavailable metrics. Conclude with a provisional binding force and one positioning response. Optional real-market extension: replace fictional inputs with named, dated primary disclosures, keep geography/period/industry boundaries consistent, and distinguish reported facts from your interpretation.","quiz":[{"q":"In the fictional platform case, diners are individually small but can compare prices and switch apps easily. Which conclusion is best supported?","o":["Fragmentation limits bargaining, but easy switching and price comparison can increase pressure.","Small individual orders prove buyers cannot exert meaningful price pressure.","Easy app installation proves strong buyer power in every segment.","Habit outweighs price sensitivity, so low churn can be assumed."],"a":0},{"q":"In the fictional open-protocol case, which is the best entry-barrier assessment?","o":["Existing incumbents make protocol entrants irrelevant until they attain equal market share.","Interoperability may lower setup barriers; density, trust and supply still need testing.","Shared catalogs remove entry barriers even without reliable delivery and customer trust.","A protocol raises every entry barrier because more participants must coordinate."],"a":1},{"q":"In the defined platform model, treat restaurants as suppliers of differentiated meal access to diners. A must-have chain negotiating better platform commission terms illustrates stronger:","o":["Rivalry between platforms offering the same delivery service.","Substitution by other ways of obtaining dinner.","Supplier power of differentiated meal providers over the platform.","Buyer power of individual diners over the platform."],"a":2},{"q":"Which is the strongest outcome evidence here to investigate structural attractiveness, while still checking firm position, capital measurement and the cycle?","o":["High order growth during a period of strong demand.","A recent increase in the leading platform's commission rate.","Sustained excess capital returns across incumbents, with measurement and cycle checks.","Several years of increasing revenue per active customer."],"a":2},{"q":"Porter's framework says industry structure matters because:","o":["Industry structure determines identical returns for firms serving the same buyers.","A firms own activities determine profitability independently of industry structure.","Large market shares remove the need to assess suppliers and substitutes.","Forces affect value sharing; both industry structure and relative position shape returns."],"a":3},{"q":"Which evidence would most support a hypothesis that a profitable niche is protected against entry?","o":["Evidence that current incumbents have announced ambitious long-term growth targets.","Evidence that its first entrant launched earlier than other firms considered entering.","Evidence that a barrier makes likely entrants' economics unattractive at viable scale.","Evidence that the niche currently earns a higher gross margin than nearby markets."],"a":2},{"q":"For the defined restaurant aggregation/delivery industry, which is the clearest substitute rather than another delivery-platform rival?","o":["A tiffin subscription meeting dinner needs in a different way.","A smaller platform using a shared catalog and delivery network.","A second app aggregating and delivering restaurant orders.","A restaurant-order platform competing primarily through lower delivery fees."],"a":0},{"q":"What can be concluded about cloud-kitchen supplier power without data on concentration, brand preference or alternative channels?","o":["Being a cloud kitchen necessarily makes a restaurant interchangeable to diners.","Power depends on concentration, differentiation and alternatives, not the kitchen label alone.","An own-ordering channel always gives kitchens more power than the largest platform.","Kitchen fragmentation eliminates leverage even when some brands attract loyal demand."],"a":1},{"q":"Which error can make a Five Forces assessment hard to interpret?","o":["Checking whether restaurants are suppliers or customers at the chosen value-chain level.","Mixing incompatible geographies, needs or value-chain levels without explaining the boundary.","Using different force ratings for two separately defined geographic markets.","Considering how customer switching changes as a market evolves over time."],"a":1},{"q":"In a hypothetical comparison, Firm A earns30% ROIC and its industry5%. What should an analyst investigate next?","o":["Check comparable returns, period and capital, then investigate positioning and activities.","Treat the higher return as proof that industry forces do not apply.","Infer lower supplier power from the return gap without checking cost or capital data.","Assume both firms returns must converge before investigating their business models."],"a":0}]},"32":{"objectives":["Classify fictional competitive-advantage profiles with the five-source moat taxonomy, distinguish a hypothesis from demonstrated durability, and name evidence that could challenge each."],"videos":[{"id":"YFS5JBgz1Xc","title":"The Little Book That Builds Wealth - Pat Dorsey, Talks at Google","by":"Talks at Google","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Dynamics of Network Effects - Andreessen Horowitz","url":"https://a16z.com/the-dynamics-of-network-effects/","note":""},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Five Sources of Moat - Morningstar","url":"https://www.morningstar.in/posts/10000/","note":"Primary Paul Larson interview: network effects, switching costs, intangible assets, efficient scale and cost advantage; assess duration of excess returns, not just a good year."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Morningstar Economic Moat Rating","url":"https://www.morningstar.com/stocks/morningstar-economic-moat-rating-3","note":"Public explanation of the five-source taxonomy and expected duration. A moat is not automatically a recommendation to buy at any price."}],"exercise":"Classroom profiles are invented, not current claims about Zerodha, DMart, Dream11, IRCTC or boAt. Use the Morningstar five-source taxonomy; do not assume a famous brand or high market share proves a moat. A: a broker processes similar customer transactions at a measured lower operating cost per transaction than matched peers; customer onboarding and revenue mix differ and need checking. B: a marketplace says more sellers improve buyer choice and more buyers attract sellers, but users can list on several platforms and no liquidity series is supplied. C: a business has a government license that explicitly blocks rivals in the stated classroom scenario; renewal and rule-change risks remain. D: a local utility serves a limited-demand district where a modeled second network would not earn its cost of capital; the estimate depends on demand and construction-cost assumptions. E: an audio brand is widely recognized, but no evidence of willingness to pay, repeat choice, durable cost advantage or returns is supplied. (a) For each, identify the most plausible moat source or insufficient-evidence status, plus a competing explanation. (b) Specify at least two measures, consistent denominators, time horizon and evidence that would challenge durability; include return on invested capital versus cost of capital where meaningful. (c) Propose a plausible rival attack and what observation would show it succeeding. Key distinctions: C suggests intangible-assets/license protection, not efficient scale merely because it is exclusive; D suggests efficient scale only if limited-market economics survive testing. A is a cost-advantage hypothesis, B a network-effect hypothesis, E unresolved. Market share, SSSG, searches or gross margin alone cannot prove a widening moat. This is analytical practice, not investment advice or permission to trade.","quiz":[{"q":"A fictional broker has a verified lower like-for-like cost per transaction than matched rivals, after adjusting for service quality and business mix. This most directly supports a hypothesis of:","o":["Cost advantage is supported; persistence and alternative explanations still need testing.","Efficient scale is established because the broker processes many customer transactions.","Switching costs are established because the broker charges less for each transaction.","Network effects are established because lower costs attract additional customer accounts."],"a":0},{"q":"A fictional marketplace gains buyer value from additional relevant sellers, which attracts buyers and then more sellers. This mechanism is:","o":["Scale economies: additional output lowers operating cost without changing user value.","Switching costs: users become unable to move transactions to another marketplace.","Cross-side network effects: relevant supply attracts demand, which attracts relevant supply.","Intangible assets: registered users provide exclusive rights preventing rival entry."],"a":2},{"q":"A company has a famous brand but keeps losing share and pricing power. What does the assigned moat framework imply?","o":["Test durable choice, pricing and excess returns; awareness alone is insufficient.","Brand awareness alone establishes durability even when pricing power is falling.","Increasing search volume substitutes for evidence about customer choice and profitability.","Recent share losses rule out any brand advantage in every customer segment."],"a":0},{"q":"In a fictional market, a government license explicitly prevents rival entry. The most direct moat source under the assigned taxonomy is:","o":["Efficient scale, because any exclusive legal right proves a limited market.","Network effects, because exclusivity increases the number of platform participants.","Switching costs, because a license prevents customers changing their preferences.","Intangible assets through license protection; efficient scale needs a separate economic case."],"a":3},{"q":"A fictional fintech's users say they'd switch for a 0.5% better rate, and do. The implication:","o":["The observation proves that new workflow features would restore durable customer lock-in.","The behavior proves the company has no other source of competitive advantage.","This challenges switching costs for that segment; investigate other frictions and segments.","A better rate establishes that customers' switching costs are negative in every segment."],"a":2},{"q":"Which is the strongest evidence among these for investigating a claimed durable moat, subject to measurement and causal checks?","o":["Revenue grows faster than peers during a period of heavy customer subsidies.","Management repeatedly describes its brand and technology as uniquely differentiated.","The company leads market share in a rapidly expanding but loss-making segment.","Excess capital returns persist through competition or a cycle, with measurement checks."],"a":3},{"q":"Network effects differ from scale economies in that:","o":["Network effects raise user value through participants; scale economies lower unit cost with output.","Both mechanisms require contractual lock-in before they can benefit a company.","Network effects always strengthen with size; scale economies weaken once volume increases.","Scale economies increase customer value directly; network effects only reduce unit cost."],"a":0},{"q":"A fictional retailer copies a rival's store layout and headline prices but loses money. Which is the best next analytical step?","o":["Compare costs, capital and the activity system; losses alone do not identify one cause.","Infer that the rival must hold enforceable intellectual property over the copied layout.","Attribute all losses to customer loyalty before comparing service mix and invested capital.","Treat matching prices as proof that the two retailers' cost structures are identical."],"a":0},{"q":"Which outcome pair most directly tracks experienced matching value in a matrimony platform, alongside retention, quality and return measures?","o":["Revenue growth and headline valuation, without checking price changes, subsidies or capital.","Registered accounts and app downloads, without checking active-user experience or match quality.","Match rate and response time, checked alongside quality, retention and economic returns.","Total marketing expenditure and social mentions, without checking acquisition source or outcomes."],"a":2},{"q":"A fictional audio brand uses contract manufacturers and common marketplaces. What follows about its moat?","o":["Any funded competitor can copy every capability immediately because distribution is shared.","Common manufacturers rule out brand-based advantages regardless of customer behavior.","Test durable choice, cost and returns; common suppliers alone do not settle moat strength.","Using the same marketplaces proves customers see no meaningful product differentiation."],"a":2}]},"33":{"objectives":["Identify provisional jobs in rapid-delivery customer stories, then test them through consented timeline inquiry or clearly labeled simulation without inventing evidence."],"videos":[{"id":"6zfAurEA21Y","title":"Why Hire a Milkshake? - Clayton M. Christensen","by":"Moral of the Story","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Jobs to Be Done Theory - Christensen Institute","url":"https://www.christenseninstitute.org/theory/jobs-to-be-done/","note":"Accessible theory, functional/social/emotional circumstances and examples. Read the milkshake section and compare competing hires."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Clay Christensen's Milkshake Marketing - HBS Working Knowledge","url":"https://www.library.hbs.edu/working-knowledge/clay-christensens-milkshake-marketing","note":"Full institutional account of observing and interviewing customers, differing morning/afternoon jobs and circumstance-based segmentation."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Timeline worksheet","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Use paper or your existing editor: first thought, trigger, alternatives, search, purchase, use, anxiety/habit, outcome and uncertainty. No book download assumed."}],"exercise":"Study rapid grocery delivery as a customer job, not a list of demographic labels or assumed current app features. Start with three explicitly fictional prompts: I forgot an ingredient while cooking; I wanted a snack but did not want to leave home; I needed an accessory late at night. Do not assume any specific app actually stocks or delivers an accessory at that time. (a) Form a provisional progress-in-circumstance job for each, including functional, emotional or social elements where supported; name competing hires, including a local shop, borrowing, waiting or doing without. (b) Draft a neutral timeline guide: last concrete occasion, first thought, trigger, alternatives considered, search/purchase/use and outcome; ask what nearly stopped the choice. (c) If two adults voluntarily agree, a learner may interview them about their own recent rapid-delivery choice after explaining purpose, intended audience and note use; ask permission before recording, collect no payment credentials or unnecessary personal data, and do not promise anonymity you cannot provide. No assistant outreach is authorized. If consented participants are unavailable, use two explicitly labeled synthetic role-plays or analyze your own recalled purchase with its recall limits. Never report simulated participants as real users. (d) Revise jobs and alternatives using attributed observations, separate reports from inference, and name one follow-up behavior test. Two interviews can reveal useful explanations and gaps, not estimate population prevalence or establish causation. No send, recording or public disclosure is required.","quiz":[{"q":"The milkshake study's key move was analyzing purchases by:","o":["Average purchase frequency across all customers before examining different usage occasions.","Investigate progress sought in the time, place and circumstances of the purchase.","Group buyers by age and income, then design one product for the largest group.","Ask shoppers to rank desired features before observing their actual purchase context."],"a":1},{"q":"A customer wants a hole in a wall, rather than a drill for its own sake. Which analytical idea does this illustrate?","o":["Jobs-to-be-done: the desired progress, rather than the tool itself, drives demand.","Product positioning: tool specifications define the complete competitive set.","Feature prioritization: the largest number of functions creates the strongest demand.","Cost leadership: demand is primarily determined by the cheapest tool available."],"a":0},{"q":"A commuter hires a milkshake for a long drive because it stays thick and keeps hands clean; a parent hires it in the afternoon as a treat. The strategy implication:","o":["Combine both groups' preferences into a single average design before testing alternatives.","Raise the price because different usage occasions establish stronger willingness to pay.","Test distinct experiences where the jobs conflict, rather than assuming one average fits.","Prioritize the larger group because a single product cannot serve different jobs."],"a":2},{"q":"A fictional rapid-delivery customer wants a small urgent item tonight. How should the competitive set be identified?","o":["Restrict alternatives to other delivery apps because the customer wants an urgent item.","Investigate job-specific alternatives, including a local shop, borrowing, waiting or doing without.","Compare only stores carrying the same branded product, ignoring the option of waiting.","Treat every grocery store as an equal substitute regardless of timing or circumstance."],"a":1},{"q":"Which question belongs in a JTBD interview?","o":["What happened on the last occasion, which alternatives did you try, and what nearly stopped you?","Which new features would make a product like ours more appealing next year?","Which demographic group best describes people who usually buy this kind of product?","Would you agree that convenience was the main reason for choosing the service?"],"a":0},{"q":"A user abandons a budgeting app after one use but keeps a spreadsheet. What is the best JTBD next step?","o":["Conclude that interface quality caused abandonment before examining the purchase timeline.","Reconstruct the switch and test progress, workflow and habit; do not assume the cause.","Add the spreadsheet features to the app before investigating the circumstance of use.","Assume the spreadsheet is retained solely because the user avoids paying subscriptions."],"a":1},{"q":"How should push, pull, anxiety and habit be used in JTBD inquiry?","o":["Use the forces as measured quantities whose sum predicts every customer adoption decision.","Infer that a better-featured solution will overcome habit once its price is reduced.","Use the forces as qualitative inquiry prompts, not a numerical law or failure-frequency claim.","Rank anxiety as the most common barrier before collecting stories from relevant users."],"a":2},{"q":"\"Our customers are Indian women aged 25–34 in metros\" fails the JTBD test mainly because:","o":["Demographics alone miss circumstantial progress; interview stories need further testing, not causal certainty.","A narrower age band would establish why individual customers choose the service.","Shared demographics establish the same job even when purchase circumstances differ.","Age-based segments are never useful for any commercial or product-analysis decision."],"a":0},{"q":"A fictional investing app considers bundling several services. In JTBD terms, the idea is stronger when:","o":["Treat higher feature counts as sufficient evidence that customers want an integrated bundle.","Add every service requested by one customer because observed requests establish market demand.","Bundle services whenever they sit in the same product category, regardless of circumstance.","Test whether related progress and compatible circumstances outweigh the friction the bundle adds."],"a":3},{"q":"Which is the clearest starting evidence for a job hypothesis rather than only a feature request?","o":["Users describe struggling progress in context and existing efforts or workarounds to achieve it.","Respondents rank an idea highly before describing any related struggle or purchase behavior.","Users name an attractive feature, without a concrete occasion or an alternative they tried.","A competitor launches a feature that appears frequently in social-media discussions."],"a":0}]},"34":{"objectives":["Build two nine-block classroom business-model canvases, separating supplied evidence, hypotheses and unknowns; connect customer value, delivery, revenue and costs."],"videos":[{"id":"QoAOzMTLP5s","title":"Business Model Canvas Explained — Strategyzer","by":"Strategyzer","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Business Model Canvas — Strategyzer library","url":"https://www.strategyzer.com/library/the-business-model-canvas","note":""},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Value Proposition Canvas — Strategyzer","url":"https://www.strategyzer.com/canvas/value-proposition-canvas","note":""},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Business Model Canvas — official template","url":"https://www.strategyzer.com/canvas/business-model-canvas","note":"Official page says its template is free; paid playbooks are separate. Use paper with the nine headings below if a download asks for signup."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical, not current Razorpay or CRED facts. Make one canvas per model. MerchantFlow serves small online merchants, offers payment acceptance and reconciliation, reaches merchants through its website and reseller referrals, and provides self-service onboarding plus support. Merchants pay a monthly software subscription; processing fees are only a proposed revenue stream. Activities include software operations and support; resources include software and trained staff; external processors are partners, not owned assets. Costs include staff, hosting, acquisition and processor charges, with amounts unknown. MemberCircle serves adults seeking bill reminders, offers reminders plus optional shopping benefits, reaches members through referrals, and provides app-based service. It has software and a membership database with consent/use limits; merchant partners supply offers. Staff and rewards are costs. No revenue is verified: partner referral fees are hypotheses. (a) Fill customer segments, value propositions, channels, customer relationships, revenue streams, key resources, key activities, key partnerships and cost structure. Mark unknowns instead of inventing them. Distinguish the people served from a database used as a resource. (b) Select and justify the highest-risk assumption for each model, without a preassigned answer. (c) Map one segment in a Value Proposition Canvas: jobs/pains/gains versus products and services/pain relievers/gain creators; name evidence needed for fit. (d) Fictional shock: processor charges rise20% and a merchant ends reward funding. Trace affected blocks and feasible alternatives. This is not current payment law or a forecast of company failure. Optional real-company extension: use named, dated primary disclosures; do not infer valuation assumptions or actual revenue without evidence.","quiz":[{"q":"A fictional canvas describes high-credit-score adults as the people it chooses to serve. Which block?","o":["Key resources: software and the controlled database.","Revenue streams: payments received by the model.","Channels: routes used to reach those people.","Customer segments: the people the model serves."],"a":3},{"q":"A fictional service depends on external processors. Which partner-risk assessment is sound?","o":["Assume replacement is costless because the company does not own the partner infrastructure.","Treat a signed service agreement as eliminating all future operational and continuity risk.","Check critical dependencies, contract terms and alternatives; dependence alone does not prove failure.","Classify every external processor as an owned resource because its service supports the product."],"a":2},{"q":"The Value Proposition Canvas pairs jobs, pains and gains with:","o":["Key partners, internal costs and market size, with fit determined only by product specifications.","Customer segments, channels and revenue totals, with fit proven by a completed diagram.","Products and services, pain relievers and gain creators, with customer evidence needed for fit.","Competitor features, brand slogans and launch dates, with fit established through executive approval."],"a":2},{"q":"Four possible revenue streams are listed, without evidence of paid transactions. What follows?","o":["They establish product-market fit because several proposed streams reduce dependence on one customer.","They are hypotheses to test; the list alone proves neither monetization success nor failure.","They prove no viable model exists, even if later evidence validates one proposed revenue stream.","They become actual revenue streams once management includes them in a dated business-model canvas."],"a":1},{"q":"A fictional retailer owns rather than leases premises. How should this enter its canvas?","o":["Record no occupancy costs because buying premises eliminates rent from all later operating periods.","Infer unbeatable prices because owned premises necessarily cost less than comparable leased premises.","Treat ownership only as a customer-segment choice, without effects on resources or cost structure.","Include capital, maintenance, financing and opportunity costs; ownership does not guarantee lower total cost."],"a":3},{"q":"Which evidence most directly supports value-proposition/customer-segment fit?","o":["The company lists the same features as a successful competitor serving a different customer segment.","Management agrees the offer has more features, without checking use, repeat choice or willingness to pay.","Relevant customers choose and keep using the offer for the job, with payment economics tested.","The total market is large, even though intended customers have not tested or chosen the offer."],"a":2},{"q":"In a fictional contract shock, a platform loses a previously received payment. The first canvas implication is:","o":["Move the lost payment to key resources so the model retains its originally planned income total.","Remove the customer segment automatically because a revenue change eliminates the need it was serving.","Keep the original income because the canvas records business intentions rather than operating evidence.","Revise revenue and trace delivery, cost and alternative responses without assuming the customer job disappears."],"a":3},{"q":"Why is filling an unknown block with an unsupported aspiration a problem?","o":["It is acceptable once the aspiration has an owner and a delivery date in the plan.","It hides a hypothesis as fact; label uncertainty and define evidence needed to test it.","It proves the entire canvas must remain blank until every financial figure has been independently audited.","It matters only for revenue because other business-model blocks do not require supporting evidence."],"a":1},{"q":"CAC is Rs800 and first-order contribution Rs300. Can five-month cash payback be established?","o":["No; repeat timing, contribution, retention and relevant cash costs are still needed.","Yes; positive first-order contribution proves all acquired customers repay their CAC within five months.","No customer can pay back because first-order contribution is lower than acquisition cost.","Yes; first-order contribution divided by CAC gives the number of months to pay back."],"a":0},{"q":"What is the strongest method for comparing two classroom canvases?","o":["Choose the canvas with more proposed revenue streams even if neither has validated payments.","Rank feature counts first, assuming the larger feature set establishes stronger customer fit.","Compare names and ownership structures without examining how each model serves its chosen segment.","Trace evidenced segment/value choices through delivery, revenue and costs, clearly labeling unknowns."],"a":3}]},"35":{"objectives":["Reassess fictional CityX after adjacent entry, calculate a narrowly scoped fee contribution gap, and connect industry forces, customer jobs and business-model choices."],"videos":[{"id":"Dfp23xSqpdk","title":"Porter's 5 Forces EXPLAINED | B2U","by":"Business To You","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Five Forces - Harvard Institute","url":"https://www.isc.hbs.edu/strategy/business-strategy/Pages/the-five-forces.aspx","note":"Accessible full primary framework. HBR 2008 is not assumed fully available."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Porter's five forces guide (UCF Libraries)","url":"https://guides.ucf.edu/man4720/porters-five-forces","note":"Public guide includes a Porter excerpt and research-source guidance. Institutional databases may require login; this is not the full HBR article."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Screener.in","url":"https://www.screener.in/","note":"Free tool - pull one listed Indian company's ratios while you test the forces."}],"exercise":"Classroom update to fictional CityX, not an observed company event. An adjacent operator starts restaurant delivery using some existing dispatch technology and capacity, and is reported to gain share; no validated time series is supplied. Fee received Rs25/order, rider payout Rs60/order,50000orders/day for30days, food order value Rs400. Exclude commission, advertising, tax, refunds, support, payment costs, fixed costs, customer acquisition and working capital. (a) Calculate fee-minus-rider contribution per order and month, not total business loss. (b) Find additional revenue as a percent of order value needed to offset that narrow gap if it has no additional cost; compare with a scenario where earning each additional rupee costs20paise. (c) Reassess prior entry barriers AND current rivalry. Shared assets may reduce setup investment, but spare capacity, upgrades, opportunity cost and additional investment remain unknown; do not assume zero. (d) Name a measurable operating series plus cost/retention evidence that could challenge your provisional industry assessment. No single metric proves structure caused an outcome. (e) Trace one revenue and one cost response through the business-model canvas and the customer job. Check: -Rs35/order and-Rs5.25Cr/month. Gap-closing revenue with no added cost Rs35/order=8.75% of order value; at20% incremental revenue cost,35/0.8=Rs43.75/order=10.9375%. Neither establishes full-company breakeven or attainable commercial terms.","quiz":[{"q":"In the supplied review model, Rs25 fee minus Rs60 rider payout equals:","o":["-Rs35/order","Rs35/order","-Rs85/order","Rs60/order"],"a":0},{"q":"At50000orders/day for30days, the fee-minus-rider monthly amount is:","o":["-Rs5.25Cr","-Rs52.5Cr","Rs5.25Cr","-Rs0.525Cr"],"a":0},{"q":"With no added cost, which order-value revenue offsets Rs35 on a Rs400order?","o":["6.25%","10.94%","15.00%","8.75%"],"a":3},{"q":"At20% cost of additional revenue, which revenue offsets a Rs35gap?","o":["Rs35.00/order","Rs42.00/order","Rs28.00/order","Rs43.75/order"],"a":3},{"q":"What does closing this fee-minus-rider gap establish?","o":["Only that narrow gap closes under its exclusions, not full-company breakeven.","The whole company is profitable because other costs cannot change this calculation.","The required commission is attainable because its algebraic breakeven rate exists.","Fixed and acquisition costs are covered automatically once rider payouts are met."],"a":0},{"q":"An adjacent operator enters using some existing network assets. Which conclusion is sound?","o":["Only substitute pressure changes because a new restaurant-delivery platform serves a different need.","Reassess prior entry barriers and current rivalry; shared capacity is not necessarily free.","Entry investment becomes zero because dispatch technology and some assets already exist.","Restaurant supplier power disappears because an additional platform now competes for orders."],"a":1},{"q":"A D2C skincare brand's strategy deck reads: \"Become India's #1 beauty brand by FY27 through relentless innovation and customer obsession.\" What is the core defect under Rumelt's kernel?","o":["It is a goal masquerading as strategy - there is no diagnosis of the obstacle and no guiding policy","The statement fails because innovation and customer focus can never be combined within a coherent competitive strategy.","The statement is already a full strategy because the intended market position and deadline specify how the company will compete.","The only missing element is a budget; adding a spend total would establish the diagnosis and guiding policy."],"a":0},{"q":"A new CEO announces 47 \"strategic priorities\" for the year. Which bad-strategy hallmark is this?","o":["Blue-sky objectives: goals beyond the firms current reach","Failure to face the challenge: no obstacle acknowledged","Bad strategic objectives - a failure to choose","Fluff: unclear language hiding the absence of thought"],"a":2},{"q":"A fictional snack company, after diagnosing a channel-access problem, adopts a policy of serving one defined segment through specialist stores and declining mass-market expansion. This sentence is its:","o":["Guiding policy","Causal diagnosis","Outcome target","Individual action"],"a":0},{"q":"A startup has 3 months of runway. Which is the best *proximate objective* (Rumelt's term)?","o":["Reach a margin target in two quarters without addressing the current three-month liquidity constraint.","Pursue category leadership through expansion now, assuming additional funding will become available.","Reduce cash burn within eight weeks using verified cost changes and monitor weekly liquidity, within the current three-month horizon","Target an IPO in several years, without testing whether cash lasts through the next quarter."],"a":2},{"q":"\"Our strategy for FY28: invest ₹500 crore in marketing, open 200 stores, hire 400 engineers.\" Roger Martin would call this:","o":["A complete business model, because stores, marketing and engineers supply all required revenue and cost assumptions.","An integrated competitive strategy, because committing substantial resources establishes how customers will choose the firm.","A plan - a list of controllable resource allocations with no theory of how the firm wins","A guiding policy, because assigning a spend total identifies the obstacle and excludes alternative customer segments."],"a":2},{"q":"A strategy that cannot specify what customers will do differently is best described as:","o":["An agile strategy, since frequent activity updates substitute for a theory about the customer response.","A conservative strategy, since avoiding customer-choice assumptions reduces uncertainty without affecting the competitive outcome.","A diversified strategy, since serving several segments removes the need to explain why any customer would switch.","A hope, not a strategy - strategy requires a theory of cause and effect about customer choice"],"a":3},{"q":"Martin argues a good strategy makes you *uncomfortable* because:","o":["It is normally incorrect, so managers should expect discomfort whenever a strategy is described in precise terms.","It avoids uncertainty by refusing to name assumptions about customers or competitors before results arrive.","It is a bet - it rules out options and can't be proven right in advance","It must cost more than a plan because strategic work always requires more assets and people."],"a":2},{"q":"In the fictional platform case, diners are individually small but can compare prices and switch apps easily. Which conclusion is best supported?","o":["Habit outweighs price sensitivity, so low churn can be assumed.","Easy app installation proves strong buyer power in every segment.","Fragmentation limits bargaining, but easy switching and price comparison can increase pressure.","Small individual orders prove buyers cannot exert meaningful price pressure."],"a":2},{"q":"In the fictional open-protocol case, which is the best entry-barrier assessment?","o":["Existing incumbents make protocol entrants irrelevant until they attain equal market share.","Interoperability may lower setup barriers; density, trust and supply still need testing.","A protocol raises every entry barrier because more participants must coordinate.","Shared catalogs remove entry barriers even without reliable delivery and customer trust."],"a":1},{"q":"In the defined platform model, treat restaurants as suppliers of differentiated meal access to diners. A must-have chain negotiating better platform commission terms illustrates stronger:","o":["Rivalry between platforms offering the same delivery service.","Supplier power of differentiated meal providers over the platform.","Substitution by other ways of obtaining dinner.","Buyer power of individual diners over the platform."],"a":1},{"q":"For the defined restaurant aggregation/delivery industry, which is the clearest substitute rather than another delivery-platform rival?","o":["A tiffin subscription meeting dinner needs in a different way.","A second app aggregating and delivering restaurant orders.","A restaurant-order platform competing primarily through lower delivery fees.","A smaller platform using a shared catalog and delivery network."],"a":0},{"q":"In a fictional market, a government license explicitly prevents rival entry. The most direct moat source under the assigned taxonomy is:","o":["Network effects, because exclusivity increases the number of platform participants.","Switching costs, because a license prevents customers changing their preferences.","Efficient scale, because any exclusive legal right proves a limited market.","Intangible assets through license protection; efficient scale needs a separate economic case."],"a":3},{"q":"Network effects differ from scale economies in that:","o":["Network effects raise user value through participants; scale economies lower unit cost with output.","Network effects always strengthen with size; scale economies weaken once volume increases.","Scale economies increase customer value directly; network effects only reduce unit cost.","Both mechanisms require contractual lock-in before they can benefit a company."],"a":0},{"q":"A fictional audio brand uses contract manufacturers and common marketplaces. What follows about its moat?","o":["Using the same marketplaces proves customers see no meaningful product differentiation.","Common manufacturers rule out brand-based advantages regardless of customer behavior.","Test durable choice, cost and returns; common suppliers alone do not settle moat strength.","Any funded competitor can copy every capability immediately because distribution is shared."],"a":2},{"q":"Which question belongs in a JTBD interview?","o":["Which new features would make a product like ours more appealing next year?","Which demographic group best describes people who usually buy this kind of product?","Would you agree that convenience was the main reason for choosing the service?","What happened on the last occasion, which alternatives did you try, and what nearly stopped you?"],"a":3},{"q":"How should push, pull, anxiety and habit be used in JTBD inquiry?","o":["Rank anxiety as the most common barrier before collecting stories from relevant users.","Infer that a better-featured solution will overcome habit once its price is reduced.","Use the forces as qualitative inquiry prompts, not a numerical law or failure-frequency claim.","Use the forces as measured quantities whose sum predicts every customer adoption decision."],"a":2},{"q":"A fictional investing app considers bundling several services. In JTBD terms, the idea is stronger when:","o":["Test whether related progress and compatible circumstances outweigh the friction the bundle adds.","Add every service requested by one customer because observed requests establish market demand.","Treat higher feature counts as sufficient evidence that customers want an integrated bundle.","Bundle services whenever they sit in the same product category, regardless of circumstance."],"a":0},{"q":"A fictional canvas describes high-credit-score adults as the people it chooses to serve. Which block?","o":["Key resources: software and the controlled database.","Revenue streams: payments received by the model.","Customer segments: the people the model serves.","Channels: routes used to reach those people."],"a":2},{"q":"CAC is Rs800 and first-order contribution Rs300. Can five-month cash payback be established?","o":["Yes; first-order contribution divided by CAC gives the number of months to pay back.","No customer can pay back because first-order contribution is lower than acquisition cost.","Yes; positive first-order contribution proves all acquired customers repay their CAC within five months.","No; repeat timing, contribution, retention and relevant cash costs are still needed."],"a":3}]},"36":{"objectives":["Compare feasible market, safeguarded partnership and internal governance using specificity, adaptation, frequency and internal costs; distinguish vertical stages from diversification."],"videos":[{"id":"B-5IV2jnkrU","title":"What is Transaction Cost Economics (TCE)? — From A Business Professor","by":"Business School 101","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Economic governance - Nobel2009 academy release","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/2009/press-release/","note":"Read the Williamson section: governance alternatives, relationship-specific assets and limits of authority."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Managing transactions - Nobel speedread","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/2009/speedread/","note":"Public explanation of firm boundaries and interdependence, not automatic acquisition advice."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Governance comparison worksheet","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Use paper or an existing editor: focal transaction, redeployability, frequency, adaptation, safeguards, internal costs and evidence gaps."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical, not Zomato, Tata or Reliance facts. Compare spot market, safeguarded long-term partnership and ownership, not instructions to transact. A: a food manufacturer needs packaging tooling with low value outside its product, frequent orders and unpredictable design changes; performance is partly measurable but adaptation is hard to specify. B: it buys standard cartons from ten credible suppliers with comparable specifications, low switching cost and no dedicated tooling. C: it considers buying a telecom business that neither supplies nor distributes its food. D: it considers owning retail outlets selling its food. (a) Identify supported specificity/hold-up risks; frequency alone does not prove ownership is best. (b) Compare feasible safeguards, measures and internal capabilities/costs for A and B. No total governance costs are supplied, so ownership cannot be chosen conclusively from these facts alone. (c) Under this boundary C is diversifying expansion, not a vertical stage simply because of common ownership; D is forward-stage ownership. (d) Name evidence that could favor partnership even for A, and a review trigger. Include investment, management complexity, incentives, capacity imbalance and failure risk. Exit is a separate alternative with opportunity costs and commitments to assess, not just another label for integration.","quiz":[{"q":"What is the core firm-boundary comparison in the assigned material?","o":["The largest company size achievable regardless of coordination and production costs.","The revenue target that requires the greatest external investment over five years.","Whether all important suppliers can be acquired at their prevailing market valuations.","Costs and benefits of feasible internal, market and other governance arrangements."],"a":3},{"q":"Why can relationship-specific tooling create a governance problem?","o":["Low outside value can expose a party to renegotiation or hold-up after investment.","Specialized tooling guarantees internal ownership has no management or financing cost.","Low resale value proves the underlying transaction should never be undertaken at all.","Specialized assets necessarily have a lower purchase price than comparable generic alternatives."],"a":0},{"q":"CaseA has specific tooling and difficult adaptation. What follows?","o":["Use spot purchases automatically because frequent trade guarantees strong supplier competition.","Ignore adaptation terms because tooling ownership guarantees all future performance measures.","Compare ownership with feasible contractual safeguards and internal costs before deciding.","Acquire the supplier automatically because asset specificity settles the governance choice."],"a":2},{"q":"A company calls an outsourced algorithm core. What else is needed to assess specificity?","o":["Only vendor size, because large suppliers cannot act opportunistically.","Redeployability, IP/control rights, adaptation, safeguards and credible alternatives.","Only the core label, because importance proves relationship-specific investment.","Only code length, because larger programs necessarily require internal governance."],"a":1},{"q":"Buying an unrelated telecom operation in the supplied food-company case is:","o":["Forward integration because telecom users might also become buyers of food.","Diversification, not automatically a vertical stage of the food supply chain.","Backward integration because digital services are inputs to every possible manufacturer.","Taper integration because common ownership now spans more than one industry."],"a":1},{"q":"Producing some input internally while buying some externally can:","o":["Remove performance specifications because internal and external outputs are automatically equivalent.","Eliminate supplier risk because outside firms must match internal production at no cost.","Retain knowledge and outside benchmarks, with duplication and coordination costs to assess.","Guarantee lowest total costs regardless of scale, capacity balance or managerial capability."],"a":2},{"q":"A multi-category bundle is offered as the reason for common ownership. What should be tested?","o":["Whether higher parent valuation proves each acquisition creates a durable operating advantage.","Value beyond feasible contracts or partnerships, net of coordination and capital costs.","Whether every brand can share an app, treating interface integration as sufficient proof.","Whether cross-subsidies are possible, without considering customer benefit or opportunity cost."],"a":1},{"q":"Which can make internal governance less attractive than market supply?","o":["Market alternatives no longer changing once the firm internalizes production.","Stronger competitive price discipline automatically imposed on internal than external units.","Specific investments becoming freely reversible without integration or capacity costs.","Management costs, weaker incentives or capability gaps exceeding coordination benefits."],"a":3},{"q":"Hard-to-measure performance and unpredictable adaptation suggest:","o":["Contracts are impossible under every operational and legal design available.","The transaction is necessarily infrequent because measurement determines order volume.","Ownership is optimal without estimating management, investment or incentive effects.","Contracting hazards to compare with safeguards and costs of internal authority."],"a":3},{"q":"Which is forward vertical integration under the supplied food-company boundary?","o":["Buying unrelated telecom operations that neither supply nor distribute its food.","Owning retail outlets selling the manufacturer's food to final customers.","Signing a carton contract with an interchangeable independent supplier.","Holding software-company shares without controlling its service to the manufacturer."],"a":1}]},"37":{"objectives":["Separate target standalone value, control, net synergy and price; test deal discipline without treating promises or accounting accretion as realized value."],"videos":[{"id":"yXqYuXzXFYE","title":"Session 24: Acquirers' Anonymous — Seven Steps to Sobriety — Aswath Damodaran","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Acquirers Anonymous - Damodaran lecture slides","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/country/AcqAnon2024Short.pdf","note":"Primary teaching slides. Read pages 22-57 on risk, control and synergy; pages 72-83 on pricing, conflicts and accountability. Historical study claims are not current universal failure rates."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Deal-value worksheet","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Use paper or an existing editor. Record standalone value, incremental benefits, integration cost, price, assumptions, owners and later evidence. No subscription required."}],"exercise":"Classroom hypothetical, not a verdict on an actual acquisition. All figures are present values in INR crore on the same valuation date and risk basis. Target standalone value 100; combination-specific gross synergy 30; integration cost 8; price 115. Control improvements are already included in the standalone 100 and must not be added again. (a) Calculate net synergy 22, maximum zero-NPV price 122, and buyer gain 7 at 115. Explain why zero-NPV ceiling is not a recommended bid and why paying any premium does not surrender all synergy. (b) Proposed recurring cost savings need owners, dates, implementation costs and retention/service-risk checks; capability benefits need an adoption mechanism and measurable cash-flow assumptions, not an automatic speculative label. (c) Compare buy, build, partner and walk-away alternatives, including opportunity costs. A failed-to-close negotiation has no post-acquisition integration outcome. (d) For an optional real completed deal, verify announcement versus completion, source the original thesis and the later observation window from filings, reconcile outcomes with a counterfactual and label attribution limits. A minority stake is not automatically an acquisition of control. Without these records report insufficient evidence, not success or failure.","quiz":[{"q":"In the supplied acquisition case, what is net synergy after integration cost?","o":["22 crore, subtracting the 8 cost from 30 gross incremental benefit.","30 crore, treating integration spending as already included in standalone value.","15 crore, using purchase premium as the measure of incremental benefit.","38 crore, adding integration expenditure to the gross combination benefit."],"a":0},{"q":"At the supplied 115 price, what is the buyer value gain?","o":["15 crore, since the premium paid over 100 standalone measures buyer gain.","7 crore, since 100 standalone plus 22 net synergy minus 115 price is 7.","37 crore, adding the 15 price premium to the 22 net combination benefit.","22 crore, since all net synergy remains regardless of the purchase price."],"a":1},{"q":"What does the 122 zero-NPV ceiling mean under the stated assumptions?","o":["Paying 122 leaves no modeled incremental buyer gain before omitted risks.","Paying 122 shows that the target standalone business is itself worth 122.","Paying 122 is the recommended bid because buyers should always pay full value.","Paying 122 guarantees a positive realized return regardless of integration results."],"a":0},{"q":"How should cost-saving and capability claims be assessed?","o":["Approve cost savings as controllable, while rejecting every capability benefit as speculation.","Value capability benefits from strategic fit alone, while omitting implementation dependencies.","Accept all forecasts with named owners, without stress-testing execution or retention effects.","Test mechanisms, costs, timing and evidence for each, rather than labels alone."],"a":3},{"q":"What does first-year EPS accretion establish about deal value?","o":["It establishes that integration costs have no effect on shareholder value.","It does not by itself show that the purchase price is below value received.","It proves synergy has been realized rather than merely projected in forecasts.","It establishes positive acquisition NPV whenever the buyer uses debt financing."],"a":1},{"q":"Which risk basis belongs in standalone target valuation?","o":["The combined revenue growth target, used without a separate assessment of risk.","The target share-price premium, used directly as the cash-flow discount rate.","The lowest borrowing rate available anywhere within the acquiring corporate group.","The risk of target cash flows, not automatically the safer acquirer funding rate."],"a":3},{"q":"Why not add control value again in this supplied case?","o":["Every acquisition premium already represents only control and never synergy.","Control changes cannot generate any operating cash-flow improvement in practice.","The integration cost 8 automatically cancels every potential control improvement.","Those improvements are already included in the stated 100 standalone valuation."],"a":3},{"q":"What supports an outcome audit of a real acquisition?","o":["Announcement date alone, with projected synergies treated as observed results.","Completion status, original thesis, dated results and explicit attribution limits.","A minority investment headline, assumed to transfer operating control at once.","A canceled negotiation, assessed as if integration ran for two completed years."],"a":1},{"q":"What is the disciplined response when a bid exceeds the supported value ceiling?","o":["Use rival bids as proof that their common price equals intrinsic target value.","Treat management commitment to the deal as evidence of a missing valuation benefit.","Reassess assumptions and alternatives; willingness to walk away can protect value.","Raise the synergy forecast until the preferred purchase price appears justified."],"a":2},{"q":"What should be concluded from bidding-war risk evidence?","o":["Bidding contests are harmless whenever a fairness opinion supports the transaction.","Every winning auction bid necessarily exceeds the winning buyer's own economic value.","Any competing bidder makes target standalone cash flows impossible to estimate.","Competition can encourage overpayment; a universal single-best predictor is not established."],"a":3}]},"38":{"objectives":["Solve a supplied one-shot game and compare known-end and continuing repeated interaction; distinguish model incentives from unverified company-history claims."],"videos":[{"id":"nM3rTU927io","title":"Introduction: five first lessons — Game Theory with Ben Polak, YaleCourses","by":"YaleCourses","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Introduction: Five First Lessons - publisher transcript","url":"https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-159/lecture-1","note":"Payoffs, dominance, prisoner dilemma and coordination; the assigned intro video only previews repeated interaction."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Repeated Games: Cheating, Punishment, and Outsourcing","url":"https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-159/lecture-22","note":"Read the publisher transcript on credible continuation incentives, grim trigger and its limits. This supplies the repeated-game extension missing from the intro."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Payoff and continuation worksheet","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Paper or an existing editor is enough. State payoffs, information, continuation assumptions and limitations."}],"exercise":"Classroom model of two teammates supplying effort, not a reconstruction of Jio/Airtel or advice to coordinate prices. Each simultaneously chooses C(contribute) or D(shirk). Ordered payoffs (A, B), in utility units: CC=(2,2), CD=(-1,3), DC=(3,-1), DD=(0,0). (a) Check both players' best responses: D strictly dominates C, DD is the unique one-shot Nash equilibrium, and CC gives both more than DD. (b) With two known rounds, common knowledge of this game and no other payoff or reputation effects, solve backward: DD in both rounds. Repetition alone does not ensure cooperation. (c) For potentially unending play, let 0<=delta<1 be the effective per-period continuation/discount factor, with perfect observation and no errors. Test grim trigger: start C, continue C while no D has occurred, then D forever. Cooperation value 2/(1-delta); optimal deviation 3 then 0 forever. The condition is delta>=1/3; at equality the player is indifferent. Compare delta=.25 and delta=.5, and verify DD is a credible continuation. (d) Discuss observation errors, uncertain payoffs and termination risk. These stylized incentives do not establish historical telecom payoffs or legal permission for competitor coordination. Do not contact competitors or make pricing agreements.","quiz":[{"q":"In the supplied one-shot matrix, what does A prefer if B contributes?","o":["Contribute: 2 from C exceeds 3 from D when B chooses C.","The matrix cannot compare choices without future rounds.","Either: the two actions give A equal payoffs against C.","Shirk: 3 from D exceeds 2 from C when B chooses C."],"a":3},{"q":"Which is the unique one-shot Nash outcome in the supplied matrix?","o":["DD, because each plays a best response to the other.","CC, because it gives both more than mutual shirking.","DC, because A earns the highest individual stage payoff.","CD, because B earns the highest individual stage payoff."],"a":0},{"q":"What does Nash equilibrium require?","o":["The largest possible sum of payoffs across the players in the game.","No profitable unilateral strategy change given the other players' strategies.","An agreement among the players to keep their strategies unchanged.","The same payoff for all players regardless of their available strategies."],"a":1},{"q":"What follows in two known rounds under the exercise assumptions?","o":["DD in the last round and, by backward reasoning, DD in the first.","CC in both rounds because future rewards discipline the final decision.","DD in the first round and CC in the last because the game is ending.","CC in the first round because every repeated game sustains cooperation."],"a":0},{"q":"At delta=.5, what is the value of perpetual mutual contribution?","o":["4 utility units, using 2/(1-.5) under the stated model.","2 utility units, counting the current contribution payoff only.","6 utility units, adding the deviation payoff to future cooperation.","3 utility units, substituting the one-period shirking temptation."],"a":0},{"q":"Under grim trigger, what is the optimal one-time deviation payoff from cooperation?","o":["0 now and 2 thereafter, assuming no punishment is triggered.","3 now and 0 thereafter, since the other player then uses D.","3 now and 2 thereafter, assuming the other ignores the deviation.","2 now and 3 thereafter, assuming both reward the deviation."],"a":1},{"q":"At delta=.25, is grim-trigger cooperation incentive-compatible here?","o":["No: DD ceases to be a stage equilibrium when continuation is possible.","No: 2/(1-.25) is below 3, so the immediate deviation pays more.","Yes: positive continuation probability alone guarantees cooperation.","Yes: the deviation payoff becomes 0 in the period of the deviation."],"a":1},{"q":"What happens exactly at delta=1/3 under the supplied assumptions?","o":["Cooperation strictly dominates every possible strategy at every history.","The stage-game payoffs change so that mutual shirking is no longer Nash.","The continuation game is guaranteed to end immediately after this round.","The player is indifferent; the weak no-deviation condition is satisfied."],"a":3},{"q":"Why is DD a credible punishment continuation in this model?","o":["When the other uses D, choosing D gives 0 rather than -1 from C.","It makes the punished player strictly prefer contributing to shirking.","It maximizes the total payoff of the two players in the stage game.","It requires an enforceable external agreement to change stage payoffs."],"a":0},{"q":"What limits transfer of this model to an actual industry?","o":["A familiar company name makes the supplied utilities observed market profits.","Observed price cuts alone reveal every counterfactual payoff and strategy.","Unknown payoffs, monitoring, continuation and legal constraints need separate evidence.","Fewer rivals guarantee stable cooperation irrespective of asymmetry or entry."],"a":2}]},"39":{"objectives":["Solve a fully specified two-player entry game and test commitment by continuation incentives; separate subgame perfection from an unsupported public threat."],"videos":[{"id":"6wkV5XFkzsY","title":"Nash Equilibrium in Extensive Games: The Case of Entry-Deterrence — Selcuk Ozyurt","by":"selcuk ozyurt","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Backward Induction: Chess, Strategies, and Credible Threats","url":"https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-159/lecture-15","note":"Publisher transcript, especially economic entry example at 01:01:56. Includes complete contingent plans and the difference between Nash threats and backward induction."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Coopetition - secondary overview","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coopetition","note":"Cooperation and competition may coexist; any proposed shared infrastructure still needs value, governance and legal assessment."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Game Theory Explorer","url":"https://www.gametheoryexplorer.org/","note":"Optional interface. Manual backward induction is sufficient; no solver or export is required."}],"exercise":"Fictional two-player entry model, not a joint decision by named real incumbents. Ordered payoffs (entrant, incumbent) are present values in INR crore on one common basis. Entrant chooses Out or In. Out gives (0,100). After In, incumbent chooses Fight=(-80,-200) or Accommodate=(40,-100). All figures already include future operating cash flows; do not mix annual losses with present values. (a) Work backward: -100>-200 so accommodate; 40>0 so enter. Give the full contingent strategy profile, not just the observed outcome. (b) A public promise with no change in these payoffs does not change this solution. Nor does a sunk payment that subtracts the same amount from both incumbent continuation options. (c) A different, hypothetical operational design changes continuation payoffs after In to Fight=(-80,20), Accommodate=(40,10), and Out=(0,80). Solve again: fight after entry, so stay out. These supplied payoffs, not the label sunk capacity, create credibility. The design is preferred to baseline entry/accommodation by this stylized incumbent (80 versus -100), but no real investment recommendation follows. (d) As a separate proposal, optional shared logistics might generate 20 total net surplus after costs; no division or implementation mechanism is supplied. Explain why positive total surplus need not make each party better off, why supplier/complementor roles may overlap, and why governance, data separation and legal review matter. No outreach or competitor coordination is authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"After entry in the base case, which action does the incumbent prefer?","o":["Either, since both continuation actions have identical present values.","Out, since the incumbent can reverse the entrant's earlier choice.","Fight, since -200 represents the smaller absolute accounting loss.","Accommodate, since -100 is greater than -200 under the supplied payoffs."],"a":3},{"q":"What is the base-case subgame-perfect strategy profile?","o":["Entrant chooses In; incumbent would Fight after In.","Entrant chooses Out; incumbent would Accommodate after In.","Entrant chooses In; incumbent would Accommodate after In.","Entrant chooses Out; incumbent would Fight after In."],"a":2},{"q":"What does subgame-perfect equilibrium require?","o":["An observed outcome only, without specifying any off-path strategies.","A payoff-maximizing allocation for the whole industry at the initial node.","Identical actions by every player whenever their decision times differ.","A Nash equilibrium in every subgame, including ones not reached on the path."],"a":3},{"q":"What does a public threat change if the base-case payoffs and information stay fixed?","o":["It makes fighting optimal because all public statements remove accommodation.","It replaces the entrant's zero outside option with the incumbent's 100 payoff.","It converts the sequential game into a simultaneous commitment equilibrium.","It supplies no new incentive to fight; the backward-induction solution remains."],"a":3},{"q":"A sunk cost subtracts 10 from both incumbent actions after entry. What follows?","o":["Both actions become equally valuable because their losses have increased.","Accommodation still beats fighting: -110 is greater than -210.","The entrant automatically loses 40 because every fixed cost raises marginal cost.","Fighting becomes credible because any sunk spending reverses the ranking."],"a":1},{"q":"In the alternative operational-design case, what happens after entry?","o":["Neither action can be compared because payoffs are now positive.","Accommodate remains optimal because 40 is the entrant's higher payoff.","Fight is optimal because every operational investment is irreversible.","Fight is optimal for the incumbent because 20 exceeds 10."],"a":3},{"q":"What is the alternative-design equilibrium outcome?","o":["Stay out: the entrant receives the incumbent's 80 outside payoff.","Enter: incumbent fighting gives entrant 20 rather than -80.","Stay out: anticipated fighting gives entrant -80 versus 0 outside.","Enter: anticipated accommodation gives entrant 40 versus 0 outside."],"a":2},{"q":"Why specify actions at nodes that the equilibrium path does not reach?","o":["They allow losses at unreached nodes to be ignored in testing threats.","They determine credible continuation incentives used in backward reasoning.","They guarantee that every possible node will eventually be observed.","They make the game repeated even when it has only one entry decision."],"a":1},{"q":"A shared-logistics proposal has 20 total net surplus but no division rule. What follows?","o":["Both parties necessarily receive 10 because cooperation always splits value equally.","Potential joint value does not establish individual gains or implementability.","Competition has ended because any shared activity removes product-market rivalry.","Every participant benefits because positive total value rules out distribution conflict."],"a":1},{"q":"What is a careful practical use of the supplied entry model?","o":["Predict every incumbent response from firm size without estimating continuation values.","Treat any prebuilt capacity as proof that the entrant's outside option is negative.","Test incentives and missing assumptions; do not infer actual threats are never carried out.","Assume a public matching promise is binding without examining incentives or constraints."],"a":2}]},"40":{"objectives":["Build an evidence-labeled five-choice cascade, distinguishing what a company offers, what it promotes and what is only an analyst hypothesis."],"videos":[{"id":"trax_OMOPcU","title":"How Strategy Really Works: Roger Martin (Playing to Win) — Rotman School of Management","by":"Rotman School of Management","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Strategy Choice Cascade - Roger Martin","url":"https://rogerlmartin.com/thought-pillars/strategy","note":"Primary five interrelated choices. The published order is a framework, not a one-way sequence that forbids iteration."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Role of Management Systems in Strategy","url":"https://rogerlmartin.substack.com/p/2020-10-05_the-role-of-management-systems-in-strategy-571583546fbehtml","note":"Primary author explanation: resource allocation, decision rights and measurement support distinctive capabilities; systems are more than dashboards."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Zerodha introducing MTF - 19 December 2024","url":"https://zerodha.com/z-connect/updates/introducing-margin-trade-funding-mtf-on-kite","note":"Offering a leveraged product differs from saying it will not be pushed. Attribute the latter as the company statement, not a verified behavior audit."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Zerodha MTF product page","url":"https://zerodha.com/mtf/","note":"Current existence check: margin funding is offered. Do not retain a no-margin-products premise."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Five-choice evidence worksheet","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Paper or an existing editor is enough; no paid strategy software required."}],"exercise":"Use Roger Martin's five interrelated choices to write an analyst reconstruction of Zerodha, not an authenticated internal strategy. For every claim label company-stated fact, supported inference or unverified hypothesis, with a date and source. The 19 December 2024 MTF launch says Zerodha offers margin funding while stating it will not push customers to trade; the current product page confirms the offering. Do not equate not pushing leverage with not selling margin products, or treat the stated policy as an observed conduct audit. Map winning aspiration, where to play, how to win, needed capabilities and supporting management systems. Publicly unknown internal metrics/decision rights remain unknown, not invented. Propose strategy-linked systems separately. Compare one coherent alternative and identify conditions that must hold. Stress-test a hypothetical sustained zero-fee rival or hypothetical fee restriction; these are scenarios, not claims about an enacted SEBI rule. Explain which linked choices need review under your assumptions, not a universally correct first box.","quiz":[{"q":"What is the published five-choice cascade order?","o":["Aspiration; where to play; how to win; capabilities; management systems.","Where to play; management systems; capabilities; aspiration; how to win.","Aspiration; capabilities; management systems; where to play; how to win.","Management systems; aspiration; how to win; capabilities; where to play."],"a":0},{"q":"A chosen customer segment without a proposed advantage is:","o":["A proven winning aspiration because segment size establishes competitive success.","An incomplete where-to-play/how-to-win logic requiring further choices.","A management-system design because segment choice allocates decision rights.","A complete strategy because selecting a segment establishes defensible advantage."],"a":1},{"q":"What follows from the supplied Zerodha MTF sources?","o":["It offers funding, proving every customer is actively encouraged to use leverage.","It stopped offering funding because the launch statement acknowledges borrowing risk.","It does not offer margin products because it says it will not push trading.","It offers margin funding; its launch statement says it will not push trading."],"a":3},{"q":"How should a public-evidence cascade describe unknown internal decision systems?","o":["Infer the exact internal scorecard from the company's general customer-first slogan.","Treat industry-standard dashboards as verified internal practice without a source.","Mark them unknown and separate proposed systems from documented company practice.","Omit the systems choice because public evidence gaps make it irrelevant to strategy."],"a":2},{"q":"What is missing from a how-to-win claim that says only superior execution?","o":["The total number of execution tasks, since a longer task list itself defines advantage.","The specific advantage and linked activities that serve the chosen customers.","A generic best-practice label, since copying an operating process establishes how to win.","The forecast growth target, since a desired revenue outcome alone specifies the position."],"a":1},{"q":"How should required capabilities be selected?","o":["Work backward from the chosen advantage to activities needed to deliver it.","List every professional skill equally, without linking skills to strategic choices.","Copy competitors' staffing charts because parity always creates durable advantage.","Choose only capabilities already owned, without assessing whether the strategy needs more."],"a":0},{"q":"Which management-system example is most clearly strategy-linked?","o":["A larger monthly slide pack with no effect on resources, decisions or capabilities.","An unprioritized list of all reporting tools used by any department in the firm.","A generic attendance dashboard presented as sufficient proof of customer advantage.","Defined investment and decision rights that build a needed distinctive capability."],"a":3},{"q":"What can explicit resource constraints contribute to strategic choice?","o":["Guarantee a defensible position whenever the available budget is smaller than rivals.","Expose trade-offs and assumptions, without guaranteeing the choice will succeed.","Make every feasible option equally attractive after all alternatives are listed.","Remove the need to compare customer value because constrained firms cannot choose."],"a":1},{"q":"A hypothetical zero-fee rival appears. What should the analyst do?","o":["Change how-to-win automatically because no other cascade choice can be affected.","Keep all choices unchanged because an aspiration prevents competitor effects.","Replace internal metrics first regardless of customer response or unit economics.","Test affected advantage assumptions and linked choices rather than mandate one first box."],"a":3},{"q":"Why compare two coherent strategic alternatives?","o":["Expose different assumptions, sacrifices and tests rather than defend one default.","Avoid evidence collection because logical consistency establishes customer demand.","Guarantee the preferred plan is best once a second presentation has been produced.","Prove both options can be pursued fully without any resource or positioning trade-off."],"a":0}]},"41":{"objectives":["Compare strategies across explicitly hypothetical futures using decision-relevant uncertainties, affordable options and observable triggers; avoid treating scenarios as predictions or generic cash advice."],"videos":[{"id":"exPbo9djgGw","title":"Scenario Planning: How Shell Predicted the Future — The Mastery Project","by":"The Mastery Project","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Strategy under uncertainty - McKinsey","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/strategy-under-uncertainty","note":"Full four-level framework, postures and moves. The June 2000 page adapts 1997 work; historical company examples are not current facts. It permits single forecasts when residual uncertainty is immaterial and includes big bets as well as options."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Behind the scenes of Shell scenarios","url":"https://blogs.shell.com/2017/09/11/behind-the-scenes-of-the-shell-scenarios/","note":"Primary company account of plausible assumptions, quantification and stretching management thinking. Not proof that Shell predicted exact events or outperformed every rival."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Scenario comparison worksheet","url":"https://www.google.com/docs/about/","note":"Use paper or an existing editor: decision, axes, coherent futures, strategy tests, costed options, signposts, owners and review rules."}],"exercise":"Fictional regional delivery company, not a factual recommendation for Nykaa, Delhivery or MakeMyTrip. Decision: commit to a fixed-capacity expansion now or run a 90-day limited pilot. Two uncertain demand and fuel-cost dimensions may be crossed only if the joint futures are coherent; a 2x2 is a thinking aid, not four equally likely or exhaustive forecasts. Hold service standards and a stated cash limit constant. Compare Strategy A expansion and Strategy B staged pilot in every quadrant, including upside forgone, cash exposure, delivery capacity, learning value and irreversibility. State missing payoffs before claiming one is best. Propose three observable signposts with baseline, source, threshold, date, owner and action; name what they lead relative to the decision, publication delay and false-signal risk. Use only accessible public or authorized internal data, not assumed private rival shipment figures. A 90-day window may be inconclusive. Identify no-regrets candidates across the considered futures, testing cost and opportunity cost. Do not automatically cut discounts or delay supplier payments: demand, contribution, service, agreed terms and supplier stability must be checked. Some uncertainty can justify shaping bets; options have costs and do not always dominate.","quiz":[{"q":"What is a scenario in this exercise?","o":["A plausible conditional future used to test choices, not an exact prediction.","An historical fact treated as certain evidence about every future market state.","A forecast whose assigned probability proves the future must follow that path.","A strategy chosen by management, independent of external assumptions or events."],"a":0},{"q":"How does the source define level-one residual uncertainty?","o":["All future variables are known perfectly, including every competitor's unannounced decision.","The future is wholly unknowable, making even qualitative situation analysis impossible.","Remaining uncertainty is immaterial to the strategic decision, allowing a useful single forecast.","There are exactly four equally probable outcomes requiring four different strategies."],"a":2},{"q":"What distinguishes level-three from level-two uncertainty in the source?","o":["Level three has no identifiable variables, while level two has no uncertainty.","Level three has a range of futures without natural discrete scenarios.","Level three permits no analysis, while level two guarantees a single forecast.","Level three requires precisely three scenarios, while level two requires two."],"a":1},{"q":"What is required before crossing two proposed scenario axes?","o":["Choose the easiest data to obtain regardless of effect on the strategic decision.","Assume independence because every pair of uncertain variables forms a valid grid.","Assign 25 percent probability to each quadrant before considering dependencies or evidence.","Check decision relevance, uncertainty and whether the resulting combinations are coherent."],"a":3},{"q":"What does a no-regrets candidate mean here?","o":["Expected to help across considered plausible futures, subject to costs and assumptions.","A move with zero spending because any paid action necessarily creates regret.","An action that benefits every stakeholder equally without any distribution trade-off.","Guaranteed free of risk in every conceivable future regardless of implementation cost."],"a":0},{"q":"What is the main attraction of a staged option?","o":["It eliminates every risk by postponing all commitments until uncertainty disappears.","It can limit exposure and buy learning while preserving a later choice to scale.","It guarantees eventual expansion can be undertaken at no additional cost.","It always beats a shaping investment regardless of timing or opportunity costs."],"a":1},{"q":"What does the sourced framework say about big bets?","o":["They are no-regrets actions because all shaping strategies succeed by definition.","They must be rejected whenever any relevant future variable remains uncertain.","They replace the need to estimate downside exposure or monitor changing assumptions.","They are an available move, often linked to shaping, not universally prohibited."],"a":3},{"q":"What makes a proposed leading indicator usable for this decision?","o":["A private rival's metric presumed available without access or reporting evidence.","A highly precise historical series with no relationship to future capacity needs.","A causal rationale, available timely data and a threshold tied to an action.","A familiar label even when its publication comes after the expansion decision."],"a":2},{"q":"What follows if signposts remain mixed after 90 days?","o":["Declare the favorable quadrant because no hostile forecast has been conclusively proved.","Discard the scenarios because every valid signpost must resolve uncertainty in 90 days.","Treat the pilot as proof of national demand regardless of its sample and limitations.","Record the uncertainty and apply the pre-set review rule rather than force a quadrant."],"a":3},{"q":"Why is automatically extending payables a poor hostile-scenario rule?","o":["Suppliers must accept delayed payments whenever a scenario includes high fuel costs.","Terms, supplier effects and total economics can make it harmful or unavailable.","Lower reported spending proves the same service can be maintained without consequences.","Payment timing never affects cash exposure in any operating business."],"a":1}]},"42":{"objectives":["Re-solve a fully specified capacity/entry case, reconcile incremental and ex-ante values, and apply governance and scenario assumptions rather than memorize unrelated company claims."],"videos":[],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Credible threats - Yale publisher transcript","url":"https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-159/lecture-15","note":"Economic entry example and complete contingent plans. Video-free review day; this is an optional transcript, not a promised pending video."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Economic governance - Nobel2009 academy release","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/2009/press-release/","note":"Specific assets, adaptation and costs of feasible governance alternatives; not a mechanical three-test ownership score."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Strategy under uncertainty","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/strategy-under-uncertainty","note":"Review assumptions, decision exposure and contingent moves; cash and opportunity costs remain relevant."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Game Theory Explorer - optional","url":"https://www.gametheoryexplorer.org/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Manual backward induction is sufficient."}],"exercise":"Fictional entrant E and incumbent I, not actual quick-commerce investment facts. All payoffs are present values in INR crore relative to the same no-entry/no-build baseline. Entrant outside payoff 0; entry gives -80 if fought or +40 if accommodated. Incumbent may prebuild usable capacity for 60 once. Without prebuild, fight requires 60 build+80 operating loss=-140, accommodation=-100, and no entry=0. With prebuild already paid, continuation fight=-80, accommodation=-100, no entry=0; adding the common sunk -60 gives total -140,-160,-60 respectively. The physical capacity, not merely subtracting a sunk constant, removes an otherwise action-specific future build requirement. Assume capacity works as specified, entrant observes it, complete information and no other opportunity costs or benefits. (a) No-build: accommodate then entrant enters; build: fight then entrant stays out. State full contingent strategies. (b) Ex ante, compare build/deterrence -60 with no-build/accommodation -100: gain 40 under assumptions, not guaranteed real profitability. Do not subtract 60 twice. (c) A sunk expenditure with no changed feasible actions or differential future costs would not change the ranking. (d) For owning versus leasing entrant stores, specificity, adaptation, frequency and contractibility are insufficient alone: compare feasible safeguards, finance, management costs and actual exit terms before recommending. (e) If ad CPM doubles, re-estimate every payoff materially exposed to paid acquisition using acquisition mix, volume and timing. Mostly organic does not automatically make the war-loss payoff the uniquely correct first number. No paid-share or spending data are supplied, so justify sensitivity rather than claim a forced answer.","quiz":[{"q":"Without prebuild, what is the incumbent best response after entry?","o":["Stay out: the incumbent chooses the entrant's initial action.","Either: future construction 60 does not enter either action payoff.","Accommodate: -100 is greater than the -140 fight payoff.","Fight: -140 is greater than the -100 accommodation payoff."],"a":2},{"q":"Without prebuild, what does the entrant do by backward induction?","o":["Stay out: the outside option is the incumbent's 140 construction cost.","Enter: the entrant receives the incumbent's -100 loss as revenue.","Stay out: anticipated fighting gives -80 rather than 0 outside.","Enter: anticipated accommodation gives 40 rather than 0 outside."],"a":3},{"q":"After usable capacity is built, which continuation comparison is correct?","o":["Fight -80 versus accommodate -100, before the common sunk 60.","Fight -140 versus accommodate -100, charging the sunk build only to fight.","Fight -80 versus accommodate -40, treating past build as a future saving.","Fight -20 versus accommodate -100, adding the build as a second benefit."],"a":0},{"q":"With observable usable prebuild and stated payoffs, what is the entrant choice?","o":["Stay out: credible fighting gives -80 versus 0 outside.","Stay out: the entrant itself must pay the incumbent's 60 build cost.","Enter: accommodation is still the incumbent's optimal continuation.","Enter: every irreversible investment guarantees profitable new entry."],"a":0},{"q":"What is the incumbent total payoff if it builds and successfully deters?","o":["-120, charging the same construction expenditure at two decision stages.","40, treating avoided loss as the total payoff rather than relative gain.","0, because costs already paid cannot affect the ex-ante decision.","-60, since the one-time capacity cost remains despite no entry."],"a":3},{"q":"Relative to no-build/accommodation, what is the modeled ex-ante gain?","o":["100, counting the full deterred loss without the 60 build expenditure.","40, comparing -60 with -100 on the common baseline.","60, treating the sunk build expenditure as the benefit of deterrence.","140, adding the construction expenditure to the avoided 100 loss."],"a":1},{"q":"Why can the supplied prebuild alter continuation incentives?","o":["The announcement alone forces the entrant to treat accommodation as impossible.","The same constant is subtracted from both actions and therefore reverses their order.","Usable capacity removes an otherwise fight-specific future construction requirement.","Any spending labeled sunk makes a public threat optimal regardless of future costs."],"a":2},{"q":"A failed project spends 60 but creates no usable capacity or payoff change. What follows?","o":["Entry is deterred automatically because the original no-build fight cost disappears.","The common sunk loss alone does not reverse the original continuation ranking.","Fighting becomes credible because every sunk project is a commitment device.","Accommodation becomes profitable because failed assets have no book depreciation."],"a":1},{"q":"What should an entry-game strategy specify?","o":["Only the final observed outcome because unused actions cannot affect credibility.","Only the incumbent's public statement without any ranking of continuation payoffs.","The entrant choice and incumbent response if entry occurs, including off-path actions.","A list of historical firm sizes without the entrant's outside option or incentives."],"a":2},{"q":"Which is correct about the 60 already spent after prebuild?","o":["Add it to accommodation because sunk costs are always recovered through cooperation.","Subtract it only from fighting because that is the action the build might enable.","Subtract it from every continuation for total values, not selectively to rank actions.","Ignore it at every stage, including the initial decision to spend the money."],"a":2},{"q":"What is needed before choosing own versus lease for entrant stores?","o":["Only high transaction frequency, because frequent use guarantees ownership is cheapest.","Only a six-month exit label, because every exit clause has zero cost or restriction.","Only the word core, because strategic importance establishes asset specificity.","Feasible terms, safeguards and internal costs alongside transaction-specific hazards."],"a":3},{"q":"What does low redeployability of dedicated tooling indicate?","o":["Potential relationship dependence, whose safeguards and governance costs need comparison.","Proof that the investment has no use even within the intended trading relationship.","A guarantee that a short lease can always terminate without economic consequences.","Proof that ownership eliminates all incentive, financing and management costs."],"a":0},{"q":"Under the revised D37 PV case, net synergy equals:","o":["7 from buyer gain, treating price premium as synergy.","22 from 30 gross benefit less 8 integration cost.","15 from 115 purchase price less 100 standalone value.","38 from 30 gross benefit plus 8 integration cost."],"a":1},{"q":"Under that same case, paying 115 creates buyer gain of:","o":["15 because any purchase premium is itself a benefit to the buyer.","30 because gross synergy should exclude all integration spending.","7 because 100+22-115=7 under the supplied assumptions.","22 because net synergy belongs to the buyer at every purchase price."],"a":2},{"q":"Why is EPS accretion not a sufficient value test?","o":["Financing and accounting can raise EPS without a positive acquisition NPV.","EPS can never change when an acquisition is financed with borrowed funds.","Every accretive transaction necessarily transfers all buyer value to the seller.","Only revenue matters because acquisition price and risk do not affect value."],"a":0},{"q":"Which is the revised D38 stage-game Nash outcome?","o":["CC, since collective payoff is greater than mutual shirking.","DD, since D beats C against either action in the supplied matrix.","DC, because asymmetric outcomes cannot admit unilateral deviations.","CD, because the contributor receives the greater individual payoff."],"a":1},{"q":"What supports grim-trigger cooperation in revised D38?","o":["A public promise to cooperate regardless of the other player's observed actions.","Repetition alone, regardless of termination, monitoring or the stage-game incentives.","Sufficient continuation weight and credible punishment under the stated monitoring assumptions.","A guaranteed final cooperative round that makes earlier punishment unnecessary."],"a":2},{"q":"What happens at delta=1/3 in that supplied repeated game?","o":["Cooperation and the best immediate deviation have equal value.","The stage payoffs change from DD=(0,0) to CC=(2,2) automatically.","Cooperation strictly dominates shirking at every history of play.","The players know the game ends after exactly three rounds."],"a":0},{"q":"What is Martin's five-choice order in the assigned primary page?","o":["Aspiration; where to play; how to win; capabilities; management systems.","Aspiration; capabilities; systems; where to play; how to win.","Where to play; systems; capabilities; aspiration; how to win.","Systems; how to win; aspiration; where to play; capabilities."],"a":0},{"q":"Which is a strategy-linked management-system choice?","o":["An attendance metric presented as universally superior for every strategy.","A list of all software packages without any link to capabilities or advantage.","Allocation and decision rules that build the capability required for the chosen advantage.","A larger report generated without changing resources, decisions or learning."],"a":2},{"q":"What does Zerodha's sourced MTF launch distinguish?","o":["Closing the funding product from promising to raise its financing revenue.","Prohibiting all leverage from encouraging every customer to borrow for investing.","Rejecting every margin product from allowing customers to buy unleveraged stocks.","Offering margin funding from stating it will not push customers to trade."],"a":3},{"q":"When ad CPM doubles, which payoff must be re-estimated first?","o":["Always the +40 accommodation payoff, even when all entry is organic and ad spending zero.","There is no forced answer without paid-acquisition exposure, timing and volume assumptions.","Neither payoff, because acquisition spending can never affect entry incentives.","Always the -80 war payoff, because organic acquisition affects no other cash flows."],"a":1},{"q":"How should the 2x2 scenario grid be interpreted?","o":["Two strategies repeated twice without any separate external uncertainty assumptions.","Four proven facts about future markets that remove uncertainty from the decision.","Four forecasts each with 25 percent probability because two binary axes were drawn.","A limited set of coherent conditional futures, not necessarily exhaustive or equally likely."],"a":3},{"q":"What is required of a monitoring signpost?","o":["Available timely data, a decision link, threshold and response, with false-signal limits.","An observed revenue decline automatically proving which external scenario caused it.","A private competitor's number assumed observable without a data source or permission.","A familiar label with no publication date because every indicator leads financial results."],"a":0},{"q":"What is a careful view of a shared infrastructure proposal?","o":["Treat positive joint surplus as proof that every participant benefits equally.","Test total and individual net benefits, governance and constraints against feasible alternatives.","Assume shared assets require no incremental investment, adaptation or coordination cost.","Approve common ownership whenever users can access several categories in one interface."],"a":1}]},"43":{"objectives":["Reconcile a fictional fund budget and exit-ownership sensitivity; distinguish gross proceeds, LP net returns and distribution assumptions from a universal venture screen."],"videos":[{"id":"1QVaXlwPpjk","title":"The Power Law: Unlocking Venture Capital's Secrets w/ Clay Finck (TIP 693)","by":"The Investor’s Podcast","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"AngelList power-law analysis - 2020","url":"https://www.angellist.com/blog/what-angellist-data-says-about-power-law-returns-in-venture-capital","note":"Read sample selection, realized/unrealized marks, fee assumptions and diversification conclusion. Platform-sample analysis is not a guarantee for all funds."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Power Laws in Venture Portfolio Construction","url":"https://reactionwheel.net/2017/12/power-laws-in-venture-portfolio-construction.html","note":"Independent practitioner analysis, including the concluding admission that its alpha 1.98 and outcome buckets are assumptions needing research. Compare with AngelList rather than force agreement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA glossary - DPI and distributions","url":"https://ilpa.org/resources-tools/private-equity-101/private-equity-glossary/","note":"Targeted definitions distinguish contributions and distributions; distributions can be cash or securities."}],"exercise":"Fictional $50 million fund, not the actual terms or track record of Blume, Kalaari or Stellaris. All $50 million is called. No other expenses, tax, recycling or debt. Fees are exactly 2 percent of $50 million each year for 10 years, totaling $10 million. Allocate the remaining $40 million as $20 million initial investments (25 positions of $0.8 million) and $20 million reserves. (a) Reconcile the budget. (b) Define \"return the fund\" narrowly as one holding generating $50 million gross fund proceeds before carry, with other proceeds zero. At exit ownership of 5, 8 and 12 percent, required equity exit values are $1,000 million, $625 million and $416.667 million. Assume ordinary pro-rata equity, no preference or transaction-cost effects. Entry ownership of 12 percent diluted to 8 percent means using 8 percent at exit. (c) In a separate fully realized whole-fund example, return all $50 million LP capital first, then charge 20 percent carry on remaining profit, with no hurdle or catch-up. Gross proceeds of $50 million give net proceeds of $50 million and no carry. Gross proceeds of $112.5 million give LP net proceeds of $100 million. Timing is still needed for IRR. (d) Compare the readings' assumptions and conclusions: extreme winners can matter without proving that every fund must make only whole-fund-return bets or use exactly one or two winners. Optional real track records require dated primary reporting with metric, currency, gross/net basis, paid-in denominator and realized/unrealized status. Without evidence, say unverified rather than infer real returns from this model. Deliverable: one-page budget, ownership sensitivity table and a paragraph on portfolio trade-offs.","quiz":[{"q":"Under the stated flat-fee $50 million model, lifetime fees are:","o":["$20 million, counting the same annual fee twice.","$5 million, charging the rate for only five years.","$10 million, using 0.02 times 50 times 10.","$1 million, charging 2 percent only once."],"a":2},{"q":"What is the reconciled investment budget after those fees?","o":["$40 million, split 20 initial and 20 reserves.","$30 million, subtracting both fee budget and reserves as expenses.","$35 million, leaving the unassigned 5 unexplained.","$50 million, treating fees as funded outside the commitment."],"a":0},{"q":"At 8 percent exit ownership, what equity value yields 50 gross proceeds?","o":["$1000 million, using the separate 5 percent case.","$416.667 million, using 12 percent entry ownership.","$625 million, dividing 50 by 0.08.","$50 million, ignoring the ownership fraction."],"a":2},{"q":"At 5 percent exit ownership, what equity value yields 50 gross proceeds?","o":["$50 million, treating all exit equity as fund-owned.","$250 million, multiplying the target by five.","$625 million, retaining 8 percent despite further dilution.","$1000 million, dividing 50 by 0.05."],"a":3},{"q":"A fund owns 10 percent at a 300 million equity exit. Gross proceeds are:","o":["$20 million by using initial cheque size as an exit participation ratio.","$45 million by applying the earlier 15 percent entry holding after dilution.","$300 million by treating the company equity value as fund proceeds.","$30 million before preferences, costs or fund-level deductions not supplied."],"a":3},{"q":"At 3.5 TVPI and 0.8 DPI on the same net basis, what is RVPI?","o":["2.7 times paid-in, an unrealized value estimate rather than a distribution.","3.5 times paid-in, because total value excludes all distributions.","4.3 times paid-in, adding distributed and total values a second time.",".8 times paid-in, because residual and distributed values must match."],"a":0},{"q":"With one initial outflow and one final inflow only, which has higher annualized return?","o":["5x over 11 years, because a larger multiple always gives a higher annualized rate.","Cannot compare even with the stated single-outflow/single-inflow assumption.","2.5x over 4 years, compared with 5x over 11 years.","They are equal because both final proceeds exceed their initial cost."],"a":2},{"q":"In the specified whole-fund waterfall, gross 112.5 gives LP net proceeds of:","o":["$62.5 million, treating profit before carry as all LP proceeds.","$100 million, returning 50 plus 80 percent of 62.5 profit.","$90 million, charging 20 percent carry on all 112.5 including principal.","$112.5 million, ignoring carry once capital has been returned."],"a":1},{"q":"What do the supplied power-law readings support carefully?","o":["Every fund must select only bets that individually return its entire committed capital.","A fitted or assumed alpha from one analysis establishes all future venture outcomes.","Outlier exposure matters, but portfolio rules depend on data and model assumptions.","Exactly two investments will return at least 20x whenever a fund holds 25 positions."],"a":2},{"q":"What should be checked before calling a quoted fund multiple an LP return?","o":["Only the largest portfolio-company headline valuation as of its latest round.","Only the manager name, because familiar funds use identical reporting definitions.","Only committed fund size, regardless of contributions or distribution timing.","Date, metric, denominator, currency, gross/net and realized versus residual value."],"a":3}]},"44":{"objectives":["Build a fully specified fictional cash-flow and valuation model, reconciling integer deal counts, calls, fees, carry and residual NAV; compare dated metrics without inferring IRR from DPI alone."],"videos":[{"id":"bGhv_Kox5RA","title":"PE Fund Performance Metrics: TVPI vs. MOIC vs. DPI and Gross vs. Net IRR","by":"Mergers & Inquisitions / Breaking Into Wall Street","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA performance-template definitions - granular methodology","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Read distribution definitions on pages 3-4 and fund-level net IRR/TVPI/gross methodology on pages 7-8. LP cash flows, NAV, fees and carry must be consistently classified."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Money-weighted IRR versus time-weighted returns - CFA Institute","url":"https://rpc.cfainstitute.org/research/cfa-digest/2017/04/using-brinson-attribution-to-explain-the-differences-between-time-weighted-twr-and-money-weigh","note":"Read the public digest distinguishing cash-flow-weighted IRR from geometrically compounded time-weighted returns. This is the digest, not the underlying journal paper."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Fund metrics overview - McGinnis","url":"https://mcginniscommawill.com/guides/venture-capital-deals-guide/08-dpi-rvpi-tvpi-irr/","note":"Read basic formulas. Cross-check against ILPA: distributions need not all be cash and IRR is money-weighted, not time-weighted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"PME methods - Carta","url":"https://carta.com/learn/private-funds/management/fund-performance/pme/","note":"Read cash-flow/index alignment and method differences. Caution: this page labels IRR time-weighted incorrectly; use cash-flow-based money-weighted IRR and do not treat PME as pure risk-adjusted skill proof."}],"exercise":"Fictional classroom model in INR crore, not actual AIF terms, tax treatment or FX results. Commitment is 300 but only 159 is called at time 0. The uncalled 141 is not an asset or paid-in denominator. Invest 120 at time 0 in 30 equal positions of 4; the remaining 39 is a non-interest-bearing cash reserve. Fees are 6 at each year-end from 1 through 5 (2 percent of 300), then 1.8 at each year-end from 6 through 10 (1.5 percent of the fixed original investment cost of 120). Total fees are 39. No other expenses, debt, recycling, tax or follow-on investments. Use integer outcomes: six zeros, eighteen at 2x, four at 7x and two at 25x. At year-end 6, write off the six zeros and exit six of the 2x holdings for gross proceeds of 48. Exit six more 2x holdings for 48 in year 7; the final six 2x holdings and two 7x holdings for 104 in year 8; the remaining two 7x holdings for 56 in year 9; and the two 25x holdings for 200 in year 10. Until the stated exit or write-off, assume fair value equals original cost, an artificial classroom convention, not valuation guidance. Gross NAV is cash plus remaining holdings after each fee and exit; all gross exit proceeds are distributed immediately. Carry is 20 percent of cumulative gross distributions above 159, with no hurdle or catch-up. Carry paid each year is the increase in that cumulative amount; the rest goes to LPs. For interim net NAV, accrue total carry of 20 percent times max(cumulative gross distributions + gross NAV - 159, 0). Deduct only unpaid carry from gross NAV, not previously paid carry again. Compute annual net DPI, RVPI, TVPI and money-weighted IRR using actual LP flows and terminal net NAV at each measurement date. Net TVPI must equal net DPI + net RVPI. Portfolio gross MOIC uses 120 investment cost, not 159 paid-in. Terminal gross proceeds are 456, gross MOIC is 3.8, carry is 59.4 and LP distributions are 396.6. Terminal net TVPI = net DPI = 396.6/159; RVPI is zero. Flex one 25x holding to zero at year 10, not both winners. Deliverable: spreadsheet, separate ratio and percentage-return charts, a defined J-curve trough measure and sensitivity explanation. Mark undefined IRR roots rather than inventing rates. No fixed year 1-3 trough is presumed.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D44 fund model\"><h3>Build it, then check the native model</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d44-fund-practice.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable D44 fund workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d44-fund-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">inspect its eight-sheet print view (PDF)</a>. Rebuild the model on a blank sheet first; the worked formulas are a checking aid, not evidence you performed the task. Yellow inputs, purple formulas and blank practice responses separate assumptions, calculation and your own work.</p><p>The workbook implements the stated thirty-holding exit/write-off schedule, fee-reserve cash, gross and net NAV, paid versus unpaid accrued carry, LP distributions, DPI/RVPI/TVPI, annual money-weighted IRR snapshots and separate ratio/percentage charts. The baseline checks to 456 gross proceeds, 59.4 carry and 396.6 LP distributions; net terminal DPI is 396.6/159 = 2.49434x. The defined end-year net-TVPI trough is 0.8x in year 6, while the IRR low point is a different statistic. Do not presume a universal early trough.</p><p>Change only the second 25x winner to zero on Inputs. The year-10 downside checks to 356 gross proceeds, 39.4 carry and 316.6 LP distributions, with net DPI 316.6/159 = 1.99119x. Check the no-carry case separately. A fee-reserve shortfall suppresses net return metrics rather than silently inventing capital calls. Year-0 IRR is undefined. Interim IRR includes the measurement-date net NAV once and excludes later artificial zero-flow years; it is not the realized return until all holdings are realized.</p><p>Native LibreOffice recalculation was compared with an independent exact-rational schedule and independently solved IRRs: 231 baseline numeric checks, ten downside checks, five zero-carry checks and two reserve checks passed, plus undefined/suppression checks. This is specific authored-model execution, not Excel/Sheets compatibility clearance, current AIF/legal/tax terms, real investment performance or independent learner transfer. No macros, external links, account or saved learner progress are created.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"A same-basis TVPI 2.4 and RVPI 1.7 imply DPI of:","o":[".7, because 2.4 minus 1.7=.7.","1.4, because total value should be halved.","4.1, because total and residual values should be added.",".6, because residual value is the distributions denominator."],"a":0},{"q":"Two funds reach 2x DPI in 6 and 12 years. What is needed to compare IRR?","o":["The dated contributions and distributions, plus any residual NAV at the comparison date.","Only the ending multiple, because timing has no role in a money-weighted return.","Only the commitment amount, because uncalled capital is always an invested cash outflow.","Only the completion year, because identical DPI uniquely determines every cash-flow pattern."],"a":0},{"q":"What does a PME comparison need?","o":["Only the distribution multiple because all PME methods produce that same ratio.","A stated method, dated fund flows, residual NAV and an appropriate index/currency basis.","Only a fund IRR and an index point-to-point return across the same calendar years.","Only committed capital and the highest public-index price in the reporting period."],"a":1},{"q":"How should an unrealized 10x mark be read?","o":["As guaranteed exit proceeds whenever a third-party auditor reviewed the fund accounts.","As cash already returned because the latest financing round has a published price.","As a valuation estimate to assess for date, rights, method and risks, not a completed sale.","As necessarily worthless because every unsold investment must have zero economic value."],"a":2},{"q":"In the supplied model, what is total LP paid-in?","o":["120, because fees and cash reserves are excluded from LP capital calls.","300, because all committed capital is automatically contributed at launch.","39, because only operating expenses count toward the LP denominator.","159, because 141 of the 300 commitment is uncalled."],"a":3},{"q":"What is lifetime management-fee spending under the explicit model?","o":["9, omitting the committed-capital fee in years 1-5.","39, from 5 times 6 plus 5 times 1.8.","60, extending the first five years 2 percent rate across all 10 years.","30, omitting the fixed-cost-basis fee in years 6-10."],"a":1},{"q":"What is the fully realized portfolio gross MOIC?","o":["2.494, dividing 396.6 by 159 and labeling it before fees/carry.","2.868, dividing 456 by 159 and calling the result portfolio gross MOIC.","7.6, counting the same 456 proceeds twice before dividing by 120.","3.8, dividing 456 gross proceeds by 120 investment cost."],"a":3},{"q":"Under the specified waterfall, terminal carried interest is:","o":["91.2, charging 20 percent of all 456 without returning LP capital.","59.4, equal to 20 percent of (456 - 159).","67.2, returning only 120 before carry despite the stated 159 capital threshold.","39, equating the management-fee reserve with performance compensation."],"a":1},{"q":"At terminal liquidation, which net metric relation holds?","o":["RVPI=TVPI because every sold holding remains an unrealized asset.","DPI=TVPI=396.6/159 and RVPI=0 under the model.","TVPI=DPI+1 because residual NAV always equals called capital at liquidation.","DPI=456/120 because all LP net metrics exclude fund fees by definition."],"a":1},{"q":"Which is a careful interpretation of lower dollar than rupee net returns?","o":["The gap alone proves currency depreciation caused every difference in performance.","Carry must be charged again in dollars because it cannot appear in rupee net returns.","Distribution timing can be ignored because all exchange rates are fixed for a fund life.","Check dated FX conversion and other reporting differences before attributing the entire gap."],"a":3}]},"45":{"objectives":["Write an evidence-testable sector thesis and sourcing plan with mandate fit, channel measures and falsification, without universal hit-rate claims or unauthorized outreach."],"videos":[{"id":"AL_Y3U9Rais","title":"Sourcing Top VC Deals","by":"VC Lab","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Deal sourcing - Carta","url":"https://carta.com/learn/private-funds/management/deal-flow/deal-sourcing/","note":"Full process/channel overview. It defines proprietary as exclusive/off-market; relationship advantage alone does not establish exclusivity."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Deal sourcing guide - PitchBook","url":"https://pitchbook.com/blog/deal-sourcing-guide","note":"Public vendor overview; it is not an empirical ranking proving one channel always produces the best fund-returning firms."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"OpenVC - optional mandate research","url":"https://www.openvc.app","note":"Public investor-profile listings are leads, not verified performance or live access. Count/access/features change; verify each relevant fund on its own site. No submissions or outreach needed."}],"exercise":"Choose an Indian sector for a research-only investment thesis, not a recommendation to invest or contact anyone. State target stage, check range, ownership needs, follow-on policy, geography and relevant mandate limits. Build an evidence ledger for why now with dated public primary sources where possible, distinguish structural change from hype, and state contrary evidence. Map 15-20 actual firms only if source-verified; otherwise mark the market map incomplete rather than invent firms or private metrics. Separate one-page decision thesis from an appendix for the map/sources. Explain excluded layers and opportunity costs, not claim narrow mandates are automatically wrong. Design three candidate sourcing channels as hypotheses, with public examples only where verified, time budget, qualified opportunities per hour, access/acceptance assumptions, conversion stages and a later outcome measure. Warm referrals can help but may create network blind spots; test channels rather than assert universal Indian-network or historic-hit-rate rankings. Give a pre-specified, observable falsifier with threshold, source, horizon and action, plus a review rule for ambiguous evidence. The 2000 decks/8 investments ratio alone cannot identify the binding return constraint; access, selection, valuation, allocation and portfolio support all need evidence. This assignment authorizes desk research and drafts only, no outreach, applications, private-data access or investment commitments.","quiz":[{"q":"A fund sees 2000 decks and makes 8 investments. What follows from that ratio alone?","o":["Selection quality must be the only return constraint because conversion is low.","Valuation cannot matter because each investment passed a competitive selection process.","Access is necessarily excellent because a large deck count proves top-deal allocation.","Conversion is 0.4 percent; this alone does not identify the binding return constraint."],"a":3},{"q":"Under the supplied Carta definition, what makes sourcing proprietary?","o":["An exclusive/off-market opportunity unavailable broadly, not merely a warm referral.","Any investment the fund ultimately wins regardless of how broadly it was marketed.","Any inbound deck because receiving a message proves other investors have not seen it.","Any opportunity introduced by a trusted person even if all competing funds can bid."],"a":0},{"q":"What is a real trade-off of a narrower mandate?","o":["Focus may improve, but excluded opportunities still have a cost; assess fit and edge.","It guarantees outperformance because fewer categories always improve selection skill.","It removes diversification and portfolio construction from every investment decision.","It must fail because every fund requires exposure to every possible industry winner."],"a":0},{"q":"How should a claim that warm referrals have the highest hit-rate be tested?","o":["Accept it from the number of well-known founders in the fund's personal network.","Use comparable channel cohorts, denominators, access and realized-outcome evidence.","Assume it holds in India because a global vendor recommends relationship building.","Compare only raw introductions, without recording which met the same mandate."],"a":1},{"q":"What should a why-now argument supply?","o":["A claim that timing always kills more startups than product errors in every sector.","A fixed calendar forecast treated as sufficient evidence of future demand.","A fashionable category label used as proof that customers are ready to adopt.","Dated evidence for adoption/economics changes, with contrary evidence assessed."],"a":3},{"q":"What makes a market map useful for selection?","o":["Source-verified firms, defined layers, fit criteria and explicit information gaps.","A target of 20 company names filled with guesses when public evidence is missing.","A shortlist ranked only by publicity because awareness guarantees fund economics.","The largest possible list without indicating business model, stage or mandate fit."],"a":0},{"q":"How should proposed Indian operator-network sourcing be assessed?","o":["As proprietary automatically because former employees share a previous employer.","As proof of allocation because a fund can identify founders from public biographies.","As the universally most durable channel regardless of sector, stage or fund access.","As a channel hypothesis with access, bias, qualified flow and outcome measures."],"a":3},{"q":"A fund website lists 12 focus sectors. What is the careful interpretation?","o":["The sector count alone proves the fund lacks a real thesis and cannot choose.","The count proves all 12 sectors receive equal investment resources every year.","Check stage, check sizes, exclusions and actual deployment before judging the mandate.","A broad list establishes a legal requirement that the fund must invest in each sector."],"a":2},{"q":"What is a useful thesis falsifier?","o":["A broad statement that the team will change its view whenever the story feels weaker.","A performance target that is always revised after the original threshold is missed.","A forecast made unfalsifiable by treating every negative signal as proof of future upside.","Observable contrary evidence with threshold, data source, horizon and a decision response."],"a":3},{"q":"What should a $250,000 check-size constraint inform?","o":["A mandatory nonmetro-only strategy regardless of valuations, sector or capital needs.","A rule to chase every hot auction because publicity compensates for check limitations.","Stage, ownership, follow-on and access needs, rather than geographic stereotypes.","A guarantee the fund can lead any round that includes a seed-stage company."],"a":2}]},"46":{"objectives":["Design and test a 30-day research-only sourcing workflow with measurable funnel hypotheses, dated public evidence and explicit access limits."],"videos":[{"id":"36u2yvHovNw","title":"How to Be a VC/Angel Investor: Sourcing","by":"Mary Gao","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"How VCs sourced deals - 2016 survey","url":"https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w22587/w22587.pdf","note":"Read methods, section 3.1 and funnel evidence. The 2015-16 U.S.-heavy institutional-VC survey describes practices, not a universal current Indian channel ranking."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sourcing channels - Carta","url":"https://carta.com/learn/private-funds/management/deal-flow/deal-sourcing/","note":"Use channels as candidates to test, not a guaranteed ordering of returns."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Crunchbase - optional discovery","url":"https://www.crunchbase.com","note":"Free firmographics and paid research features differ. Do not assume a free account supplies a complete sector funding history; use dated public company/investor announcements as alternatives. No account creation, payment or outreach is required."}],"exercise":"Carry the D45 mandate into a 30-day plan and run its desk-research portion for one week. Define three observable triggers as research leads, not proof of fundability: for each, record event date, detection date, source, mandate link, plausible false positive and expiration rule. Identify up to three genuinely available public sources per trigger, leaving gaps explicit instead of treating private MAU, employment rumors or paid databases as accessible facts. Choose weekly screening volumes as capacity assumptions, not industry benchmarks. Build a five-criterion rubric with definitions, evidence standards, weights and a missing-data policy; a score does not replace investment judgment. Log up to 20 source-verified companies with timestamp, evidence, pass/track/deeper-research reason, uncertainty and next desk-research step. If fewer are verifiable, mark the log incomplete rather than invent records. Draft first-call and outreach plans only; do not contact founders, customers, ex-employees or intermediaries under this assignment. Diagnose low funnel conversion using fit, analyst/partner capacity, founder acceptance and stage definitions before changing triggers. Compare candidate channels on qualified opportunities per hour, cost, access and eventual outcomes; being first is only a proxy and a six-month sample cannot establish realized fund returns. Review passes without hindsight: what was knowable then versus learned later? Deliverable: 30-day plan, one-week decision log and an explicit list of tests that need separate access or outreach approval.","quiz":[{"q":"200 companies are flagged and ten first calls occur. What should be checked before changing triggers?","o":["Tighten trigger thresholds first, treating unbooked calls as screened-out companies before separating rejection from capacity.","Increase lead volume first, assuming the current call rate reflects available partner capacity rather than constraints.","Reduce screening volume first, using the call count as the target without checking qualified opportunities that were missed.","Fit, stage definitions, capacity and acceptance, since counts alone do not identify the bottleneck."],"a":3},{"q":"What can make a trigger useful relative to a static list?","o":["An earlier event date alone, without checking whether detection was early enough to affect the decision.","A higher count of flagged companies alone, without measuring how many fit the target stage and mandate.","A timely, verifiable mandate-linked event, with false positives and observation lag tested.","More precise event wording alone, without testing the data source or the rate of false positives."],"a":2},{"q":"What is a possible benefit of tracking a company for nine months?","o":["Treating repeated founder updates as independently verified progress without checking their underlying evidence.","Treating familiarity as sufficient conviction without testing whether the original thesis changed during monitoring.","Dated evidence of progress may speed assessment, while prior familiarity can also bias judgment.","Treating early contact as evidence of likely allocation without checking the founder's process or competing offers."],"a":2},{"q":"Two of forty first calls become partner meetings. How should this be interpreted?","o":["A five-percent conversion to assess against comparable cohorts, stage fit, cost and outcomes.","Judge the analyst by the five-percent rate alone, without comparing mandate, stage definitions or partner capacity.","Declare the funnel healthy from the two accepted meetings, without examining missed opportunities or time cost.","Increase the meeting target using another fund's conversion rate before checking that its funnel stages match."],"a":0},{"q":"A current TAM estimate is small. What is an evidence-based follow-up?","o":["Replace the current estimate with an adjacent-category total before verifying which buyers the product can reach.","Test reachable segments, expansion mechanisms, price and competition rather than presume future growth.","Assume future regulatory change expands the market before specifying its adoption mechanism or timing.","Use a low valuation to offset the small TAM before testing whether the exit can meet the fund's economics."],"a":1},{"q":"Why retain dated reasons for passing on an opportunity?","o":["Use later financing valuation as the main outcome label without distinguishing a new mark from a realized return.","Judge only passed companies that became prominent, leaving unremarkable passes out of the comparison cohort.","To compare decisions with later evidence while separating knowable facts from hindsight.","Rewrite the original reason using later product changes, obscuring what was actually known at the decision date."],"a":2},{"q":"A company is already in a competitive process. What should guide the decision?","o":["Pass because competition alone is treated as evidence that the price has already exceeded investment value.","Match the latest terms before checking whether this fund's ownership target and risk assumptions still work.","Shorten diligence to meet the deadline without identifying which unresolved risks could reverse the decision.","Mandate, evidence, price, access and time to test key risks, not an automatic auction rule."],"a":3},{"q":"How should a regional incubator channel be evaluated?","o":["As a hypothesis using qualified flow, access, cost, bias and later outcomes in the target segment.","Rank it from introduction volume without separating mandate-qualified companies from general ecosystem activity.","Rank it from local familiarity without measuring blind spots, access costs or the outcomes of comparable leads.","Rank it from the number of respected intermediaries without checking which introductions converted to usable evidence."],"a":0},{"q":"A passed company later raises a hot round. What is the right review?","o":["Count the pass as a lost realized profit using the next financing valuation, without considering access or exit risk.","Revise the rubric to fit this single success without comparing the full cohort of original passes and investments.","Treat later product changes as errors in the seed memo without separating predictable developments from new information.","Check the original evidence and decision rule, new information, price and whether an error was knowable."],"a":3},{"q":"Founders report that your call came before other funds. What does that show?","o":["Use it as the main performance outcome without checking allocation, investment price or retained ownership.","Use the interview percentage without recording nonresponses or the comparability of the founders contacted.","A potentially useful timing proxy needing validation, not proof of allocation or realized return edge.","Treat early contact as sufficient evidence of selection edge before examining downstream decisions and outcomes."],"a":2}]},"47":{"objectives":["Write a five-day public-evidence diligence memo that distinguishes facts, claims, hypotheses and unresolved private-data needs."],"videos":[{"id":"I6iQU4355Ng","title":"Quick Study: How Venture Capitalists Make Decisions","by":"Stanford Graduate School of Business","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Stanford summary of the VC survey (2017)","url":"https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/do-funders-care-more-about-your-team-your-idea-or-your-passion","note":"Team emphasis is a dated survey finding with industry/stage variation, not proof that one trait guarantees outcomes."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Original VC decision survey","url":"https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w22587/w22587.pdf","note":"Read methods and Tables 7-9. Average deal-close time is not active diligence time or a mandatory schedule; the working paper is a 2016 version."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Diligence workstreams - 1752vc","url":"https://www.1752.vc/learn/venture-capital-due-diligence-investor-guide","note":"Distinguish reported survey averages from the author's opinions about highest-signal hours and sample timelines."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Wellfound - optional public hiring leads","url":"https://wellfound.com","note":"Hiring marketplace, not audited company data. Job listings can suggest hiring intent; do not infer exact headcount, hiring velocity, financials or complete funding history from a landing page."}],"exercise":"Choose a real Indian startup with enough public evidence, or use a clearly fictional case if public data is inadequate. This five-day classroom sprint is not full investment, legal or technical diligence. Day 1: define mandate, dated decision question, metric definitions and material risks. Day 2: triangulate public product, company, investor and customer-review sources; note incentives, sample selection, date and contradictions. Day 3: reconcile growth, retention and unit-economics claims only where evidence permits. If public records are incomplete, write unknown, not a made-up private cohort or guessed financial figure. Day 4: draft two customer interview guides and a reference-check plan, but do not make calls or contact anyone without separate authority. Minimize personal data and preserve consent/confidentiality boundaries. A real interview quote requires an actual authorized conversation and accurate record; never invent quotes. Day 5: two-page provisional memo with evidence ledger, top three material risks, cheapest permitted next test, decision sensitivity and pass/track/further-diligence conclusion. If an invest view is included, label it a conditional research judgment, not a commitment or recommendation to transact. Confidential data may be replaced by aggregated, redacted or independently checked evidence; an unresolved material gap can block conviction without proving dishonesty. Example: if beginning customer count is 100, monthly logo churn is eight percent and ending count is 140, an assumed no-other-movement bridge requires 48 new customers. If instead growth refers to revenue, that customer calculation cannot be inferred. Eight percent constant monthly cohort churn leaves about 36.8 percent after 12 months; it does not by itself prove total growth must asymptote.","quiz":[{"q":"What does the supplied 2015-16 VC survey support?","o":["Apply the overall sample ranking to every stage without checking its healthcare and later-stage variation.","Treat stated investor preferences as a causal estimate of which factor actually produced investment returns.","Respondents often ranked team highest, with stage/industry variation and sample limits.","Read the historical U.S.-heavy survey as a current Indian industry benchmark without testing external validity."],"a":2},{"q":"If beginning logos are 100, eight churn and ending logos are 140, required new logos are:","o":["40, using the difference between ending and beginning counts without replacing churned customers.","32, subtracting eight churned customers from the forty-customer net increase instead of adding them.","148, adding churn to the ending customer count without subtracting the starting base.","48, under the stated assumption of no other customer-count movements."],"a":3},{"q":"What supports a founder-market-fit assessment?","o":["A specific customer insight taken at face value without verifying whether users share it or competitors know it.","Prior sector employment used as a proxy without checking the founder's actual role, learning or current execution.","A previous successful exit weighted heavily without testing whether its drivers transfer to this market.","Specific domain insight and execution evidence tested against customers and alternative explanations."],"a":3},{"q":"Two nonpaying users praise a product. What is a careful conclusion?","o":["Treat the praise as willingness-to-pay evidence without checking who pays or whether incentives affected the feedback.","Reject demand because these users pay nothing, without examining an advertiser, employer or other payer model.","Limited positive feedback; test representative behavior and the actual payer/revenue model.","Infer repeat engagement from positive interview descriptions before examining behavior over a defined period."],"a":2},{"q":"A founder declines to share raw cohort data. What is a proportionate next step?","o":["Mark the refusal itself as negative retention evidence before checking whether a permitted verification route exists.","Sign an NDA and treat the unresolved cohort claim as verified without obtaining any acceptable evidence.","Accept a selected customer-reference list as a complete substitute without testing whether it covers churned users.","Seek permitted aggregate or redacted verification and assess any remaining material evidence gap."],"a":3},{"q":"What is a useful engagement measure?","o":["The weekly active-user share without defining the core action or testing whether the same users return.","Retained cohorts performing the core value action, with period and user definitions stated.","Total registrations net of duplicates, but without separating inactive accounts from retained users.","Session frequency across all users, but without checking whether sessions perform a meaningful product action."],"a":1},{"q":"An ex-employee claims repeated leadership turnover. How should it enter a memo?","o":["Use public profile dates as full corroboration without verifying role scope, overlap or reasons for departure.","Include the claim anonymously as a conclusion without recording its source incentives or conflicting evidence.","Attribute it as a claim and corroborate dates, role definitions and context before weighting it.","Discount the claim entirely because the source left the company, without testing independently observable details."],"a":2},{"q":"How should a seed-stage moat hypothesis be tested?","o":["Use a patent count as the moat score before assessing enforceability, substitutes or the economics of adoption.","Use current customer satisfaction as switching-cost evidence without testing contracts, workflow changes or alternatives.","Use an early product lead as proof of durable advantage without assessing imitation speed and required investment.","Identify a plausible mechanism, evidence, limits and tests of whether it can persist as the firm grows."],"a":3},{"q":"What does a why-now diligence test examine?","o":["An exogenous trend named in the deck without testing its effect on reachable buyers or commercial constraints.","A large future market estimate without identifying what changed or how the firm can capture demand.","A dated change linking opportunity to customer adoption or economics, including contrary evidence.","A competitor funding announcement treated as sufficient adoption evidence without customer or economic corroboration."],"a":2},{"q":"How should word-of-mouth growth be investigated?","o":["Use the unpaid-signup share as the referral share without separating search, direct visits or unknown attribution.","Reconcile cohort acquisition sources, referrals, incentives and retention rather than infer causality.","Use referral counts without checking incentives, acquired-user retention or the cost of generating each referral.","Use the reported k-factor without reconciling its denominator, measurement period or changes between cohorts."],"a":1}]},"48":{"objectives":["Reconstruct a decision at a fixed historical cutoff without hindsight, or audit a fictional diligence packet with explicit gaps and conditional conclusions."],"videos":[{"id":"SsNa4ntaWcQ","title":"Mastering Venture Fundraising: What Investors Really Look For | 2025 ABH Summit Workshop","by":"Africa’s Business Heroes","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO sample VC diligence request list","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/07/Cooley-GO-Tip-Sheet-Sample-VC-Due-Diligence-Request-List.pdf","note":"Read the complete sample and its landing-page disclaimer. U.S.-oriented categories are prompts, not Indian legal requirements; licenses and assignment exceptions also need examination."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO scope and legal disclaimer","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/documents/sample-vc-due-diligence-request-list/","note":"Tailor documents to transaction and jurisdiction with qualified counsel. A sample list is not legal advice or an exhaustive risk test."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Diligence checklist - Startup Fundraising","url":"https://startupfundraising.com/due-diligence-checklist","note":"Illustrative corporate, financial and commercial list. U.S. tax/equity terms are not automatically applicable in India; timing claims are not universal."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Startup India - optional certificate validation","url":"https://www.startupindia.gov.in","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. The official landing page exposes recognition and certificate-validation routes."}],"exercise":"Default to a fictional troubled startup, not allegations about BYJU'S, GoMechanic, Trell or Zilingo. A real-company alternative requires dated primary records and a fixed investment-decision cutoff; separate allegations, admissions, findings and later-discovered information, and do not assert that public evidence necessarily would have prevented a loss. Fictional packet at the cutoff: (1) the deck labels 120 as revenue, while a separate dashboard shows 120 GMV, discounts 20 and collections 80; accounting basis, refunds, principal/agent status and timing reconciliation are missing; (2) company age is 24 months but only six monthly financial statements are supplied, with no explanation; (3) the founder personally holds a core IP registration, while assignment, license, exclusions and third-party rights have not been supplied; (4) three of five selected retained users say they would be very disappointed without the product, while independently reported annual logo churn is 50 percent with no cohort bridge; (5) one customer is 45 percent of reported revenue, but contract term, renewal status, payment performance and concentration basis are missing; (6) the product demo uses substantial manual operations behind an API, but labor costs and automation assumptions are absent. These are risk signals and gaps, not proof of fraud, lack of demand or a specific legal outcome. Build a five-row risk table with packet evidence, precise question, checklist category, evidence needed and conditional action; explicitly record the sixth issue even if not in the top five. Reconcile financial categories rather than equate GMV, revenue, collection or demand. Investigate IP rights with jurisdiction-appropriate counsel rather than assume ownership alone exhausts usable rights. Sampling and churn do not establish that churned users never activated or that narrow PMF is proven. Give a 48-hour test plan ranked by material decision sensitivity, evidence access, cost and timing; unresolved decisive legal/financial risks are not waived by a short deadline. Deliverable: risk table and a paragraph on what a checklist can and cannot establish. Public research and draft questions only; no calls, backchannels, private records or investment commitment.","quiz":[{"q":"Which financial test is justified without unsupported claims about what founders most often fake?","o":["Reconcile reported revenue, contracts, invoices and collections under the stated accounting basis.","Compare collections with the deck total without adjusting for timing, refunds, advances or the revenue-recognition basis.","Compare reported revenue with GMV without checking whether the company acts as principal or agent.","Use bank receipts as the sole verification source without reconciling contracts, invoices and accounting periods."],"a":0},{"q":"A 24-month-old company supplies only six monthly statements. What should happen?","o":["Treat the six supplied months as representative without checking why the earlier eighteen months are absent.","Prioritize the total number of data-room files rather than whether the material reporting periods are covered.","Infer weak demand from the missing statements before obtaining an explanation or other contemporaneous evidence.","Request the missing periods or explanation and assess material gaps, rather than count files."],"a":3},{"q":"What does the fictional GMV/discount/collections mismatch establish?","o":["Subtract discounts from GMV and label the result revenue without checking accounting basis, refunds or timing.","Treat collections as the only demand measure without reconciling outstanding invoices and deferred receipts.","A reconciliation need, not by itself fraud, recognized revenue or absence of customer demand.","Treat a discount gap as sufficient evidence of uneconomic growth without acquisition, margin or repeat-purchase data."],"a":2},{"q":"Core IP is registered to the founder personally. What is the appropriate response?","o":["Require assignment as the only possible remedy before reviewing any enforceable license or third-party restrictions.","Rely on founder control alone without checking rights held by the company or obligations if the founder leaves.","Review chain of title, usable rights, licenses and restrictions with counsel before relying on the asset.","Reduce the valuation for IP uncertainty without determining whether the company can legally use the necessary asset."],"a":2},{"q":"Three of five selected users love the product but annual churn is 50 percent. What follows?","o":["Check sample selection, definitions and cohorts; neither narrow PMF nor churn causes are proven.","Conclude that PMF is real but narrow before checking whether interviewed and churned customers represent the same segment.","Prefer the three positive interviews over the churn figure without checking selection bias or churn definitions.","Use the annual churn figure to dismiss all favorable user evidence without separating cohorts and product use cases."],"a":0},{"q":"How can three enterprise pilots be distinguished from repeatable paying revenue?","o":["Use logo count as the traction metric without checking whether pilots are paid, time-limited or likely to convert.","Use signed pilot value as recurring revenue without checking delivery, renewal terms and the recognition basis.","Check paid terms, recognized revenue, conversion and timing while noting a small sample.","Use conversion from these three pilots as a stable benchmark without noting sample size or selection."],"a":2},{"q":"A workflow marketed as AI uses manual operations. What should be investigated?","o":["Recalculate margins assuming no labor cost was recorded before checking the existing cost classification.","Actual claims, labor, quality, margins and scaling assumptions before deciding materiality or intent.","Treat the manual workflow as a failed automation thesis without testing its planned transition, cost or quality limits.","Accept current margins as scalable without checking whether manual work grows linearly with transaction volume."],"a":1},{"q":"What should guide a 48-hour diligence sprint?","o":["Rank tests by ease alone, leaving a difficult but decisive title or financial-reconciliation issue unresolved.","Material risks and decisive available tests, without waiving unresolved essential legal or financial checks.","Focus on the three modeled operating sensitivities while excluding a material unmodeled legal risk.","Use the deadline to accept management explanations for all missing evidence without recording decision consequences."],"a":1},{"q":"All references were selected by current management. What is the main gap?","o":["Add more management-nominated references without diversifying the selection process or testing contrary evidence.","Treat the common selection route as a character problem rather than a limitation to address through corroboration.","Selection bias; plan diverse corroboration through authorized, privacy-respecting sources.","Seek informal off-list opinions without first defining permitted contacts, privacy limits or the claims to test."],"a":2},{"q":"One customer is 45 percent of revenue. What is a careful response?","o":["Apply a fixed concentration cutoff without checking contract duration, customer quality or alternative demand.","Treat a multiyear contract as eliminating concentration risk without assessing termination, payment or renewal exposure.","Seek a price discount as the whole remedy without testing the cash-flow impact of losing the customer.","Test contract, renewal, margins, cash collection and alternatives before deciding risk acceptability."],"a":3}]},"49":{"objectives":["Re-solve a fully specified down-year fund case, separating commitment, paid-in, fees, carry, gross proceeds and net DPI; apply evidence limits from D43-D48."],"videos":[{"id":"oZoxVuXrIUs","title":"How VC Funds Work ft Rajiv Raghunandan from Arali Ventures | How VCs think EP 2","by":"Rainmatter by Zerodha","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA net return and distribution definitions","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Review distribution definitions and fund-level net metrics, particularly paid-in rather than uncalled commitment."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"VC decision survey - selected review","url":"https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w22587/w22587.pdf","note":"Review sourcing/selection and sample limits from D46-D47, not universal current Indian practice."}],"exercise":"Fictional classroom fund in INR crore, not actual AIF terms. Preserve the original down-year exercise arithmetic: commitment 400; investments 300 across 20 equal positions of 15; flat management fees of 2 percent of 400 annually in years 1-5 and 2 percent of the fixed original investment cost of 300 annually in years 6-10. Fees are therefore 40+30=70. Total called capital is exactly 370, funding investments plus fees; the remaining 30 is uncalled, not an asset or paid-in denominator. No other expenses, tax, debt, recycling, follow-on, preferred-return hurdle or catch-up. All positions are fully realized and all distributions are aggregated into one terminal event; there is no residual NAV. Do not compute IRR because cash-flow dates are deliberately omitted. Whole-fund carry is 20 percent of gross terminal proceeds above all 370 contributed capital, with zero carry if proceeds are below that threshold. Base outcomes: 12 zeros, five at 1x, two at 3x, one at 12x. Compute fees, gross proceeds 345, carry zero, LP net 345 and net DPI = TVPI = 345/370 = 0.93243, RVPI zero. Sensitivity changes only the single winner to 25x: gross 540, carry 34, LP net 506 and net DPI = TVPI = 506/370 = 1.36757. The winner alone produces 375 gross, above 300 investment cost and 370 paid-in but below 400 commitment. It is not a guarantee of LP net return because other outcomes and carry also matter. Gross portfolio MOIC uses 300, not 370 or 400. Deliverable: a reconciliation table on both bases and a paragraph on which conclusion changes with the winner. Review the evidence limitations in D43-D48 rather than apply universal channel, churn or fund-returner rules.","quiz":[{"q":"What are total fees under the stated two-stage fee basis?","o":["60, using invested capital as the fee basis in all ten years.","70, from five years at 8 plus five years at 6.","40, omitting fees in years six through ten.","80, using committed capital as the fee basis in all ten years."],"a":1},{"q":"How much is contributed capital under the explicit model?","o":["400, substituting total commitment for actual called capital.","330, adding the uncalled balance rather than the fee budget.","370, comprising 300 investments and 70 fees, with 30 uncalled.","300, excluding fee contributions from the LP paid-in denominator."],"a":2},{"q":"What are base-case gross terminal proceeds?","o":["345, from 75 at 1x, 90 at 3x and 180 at 12x.","370, using investment plus fee contributions as the proceeds total.","525, counting the 12x winner proceeds a second time.","540, substituting the separate 25x winner sensitivity."],"a":0},{"q":"What carry is paid in the base case?","o":["9, charging 20 percent of the 45 gain over investment cost instead of paid-in.","Zero, because gross proceeds 345 are below the 370 capital-return threshold.","69, charging 20 percent of gross proceeds without returning LP capital.","14, treating the management-fee amount as performance profit."],"a":1},{"q":"What is base-case net DPI?","o":["345/400 = 0.8625, using uncalled commitment in the denominator.","345/370 = 0.93243, with no carry and no residual NAV.","370/345 = 1.07246, reversing distributions and paid-in.","345/300 = 1.15, using portfolio investment cost instead of LP paid-in."],"a":1},{"q":"After changing the one winner to 25x, gross proceeds are:","o":["540, adding 375 winner proceeds to the unchanged 165 from other winners.","345, retaining the original 12x winner despite the changed assumption.","375, omitting proceeds from the five 1x and two 3x positions.","570, adding the uncalled commitment as if it were investment proceeds."],"a":0},{"q":"Under that sensitivity, what carry is paid?","o":["34, equal to 20 percent of (540 - 370).","108, charging 20 percent on all 540 including returned capital.","48, returning only 300 investment cost before the carry calculation.","28, treating the entire 400 commitment as contributed capital."],"a":0},{"q":"Under that sensitivity, what net DPI does the LP receive?","o":["540/370 = 1.45946 before deducting carry from LP proceeds.","506/300 = 1.68667 using investment cost as the net LP denominator.","506/400 = 1.265 using uncalled commitment as the denominator.","506/370 = 1.36757 after deducting 34 carry."],"a":3},{"q":"Which comparison describes the 25x winner gross proceeds of 375?","o":["Above investment cost 300 and paid-in 370, but below committed capital 400.","Below paid-in because the uncalled 30 must first be added to the 370 capital call.","Above all three bases because investment cost, paid-in and commitment are equivalent.","Equal to net LP proceeds because no other holding or carry affects fund distributions."],"a":0},{"q":"Can this timing-free terminal model determine an annual IRR?","o":["No; IRR can never be calculated for a fully realized fund even if dated cash flows are later supplied.","No; dated contributions and distributions are needed, even though multiples are computable.","Yes; net DPI alone uniquely fixes annual return independently of fund duration or cash-flow dates.","Yes; total management fees identify all the dates and amounts of every LP contribution."],"a":1},{"q":"At 3.5 TVPI and 0.8 DPI on the same net basis, what is RVPI?","o":[".8 times paid-in, because residual and distributed values must match.","3.5 times paid-in, because total value excludes all distributions.","2.7 times paid-in, an unrealized value estimate rather than a distribution.","4.3 times paid-in, adding distributed and total values a second time."],"a":2},{"q":"With one initial outflow and one final inflow only, which has higher annualized return?","o":["2.5x over 4 years, compared with 5x over 11 years.","Cannot compare even with the stated single-outflow/single-inflow assumption.","5x over 11 years, because a larger multiple always gives a higher annualized rate.","They are equal because both final proceeds exceed their initial cost."],"a":0},{"q":"What do the supplied power-law readings support carefully?","o":["Exactly two investments will return at least 20x whenever a fund holds 25 positions.","A fitted or assumed alpha from one analysis establishes all future venture outcomes.","Outlier exposure matters, but portfolio rules depend on data and model assumptions.","Every fund must select only bets that individually return its entire committed capital."],"a":2},{"q":"Two funds reach 2x DPI in 6 and 12 years. What is needed to compare IRR?","o":["The dated contributions and distributions, plus any residual NAV at the comparison date.","Only the ending multiple, because timing has no role in a money-weighted return.","Only the completion year, because identical DPI uniquely determines every cash-flow pattern.","Only the commitment amount, because uncalled capital is always an invested cash outflow."],"a":0},{"q":"What does a PME comparison need?","o":["A stated method, dated fund flows, residual NAV and an appropriate index/currency basis.","Only committed capital and the highest public-index price in the reporting period.","Only the distribution multiple because all PME methods produce that same ratio.","Only a fund IRR and an index point-to-point return across the same calendar years."],"a":0},{"q":"How should an unrealized 10x mark be read?","o":["As cash already returned because the latest financing round has a published price.","As a valuation estimate to assess for date, rights, method and risks, not a completed sale.","As guaranteed exit proceeds whenever a third-party auditor reviewed the fund accounts.","As necessarily worthless because every unsold investment must have zero economic value."],"a":1},{"q":"Under the supplied Carta definition, what makes sourcing proprietary?","o":["Any inbound deck because receiving a message proves other investors have not seen it.","Any investment the fund ultimately wins regardless of how broadly it was marketed.","Any opportunity introduced by a trusted person even if all competing funds can bid.","An exclusive/off-market opportunity not broadly available, not merely a friendly referral."],"a":3},{"q":"What is a real trade-off of a narrower mandate?","o":["It must fail because every fund requires exposure to every possible industry winner.","It removes diversification and portfolio construction from every investment decision.","It guarantees outperformance because fewer categories always improve selection skill.","It can improve focus while excluding opportunities; assess fit, edge and opportunity cost."],"a":3},{"q":"What is a useful thesis falsifier?","o":["A forecast made unfalsifiable by treating every negative signal as proof of future upside.","A performance target that is always revised after the original threshold is missed.","Observable contrary evidence with threshold, data source, horizon and a decision response.","A broad statement that the team will change its view whenever the story feels weaker."],"a":2},{"q":"200 companies are flagged and ten first calls occur. What should be checked before changing triggers?","o":["Tighten trigger thresholds first, treating unbooked calls as screened-out companies before separating rejection from capacity.","Increase lead volume first, assuming the current call rate reflects available partner capacity rather than constraints.","Fit, stage definitions, capacity and acceptance, since counts alone do not identify the bottleneck.","Reduce screening volume first, using the call count as the target without checking qualified opportunities that were missed."],"a":2},{"q":"Founders report that your call came before other funds. What does that show?","o":["Use it as the main performance outcome without checking allocation, investment price or retained ownership.","Use the interview percentage without recording nonresponses or the comparability of the founders contacted.","Treat early contact as sufficient evidence of selection edge before examining downstream decisions and outcomes.","A potentially useful timing proxy needing validation, not proof of allocation or realized return edge."],"a":3},{"q":"Two nonpaying users praise a product. What is a careful conclusion?","o":["Infer repeat engagement from positive interview descriptions before examining behavior over a defined period.","Reject demand because these users pay nothing, without examining an advertiser, employer or other payer model.","Limited positive feedback; test representative behavior and the actual payer/revenue model.","Treat the praise as willingness-to-pay evidence without checking who pays or whether incentives affected the feedback."],"a":2},{"q":"A founder declines to share raw cohort data. What is a proportionate next step?","o":["Mark the refusal itself as negative retention evidence before checking whether a permitted verification route exists.","Accept a selected customer-reference list as a complete substitute without testing whether it covers churned users.","Sign an NDA and treat the unresolved cohort claim as verified without obtaining any acceptable evidence.","Seek permitted aggregate or redacted verification and assess any remaining material evidence gap."],"a":3},{"q":"Three of five selected users love the product but annual churn is 50 percent. What follows?","o":["Conclude that PMF is real but narrow before checking whether interviewed and churned customers represent the same segment.","Use the annual churn figure to dismiss all favorable user evidence without separating cohorts and product use cases.","Check sample selection, definitions and cohorts; neither narrow PMF nor churn causes are proven.","Prefer the three positive interviews over the churn figure without checking selection bias or churn definitions."],"a":2},{"q":"A workflow marketed as AI uses manual operations. What should be investigated?","o":["Recalculate margins assuming no labor cost was recorded before checking the existing cost classification.","Accept current margins as scalable without checking whether manual work grows linearly with transaction volume.","Treat the manual workflow as a failed automation thesis without testing its planned transition, cost or quality limits.","Actual claims, labor, quality, margins and scaling assumptions before deciding materiality or intent."],"a":3}]},"50":{"objectives":["Model option-pool timing on explicit fully diluted denominators and distinguish SAFE cap-implied ownership from final priced-round ownership."],"videos":[{"id":"D5s_DqfBfUk","title":"The 3 Term-Sheet Clauses That Actually Cost Founders (and LPs) Money | Syndicate Room EP27","by":"AdValorem - Invest. Build. Give.","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC post-money SAFE user guide","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/assets/ycdc/Primer%20for%20post-money%20safe%20v1.1-2af8129e12effd9638eeab383b7309142c8f415e5cdb0bc210d573f779177a1c.pdf","note":"Read quick start, Q&A B and C, and Appendix II Example 1. Cap-implied ownership is before new financing money and its new pool; compare actual share prices. U.S. educational mechanics, not authorization or an Indian legal template."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO - term-sheet negotiation","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/negotiating-term-sheets/","note":"Read valuation/dilution, liquidation, governance and advisor caveats. Older U.S. practice commentary, not universal Indian terms or rules."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NVCA model-document index","url":"https://nvca.org/model-legal-documents/","note":"Index of U.S. model forms and alternatives. Read relevant alternatives with their definitions and footnotes; these U.S. templates are not a substitute for current Indian requirements or qualified local counsel."}],"exercise":"Fictional educational cap tables in INR crore, not real Indian instruments, legal advice or authority to issue securities. Founders initially own 100 shares; no options, promises, warrants, convertibles or other holders. Scenario A: priced round at 40 pre-money including a new pool, 10 new money; the unallocated pool is exactly 15 percent of fully diluted POST-closing shares and is funded entirely within the pre-money capitalization. Investor ownership is 20 percent; pool 15 percent; founders 65 percent. Solve actual shares and round price. Scenario B: same 40 pre-money and 10 raise, first close with founders 80 percent/investor 20 percent, then create a new pool equal to 15 percent of final fully diluted shares, diluting both holders proportionately. Final founders 68 percent/investor 17 percent/pool 15 percent. This is a negotiated alternative, not a claim that changing a label leaves the investor's original economics intact. Scenario C is separate, NOT an equal-cash comparison: raise 10 on one standard-style post-money SAFE with cap 50, no discount, no existing pool and no other instruments; then raise 25 at 100 fully diluted pre-money including SAFE conversion and a new unallocated pool targeted at 15 percent POST-closing. No pro rata follow-on. Use the lower actual SAFE or new-money share price; under these inputs the cap binds. Before the new pool and priced money, SAFE-implied ownership is 20 percent and founders 80 percent; after both, founders 52 percent, SAFE 13 percent, new money 20 percent and pool 15 percent. Total financing in C is 35, not 10. State both stages, do not call SAFE ownership fixed forever or equal to issued stock at signing. Deliverable: three share-count tables whose percentages each total 100, with pre-money/post-money and economic-versus-voting-control distinctions. No specific Indian CCPS-law requirement is inferred from U.S. templates or a secondary explainer.","quiz":[{"q":"In scenario A, what are final founders/investor/pool percentages?","o":["65/20/15, with the pool measured after closing but funded within pre-money.","68/17/15, applying the different pro-rata post-closing pool scenario instead.","68/20/12, treating the pool as fifteen percent of pre-round rather than post-round shares.","80/20/0, pricing the new money but omitting the required new pool."],"a":0},{"q":"In scenario B, who bears the post-closing pool dilution?","o":["Neither holder, since reserving a pool does not change any fully diluted ownership calculation.","Only the investor, leaving founders at their initial post-financing eighty percent.","Both existing founders and the just-funded investor, yielding 68 and 17 percent.","Only founders, retaining the investors twenty percent despite the stated pro-rata issuance."],"a":2},{"q":"What does 1x non-participating preferred mean in a simplified single-class exit?","o":["Receive the original investment even when net available equity proceeds cannot cover it.","Choose preference or as-converted proceeds, subject to available assets and specified priority.","Receive the original investment and then the full as-converted share of remaining proceeds.","Convert at every exit regardless of whether the preference gives a higher available payout."],"a":1},{"q":"Why compare pool timing and valuation numerically?","o":["Pool timing always matters more than a five-percent valuation gap regardless of the pool size.","A higher headline valuation necessarily offsets any increase in the pool required pre-money.","An identical target pool percentage necessarily leaves new-investor economics unchanged under both timings.","Their ownership effects depend on size, denominator and terms, not a universal priority rule."],"a":3},{"q":"What does a triggered full-ratchet clause generally change, subject to its actual exceptions?","o":["The conversion price in proportion to new shares issued using a weighted-average formula.","The company's total share count without changing the protected investor's conversion entitlement.","The protected conversion price to the specified lower issue price, not a fixed ownership multiplier.","The original cash investment amount so the investor receives extra principal repayment automatically."],"a":2},{"q":"How is a standard-style cap-only post-money SAFE priced at equity financing?","o":["Apply an assumed twenty-percent discount despite the stated absence of any discount term.","Compare the actual SAFE Price with the new-money price and use the investor-favorable share result.","Always use investment divided by cap as the holder's final post-financing percentage without later dilution.","Compare headline valuation numbers alone without checking their different capitalization denominators."],"a":1},{"q":"In scenario C, what are final founder/SAFE/new-money/pool percentages?","o":["52/13/20/15, after the binding-cap SAFE is diluted by new pool and new money.","45/20/20/15, protecting the SAFE from a pool increase and priced financing that dilute it.","65/0/20/15, treating the earlier SAFE proceeds as if they created no conversion shares.","64/16/20/0, applying new-money dilution but omitting the required new option pool."],"a":0},{"q":"What do pro rata rights normally provide under their specified contractual scope?","o":["A right to receive both liquidation preference and common participation regardless of share terms.","An option to invest additional money in a covered round, not automatic free anti-dilution or control.","A guarantee of full investment allocation at every future round irrespective of the agreed limits.","An automatic share top-up without paying for the added ownership after each financing."],"a":1},{"q":"What should a fully diluted conversion model include?","o":["Only the latest financing terms, assuming earlier notes and side letters have identical conversion mechanics.","All SAFEs converted mechanically at their caps without checking lower round-price conversion rights.","Only issued shares, leaving ungranted pools and promised options out of the pricing denominator.","Actual instruments, caps/prices, pools, promises, interest where applicable and relevant side-letter terms."],"a":3},{"q":"What can these U.S.-style calculations establish about a real Indian round?","o":["That Indian law requires every priced venture investment to use the same preferred-share structure.","That a cap-table percentage alone establishes legal voting control and priority in every exit.","Illustrative economics only; instrument validity, rights and compliance require current local-law review.","That a published U.S. SAFE can be used unchanged for an Indian company without legal review."],"a":2}]},"51":{"objectives":["Reconcile a three-round as-converted cap table and a preference waterfall with specified pool denominator, seniority and employee-pool treatment."],"videos":[{"id":"jhpTdGqY1Rw","title":"How to understand the YC SAFE and dilution | Ep 35 | Uncapped Notes","by":"Hustle Fund","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC post-money SAFE user guide","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/assets/ycdc/Primer%20for%20post-money%20safe%20v1.1-2af8129e12effd9638eeab383b7309142c8f415e5cdb0bc210d573f779177a1c.pdf","note":"Read quick start, Q&A B and C, and Appendix II Example 1. Cap-implied ownership is before new financing money and its new pool; compare actual share prices. U.S. educational mechanics, not authorization or an Indian legal template."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO - term-sheet negotiation","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/negotiating-term-sheets/","note":"Read valuation/dilution, liquidation, governance and advisor caveats. Older U.S. practice commentary, not universal Indian terms or rules."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NVCA model-document index","url":"https://nvca.org/model-legal-documents/","note":"Index of U.S. model forms and alternatives. Read relevant alternatives with their definitions and footnotes; these U.S. templates are not a substitute for current Indian requirements or qualified local counsel."}],"exercise":"Fictional educational share model in INR crore using explicitly assumed U.S.-style post-money SAFE mechanics, not an executable Indian security or legal advice. Start with founders 100 shares and no other securities/options/promises. Seed SAFE 1 invests 2 at cap 20; SAFE 2 invests 3 at cap 30; no discount, interest, MFN or pro rata side letter. Both caps bind at Series A. Before its new pool/new money, SAFE-implied ownership is 10 percent each and founders 80 percent, not issued voting stock. Series A raises 25 at 100 fully diluted pre-money INCLUDING conversions and a NEW unallocated pool equal to 12 percent of POST-A fully diluted shares; the pool is funded within the pre-money capitalization. After A: founders 54.4 percent, each seed 6.8 percent, A 20 percent, pool 12 percent. Series B raises 80 at 320 fully diluted pre-money; no top-up, grants, exercises, new instruments or follow-on. After B: founders 43.52 percent, each seed 5.44 percent, A 16 percent, B 20 percent, pool 9.6 percent. Founders cross below 50 percent at B, not below 25 percent in these rounds; economic percentage alone does not establish voting/board control. At exit, assume all pool shares remain unissued and are cancelled without consideration, so renormalize outstanding holders by 90.4 percent; do not pay a fictional pool. Exit proceeds 600 or 150 are net equity proceeds available after all debt, tax and transaction expenses. Each investor has optional 1x non-participating preference on original investment (seed 2 and 3, A 25, B 80), no dividends or participation. B is senior to A; A senior to the seed classes, which rank equally with each other. Elect preference OR convert, not both. With all classes converted at 600, payouts are 600 times each holder's final FD percentage divided by 90.4; each must prefer this to its preference given other elections. At 150, B takes 80, A takes 25, seeds convert and the remaining 45 splits founders 80 percent/seed 1 ten percent/seed 2 ten percent: 36, 4.5, 4.5. Check all election incentives, priorities and payout totals. Deliverable: per-round share-count/ownership grid, both exit waterfalls and one sensitivity where a legal/economic assumption changes. A spreadsheet suffices; no software signup, real fundraising, document sending or account action is needed.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D51 cap-table lab\"><h3>Rebuild the shares and test every election</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d51-cap-table-practice.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable D51 cap-table workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d51-cap-table-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">inspect its seven-sheet print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own share-count grid first, then compare the worked formulas. The original financing, pool and preference assumptions above remain the case; this is not a generalized legal instrument or real financing model.</p><p>The workbook reconciles implied SAFE conversion counts, post-A/post-B fully diluted shares, actual conversion prices and pool-cancelled exit ownership. Its exit sheets enumerate all sixteen exclusive preference/conversion profiles and compare each investor's payout with a unilateral change, holding other elections fixed. At 600 all convert. At 150 B and A take preferences, seeds convert, and founders/seed1/seed2/A/B receive 36/4.5/4.5/25/80. A non-participating holder never takes both its preference and a residual conversion share.</p><p>Change only the yellow sensitivity net-exit cell, initially 300. The stable outcome there is B preference with all others converted: 136/17/17/50/80. Test exhausted proceeds and exact ties at 100, 20, 3 and zero; all rows still allocate the entire non-negative exit without paying an unissued pool. Equal-ranking elected seed preferences split an inadequate available amount in proportion to their original claims. Multiple weakly stable profiles are shown as ties, not a fabricated unique choice.</p><p>Native LibreOffice recalculation passed 47 share/price checks, 339 checks across the three baseline exit tables, 452 checks across four changed-exit tables and 113 restored-baseline checks against an independent exact-rational evaluator. Every profile checks five holder payouts, the total and the no-profitable-unilateral-change test. All seven PDF sheets were visually inspected. This is authored execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner transfer, security enforceability or current Indian legal/tax advice. No macros, external links, account, securities issuance, document sending or learner-progress changes are created.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Under the binding caps before new pool/new money, total SAFE-implied ownership is:","o":["16.7 percent, dividing total investment by the larger cap without applying each agreement.","25 percent, comparing the two investments with the smaller cap as if terms matched.","20 percent, adding 2/20 and 3/30 on the specified post-money basis.","18.18 percent, sequentially diluting the first post-money SAFE with the second."],"a":2},{"q":"At Series A, the specified new pool dilutes whom?","o":["Only founders because post-money SAFEs are protected from every future pool increase.","Only seed holders because their securities convert immediately before the pool is adopted.","Founders and converting seed holders within pre-money; new A money receives twenty percent.","All holders proportionately including A while A retains its originally priced twenty percent."],"a":2},{"q":"After A, founder ownership is:","o":["48 percent, subtracting twenty seed points, twenty A points and twelve pool points from founders initial 100 percent.","54.4 percent, equal to eighty percent of the sixty-eight percent remaining after A and its pool.","56.32 percent, treating twelve percent as a pre-A pool share rather than the stated post-A target.","64 percent, applying the A money dilution but omitting the new twelve-percent post-A pool."],"a":1},{"q":"After B with no further pool change, founder ownership is:","o":["34.4 percent, subtracting twenty percentage points instead of applying proportional dilution.","54.4 percent, leaving founders undiluted while issuing twenty percent new B ownership.","25 percent, inferring a threshold crossing without calculating the actual new share count.","43.52 percent, multiplying the post-A 54.4 percent by eighty percent."],"a":3},{"q":"What is needed to compute the seed share-price discount to A?","o":["Only the seed investment size, because the next round's share price does not affect any discount.","Only twenty divided by one hundred, because all capitalization denominators are identical by definition.","Both actual share prices on their defined capitalizations, not just the cap/pre-money ratio.","Only the final ownership percentages, ignoring how much money purchased each investor's shares."],"a":2},{"q":"At the 600 exit, how is B's all-converted ownership calculated?","o":["20 percent of available proceeds while assigning 9.6 percent to a pool that receives no consideration.","A 20/90.4 fraction of proceeds, about 22.12 percent, after cancelling the unissued pool.","80/600 percent of available proceeds, treating preference amount as conversion ownership.","25 percent of available proceeds by dividing twenty by the founders original eighty percent."],"a":1},{"q":"At the specified 150 exit, B and A take preferences. What do founders and each seed receive?","o":["36, 4.5 and 4.5 after both seed holders choose conversion into the forty-five residual.","65.28, 8.16 and 8.16 by converting all classes and ignoring B's and A's preference elections.","40, 2 and 3 after forcing both seeds to take lower preferences without testing their elections.","36, 2 and 3 by mixing converted founder proceeds with seed preferences and leaving proceeds unallocated."],"a":0},{"q":"For an eight-percent note over two years, how should interest be modeled?","o":["Use the actual simple/compound accrual and cash-versus-conversion terms in the note.","Assume all interest is paid in cash at conversion even when the contract specifies conversion.","Automatically compound it and convert every interest amount at the principal price regardless of terms.","Ignore interest in ownership modeling because only cash principal can ever create conversion shares."],"a":0},{"q":"A five investment at fifty post-money cap implies ten percent at what stage?","o":["As already-issued voting equity at signing before any conversion event or corporate action.","As a fixed exit share even if the actual round-price conversion gives the holder more shares.","Before subsequent new-money/pool dilution when the cap binds, not an eternal final ownership floor.","After every priced round regardless of new financing amounts or pool increases required by its terms."],"a":2},{"q":"Which threshold conclusion is supported by this model?","o":["Founders fall below fifty percent at B and never below twenty-five percent in these rounds.","Founders fall below fifty percent at A because its headline valuation exceeds both seed caps.","Founders keep voting control automatically at B because economic ownership is above twenty-five percent.","Founders fall below twenty-five percent at B because all rounds reduce ownership by fifty percent."],"a":0}]},"52":{"objectives":["Separate an investor-specific return-based entry ceiling from market and cash-flow indications, using consistent enterprise/equity values."],"videos":[{"id":"ynFT3RFIf4w","title":"Valuation: The \"Venture Capital\" (VC) method","by":"Luke Stein","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"IPEV 2025 valuation guidelines","url":"https://www.privateequityvaluation.com/Portals/0/Documents/Guidelines/2025%20IPEV%20Valuation%20Guidelines.pdf","note":"Read scope/application, sections 1.1-1.6, 2.1-2.3, 3.2-3.4 and 3.7. Fair value uses market-participant assumptions at the measurement date; a personal target return does not itself establish reporting fair value. DCF can apply to startups but is sensitive to forecasts and terminal value."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Bill Payne scorecard method - scope and worked example","url":"https://angelcapitalassociation.org/blog/blog-scorecard-valuation-methodology-rev-2019-establishing-the-valuation-of-pre-revenue-start-up-companies/","note":"Read the method and its limitations: regional/sector/stage benchmark for pre-revenue or minimal-revenue ventures. Do not force a revenue-bearing Series A company into an unsuitable peer set. Historical U.S. dollar ranges are not current Indian benchmarks."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Equidam VC-method explanation","url":"https://www.equidam.com/vc-method-startup-valuation/","note":"Compare the vendor implementation with a transparent return/dilution model. Its stage-specific success/return assumptions are not universal rules; do not double-count dilution already embedded in a chosen rate."}],"exercise":"Default fictional teaching case in INR crore, not a real-company valuation or current market-data claim. An established-revenue SaaS firm has current ARR 8, debt 3 and surplus cash 1; no other assets, senior claims, preferences or dilution at the measurement date. Three approaches have DIFFERENT bases and forecasts and need not agree. Explain those differences; do not simply average their outputs. (1) Investor-specific VC entry ceiling: a conditional successful exit in year 6 has revenue 100, enterprise-value/revenue multiple 8, debt 30 and surplus cash 10, with no fees/other claims. Exit equity value is 780, not 800. Initial investor retains 60 percent of its purchased ownership by exit and makes no follow-on investment; all equity receives pro-rata proceeds. Required terminal gross investment multiple is 10x, not an annual rate. Maximum equity post-money today is 780*0.60/10=46.8 and pre-money is 36.8 for a 10 raise. This conditional hurdle calculation is not a probability-weighted return guarantee or automatically reporting fair value. (2) Current market cross-check: fictional comparable sales imply enterprise-value/current-ARR multiples 6 and 8 on the SAME accounting/ARR basis. Apply to current ARR then subtract current net debt 2: equity PRE-money indications 46 and 62. Do not mix exit-year revenue, funding-round equity valuations and enterprise multiples. These invented comps are not evidence of actual Indian transaction prices. (3) Illustrative operating DCF: year-end free cash flow to firm over years 1-6 is -4,-2,1,5,10,16; discount rate 25 percent, perpetual growth after year 6 is 3 percent. Terminal EV at year 6 is 16*1.03/(.25-.03). Discount all six cash flows plus terminal EV, then subtract current net debt 2 once. The DCF is a separate forecast, not an additional terminal value to add to the VC-method exit. Do not add financing proceeds to operating FCFF. Scorecard is an applicability critique, not a compulsory fourth number: this revenue-bearing company does not match Payne's pre/minimal-revenue benchmark. Deliverable: three labeled indications, sensitivity table across exit multiples/retention/return or DCF rate/growth, and an explanation of evidence gaps and which assumptions would justify a decision. A real-company extension must use dated retrievable sources and verified transaction/metric definitions; mark undisclosed consideration or revenue unknown, never invent two exits.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D52 valuation lab\"><h3>Reconcile three valuations without averaging away the differences</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d52-valuation-practice.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable D52 valuation workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d52-valuation-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">inspect its seven-sheet print view (PDF)</a>. Build independently first, then use Start, Inputs, VC, Comps, DCF, Sensitivity and Practice as a checking aid. The original fictional case, assessment and method boundaries are retained.</p><p>The conditional VC entry ceiling is equity post-money 46.8 and pre-money 36.8 crore, not a fair-value guarantee. Current invented EV/ARR comps give pre-money equity 46 and 62. The independent operating DCF gives enterprise value 25.188073 and equity pre-money 23.188073, with terminal PV about 77.96 percent of EV. Different forecasts, dates, risk and return bases explain why the numbers need not agree. Do not average or sum them, add VC exit value to the DCF, or add raise proceeds to operating FCFF.</p><p>Native LibreOffice matched an independent rational evaluator on 64 baseline bridge, ownership, DCF and sensitivity checks; 25 controlled-change checks covered current debt/cash, exit debt, retention and raise. An inserted Inputs row retained all 64 checks, and restoration passed 64 again. Ten invalid-input cases plus zero retention, infeasible raise, negative equity and a broken reference were tested. Invalid or undefined values stay unavailable; negative equity is not silently floored, and a mathematically balanced investor return is not proof that a raise can fit below the ceiling.</p><p>All seven print sheets and mobile/desktop links were visually inspected; saved XLSX caches checked. IPEV 2025 specified sections, the ACA/Payne scorecard article and the Equidam implementation were re-read. Fictional inputs are not verified market transactions. Scorecard remains an applicability critique for this established-revenue case; historical U.S. benchmark ranges and vendor stage probabilities are not adopted as current Indian evidence. This is authored native execution, not Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner transfer, actual investment approval or reporting fair value. No macros, external workbook links, accounts or learner-progress changes.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"With equity exit proceeds of 800 crore, a 10x target and no future dilution, what is maximum equity post-money?","o":["800 crore, treating the future equity exit proceeds as today's entry valuation.","90 crore, adding the proposed ten raise to a value already defined as post-money.","8 crore, dividing again by the ten-unit investment rather than solving post-money.","80 crore, dividing exit equity proceeds by the specified total return multiple."],"a":3},{"q":"For the same 800-crore equity exit and 10x target, what is maximum post-money with 40 percent future ownership dilution?","o":["32 crore, treating the forty percent diluted away as the fraction retained.","133.33 crore, dividing by retention rather than reducing the exit proceeds attributable.","48 crore, retaining sixty percent of the initial ownership through exit.","80 crore, omitting future dilution from the otherwise unchanged hurdle calculation."],"a":2},{"q":"In the default case, exit EV 800, debt 30 and surplus cash 10 produce how much exit equity?","o":["760 crore, subtracting cash along with debt from the stated enterprise value.","780 crore, subtracting debt and adding the specified surplus cash once.","820 crore, adding debt and subtracting cash as if solving enterprise value.","800 crore, assuming enterprise and equity value agree despite the given debt/cash."],"a":1},{"q":"In the default case, maximum post-money 46.8 with a 10-crore raise implies what pre-money?","o":["46.8 crore pre-money, treating post-money as a separate pre-investment estimate.","36.8 crore pre-money, because the investment is included in post-money.","28.08 crore pre-money, applying the stated ownership retention a second time.","56.8 crore pre-money, adding the investment instead of subtracting it."],"a":1},{"q":"Ten times over exactly six years, with no interim cash flows, means what annualized gross return?","o":["About 46.78 percent, from ten to the one-sixth power minus one.","About 166.67 percent, from dividing the ten-times multiple directly by six years.","About 100 percent, treating a ten-times terminal multiple as a doubling every year.","About 38.95 percent, using seven annual periods for a six-year holding period."],"a":0},{"q":"Why is DCF not automatically excluded just because a company is a startup?","o":["It can model projected cash flows, but forecast/rate/terminal assumptions may be highly uncertain.","It yields the same value as any VC target-return method when both use the same company name.","It uses observable prices alone, so forecasting uncertainty disappears once a rate is chosen.","It needs only a terminal revenue multiple, so detailed cash-flow assumptions can always be omitted."],"a":0},{"q":"For the current fictional comps, 6-8x ARR of 8 and net debt of 2 imply what equity pre-money range?","o":["48-64 crore, leaving enterprise-value outputs labeled as equity despite current net debt.","600-800 crore, applying current-ARR multiples to exit-year revenue instead of current ARR.","46-62 crore, after converting the enterprise-value range to equity value.","56-72 crore, adding the prospective ten raise but still calling the result pre-money."],"a":2},{"q":"How should Payne's scorecard fit this revenue-bearing Series A case?","o":["Apply its historical U.S. dollar valuation range directly as a current Indian rupee benchmark.","Treat the weighted score as audited reporting fair value independent of its underlying benchmark quality.","Explain the pre/minimal-revenue benchmark mismatch rather than force a misleading comparable score.","Use one funded peer's headline round valuation without matching stage, geography or security rights."],"a":2},{"q":"Which comparable-selection approach is most defensible?","o":["Use the highest recent funding-round equity valuation as an enterprise exit multiple for the target.","Match metrics, date, growth, margins, rights and markets; justify adjustments instead of a fixed nationality haircut.","Use domestic exits alone even when consideration, revenue basis and security rights are undisclosed.","Apply the same twenty-percent haircut to every global comp without examining individual differences."],"a":1},{"q":"What does an investor-specific 10x hurdle calculation establish?","o":["A portfolio-level ten-times outcome regardless of check sizes, failures, follow-ons, fees or carry.","An entry ceiling under its stated assumptions, not guaranteed portfolio returns or reporting fair value.","A market-participant fair-value conclusion even if its inputs represent only that investor's constraints.","An annual return input, so ten should be used as the discount rate for each projected year."],"a":1}]},"53":{"objectives":["Role-play a valuation negotiation with explicit return assumptions, cap-table effects and a legitimate no-deal outcome."],"videos":[{"id":"LhIU50Yh54M","title":"Understanding Valuation In Venture Capital | Part#1 | Comps, Checklists & Score Card","by":"Professor Claudia Zeisberger","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"NFX - eight fundraising negotiation skills","url":"https://www.nfx.com/post/8-top-skills-founders-startup-fundraising","note":"Read as a founder-side 2019 practitioner view, not guaranteed tactics or current Indian market norms. Compare pre/post-money, pool sizing and deadlines; do not misstate other investors' interest."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO - term-sheet negotiation","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/negotiating-term-sheets/","note":"Price, dilution, preference and governance interact. Review a hypothetical term package and seek relevant counsel for real agreements; no universally cheapest concession."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NFX Signal public directory","url":"https://signal.nfx.com","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Optional public investor-discovery reference. Directory presence, stated stage or check size is not a verified competing offer, transaction valuation, complete CRM or permission to contact anyone."}],"exercise":"Fictional solo role-play in INR crore, not a live negotiation or real term sheet. SaaS current ARR 8; 15 percent month-on-month growth is a reported short observation, NOT a forecast to extrapolate forever. Net cash burn is .6 per month and unrestricted cash is 8.4: 14 months of static runway before fees or operational changes. Raise 20. Founder opens at 120 pre-money and currently declines below 90 pre; no verified competing offer. Investor assumes year-6 net equity exit proceeds 1200, retains 60 percent of initial ownership through future rounds, invests no more later and requires 8x gross terminal cash return. Under these explicit assumptions its max post-money is 90, hence max pre-money 70. At 120 pre, expected successful-case gross MOIC on this model is 1200*.6/140=5.142857x; an 8x case at that price requires exit equity 1866.666667 if retention is unchanged. Neither forecast is a fact or fair-value certification. Equity exit proceeds are shared pro rata on an as-converted basis in this baseline; changing preference terms requires recomputing the relevant waterfall. Initially founders own everything, no options, SAFE, note or other holder. At either price, if a new pool equals 10 percent POST-closing and is funded within pre-money, investor owns 20/(pre+20), pool 10 percent and founders the remainder. Keep initial pool dilution separate from the future retention assumption. Script an exchange that tests assumptions, alternatives and verifiable evidence; then compare an explicit term package including pool basis, preference and governance. No agreement is a valid final outcome while founder minimum 90 and investor maximum 70 remain unchanged. Speed, more money, a board seat or a pro-rata right cannot erase a return constraint without a justified changed assumption or explicit mandate exception. Compare an extension request for a short deadline without inventing offers, promising a close or treating a fixed five-day extension as universal. A larger lower-priced round can help or harm founders depending on cash needs, milestones and dilution. Deliverable: private fictional decision log and NON-BINDING draft term summary or reasoned no-deal; no messages, invitations, signatures or real fundraising. State why static post-raise runway of 28.4/.6=47.33 months is only a constant-burn illustration, not a forecast under growth/hiring.","quiz":[{"q":"With exit equity 1200, retention 60 percent, an 8x target and a 20-crore raise, what is maximum pre-money?","o":["70 crore, since maximum post-money is ninety before subtracting the twenty raise.","130 crore, omitting dilution before subtracting the requested twenty raise.","90 crore, leaving the target post-money amount incorrectly labeled as pre-money.","40 crore, multiplying the exit by the forty percent ownership lost instead of retained."],"a":0},{"q":"At 120 pre-money and a 20-crore raise, what gross investment MOIC do the same successful-exit assumptions imply?","o":["About 6x, dividing by 120 pre-money instead of the investor's actual ownership denominator.","About 8x, assuming the stated target is achieved merely because the investor accepted the price.","About 5.14x, using 1200 times .6 divided by 140 post-money before any fund-level fees or carry.","About 8.57x, leaving out the specified future dilution of the investor's initial stake."],"a":2},{"q":"Founder minimum 90 pre-money and investor ceiling 70 pre-money remain unchanged. What is a valid outcome?","o":["Close at ninety pre because non-price friendliness automatically offsets the investor's return shortfall.","Close at eighty pre because splitting the gap satisfies both unchanged reservation prices.","Record seventy pre as agreed because the investor's spreadsheet is binding on the founder.","No deal or defer, unless evidence changes assumptions or an authorized exception changes constraints."],"a":3},{"q":"For a 20-crore investment, investor ownership of 25 percent versus 20 percent implies which post-money valuations?","o":["60 versus 80 crore, reporting the pre-money values as if they were post-money.","100 versus 80 crore, reversing which ownership percentage requires the higher total value.","80 versus 100 crore pre-money, adding no adjustment for the cash investment.","80 versus 100 crore, with corresponding pre-money values 60 versus 80 crore."],"a":3},{"q":"At 70 pre-money, a 20-crore raise and a 10 percent post-closing pool funded pre-money, what do founders retain?","o":["About 67.78 percent, after 22.22 percent investor ownership and the ten-percent pool.","About 57.78 percent, deducting the ten-percent pool twice after computing investor ownership.","70 percent, treating the investor's twenty cash units as twenty ownership points.","About 77.78 percent, deducting the investor only and omitting the new pool."],"a":0},{"q":"What does fifteen-percent MoM growth imply if mechanically compounded for twelve months?","o":["180 percent annual growth, adding monthly percentages instead of compounding them.","About 535 percent annual growth, reporting the final-to-initial ratio as the increase.","About 435 percent annual growth, a calculation rather than evidence it will persist.","About 15 percent annual growth, treating the monthly observation as a yearly percentage."],"a":2},{"q":"Cash of 8.4, monthly net burn of .6 and new cash of 20 imply what static runway after financing?","o":["About 33.33 months, excluding the existing cash while calling the calculation total runway.","About 47.33 months guaranteed despite the stated need to model growth, hiring and spending changes.","Fourteen months, excluding the new cash while calling the calculation post-financing runway.","About 47.33 months if burn stays constant and no fees/cash restrictions apply."],"a":3},{"q":"Which claim about competing offers belongs in the role-play?","o":["Assume an unverified verbal number includes the same pool, preference and closing conditions.","Treat a directory listing as a firm term sheet because the fund advertises the relevant stage.","State that none is verified and test alternatives without inventing another fund's commitment.","Present general interest as an unconditional offer when it would make the negotiating anchor stronger."],"a":2},{"q":"How should non-price concessions be assessed?","o":["Model dilution/exit economics and governance obligations together; no term is universally the cheapest trade.","Treat extra pro-rata rights as free because their future allocation and dilution effects are uncertain.","Treat a board seat as costless because it changes no line in the immediate ownership table.","Use faster signing to certify that an otherwise out-of-mandate price now meets the original return model."],"a":0},{"q":"What makes a larger lower-priced round reasonable for a founder?","o":["A lower price alone, which necessarily gives founders more absolute exit proceeds regardless of later performance.","A supported cash-and-milestone plan showing whether the added runway is worth the additional dilution.","More investor ownership alone, which necessarily improves founder incentives regardless of spending needs.","A larger bank balance alone, which necessarily removes future financing risk irrespective of burn changes."],"a":1}]},"54":{"objectives":["Run a fictional IC review with independent evidence, specific dissent, explicit conditions and testable forecasts, without confusing a vote with funding authority."],"videos":[{"id":"htDSPu09ciY","title":"Investment memorandum and how VCs make decisions, mistakes and biases","by":"Ask VC","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Malenko and coauthors - committee voting research","url":"https://www.hbs.edu/ris/Publication%20Files/21-131_c118f5f9-e553-4110-ab71-d29fa5bf0439.pdf","note":"Read abstract and survey section 2. Large U.S. multistage VC respondents used different rules by stage; the 2023 working-paper model does not prove one voting rule works everywhere or establish current Indian practice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Bessemer historical Shopify memo","url":"https://www.bvp.com/memos/shopify","note":"Read the historical request, risks and outcomes separately from later annotations. Published text has different pre-money descriptions; flag the conflict rather than silently selecting one. A selected successful case is not a base-rate study or evidence that every fund should imitate it."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Brier score for binary probability forecasts","url":"https://scores.readthedocs.io/en/latest/tutorials/Brier_Score.html","note":"Read the direct-probability definition and first example: mean squared probability error, lower is better. Do not confuse the ensemble correction with a single forecast, or a 1-10 confidence rating with a probability. Brier score is not pure calibration alone."}],"exercise":"Use the fictional D53 case or a memo you are already authorized to use; default to a solo written simulation, not a live external IC. In one page separate dated observed evidence, assumptions, missing checks, return math, the strongest case for and against, material risks and explicit falsifiers. Keep a fuller risk register behind the prioritized summary; a top-three summary is a teaching aid, not permission to omit a fourth critical legal or operational risk. For the mock procedure, write independent initial views before reading the sponsor's recommendation, assign an evidence-based challenger and record the strongest objection and response. Use a stated mock voting rule for this exercise; actual investment rules, quorum, vetoes and signing authority come from the fund's governing documents, not this lesson. No vote or conditional approval authorizes money movement. If retention is a condition, record metric/cohort/date/source, required result, owner, due date, verifier and what happens on failure; do not mark it satisfied without evidence. Allow defer or pass. Optional subjective confidence 1-10 is labeled as such, not treated as a calibrated probability or fund-performance metric. Separately record at least two precise binary forecasts with probabilities 0-1, resolution dates and observable criteria, such as whether a defined customer cohort exceeds a specified renewal threshold by its anniversary. Leave outcomes unresolved until evidence arrives. For a synthetic teaching check, probability .7 with outcome 0 has Brier error .49; with outcome 1 it is .09. Compare multiple resolved forecasts and relevant baselines; one realized deal or a high confidence label does not prove calibration or skill. Retain the original memo, contrary evidence, decision and later changes; distinguish process quality from outcome luck and avoid hindsight rewriting. Deliverable: one-page private mock memo, dissent/conditions log and forecast table, not outreach, a binding investment decision or a real payment.","quiz":[{"q":"What is the purpose of independent initial views in this mock IC?","o":["Replace documentary evidence with private conviction so the sponsor cannot challenge a weak forecast.","Guarantee superior returns by ensuring the committee has more dissenters than supporting members.","Eliminate any need for discussion once each participant has independently chosen an initial side.","Preserve evidence-based starting judgments before sponsor or senior-member opinions influence the discussion."],"a":3},{"q":"A retention check is an approval condition. What belongs in its record?","o":["A general intention to confirm retention after funding, with no specific evidence or pass criterion.","A positive market benchmark substituted for the company cohort evidence named in the condition.","Defined metric/cohort/source, threshold, owner, deadline, verifier and consequences if not met.","A verbal founder's assurance labeled complete because the committee already voted to approve conditionally."],"a":2},{"q":"The strongest dissent comes from a high-status partner. How should it be evaluated?","o":["Reject it automatically because independent judgment means ignoring experience and any historical results.","Test its evidence and mechanism, use relevant expertise and record the unresolved issue and response.","Count it twice without examining its substance so the voting total reflects the partner's seniority.","Accept it automatically because professional status proves the objection is correct in this particular case."],"a":1},{"q":"A participant writes confidence 8 out of 10. What can be concluded?","o":["It is a subjective rating unless mapped to a defined probabilistic event; it alone proves no calibration.","Averaging this rating with others produces a calibrated probability without shared event definitions.","It is automatically an eighty-percent probability of a specified investment outcome even without resolution criteria.","It establishes forecasting skill once a single favorable outcome occurs, even without a sample or baseline."],"a":0},{"q":"For a direct binary forecast p = .7 and resolved outcome y = 0, what is the Brier error?","o":["-.7, using a signed forecast error instead of the non-negative squared error.",".09, using the occurred-event result even though the defined event did not occur.",".49, from squaring the difference between the probability and the resolved outcome.",".7, treating the forecast probability itself as the squared probability error."],"a":2},{"q":"Fourteen risks are listed behind a top-three IC summary. What is appropriate?","o":["Discard every risk after the third because a short memo makes those risks immaterial by definition.","Treat the length of the risk list as sufficient evidence that diligence quality is high.","Assign equal probability and impact to all fourteen risks without examining company-specific evidence.","Prioritize material risks but retain the register and do not omit another critical unresolved issue."],"a":3},{"q":"How should the historical published Shopify memo be used?","o":["Take one successful selected memo as proof its voting process beats alternatives across all VC funds.","Separate original evidence from later annotations and flag conflicting price descriptions without hindsight certainty.","Treat later company metrics in retrospective comments as original 2010 evidence for the entry decision.","Resolve every original uncertainty using known later success and rewrite it as what investors knew then."],"a":1},{"q":"An IC approved every case seen in six months. What must be checked before diagnosing a low bar?","o":["Only final exits, because favorable returns would prove that no process review could be useful.","Number and stage of screened/IC cases, selection filters, mandate and actual evidence behind decisions.","Only vote counts, because a unanimous committee outcome independently establishes strong sourcing quality.","Only elapsed months, because a universal rejection rate applies at every point in every fund's funnel."],"a":1},{"q":"What voting rule applies to an actual investment decision?","o":["The fund's governing authority and applicable process; surveyed stage-specific practices are not universal permission.","Always unanimity because any dissent proves that the investment lacks sufficient evidence.","Always one enthusiastic champion because a selected U.S. study requires every committee to use that rule.","Always a simple majority because an educational mock vote overrides formal vetoes and signing authority."],"a":0},{"q":"What does the mock approval authorize outside this exercise?","o":["Nothing binding: real commitments, funding and communications require actual authority and satisfied conditions.","A founder message announcing the round as committed, because a classroom majority implies the investor's consent.","A payment once the memo is saved, because completion of documentation substitutes for spending approval.","A real term-sheet signature while conditions are unresolved, because the mock vote supplied blanket execution authority."],"a":0}]},"55":{"objectives":["Write comparable evidence-based invest/pass/defer analyses without forcing opposite conclusions or hiding unresolved metric definitions."],"videos":[{"id":"zecZ3ldtc4c","title":"Inside a VC Deal Memo: What Actually Goes In (and Why It Matters)","by":"Gabriel Jarrosson | Lobster Capital","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Flyer One Ventures - memo practice","url":"https://flyerone.vc/post/im-a-vc-and-heres-how-i-write-my-investment-memos","note":"Read as one fund's process: evidence, pre-parade/pre-mortem, product, team, market and references. Its roughly 3000-word memo and veto rule are not universal requirements."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"1752vc - investor memo guide","url":"https://www.1752.vc/learn/venture-capital-investment-memo-investor-guide","note":"Use its section checklist to separate evidence, assumptions, return scenarios and recommendation. Its stated lengths vary by firm; commercial examples and cited averages are not a mandatory fund policy."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Visible - public memo outline","url":"https://visible.vc/blog/investment-memo-template/","note":"Optional public fill-in outline, not a required signup or proof a completed template is fund-grade. Keep an independent risk/diligence record; do not assume a founder-facing fundraising brief is the same as an internal IC memo."}],"exercise":"Default private fictional comparison in INR crore, not a public recommendation about named companies. Two B2B SaaS cases, A and B, have hypothetical successful year-6 net equity exit proceeds 150 each; no special preferred rights, debt or fees remain beyond those reflected in those proceeds. Each offers a 5 investment with no follow-on; assume 70 percent retention of purchased ownership through later dilution. A offers 25 equity post-money; B offers 50 equity post-money. A implies 20 percent initial ownership, 14 percent exit ownership, 21 gross proceeds and 4.2x gross MOIC. B implies 10 percent initial, 7 percent exit, 10.5 proceeds and 2.1x. A teaching mandate requires at least 4x in this conditional scenario, not an annual rate, probability-weighted expectation or universal VC bar. A meets that arithmetic and B does not, but this alone does not clear diligence or authorize investment. Both have meaningful revenue; do not replace verification with a scorecard or a headline market multiple. Fictional A packet has a deck labeled 3 percent quarterly revenue churn and a cohort table labeled 8 percent monthly logo churn: reconcile metric definitions, denominator, cohorts, periods, date and gross-versus-net treatment before declaring contradiction or picking one. Neither figure is independently verified. B lacks a retrievable renewal-cohort record. Write a two-page provisional INVEST/DEFER analysis for A and a one-page PASS/DEFER analysis for B using the same evidence checklist; these page limits are exercise constraints only. A is allowed to remain defer/pass if material checks fail, and do not manufacture opposite answers. Record observed/supplied claims versus assumptions versus unknowns, dated sources for any real-company extension, terms, product/customer evidence, unit economics, market-sizing basis, return scenarios, risks/falsifiers and an uncertainty register with owner, feasible test, cost, timing and decision impact. Label assumptions, unknowns and unverified claims rather than invent customer quotes, calls or contracts. End each memo with what evidence or terms would change the decision. Preserve contrary evidence, not only future winners; use a later revision log, not hindsight editing of the original. Deliverable: private draft memos plus comparison note, not an actual fund decision, disclosure, outreach or payment.","quiz":[{"q":"In the fictional A offer, what conditional gross investment MOIC follows from the stated inputs?","o":["4.2x, from twenty-percent initial ownership times seventy-percent retention times 150 exit divided by 5 invested.","5.25x, dividing the original investment by pre-money twenty rather than post-money twenty-five.","2.1x, applying B's fifty post-money terms instead of A's twenty-five post-money terms.","6x, keeping the initial twenty-percent stake unchanged through the stated future dilution."],"a":0},{"q":"What does A's 4.2x result establish against the teaching 4x bar?","o":["That the investment is authorized because a hypothetical return formula crossed its stated threshold.","That the full fund's net return exceeds 4x regardless of other investments, fees, carry or follow-ons.","That this conditional scenario clears its arithmetic bar, not that diligence is complete or returns guaranteed.","That the expected probability-weighted investment return is 4.2x even though no scenario probabilities were given."],"a":2},{"q":"A deck says 3 percent churn and a table says 8 percent. What should the memo do first?","o":["Automatically choose three percent because a founder-issued presentation is authoritative over the cohort table.","Average them to five-and-a-half percent, treating differing definitions as independent estimates of one metric.","Reconcile period, cohort, metric, denominator and gross/net treatment, preserving both sources and unresolved gaps.","Automatically choose eight percent because the larger number must be the more accurate diligence result."],"a":2},{"q":"How should the two memos reach their recommendations?","o":["Force one invest and one pass even if material diligence gaps make both investments unjustified.","Use the same evidence standard and permit defer or matching conclusions when the evidence supports them.","Assign the larger company invest because a larger headline post-money necessarily means less investment risk.","Give the cheaper company a final invest recommendation solely because its conditional MOIC is higher."],"a":1},{"q":"What is a useful uncertainty-register entry?","o":["A confidence adjective replacing the missing measurement so the core memo can appear complete.","A list of unknowns marked low risk whenever resolving them would delay the desired closing date.","A generic mitigation statement without identifying who will test the issue or what evidence would matter.","An explicit unknown with owner, feasible test, cost/time and decision impact, including if it cannot be resolved."],"a":3},{"q":"How should memo length be chosen?","o":["Follow the actual audience/process and preserve material detail; the exercise page limit is not a universal rule.","Use page count alone to approve completeness even when major terms or contradictory evidence are missing.","Always limit every growth-stage memo to three pages because compression proves the investment thesis is correct.","Always use the longest published template because more sections necessarily establish greater diligence quality."],"a":0},{"q":"A TAM claim says 50 billion. What additional analysis is needed?","o":["A larger top-down headline that makes the opportunity seem sufficient for any proposed entry price.","A five-year target revenue alone, relabeled TAM without estimating total potential customer spending.","An assumed market share without checking addressable customer counts, pricing or scope of the estimate.","Definition/date/source plus bottom-up customer and spending bounds, serviceable market and plausible obtainable sales."],"a":3},{"q":"What is the purpose of a pass memo and stated flip conditions?","o":["Preserve contemporaneous reasons and evidence needed for re-evaluation, while learning from passes and investments.","Treat every pass as a permanent ban regardless of changing terms, evidence or the fund's mandate.","Guarantee the fund re-enters whenever a company later announces a higher headline funding valuation.","Rewrite the original pass after later success so it looks like the eventual outcome was already anticipated."],"a":0},{"q":"What is supportable about an assigned speaker's memo philosophy without a verified transcript?","o":["Use the quiz's old answer key itself as the source for what the speaker said in the video.","Treat another author's memo article as proof that the assigned speaker made exactly the same point.","State the module principle on its own evidence, not a claimed quotation or attribution to the unreviewed video.","Attribute a familiar industry maxim to the speaker because it matches the video's general title."],"a":2},{"q":"How should a material risk be handled after it is listed?","o":["Call it the most common failure across all funds without a study defining sample and failure categories.","Assess decision impact, evidence and action/conditions; documentation alone does not mean it was addressed.","Drop it from the recommendation when no cheap test exists, because expensive uncertainty is not actionable.","Treat mentioning it in an appendix as sufficient mitigation even if nobody evaluates its consequences."],"a":1}]},"56":{"objectives":["Recompute simple and multiclass participating-preference waterfalls with explicit pool treatment, priority, elections and inclusive caps; review D50-D55 through applied evidence."],"videos":[{"id":"YyYwg-f46PY","title":"How to Raise Money from a Venture Investor","by":"a 16 z","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO - preferred stock and participation","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/glossary/preferred-stock/","note":"Read the participating versus non-participating distinction. Rights are negotiated; U.S. explanatory material does not establish Indian law or the terms of a real security."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO - liquidation preference","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/glossary/liquidation-preference/","note":"Priority determines who is paid first; use actual instruments, available net proceeds and explicit conversion choices. Model this exercise's stated fictional terms, not a universal legal waterfall."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC post-money SAFE primer","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/assets/ycdc/Primer%20for%20post-money%20safe%20v1.1-2af8129e12effd9638eeab383b7309142c8f415e5cdb0bc210d573f779177a1c.pdf","note":"Review new-pool/new-money dilution, share-price conversion and liquidation-capitalization distinctions. D51 has already converted its SAFEs into preferred classes; do not apply an unconverted-SAFE liquidity formula at exit. A private spreadsheet suffices, no account signup required."}],"exercise":"Two DISTINCT fictional cash-sale models in INR crore. Proceeds are net equity cash after debt, taxes and transaction expenses; no dividends, warrants, forced conversion or other claims. Model S preserves the original simple illustration: B invested 80 and owns 20 percent of OUTSTANDING as-converted shares; all other 80 percent is issued common, with no unused pool and no other preferred holders. B can take 2x original investment(160) then participate at 20 percent in residual cash, or waive preference and convert for 20 percent of all proceeds. At exits 200/400/1200, uncapped B receives 168/208/368 and other common receives 32/192/832. For exits at least 160, participation exceeds conversion by 128; no voluntary conversion yields MORE under these assumptions. With a 3x TOTAL payout cap including the 160 preference, cap is 240, reached on participation at exit 560; conversion ties 240 at exit 1200 and wins ABOVE 1200. Do not interpret 3x as a cap on participation alone.\nModel L is the actual link to corrected D51 and has different results. Use its post-B fully diluted stakes: founders 43.52 percent, seeds 5.44 percent each, A 16 percent, B 20 percent, unused unissued pool 9.6 percent. Cancel the pool for no consideration at exit. Seed original investments 2 and 3, A 25, B 80. Change ONLY B to 2x participating preferred, senior to A 1x non-participating; A senior to seeds 1x non-participating, which rank equally with each other. B participates in residual alongside founders and those older classes that elect conversion; older classes taking preference do not also participate. B may instead fully convert and waive its preference/participation rights. Decide each class's preference/conversion consistently and reconcile total payouts. At 200 all older classes take preferences(A 25, seeds 2 and 3); residual 10 after those and B's 160 is shared by founder and B at 43.52:20, giving B 163.148615/founder 6.851385. At 400, older classes convert: B 213.097345, founder 115.539823, each seed 14.442478, A 42.477876. At 1200 uncapped B 390.088496, founder 500.672566, each seed 62.584071, A 184.070796; older classes convert. With an inclusive 3x cap on B, its total participating payout is capped at 240; at 1200 it instead converts, all older classes also convert, giving B 265.486726/founder 577.699115/seeds 72.212389 each/A 212.389381. In the high-exit region where older classes convert, conversion ties the 240 cap at 1084.8 because B's issued ownership is 20/90.4, not 20 percent. No cap changes the underlying share count. At 200/400 the 3x cap does not bind. Deliverable: both models, all holder payouts/elections at all three exits, checks that sums equal available cash, and a sensitivity explaining a changed priority, pool exercise or cap definition. Do not copy Model S outputs into Model L or treat this exercise as a real transaction.","quiz":[{"q":"In Model S at exit 200, what does uncapped participating B receive?","o":["40, using pure conversion and ignoring the specified participating preference.","200, taking the entire exit despite enough proceeds remaining for other common.","192, subtracting 20 percent of forty from the whole exit instead of adding participation.","168, equal to 160 preference plus 20 percent of the remaining 40."],"a":3},{"q":"In Model S at exit 400, what does uncapped participating B receive?","o":["160, taking only the two-times preference and omitting residual participation.","240, adding the 160 preference to 20 percent of the full 400 without reducing residual.","80, applying pure conversion rather than the chosen participating preference.","208, equal to 160 plus 20 percent of the remaining 240."],"a":3},{"q":"In Model S at exit 1200, what does uncapped participating B receive?","o":["1040, assigning the residual to B alone while ignoring the other common owners.","400, adding 160 to 20 percent of the full exit rather than the residual.","368, equal to 160 plus 20 percent of the remaining 1040.","240, applying conversion or the capped result despite the uncapped participating terms."],"a":2},{"q":"In Model S, other common combined receives what at 200/400/1200?","o":["8/48/208, mistakenly allocating twenty percent of the post-preference residual to other common.","40/80/240, assigning other common the pure-conversion proceeds of B.","32/192/832, allocating the exact remaining proceeds after B's 168/208/368 preferred-path payouts.","160/320/960, ignoring the initial 160 preference before distributing common proceeds."],"a":2},{"q":"For Model S exits at least 160, participating proceeds exceed conversion by how much?","o":["160, treating the preference as an extra payment without its effect on residual participation.","32, counting only the twenty-percent reduction in participation from the first 160.","Zero, assuming participation and optional conversion are identical at all positive exits.","128, from the 160 preference less the 20 percent participation foregone on that first 160."],"a":3},{"q":"With Model S's inclusive 3x cap, when does conversion tie the 240 cap?","o":["At 1200 exit; it wins above that level, not strictly at the tie.","At 560 exit, confusing the participation-cap binding point with conversion reaching the cap.","At 800 exit, dividing the preference 160 by 20 percent instead of the total cap 240.","At 240 exit, treating the cap amount itself as the company's exit threshold."],"a":0},{"q":"After cancelling Model L's unissued pool, B's all-converted share is:","o":["80/400, using the financing valuation ratio without removing the cancelled pool from outstanding shares.","20/90.4, approximately 22.12 percent, after cancelling the unused pool from the fully diluted denominator.","20/80, dividing by founders original ownership rather than total issued ownership at exit.","20 percent, retaining the unused pool in the payout denominator even though it receives nothing."],"a":1},{"q":"In Model L at 200, why does B receive about 163.148615 rather than 168?","o":["Every class converts and B receives its fully diluted twenty-percent stake of the entire exit.","B takes only a one-times preference because participation automatically reduces the two-times multiple.","Older classes take 30 of preferences; the remaining 10 splits among founder and B at 43.52:20.","The unissued pool receives 9.6 percent of proceeds before the investor receives its preference."],"a":2},{"q":"In Model L at 400, with older holders converting, B's uncapped payout is approximately:","o":["213.097345, equal to 160 plus 20/90.4 of the 240 residual.","88.495575, taking conversion proceeds instead of the higher uncapped participating amount.","208, using Model S's twenty-percent outstanding fraction despite cancelled pool shares.","240, imposing the total cap even though actual participating payout is below that cap."],"a":0},{"q":"In Model L at 1200 with an inclusive 3x cap, what does B choose?","o":["Convert for 265.486726, exceeding the participating cap 240 on its 20/90.4 issued fraction.","Take 368, importing the uncapped Model S payoff despite both the cap and different denominator.","Take cap 240, treating conversion as forbidden merely because participation is capped.","Convert for 240, retaining the unissued pool in the payout denominator after it is cancelled."],"a":0},{"q":"Why must Model S and Model L remain separate?","o":["A twenty-percent fully diluted financing stake fixes twenty-percent exit proceeds regardless of pool treatment or share-class rights.","They differ in outstanding-share denominator and other preferred claims/elections, so identical B entry terms do not imply identical payouts.","Cancelling unused pool shares changes a label only, leaving every preferred and common cash amount mechanically identical.","Both models must match because B invested the same eighty at the same three-hundred-twenty pre-money valuation."],"a":1},{"q":"What does the three-times cap mean in the specified models?","o":["A change to the investor's ownership percentage so exactly three times as many common shares are outstanding.","A cap on all payouts including conversion, so the investor can never earn more than three times its investment.","A cap only on participation, allowing the original preference on top of three times the investment.","A total preferred-path payout limit including preference and participation; optional full conversion is a separate choice."],"a":3},{"q":"What happens to post-money SAFE cap-implied ownership at later financing?","o":["New money and the newly negotiated pool can dilute it; compare actual conversion share prices under its terms.","It depends only on the next round headline valuation, without any need to inspect capitalization definitions.","It remains the final ownership percentage through all later financing because the cap is called post-money.","It is already issued voting equity at signing even if the contract has not reached a conversion event."],"a":0},{"q":"Who bears a pool targeted post-closing but funded within pre-money?","o":["Nobody bears dilution from an unissued pool because fully diluted ownership includes only shares already issued.","All holders including new money are diluted identically while its agreed post-closing percentage remains mathematically unchanged.","Pre-existing and converting holders bear that pool creation; the new money is priced on the agreed pre-money basis.","Only founders bear every possible pool change because converting holders cannot ever be diluted by a new pool."],"a":2},{"q":"What does full-ratchet protection generally change, subject to actual terms?","o":["The market value of the whole company, so no capitalization-table change is needed when protection is triggered.","The protected conversion price/share entitlement at the triggering lower issue price, not a guaranteed doubling of percentage ownership.","Only the number of new financing shares in a weighted-average formula, leaving protected conversion price fixed.","The original cash investment and preference principal, both automatically doubling when the new share price halves."],"a":1},{"q":"What do scoped pro-rata rights ordinarily permit?","o":["Additional investment in the covered round on defined terms, not free shares, guaranteed allocation forever or voting control.","Automatic free shares maintaining ownership without investing additional money into any subsequent financing.","A guaranteed right to lead every future round regardless of the contract or the company's allocation decisions.","A payout of preference plus common participation even when the security is expressly non-participating."],"a":0},{"q":"What do an 800 equity exit, 10x target and 60 percent retention imply for entry?","o":["80 post-money because a high return multiple already makes any separate stated dilution irrelevant.","48 pre-money regardless of the capital raised because the formula cannot distinguish pre from post.","48 post-money under those assumptions; it is not an investor-independent reporting fair-value conclusion.","133.33 post-money because the retained ownership fraction belongs in the denominator of value."],"a":2},{"q":"How should a revenue-bearing company be handled if a scorecard benchmark covers pre-revenue peers?","o":["Replace company revenue with zero so the company formally qualifies for the published pre-revenue comparison.","Use it unchanged because every company can be scored subjectively even when the reference population does not match.","Flag the scope mismatch and use suitable evidence/methods, rather than force the benchmark to produce a number.","Apply historical U.S. dollar benchmark values directly as current Indian rupee valuations without market evidence."],"a":2},{"q":"What should happen when unchanged founder and investor reservation prices do not overlap?","o":["Treat a faster close as enough to meet the original return multiple without recomputing any economics.","Record no-deal or defer unless evidence or an authorized exception changes the constraints; do not manufacture agreement.","Choose their midpoint and call both constraints satisfied even though neither reservation price has changed.","Treat an unverified competing offer as binding evidence that the investor must accept the higher ask."],"a":1},{"q":"How should a short deadline affect the negotiation?","o":["Assess actual constraints, seek appropriate review time and alternatives, without assuming legality, intent or one fixed extension.","Always refuse the investor because any short deadline proves misconduct independent of context.","Always sign within forty-eight hours because published term-sheet deadlines are inherently fair and enforceable everywhere.","Assume a five-day extension will be granted because a prior article described asking for more time."],"a":0},{"q":"What turns a conditional mock IC approval into satisfied diligence evidence?","o":["A high sponsor confidence rating replaces the named independent source if it would otherwise delay closing.","The defined verifier checks the specified evidence/threshold; the approval wording itself does not complete the check.","A favorable market average substitutes for the company-specific retention check required in the recorded condition.","A majority vote automatically resolves the factual condition even if the requested cohort data never arrives."],"a":1},{"q":"What can a confidence 8 out of 10 label prove by itself?","o":["No calibrated probability or skill; forecasts need defined events, probabilities, resolution and enough comparable observations.","A reliable expected fund return because a ten-point confidence scale captures investment-risk probabilities automatically.","Superior decision skill whenever a majority of other committee members assigns a lower confidence label.","An eighty-percent chance of any investment outcome regardless of the absence of an event or horizon."],"a":0},{"q":"How should 3 percent versus 8 percent churn claims be handled?","o":["Select the larger number automatically because conservatism proves that it is the accurate company metric.","Use the deck number automatically because the founder should control the memo narrative during fundraising.","Preserve both sources and reconcile metric, denominator, cohort and period before choosing an estimate or calling a contradiction.","Average both numbers even when one is quarterly revenue churn and the other monthly logo churn."],"a":2},{"q":"What does a pass memo contribute?","o":["Contemporaneous evidence/terms and flip conditions for later re-evaluation, including mistakes without hindsight rewriting.","An irrevocable restriction on reconsidering the company after evidence, mandate or price has changed.","A guarantee of investment if the company later raises a higher-priced round from a familiar investor.","A record to rewrite after each successful exit so the old judgment appears consistent with later information."],"a":0},{"q":"How should material risk and uncertainty be summarized?","o":["Treat an uncertainty register as proof that every listed gap has been resolved or priced accurately.","Prioritize the decision drivers while retaining other critical issues, evidence gaps and accountable tests in the full record.","Delete every issue beyond the top three because page limits determine which risks are financially material.","Describe a risk as the most common failure across funds without a relevant study defining that comparison."],"a":1}]},"57":{"objectives":["Decide what a hospital-workflow AI pilot can establish using separate usage, cohort retention, delivery economics and cash evidence; identify the next bounded test rather than infer safety or investment merit from one metric."],"videos":[{"id":"nlbX3vfNzcI","title":"Why Your Low Margin AI Company Must Be 6x Larger Than SaaS Peers | The SaaS CFO | AI Tips","by":"The SaaS CFO","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. The six-times example is conditional on its EBITDA/OpEx/ARPA model, not a universal rule; quiz calculations use the written exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"a16z - AI delivery economics (2020 practitioner essay)","url":"https://a16z.com/the-new-business-of-ai-and-how-its-different-from-traditional-software/","note":"Read the cloud-cost and human-in-the-loop mechanisms. Historical interview-based margins are not current industry norms, accounting rules or a universal revenue-multiple requirement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ChartMogul - Net MRR Retention","url":"https://help.chartmogul.com/article/143-chart-net-mrr-retention","note":"Use a fixed opening cohort, completed comparison period, stated movement definitions and no new-business revenue. Reactivation and segmentation settings can alter reported retention; the exercise states its own exclusions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"First Round - measuring product-market fit","url":"https://review.firstround.com/how-to-measure-product-market-fit/","note":"Read as practitioner guidance for customer learning. A forty-percent disappointment survey result or usage growth is not proof of clinical safety, causal time savings, paid renewal or profitability."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The SaaS CFO - written companion to the assigned episode","url":"https://www.thesaascfo.com/the-real-economics-of-saas-versus-ai-companies/","note":"Read the separate equal-gross-profit, equal-EBITDA and pricing scenarios. The six-times EBITDA example holds an OpEx profile and ARPA assumptions; it is not a universal AI revenue requirement. Written prose is not a verbatim video transcript; embedded P&L images/model need their own inspection."}],"exercise":"Fictional Indian hospital-administration AI vendor, not a real company or clinical deployment recommendation. Decide whether to authorize further internal evaluation of a proposed flat-fee pricing pilot, not a purchase or a live workflow change. Completed baseline month: recognized net revenue Rs 20 lakh; the exercise defines the complete cost of revenue as inference 4, direct review 3 and other delivery 1 lakh. Gross profit is 12 lakh, gross margin 60 percent. In a separate hypothetical next-month stress, recognized revenue remains 20 while inference rises to 6 with review 3 and other delivery 1 unchanged: gross profit 10 and margin 50 percent. Delivery cost is not inference alone. In a one-customer flat-fee cohort, MRR stays 1 lakh while tasks double 10,000 to 20,000; this is 100 percent revenue retention, not 200 percent. More usage can increase costs without increasing revenue; calling usage retained revenue misstates measurement, it does not itself cause churn. No paid-renewal result or safety benefit is supplied. First compute a causal chain from pricing/task volume to billed/recognized revenue, task-mix-adjusted inference/review costs, cohort retention and cash, stating what is assumed rather than observed. Compare a capped-usage tier, a paid usage tier and continued flat-fee evaluation without assuming customer acceptance. Report volume and unit-cost sensitivities, contractual allowances/retries/minimums, quality and latency guardrails and a feasible matched comparison accounting for staffing/task mix. For an independent annual incremental project screen ONLY: new revenue 10 crore, variable costs 6 crore, avoidable incremental fixed cash costs 1 crore give 3 crore before working-capital changes, capex, taxes and financing. This is not the vendor's monthly gross margin or cash flow. Bridge profit to cash by identifying collection/payment timing, receivables, prepayments and capex; no cash-flow amount is supplied. State two material assumptions, a falsifier, an owner, a dated evaluation gate and a stop condition chosen before results. Required human review stays until appropriately authorized context-specific evidence supports change; a margin model is not clinical or legal approval. Deliverable: private two-page provisional decision note plus cost/cohort bridge; unknowns remain unknown, no invented contracts or patient data.","quiz":[{"q":"The flat-fee customer doubles tasks but keeps MRR at Rs 1 lakh. What does this establish?","o":["Paid demand doubled because the customer used twice its previous volume without paying more.","Usage rose while this customer retained 100 percent of revenue; delivery costs still need measurement.","Gross profit retained 100 percent because unchanged subscription fees imply unchanged delivery costs.","Recurring revenue retained 200 percent because the completed-task count doubled within the same customer."],"a":1},{"q":"In the baseline month, what complete delivery-cost calculation gives gross margin?","o":["Costs 4+3+1 divided by revenue 20 give a reported gross margin of 40 percent.","Revenue 20 less inference 4 and review 3 leaves 13 profit; 13/20 is 65 percent.","Revenue 20 less inference 4 leaves 16 gross profit; 16/20 is 80 percent.","Revenue 20 less costs 4+3+1 leaves 12 gross profit; 12/20 is 60 percent."],"a":3},{"q":"The stress holds revenue 20 and changes inference 4 to 6, with review 3 and other 1. What follows?","o":["Gross profit falls 12 to 10 lakh and gross margin falls 60 to 50 percent.","Gross margin falls 60 to 40 percent because every rupee of inference increase reduces margin twice.","Gross profit rises 12 to 14 lakh because higher inference spending itself creates recognized revenue.","Gross profit stays 12 lakh because fixed-fee revenue is unchanged despite higher delivery costs."],"a":0},{"q":"A slide calls twice as many tasks 200 percent NRR. What is the central error?","o":["It proves actual churn increased because the slide used the wrong metric label for task growth.","It understates NRR because all new-company revenue should also enter this customer's retention ratio.","It proves clinical productivity improved because retained task volume measures downstream patient outcomes.","It substitutes usage for a revenue-cohort measure; it does not demonstrate fee expansion or renewal."],"a":3},{"q":"What would most directly test whether a proposed paid usage tier improves economics?","o":["Measure accepted pricing, retained cohort revenue and full incremental delivery cost under quality guardrails.","Select the best-paying new customer and treat its margin as the retained economics of every customer.","Assume all existing users accept the tier and multiply old usage by the proposed higher price.","Compare inference cost alone before and after while excluding reviewers and customer downgrades."],"a":0},{"q":"Independent annual project: revenue 10 crore, variable costs 6 and avoidable incremental fixed costs 1. What remains?","o":["6 crore after those specified costs, treating variable delivery expenditure as the contribution itself.","4 crore after those specified costs, keeping the avoidable incremental fixed costs outside the screen.","3 crore after those specified costs, before working capital, capex, tax and financing effects.","3 crore of free cash flow, without needing collections, capital spending or payment timing."],"a":2},{"q":"A gross-margin improvement coincides with rising cash use. What is needed?","o":["A bridge through other expenses, collection/payment timing, working capital and capex before a cash claim.","An NRR calculation alone, because cohort expansion includes receivable collection and investment spending.","A recognized-revenue chart alone, because booking revenue proves customers already paid in cash.","A gross-margin percentage alone, because a higher percentage necessarily lowers all monthly cash outflow."],"a":0},{"q":"What is the sound interpretation of the assigned video's six-times title without a verified derivation?","o":["A six-times revenue multiple establishes equal company value without considering growth, risk or expenses.","Every AI vendor with half a peer's margin needs six times revenue to produce equal gross profit.","No universal multiplier: at positive margins, equal gross profit requires revenue inversely proportional to margin.","Every AI vendor must be six times larger regardless of its margins, capital needs or comparison basis."],"a":2},{"q":"The proposed pilot removes required review but supplies no workflow-specific error evaluation. What should the analysis do?","o":["Treat unchanged reported task count as evidence that removing reviewers cannot affect quality or safety.","Retain the requirement while seeking authorized quality evidence; financial upside alone does not establish safe change.","Authorize removal if the expected inference savings exceed review cost, without testing error or harm.","Authorize removal whenever a general model benchmark scores well, independent of the local workflow."],"a":1},{"q":"A pilot shows faster work, but staffing and task mix changed. What should its evaluation gate require?","o":["Customer satisfaction alone, treating perceived speed as an identified causal effect on all workflows.","A credible comparison with consistent time/quality measures and the staffing/task-mix differences addressed.","Revenue retention alone, treating renewal as a direct measurement of minutes saved at equal quality.","The before/after average alone, assuming every contemporaneous operational change came from the AI tool."],"a":1}]},"58":{"objectives":["Calculate and reconcile synthetic AI delivery costs, gross margin, fixed-cohort NRR/GRR and per-task contribution with explicit units, periods and accounting assumptions."],"videos":[{"id":"nlbX3vfNzcI","title":"Why Your Low Margin AI Company Must Be 6x Larger Than SaaS Peers | The SaaS CFO | AI Tips","by":"The SaaS CFO","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. The six-times example is conditional on its EBITDA/OpEx/ARPA model, not a universal rule; quiz calculations use the written exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ChartMogul - Net MRR Retention","url":"https://help.chartmogul.com/article/143-chart-net-mrr-retention","note":"Use the complete-period formula with explicit cohort, expansion, contraction, churn and reactivation treatment. Do not substitute new sales, tasks or an in-progress projection."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ChartMogul - Gross MRR Retention","url":"https://help.chartmogul.com/article/142-chart-gross-mrr-retention","note":"For the stated no-reactivation exercise, GRR=(opening MRR-churn-contraction)/opening MRR. Expansion is excluded; keep the period, cohort and denominator identical to NRR."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"a16z - AI delivery economics (historical practitioner context)","url":"https://a16z.com/the-new-business-of-ai-and-how-its-different-from-traditional-software/","note":"Use cloud and human-delivery cost mechanisms as prompts to reconcile the stated cost ledger, not as an accounting standard or a current margin benchmark. A private spreadsheet is enough; no account signup needed."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The SaaS CFO - written companion to the assigned episode","url":"https://www.thesaascfo.com/the-real-economics-of-saas-versus-ai-companies/","note":"Read the separate equal-gross-profit, equal-EBITDA and pricing scenarios. The six-times EBITDA example holds an OpEx profile and ARPA assumptions; it is not a universal AI revenue requirement. Written prose is not a verbatim video transcript; embedded P&L images/model need their own inspection."}],"exercise":"Private spreadsheet lab using the explicitly synthetic cases below; every case is independent unless linked. All currency is INR. Label source versus assumption, units and completed period. Case A: monthly recognized net revenue 20 lakh, complete stated cost of revenue inference 4+direct human review 3+other delivery 1 lakh; gross profit 12, margin 60 percent. Stress only inference to 6: profit 10, margin 50 percent. Case B: 50,000 completed tasks at exactly Rs 6 inference each, no retries, failures, minimums or extra inference costs: 3 lakh total. Case C: one fixed flat-fee customer, MRR 1 lakh unchanged, tasks 10,000 to 20,000, no fee expansion/churn: 100 percent revenue retention. Case D: fixed opening cohort MRR 10 lakh, churn 1, contraction 1, expansion 3, no reactivation/FX changes/acquisition adjustments; new-business MRR 4 is outside that cohort. Completed-period ending cohort MRR 11; NRR 110 percent, GRR 80 percent. Total company ending MRR would be 15, but 150 percent is not cohort NRR. Case E is a separate steady-state monthly run-rate comparison: cohort recurring revenue is assumed to equal the stated MRR in each comparison month, with no proration, nonrecurring fees or recognition timing difference. Revenue 10->11 lakh, complete cost of revenue 4->6; gross profit 6->5, margin 60 percent->45.4545 percent despite 110 percent revenue retention. These equalities are exercise assumptions, not a general claim that MRR is recognized revenue. Case F: Rs 120 per completed task less variable inference 30, required review 40 and other variable delivery 10 leaves Rs 40 contribution BEFORE fixed costs; do not label it company net profit or gross margin without reconciling the complete cost-of-revenue policy. Reconcile inference volume times unit cost; keep other delivery costs separate and stress retries/task mix explicitly. Do not remove required review solely for margin, and do not infer causal time savings from a before/after survey with staffing/task-mix changes. Public-company extension is optional only when a retrievable filing supplies the actual relevant figures/definitions: a stock-screening landing page cannot supply a private vendor's cohorts. Do not fabricate missing input data or clinical evidence. Deliverable: formulas, unit checks, cohort movement bridge, cost/profit bridge, one sensitivity and evidence gaps; no outreach, account creation or real deployment.","quiz":[{"q":"Case A: revenue 20 lakh; complete cost of revenue inference 4, review 3, other 1. Gross margin is:","o":["80 percent, from 16 gross profit divided by 20 revenue.","65 percent, from thirteen lakh divided by twenty lakh revenue.","60 percent, from 12 gross profit divided by 20 revenue.","40 percent, from eight lakh divided by twenty lakh revenue."],"a":2},{"q":"Case A stress: revenue 20 lakh, inference 6, review 3, other 1. Revised gross margin is:","o":["50 percent, from 10 gross profit divided by 20 revenue.","55 percent, from eleven lakh divided by twenty lakh revenue.","60 percent, from twelve lakh divided by twenty lakh revenue.","70 percent, from fourteen lakh divided by twenty lakh revenue."],"a":0},{"q":"Case B: 50,000 completed tasks at exactly Rs 6 inference each; no extras. Total inference cost is:","o":["Rs 0.3 lakh, equivalent to charging Rs 0.60 for each stated task.","Rs 30 lakh, equivalent to charging Rs 60 for each stated task.","Rs 6 lakh, equivalent to charging Rs 12 for each stated task.","Rs 3 lakh, equal to Rs 300,000 for the stated tasks."],"a":3},{"q":"Case C: one customer MRR remains 1 lakh; tasks double 10,000 to 20,000; no fee change/churn. Revenue retention is:","o":["0 percent, assuming no expansion means no revenue was retained.","100 percent, since the same customer's recurring fee is unchanged.","200 percent, using the usage growth ratio instead of recurring revenue.","50 percent, using revenue per task instead of cohort revenue retention."],"a":1},{"q":"Case D: opening MRR 10, churn 1, contraction 1, expansion 3, no reactivation; new business 4 outside cohort. NRR is:","o":["150 percent, from ten less one less one plus three plus four, divided by ten.","110 percent, from the opening cohort's ending 11 divided by 10.","130 percent, from ten plus three, divided by ten.","80 percent, from ten less one less one, divided by ten."],"a":1},{"q":"Case D with the same opening cohort and period. GRR excluding expansion/reactivation is:","o":["80 percent, from opening 10 less churn 1 and contraction 1, divided by 10.","90 percent, from opening ten less churn one, divided by ten.","100 percent, from capping the calculated net revenue retention of 110 percent at 100 percent.","110 percent, from ten less one less one plus three, divided by ten."],"a":0},{"q":"Case E steady-state monthly comparison: cohort revenue 10 to 11 lakh, cost of revenue 4 to 6. Gross profit:","o":["Stays 6 lakh, assuming retained revenue automatically preserves gross-profit rupees.","Rises 10 to 11 lakh, treating the full revenue figure as gross profit.","Rises 6 to 7 lakh, holding delivery cost at 4 despite the stated cost increase.","Falls 6 to 5 lakh even though the same cohort retains 110 percent of revenue."],"a":3},{"q":"Case F: price Rs 120, variable inference 30, review 40, other delivery 10. Contribution before fixed costs is:","o":["Rs 90 per task, from price 120 less inference cost 30.","Rs 80 per task, from price 120 less required review 40.","Rs 40 per task, from price 120 less inference 30, review 40 and other 10.","Rs 50 per task, from price 120 less inference 30 and required review 40."],"a":2},{"q":"Required hospital-workflow review costs money, but no error/harm evaluation supports removal. Sound response?","o":["Remove review based on a generic benchmark, assuming the local task distribution needs no testing.","Retain review while evaluating context-specific risks; margin arithmetic alone cannot establish safe deployment.","Remove review once projected contribution rises, treating financial benefit as a sufficient safety evaluation.","Remove review during peak demand, assuming higher volume makes the unmeasured error risk acceptable."],"a":1},{"q":"The pilot shows faster work, but staff and task mix changed. Which evidence best tests causality?","o":["A before/after speed average alone, attributing all differences to the tool without a comparison.","Only satisfied-user testimonials, excluding other workflows and those who stopped using the tool.","Only the vendor's gross margin, treating delivery efficiency as measured customer time savings.","A credible comparison using consistent task/time/quality measures and accounting for the concurrent changes."],"a":3}]},"59":{"objectives":["Design a bounded fictional Pune grocery-marketplace evaluation using local match quality, capacity and complete-cohort contribution payback, rather than infer durable network effects from subsidized aggregate growth."],"videos":[{"id":"LznOHjLLBQ8","title":"EM Group Chat #094: Solving The Cold Start Problem With Andrew Chen","by":"Everything Marketplaces","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use the written synthetic assumptions for calculations."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"a16z - marketplace metric definitions","url":"https://a16z.com/13-metrics-for-marketplace-companies/","note":"Read match rate, market depth, take rate, unit economics and local retention. Metrics depend on the business and geography; growth or correlation alone does not identify network effects."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Andrew Chen - social-product cold start","url":"https://andrewchen.com/how-to-solve-the-cold-start-problem-for-social-products/","note":"Read local saturation as a hypothesis to adapt, not a direct grocery-playbook validation. Social user-density examples and numeric engagement suggestions are not universal Pune marketplace thresholds."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"a16z - cohort GMV retention","url":"https://a16z.com/gmv-retention-the-marketplace-metric-most-ignore/","note":"Distinguish buyer/seller cohorts and GMV retention from user retention, revenue and contribution. Historical selected-company benchmarks are not a current universal target; cohort cost/payback still needs reconciliation."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Everything Marketplaces - assigned recording and chapter outline","url":"https://www.everythingmarketplaces.com/c/andrew-chen-cold-start-problem","note":"Publisher description identifies cold-start, atomic-network measurement and expansion topics. This is an outline, not a transcript or proof every quantitative exercise is taught in the recording."}],"exercise":"Fictional Pune grocery marketplace, not a real launch or subsidy authorization. Decide whether a narrow-neighborhood evaluation justifies a later expansion proposal; no actual spending or rollout follows. Specify area, service hours, product availability, promised delivery/quality level and capacity before counting matches. A daily stress has 1,200 valid requests but couriers can complete at most 900 at that service level, with no outsourcing or backlog. Fulfilled orders cannot exceed 900, and reaching 900 is not guaranteed. More buyer acquisition alone cannot overcome the bound. Define request fill as fulfilled eligible requests divided by all eligible requests in the SAME area and window. Log cancellations, stockouts, duplicates and failures using predeclared eligibility rules. Inspect merchant availability and courier utilization separately, not just a city average. This matching concept differs from cash liquidity.\nSeparate order case: basket Rs 500 at 12 percent commission produces Rs 60 platform fee before platform-funded customer discount 20, merchant incentive 10 and variable fulfillment/payment costs 25, leaving Rs 5 contribution. Assume a simplified agent-like commission ledger, no other fees, taxes or refunds; this is not a real-company accounting conclusion. Count each incentive once, separating order costs already included from acquisition costs outside contribution. Separate monthly plan: 40,000 orders at Rs 5 contribution less Rs 3 lakh future avoidable fixed operating costs gives Rs 1 lakh loss before tax. Fixed does not automatically mean sunk. Separate customer path: acquisition cost Rs 120 once, outside contribution; first order contributes minus 20 and each later order 35. Four later orders recover 140, so five total orders break even only CONDITIONAL on that path. No elapsed month or expected cohort LTV is supplied. Actual payback must include nonreturners, complete observation windows and the distribution and timing of repeat contribution. Include merchant onboarding costs where relevant. Another independent mature cohort has 200 first-time paying buyers and 80 who repeat within 30 days: defined repeat rate 40 percent, not GMV retention or evidence every buyer reaches five orders.\nCompare a narrow-area service/supply improvement with a capped demand-subsidy hypothesis, stating owner, evaluation date, cost ceiling requiring separate approval, quality guardrails and stop/expand criteria. Subsidies can help matching temporarily or worsen oversubscription; more volume cannot cure negative contribution under unchanged unit economics. Use a credible comparison accounting for holidays, merchant coverage and courier changes. Independent annual project screen: revenue 10 crore less variable costs 6 crore and avoidable incremental fixed costs 1 crore leaves 3 crore after those costs, not free cash flow or the monthly city result. Bridge collections, merchant settlements, courier payments, refunds and working capital separately. Deliverable: private provisional recommendation and one-city model with assumptions/unknowns; no real outreach, launch or payments.","quiz":[{"q":"A Pune evaluation has 1200 valid daily requests and at most 900 feasible completions at the promised service level. What follows?","o":["2100 completions are feasible by adding the demand and completion-capacity figures.","1200 completions are feasible because demand itself expands the stated courier capacity.","900 completions are guaranteed because the capacity estimate is also the observed fill count.","900 is an upper bound; more buyer acquisition alone cannot guarantee more fulfilled orders."],"a":3},{"q":"What is the most useful local request-fill definition for this evaluation?","o":["Fulfilled requests divided by registered city users, regardless of who requested in the window.","Fulfilled eligible requests divided by all eligible requests in the same area and service window.","All eligible requests divided by fulfilled requests, with a high value interpreted as better fill.","Total annual orders divided by merchant listings, regardless of availability or neighborhood."],"a":1},{"q":"Why does a citywide user count not establish local network liquidity?","o":["It always understates liquidity because every registered user must transact in every neighborhood.","It can hide mismatched time, neighborhood, inventory and capacity despite a large total user base.","It makes inventory and courier availability irrelevant because city totals already include both.","It establishes matching quality once registrations rise, even if completed-request share falls."],"a":1},{"q":"At basket 500 and 12 percent commission, what is the fee before the specified discounts and costs?","o":["Rs 6 platform commission, from interpreting twelve percent as one-point-two percent.","Rs 500 platform commission, because all customer basket spending belongs to the platform.","Rs 440 platform commission, because the merchant share is the same as platform revenue.","Rs 60 platform commission, distinct from the Rs 500 transacted basket value."],"a":3},{"q":"Which both-side incentive treatment makes the stated order contribution meaningful?","o":["Deduct customer and merchant incentives once, with their funding and acquisition-versus-order scope clear.","Deduct each order discount in contribution and again in CAC without checking duplicated spending.","Ignore merchant incentives because payments to the supply side cannot affect marketplace economics.","Exclude all incentives from both contribution and acquisition because they are labeled marketing."],"a":0},{"q":"Monthly 40000 orders at Rs 5 contribution and Rs 3 lakh avoidable fixed costs produce:","o":["Rs 1 lakh operating loss before tax under that ledger, not Rs 2 lakh operating profit.","Rs 3 lakh operating profit before tax because the fixed-cost budget is also the profit target.","Rs 2 lakh operating profit before tax because fixed operating costs do not enter the city screen.","Rs 1 lakh positive cash flow because a contribution result directly measures collections and capex."],"a":0},{"q":"What does the five-total-order acquisition-payback example establish?","o":["A 40 percent repeat-customer rate proves five total orders for every buyer in the opening cohort.","The cohort already paid back because the model assigns all nonreturners four repeat purchases.","A conditional path can recover acquisition cost at order five; realized cohort timing remains unknown.","Every acquired customer recovers acquisition cost in five months regardless of actual order timing."],"a":2},{"q":"How should future avoidable fixed city-launch costs enter an expansion decision?","o":["Allocate every historical headquarters cost and call all of it incremental city-launch spending.","Include the incremental avoidable costs; fixed describes volume behavior, not automatically sunk status.","Exclude them as sunk solely because they do not vary with the stated monthly order volume.","Count them as variable costs per order at every possible volume without checking the horizon."],"a":1},{"q":"Subsidized orders rise while merchant coverage and holidays change. What is needed before a causal profit claim?","o":["The gross-order chart alone, because rising orders identifies profitable demand caused by the subsidy.","Only repeat buyers who completed five orders, excluding acquired customers who never returned.","A credible comparison, mature cohorts and costs after both-side incentives, with service quality checked.","A selected best launch day annualized, without reconciling holidays or changes in supply coverage."],"a":2},{"q":"Independent annual project: revenue 10 crore, variable 6, avoidable incremental fixed 1. What is the 3 crore result?","o":["A remainder after the specified costs, before working-capital, capex, tax and financing effects.","The same as monthly city profit because the city model and annual project share a marketplace theme.","A guaranteed expansion payoff even though demand, service capacity and repeat outcomes are uncertain.","A complete free-cash-flow amount even though collections, settlement timing and capex are absent."],"a":0}]},"60":{"objectives":["Compute synthetic commission revenue, both-side incentive contribution, capacity bounds, defined fill/repeat rates and conditional acquisition payback without double-counting or treating GMV as platform revenue."],"videos":[{"id":"LznOHjLLBQ8","title":"EM Group Chat #094: Solving The Cold Start Problem With Andrew Chen","by":"Everything Marketplaces","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use the written synthetic assumptions for calculations."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"a16z - GMV versus platform revenue","url":"https://a16z.com/13-metrics-for-marketplace-companies/","note":"Use explicit transaction value, fee basis, refunds, incentives, match definitions and local cohorts. The exercise specifies a simplified agent-like commission ledger, not a real-company accounting conclusion."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"a16z - cohort retention definitions","url":"https://a16z.com/gmv-retention-the-marketplace-metric-most-ignore/","note":"A same-cohort spend ratio can exceed 100 percent while user retention cannot. Neither establishes contribution payback without the related costs and realized repeat distribution."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - chapter 7 cost and profit summary","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-microeconomics-3e/pages/7-key-concepts-and-summary","note":"Read revenue less cost and fixed/variable distinctions under a stated horizon. Its short-run fixed-cost/sunk-cost simplification does not make future avoidable launch costs sunk. Contribution-based payback still needs the stated cost ledger."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Everything Marketplaces - assigned recording and chapter outline","url":"https://www.everythingmarketplaces.com/c/andrew-chen-cold-start-problem","note":"Publisher description identifies cold-start, atomic-network measurement and expansion topics. This is an outline, not a transcript or proof every quantitative exercise is taught in the recording."}],"exercise":"Private spreadsheet lab. Cases are independent unless explicitly linked. Show INR units, completed observation windows, formulas and assumptions.\nA: monthly fulfilled GMV Rs 50 lakh, commission 12 percent, no other revenue, taxes/refunds ignored: commission revenue 6 lakh, not 50 lakh.\nB: basket Rs 500 at 12 percent commission gives 60 fee, less platform customer discount 20, merchant incentive 10 and variable fulfillment/payment cost 25 leaves 5 contribution per order. No other costs in this definition.\nC: 40,000 orders at Rs 5 contribution gives 2 lakh, less avoidable fixed monthly operating costs 3 lakh gives 1 lakh loss before tax, not company net profit or free cash flow.\nD: acquisition Rs 120 once outside contribution, first order contribution minus 20, later orders 35 each. Cumulative balance=-120-20+35*(n-1), needing five total orders. Four orders leave minus 35; five reach zero. This is conditional order count, not a promised month or expected cohort payback.\nE: 1,000 valid requests, 750 fulfilled, same city/hour: defined request fill rate 75 percent. Do not invert it or confuse it with cash liquidity.\nF: 1,200 daily requests, maximum completion capacity 900, no outsourcing/backlog: fulfilled orders at most 900, not guaranteed 900 or demand plus capacity.\nG: 200 January first-time paying customers, all with full 30-day follow-up; 80 repeat with at least one more paid order: defined repeat-customer rate 40 percent. No GMV/contribution retention conclusion follows.\nH: Rs 20 contribution per order after all variable costs/incentives, monthly fixed cost 4 lakh, no other costs/cannibalization: 20,000 orders break even conditional on constant contribution and sufficient capacity.\nI: Rs 15 contribution per order after current incentives, extra platform-funded discount 20 with nothing else changed: minus 5 contribution; more volume deepens the loss under these assumptions.\nJ: a campaign coincides with holidays, merchant coverage and courier changes. Use a credible comparison, mature cohorts, all incentives and service quality before claiming profitable repeat growth caused by the campaign.\nOptional real-company extension requires retrievable sources with matching city/cohort definitions. A stock-screening landing page cannot supply private Pune cohort data. Reconcile GMV, fees, discounts and settlements without double-counting; merchant payout cash is not automatically a platform expense. Build acquisition-plus-cumulative-contribution and working-capital cash bridges separately. Deliverable: unit-checked formulas, denominator log, sensitivity and evidence gaps; no signup, real rollout, subsidy or investment required.","quiz":[{"q":"Case A: monthly fulfilled GMV Rs 50 lakh, commission 12 percent, no other revenue, taxes/refunds ignored. Commission revenue?","o":["Rs 0.6 lakh, from 50 lakh multiplied by 0.012.","Rs 50 lakh, from the full transacted basket value.","Rs 44 lakh, from 50 lakh less 6 lakh commission.","Rs 6 lakh, from 50 lakh multiplied by 0.12."],"a":3},{"q":"Case B: basket 500, fee 12 percent, customer discount 20, merchant incentive 10, variable delivery/payment 25. Contribution?","o":["Rs 15 per order, from 60 less discount 20 less delivery 25.","Rs 5 per order, from 60 less 20 less 10 less 25.","Rs 35 per order, from 60 less delivery/payment 25.","Rs 60 per order, from 500 multiplied by 12 percent."],"a":1},{"q":"Case C: 40,000 orders at Rs 5 contribution, avoidable fixed cost 3 lakh/month. Operating result before tax?","o":["Rs 2 lakh profit, from order count multiplied by contribution alone.","Rs 1 lakh loss, from 2 lakh contribution less 3 lakh fixed cost.","Rs 3 lakh profit, equal to the monthly fixed-cost budget.","Rs 5 lakh profit, adding contribution and the fixed-cost budget."],"a":1},{"q":"Case D: acquisition 120 once, first-order contribution minus 20, later orders 35 each. Minimum total orders for payback?","o":["3 total orders, with two repeat contributions assumed sufficient.","6 total orders, with five repeat contributions assumed necessary.","5 total orders, with four repeat contributions: -120-20+4*35=0.","4 total orders, with three repeat contributions assumed sufficient."],"a":2},{"q":"Case E: fulfilled valid requests 750, all valid requests 1,000, same city/hour. Defined request fill rate?","o":["25 percent, from 250 unfulfilled divided by 1,000.","133.3 percent, from 1,000 divided by 750 fulfilled.","100 percent, from 750 fulfilled divided by 750 fulfilled.","75 percent, from 750 divided by 1,000 requests."],"a":3},{"q":"Case F: demand 1,200 orders/day, maximum feasible completions 900, no outsourcing/backlog. Fulfilled-order upper bound?","o":["300 orders/day, using the unserved gap as the maximum.","1,200 orders/day, using demand as the completion capacity.","900 orders/day, still subject to other matching constraints.","2,100 orders/day, adding demand and maximum capacity."],"a":2},{"q":"Case G: 200 first-time paying customers, 80 repeat within 30 days, all complete windows. Defined repeat-customer rate?","o":["40 orders per customer, treating a customer ratio as an order count.","40 percent, from 80 repeat customers divided by the 200-customer cohort.","140 percent, counting the 200 original buyers plus 80 repeat customers.","80 percent, treating the repeat-customer count as the percentage rate."],"a":1},{"q":"Case H: contribution Rs 20/order, monthly fixed cost 4 lakh, no other costs/cannibalization. Monthly breakeven volume?","o":["200,000 orders, from 4,000,000 divided by 20.","2,000 orders, from 40,000 divided by 20.","20,000 orders, from 400,000 divided by 20.","8,000 orders, from 400,000 divided by 50."],"a":2},{"q":"Case I: contribution Rs 15/order after incentives, extra platform discount 20, no other change. New contribution?","o":["Rs 5/order, from 20 less 15.","Rs 35/order, from 15 plus 20.","Rs 15/order, retaining the original contribution.","Minus Rs 5/order, from 15 less 20."],"a":3},{"q":"Case J: orders rise alongside holiday, merchant and courier changes. Sound test before a causal profitable-repeat claim?","o":["Use a credible comparison, complete cohorts and contribution after both-side subsidies; check quality and payback.","Use gross order growth as proof, without comparing concurrent supply or calendar changes.","Annualize the best day, assuming every acquired buyer repeats at the best observed frequency.","Exclude incentives because marketing classification makes them financially irrelevant to growth."],"a":0}]},"61":{"objectives":["Map a fictional covered commercial-bank/LSP arrangement by actual role, contract and fund flow, separating legal permissions, app economics and bank credit risk rather than treating regulatory status as a financial metric."],"videos":[{"id":"f_pi8eoBRFU","title":"Fintech Body FACE Drafts Self Regulatory Rules For Digital Lending May Apply For SRO Status With RBI","by":"CNBC-TV18","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. The FACE/SRO news title is not proof of current regulatory status or an independent lending permission; use the dated official written source and explicit exercise assumptions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"RBI - covered commercial-bank credit facilities, updated 1 October 2026","url":"https://rbidocs.rbi.org.in/rdocs/notification/PDFs/154MD.PDF","note":"Read scope para 3, LSP definition 4(1)(xxii), bank responsibility 6(7), funds/fees 10 and DLA-directory caveats 18(5)-(6). This instrument excludes small finance, payment and local area banks from its commercial-bank definition; do not generalize to all lenders. Read full exceptions, not only headings."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"RBI - current official consolidated source index","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=13156","note":"Resolve the current operative version and applicable bank type. A partnership, directory entry, SRO membership or homepage link is not proof of an independent lending permission. Cross-referenced outsourcing/KFS/data/other-law checks remain necessary for a real arrangement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Basel Framework - expected-loss amount and provisions","url":"https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/standard/cre/35/inforce/2023-01-01/published/2020-03-27","note":"Read CRE 35.1-35.3 and the provisions distinction. A simplified performing-exposure teaching amount uses PD*LGD*EAD; this is not a calculation of Indian statutory provision, capital charge, lifetime accounting ECL or realized cash loss."}],"exercise":"Private fictional credit-distribution analysis, not a loan, legal opinion or launch. The lender is a banking company covered by para 3 of RBI Commercial Banks-Credit Facilities Directions, 2025, updated 1 October 2026. It is not a small finance, payment or local area bank, which that definition excludes. The independent app acts solely as a contractual lending service provider (LSP); no independent lending permission is supplied. A commercial contract, SRO membership or DLA-directory entry is not proof of such permission. Read definition 4(1)(xxii), including its payment-aggregator-only proviso, and para 6: agreed roles, due diligence, monitoring and continuing bank responsibility. Map acquisition, credit decisions, funding, disbursement, fees, servicing, collections, complaints and data to the actual entity, contract and permission. Para 18(5)-(6): bank-submitted CIMS data is not validated by RBI, and inclusion is not RBI registration, authorization or endorsement.\nPara 10(4) requires fees payable to the LSP to be paid directly by the bank, not separately charged or collected from borrowers by the LSP. This does not say a bank can never charge lawful disclosed borrower fees. Read the whole fund-flow provision: statutory/regulatory, co-lending and specific-end-beneficiary disbursal exceptions; salary repayment through the employer under its conditions; and delinquent cash recovery with full same-day borrower-account credit and no LSP extraction of recovery fees. An ordinary repayment pool controlled by the app is not justified merely by consent or contract. KFS/disclosure, outsourcing, data and other-law checks remain additional requirements. This is not complete legal clearance.\nSeparate app screen: bank fee Rs 800/disbursal, stated variable acquisition/verification/servicing cost 500, no DLG, risk sharing, clawback or other variable cost: contribution 300. Fixed operating cost 6 lakh/month implies 2,000 disbursals/month to cover those costs, conditional on constant unit economics and capacity. Bank principal is not app revenue. Independent one-year bank case: 1,000 performing exposures, PD 4 percent, EAD 50,000 and conditional LGD 60 percent give teaching expected loss 1,200 each or 12 lakh total. Expected income 30 lakh minus funding 12, LSP fees 4, other variable servicing 2 and expected loss 12 gives zero contribution before fixed costs/tax. This case is independent of the 800-per-loan app illustration, whose fee on 1,000 loans would be 8 lakh, not 4 lakh. Stress PD to 8 percent: EL becomes 2,400 each or 24 lakh total; with every other bank line unchanged, contribution becomes minus 12 lakh. These are forecasts, not validated actual losses, statutory provisions or capital requirements. No credit transfer is assumed, not a claim that an LSP can never provide a valid DLG. A real guarantee would require its own eligibility, terms, portfolio, cap, invocation, NPA/provisioning and exception checks under paras 19-29.\nIndependent annual project: revenue 10 crore minus variable costs 6 and avoidable incremental fixed costs 1 leaves 3 after those costs, not monthly app/bank results or free cash flow. Map payment/settlement timing separately. Compare like borrower vintages over a consistent mature risk horizon when channels, borrowers or terms change. Deliverable: private regulatory-perimeter source table, separate app/bank waterfalls, stress, unknowns and a gated recommendation. No borrower data, outreach, account creation, guarantee or actual lending.","quiz":[{"q":"A fictional app acts solely as a covered bank's contractual LSP. What follows?","o":["The LSP role grants no independent lending permission; check entity, contract and scope.","The bank ceases regulatory responsibility once acquisition or servicing is outsourced to the app.","The partner bank's permission automatically transfers to the app for any independent lending product.","A commercial contract removes the need to assess outsourcing and borrower-protection obligations."],"a":0},{"q":"The app appears in a bank-submitted DLA directory. Under para 18(5)-(6), this establishes:","o":["A release of the submitting bank from responsibility for the accuracy of the submitted data.","An independent RBI lending license for every product shown on that application's interface.","Bank-submitted inclusion is not RBI registration, validation or endorsement of the app.","RBI approval of the app's complete business model and each future contractual arrangement."],"a":2},{"q":"Which scope statement correctly frames this written commercial-bank instrument?","o":["Para 3 excludes small finance, payment and local area banks; check their regimes separately.","It automatically covers every app with a bank partnership even when the app is itself unlicensed.","It defines every NBFC and payment company as a commercial bank for all digital lending activities.","It treats a local area bank as covered by this instrument solely because its name includes bank."],"a":0},{"q":"The bank owes its LSP a service fee. What does para 10(4) require for that fee?","o":["LSP collects it separately from the borrower whenever a general marketing consent box was checked.","The rule forbids all disclosed fees charged by the bank, regardless of product or other law.","LSP deducts it from borrower repayments in its own pool account before remitting the balance.","Bank pays it directly; the LSP does not separately charge or collect it from the borrower."],"a":3},{"q":"How should fund-flow analysis handle the rule and its exceptions?","o":["Treat a specific end-use disbursal exception as permission for every unrelated collection arrangement.","Omit delinquent cash recovery and salary-repayment provisos because absolute rules are simpler to teach.","Treat every borrower consent as an unrestricted exception allowing the app to control all repayments.","Apply the full rule to the facts; an exception label alone does not authorize an LSP pool."],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic app receives 800 and incurs 500 variable cost per disbursal, without risk transfer. Contribution is:","o":["Rs 300 after the stated variable costs, before fixed costs and unspecified cash/tax effects.","Minus Rs 300 after the stated variable costs, reversing the fee-less-cost subtraction.","Rs 800 after the stated variable costs, treating all fee revenue as contribution.","Rs 500 after the stated variable costs, treating variable spending itself as contribution."],"a":0},{"q":"One-year bank model has 1000 exposures with PD 4 percent, EAD 50000, LGD 60 percent. Teaching EL is:","o":["Rs 30000 per exposure and 3 crore aggregate, using default severity as an unconditional loss.","Rs 1200 per exposure and 12 lakh aggregate, not a guaranteed realized loss or statutory provision.","Rs 2000 per exposure and 20 lakh aggregate, excluding conditional loss severity from the amount.","Rs 120 per exposure and 1.2 lakh aggregate, reducing the specified PD by a factor of ten."],"a":1},{"q":"Why must app and bank waterfalls remain separate in this no-DLG exercise?","o":["The bank may count principal disbursed as fee income because the app sourced the customer.","The app must bear all bank credit losses even though the specified arrangement transfers no credit risk.","The app and bank may both count the same borrower principal as their own platform revenue.","App service fees and bank credit exposure differ; these cases also use separate fee inputs."],"a":3},{"q":"Independent annual project 10-6-1=3 crore. What does this figure establish?","o":["A regulatory clearance because positive modeled contribution proves all required permissions exist.","A remainder after stated variable and avoidable fixed costs, before omitted cash, tax and financing effects.","The monthly bank cohort contribution even though the supplied annual project is a separate case.","A complete free-cash-flow result without collections, settlement timing or capital-spending inputs."],"a":1},{"q":"Disbursals grow after channel, borrower mix and terms change. Sound next test?","o":["Use downloads and loan-principal growth alone as evidence that both entities risk-adjusted returns improved.","Treat faster approval as proof of lower defaults without observing a consistent credit-performance horizon.","Compare mature like vintages and loss horizons; reconcile costs and verify entity/contract roles.","Exclude immature or nonperforming borrowers silently so the new channel appears comparable to older cohorts."],"a":2}]},"62":{"objectives":["Calculate separate synthetic LSP and bank economics under one-year loss assumptions, distinguish principal from fee revenue, and apply the scoped official LSP fee rule without claiming complete legal clearance."],"videos":[{"id":"f_pi8eoBRFU","title":"Fintech Body FACE Drafts Self Regulatory Rules For Digital Lending May Apply For SRO Status With RBI","by":"CNBC-TV18","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. The FACE/SRO news title is not proof of current regulatory status or an independent lending permission; use the dated official written source and explicit exercise assumptions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"RBI - covered commercial-bank credit facilities, updated 1 October 2026","url":"https://rbidocs.rbi.org.in/rdocs/notification/PDFs/154MD.PDF","note":"Read scope para 3, LSP definition 4(1)(xxii), bank responsibility 6(7), funds/fees 10 and DLA-directory caveats 18(5)-(6). This instrument excludes small finance, payment and local area banks from its commercial-bank definition; do not generalize to all lenders. Read full exceptions, not only headings."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"RBI - current official consolidated source index","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=13156","note":"Resolve the current operative version and applicable bank type. A partnership, directory entry, SRO membership or homepage link is not proof of an independent lending permission. Cross-referenced outsourcing/KFS/data/other-law checks remain necessary for a real arrangement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Basel Framework - expected-loss amount and provisions","url":"https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/standard/cre/35/inforce/2023-01-01/published/2020-03-27","note":"Read CRE 35.1-35.3 and the provisions distinction. A simplified performing-exposure teaching amount uses PD*LGD*EAD; this is not a calculation of Indian statutory provision, capital charge, lifetime accounting ECL or realized cash loss."}],"exercise":"Private independent synthetic cases in INR, not real underwriting or a provisioning model. Written rule case: a banking company covered by para 3 of RBI Commercial Banks-Credit Facilities Directions, 2025, updated 1 October 2026, not a small finance, payment or local area bank.\nA: bank fee Rs 800/disbursal less complete stated variable app acquisition/verification/servicing cost 500 gives contribution 300. No DLG, credit sharing, clawback or other variable app cost.\nB: one-year performing exposure, PD 4 percent, EAD 50,000 and conditional LGD 60 percent. Teaching expected loss amount=PD*EAD*LGD=1,200, not actual default, lifetime accounting ECL or statutory provision. For 1,000 identical exposures, expected loss sums to 12 lakh. Independence is unnecessary for this linear sum of marginal expectations; dependence affects the loss distribution.\nC: independent one-year bank case with nonoverlapping categories: expected income 30 lakh, funding 12, LSP fees 4, variable servicing 2, expected loss 12 gives zero before fixed costs/tax. Do not import the 800-per-loan app fee from A. Stress B to PD 8 percent with EAD/LGD unchanged: 2,400 per exposure. For 1,000 exposures and all C's other lines unchanged, EL is 24 lakh and contribution minus 12 lakh.\nD: app contribution 300/disbursal and fixed operating costs 6 lakh/month imply 2,000 monthly loans to cover these costs, conditional on capacity and constant unit costs. No bank credit loss is charged to the app under this no-risk-transfer assumption.\nE: 1,000 bank loans at 50,000 principal and app fee 800/loan give 5 crore principal disbursed and 8 lakh app fee revenue, not 5 crore app revenue or profit.\nF: para 10(4) requires the bank to pay fees payable to its LSP directly; the LSP must not separately charge or collect them from borrowers. Read all para 10 exceptions before broad fund-flow conclusions; this is not a prohibition of every lawful disclosed bank fee. Para 18(6): a DLA-directory entry is not RBI registration, authorization or endorsement. A partnership alone grants no independent lender permission.\nG: channel growth with changed borrower mix/terms needs comparable mature vintages, consistent horizons, separate bank/app costs, loss stresses and verified regulated/contractual roles. Keep expected contribution, provisions/capital and cash timing separate. Deliverable: formulas, units/horizons, cost scope, paragraph evidence, remaining legal checks and sensitivity; no actual loan, guarantee, collection or outreach.","quiz":[{"q":"In this covered-bank case, what does the app acting only as an LSP establish?","o":["Automatic inheritance of all the partner bank's independent lending permissions.","A transfer of all bank regulatory responsibility to the app alone.","An exemption from outsourcing rules for all activities shown in the app.","An agency role within verified scope, not automatic independent lending permission."],"a":3},{"q":"Case A: bank fee Rs 800, stated variable app cost 500, no risk sharing. Contribution?","o":["Rs 300, from 800 less 500 before fixed costs.","Minus Rs 300, from 500 less 800.","Rs 800, from the full bank service fee before deductions.","Rs 500, from the variable-cost amount alone."],"a":0},{"q":"Case B: one-year PD 4 percent, EAD 50,000, LGD 60 percent. Teaching EL amount per exposure?","o":["Rs 120, from 0.004*50,000*0.60.","Rs 1,200, from 0.04*50,000*0.60.","Rs 30,000, from 50,000*0.60.","Rs 2,000, from 0.04*50,000."],"a":1},{"q":"Case B: 1,000 identical exposures, one-year expected loss 1,200 each, no risk transfer. Aggregate bank EL?","o":["Rs 12 lakh, from 1,000 multiplied by 1,200.","Zero, because the separate app sourced the loans.","Rs 1.2 lakh, from 100 multiplied by 1,200.","Rs 120 lakh, from 10,000 multiplied by 1,200."],"a":0},{"q":"Case C: one-year income 30 lakh, funding 12, LSP fees 4, servicing 2, EL 12. Contribution before fixed costs/tax?","o":["Zero, from 30 less 12 less 4 less 2 less 12.","Rs 30 lakh, from the income amount alone.","Rs 4 lakh, from 30 less 12 less 2 less 12.","Rs 12 lakh, from 30 less 12 less 4 less 2."],"a":0},{"q":"Stress Case B: one-year PD 8 percent, EAD 50,000, LGD 60 percent. New EL per exposure?","o":["Rs 24,000, from 0.80*50,000*0.60.","Rs 4,800, from 0.16*50,000*0.60.","Rs 1,200, from 0.04*50,000*0.60.","Rs 2,400, from 0.08*50,000*0.60."],"a":3},{"q":"Case D: contribution Rs 300/disbursal, fixed operating cost 6 lakh/month. Monthly breakeven count?","o":["500 loans, from 150,000 divided by 300.","200 loans, from 60,000 divided by 300.","20,000 loans, from 6,000,000 divided by 300.","2,000 loans, from 600,000 divided by 300."],"a":3},{"q":"Case E: 1,000 bank loans of Rs 50,000, app fee 800 each, no other app revenue. Principal disbursed and app revenue?","o":["Rs 50 lakh principal and Rs 80 lakh app fees.","Rs 5 crore principal and Rs 8 lakh app fees.","Rs 5 crore principal and Rs 5 crore app fees.","Rs 8 lakh principal and Rs 5 crore app fees."],"a":1},{"q":"Case F: a covered bank owes its LSP a service fee. Which matches para 10(4)?","o":["A partnership label waives all fee-disclosure and borrower-protection requirements.","Every repayment must pass through an LSP pool so the fee can be deducted first.","The bank pays the LSP directly; the LSP does not separately charge or collect that fee from borrowers.","The LSP separately collects the fee from borrowers after showing a bank-partnership badge."],"a":2},{"q":"Case G: channels, borrower mix and terms change along with disbursals. Evidence needed before better risk-adjusted economics?","o":["Approval speed alone, treating faster underwriting as proof that future losses are lower.","Loan-principal growth alone, treating every disbursed rupee as platform fee revenue.","Comparable mature cohorts, consistent risk horizons, separate app/bank cost-loss bridges and verified contract scope.","Only performing loans, dropping immature and defaulted exposures without disclosure."],"a":2}]},"63":{"objectives":["Transfer D57-D62 economics and evidence distinctions to changed numbers without notes, then choose remediation from actual error evidence rather than claim the app measured two skill areas."],"videos":[{"id":"f_pi8eoBRFU","title":"Fintech Body FACE Drafts Self Regulatory Rules For Digital Lending May Apply For SRO Status With RBI","by":"CNBC-TV18","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. This fintech context clip is not an overview of every review topic; complete the mixed-topic transfer work using the written sources and fictional assumptions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - retrieval practice and interleaving","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Read the distinction between recalling and re-reading, and choosing among mixed problem types. This research summary supports practice design, not a measured learning gain for this product."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"D57-D62 metric and regulatory source review","url":"https://a16z.com/13-metrics-for-marketplace-companies/","note":"After the closed-notes attempt, revisit relevant cohort, GMV/revenue and local matching definitions. For the lending item use the dated RBI source in D61-D62; do not infer legal clearance from a quiz score."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"RBI - scoped commercial-bank rule reference","url":"https://rbidocs.rbi.org.in/rdocs/notification/PDFs/154MD.PDF","note":"For the transfer item, re-check para 3 scope and 10(4) after the attempt. Other laws, exceptions and actual entity permissions remain separate checks."}],"exercise":"Review day: first solve a fresh mixed variant without notes, then check sources and explain each error. The app stores day score/timestamp, not per-question mistakes or a measured skill profile. Keep a private manual log of question, answer, correct reasoning, error cause, source and next transfer test. Select two remediation areas from actual observed errors; if errors are absent or too few, call the choice provisional rather than invent weaknesses. Do not choose by confidence alone or treat familiar answer recognition as transfer mastery. Retest after a delay with changed inputs/context, explaining the formula and denominator before reading options.\nFresh independent synthetic cases: recognized monthly AI revenue 30 lakh and complete stated delivery cost inference 9+review 6+other 3 give 12 gross profit/40 percent margin. Opening-cohort MRR 20, churn 2, contraction 1, expansion 5, no reactivation, new business 8 excluded gives 22 ending cohort/110 percent NRR and 85 percent GRR. Marketplace GMV 80 lakh *10 percent fee gives 8 lakh commission. Basket 600*10 percent fee minus customer discount 15,merchant incentive 10,variable delivery 30 gives 5 contribution/order. Acquisition 100 once outside contribution plus first order-10 and later 30 each needs five total orders to recover 110. Defined local fill 840/1200=70 percent; complete 30-day repeat cohort 90/300=30 percent, not GMV retention. Bank-only credit case PD 5 percent,EAD 40000,LGD 50 percent gives one-year teaching expected loss 1000, not the app's service revenue or statutory provision. In the covered commercial-bank/LSP case the bank pays its LSP fees directly, not separately collected by the LSP from borrowers; keep scope/exceptions. Independent annual project 10-6-1=3 crore after stated costs, not free cash flow. Write which contrary observation would alter a provisional decision and recompute it; do not silently erase earlier reasoning. Deliverable: manual error log, corrected formulas and two delayed transfer prompts, not a claim of psychometric assessment or product-measured mastery.","quiz":[{"q":"AI revenue 30 lakh, complete delivery costs 9+6+3. Gross profit and margin?","o":["18 lakh and 60 percent, treating total cost as profit.","12 lakh and 40 percent, using all stated costs.","21 lakh and 70 percent, subtracting inference alone.","15 lakh and 50 percent, leaving out other delivery costs."],"a":1},{"q":"Opening MRR 20, churn 2, contraction 1, expansion 5, new business 8 outside the cohort. NRR?","o":["110 percent, from ending cohort 22 divided by starting 20.","125 percent, from 20 plus expansion 5 without subtracting losses.","150 percent, from company ending 30 divided by starting 20.","85 percent, from 17 before expansion divided by starting 20."],"a":0},{"q":"Same opening cohort 20, churn 2, contraction 1, expansion 5. GRR excluding expansion?","o":["90 percent, from (20-2)/20.","110 percent, from (20-2-1+5)/20.","85 percent, from (20-2-1)/20.","100 percent, from capping net retention at 100."],"a":2},{"q":"Basket 600, commission 10 percent, customer discount 15, merchant incentive 10, delivery 30. Contribution?","o":["Rs 30, from 60-30 delivery cost.","Rs 60, from the commission alone.","Rs 15, from 60-15-30 without merchant incentive.","Rs 5, from 60-15-10-30."],"a":3},{"q":"Acquisition 100 outside contribution, first order minus 10, later orders 30 each. Minimum total orders for payback?","o":["Four orders, since three repeats are assumed to cover 110 needed.","Six orders, since five repeats are assumed necessary to cover 110.","Three orders, since two repeats are assumed to cover 110 needed.","Five orders, since four repeats produce 120 against 110 needed."],"a":3},{"q":"Defined local fill is 840/1,200 and mature 30-day repeat is 90/300. Results?","o":["70 percent GMV retention and 30 percent cash return, relabeling both.","142.86 percent fill and 333.33 percent repeat, inverting both ratios.","70 percent fill and 30 percent repeat, with different denominators.","30 percent fill and 70 percent repeat, swapping the two measures."],"a":2},{"q":"One-year bank teaching PD 5 percent, EAD 40,000, LGD 50 percent. EL amount?","o":["Rs 100, from 0.005*40,000*0.50.","Rs 1,000, from 0.05*40,000*0.50.","Rs 20,000, from 40,000*0.50.","Rs 2,000, from 0.05*40,000."],"a":1},{"q":"A covered bank owes its LSP fees. Which transfer of the scoped rule is sound?","o":["The LSP may separately collect its service fees if the bank discloses their amount.","An LSP platform pool is the default route when the borrower gives general consent.","Bank pays directly; LSP does not separately collect those fees from borrowers.","A commercial partnership alone permits the LSP to lend on its own balance sheet."],"a":2},{"q":"How should two remediation areas be chosen?","o":["Use actual attempted-error evidence; keep the choice provisional when evidence is sparse.","Retest the same familiar items and use recognition accuracy as the transfer measure.","Use the lowest stored day scores as a measured diagnosis of question-level skills.","Choose the two least familiar topics from confidence ratings without attempting them."],"a":0},{"q":"Independent annual 10-6-1=3 crore plus new contrary evidence. Sound review response?","o":["Keep the stated-cost result, separate cash gaps and revise affected assumptions with a change log.","Change the conclusion when evidence disagrees without recomputing the affected formula.","Keep the profit calculation as the cash-flow result and revise only the narrative.","Replace the earlier reasoning with the new answer without recording the changed input."],"a":0}]},"64":{"objectives":["Evaluate representative-feed pilot evidence, saleable product quality, throughput and cost before extrapolating battery-recycling lab recovery to commercial scale or authorizing a capital tranche."],"videos":[{"id":"p9PNTohyrY8","title":"Is Your Deep-Tech Startup Actually Ready for Investors? Here's How to Know.","by":"SEMSCIO","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use this as deep-tech investor orientation. Complete the process and cost calculations from the written fictional cases and the dated research examples, not as proof of a real plant's performance."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"DOE/ReCell - recycling modeling and scale-up","url":"https://www.energy.gov/cmei/vehicles/articles/modeling-and-analysis-battery-recycling","note":"Historical 2020 public research presentation: bench-to-scale milestones, materials, equipment, transport, waste, supply and lifecycle costs. Read as mechanisms and model boundaries, not current Indian costs or proof this fictional pilot works."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Ames Laboratory - recycling technoeconomic scenario","url":"https://www.ameslab.gov/cmi/research-highlights/economic-analysis-of-electrochemical-lithium-ion-battery-recycling","note":"Read its explicit 80,000 tons/year scenario, feed-cost sensitivity and remaining optimization needs. The reported purity and cost belong to that process/scenario, not every feed chemistry or commercial plant."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NASA - technology maturity context","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/somd/space-communications-navigation-program/technology-readiness-levels/","note":"Distinguish proof of concept, integrated testing and representative prototype evidence. This NASA page uses space/flight context; do not assign a battery plant a certified TRL or infer permits/customer acceptance from a label."}],"exercise":"Fictional recycling pilot, not a real facility approval or operating instruction. A clean 1,000 kg dry-feed sample contains target material 20 percent: 200 kg target available. Recovering 160 kg target is 80 percent recovery. If the product weighs 200 kg, purity is also 80 percent, with a different denominator. No selling price or battery performance follows from purity alone. Trace feed, target, product, waste, moisture, contamination and recycle streams. Sequential 90 percent then 80 percent retention gives 72 percent overall with no extra target/recycle, not 85 or 170 percent. Do not multiply a stage again if the input recovery already includes it.\nPredefine the pilot gate: representative chemistry/feed and supply, sustained throughput/availability, mass/energy balances, saleable specification and customer performance, complete feed/transport/energy/reagent/labor/waste costs, maintenance/capex, cash and safety/permit checks. Name owner, date, evidence thresholds, budget needing separate approval and stop/continue criteria. A patent search or bench yield is not plant reliability, freedom to operate, permission or an offtake contract. No Indian legal threshold is inferred from an IP homepage.\nIndependent monthly case: running input 2 tonnes/hour*8 hours/day*20 days/month*75 percent availability=240 tonnes, no other losses. All saleable outputs earn 50,000/input tonne; full stated variable costs 35,000/input tonne give contribution 15,000. Fixed costs 30 lakh/month imply 200 input tonnes breakeven; at 240, result is 6 lakh before depreciation, interest and tax. Saleable-output tonnes need not equal input tonnes. Independent ratio case: installed capex 12 crore/design input capacity 2,400 tonnes/year=50,000 rupees per annual tonne of capacity. This is not processing cost or actual output. It is not the same plant case as 240 tonnes/month, which repeats to 2,880/year if sustained for 12 months. Reconcile design, schedule, downtime and feed basis before combining. Lower capital intensity alone does not establish better economics or permission. Independent annual 10 crore revenue less variable 6 and avoidable fixed 1 gives 3 after those costs, not full cash flow. Stress mixed-feed quality, saleable yield, prices, waste costs and ramp-up coherently. Deliverable: private milestone-gated plan, numeric bridge and evidence gaps, no actual capital release.","quiz":[{"q":"Feed has 200 kg target; 160 kg target is recovered in 200 kg product. Recovery versus purity?","o":["Both 80 percent here, but feed-target and product-mass denominators differ.","Recovery 16 percent and purity 80 percent, using 1,000 kg total feed for recovery.","Both 160 percent, treating recovered kilograms as the percentage itself.","Recovery 80 percent and purity 125 percent, reversing the purity denominator."],"a":0},{"q":"Stages retain 90 percent then 80 percent of remaining target, no extra input/recycle. Overall recovery?","o":["170 percent, adding the separate stage percentages.","85 percent, averaging the two stage percentages equally.","80 percent, keeping only the second stage percentage.","72 percent, from 0.90 x 0.80."],"a":3},{"q":"Why is a clean-feed bench result insufficient for a commercial scale-up decision?","o":["It leaves throughput unresolved, but clean-feed recovery can remain the mixed-feed forecast.","It requires a longer bench run, with the same clean feed standing in for purchased feed.","Mixed-feed quality, sustained throughput, total costs and safety/permit evidence remain open.","It needs a larger equipment quote, after which mass recovery establishes saleable quality."],"a":2},{"q":"Input 2 tonnes/hour, 8 hours/day, 20 days/month, availability 75 percent, no other loss. Monthly feed?","o":["240 tonnes, from 2*8*20*0.75.","120 tonnes, from 1*8*20*0.75.","30 tonnes, from 2*20*0.75.","320 tonnes, from 2*8*20."],"a":0},{"q":"Receipts 50,000/input tonne, complete variable cost 35,000/input tonne. Contribution?","o":["Rs 50,000/input tonne, from total saleable-output receipts alone.","Rs 15,000/input tonne, before fixed costs and omitted items.","Rs 35,000/input tonne, from the variable-cost amount alone.","Rs 85,000/input tonne, adding receipts and variable costs."],"a":1},{"q":"Installed capex 12 crore, design input 2,400 tonnes/year. Capital intensity?","o":["Rs 5,000 per annual tonne of capacity, using one-tenth capex.","Rs 50,000 variable cost per input tonne actually processed.","Rs 50,000 per annual tonne of capacity, not processing cost.","Rs 5 lakh per annual tonne of capacity, using ten-times capex."],"a":2},{"q":"Why keep 240 monthly throughput and 2,400 annual design-capacity cases independent?","o":["The two cases reconcile if one uses gross feed and the other product without a bridge.","The monthly case can use ten months per year without a separate schedule assumption.","The annual design input can be treated as realized output without a yield adjustment.","The first repeats to 2,880/year; reconcile schedules and capacity before merging cases."],"a":3},{"q":"Mixed feed lowers saleable output and raises waste cost, while clean-feed lab yield repeats. Sound response?","o":["Reconcile feed, product, waste, throughput, costs and cash before revisiting the gate.","Update disposal costs but retain clean-feed saleable recovery across all purchased feeds.","Lower product revenue but retain capacity utilization despite the changed process load.","Adjust reported margin but leave collection timing and ramp-up cash needs unchanged."],"a":0},{"q":"Independent annual project: revenue 10, variable 6, avoidable fixed 1 crore. Remainder?","o":["4 crore after those costs, excluding the stated avoidable fixed cost.","3 crore guaranteed cash balance without collection or capex information.","6 crore profit, taking the variable-cost amount itself as the payoff.","3 crore after those costs, not complete free cash flow or approval."],"a":3},{"q":"Which milestone supports a next scale-up evaluation?","o":["Repeated bench recovery, equipment nameplate capacity and a quote using clean-feed costs.","Predeclared feed, output, throughput and cost tests, with an owner, safety checks and stop rules.","A pilot yield average, expected product price and budget without product acceptance tests.","A lab quality certificate, forecast full utilization and a permit application still pending."],"a":1}]},"65":{"objectives":["Compute synthetic target-material recovery, product purity, staged recovery, effective throughput and input-based economics while keeping independent capacity cases and capital-intensity units explicit."],"videos":[{"id":"p9PNTohyrY8","title":"Is Your Deep-Tech Startup Actually Ready for Investors? Here's How to Know.","by":"SEMSCIO","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use this as deep-tech investor orientation. Complete the process and cost calculations from the written fictional cases and the dated research examples, not as proof of a real plant's performance."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"DOE/ReCell - recycling modeling and scale-up","url":"https://www.energy.gov/cmei/vehicles/articles/modeling-and-analysis-battery-recycling","note":"Historical 2020 public research presentation: bench-to-scale milestones, materials, equipment, transport, waste, supply and lifecycle costs. Read as mechanisms and model boundaries, not current Indian costs or proof this fictional pilot works."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Ames Laboratory - recycling technoeconomic scenario","url":"https://www.ameslab.gov/cmi/research-highlights/economic-analysis-of-electrochemical-lithium-ion-battery-recycling","note":"Read its explicit 80,000 tons/year scenario, feed-cost sensitivity and remaining optimization needs. The reported purity and cost belong to that process/scenario, not every feed chemistry or commercial plant."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NASA - technology maturity context","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/somd/space-communications-navigation-program/technology-readiness-levels/","note":"Distinguish proof of concept, integrated testing and representative prototype evidence. This NASA page uses space/flight context; do not assign a battery plant a certified TRL or infer permits/customer acceptance from a label."}],"exercise":"Private independent synthetic cases unless linked. Show units and dry-feed/target/product denominators. A: 1,000 kg feed contains 20 percent target=200 kg; 80 percent target recovery=160 kg. B: 200 kg product contains 160 kg target=80 percent purity, not automatically qualified product. C: sequential retentions 90 and 80 percent, no extra target/recycle=72 percent overall. D: 2 input tonnes/hour, 8 hours/day, 20 days/month, 75 percent running availability, no other losses=240 input tonnes/month. E: all saleable receipts 50,000/input tonne less complete stated variable feed/transport/energy/chemical/waste costs 35,000/input tonne=15,000 contribution. F: 30 lakh fixed monthly costs/15,000 contribution=200 input tonnes breakeven, conditional on capacity and constant economics. G: 240*15,000 less 30 lakh=6 lakh before depreciation, interest and tax, not full free cash flow. H: installed capex 12 crore/design input 2,400 tonnes/year=50,000 rupees per annual tonne of capacity, not variable processing cost. H is independent of D/G, whose monthly inputs repeat to 2,880/year for 12 months. I: a clean-feed bench result does not authorize scale-up; require representative mass balance, product quality/performance, sustained throughput, full costs, safety/permit evidence and appropriate authority. J: changed feed and disposal costs require an updated output/cost/throughput/cash bridge and a new gate decision. These are not current commodity prices or real-company data. No signup, filing, facility operation or payment required. Deliverable: formulas, denominator log, sensitivity and gated recommendation.","quiz":[{"q":"A: 1,000 kg dry feed, target 20 percent, target recovery 80 percent. Target recovered?","o":["16 kg, from 1,000*0.02*0.80.","800 kg, from 1,000*0.80.","200 kg, from 1,000*0.20.","160 kg, from 1,000*0.20*0.80."],"a":3},{"q":"B: 200 kg product contains 160 kg target. Defined purity?","o":["80 percent, from 160/200.","125 percent, from 200/160.","20 percent, from 40/200 impurity.","100 percent, from 200/200."],"a":0},{"q":"C: Sequential retentions 90 then 80 percent, no extra target/recycle. Overall?","o":["80 percent, from the last stage alone.","85 percent, from (90+80)/2.","170 percent, from 90+80.","72 percent, from 0.90*0.80."],"a":3},{"q":"D: 2 input tonnes/hour, 8 hours/day, 20 days/month, availability 75 percent. Monthly input?","o":["320 tonnes, from 2*8*20.","240 tonnes, from 2*8*20*0.75.","120 tonnes, from 1*8*20*0.75.","30 tonnes, from 2*20*0.75."],"a":1},{"q":"E: Receipts 50,000/input tonne, full stated variable cost 35,000/input tonne. Contribution?","o":["Rs 85,000, from 50,000+35,000.","Rs 35,000, from the cost amount alone.","Rs 15,000, from 50,000-35,000.","Rs 50,000, from gross receipts alone."],"a":2},{"q":"F: Contribution 15,000/input tonne, fixed cost 30 lakh/month. Monthly breakeven feed?","o":["20 tonnes, from 300,000/15,000.","200 tonnes, from 3,000,000/15,000.","2,000 tonnes, from 30,000,000/15,000.","60 tonnes, from 3,000,000/50,000 gross receipts."],"a":1},{"q":"G: 240 input tonnes*15,000 contribution less 30 lakh fixed. Before depreciation, interest and tax?","o":["Rs 6 lakh loss, from 30 lakh-36 lakh.","Rs 36 lakh profit, from contribution before fixed costs.","Rs 6 lakh profit, from 36 lakh-30 lakh.","Rs 30 lakh profit, from the fixed-cost budget alone."],"a":2},{"q":"H: Capex 12 crore, design input 2,400 tonnes/year. Capital intensity?","o":["Rs 5,000 per annual tonne of capacity, from 12,000,000/2,400.","Rs 50,000 variable processing cost/tonne, treating capital ratio as operating cost.","Rs 50,000 per annual tonne of capacity, from 120,000,000/2,400.","Rs 5 lakh per annual tonne of capacity, from 1,200,000,000/2,400."],"a":2},{"q":"A clean-feed bench run shows strong recovery. Evidence for a scale-up evaluation gate?","o":["Pilot average recovery, expected product price and budget without customer performance data.","Representative mass balance, product quality/performance, sustained throughput and full cost/safety/permit evidence.","Repeatable bench yield, equipment nameplate capacity and clean-feed variable-cost estimates.","Lab quality certificate, forecast full utilization and a permit application awaiting approval."],"a":1},{"q":"Mixed feed lowers saleable recovery and raises disposal costs. What should the model do?","o":["Update feed-to-product balance, costs, throughput and cash, then reconsider the gate.","Adjust product revenue but retain throughput despite added separation and processing load.","Update waste cost but retain the clean-feed recovery assumption for all purchased input.","Recompute operating margin while leaving collection timing and ramp-up cash unchanged."],"a":0}]},"66":{"objectives":["Allocate a fictional fund budget among fees, initial checks, follow-ons and cash buffer, then distinguish reserved from spent capital, actual ownership rights and gross investment versus net LP outcomes."],"videos":[{"id":"a4aUX5u90oA","title":"If You Know Nothing About Venture Capital, Watch This First | Forbes","by":"Forbes","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. This is general VC orientation; use the written fictional assumptions for the reserve budget, ownership and fund-waterfall calculations."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Fred Wilson/AVC - reserves practice (2017)","url":"https://avc.com/2017/01/reserves/","note":"Read company-by-company round timing/size/probability planning and finite fund capacity. Half-for-reserves, recycling limits and model confidence are USV examples, not universal mandates or guarantees every investor follows on."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Fred Wilson/AVC - fund economics (2014)","url":"https://avc.com/2014/05/vc-fund-economics/","note":"Read the stated fee/carry incentives and different fee periods. Historical 2.5/20 terms are one firm's practice, not current legal terms for a fictional Indian fund. Actual fund documents govern."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC post-money SAFE primer - dilution context","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/assets/ycdc/Primer%20for%20post-money%20safe%20v1.1-2af8129e12effd9638eeab383b7309142c8f415e5cdb0bc210d573f779177a1c.pdf","note":"Revisit share-price/capitalization and later-round dilution distinctions. A pro-rata right requires investment under its scope, not free ownership preservation or guaranteed future allocation. Use a private spreadsheet, no signup required."}],"exercise":"Fictional fund: commitment 100 crore, all eventually called, no leverage/recycling/tax. Lifetime fees/expenses 15 are inside commitment. Budget: initial 20 checks*2=40, follow-on 35, cash buffer 10; fees 15+40+35+10=100. Reserve means planned allocation, not paid cash, a promise, or free pro-rata entitlement. Model each company's round timing, size, probability, terms and joint downside, including no-follow-on choices. Equal reserves are not mandatory. Spending all 85 after-fee capacity initially leaves no reserve/buffer in this budget, but zero reserves can be deliberate under another mandate; it is not universally wrong.\nIndependent ownership case: stake 20 percent before a round raising 10 at 40 pre, no other shares/pool/conversions. No follow-on dilutes it to 16 percent. Investing 2 as part of the total 10 retains 20 percent: old stake value 8+new 2, divided by 50 post. Actual rights, allocation and terms must allow it; preserving ownership at any price does not guarantee better returns.\nBudget path: ten companies receive 3 each follow-on=30. Invested initial 40+30=70, fees 15 spent, cash 15 unused (unspent reserve 5+buffer 10). Assume all holdings exit for 210 cash, no NAV/liabilities. Gross investment MOIC=210/70=3 x. Return unused 15 too:225 total cash before carry, with fees already spent. Simplified whole-fund carry 20 percent of profit after returning all 100 called capital including fees, no hurdle/catch-up/GP commitment or clawback issues: profit 125, carry 25, LP cash 200 or 2 x net MOIC. This is not annual IRR or a real LPA waterfall. Do not subtract 100 returned capital again from LP cash. Actual terms and distribution dates matter. Stress lower exits, simultaneous rounds, fee overruns and correlated losses; reserved money itself creates no investment gain. Independent operating project 10-6-1=3 crore is a separate stated-cost remainder, not fund-return math. Deliverable: private 20-row timing/capital model, ownership cases, gross/net reconciliation and stress; no fund transfer or investment authority.","quiz":[{"q":"Commitment 100 crore, lifetime fees 15, initial 40, follow-on budget 35, cash buffer 10. Correct budget?","o":["100 initial plus 35 follow-on, excluding fees/buffer from commitment.","85 initial plus 35 follow-on, excluding reserves from the budget.","15+40+35+10=100; investment and buffer budget after fees is 85.","40 initial plus 50 follow-on plus 15 fees, with no funding gap."],"a":2},{"q":"Twenty initial checks of 2 crore each. Amount and combined follow-on/buffer budget after 15 fees?","o":["55 initial and 45 remaining, counting fees as investment.","40 initial and 60 remaining, from 100-40.","20 initial and 65 remaining, from 100-15-20.","40 initial and 45 remaining, from 100-15-40."],"a":3},{"q":"What does a 35 crore reserve allocation establish?","o":["A committed company liability now, because the internal model assigns a future funding round.","A pro-rata ownership guarantee now, because planned reserve capacity matches expected needs.","A planned budget, not disbursed cash or an unconditional promise to a company.","Invested capital for MOIC now, because the allocation is earmarked for portfolio companies."],"a":2},{"q":"Fund owns 20 percent; round raises 10 at 40 pre, no other shares. Stake with no follow-on?","o":["16 percent, from old stake value 8 divided by 50 post.","20 percent, retaining pre-round ownership without new money.","25 percent, from new money 10 divided by pre-money 40.","10 percent, halving the stake without using the round size."],"a":0},{"q":"Same total raise 10 at 40 pre includes fund investment 2. Post-round ownership?","o":["25 percent, dividing the 10 stake value by pre-money 40.","20 percent, from old value 8 plus new 2, divided by 50.","16 percent, using no-follow-on ownership despite the investment.","24 percent, adding 2/50 without diluting the old 20 percent."],"a":1},{"q":"Ten companies receive 3 crore follow-on each after 40 initial. Invested and unused cash, after 15 fees?","o":["70 invested and 15 cash, reconciling to 100 called with fees.","85 invested and zero cash, assuming the whole reserve was spent.","70 invested and 30 cash, ignoring the 15 spent on fees.","100 invested and 15 cash, counting fees/buffer as invested capital."],"a":0},{"q":"Invested 70 produces 210 gross exit cash, no NAV. Gross investment MOIC?","o":["3.21x, adding unused cash 15 to 210.","2x, from profit 140 divided by 70.","3x, from 210 divided by 70 invested.","2.1x, from 210 divided by 100 called."],"a":2},{"q":"Exit 210 plus unused cash 15, called 100, carry 20 percent after returning all 100, no other terms. Carry and LP cash?","o":["Carry 25, LP cash 200, from 225 less 25 carry.","Carry 25, LP cash 100, subtracting returned capital twice.","Carry 42, LP cash 183, charging 20 percent of exits 210.","Carry 28, LP cash 197, charging on investment profit 140."],"a":0},{"q":"How should reserve policy and return comparisons be judged?","o":["Compare gross investment MOIC across funds without adjusting for LP fees or carry terms.","Use actual mandate, terms, future checks, timing and risks; net MOIC is not annual IRR.","Choose the ownership-preserving path without comparing new pricing and incremental returns.","Set every reserve policy to half of commitment using the historical fund example as a rule."],"a":1},{"q":"Independent operating project 10-6-1=3 crore. How does it relate to the fund?","o":["A 3x gross investment multiple, from comparing project remainder with avoidable fixed cost.","A 3 crore addition to available reserves now, even before project cash is distributed.","A 3 percent annual fund return, from 3 project profit divided by 100 commitment.","A stated-cost operating remainder, not fund MOIC, carry or complete cash flow."],"a":3}]},"67":{"objectives":["Reconcile a fictional fund's fees, initial checks and finite follow-on budget, then distinguish gross portfolio MOIC, net LP TVPI and dilution under explicit assumptions."],"videos":[{"id":"a4aUX5u90oA","title":"If You Know Nothing About Venture Capital, Watch This First | Forbes","by":"Forbes","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. General VC orientation; use the written fictional assumptions for reserves, ownership and fund performance calculations."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"AVC - finite fund reserves (2017)","url":"https://avc.com/2017/01/reserves/","note":"Read timing, size and probability of future rounds and fund capacity. Half-reserve and recycling practices describe USV, not a universal rule or funding guarantee."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA - performance definitions (2025)","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Read invested capital, portfolio-level gross MOIC and fund-level net TVPI. Contributions include fee/expense calls; NAV is not distributed cash. The exercise excludes subscription facilities and recycling. Actual fund documents govern."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"AVC - fund economics (2014)","url":"https://avc.com/2014/05/vc-fund-economics/","note":"Historical fee/carry example, not a universal current fee schedule or terms for a real Indian fund. Use private arithmetic or a spreadsheet, no account required."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic cases, not a real fund recommendation or Indian filing. A: commitment 100 crore, lifetime fees/expenses 20 inside commitment, no borrowing/recycling/extra funds: 80 available for investments. Half of 80 reserved for follow-ons leaves 40 initial, or 2 each across 20 companies. Eight 3-crore follow-ons use 24 and leave 16 of 40 reserves. Reserve allocation is not already invested cash or a promise to fund a company. Stress 12 checks*4=48 against 40 reserve: 8 shortfall. Resolve through permitted changed plans/rounds, not assumed hidden capital. Spending all 80 initially leaves no follow-on capacity in this budget; another mandate may intentionally choose no reserves. Model 20 companies with dates, checks, probabilities and correlated downside, not a guaranteed loss-adjusted return.\nIndependent ownership: 2 at 20 post buys 10 percent, no pool/convertibles or other adjustments.10 percent existing diluted by new shares equal 25 percent post becomes 7.5 percent. A permitted 10 percent share of a total 10 crore primary raise needs 1 crore included within that raise to maintain 10 percent, no other issuances; an actual right/allocation is not automatic.\nIndependent realized portfolio: 20 checks*2=40 invested, no follow-ons.10 return zero; 6 return 1x=12; 3 return 3x=18; 1 returns 10x=20; total 50/40=1.25x gross portfolio MOIC before fund fees/carry. Do not add unused reserves to investment proceeds or call this net LP return. Separate reporting-date LP case: 100 contributed for all purposes, 60 distributions and 50 remaining NAV attributable to those LPs net of accrued fees/expenses/carry, no facility: net TVPI=(60+50)/100=1.1x, not DPI or IRR or 110 cash returned. NAV may change and is not cash already distributed. These portfolio and LP cases are independent; do not force them into one budget. Record all calls/distributions/fees/NAV dates; no annual IRR from a multiple alone. Deliverable: private 20 row capital/timing stress model, denominator log and decision thresholds. No fund transfer or investment commitment.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic Rs 100 crore seed fund: lifetime management fees and fund expenses together are Rs 20 crore. No borrowing, recycling or other funding is allowed. What total capital remains available for portfolio investments?","o":["Rs 20 crore","Rs 80 crore","Rs 120 crore","Rs 100 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic allocation: the fund has Rs 80 crore investable after all fees and expenses. It reserves 50% of this investable amount for follow-ons and makes equal initial investments in 20 companies. What is the initial check per company?","o":["Rs 1 crore","Rs 2 crore","Rs 5 crore","Rs 4 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic follow-on demand: eight companies each qualify for a Rs 3 crore follow-on check. Follow-on reserves total Rs 40 crore and no other reserve use has occurred. How much reserve remains after those checks?","o":["Rs 24 crore","Rs 8 crore","Rs 64 crore","Rs 16 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic priced equity round: a fund invests Rs 2 crore at a Rs 20 crore post-money valuation. There are no convertibles, option-pool changes or other ownership adjustments. What ownership does this check buy immediately after the round?","o":["1%","10%","11.1%","20%"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic dilution: the fund owns 10% immediately before a round. It does not invest. New investors receive 25% of the company after the round, and no other dilution occurs. What is the fund's post-round ownership?","o":["7.5%","10%","2.5%","12.5%"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic pro-rata round: a fund owns 10% before a Rs 10 crore primary equity raise and may buy exactly 10% of the newly issued shares at the same price as other investors. No other share issuance or pool change occurs. What check maintains its 10% holding?","o":["Rs 10 crore","Rs 0.1 crore","Rs 1 crore","Rs 2.5 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic fully realized portfolio: 20 investments cost Rs 2 crore each, with no follow-ons. Ten return zero; six return 1x invested cost; three return 3x; one returns 10x. What is gross portfolio MOIC before fund fees, expenses and carry?","o":["0.75x","2x","1.25x","10x"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic LP metric at one reporting date: fee-paying LPs have contributed Rs 100 crore in total, received Rs 60 crore in distributions and have Rs 50 crore remaining NAV attributable to them, net of accrued fees, expenses and carry. There is no subscription facility. What is net TVPI?","o":["1.1x","0.6x","1.5x","2.1x"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic reserve stress: a Rs 40 crore follow-on pool must fund 12 approved checks of Rs 4 crore each. No recycling, borrowing or added commitments are allowed. What is the funding shortfall?","o":["Rs 4 crore","Zero","Rs 12 crore","Rs 8 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Model target needs large step-ups in every follow-on company. Strongest next review?","o":["Stress exit prices only, with unchanged graduation, checks and timing across all scenarios.","Stress gross return only, with fee/expense calls and unused capital outside the reconciliation.","Stress graduation, checks, dilution, losses, timing and fees; show shortfalls and reversal thresholds.","Stress each company independently, treating simultaneous round demand as diversified away."],"a":2}]},"68":{"objectives":["Compare fictional financing packages on founder exit proceeds, preference elections, closing certainty, control and a realistic BATNA, not headline valuation alone."],"videos":[{"id":"RfTalFEeKKE","title":"The Harvard Principles of Negotiation","by":"Erich Pommer Institut","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Negotiation orientation; compute the written fictional waterfall separately. A title does not establish instruction on every preference or conversion case."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"PON - bargaining with a BATNA","url":"https://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/batna/negotiation-and-bargaining-with-your-batna-in-mind/","note":"Read how attraction to agreement can lead to an outcome worse than the available alternative. Compare realistic alternatives, not an invented or uncertain competing offer treated as assured. This is a research summary, not this company's negotiation evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Stock Legal - preference and document mechanics (2026)","url":"https://www.stocklegal.com/blog/raising-capital-using-the-national-venture-capital-association-nvca-model-documents","note":"US venture-financing explanation: non-participating versus participating preferences, control terms and final-document review. Not Indian legal advice or proof that these clauses are enforceable for a particular transaction."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"McCarter - Anatomy of a Term Sheet (Q4 2020)","url":"https://www.mccarter.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/AnatomyTermSheet_Q42020_FINAL.pdf","note":"Historical US explanatory pamphlet and attached model. Read the liquidation-preference and optional-conversion sections for the alternatives used in this fictional lab; not current Indian law or a current model-document certification. Some term-sheet provisions can bind; actual documents need qualified counsel."}],"exercise":"Fictional negotiation, not an actual offer or legal opinion. First distinguish cash raised into the company from shareholder cash at a later exit, and gross purchase price from distributable equity. Independent acquisition bridge: buyer 30 crore less stated debt 4 and transaction costs 1 leaves 25 for equity, ignoring cash adjustments/tax/other claims. Debt settlement and transaction definitions must be checked in a real deal; do not mechanically subtract debt twice if the quoted amount is already equity proceeds.\nFor the following independent single-investor waterfall, use equity proceeds after all senior claims/costs. Investor put in 10 crore, owns 25 percent as converted; founders own all other common. No options, dividends, convertibles, other classes, taxes or claims. Non-participating 1x investor takes the greater of available preference up to 10 or 25 percent of equity, not both. At 30 equity investor 10/founders 20; at 60 investor 15/founders 45. Election ties at 40. At 5 equity investor 5/founders zero, not a guaranteed 10 recovery. Uncapped 1x participation at 30 gives investor 10+25 percent *20=15/founders 15. With total payout capped at 2x=20 and option to convert fully, at 60 participating investor 22.5 before cap, capped 20 versus conversion 15, so investor 20/founders 40. At 100, conversion 25 beats cap 20: investor 25/founders 75. A 2x non-participating preference at 30 pays 20/founders 10; the multiple is not participation.\nIndependent multi-class shortfall: equity 15, B preference 12 senior to A 8, neither converts/participates: B 12/A 3/common zero. If instead equal rank and documents allocate the shortfall proportionally to preference amounts 20, A 6/B 9/common zero. Do not generalize these fixed-election examples to all interacting conversion stacks.\nBuild a term comparison and exit grid at 5/30/40/60/100. Reconcile investor+founder distributions to equity in every row. Compare amount/timing/conditions of funding, dilution/options, dividends/seniority/conversion, vetoes/board, no-shop/confidentiality, costs and alternatives. An unsigned higher-valuation offer with uncertain close is not cash in the bank. Record the realistic BATNA, deadline/runway, reservation package and evidence that would change it. No single universally best deal from headline valuation or a single exit. Have qualified counsel review actual documents and local rules; a model or term sheet is not automatically the final governing document. Some clauses may bind even without a completed financing. Deliverable: private negotiation sheet, equity bridge and waterfall with conditions, not a signed offer, outreach or commitment.","quiz":[{"q":"Investor paid 10 crore, owns 25 percent as converted, 1x non-participating; equity proceeds 5 crore, no other claims. Investor and founders receive?","o":["Investor 5 and founders 0, limited by available equity.","Investor 10 and founders 0, treating preference as guaranteed repayment.","Investor 5 and founders 3.75, counting the same equity proceeds twice.","Investor 1.25 and founders 3.75, forcing common conversion only."],"a":0},{"q":"Investor 10/25 percent, 1x non-participating; founders all other common, equity 30, no other claims. Founder payout?","o":["20 crore after investor chooses 10 over 7.5.","12.5 crore after subtracting preference and 25 percent of all equity.","22.5 crore after assuming conversion only.","15 crore after adding participation to the preference."],"a":0},{"q":"Same 1x non-participating at equity 60. Investor payout?","o":["25 crore, preference plus 25 percent of full equity.","22.5 crore, preference plus 25 percent of residual.","10 crore, preference always beats conversion.","15 crore, conversion exceeds 10 preference."],"a":3},{"q":"Same investor 10/25 percent, 1x non-participating. Indifference equity value?","o":["2.5 crore, 10*0.25.","25 crore, the percentage treated as value.","10 crore, the invested amount alone.","40 crore, 10/0.25."],"a":3},{"q":"Uncapped 1x participating, investor 10/25 percent, equity 30, no other claims. Founder payout?","o":["22.5 crore, treating all shares as common.","7.5 crore, assigning investor ownership to founders.","15 crore, 75 percent of 20 after preference.","20 crore, ignoring investor participation."],"a":2},{"q":"1x participating, total 2x cap and conversion option; investor 10/25 percent, equity 60. Founder payout?","o":["50 crore, paying preference without participation.","45 crore, forcing conversion although preference pays more.","40 crore, investor chooses cap 20 over conversion 15.","37.5 crore, ignoring the total payout cap."],"a":2},{"q":"Same capped investor, equity 100. Investor optimizes election. Founder payout?","o":["75 crore, conversion 25 beats cap 20.","90 crore, paying only the 1x preference.","67.5 crore, ignoring the participation cap and conversion.","80 crore, treating cap 20 as a limit on common conversion."],"a":0},{"q":"B 12 senior to A 8; equity 15, both remain preferred/no participation, no other claims. A payout?","o":["8 crore from paying A in full before senior B.","3 crore after B takes 12.","6 crore from equal-rank proportional allocation.","7.5 crore from equal division of available equity."],"a":1},{"q":"Same preference amounts but equal rank, proportional shortfall per documents, equity 15. A payout?","o":["8 crore from paying A in full.","7.5 crore from equal division.","3 crore after prioritizing B.","6 crore from 15*8/20."],"a":3},{"q":"Higher valuation offer has uncertain close and extra veto/participation terms. Sound comparison?","o":["Treat conditional offered money as assured funding and compare only modeled exit payoffs.","Use ownership dilution alone while leaving preference and control terms for later review.","Test realistic funding timing, control, exit waterfalls and BATNA before choosing the package.","Choose using the most favorable exit without downside or runway-related close scenarios."],"a":2}]},"69":{"objectives":["Compute founder proceeds in distinct synthetic non-participating, participating, capped and seniority cases while preserving equity-proceeds and election assumptions."],"videos":[{"id":"RfTalFEeKKE","title":"The Harvard Principles of Negotiation","by":"Erich Pommer Institut","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Negotiation orientation; compute the written fictional waterfall separately. A title does not establish instruction on every preference or conversion case."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"PON - bargaining with a BATNA","url":"https://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/batna/negotiation-and-bargaining-with-your-batna-in-mind/","note":"Read how attraction to agreement can lead to an outcome worse than the available alternative. Compare realistic alternatives, not an invented or uncertain competing offer treated as assured. This is a research summary, not this company's negotiation evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Stock Legal - preference and document mechanics (2026)","url":"https://www.stocklegal.com/blog/raising-capital-using-the-national-venture-capital-association-nvca-model-documents","note":"US venture-financing explanation: non-participating versus participating preferences, control terms and final-document review. Not Indian legal advice or proof that these clauses are enforceable for a particular transaction."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"McCarter - Anatomy of a Term Sheet (Q4 2020)","url":"https://www.mccarter.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/AnatomyTermSheet_Q42020_FINAL.pdf","note":"Historical US explanatory pamphlet and attached model. Read the liquidation-preference and optional-conversion sections for the alternatives used in this fictional lab; not current Indian law or a current model-document certification. Some term-sheet provisions can bind; actual documents need qualified counsel."}],"exercise":"Independent synthetic numerical cases. All money amounts are crore rupees. State each formula and reconcile investor plus founder proceeds to equity cash. Do not combine independent terms into one company.\nA: Buyer pays 30 total; agreed waterfall pays debt 4 and transaction costs 1 first, no other adjustments: equity 25. If a quoted transaction amount is already equity proceeds, do not subtract debt twice.\nB-D: Investor paid 10, owns 25 percent as converted, founders own all other common, 1x non-participating preference, no debt/cost/dividend/option/other claims. Investor takes the higher available preference or conversion, not both. Equity 30: investor 10 versus conversion 7.5, founders 20. Equity 60: investor converts for 15, founders 45. Election crossover 10/0.25=40. At equity below 10, payment cannot exceed available equity.\nE: Same 10 invested and 25 percent ownership, but 1x uncapped participation at equity 30: investor 10+0.25*(30-10)=15, founders 15. F: Same 1x participation with total payout cap 2x invested capital and right to convert fully, equity 60: uncapped 22.5, capped 20 versus conversion 15, founders 40. At equity 100 conversion 25 beats cap 20, so the cap does not limit common conversion. G: Instead 2x non-participating at equity 30 gives preference 20 versus conversion 7.5, founders 10.\nH: B preference 12 senior to A preference 8; equity 15, both remain preferred with no participation or other claims: B 12/A 3/common zero. I: Equal ranking instead, with shortfall explicitly proportional to preferences: A=15*8/20=6, B=9, common zero. These fixed-election shortfalls are not a general solver for interacting conversion classes.\nJ: Compare a genuinely available financing alternative on funding certainty, timing, runway, control and founder proceeds, not valuation alone. An unsigned conditional offer is not assured cash. Document a reservation package, alternatives and decision-changing evidence. Have qualified counsel check actual documents and local rules. US explanatory/model documents illustrate mechanics, not enforceability of a real Indian transaction. Some obligations may bind before a completed financing. Deliverable: private waterfall grid, equity bridge, assumption/election log and term comparison. No actual signing, negotiation message or commitment.","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic acquisition: the buyer pays Rs 30 crore total. The agreed waterfall first pays Rs 4 crore debt and Rs 1 crore transaction costs. Ignore cash adjustments, taxes and other claims. How much is left to distribute to shareholders?","o":["Rs 29 crore","Rs 35 crore","Rs 30 crore","Rs 25 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic one-investor cap table: the investor paid Rs 10 crore, owns 25% as converted and has a 1x non-participating preference. Founders own all remaining common shares. Equity proceeds are Rs 30 crore, with no debt, costs, dividends, options or other claims. How much do founders receive if the investor chooses the higher payout?","o":["Rs 30 crore","Rs 22.5 crore","Rs 20 crore","Rs 12.5 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic one-investor cap table: investor paid Rs 10 crore, owns 25% as converted, and has a 1x non-participating preference. Founders own all other common shares. At Rs 60 crore equity proceeds, with no other claims, how much do founders receive after the investor optimizes its election?","o":["Rs 50 crore","Rs 45 crore","Rs 40 crore","Rs 35 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Synthetic single class: Rs 10 crore invested, 25% as-converted ownership and 1x non-participating preference, with no other claims. At what total equity proceeds is the investor indifferent between preference and conversion?","o":["Rs 40 crore","Rs 2.5 crore","Rs 10 crore","Rs 25 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic one-investor cap table: Rs 10 crore invested, 25% as-converted ownership, 1x uncapped participating preferred; founders own all remaining common. Equity proceeds are Rs 30 crore, no other claims. What do founders receive under these stated participation terms?","o":["Rs 20 crore","Rs 22.5 crore","Rs 15 crore","Rs 7.5 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Synthetic one-investor cap table: Rs 10 crore invested, 25% as converted, 1x participating preference with total payout capped at 2x invested capital; investor may instead convert fully. Founders own all other common. At Rs 60 crore equity proceeds and no other claims, what do founders receive?","o":["Rs 45 crore","Rs 37.5 crore","Rs 50 crore","Rs 40 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic one-investor cap table: investor paid Rs 10 crore, owns 25% as converted and negotiates a 2x non-participating preference. Founders own all remaining common. At Rs 30 crore equity proceeds, no other claims, what do founders receive?","o":["Rs 5 crore","Rs 22.5 crore","Rs 20 crore","Rs 10 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic preference shortfall: Series B has a Rs 12 crore preference senior to Series A's Rs 8 crore preference. Only Rs 15 crore is available to equity. Both remain preferred; neither participates or converts; no other claims. What does Series A receive?","o":["Rs 3 crore","Rs 6 crore","Rs 8 crore","Zero"],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic preference shortfall: A and B have equal-ranking preferences of Rs 8 crore and Rs 12 crore. The documents allocate any shortfall proportionally to those preference amounts. Only Rs 15 crore is available to equity; neither participates or converts; no other claims. What does A receive?","o":["Rs 6 crore","Rs 8 crore","Rs 3 crore","Rs 7.5 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"An unsigned alternative has higher valuation, uncertain funding, extra vetoes and participation. Strongest BATNA comparison?","o":["Compare headline dilution with funding timing held equal despite the alternative's unresolved closing conditions.","Compare exit proceeds using the offered ownership alone, treating preferences as a later legal detail.","Compare realistic close/timing/control and exit proceeds; set a reservation package and have counsel check documents.","Compare the best modeled exit for each offer without testing downside, vetoes or runway."],"a":2}]},"70":{"objectives":["Transfer recycling, fund and preference mechanics to fresh numbers, then choose delayed remediation from an actual private error log, not an app-measured skill profile."],"videos":[{"id":"RfTalFEeKKE","title":"The Harvard Principles of Negotiation","by":"Erich Pommer Institut","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. This negotiation clip is context, not an overview of all mixed review topics. Use the written cases and sources for the calculations."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - retrieval and mixed practice","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Attempt changed-number mixed problems without notes, explain the formula, then check sources. This research summary is not product-measured mastery."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA - fund denominator check","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Revisit portfolio gross MOIC versus LP net TVPI, fee calls and remaining NAV after the closed-notes attempt."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Stock Legal - preference mechanics","url":"https://www.stocklegal.com/blog/raising-capital-using-the-national-venture-capital-association-nvca-model-documents","note":"Revisit elections and participation using the explicit fictional terms. US explanation, not actual Indian transaction advice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"DOE/ReCell - recycling model boundaries","url":"https://www.energy.gov/cmei/vehicles/articles/modeling-and-analysis-battery-recycling","note":"Dated 2020 research example for representative evidence, cost and scale boundaries; not current Indian commodity costs or plant approval."}],"exercise":"Review day. Attempt changed-number mixed cases without notes, explain formulas, then check sources. Keep a private manual error log: attempted answer, units, correct reasoning, cause and next transfer test. App day score/timestamp does not supply per-question errors or a measured two-skill diagnosis. Choose two remediation areas from actual observed errors; when too few errors exist, label the choice provisional. Retest after a delay rather than repeat recognized options.\nIndependent recycling cases: 1,200 kg dry feed contains target 25 percent; 80 percent recovery gives 240 kg target. If 300 kg product contains that 240 kg target, purity is 80 percent, with a different denominator. Purity alone does not establish selling price or end-use performance. Sequential retentions 95 percent and 80 percent give 76 percent with no added target/recycle. Input 3 tonnes/hour*6 hours/day*20 days/month*80 percent availability=288 tonnes/month with no other losses. Complete stated receipts 45,000 less variable costs 30,000 per input tonne give contribution 15,000. Fixed monthly costs 36 lakh imply 240 tonnes breakeven and a result of 7.2 lakh at 288 tonnes, before depreciation, interest and tax. This is not free cash flow or the same case as a separate annual design-capacity ratio.\nIndependent fund: commitment 120 crore, lifetime fees/expenses 20 inside commitment, initial investment 50, planned reserves 40, buffer 10. Ten follow-ons of 3 use 30: invested 80, fees paid 20, unused cash 20. No borrowing or recycling. Independent LP report: contributions 80, distributions 30, remaining net NAV 50, no facility: net TVPI=(30+50)/80=1x, not 1x cash already returned or annual IRR.\nIndependent preference: investor paid 8, owns 20 percent as converted, 1x non-participating, founders own all other common, no other claims. At equity 25 investor takes 8 over conversion 5, leaving founders 17. At equity 60 investor converts for 12, leaving founders 48; crossover 40. Separate fixed-election classes: senior preference 15, junior 10, equity 18, no participation/conversion/other claims gives junior 3; if equal ranking with proportional shortfall, junior receives 18*10/25=7.2. Do not mix these scenarios or call them real-company facts or legal clearance. New evidence changes affected inputs and recomputed conclusions visibly. Deliverable: calculations, source/denominator log, actual private error evidence and two delayed transfer prompts. No psychometric certification, plant permission, actual offer or commitment.","quiz":[{"q":"1200 kg feed, 25 percent target, 80 percent recovery. Recovered target?","o":["60 kg, 1200*.25*.2.","300 kg, 1200*.25.","240 kg, 1200*.25*.8.","960 kg, 1200*.8."],"a":2},{"q":"Product 300 kg contains 240 kg target. Defined purity?","o":["80 percent, 240/300.","125 percent, 300/240.","20 percent, 60/300 impurity.","100 percent, 300/300."],"a":0},{"q":"Sequential target retentions 95 percent then 80 percent, no extra target/recycle. Overall?","o":["87.5 percent, (95+80)/2.","76 percent, .95*.8.","80 percent, the final stage alone.","175 percent, 95+80."],"a":1},{"q":"3 input tonnes/h*6 h/day*20 days/month*.8 availability, no other losses. Monthly input?","o":["360 tonnes, 3*6*20.","48 tonnes, 3*20*.8.","144 tonnes, 1.5*6*20*.8.","288 tonnes, 3*6*20*.8."],"a":3},{"q":"Receipts 45000 minus complete variable 30000/input tonnes, fixed 36 lakh/month. Breakeven?","o":["120 input tonnes, 3600000/30000.","240 input tonnes, 3600000/15000.","24 input tonnes, 360000/15000.","80 input tonnes, 3600000/45000."],"a":1},{"q":"120 commitment, 20 fees spent, 50 initial+30 follow-on invested, no borrowing/recycling. Unused cash?","o":["40 crore, 120-50-30.","30 crore, using follow-on spending as cash.","10 crore, using original buffer alone.","20 crore, 120-20-50-30."],"a":3},{"q":"Independent LP report: 80 contributed, 30 distributed, 50 remaining net NAV, no facility. Net TVPI?","o":["1x, (30+50)/80, not 1x cash returned.","0.375x, 30/80 using distributions only.","0.625x, 50/80 using remaining NAV only.","1.6x, 80/50 using contributions over NAV."],"a":0},{"q":"Investor 8/20 percent, 1x non-participating, equity 25, founders all other common, no other claims. Founder payout?","o":["12 crore, 25-8-5 subtracting preference and conversion.","17 crore, 25-max(8, 5).","20 crore, 25-5 assuming conversion only.","13.6 crore, .8*(25-8)assuming participation."],"a":1},{"q":"Senior 15, junior 10, equity 18, both remain preferred, no participation/other claims. Junior payout?","o":["3 crore, 18-15 after senior paid.","10 crore, paying junior fully before senior.","9 crore, 18/2 assuming equal division.","7.2 crore, 18*10/25 assuming equal rank."],"a":0},{"q":"How should two next-week remediation areas be selected?","o":["Use accuracy on repeated familiar options as proof the changed-context skill transfers.","Use lowest day scores as an app-measured diagnosis of each question-level weakness.","Use actual attempted-error evidence and delayed transfer; keep sparse-evidence choices provisional.","Use confidence ratings without checking attempts or distinguishing error causes."],"a":2}]},"71":{"objectives":["Write a bounded fictional SME market-pilot charter with a measurable objective, counterfactual, permitted evidence, decision owner and stop gates before treating analysis as business approval."],"videos":[{"id":"fFyaJl9T74g","title":"The Research Problem & Problem Statement: Plain-Language Explainer (With Examples) + FREE Templates","by":"Grad Coach","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. The publisher companion supports research-problem framing; use the written fictional charter and workbook for data-permission and cash decisions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Grad Coach - research problem and statement","url":"https://gradcoach.com/research-problem-statement/","note":"Publisher article embeds the assigned video and explains problem, consequence, research gap and practical feasibility. Academic research framing, not SME consent, sponsor approval or a cash forecast. Written companion, not a verbatim transcript."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - problem, alternatives and business model","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"Use the problem, current alternatives, market and business-model prompts as an outline. This pitching guide does not establish sponsor consent, data rights, a project cash forecast or approval."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - research records and privacy practice","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/taking-notes-and-recording-user-research-sessions","note":"Read consent, permitted uses, minimum identifying details, secure handling and permission before copying materials. UK service-research guidance is an ethical workflow reference here, not an Indian legal-compliance finding. Use the fictional case by default."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - observations, findings and actions","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/analyse-a-research-session","note":"Keep observed evidence separate from interpretation and proposed action. Preserve material contradictions rather than discard them because they are inconvenient. A classroom self-check is not independent business approval."}],"exercise":"Tabletop case, not a claim that a real SME or sponsor consented. Use a fictional industrial-components SME deciding whether to explore segment A, segment B or defer. The learner plays the analyst; a fictional sponsor role can review the draft. Label every invented name, figure and response as synthetic. A signature box or self-check does not prove real approval. Do not contact a business, customer or employee, copy private records, sign terms or spend money to finish the lab. For any later real project, first verify an actual owner's authority, permitted data/use/audience, participant permission where relevant and applicable requirements; stop affected real work if those are absent. Public filings are not a source of private customer-level evidence.\nDraft a one-page scope: decision and sponsor role; intended private audience; 12-week pilot horizon; alternatives A/B/defer; current-service counterfactual; objective and source/definition for every measure; included/excluded work; assumptions, evidence gaps and reviewers. Illustrative objective: at least 2 lakh incremental contribution after all stated variable and avoidable fixed costs over 12 weeks, peak incremental cash draw no more than 3 lakh, and at least 95 percent on-time deliveries among the defined eligible pilot deliveries. These are proposed synthetic thresholds, not signed-off commitments or a claim they can all be met. Define order eligibility, promised date, exclusions, cancelled orders and a fixed window before measuring delivery performance. If there are no eligible deliveries, report insufficient evidence rather than 100 percent on-time performance. Do not equate revenue, profit, cash or a sponsor's enthusiasm.\nBaseline fictional current-service month: revenue 10 lakh, variable costs 7 lakh, avoidable fixed costs 2 lakh leaves 1 lakh after those costs, not complete cash flow. Do not mix that one-month baseline with 12-week pilot totals without a time bridge. Ask which labor, working capital, cannibalization, financing, quality and capacity effects would change the counterfactual; a no-action alternative can still have costs or risks in reality. Predeclare base/upside/downside and reversal thresholds. Any planned access, outreach, data sharing or spending remains outside classroom authority. Assign fictional role owners at day 0/30/60/90 for permission, evidence, cash/quality review and stop/continue decisions; do not pretend every gate lands exactly at a 12-week endpoint. Deliverable: a private draft charter with an evidence/permission register, strongest objection and unresolved sign-off, unless a real authorized reviewer actually gave it.<section aria-label=\"Native D71 practice\"><h3>Private charter and evidence gates</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d71-charter.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">7-sheet editable lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d71-charter.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">7-page print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own before checking the reference. Original synthetic case and rubric remain intact.</p><p>Original one-month 10/7/2 lakh INR remainder is 1, not a 12-week forecast. Proposed contribution 2/draw 3/on-time 95% are draft targets, not signed-off commitments or feasible A/B estimates. Alternatives share the same 12-week horizon; missing contribution, dated cash and delivery evidence stay UNKNOWN. Count definitions precede a guarded on-time denominator. Day 84 is twelve weeks; day 90 is a later review. Permission and evidence registers, fictional gates, strongest authored objection and conditional response are included. Actual learner/reviewer/transfer fields remain blank. Two formula outputs tested, denominator 19/20 and 18/20 checked; six invalid count and three invalid money cases leave affected outputs undefined. Blank actual quality is intentionally #N/A, not zero or 100%.</p><p>Authored LibreOffice execution plus independent arithmetic only. Static prose is Case-v1 and must be revised after changed inputs. No actual learner, reviewer, sponsor approval, empirical-source reading, full AV, Excel/Sheets parity, account flow, business commitment or overall 10/10 is established. Private solo classroom work requires no real records, contacts, spending or recording.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Name the fictional sponsor and analyst roles, private audience, decision and 12-week horizon; do not claim a real SME or owner consented.","Define the proposed contribution, peak-cash and eligible-delivery measures and thresholds; distinguish the one-month baseline from pilot totals.","Compare segment A, segment B and defer on the same horizon; state what belongs inside and outside the pilot.","Mandatory provenance: label all fictional inputs; record each real source, date, unit, permitted use/audience and unresolved data or participant permissions.","Trace operating assumptions to contribution, receivables, cash, capacity and possible cannibalization rather than equate sales with cash.","Test the scope failure: no real data, outreach, signature or spending without the relevant authority; a public filing does not grant private customer access.","Mandatory downside/reversal: state coherent changed assumptions, the threshold that changes the recommendation and material evidence still missing.","Separate observations, estimates, judgments and proposed targets; make unresolved sign-off and uncertainty visible.","Mandatory owner/gate: assign fictional day 0/30/60/90 role owners, permission/cash/quality checks and measurable stop/continue conditions.","Deliver the private draft charter, strongest objection and revision log; self-critique if no reviewer exists, without calling it independent approval."]}},"72":{"objectives":["Test a fictional market-pilot charter with same-horizon contribution, receivables and cash bridges, separating endpoint cash from peak funding and proposed targets from approved actions."],"videos":[{"id":"fFyaJl9T74g","title":"The Research Problem & Problem Statement: Plain-Language Explainer (With Examples) + FREE Templates","by":"Grad Coach","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. The publisher companion supports research-problem framing; use the written fictional charter and workbook for data-permission and cash decisions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Grad Coach - research problem and statement","url":"https://gradcoach.com/research-problem-statement/","note":"Publisher article embeds the assigned video and explains problem, consequence, research gap and practical feasibility. Academic research framing, not SME consent, sponsor approval or a cash forecast. Written companion, not a verbatim transcript."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - problem, alternatives and business model","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"Use the problem, current alternatives, market and business-model prompts as an outline. This pitching guide does not establish sponsor consent, data rights, a project cash forecast or approval."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - research records and privacy practice","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/taking-notes-and-recording-user-research-sessions","note":"Read consent, permitted uses, minimum identifying details, secure handling and permission before copying materials. UK service-research guidance is an ethical workflow reference here, not an Indian legal-compliance finding. Use the fictional case by default."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - observations, findings and actions","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/analyse-a-research-session","note":"Keep observed evidence separate from interpretation and proposed action. Preserve material contradictions rather than discard them because they are inconvenient. A classroom self-check is not independent business approval."}],"exercise":"Synthetic workbook following D71, not actual company data or sponsor approval. All amounts below are lakh rupees over the same 12-week horizon; A/B/defer are mutually exclusive. Assume no cannibalization or capacity conflict solely for this teaching case, and list evidence needed to test those assumptions. Variable and avoidable fixed costs are complete stated incremental costs and are paid within the horizon; no unpaid payables, tax, financing, depreciation or other cash effects are included. Upfront equipment spending is separate capex, not an extra variable-cost line. Uncollected recognized revenue becomes end-period receivables, with no bad debts assumed.\nA: recognized revenue 9, variable cost 5.4, avoidable fixed 1.2, collections 6.3, capex 1. Contribution after those operating costs=2.4. Receivables=2.7. Endpoint incremental cash=6.3-5.4-1.2-1=-1.3, also 2.4-2.7-1=-1.3. B: revenue 12, variable 8.4, fixed 2.4, collections 8.4, capex 2. Operating remainder=1.2; receivables=3.6; endpoint cash=8.4-8.4-2.4-2=-4.4, also 1.2-3.6-2=-4.4. Defer has zero incremental revenue/cost/cash in this stated case, not universally in real life. Revenue ranking B>A does not rank contribution or funding need the same way.\nProposed classroom gates: at least 2 contribution, at most 3 peak cash draw, at least 95 percent on-time delivery under the D71 definition. A clears the operating threshold in base assumptions; B fails it and its endpoint draw already exceeds the cash cap. A's 1.3 endpoint draw does not prove its peak draw stays below 3. Build dated weekly receipts, payment and capex flows; no delivery-quality evidence is supplied, so that gate also remains unresolved. No pilot is fully approved by these totals.\nA downside: revenue 6, variable cost still 60 percent of revenue=3.6, fixed 1.2, collections 70 percent=4.2, capex 1. Operating remainder=1.2, receivables=1.8, endpoint cash=-1.6. With the stated constant 40 percent contribution margin and fixed 1.2, revenue 3 is operating breakeven; revenue 8 is needed for the proposed 2 contribution target. Neither threshold proves demand, collection, peak cash or quality. Do not use an unexplained percentage haircut on every line. Deliverable: labeled input/source/permission log, A/B/defer table, two-way cash reconciliation, dated funding schedule and conditional recommendation. Mark fictional sponsor review as fictional; no actual signature, customer-data access or capital release is required or authorized.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D72 cash timing lab\"><h3>Same cash total, different funding need</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d72-cash-timing-practice.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable D72 cash-timing workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d72-cash-timing-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read its nine-sheet print view (PDF)</a>. Keep your own first attempt. The original case and rubric are unchanged. The workbook adds explicitly invented A-only timing assumptions, not dates recovered from the original case. Inputs are yellow; formulas and an assumption registry make each result traceable.</p><p>For the original 12-week totals in lakh INR, A has operating remainder 2.4, receivables 2.7 and ending incremental cash minus 1.3; B has 1.2, 3.6 and minus 4.4. A downside has 1.2, 1.8 and minus 1.6. Defer is zero. Direct collections-minus-payments and indirect operating-minus-receivables-minus-capex bridges reconcile. A's constant 40% contribution margin implies break-even revenue 3 and revenue 8 for operating remainder 2. The original peak draw remains unknown without dated events.</p><p>The new weekly-end schedules all preserve A totals and endpoint minus 1.3: staged receipts/payments give peak draw 1.3; early costs give 5.5; all receipts in week 12 give 7.05, first reached at week 11. The proposed cash cap is 3. Moving the late 6.3 receipt to week 0 changes peak draw to 1.3 without changing endpoint cash. A passing week-end snapshot is conditional on that invented schedule; payment before receipt within a week can require more funding. Quality stays UNKNOWN. These are classroom candidates, not permission or a real capital decision.</p><p>Native LibreOffice recalculation passed 170 baseline and 170 restored comparisons against independent rational calculations, plus the three moved-receipt checks. Controlled invalid totals, negative/text/blank receipts, collections above revenue, a broken reference and a zero-revenue denominator were tested. Invalid or undefined outputs stay flagged instead of becoming fake zeroes. All nine print sheets and saved formula caches were inspected. The Failure log records authored model tests, not a learner attempt, independent review or Excel/Sheets parity. No macros, external links, real company data, account access or learner-progress changes are included.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Label this a synthetic 12-week workbook with fictional review roles and A/B/defer alternatives, not an approved company pilot.","Compute A operating remainder 2.4 and B 1.2; keep the proposed 2-lakh target separate from the current-service baseline.","Use the same horizon and incremental basis; state no cannibalization/capacity conflict as assumptions and include defer explicitly.","Mandatory provenance: log each input, source, unit/date and synthetic status; retain data-use/audience permissions as unresolved for any real application.","Reconcile A receivables 2.7/cash-1.3 and B receivables 3.6/cash-4.4 from both collections and operating-remainder bridges, without double-counting capex.","Distinguish endpoint cash from peak draw; require a dated cash schedule and missing quality evidence before claiming all proposed gates are met.","Mandatory downside/reversal: recompute A revenue 6/operating 1.2/cash-1.6; distinguish revenue 3 operating breakeven from revenue 8 for the 2-lakh target.","Explain the omitted real-world tax, financing, bad-debt and timing effects; do not turn synthetic sensitivity into a demand forecast.","Mandatory owner/gate: name fictional permission, weekly-cash and quality-review owners with day 0/30/60/90 stop/continue decisions, no actual capital release.","Deliver the private comparison, input log and conditional recommendation; record the strongest objection and correction without inventing a real sign-off."]}},"73":{"objectives":["Build a private fictional B2B evidence room that separates sampled interest, documented past behavior, buying authority and paid-test unit economics without cherry-picking or invented consent."],"videos":[{"id":"qAws7eXItMk","title":"Lecture 16 - How to Run a User Interview (Emmett Shear)","by":"YC Root Access","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Interview context accompanies the written evidence-room lab. The linked 2019 publisher transcript is a separate interview lesson, not a transcript of this assigned recording."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC - interview behavior and context (2019 publisher transcript)","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/startup-school-week-1-recap-kevin-hale-and-eric-migicovsky","note":"Read the Eric Migicovsky section, 15:19-38:21, for past events, current workarounds, cost/frequency and purchasing authority rather than compliments/hypotheticals. This is a different talk from the assigned Emmett Shear recording, not its transcript. No 40-percent PMF threshold, collecting contact details or taking payments is required by this lab."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - notes, permitted use and privacy","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/taking-notes-and-recording-user-research-sessions","note":"Consent and scope matter before notes, recordings or copying materials. Prefer the fictional rows; real recordings/customer information are not required. UK service-research guidance does not establish compliance with Indian law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - evidence to findings to action","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/analyse-a-research-session","note":"Separate observations, interpretations and actions, retain source links and contradictions. Sorting themes does not justify deleting a material negative account or treating sample interest as market demand."}],"exercise":"Fictional SME segment evidence room, not actual interviews or a consenting business. Use invented account IDs, observations and source artifacts labeled SYNTHETIC. Do not fabricate quotations, permission records, names, signatures or real-company citations. A hypothetical sponsor is a role, not actual permission. No outreach, recordings, payments, private customer records, public upload or signup is needed. Any later real research needs independently verified scope, participant/data permission, secure handling, permitted audience and applicable requirements. A business sponsor's approval alone is not a participant's recording permission.\nPredefine eligible account/segment, recruitment channel, invited/completed denominators, time window and nonresponse. Include skeptical customers, lost prospects and current alternatives rather than only enthusiasts; that sampling plan is proposed, not proof of representative research. Use the D74 fictional 13-completion ledger. Separate four evidence types: generic interest; a specific recent problem/current workaround and its source; purchasing authority/budget; a paid test with transaction and delivery-cost records. Each supports a different inference. Stated willingness is not payment, and a synthetic payment is not real market validation. One paid test is not repeat demand, causality or profitable scale.\nInterview guide for a later separately authorized study: ask about the last actual occurrence, context, impact/frequency, workaround/cost, decision process and what failed. Do not pitch first, lead with the desired feature or treat a compliment as a buying commitment. Participant burden, consent and data minimization still apply; a speaker's advice to collect many details does not override this boundary. In the tabletop, write clearly labeled invented paraphrases, not quotation marks suggesting real speech.\nEvidence ledger fields: account/segment, eligibility/recruitment, invitation and completion status, event window, observation/source reference, corroborating artifact, cost/frequency, buyer/authority, test/payment status, permission/audience/retention status, contradiction and confidence. Give each conclusion a traceable path from observation to interpretation to proposed next test. Remove duplicates, show nonresponse and disconfirming evidence. Pseudonymous IDs do not by themselves prove true anonymity. Retain a negative account even if isolated when it could reverse the decision. Deliverable: private synthetic ledger, interview guide, evidence hierarchy and next-test plan with a material objection. No claim of completed real interviews, statistical representativeness, legal clearance or business sign-off.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D73 D74 denominator lab\"><h3>Keep the accounts. Change the denominator.</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d73-d74-evidence-room.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable D73-D74 denominator and paid-test workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d73-d74-evidence-room.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read its eight-page print view (PDF)</a>. Seven sheets retain a 40-account synthetic roster, 13 completed-account code rows, named-denominator funnel, unit economics, codebook and authored failure log. This is counting and economics practice alongside the original evidence-room task, not a substitute for the qualitative observation-to-interpretation register or actual permission checks. The original case and rubric are unchanged.</p><p>The baseline reconciles A 20 eligible/12 invited/8 completed and B 20/10/5. Invited nonresponses are 4 and 5, separate from the completed skeptics A07/A08/B05. Interest is 6/8 versus 4/5; past workaround 5/8 versus 1/5; buyer-budget 3/8 versus 1/5; paid tests 2/8 versus 0/5. Tests among invitees are separately 2/12 versus 0/10. Synthetic artifact IDs are invented labels, not real invoices or quotations. No single demand score or population inference is justified by these codes.</p><p>Changed context: mark A01 and B01 ineligible without deleting either row. A then has 19 eligible/11 invited/7 completed, interest 5, workaround 4, buyer-budget 2 and one paid test; B has 19/9/4, interest 3 and zero workaround/buyer-budget/paid-test codes. Every rate uses its corresponding new stage denominator. A economics changes from two-test revenue INR 6000, variable 3600, contribution 2400 and after-setup remainder 1400 to one-test remainder 200. Extra rework 2000 gives minus 600 in the base and minus 1800 after the eligibility change. Neither remainder is actual cash flow, repeat demand or an authorized budget.</p><p>Native LibreOffice recalculation passed 103 baseline, 103 changed-context and 103 restored checks against independent exact-rational calculations. Duplicate IDs, absent synthetic artifact labels, negative/2/text/blank binary codes, completion without invitation, a nonresponse wrongly counted as completed, a broken count reference and invalid economics inputs were tested. Filtering all B accounts out leaves its six zero-denominator rates undefined, not 0%. Saved XLSX has 116 complete formula caches, no error cells, macros or external links. All eight print pages and changed mobile/desktop course states were inspected. This is authored native execution only, not independent learner validation, Excel/Sheets parity or real market evidence. No outreach, real payments, recordings, participant records, account access or learner-progress change is requested or created.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Label the project, sponsor role, account IDs and artifacts synthetic; state the private decision, audience and research question without invented real consent.","Define eligibility, segment, channel, window and invitation/completion denominators; distinguish nonresponse, refusal and completed negative evidence.","Include alternatives and skeptical/lost accounts; explain convenience/recruitment bias rather than claim the sample is representative.","Mandatory provenance: link each invented observation to a labeled synthetic row/artifact and separate fact, paraphrase, interpretation and permission status.","Separate interest, past behavior/workaround, authority/budget and paid-test evidence; connect cost-to-serve to contribution, not just expressed enthusiasm.","Use past-event neutral interview prompts and preserve material contradictions; no actual recording, customer-data use or outreach is needed or approved.","Mandatory downside/reversal: name a disconfirming observation and a cost/demand assumption that would reverse the proposed next test.","Keep duplicates, missing evidence, nonresponse, uncertainty and access/retention limits visible; IDs alone do not guarantee anonymity.","Mandatory owner/gate: assign fictional evidence/privacy/cost owners, day 0/30/60/90 reviews and permission/cost/data-quality stop conditions.","Deliver the private evidence-room draft, guide and strongest objection; distinguish tabletop self-check from independent validation or business sign-off."]}},"74":{"objectives":["Reconcile a synthetic B2B interview funnel and paid-test contribution, keeping denominators, observation strength, nonresponse and launch uncertainty explicit."],"videos":[{"id":"qAws7eXItMk","title":"Lecture 16 - How to Run a User Interview (Emmett Shear)","by":"YC Root Access","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Interview context accompanies the written evidence-room lab. The linked 2019 publisher transcript is a separate interview lesson, not a transcript of this assigned recording."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC - interview behavior and context (2019 publisher transcript)","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/startup-school-week-1-recap-kevin-hale-and-eric-migicovsky","note":"Read the Eric Migicovsky section, 15:19-38:21, for past events, current workarounds, cost/frequency and purchasing authority rather than compliments/hypotheticals. This is a different talk from the assigned Emmett Shear recording, not its transcript. No 40-percent PMF threshold, collecting contact details or taking payments is required by this lab."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - notes, permitted use and privacy","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/taking-notes-and-recording-user-research-sessions","note":"Consent and scope matter before notes, recordings or copying materials. Prefer the fictional rows; real recordings/customer information are not required. UK service-research guidance does not establish compliance with Indian law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - evidence to findings to action","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/analyse-a-research-session","note":"Separate observations, interpretations and actions, retain source links and contradictions. Sorting themes does not justify deleting a material negative account or treating sample interest as market demand."}],"exercise":"All accounts, events and payment records are invented for a private workbook, not real customer evidence. Eligible rosters contain 20 accounts in A and 20 in B. Invitations: A12, B10. Completed interviews: A8, B5. Remaining invited accounts are nonresponses, not negative interviews or proven lack of need. Completed IDs A01-A08 and B01-B05 identify unique fictional accounts.\nCreate 13 rows with these synthetic codes. General interest: A01-A06 and B01-B04. Documented past-month paid workaround: A01-A05 and B01. Purchasing authority plus stated budget: A01-A03 and B01. Completed paid tests: A01-A02 and no B accounts. A07/A08 and B05 give non-enthusiastic or skeptical observations that must remain visible. These are distinct codes, not one interchangeable interest score. Even the coded past behavior and budget statements are not independently audited facts. Add a fictional artifact reference for each positive code without fabricating a real invoice or quotation.\nInvitation-to-completion rates: 8/12=66.67 percent and 5/10=50 percent. Completed-sample interest: 6/8=75 percent and 4/5=80 percent. The larger B interest percentage does not establish a larger or better market. Past-workaround rates among completions are 5/8 versus 1/5; authority/budget 3/8 versus 1/5; completed paid tests 2/8 versus 0/5. Tests among invitees are 2/12 versus 0/10. Always name denominator and stage. Do not infer population rates or confidence intervals without an appropriate design, or compare an A numerator with a B denominator.\nIndependent synthetic A paid-test economics: two delivered tests at 3,000 rupees each, complete stated variable cost 1,800 each, plus avoidable one-time setup cost 1,000. Revenue=6,000; total variable cost=3,600; contribution=2,400; remainder after setup=1,400. The 1,400 remainder is not revenue, cash flow or a forecast of recurring profit. Collections, tax, fixed overhead, acquisition, refunds and repeat demand remain outside these stated figures and need separate evidence; no actual collection is requested. If one test incurs extra rework cost 2,000 outside the original variable estimate, remainder falls to minus 600. Revenue per test alone does not show cost-to-serve or margin. Do not assume a B price or full-market revenue forecast from these A figures.\nDeliverable: private 13-row synthetic ledger, invitation/completion/code reconciliation, unit-cost sensitivity, material contradictory observation and conditional next-test plan. Name fictional evidence/cost owners and day 0/30/60/90 stop/continue gates, not actual outreach or pilot approval. A future real test needs separate permission, agreed terms and cost authority. Self-check is not independent verification; do not claim real customer consent or completed fieldwork.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D73 D74 denominator lab\"><h3>Keep the accounts. Change the denominator.</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d73-d74-evidence-room.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable D73-D74 denominator and paid-test workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d73-d74-evidence-room.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read its eight-page print view (PDF)</a>. Seven sheets retain a 40-account synthetic roster, 13 completed-account code rows, named-denominator funnel, unit economics, codebook and authored failure log. This is counting and economics practice alongside the original evidence-room task, not a substitute for the qualitative observation-to-interpretation register or actual permission checks. The original case and rubric are unchanged.</p><p>The baseline reconciles A 20 eligible/12 invited/8 completed and B 20/10/5. Invited nonresponses are 4 and 5, separate from the completed skeptics A07/A08/B05. Interest is 6/8 versus 4/5; past workaround 5/8 versus 1/5; buyer-budget 3/8 versus 1/5; paid tests 2/8 versus 0/5. Tests among invitees are separately 2/12 versus 0/10. Synthetic artifact IDs are invented labels, not real invoices or quotations. No single demand score or population inference is justified by these codes.</p><p>Changed context: mark A01 and B01 ineligible without deleting either row. A then has 19 eligible/11 invited/7 completed, interest 5, workaround 4, buyer-budget 2 and one paid test; B has 19/9/4, interest 3 and zero workaround/buyer-budget/paid-test codes. Every rate uses its corresponding new stage denominator. A economics changes from two-test revenue INR 6000, variable 3600, contribution 2400 and after-setup remainder 1400 to one-test remainder 200. Extra rework 2000 gives minus 600 in the base and minus 1800 after the eligibility change. Neither remainder is actual cash flow, repeat demand or an authorized budget.</p><p>Native LibreOffice recalculation passed 103 baseline, 103 changed-context and 103 restored checks against independent exact-rational calculations. Duplicate IDs, absent synthetic artifact labels, negative/2/text/blank binary codes, completion without invitation, a nonresponse wrongly counted as completed, a broken count reference and invalid economics inputs were tested. Filtering all B accounts out leaves its six zero-denominator rates undefined, not 0%. Saved XLSX has 116 complete formula caches, no error cells, macros or external links. All eight print pages and changed mobile/desktop course states were inspected. This is authored native execution only, not independent learner validation, Excel/Sheets parity or real market evidence. No outreach, real payments, recordings, participant records, account access or learner-progress change is requested or created.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Build 13 unique synthetic completed-account rows with segment eligibility, invitation/completion status and clearly invented artifact references.","Reconcile A:12 invited/8 completed and B:10 invited/5 completed; label 66.67 percent/50 percent completion rates and their windows.","Keep skeptical accounts and nonresponses visible; compare A/B and defer without assuming interview interest is population demand.","Mandatory provenance: retain per-account code/artifact definitions, synthetic status and source/date/unit; do not invent real invoices, quotations or consent.","Reconcile interest 6/8 versus 4/5, workaround 5/8 versus 1/5, buyer-budget 3/8 versus 1/5 and tests 2/8 versus 0/5; name the stages and denominators.","Compute two-test revenue 6,000, variable cost 3,600, contribution 2,400 and after-setup remainder 1,400; keep collections/overheads/repeat demand separate.","Mandatory downside/reversal: add 2,000 rework cost to show minus 600 remainder; explain why the higher B interest percentage cannot alone choose a launch.","Separate evidence strength, missing records and recruitment bias from interpretation; do not claim statistical representativeness, causal lift or real validation.","Mandatory owner/gate: assign fictional evidence/cost/permission owners, day 0/30/60/90 reviews and separate authority before any real paid pilot.","Deliver the private 13-row ledger, unit bridge and conditional next-test plan; log an objection and correction without inventing fieldwork or sign-off."]}},"75":{"objectives":["Frame a fictional build/partner/defer decision with same-horizon net payoffs, explicit uncertainty, funding constraints and subjective tradeoffs rather than incompatible comparisons or automatic spending approval."],"videos":[{"id":"ydvnVw80I_8","title":"Decision Analysis 3: Decision Trees","by":"Joshua Emmanuel","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. The written fictional decision tree supplies payoffs, probability and funding assumptions; the MIT readings are separate teaching sources, not a transcript of this assigned recording."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.053 - decision trees (2013)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-053-optimization-methods-in-management-science-spring-2013/4dd30003fe23dc89d27f55b500c15d15_MIT15_053S13_lec18.pdf","note":"Read chronology, decisions versus chance events, subjective probabilities, backward evaluation and sensitivity. Use the stated hypothetical risk-neutral monetary objective, not a claim that highest expected money is always right or funded."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.053 - value of information (2013)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-053-optimization-methods-in-management-science-spring-2013/b68f3b255275ffa42099958e373248b1_MIT15_053S13_lec19.pdf","note":"Read information before a decision and value relative to the best no-information action. Perfect information is hypothetical and an upper bound; real pilots need signal accuracy, conditional probabilities, cost and delay. Historical gambling/medical examples are teaching context, not current probabilities or advice."}],"exercise":"Fictional SME decision, not an actual business authorization. Choose among build, partner and defer over the same 12-month horizon. State the decision owner as a fictional role and keep the analysis private. Define a common favorable/unfavorable market state; a choice is not a random event and alternatives do not occur simultaneously. Predeclare outcomes, dates, variable/avoidable fixed costs, equipment/residual value, working capital, timing and discount basis. Exclude historical sunk costs from incremental choice while including real future exit/termination obligations. Delay can change payoff, available options and evidence; it is not universally free.\nTeaching model: every endpoint below is a net present monetary payoff in lakh rupees at the same decision date, already after all case-specific investment/operating costs. Build: favorable 12, unfavorable minus 8. Partner: favorable 6, unfavorable 1. Defer: zero in either state. The common favorable-state probability is a synthetic assumption, not measured demand. Use p=0.65 and 1-p=0.35. Expected build=5; partner=4.25; defer=0 under a risk-neutral monetary objective. Expected payoff is neither a promised return nor cash available today. Build has an unfavorable loss 8 even though its average is highest. Do not subtract initial investment again from endpoints that already include it. Subjective risk tolerance, hard constraints or a different objective can change the preferred action.\nIndependent liquidity screen for this same decision: build needs 10 at day 0; partner needs 2; available uncommitted cash 6 and no verified financing. The build action is not currently financeable under that screen; do not pretend its expected payoff funds the upfront outlay. Partner passing this initial check does not prove its full cash path is safe. A private model cannot authorize a purchase, borrowing, contract or partner commitment.\nOptional subjective matrix, separate from monetary payoffs: proposed weights cost 40 percent/time 30 percent/control 30 percent, each criterion scored 1-5 with higher better. Illustrative build scores 2/2/5 give 2.9; partner 4/4/2 gives 3.4; defer 5/1/1 gives 2.6. These are invented preferences, not measured economic value. Define scales, avoid double counting and test weights; do not add 3.4 points to 4.25 lakh or let a score overrule an unmet legal, safety or funding condition. Explain why rankings differ and what the owner values before recommending.\nReversibility is a contractual/operating fact to check, not a label attached to every partnership. Record termination fees, sunk integration, timing and data/IP dependence where relevant. Deliverable: private decision framing, same-basis tree, constraint screen, matrix and conditional triggers. Identify the strongest objection, actual evidence still needed and fictional day 0/30/60/90 review owners; no real spending or third-party contact is required. The monetary ranking and any stated perfect-information calculation first ignore the separate financing constraint to isolate the tree arithmetic. With the hard cash screen applied and no new financing, build is unavailable in both states. Partner then beats defer in both states, so perfect market-state information alone has zero gross value within that restricted action set. Information does not create financing; recompute the baseline and informed choices whenever the feasible set changes.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D75 D76 decision lab\"><h3>Can information change a funded choice?</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d75-d76-decision-lab.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable decision and information workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d75-d76-decision-lab.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read the seven-page print view (PDF)</a>. Seven sheets retain the original 12-month, decision-date, already-net lakh-INR payoffs, p 0.65, day-0 needs 10/2, available cash 6 and separate subjective matrix. Original cases and rubrics are unchanged. The artifact supports formulas and constraint reasoning, not real financing or approval.</p><p>Baseline build EV 5 exceeds partner 4.25; crossover p 0.60; unrestricted perfect-information payoff 8.15 gives gross EVPI 3.15. But build has an upfront shortfall 4. With that hard cash screen, partner wins in both states and state information alone has zero gross value. The sensitivity sheet recomputes 21 probabilities from 0 to 1 using the current feasible set. Matrix points 2.9/3.4/2.6 stay separate from monetary payoffs.</p><p>New explicitly invented transfer: P(positive | favorable)=0.8 and P(positive | unfavorable)=0.2, immediate research cost 2 before the action, no delay or changed options. Joint probabilities yield P(positive)=0.59 and favorable posterior 0.52/0.59, not certainty or the prior. Unrestricted sample-information payoff 6.74 gives gross EVSI 1.74, below EVPI 3.15. After cost, payoff 4.74 is 0.26 below no research. With cash 6, cost 2 leaves 4 and only partner/defer funded; research payoff 2.25 is 2 below the cash-screened baseline. With cash 2, cost 2 leaves only defer: payoff minus 2, change minus 6.25. These signals/timing are authored assumptions, not observed pilot accuracy, a research recommendation or a spending allowance.</p><p>Native LibreOffice passed 184 baseline, 184 probability-transfer, 184 cash-transfer and 184 restored exact-rational checks, plus funded-build, lower-cost, prior-endpoint, uninformative/perfect/impossible-signal tests. Zero-probability signals have undefined posteriors but zero joint branch contribution. Negative/out-of-range/text/blank inputs, unaffordable research, bad weights/scores, parallel slopes and a broken reference were tested. Saved XLSX has 184 cached formulas, seven sheets, no errors, macros or external links. All seven print pages and changed 320/390/1280 course states were visually inspected. This is authored native execution, not independent learner transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, full cash-path safety or actual market evidence. No research purchase, borrowing, contract, third-party contact, account or learner-progress change was made.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Name the fictional owner, private audience, build/partner/defer alternatives and same 12-month decision horizon.","Define favorable/unfavorable states, endpoint units and already-net present-value basis, rather than mix revenue, profit, cash and points.","Distinguish decision choices from chance events; include defer and identify which future costs and exit obligations belong in each alternative.","Mandatory provenance: mark all probabilities, payoffs, scores and weights synthetic; record actual source/date/units and unresolved evidence.","Compute build EV 5 versus partner 4.25 at p 0.65, and screen 10/2 upfront needs against 6 cash without spending expected payoff.","Explain downside loss, reversibility and funding/operating constraints; do not let a matrix authorize an otherwise infeasible action.","Mandatory downside/reversal: test probability/payoff changes and the 0.60 build/partner crossover; state what evidence changes the recommendation.","Define subjective matrix scales/weights, avoid double counting and separate 2.9/3.4/2.6 points from monetary payoffs.","Mandatory owner/gate: assign fictional day 0/30/60/90 evidence/funding/contract-review owners with stop/continue conditions, no actual release.","Deliver the private decision tree, constraint log and strongest objection; state missing authority and whether the critique changes the provisional recommendation."]}},"76":{"objectives":["Compute synthetic build/partner/defer expected values, reversal probability and perfect-information bounds while keeping pilot quality and funding as separate unresolved tests."],"videos":[{"id":"ydvnVw80I_8","title":"Decision Analysis 3: Decision Trees","by":"Joshua Emmanuel","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. The written fictional decision tree supplies payoffs, probability and funding assumptions; the MIT readings are separate teaching sources, not a transcript of this assigned recording."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.053 - decision trees (2013)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-053-optimization-methods-in-management-science-spring-2013/4dd30003fe23dc89d27f55b500c15d15_MIT15_053S13_lec18.pdf","note":"Read chronology, decisions versus chance events, subjective probabilities, backward evaluation and sensitivity. Use the stated hypothetical risk-neutral monetary objective, not a claim that highest expected money is always right or funded."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.053 - value of information (2013)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-053-optimization-methods-in-management-science-spring-2013/b68f3b255275ffa42099958e373248b1_MIT15_053S13_lec19.pdf","note":"Read information before a decision and value relative to the best no-information action. Perfect information is hypothetical and an upper bound; real pilots need signal accuracy, conditional probabilities, cost and delay. Historical gambling/medical examples are teaching context, not current probabilities or advice."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic numerical workbook, not a funded project. Same 12-month horizon and decision-date present-value basis for every net endpoint, in lakh rupees. Endpoints already include case-specific investment and operating costs: build 12 favorable/minus 8 unfavorable; partner 6/1; defer 0/0. One common favorable state has probability p, with unfavorable 1-p; mutually exclusive and exhaustive within this simplified model. The decision-maker is risk-neutral for the stated calculation. Probability and payoff assumptions are invented, not estimated from a real SME or validated by a video title.\nAt p 0.65: build 0.65*12+0.35*(-8)=5; partner 0.65*6+0.35*1=4.25; defer 0. At p 0.50: build 2, partner 3.5, defer 0. Build EV=20 p-8; partner EV=5 p+1; their crossover is p 0.60, where both equal 4. For p below 0.60 partner has higher EV; above 0.60 build has higher EV, subject to objective, constraints and payoff assumptions. Show the unfavorable loss and timing, not just the expected amount.\nPerfect information at p 0.65 would allow build in the favorable state and partner in the unfavorable state:0.65*12+0.35*1=8.15. Best without information is 5, so gross EVPI=3.15. Hypothetical instant, perfectly informative research costing 2 on the same present-value basis, with no delay or changed options, yields 6.15 total and 1.15 improvement over no research. This is not proof a real pilot has value 3.15 or is worth 2. A real imperfect pilot needs signal outcomes, conditional accuracy/posteriors, decision changes, cost and timing. Perfect information may be unavailable; cost or delay can erase value. Never replace a posterior with a prior or assume a positive signal is certainty.\nLiquidity screen: build requires 10 upfront, partner 2, current available cash 6 and no verified financing. Expected-value ranking does not remove build's 4 upfront shortfall; pilot cost may also use cash before an action and needs its own funding path. Do not subtract costs twice from already-net payoffs. Compare the private optional matrix separately from currency and preserve defer as a genuine alternative under stated costs. Deliverable: formulas, a probability sensitivity table, state-contingent choice tree, EVPI/cost bridge, downside and dated constraint log. No actual research purchase, contract, borrowing or partner commitment is approved by the result. The monetary ranking and any stated perfect-information calculation first ignore the separate financing constraint to isolate the tree arithmetic. With the hard cash screen applied and no new financing, build is unavailable in both states. Partner then beats defer in both states, so perfect market-state information alone has zero gross value within that restricted action set. Information does not create financing; recompute the baseline and informed choices whenever the feasible set changes.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D75 D76 decision lab\"><h3>Can information change a funded choice?</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d75-d76-decision-lab.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable decision and information workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d75-d76-decision-lab.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read the seven-page print view (PDF)</a>. Seven sheets retain the original 12-month, decision-date, already-net lakh-INR payoffs, p 0.65, day-0 needs 10/2, available cash 6 and separate subjective matrix. Original cases and rubrics are unchanged. The artifact supports formulas and constraint reasoning, not real financing or approval.</p><p>Baseline build EV 5 exceeds partner 4.25; crossover p 0.60; unrestricted perfect-information payoff 8.15 gives gross EVPI 3.15. But build has an upfront shortfall 4. With that hard cash screen, partner wins in both states and state information alone has zero gross value. The sensitivity sheet recomputes 21 probabilities from 0 to 1 using the current feasible set. Matrix points 2.9/3.4/2.6 stay separate from monetary payoffs.</p><p>New explicitly invented transfer: P(positive | favorable)=0.8 and P(positive | unfavorable)=0.2, immediate research cost 2 before the action, no delay or changed options. Joint probabilities yield P(positive)=0.59 and favorable posterior 0.52/0.59, not certainty or the prior. Unrestricted sample-information payoff 6.74 gives gross EVSI 1.74, below EVPI 3.15. After cost, payoff 4.74 is 0.26 below no research. With cash 6, cost 2 leaves 4 and only partner/defer funded; research payoff 2.25 is 2 below the cash-screened baseline. With cash 2, cost 2 leaves only defer: payoff minus 2, change minus 6.25. These signals/timing are authored assumptions, not observed pilot accuracy, a research recommendation or a spending allowance.</p><p>Native LibreOffice passed 184 baseline, 184 probability-transfer, 184 cash-transfer and 184 restored exact-rational checks, plus funded-build, lower-cost, prior-endpoint, uninformative/perfect/impossible-signal tests. Zero-probability signals have undefined posteriors but zero joint branch contribution. Negative/out-of-range/text/blank inputs, unaffordable research, bad weights/scores, parallel slopes and a broken reference were tested. Saved XLSX has 184 cached formulas, seven sheets, no errors, macros or external links. All seven print pages and changed 320/390/1280 course states were visually inspected. This is authored native execution, not independent learner transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, full cash-path safety or actual market evidence. No research purchase, borrowing, contract, third-party contact, account or learner-progress change was made.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Label the fictional workbook, monetary objective, common 12-month/PV basis and mutually exclusive build/partner/defer choices.","Calculate at p 0.65: build 5/partner 4.25/defer 0; at p 0.50: build 2/partner 3.5/defer 0, not guaranteed outcomes.","Use favorable and unfavorable probabilities summing to 1 and keep decisions separate from chance nodes.","Mandatory provenance: trace each payoff, probability, timing, net-cost treatment and source to an explicitly synthetic input log.","Keep already-net payoffs separate from upfront liquidity; show build 10 needed versus 6 cash and avoid subtracting investment twice.","Compute unconstrained perfect-information 8.15, baseline 5 and gross EVPI 3.15; with build infeasible and no financing, partner wins in either state and state information alone has zero value. Do not pay from a bound.","Mandatory downside/reversal: derive p 0.60 crossover, show unfavorable losses and test pilot cost/delay/signal uncertainty before any research recommendation.","Identify unvalidated subjective probabilities and missing conditional signal evidence; do not equate a positive test with certainty or EVPI with guaranteed pilot value.","Mandatory owner/gate: assign fictional evidence/cash/terms owners and day 0/30/60/90 checks; research costs and commitments need separate authority.","Deliver sensitivity, formulas and a conditional decision with the strongest objection; keep points, currency, funding feasibility and approval distinct."]}},"77":{"objectives":["Test changed-context transfer across project cash, decision uncertainty and evidence strength, then use a real private error log for provisional remediation rather than invented measured skills."],"videos":[{"id":"ydvnVw80I_8","title":"Decision Analysis 3: Decision Trees","by":"Joshua Emmanuel","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. The written fictional decision tree supplies payoffs, probability and funding assumptions; the MIT readings are separate teaching sources, not a transcript of this assigned recording."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.053 - decision trees (2013)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-053-optimization-methods-in-management-science-spring-2013/4dd30003fe23dc89d27f55b500c15d15_MIT15_053S13_lec18.pdf","note":"Read chronology, decisions versus chance events, subjective probabilities, backward evaluation and sensitivity. Use the stated hypothetical risk-neutral monetary objective, not a claim that highest expected money is always right or funded."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.053 - value of information (2013)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-053-optimization-methods-in-management-science-spring-2013/b68f3b255275ffa42099958e373248b1_MIT15_053S13_lec19.pdf","note":"Read information before a decision and value relative to the best no-information action. Perfect information is hypothetical and an upper bound; real pilots need signal accuracy, conditional probabilities, cost and delay. Historical gambling/medical examples are teaching context, not current probabilities or advice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - retrieval and mixed practice","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Closed-notes recall and mixed problem types support practice design. Product day scores are not per-question error diagnoses or measured learning gains."}],"exercise":"Review day, private fictional workbook. First solve without notes and explain formulas/units; then check sources and log attempted answer, correct reasoning, error cause and next changed-context test. Choose two remediation areas from actual observed mistakes. If errors are absent or sparse, call the choice provisional. The app stores a day score/timestamp, not question-level weak-skill measurements. A self-check or familiar-option recognition is not independent mastery certification.\nIndependent 12-week project case, amounts lakh rupees: recognized revenue 10, complete stated variable costs 6, avoidable fixed 1, collections 7, capex 2. All operating costs paid in the horizon, no other payables, tax or financing. Operating remainder 3, end receivables 3, endpoint cash 7-6-1-2=minus 2, also 3-3-2=minus 2. A proposed operating target 2.5 is met under assumptions, but peak draw and on-time quality cannot be established without dated cash and delivery evidence. A positive operating remainder is not enough to approve the pilot or claim cash generation.\nIndependent decision case, all net endpoints on a common decision-date present-value basis over 12 months, already after costs: build 14 favorable/minus 6 unfavorable; partner 7/2; defer 0. With favorable probability 0.5, build EV 4, partner EV 4.5; under the stated risk-neutral monetary objective partner is better without information. Crossover:20 p-6=5 p+2 gives p = 8/15 or 53.33 percent. At p 0.7 build 8 and partner 5.5. With perfect costless instant state information at p 0.5, choose build favorable and partner unfavorable:8. Gross EVPI=8-4.5=3.5, not a real pilot's guaranteed benefit. Explain missing signal accuracy, cost, delay and cash constraints. Do not mix these endpoints with the separate 12-week operating case.\nEvidence transfer: a larger B interview-interest fraction than A does not establish better paid demand, repeat use or contribution. Reconcile invitation/completion denominators and retain nonresponses, skeptical accounts, missing buying authority and cost-to-serve. Synthetic records remain synthetic. Sponsor approval does not replace participant permission, data rights or spending authority. New contradictory evidence changes affected inputs and recomputed conclusions visibly; keep the old reasoning and change log. Deliverable: private corrected calculations, manual error evidence, strongest objection and two delayed transfer prompts with fictional day 0/30/60/90 review roles. No actual business decision, research access or commitment is required or authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D77 practice\"><h3>Independent operating and PV decision cases</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d77-mixed-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">8-sheet editable lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d77-mixed-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">8-page print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own before checking the reference. Original synthetic case and rubric remain intact.</p><p>Project 12-week lakh INR 10 revenue/6 variable/1 fixed/7 collections/2 capex gives operating remainder 3, receivables 3 and endpoint cash -2. Dated peak, quality and actual authority are UNKNOWN even though target 2.5 passes. Separate 12-month common present-value net endpoints build 14/-6, partner 7/2, defer 0 at p=.5 give 4/4.5/0, perfect-information 8 and EVPI 3.5. At p=.7 build 8/partner 5.5, perfect 10.4/EVPI 2.4; crossover 8/15, endpoints p=0/1 yield EVPI 0. These cases are not merged. Actual signals need costs, delay and feasibility, not a promised EVPI return. Fifteen baseline formula outputs, collections/variable/probability/crossover/endpoint/restored cases, six invalid inputs and a broken reference were checked. Evidence and delayed transfer prompts do not manufacture actual errors, confidence or mastery.</p><p>Authored LibreOffice execution plus independent arithmetic only. Static prose is Case-v1 and must be revised after changed inputs. No actual learner, reviewer, sponsor approval, empirical-source reading, full AV, Excel/Sheets parity, account flow, business commitment or overall 10/10 is established. Private solo classroom work requires no real records, contacts, spending or recording.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Attempt fresh mixed problems without notes and label every case, input and fictional role; do not claim actual business consent.","Reconcile project operating 3, receivables 3 and endpoint cash minus 2 on the 12-week basis, separate from the decision-tree case.","Compare build/partner/defer at p 0.5 using the stated common PV net payoffs; show 4 versus 4.5 versus 0 and the changed p 0.7 case.","Mandatory provenance: log real sources, synthetic inputs, dates/units and actual attempted-error evidence; do not invent a question-level skill profile.","Explain why profit/endpoint cash do not establish peak funding or quality and why interview interest does not establish paid/repeat profitable demand.","Identify assumptions, wrong formulas, denominator confusions and contrary evidence, rather than repeat recognized answers or erase old reasoning.","Mandatory downside/reversal: derive 8/15 crossover and perfect-information 8 minus 4.5=3.5 bound; test cost/delay/signal limits and changed evidence.","Select two remediation areas from actual mistakes; label sparse-evidence choices provisional and separate confidence from demonstrated transfer.","Mandatory owner/gate: name private review roles, delayed changed-context tests and day 0/30/60/90 stop/review conditions without real business commitments.","Deliver corrected reasoning, error/change log and strongest objection; distinguish self-check from independent approval or psychometric mastery."]}},"78":{"objectives":["Build an explicitly synthetic Indian climate-startup sector map with separate mandate, commercial, financing and climate-evidence screens; produce five conditional diligence candidates and two private invest/pass-style memos without real commitments."],"videos":[{"id":"Wi3PiZsIfBU","title":"Decision Analysis in Venture Capital","by":"Stanford Decisions and Ethics Center","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - new venture valuation (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Read exit valuation, ownership, option pools/follow-on dilution and scenario limitations. Historical discount-rate/tax examples are not current Indian tax rules or required return benchmarks. Use the explicit synthetic common-equity model and distinguish equity from enterprise value."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA - performance definitions, granular methodology (2025)","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Distinguish committed/unfunded/invested amounts, portfolio gross MOIC and fund net TVPI/IRR. Actual fund documents govern fees, reserves, calls and carry; the case is invented."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GHG Protocol - inventory and project accounting (2023)","url":"https://ghgprotocol.org/blog/inventory-and-project-accounting","note":"Entity inventory versus system-wide project impact relative to counterfactual baseline. Avoided-emissions estimates are reported separately, not subtracted from corporate scopes. Explanatory article, not full standards/legal or credit-certification review."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Project Frame - pre-investment GHG assessment, selected sections","url":"https://projectframe.how/preinvestment-ghg-assessment","note":"Read introduction and Section 1: comparable unit, significant lifecycle effects, emissions-factor geography, baseline and uncertainty. Potential top-down impact differs from company planned bottom-up impact. Practitioner consensus, not regulation, financial advice or proof of investor additionality. Extended reading: Sections 2-4 on technology versus company SOM, recurring fleet/one-off effects, commercial-forecast consistency, attribution and debated additionality. The 15-30% long-horizon share illustration is not a universal assumption. No full appendix or cited-literature review claimed."}],"exercise":"Private teaching case. All twenty company IDs, Indian operations, sectors, stages, valuation/cash inputs and observations below are invented, not a map of real current companies or real recommendations. Do not attach a real company name to these figures. A future real-company map needs each company's original dated sources and independently qualified gaps. No outreach, diligence access, investment or fund formation is requested.\nFictional fund mandate: India operations, seed stage, initial cheque 2-5 crore, initial fully diluted post-money ownership at least 10 percent, and a proposed 4x gross single-deal base-case screen over 7 years. These are chosen teaching preferences, not market norms, legal eligibility or a guaranteed fund return. Passing a return formula is insufficient: climate evidence, economics, financing, mandate and rights remain separate. Review, defer and exclude are private screening labels, not authority to invest. A five-name diligence shortlist is not five simultaneous funded commitments.\nTable basis: all monetary inputs crore rupees. Twelve pipe-separated columns: ID, sector, geography, stage, pre-money, initial cheque, r, exit-equity downside, exit-equity base, exit-equity upside, screen, gap. r is the fraction of initial ownership retained after future dilution with NO extra fund investment; it is not percentage-point dilution. All exits are hypothetical distributable equity to common after debt and transaction claims, before fund-level fees/carry/tax, not enterprise headline. Assume fully converted common pro-rata payouts, no preferences/options added beyond the stated ownership/dilution model, no interim distributions, same 7-year exit date. Missing facts can invalidate the simplification. C20 is deliberately flagged because the purported equity basis is not substantiated; its numerical outputs are conditional only, not accepted equity proceeds.\nC01|industrial heat|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|review|Metered operating-energy evidence and embodied-emissions boundary still need independent checking.\nC02|cold-chain efficiency|India|seed|15|3|.75|30|120|240|review|Comparable service-level and refrigerant/leakage evidence remain open.\nC03|battery materials reuse|India|seed|36|4|.75|60|240|480|review|Feedstock rights, recovered-product quality and process energy remain unverified.\nC04|low-emission construction material|India|seed|24|4|.70|40|180|360|review|Durability, functional equivalence, certification and scale-up cost remain open.\nC05|farm energy efficiency|India|seed|12|3|.60|20|100|200|review|Actual adoption, water/rebound effects and sensitivity to dilution remain open.\nC06|grid software|India|seed|495|5|.50|1000|5000|10000|exclude|Initial ownership 1 percent misses fictional 10 percent minimum despite a 5x base payoff.\nC07|EV components|India|seed|20|5|.40|50|200|400|exclude|Base gross multiple 3.2x misses fictional 4x screen after stated dilution.\nC08|renewable asset operator|India|growth|20|5|.60|50|200|400|exclude|Growth stage outside seed-only mandate; headline return cannot waive mandate.\nC09|cooling efficiency|outside India|seed|15|3|.75|30|120|240|exclude|Outside fictional geography mandate, not a claim of poor business quality.\nC10|hydrogen equipment|India|seed|32|8|.60|80|320|640|exclude|Initial ticket 8 exceeds fictional 5 crore cap; cannot silently resize the offered round.\nC11|circular packaging|India|seed|8|1|.60|20|100|200|exclude|Initial ticket 1 below fictional 2 crore minimum.\nC12|water monitoring|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Climate mitigation link not established; adaptation/service benefits are separate, not automatically zero.\nC13|soil carbon measurement|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Baseline, permanence, leakage and verification unavailable; do not treat estimated carbon as issued credits.\nC14|recycling logistics|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Same avoided-tonne claim appears in another value-chain record; aggregation/attribution not resolved.\nC15|biomass heat|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Competing feedstock use, supply contract and emissions boundary unresolved.\nC16|industrial efficiency sensors|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Letters of interest only, not paid deployment or verified energy savings.\nC17|carbon capture equipment|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Energy penalty, storage terms, permanence and full financing plan unresolved.\nC18|fleet charging|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Interconnection, utilization and cash-timing evidence missing, no assumed permit approval.\nC19|textile reuse|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Imported market-size total not converted into eligible paid units or firm-specific economics.\nC20|distributed solar controls|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Exit enterprise headline includes debt; supplied equity proceeds basis needs reconciliation before use.\nMapping task: reproduce all 20 rows with sector/customer/value-chain role, geography/stage, units/dates, source-or-SYNTHETIC flag, entry ownership, financing/rights, climate boundary and strongest contradiction. Explain each review/defer/exclude reason, not a universal claim about the sector. C01-C05 are conditional diligence candidates under the supplied assumptions; missing critical evidence can still mean defer. C06-C11 demonstrate hard mandate/return exclusions, C12-C20 unresolved evidence, not proof those sectors are uninvestable. Deliver the 20-company map, five-name conditional shortlist and two private memos: C01 invest-style conditional thesis, C07 pass at stated economics. For C01 request no actual purchase: state missing operating/embodied evidence, financing, buyer/exit comparables and conditions that would reverse. For C07 exit ownership 8 percent gives 16 proceeds/5=3.2 x; equity 250 would reach 4x holding other assumptions fixed, but no buyer or execution evidence supports that price. Explicitly distinguish changed underwriting from arguing objections away. Name fictional day 0/30/60/90 owners and stop/review gates.\nOwnership formulas: initial cheque/(pre+cheque); exit ownership=initial ownership*r; gross cash proceeds=exit ownership*exit equity; gross deal MOIC=proceeds/total fund cash invested. No-follow-on assumptions keep cost equal to initial cheque; follow-ons would change both numerator ownership and denominator cost. MOIC is not annual IRR, net LP return or probability of success. If there were only a day 0 outflow and one year 7 receipt, annualized return would be MOIC^(1/7)-1, not MOIC/7; other dated cash flows require a cash-flow IRR. Do not sum multiples or use one winner as proof the fund returns 4 x.\nFive conditional diligence IDs C01-C05: initial checks 5+3+4+4+3=19 crore. Initial ownership 20 percent,16.6667 percent,10 percent,14.2857 percent,20 percent; exit ownership 12 percent,12.5 percent,7.5 percent,10 percent,12 percent. Base proceeds 24,15,18,18,12 =87; invested 19 gives 87/19=4.578947x gross for this hypothetical bundle, not an LP net multiple or forecast. Downside proceeds 6+3.75+4.5+4+2.4=20.65 gives 1.086842 x, excluding other portfolio outcomes/fund costs. Upside proceeds 48+30+36+36+24=174 gives 9.157895 x. Scenarios are not probability-weighted expected returns. C05 is exactly 4x base, not safely above the bar; if retention falls from 0.60 to 0.50 at the same exit 100, exit ownership 10 percent and MOIC 10/3=3.333333 x. C01 needs exit equity 166.666667 for 4 x; C02 needs 96; C03 needs 213.333333; C04 needs 160; C05 needs 100. These thresholds do not establish an achievable buyer or exit value.\nIndependent fund-budget screen: lifetime commitment 50 includes 10 fees/expenses in the stated fictional terms, leaving 40 for investments. Earmark 20 initial and 20 follow-on reserve. The shortlist initial 19 leaves 1 initial capacity; a proposed future follow-on schedule 4+3+5+4+5=21 would exceed 20 reserves by 1. Planned reserves are not invested cash, callable cash already on hand, a legal follow-on right or permission to invest. A future follow-on model must recompute holdings, total invested cost, timing and remaining commitments; do not keep 87/19 while silently spending 21 more. Cash availability, fees/expenses and fund documents need their own checks. Any actual decision requires its own owner approval.\nClimate impact lab, separate from financial exit arithmetic: equal-service baseline emits 0.80 tCO2e per synthetic unit and solution 0.50 including the case's stated material/manufacturing/distribution/use/disposal boundary. A hypothetical 10000 units deployed in one year gives(0.80-0.50)*10000=3000 tCO2e/year planned avoided emissions. Not realized, not a verified carbon credit, not revenue, not uniquely attributable to the investor, and not a deduction from corporate Scope 1/2/3 inventory. Under a decarbonizing baseline 0.55, same solution/units gives 500; at baseline 0.45 it gives minus 500, an increase. Missing leakage/rebound or unequal service could change the result. Document factor geography/year, functional unit, significant effects, replacement/adoption and dynamic baseline; do not multiply a TAM by invented penetration and call it actual company impact. Do not double-count an enabling supplier and final product as independent full savings. Adaptation/other environmental benefits may be important but are not interchangeable with avoided tonnes or equity returns.\n\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D78 D79 climate screen\"><h3>Can the arithmetic pass while the evidence still says defer?</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d78-d79-climate-screen.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable climate/seed screen (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d78-d79-climate-screen.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read the eleven-page print view (PDF)</a>. Eleven sheets reproduce the existing 20 invented company IDs, evidence gaps, supplied case labels and common-equity/no-follow-on assumptions. Original cases and rubrics remain unchanged. This is a synthetic classroom model, not a current company map, legal screen, actual diligence or investment recommendation.</p><p>Trace initial cheque/(pre+cheque), retained exit ownership, three scenario proceeds, base gross MOIC, target exit and two-flow annualized return. Geography/stage/cheque/ownership/return gates remain separate from evidence: C06 can show 5x while failing 1% ownership; C07 gives 3.2x and needs conditional exit equity 250 for 4x. C12-C20 keep DEFER after passing numerical gates, with C20 equity basis still unsubstantiated. The fixed C01-C05 bundle costs 19 and produces 20.65/87/174 in downside/base/upside, or 1.086842/4.578947/9.157895x. These are scenarios, not a probability-weighted forecast, net LP return or funded portfolio. Worked C01 conditional-thesis and C07 pass memo frames preserve contrary evidence and fictional review gates.</p><p>Changed-context task: C05 retention .60 to .50 lowers its base MOIC to 3.333333x and fails the formula gate. The same fixed five-ID bundle now produces base 85/19; do not erase the row or silently substitute a winner. The independent budget gives commitment 50 less fees 10 =40; initial earmark 20 less 19 leaves 1, but proposed follow-ons 21 exceed reserve 20 by1. Total proposed cash 40 fitting the envelope does not authorize transferring that initial 1 into reserves. Raising reserve to 21 without reducing the initial earmark makes total earmarks 41 exceed 40. Reserves are not called cash or follow-on rights; new ownership, cost and timing must be modeled before adding actual follow-ons.</p><p>Keep climate counterfactuals separate: annual units 10000 times lifecycle difference .80-.50 gives planned 3000 tCO2e/year; baseline .55 gives 500; baseline .45 gives minus 500, an increase rather than zero savings. Equal service, boundary, factor geography/year, adoption, leakage/rebound and attribution remain evidence gaps. These are not realized emissions, credits, corporate inventory deductions, revenue or investor-owned tonnes. Native LibreOffice passed 232 baseline, 234 changed-retention, 232 changed-ownership, 231 zero-retention, 234 reserve-transfer, 233 baseline-transfer, 232 zero-unit and 232 restored independent numeric checks, plus invalid-input and broken-reference cases. Saved XLSX has 252 cached formulas including 20 text outputs, zero error cells, no external links/macros. All 11 print pages and changed 320/390/1280 course states were inspected. Authored native execution is not independent learner transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, real diligence, emissions validation or capital authority. No company contact, investment, account or learner-progress action occurred.</p></section>\n\n<section aria-label=\"D78 D79 source cohort lab\"><h3>New sales are not the operating fleet</h3><p>Source-depth extension: read <a href=\"https://projectframe.how/preinvestment-ghg-assessment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Project Frame, Pre-Investment Considerations, Sections 2-4</a>. Potential impact models a technology class top-down from addressable/obtainable markets and adoption; planned impact tests a specific company's commercial forecast bottom-up. Company share and adoption curves are assumptions to defend, not a right to inherit the whole technology market. The source's illustrative 15-30% long-horizon share is not a default forecast for these invented companies. Check why the forecast was prepared, historical performance, production/financing capacity, customer mix and consistency with the carbon baseline.</p><p>Recurring operating effects need an active-cohort model; one-off production effects follow new units. New synthetic transfer, independent of the original single-year workbook: beginning-of-year deployments 100, 150, 200 in years 1-3, each serving the same function for three full years, no early retirement, no prior fleet. Active units are 100, 250, 450. Annual incumbent operating emissions are 0.50, 0.40, 0.30 tCO2e per active unit-year; solution operating emissions stay 0.20. Extra solution manufacturing versus the incumbent is 0.10 tCO2e per NEW unit, charged once on deployment. All other lifecycle differences are explicitly zero only for this toy case. These are authored inputs, not observed factors or a full LCA.</p><p>Annual operating avoided emissions are 30, 50, 45; extra manufacturing is 10, 15, 20. Net annual planned impact is 20, 35, 25 tCO2e, summing to 80 across this three-year window. Do not count future benefits after year 3 as if observed. Holding the incumbent baseline incorrectly at 0.50 would give 20, 60, 115, total 195, overstating this authored dynamic case by 115. Using new sales alone for recurring operations gives 20, 15, 0, total 35, missing surviving cohorts. Transfer: if lifetime is two years with retirement at the start of the third year, active units become 100, 250, 350; year-3 net is 15 and total 70. Independent cohort-by-cohort and aggregate-fleet exact-rational calculations reconcile. No financial discounting, equity return, carbon price, credit sale or offset is implied.</p><p>Attribution is a separate claim. This guidance edition does not recommend a quantitative horizontal value-chain allocation; it asks for a clear account of each contributor and acknowledgement of others. Its ownership-proportion vertical reporting illustration is debated and is not proof an investor caused that fraction, owns credits or can offset other emissions. Do not add the same full solution savings for supplier, manufacturer and shareholder. Additionality asks what would happen without the solution, including alternative low-emission options, regulation and rebound, and differs from an investor's value-add. The edition explicitly reports no final consensus on a consistent additionality method and excludes credit-certifier rules from that section. Do not adopt its historical country/vehicle examples, broad DAC assertions or 2023 consensus survey as current Indian facts or legal certification. The flux-capacitor illustration mixes T, MT and tons labels; do not reuse its numerical scale without unit reconciliation. Complete Sections 2-4 text and material fleet/forecast/attribution diagrams were inspected; the appendix and all cited papers were not reviewed here. This practitioner guidance and authored transfer do not establish actual company impact or learner transfer.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Build 20 explicitly invented company rows, preserve Indian climate/seed mapping intent and separate archetypes from real company facts.","Apply the fictional geography/stage/2-5 crore/10 percent ownership/4x return screens; no universal legal or market benchmark.","Explain C01-C05 conditional diligence, C06-C11 exclusions and C12-C20 evidence deferrals; include defer/no investment as genuine outcomes.","Mandatory provenance: label every company/input/scenario SYNTHETIC with units/date/basis; cite original teaching sources and unresolved real evidence.","Link entry ownership, dilution, exit equity, financing and total invested cost, not sector excitement or TAM directly to fund cash.","Separate climate counterfactual, planned/realized impact and inventory from investor financial returns, credits or automatic attribution.","Mandatory downside/reversal: test C01 threshold 166.666667/C07 threshold 250 and C05 retention decline; let missing evidence reverse the shortlist.","Keep conditional judgments, unverified claims and negative observations visible; do not invent buyer quotes, permissions or actual completed diligence.","Mandatory owner/gate: assign fictional day 0/30/60/90 evidence/financing/rights owners with stop/review conditions; no real capital release.","Deliver 20-row map, five conditional diligence names and C01/C07 private invest/pass-style memos with strongest objection and changed conclusion."]}},"79":{"objectives":["Reconcile ownership, future dilution, gross deal and bundle returns, fund reserves and counterfactual climate-impact sensitivity in the same explicitly synthetic twenty-company map."],"videos":[{"id":"Wi3PiZsIfBU","title":"Decision Analysis in Venture Capital","by":"Stanford Decisions and Ethics Center","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - new venture valuation (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Read exit valuation, ownership, option pools/follow-on dilution and scenario limitations. Historical discount-rate/tax examples are not current Indian tax rules or required return benchmarks. Use the explicit synthetic common-equity model and distinguish equity from enterprise value."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA - performance definitions, granular methodology (2025)","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Distinguish committed/unfunded/invested amounts, portfolio gross MOIC and fund net TVPI/IRR. Actual fund documents govern fees, reserves, calls and carry; the case is invented."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GHG Protocol - inventory and project accounting (2023)","url":"https://ghgprotocol.org/blog/inventory-and-project-accounting","note":"Entity inventory versus system-wide project impact relative to counterfactual baseline. Avoided-emissions estimates are reported separately, not subtracted from corporate scopes. Explanatory article, not full standards/legal or credit-certification review."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Project Frame - pre-investment GHG assessment, selected sections","url":"https://projectframe.how/preinvestment-ghg-assessment","note":"Read introduction and Section 1: comparable unit, significant lifecycle effects, emissions-factor geography, baseline and uncertainty. Potential top-down impact differs from company planned bottom-up impact. Practitioner consensus, not regulation, financial advice or proof of investor additionality. Extended reading: Sections 2-4 on technology versus company SOM, recurring fleet/one-off effects, commercial-forecast consistency, attribution and debated additionality. The 15-30% long-horizon share illustration is not a universal assumption. No full appendix or cited-literature review claimed."}],"exercise":"Private teaching case. All twenty company IDs, Indian operations, sectors, stages, valuation/cash inputs and observations below are invented, not a map of real current companies or real recommendations. Do not attach a real company name to these figures. A future real-company map needs each company's original dated sources and independently qualified gaps. No outreach, diligence access, investment or fund formation is requested.\nFictional fund mandate: India operations, seed stage, initial cheque 2-5 crore, initial fully diluted post-money ownership at least 10 percent, and a proposed 4x gross single-deal base-case screen over 7 years. These are chosen teaching preferences, not market norms, legal eligibility or a guaranteed fund return. Passing a return formula is insufficient: climate evidence, economics, financing, mandate and rights remain separate. Review, defer and exclude are private screening labels, not authority to invest. A five-name diligence shortlist is not five simultaneous funded commitments.\nTable basis: all monetary inputs crore rupees. Twelve pipe-separated columns: ID, sector, geography, stage, pre-money, initial cheque, r, exit-equity downside, exit-equity base, exit-equity upside, screen, gap. r is the fraction of initial ownership retained after future dilution with NO extra fund investment; it is not percentage-point dilution. All exits are hypothetical distributable equity to common after debt and transaction claims, before fund-level fees/carry/tax, not enterprise headline. Assume fully converted common pro-rata payouts, no preferences/options added beyond the stated ownership/dilution model, no interim distributions, same 7-year exit date. Missing facts can invalidate the simplification. C20 is deliberately flagged because the purported equity basis is not substantiated; its numerical outputs are conditional only, not accepted equity proceeds.\nC01|industrial heat|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|review|Metered operating-energy evidence and embodied-emissions boundary still need independent checking.\nC02|cold-chain efficiency|India|seed|15|3|.75|30|120|240|review|Comparable service-level and refrigerant/leakage evidence remain open.\nC03|battery materials reuse|India|seed|36|4|.75|60|240|480|review|Feedstock rights, recovered-product quality and process energy remain unverified.\nC04|low-emission construction material|India|seed|24|4|.70|40|180|360|review|Durability, functional equivalence, certification and scale-up cost remain open.\nC05|farm energy efficiency|India|seed|12|3|.60|20|100|200|review|Actual adoption, water/rebound effects and sensitivity to dilution remain open.\nC06|grid software|India|seed|495|5|.50|1000|5000|10000|exclude|Initial ownership 1 percent misses fictional 10 percent minimum despite a 5x base payoff.\nC07|EV components|India|seed|20|5|.40|50|200|400|exclude|Base gross multiple 3.2x misses fictional 4x screen after stated dilution.\nC08|renewable asset operator|India|growth|20|5|.60|50|200|400|exclude|Growth stage outside seed-only mandate; headline return cannot waive mandate.\nC09|cooling efficiency|outside India|seed|15|3|.75|30|120|240|exclude|Outside fictional geography mandate, not a claim of poor business quality.\nC10|hydrogen equipment|India|seed|32|8|.60|80|320|640|exclude|Initial ticket 8 exceeds fictional 5 crore cap; cannot silently resize the offered round.\nC11|circular packaging|India|seed|8|1|.60|20|100|200|exclude|Initial ticket 1 below fictional 2 crore minimum.\nC12|water monitoring|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Climate mitigation link not established; adaptation/service benefits are separate, not automatically zero.\nC13|soil carbon measurement|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Baseline, permanence, leakage and verification unavailable; do not treat estimated carbon as issued credits.\nC14|recycling logistics|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Same avoided-tonne claim appears in another value-chain record; aggregation/attribution not resolved.\nC15|biomass heat|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Competing feedstock use, supply contract and emissions boundary unresolved.\nC16|industrial efficiency sensors|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Letters of interest only, not paid deployment or verified energy savings.\nC17|carbon capture equipment|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Energy penalty, storage terms, permanence and full financing plan unresolved.\nC18|fleet charging|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Interconnection, utilization and cash-timing evidence missing, no assumed permit approval.\nC19|textile reuse|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Imported market-size total not converted into eligible paid units or firm-specific economics.\nC20|distributed solar controls|India|seed|20|5|.60|50|200|400|defer|Exit enterprise headline includes debt; supplied equity proceeds basis needs reconciliation before use.\nNumerical lab: derive every initial/exit ownership, scenario proceeds, gross MOIC and 4x exit threshold from the 20-row dataset. Reconcile why C06 can have a 5x base multiple yet fail 1 percent initial ownership, why C07 fails at 3.2x and why mandate/execution gaps survive high return projections. Compute the five-candidate bundle only under the explicit no-follow-on common-equity assumptions. Keep the independent fund-budget and climate lab separate from exit cash. Produce formulas, a source/input/change log, five conditional diligence names and two private memos C01/C07 that address the strongest objection. No automatic invest choice or actual capital allocation is requested.\nOwnership formulas: initial cheque/(pre+cheque); exit ownership=initial ownership*r; gross cash proceeds=exit ownership*exit equity; gross deal MOIC=proceeds/total fund cash invested. No-follow-on assumptions keep cost equal to initial cheque; follow-ons would change both numerator ownership and denominator cost. MOIC is not annual IRR, net LP return or probability of success. If there were only a day 0 outflow and one year 7 receipt, annualized return would be MOIC^(1/7)-1, not MOIC/7; other dated cash flows require a cash-flow IRR. Do not sum multiples or use one winner as proof the fund returns 4 x.\nFive conditional diligence IDs C01-C05: initial checks 5+3+4+4+3=19 crore. Initial ownership 20 percent,16.6667 percent,10 percent,14.2857 percent,20 percent; exit ownership 12 percent,12.5 percent,7.5 percent,10 percent,12 percent. Base proceeds 24,15,18,18,12 =87; invested 19 gives 87/19=4.578947x gross for this hypothetical bundle, not an LP net multiple or forecast. Downside proceeds 6+3.75+4.5+4+2.4=20.65 gives 1.086842 x, excluding other portfolio outcomes/fund costs. Upside proceeds 48+30+36+36+24=174 gives 9.157895 x. Scenarios are not probability-weighted expected returns. C05 is exactly 4x base, not safely above the bar; if retention falls from 0.60 to 0.50 at the same exit 100, exit ownership 10 percent and MOIC 10/3=3.333333 x. C01 needs exit equity 166.666667 for 4 x; C02 needs 96; C03 needs 213.333333; C04 needs 160; C05 needs 100. These thresholds do not establish an achievable buyer or exit value.\nIndependent fund-budget screen: lifetime commitment 50 includes 10 fees/expenses in the stated fictional terms, leaving 40 for investments. Earmark 20 initial and 20 follow-on reserve. The shortlist initial 19 leaves 1 initial capacity; a proposed future follow-on schedule 4+3+5+4+5=21 would exceed 20 reserves by 1. Planned reserves are not invested cash, callable cash already on hand, a legal follow-on right or permission to invest. A future follow-on model must recompute holdings, total invested cost, timing and remaining commitments; do not keep 87/19 while silently spending 21 more. Cash availability, fees/expenses and fund documents need their own checks. Any actual decision requires its own owner approval.\nClimate impact lab, separate from financial exit arithmetic: equal-service baseline emits 0.80 tCO2e per synthetic unit and solution 0.50 including the case's stated material/manufacturing/distribution/use/disposal boundary. A hypothetical 10000 units deployed in one year gives(0.80-0.50)*10000=3000 tCO2e/year planned avoided emissions. Not realized, not a verified carbon credit, not revenue, not uniquely attributable to the investor, and not a deduction from corporate Scope 1/2/3 inventory. Under a decarbonizing baseline 0.55, same solution/units gives 500; at baseline 0.45 it gives minus 500, an increase. Missing leakage/rebound or unequal service could change the result. Document factor geography/year, functional unit, significant effects, replacement/adoption and dynamic baseline; do not multiply a TAM by invented penetration and call it actual company impact. Do not double-count an enabling supplier and final product as independent full savings. Adaptation/other environmental benefits may be important but are not interchangeable with avoided tonnes or equity returns.\n\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D78 D79 climate screen\"><h3>Can the arithmetic pass while the evidence still says defer?</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d78-d79-climate-screen.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable climate/seed screen (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d78-d79-climate-screen.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read the eleven-page print view (PDF)</a>. Eleven sheets reproduce the existing 20 invented company IDs, evidence gaps, supplied case labels and common-equity/no-follow-on assumptions. Original cases and rubrics remain unchanged. This is a synthetic classroom model, not a current company map, legal screen, actual diligence or investment recommendation.</p><p>Trace initial cheque/(pre+cheque), retained exit ownership, three scenario proceeds, base gross MOIC, target exit and two-flow annualized return. Geography/stage/cheque/ownership/return gates remain separate from evidence: C06 can show 5x while failing 1% ownership; C07 gives 3.2x and needs conditional exit equity 250 for 4x. C12-C20 keep DEFER after passing numerical gates, with C20 equity basis still unsubstantiated. The fixed C01-C05 bundle costs 19 and produces 20.65/87/174 in downside/base/upside, or 1.086842/4.578947/9.157895x. These are scenarios, not a probability-weighted forecast, net LP return or funded portfolio. Worked C01 conditional-thesis and C07 pass memo frames preserve contrary evidence and fictional review gates.</p><p>Changed-context task: C05 retention .60 to .50 lowers its base MOIC to 3.333333x and fails the formula gate. The same fixed five-ID bundle now produces base 85/19; do not erase the row or silently substitute a winner. The independent budget gives commitment 50 less fees 10 =40; initial earmark 20 less 19 leaves 1, but proposed follow-ons 21 exceed reserve 20 by1. Total proposed cash 40 fitting the envelope does not authorize transferring that initial 1 into reserves. Raising reserve to 21 without reducing the initial earmark makes total earmarks 41 exceed 40. Reserves are not called cash or follow-on rights; new ownership, cost and timing must be modeled before adding actual follow-ons.</p><p>Keep climate counterfactuals separate: annual units 10000 times lifecycle difference .80-.50 gives planned 3000 tCO2e/year; baseline .55 gives 500; baseline .45 gives minus 500, an increase rather than zero savings. Equal service, boundary, factor geography/year, adoption, leakage/rebound and attribution remain evidence gaps. These are not realized emissions, credits, corporate inventory deductions, revenue or investor-owned tonnes. Native LibreOffice passed 232 baseline, 234 changed-retention, 232 changed-ownership, 231 zero-retention, 234 reserve-transfer, 233 baseline-transfer, 232 zero-unit and 232 restored independent numeric checks, plus invalid-input and broken-reference cases. Saved XLSX has 252 cached formulas including 20 text outputs, zero error cells, no external links/macros. All 11 print pages and changed 320/390/1280 course states were inspected. Authored native execution is not independent learner transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, real diligence, emissions validation or capital authority. No company contact, investment, account or learner-progress action occurred.</p></section>\n\n<section aria-label=\"D78 D79 source cohort lab\"><h3>New sales are not the operating fleet</h3><p>Source-depth extension: read <a href=\"https://projectframe.how/preinvestment-ghg-assessment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Project Frame, Pre-Investment Considerations, Sections 2-4</a>. Potential impact models a technology class top-down from addressable/obtainable markets and adoption; planned impact tests a specific company's commercial forecast bottom-up. Company share and adoption curves are assumptions to defend, not a right to inherit the whole technology market. The source's illustrative 15-30% long-horizon share is not a default forecast for these invented companies. Check why the forecast was prepared, historical performance, production/financing capacity, customer mix and consistency with the carbon baseline.</p><p>Recurring operating effects need an active-cohort model; one-off production effects follow new units. New synthetic transfer, independent of the original single-year workbook: beginning-of-year deployments 100, 150, 200 in years 1-3, each serving the same function for three full years, no early retirement, no prior fleet. Active units are 100, 250, 450. Annual incumbent operating emissions are 0.50, 0.40, 0.30 tCO2e per active unit-year; solution operating emissions stay 0.20. Extra solution manufacturing versus the incumbent is 0.10 tCO2e per NEW unit, charged once on deployment. All other lifecycle differences are explicitly zero only for this toy case. These are authored inputs, not observed factors or a full LCA.</p><p>Annual operating avoided emissions are 30, 50, 45; extra manufacturing is 10, 15, 20. Net annual planned impact is 20, 35, 25 tCO2e, summing to 80 across this three-year window. Do not count future benefits after year 3 as if observed. Holding the incumbent baseline incorrectly at 0.50 would give 20, 60, 115, total 195, overstating this authored dynamic case by 115. Using new sales alone for recurring operations gives 20, 15, 0, total 35, missing surviving cohorts. Transfer: if lifetime is two years with retirement at the start of the third year, active units become 100, 250, 350; year-3 net is 15 and total 70. Independent cohort-by-cohort and aggregate-fleet exact-rational calculations reconcile. No financial discounting, equity return, carbon price, credit sale or offset is implied.</p><p>Attribution is a separate claim. This guidance edition does not recommend a quantitative horizontal value-chain allocation; it asks for a clear account of each contributor and acknowledgement of others. Its ownership-proportion vertical reporting illustration is debated and is not proof an investor caused that fraction, owns credits or can offset other emissions. Do not add the same full solution savings for supplier, manufacturer and shareholder. Additionality asks what would happen without the solution, including alternative low-emission options, regulation and rebound, and differs from an investor's value-add. The edition explicitly reports no final consensus on a consistent additionality method and excludes credit-certifier rules from that section. Do not adopt its historical country/vehicle examples, broad DAC assertions or 2023 consensus survey as current Indian facts or legal certification. The flux-capacitor illustration mixes T, MT and tons labels; do not reuse its numerical scale without unit reconciliation. Complete Sections 2-4 text and material fleet/forecast/attribution diagrams were inspected; the appendix and all cited papers were not reviewed here. This practitioner guidance and authored transfer do not establish actual company impact or learner transfer.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Reproduce 20 synthetic rows and the common 7-year, fully converted/no-follow-on, net-equity-proceeds assumptions.","Compute entry cheque/(pre+cheque), exit ownership times retention, proceeds and gross MOIC with total invested cash denominator.","Reconcile 87/19 base,20.65/19 downside and 174/19 upside for C01-C05; not sum multiples or infer net LP/annual return.","Mandatory provenance: trace every fictional input, time/unit, retained-ownership fraction, debt-equity basis and formula to the model/source log.","Reconcile 50 commitment less 10 fees=40 investable,20 initial/20 reserves;19 initial leaves 1,21 future follow-ons exceeds reserve by 1.","Compute 3000/500/minus 500 annual planned avoided tCO2e at baselines 0.80/0.55/0.45; no inventory netting, credit issuance or guaranteed investor attribution.","Mandatory downside/reversal: calculate 4x exit thresholds and C05 retention 0.50=>3.333333 x; stress cash/rights/baseline and revise affected decisions.","Distinguish MOIC, dated IRR, unrealized assumptions, planned climate impact and company evidence; no inherited return formula after adding follow-ons.","Mandatory owner/gate: name fictional financing/climate/valuation reviewers and day 0/30/60/90 checks; no investment, borrowing or outreach approval.","Deliver formulas/map/five conditional candidates/two private C01/C07 memos, strongest objection and evidence that changes the recommendation."]}},"80":{"objectives":["Revise a fictional strategy after material counterevidence, separate factual/economic/permission objections from presentation issues and turn a private premortem into conditional action and review gates."],"videos":[{"id":"CEAR5Zsld6g","title":"Re-Broadcast - Using PreMortem Analysis to Make Better Decisions with Gary A. Klein","by":"Red Team Thinking","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Red Team Thinking - culture of candor and premortem (2021)","url":"https://www.redteamthinking.com/blog/creating-a-culture-of-candor","note":"Publisher written explanation: imagined failure, independent reasons, then discussing pathways before execution. Aids dissent and inquiry, not a guarantee of success, a probability estimate or evidence the failure occurred."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Gary Klein - The Pro-Mortem Method (2015)","url":"https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/seeing-what-others-dont/201510/the-pro-mortem-method","note":"Author distinguishes imagined failure from observable success outcomes; useful for evidence/signposts. Practitioner discussion, not a measured performance guarantee for this product."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Red Team Thinking - rebroadcast episode (2023)","url":"https://www.redteamthinking.com/podcasts/the-thinking-leader/episodes/2147961637","note":"Use the written explanation and fictional workbook for the specific financial/counterevidence lab."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - new venture valuation/FCF (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Read explicit cash flow/scenario reasoning and working-capital/capex bridge. Historical tax/rate examples are not current Indian rules; the case states its own exclusions and accounting basis."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - analyse research","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/analyse-a-research-session","note":"Separate raw observations from findings/actions and preserve material contrary evidence. UK practice guidance, not Indian legal compliance or proof of actual participant permission."}],"exercise":"Private fictional strategy review. The founder, participants, observations, artifacts and roles below are invented. No real interview, sponsor approval, financial commitment or access to customer data is requested. The question is whether to continue A, switch to B, redesign/stage a pilot or defer; a persuasive presentation is not the objective. A premortem assumes failure for a thought experiment to generate plausible pathways, not evidence failure already happened or an estimate of its probability. Invite independent written concerns before discussion in the imagined workshop; in this individual exercise write separate role perspectives without inventing real quotations. Do not punish dissent or call every factual objection a communication problem.\nCommon 12-week operating case, lakh rupees, all variable and avoidable fixed costs paid within the horizon; no opening working capital, inventory, other payables, taxes, debt or financing. A: recognized revenue 8, complete stated variable costs 4.8, avoidable fixed 1, collections 5.6, capex 0.8. Operating remainder 2.2; receivables 2.4; endpoint project cash 5.6-4.8-1-0.8=minus 1, also 2.2-2.4-0.8. B: revenue 10, variable 6, fixed 2, collections 7, capex 1.5. Operating remainder 2; receivables 3; endpoint cash minus 2.5. Neither operating remainder is project cash; B's larger revenue is not higher operating remainder. Hypothetical gates: operating remainder at least 2, peak project cash draw at most 3 and at least 90 percent on-time among eligible deliveries. These thresholds are invented owner preferences, not universal benchmarks or approved budgets. If the eligible delivery denominator is zero, report insufficient evidence, not 100 percent.\nDated A cash schedule, zero opening project cash: day 0 capex 0.8; day 14 variable 1.6; day 28 variable 1.6 plus fixed 0.5; day 42 collections 2.8; day 56 variable 1.6 plus fixed 0.5; day 84 collections 2.8. Cumulative cash at those checkpoints: minus 0.8, minus 2.4, minus 4.5, minus 1.7, minus 3.8, minus 1.0. With only these dated events, peak draw 4.5 occurs day 28, so A breaches the 3 limit even though its endpoint deficit is only 1 and operating remainder 2.2. B has no dated schedule: its endpoint deficit 2.5 does not prove peak draw below 3. Fictional A deliveries 20, on-time 18 gives 90 percent observed on this small invented set, not proven future reliability or permission to ignore other quality defects.\nContradictory evidence packet: O1 the initial slide calls 20 invited prospects paying customers; synthetic ledger has only 4 completed paid tests, so correct claim and denominator before arguing. O2 receipts arrive after costs in the dated schedule; recalculate the draw instead of promising that profit funds day 0. O3 a supplier cost estimate omits 1 lakh incremental rework, paid day 56, associated with the same delivered output; add it rather than dismiss as a messaging issue. O4 a founder says customers liked the prototype, but no repeat-use/payment or buying-authority evidence is supplied. O5 the planned research would use recordings without a verified permission/allowed-use record; this remains an affected-use block even if economic gates pass. O6 B has larger revenue but its operating remainder is not higher and its peak funding/quality are unknown. O7 a team role says the slide is unreadable; presentation can be fixed, but the financial/evidence objections remain. O8 positive operating numbers do not grant actual spend, contract or participant permissions. All O1-O8 are invented observations to classify, not verified customer/vendor facts.\nIf O3 is accepted, revised A variable cost 5.8 yields operating 1.2, unchanged receivables 2.4 and endpoint cash minus 2.0. Revised day 56 cash minus 4.8 becomes peak draw 4.8, worse than original 4.5. Cash draws are deficits, not negative receivables or total project costs. With 40 percent contribution before this extra 1 fixed rework burden, meeting operating target 2 at variable 60 percent, fixed 1 and extra 1 would need recognized revenue 10, not 8; this is a conditional algebraic requirement, not a forecast that customers buy 10 or collections arrive on time. If rework scales with volume, change that model and recompute. Do not fix a failed funding gate by shifting unapproved supplier obligations or assuming new financing.\nDecision-review task: build an eight-row objection log with original claim, type of objection, synthetic artifact/date/unit, independent test, affected model or permission, fictional owner, due point and action. Separate direct observations from interpretations and forecasts. Preserve the old recommendation, display the changed evidence and explain why A cannot pass the original gates, why B is not automatically the winner, and why redesign/defer remains valid. For the premortem, privately assume the 12-week plan failed, list independent plausible pathways (late cash, cost/rework, demand, quality, rights), then identify observable signposts and a specific mitigation/test for each. A plausible story is not a measured likelihood; do not sum overlapping risks into a fake failure probability. Seek alternative explanations and evidence that would disconfirm the critique as well as the thesis. Deliver the objection log, revised conditional recommendation, strongest unresolved objection and fictional day 0/30/60/90 stop/review owners. No real founder contact, secret recording, data export or capital release is required.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D80 D81 revision lab\"><h3>Would the objection change your recommendation?</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d80-d81-revision-lab.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable objection/revision lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d80-d81-revision-lab.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read the seven-page print view (PDF)</a>. Seven sheets retain the original fictional D80-D81 numbers, O1-O8 contradictions and separate operating, timing, quality and permission tests. Original cases and rubrics remain unchanged. All roles, artifacts and observations are invented; no founder contact, recording, data use, spending or approval requested.</p><p>Reconcile original A operating 2.2, receivables 2.4 and endpoint cash minus 1; B operating 2, receivables 3 and endpoint minus 2.5. The six-event A ledger gives cash minus .8, minus 2.4, minus 4.5, minus 1.7, minus 3.8, minus 1. The original peak draw 4.5 exceeds the proposed limit 3 despite endpoint deficit 1. Accepting O3 adds extra rework 1 once at day 56, not already in original variable costs: revised operating 1.2, endpoint minus 2 and peak draw 4.8. Both original and revised A fail the funding screen; revised A also fails the operating target 2. B has no dated peak/quality evidence and retains UNKNOWN rather than becoming an automatic winner.</p><p>Changed-context task: move the same rework payment from day 56 to day 14 without shifting any other obligation. Revised peak draw becomes 5.5 while endpoint stays minus 2. Test rework 0, 2 and .5, then restore 1/day 56. Cash ledger totals must reconcile with the financial bridge before a funding-gate conclusion appears. Zero eligible deliveries or invitees leaves the relevant fraction undefined, not 100 percent. Preserve denominators: 18/20 on-time is 90 percent in this small invented set; 4/20 paid tests/invitees is 20 percent, not population demand or completed-interview conversion. Revenue 10 meets operating target 2 only under constant 60 percent original variable costs and fixed extra rework 1, not guaranteed sales or feasible funding.</p><p>Use the eight-row objection log to preserve original claims, synthetic contrary artifacts, proposed independent tests/alternative explanations, changed conclusions and fictional day 0/30/60/90 owners. Do not mark proposed tests as performed research, role-played sign-off as real permission or imagined premortem failure as a measured probability. Fixing O7's slide cannot erase O1-O6/O8. Revised recommendation frame: A cannot continue under original gates; B is incomplete; redesign/defer requires a new coherent cost, collection, capacity, quality and rights case. Strongest unresolved demand/rights evidence stays visible. No capital release or real permission follows a formula pass.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice passed 75 independent numeric comparisons at baseline, 76 for earlier rework, 75 each for zero/2/.5 rework, 69 zero-quality-denominator, 74 zero-invitee-denominator and 75 restored comparisons. Ten invalid financial/count/day/target inputs, three invalid ledger entries, an unreconciled ledger, zero-revenue target and broken running-cash reference were tested. Saved XLSX has 75 cached formulas, zero errors and no external links/macros; all seven print pages and changed 320/390/1280 course states inspected. This closes bounded authored native execution, not independent learner transfer, full AV/source reading, Excel/Sheets parity, real research, actual funding or overall 10/10. Record your own attempts and delayed transfer separately.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Name the fictional thesis, private audience, same 12-week window and continue/switch/redesign/defer options without contacting a real founder.","Preserve original claim and revised conclusion; explain a causal failure pathway without treating a premortem as observed failure or probability.","Classify O1-O8 as demand, timing, cost, permission, quality or presentation; do not use clearer slides to dismiss factual contradictions.","Mandatory provenance: flag invented roles/artifacts/observations, original source/date/units, missing evidence and unverified claims.","Reconcile A 2.2 operating/2.4 receivables/1 cashdeficit versus B 2/3/2.5; distinguish endpoint from dated peak cash.","Generate independent premortem reasons, plausible alternative explanations and observable disconfirmation/signposts rather than pressure for agreement.","Mandatory downside/reversal: show A 4.5 peak draw breaching 3 and rework causing 1.2 operating/4.8 peak draw; revise rather than defend a failed gate.","Retain material dissent and recognize B cash/quality/rights gaps; role-played approval is not real permission or an independent review.","Mandatory owner/gate: assign fictional day 0/30/60/90 evidence/cash/quality/rights owners, stop/review conditions and no actual capital release.","Deliver eight-row objection/change log, revised recommendation and strongest unresolved objection with what would change it."]}},"81":{"objectives":["Recompute a fictional strategy after omitted rework and dated cash evidence, using an objection/change log that can reverse the recommendation rather than treating dissent as a communication defect."],"videos":[{"id":"CEAR5Zsld6g","title":"Re-Broadcast - Using PreMortem Analysis to Make Better Decisions with Gary A. Klein","by":"Red Team Thinking","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Red Team Thinking - culture of candor and premortem (2021)","url":"https://www.redteamthinking.com/blog/creating-a-culture-of-candor","note":"Publisher written explanation: imagined failure, independent reasons, then discussing pathways before execution. Aids dissent and inquiry, not a guarantee of success, a probability estimate or evidence the failure occurred."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Gary Klein - The Pro-Mortem Method (2015)","url":"https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/seeing-what-others-dont/201510/the-pro-mortem-method","note":"Author distinguishes imagined failure from observable success outcomes; useful for evidence/signposts. Practitioner discussion, not a measured performance guarantee for this product."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Red Team Thinking - rebroadcast episode (2023)","url":"https://www.redteamthinking.com/podcasts/the-thinking-leader/episodes/2147961637","note":"Use the written explanation and fictional workbook for the specific financial/counterevidence lab."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - new venture valuation/FCF (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Read explicit cash flow/scenario reasoning and working-capital/capex bridge. Historical tax/rate examples are not current Indian rules; the case states its own exclusions and accounting basis."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - analyse research","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/analyse-a-research-session","note":"Separate raw observations from findings/actions and preserve material contrary evidence. UK practice guidance, not Indian legal compliance or proof of actual participant permission."}],"exercise":"Private fictional strategy review. The founder, participants, observations, artifacts and roles below are invented. No real interview, sponsor approval, financial commitment or access to customer data is requested. The question is whether to continue A, switch to B, redesign/stage a pilot or defer; a persuasive presentation is not the objective. A premortem assumes failure for a thought experiment to generate plausible pathways, not evidence failure already happened or an estimate of its probability. Invite independent written concerns before discussion in the imagined workshop; in this individual exercise write separate role perspectives without inventing real quotations. Do not punish dissent or call every factual objection a communication problem.\nCommon 12-week operating case, lakh rupees, all variable and avoidable fixed costs paid within the horizon; no opening working capital, inventory, other payables, taxes, debt or financing. A: recognized revenue 8, complete stated variable costs 4.8, avoidable fixed 1, collections 5.6, capex 0.8. Operating remainder 2.2; receivables 2.4; endpoint project cash 5.6-4.8-1-0.8=minus 1, also 2.2-2.4-0.8. B: revenue 10, variable 6, fixed 2, collections 7, capex 1.5. Operating remainder 2; receivables 3; endpoint cash minus 2.5. Neither operating remainder is project cash; B's larger revenue is not higher operating remainder. Hypothetical gates: operating remainder at least 2, peak project cash draw at most 3 and at least 90 percent on-time among eligible deliveries. These thresholds are invented owner preferences, not universal benchmarks or approved budgets. If the eligible delivery denominator is zero, report insufficient evidence, not 100 percent.\nDated A cash schedule, zero opening project cash: day 0 capex 0.8; day 14 variable 1.6; day 28 variable 1.6 plus fixed 0.5; day 42 collections 2.8; day 56 variable 1.6 plus fixed 0.5; day 84 collections 2.8. Cumulative cash at those checkpoints: minus 0.8, minus 2.4, minus 4.5, minus 1.7, minus 3.8, minus 1.0. With only these dated events, peak draw 4.5 occurs day 28, so A breaches the 3 limit even though its endpoint deficit is only 1 and operating remainder 2.2. B has no dated schedule: its endpoint deficit 2.5 does not prove peak draw below 3. Fictional A deliveries 20, on-time 18 gives 90 percent observed on this small invented set, not proven future reliability or permission to ignore other quality defects.\nContradictory evidence packet: O1 the initial slide calls 20 invited prospects paying customers; synthetic ledger has only 4 completed paid tests, so correct claim and denominator before arguing. O2 receipts arrive after costs in the dated schedule; recalculate the draw instead of promising that profit funds day 0. O3 a supplier cost estimate omits 1 lakh incremental rework, paid day 56, associated with the same delivered output; add it rather than dismiss as a messaging issue. O4 a founder says customers liked the prototype, but no repeat-use/payment or buying-authority evidence is supplied. O5 the planned research would use recordings without a verified permission/allowed-use record; this remains an affected-use block even if economic gates pass. O6 B has larger revenue but its operating remainder is not higher and its peak funding/quality are unknown. O7 a team role says the slide is unreadable; presentation can be fixed, but the financial/evidence objections remain. O8 positive operating numbers do not grant actual spend, contract or participant permissions. All O1-O8 are invented observations to classify, not verified customer/vendor facts.\nIf O3 is accepted, revised A variable cost 5.8 yields operating 1.2, unchanged receivables 2.4 and endpoint cash minus 2.0. Revised day 56 cash minus 4.8 becomes peak draw 4.8, worse than original 4.5. Cash draws are deficits, not negative receivables or total project costs. With 40 percent contribution before this extra 1 fixed rework burden, meeting operating target 2 at variable 60 percent, fixed 1 and extra 1 would need recognized revenue 10, not 8; this is a conditional algebraic requirement, not a forecast that customers buy 10 or collections arrive on time. If rework scales with volume, change that model and recompute. Do not fix a failed funding gate by shifting unapproved supplier obligations or assuming new financing.\nNumerical revision lab: reconcile both initial A/B operating-receivable-end cash bridges. Build the dated A event ledger and running cash; compute original 4.5 peak draw versus 1 endpoint deficit. Add O3's 1 rework at day 56 and show revised 1.2 operating,2 endpoint deficit,4.8 peak draw. Verify zero/negative eligible denominator handling,18/20=90 percent and the 4/20=20 percent paid-test/invitation fraction, not a purchase conversion for completed interviews or population demand. Derive revenue 10 needed for the 2 operating target under the explicitly fixed-extra-rework model, without claiming the sales or cash timing is feasible. B lacks dated cash/quality evidence and cannot be approved from its 2.5 endpoint draw. Deliver formulas, eight-row evidence/objection/change log, an alternative-explanation test, and revised continue/redesign/defer choice with actual missing evidence made visible. A hypothetical staged alternative needs its own cost/collection/quality model before passing any gate. Name fictional day 0/30/60/90 review owners; no external approval, spending or contact.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D80 D81 revision lab\"><h3>Would the objection change your recommendation?</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d80-d81-revision-lab.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable objection/revision lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d80-d81-revision-lab.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read the seven-page print view (PDF)</a>. Seven sheets retain the original fictional D80-D81 numbers, O1-O8 contradictions and separate operating, timing, quality and permission tests. Original cases and rubrics remain unchanged. All roles, artifacts and observations are invented; no founder contact, recording, data use, spending or approval requested.</p><p>Reconcile original A operating 2.2, receivables 2.4 and endpoint cash minus 1; B operating 2, receivables 3 and endpoint minus 2.5. The six-event A ledger gives cash minus .8, minus 2.4, minus 4.5, minus 1.7, minus 3.8, minus 1. The original peak draw 4.5 exceeds the proposed limit 3 despite endpoint deficit 1. Accepting O3 adds extra rework 1 once at day 56, not already in original variable costs: revised operating 1.2, endpoint minus 2 and peak draw 4.8. Both original and revised A fail the funding screen; revised A also fails the operating target 2. B has no dated peak/quality evidence and retains UNKNOWN rather than becoming an automatic winner.</p><p>Changed-context task: move the same rework payment from day 56 to day 14 without shifting any other obligation. Revised peak draw becomes 5.5 while endpoint stays minus 2. Test rework 0, 2 and .5, then restore 1/day 56. Cash ledger totals must reconcile with the financial bridge before a funding-gate conclusion appears. Zero eligible deliveries or invitees leaves the relevant fraction undefined, not 100 percent. Preserve denominators: 18/20 on-time is 90 percent in this small invented set; 4/20 paid tests/invitees is 20 percent, not population demand or completed-interview conversion. Revenue 10 meets operating target 2 only under constant 60 percent original variable costs and fixed extra rework 1, not guaranteed sales or feasible funding.</p><p>Use the eight-row objection log to preserve original claims, synthetic contrary artifacts, proposed independent tests/alternative explanations, changed conclusions and fictional day 0/30/60/90 owners. Do not mark proposed tests as performed research, role-played sign-off as real permission or imagined premortem failure as a measured probability. Fixing O7's slide cannot erase O1-O6/O8. Revised recommendation frame: A cannot continue under original gates; B is incomplete; redesign/defer requires a new coherent cost, collection, capacity, quality and rights case. Strongest unresolved demand/rights evidence stays visible. No capital release or real permission follows a formula pass.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice passed 75 independent numeric comparisons at baseline, 76 for earlier rework, 75 each for zero/2/.5 rework, 69 zero-quality-denominator, 74 zero-invitee-denominator and 75 restored comparisons. Ten invalid financial/count/day/target inputs, three invalid ledger entries, an unreconciled ledger, zero-revenue target and broken running-cash reference were tested. Saved XLSX has 75 cached formulas, zero errors and no external links/macros; all seven print pages and changed 320/390/1280 course states inspected. This closes bounded authored native execution, not independent learner transfer, full AV/source reading, Excel/Sheets parity, real research, actual funding or overall 10/10. Record your own attempts and delayed transfer separately.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["State private synthetic scenario, common 12-week/zero opening working capital basis and separate operating profit, receivables, capex and cash.","Compute A 2.2/2.4/minus 1 and B 2/3/minus 2.5 initial bridges, not choose higher revenue as higher profit.","Construct six-event A running cash: minus 0.8/minus 2.4/minus 4.5/minus 1.7/minus 3.8/minus 1; original peak 4.5.","Mandatory provenance: preserve original numbers, omitted 1 rework artifact/date and revision trail; all company/participant facts remain synthetic.","Add day 56 rework 1 to show operating 1.2/end cash minus 2/peak draw 4.8; do not double-count rework or treat AR as cash.","Compute 18/20 on-time and 4/20 paid tests/invitees with named denominators; revenue 10 for target 2 under fixed-extra-rework assumptions, not guaranteed demand.","Mandatory downside/reversal: compare 3 draw/2 operating/90 percent quality gates; A failed and B unknown peak/quality cannot mean automatic switch.","Test competing explanations, uncertainty and changed-volume costs; do not assign an invented measured failure probability from a premortem.","Mandatory owner/gate: name fictional day 0/30/60/90 cash/cost/demand/rights reviewers and hold affected actions until evidence and real authority exist.","Deliver formulas, eight-row objection/change log, revised recommendation and strongest unresolved objection; redesign needs its own complete case."]}},"82":{"objectives":["Defend a private fictional SME growth recommendation using baseline-versus-incremental economics, dated company cash, quality evidence and conditional 90-day owners, rather than polished slides or simulated board approval."],"videos":[{"id":"h5qWn0SfTXA","title":"What the Best Pitch Decks Have in Common with Mike Vernal (Sequoia Capital)","by":"NFX","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - preparing a board deck","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/preparing-a-board-deck","note":"Read decision-focused calibration, actual-versus-plan metrics/challenges, working discussion and preparation using operating reports. A practitioner template, not Indian legal governance requirements, a fixed meeting length or authority to circulate board materials."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - writing a business plan","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"Clear purpose/problem/solution/market/alternatives/business model. Fundraising structure is distinct from a board decision, cash forecast or verified evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NFX - how VCs judge a startup (2020)","url":"https://www.nfx.com/post/how-vcs-judge-your-startup-on-zoom","note":"Historical publisher interview article on clarity, problem/solution/results, metric relevance and conversation. Multiple investor preferences differ; warm-introduction-only or meeting-timing advice is not a universal rule. Not a verbatim transcript of the assigned short clip."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - valuation/FCF (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Scenario and explicit cash/working-capital/capex reasoning; historical rate/tax examples are not current Indian rules. Workbook states its own simplified accounting and timing basis."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Indian SME board-style rehearsal, not a real board decision, legal governance checklist or sponsor signoff. All companies, roles, inputs, delivery records and objections are invented. Choose continue the existing plan, adopt the growth plan, redesign/stage it or defer. A polished deck cannot replace evidence, funding, participant permissions, actual governance requirements or owner authority. Do not contact board members, circulate confidential files, spend or arrange financing.\nCommon 90-day basis, lakh rupees. Existing-plan recognized revenue 30, complete stated variable costs 18, avoidable operating fixed 9, collections 27 and no new capex gives operating remainder 3, end receivables 3 and cash movement 0. Growth totals, not increments: revenue 42, variable 25.2, fixed 11, collections 33.6, new capex 3. Total operating remainder 5.8, receivables 8.4 and cash movement minus 5.6. Incremental growth versus existing: revenue 12, variable 7.2, fixed 2, operating 2.8, receivables 5.4 and capex 3; incremental cash 2.8-5.4-3=minus 5.6. Growth's 5.8 total operating is not 5.8 incremental value, and its revenue 42 is not 42 new revenue. Historical sunk spending is excluded from this forward comparison, but all case-specific future costs and obligations must be included. Operating remainder is before depreciation/interest/tax and is not net income. No opening receivables/inventory/payables, financing or taxes are assumed; all stated operating costs paid within the horizon. Any different real accounting basis needs a new bridge.\nOpening unrestricted company cash 8, no confirmed financing, proposed minimum company cash 2. Baseline cash stays 8 under its simplified balanced flows. Growth events: day 0 capex 3; day 30 collect 8/pay variable 8/pay fixed 3.5; day 60 collect 11/pay variable 8.4/pay fixed 3.5; day 90 collect 14.6/pay variable 8.8/pay fixed 4. Opening 8 becomes 5,1.5,0.6,2.4 at those checkpoints. With only these simultaneous dated net events, minimum 0.6 breaches 2 floor, even though end cash 2.4 passes. Maximum company cash reduction from opening is 7.4, not the endpoint 5.6. Intra-period ordering, deposits, debt covenants and unlisted obligations are not established; the aggregate schedule is not proof an actual firm can pay every bill. No financing is created by the expected operating benefit.\nProposed review gates: incremental operating remainder at least 2.5 over 90 days, company cash never below 2 and on-time eligible deliveries at least 95 percent. Gates are fictional owner preferences, not market or legal standards. Existing deliveries 19/20 on-time 95 percent; growth 27/30=90 percent, below the target and based on a small invented set, not a causal forecast or population reliability estimate. If no eligible deliveries exist, report insufficient evidence. Growth passes the 2.5 operating increment but fails stated cash and quality screens; do not recommend automatic rollout or claim stronger demand from larger totals alone.\nDownside growth: revenue 36, variable 21.6 at 60 percent, same fixed 11/capex 3, collections 25.2. Total operating 3.4, incremental versus baseline 0.4, receivables 10.8, project cash movement minus 10.4 and company end cash minus 2.4. No dated downside schedule is supplied, so do not invent its exact peak draw. With 60 percent variable cost/fixed 11, total operating breakeven revenue 27.5. To match existing operating 3 requires revenue 35; to deliver incremental 2.5 requires growth operating 5.5 and revenue 41.25. The 41.25 threshold is not a sales forecast, funding plan or quality guarantee. A staged redesign needs its own capacity, cost, price, collection and quality model; do not cut capex or shift payables without a supported changed plan.\nBoard-defence task: prepare a concise eight-slide rehearsal packet, not a claim that all boards require eight slides:1 decision/alternatives/private audience,2 existing baseline and changed inputs,3 incremental operating bridge,4 receivables/capex/dated company cash,5 quality/sample denominators,6 downside/reversal thresholds,7 recommendation/strongest objection,8 action register/missing approval. Put source/input IDs, units, date/window and synthetic flags beside claims. Keep calculations and conflicting evidence in an appendix rather than omit them to shorten the story. Distinguish founder fundraising narrative from board decision/accountability; the verified description of the short assigned pitch clip is not evidence for this cash model, permissions or governance.\nPrivately rehearse a timed defence. Choose and state a practice limit such as 6 minutes; use an actual timer and record duration when performed. The curriculum has not measured your time and a target is not completion. Leave a separate challenge round: answer why 5.8 is not incremental, why ending cash 2.4 does not clear the floor, what 27/30 means, and what evidence makes redesign/defer preferable. Log unknowns and commitments in an action register: fictional role, evidence/artifact, decision dependency, day 0/30/60/90 checkpoint, stop/continue criterion and needed real authority. The 90-day execution register is separate from 90 percent watched self-check unlocking. Deliver the deck/rehearsal notes/action register, strongest objection and whether the recommendation changed. No actual board resolution, distribution or investment is required.\n<section aria-label=\"Native D82 D83 board lab\"><h3>Defend the growth choice without hiding cash or quality</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d82-d83-board-lab.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Editable seven-sheet board lab (XLSX)</a> | <a href=\"/practice/top1-d82-d83-board-lab.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">seven-page print view (PDF)</a> | <a href=\"/practice/top1-d82-d83-board-practice.pptx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">editable eight-slide practice deck (PPTX)</a> | <a href=\"/practice/top1-d82-d83-board-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">deck print view (PDF)</a>. The original fictional D82-D83 case and rubric remain unchanged. This is solo synthetic practice, not an actual board decision, company disclosure, permission or completed timed defence.</p><p>One 90-day basis, lakh INR: existing revenue 30, variable 18, fixed 9, collections 27, capex 0 gives operating 3, receivables 3, cash movement 0. Growth totals 42, 25.2, 11, 33.6 and 3 give operating 5.8, receivables 8.4, cash movement minus 5.6. The increment is only 2.8 operating, less extra receivables 5.4 and capex 3. Operating remainder is before depreciation, interest and tax, not net income. This case assumes zero opening working capital, inventory, payables, taxes and financing; stated operating costs are paid within the horizon.</p><p>Opening company cash 8 becomes 5 at day 0, 1.5 at day 30, .6 at day 60 and 2.4 at day 90. Minimum .6 breaches proposed floor 2; maximum reduction 7.4 is not endpoint movement minus 5.6. Dated ledger totals must reconcile with growth inputs before the gate appears. Transfer: opening 10 makes minimum 2.6, yet quality still fails. Restore 8, then shift collections 2 from day 90 to day 30 while preserving total 33.6: minimum and ending cash 2.4, yet quality still fails. These are hypothetical changes, not authorized financing or negotiated receipts. Only simultaneous net events on specified dates are modeled; intraday ordering and unlisted obligations remain unknown.</p><p>Quality is separate: existing 19/20 is 95 percent; growth 27/30 is 90 percent and fails proposed 95 percent. Zero eligible deliveries is undefined, not 100 percent. Raising growth on-time count to 29/30 clears that sample screen, not the failed original cash floor or population-quality proof. Downside revenue 36, costs 21.6/11, collections 25.2 and capex 3 gives operating 3.4, incremental .4, receivables 10.8, movement minus 10.4 and ending cash minus 2.4. Its dates are absent: dated downside peak remains UNKNOWN. Conditional sales thresholds 27.5/35/41.25 produce zero operating/existing operating 3/incremental target 2.5 only at 60 percent variable cost and fixed 11; algebra is not a demand or funding plan.</p><p>Eight editable slides retain counterfactual, baseline/growth inputs, operating-to-cash bridge, native dated cash chart, quality denominators, downside/thresholds, strongest objection and fictional 0/30/60/90 evidence/cash/quality/rights owners. Recommendation: do not adopt the full growth plan under supplied gates; preserve existing as counterfactual and redesign/stage/defer until a complete funded, quality-tested case and actual authority exist. Do not hide the objection that growth earns 2.8 more operating and ends with 2.4 cash. Explain the earlier floor breach and failed quality. Choose a real solo timer limit, record actual elapsed time only when performed, retain objections and log changed reasoning. No timer, spoken defence, learner/panel response or recording review was performed here.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice passed 89 exact-rational baseline comparisons, 90 opening-cash, 90 earlier-collections, 85 zero-denominator, 91 quality-only and 89 restored comparisons. Eight invalid inputs, three invalid ledger events, unreconciled totals, broken references and zero revenue/contribution were tested. Saved workbook has 89 cached formulas, zero errors, no external links/macros. Seven print pages and all eight saved-deck PDF slides inspected; slide 4 callout separation fixed. Editable chart/table/text structure and eight notes checked. Changed 320/390/1280 course states inspected. Bounded authored execution is not Excel/Sheets parity, independent delayed learner transfer, actual timed defence, full AV/source coverage, real rights/funding or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Name fictional private audience, growth decision, common 90-day baseline and continue/adopt/redesign/defer choices; no real board approval.","Define measured/assumed/unknown inputs and distinguish 8-slide practice format from universal meeting requirements.","Show existing baseline, growth totals and incremental 2.8 operating/5.4 receivables/minus 5.6 cash, not a larger revenue story.","Mandatory provenance: tag all fictional inputs/artifacts, original teaching sources, dates, units and definitions; retain conflicting evidence.","Trace project movement into company cash from opening 8; show minimum 0.6 versus end 2.4 and unverified intraperiod obligations.","Defend quality 19/20 vs 27/30, target 95 percent and unknown causal/population claims; no denominator means insufficient evidence.","Mandatory downside/reversal: test 0.4 downside increment/minus 2.4 end company cash and 41.25 target revenue; cash/quality failures can change recommendation.","Record strongest material objection, unresolved data/authority and actual measured rehearsal time only if performed, not polished slides as proof.","Mandatory owner/gate: fictional day 0/30/60/90 evidence/cash/quality owners, stop/continue conditions and needed real approval, no actual capital release.","Deliver eight-slide packet, timed defence notes and action register with changed decision/evidence; a hypothetical staged plan needs its own model."]}},"83":{"objectives":["Reconcile total and incremental SME growth economics, receivables/capex/cash bridges and dated funding-floor breaches in a numerical board-defence lab with explicit downside and reversal thresholds."],"videos":[{"id":"h5qWn0SfTXA","title":"What the Best Pitch Decks Have in Common with Mike Vernal (Sequoia Capital)","by":"NFX","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - preparing a board deck","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/preparing-a-board-deck","note":"Read decision-focused calibration, actual-versus-plan metrics/challenges, working discussion and preparation using operating reports. A practitioner template, not Indian legal governance requirements, a fixed meeting length or authority to circulate board materials."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - writing a business plan","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"Clear purpose/problem/solution/market/alternatives/business model. Fundraising structure is distinct from a board decision, cash forecast or verified evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NFX - how VCs judge a startup (2020)","url":"https://www.nfx.com/post/how-vcs-judge-your-startup-on-zoom","note":"Historical publisher interview article on clarity, problem/solution/results, metric relevance and conversation. Multiple investor preferences differ; warm-introduction-only or meeting-timing advice is not a universal rule. Not a verbatim transcript of the assigned short clip."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - valuation/FCF (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Scenario and explicit cash/working-capital/capex reasoning; historical rate/tax examples are not current Indian rules. Workbook states its own simplified accounting and timing basis."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Indian SME board-style rehearsal, not a real board decision, legal governance checklist or sponsor signoff. All companies, roles, inputs, delivery records and objections are invented. Choose continue the existing plan, adopt the growth plan, redesign/stage it or defer. A polished deck cannot replace evidence, funding, participant permissions, actual governance requirements or owner authority. Do not contact board members, circulate confidential files, spend or arrange financing.\nCommon 90-day basis, lakh rupees. Existing-plan recognized revenue 30, complete stated variable costs 18, avoidable operating fixed 9, collections 27 and no new capex gives operating remainder 3, end receivables 3 and cash movement 0. Growth totals, not increments: revenue 42, variable 25.2, fixed 11, collections 33.6, new capex 3. Total operating remainder 5.8, receivables 8.4 and cash movement minus 5.6. Incremental growth versus existing: revenue 12, variable 7.2, fixed 2, operating 2.8, receivables 5.4 and capex 3; incremental cash 2.8-5.4-3=minus 5.6. Growth's 5.8 total operating is not 5.8 incremental value, and its revenue 42 is not 42 new revenue. Historical sunk spending is excluded from this forward comparison, but all case-specific future costs and obligations must be included. Operating remainder is before depreciation/interest/tax and is not net income. No opening receivables/inventory/payables, financing or taxes are assumed; all stated operating costs paid within the horizon. Any different real accounting basis needs a new bridge.\nOpening unrestricted company cash 8, no confirmed financing, proposed minimum company cash 2. Baseline cash stays 8 under its simplified balanced flows. Growth events: day 0 capex 3; day 30 collect 8/pay variable 8/pay fixed 3.5; day 60 collect 11/pay variable 8.4/pay fixed 3.5; day 90 collect 14.6/pay variable 8.8/pay fixed 4. Opening 8 becomes 5,1.5,0.6,2.4 at those checkpoints. With only these simultaneous dated net events, minimum 0.6 breaches 2 floor, even though end cash 2.4 passes. Maximum company cash reduction from opening is 7.4, not the endpoint 5.6. Intra-period ordering, deposits, debt covenants and unlisted obligations are not established; the aggregate schedule is not proof an actual firm can pay every bill. No financing is created by the expected operating benefit.\nProposed review gates: incremental operating remainder at least 2.5 over 90 days, company cash never below 2 and on-time eligible deliveries at least 95 percent. Gates are fictional owner preferences, not market or legal standards. Existing deliveries 19/20 on-time 95 percent; growth 27/30=90 percent, below the target and based on a small invented set, not a causal forecast or population reliability estimate. If no eligible deliveries exist, report insufficient evidence. Growth passes the 2.5 operating increment but fails stated cash and quality screens; do not recommend automatic rollout or claim stronger demand from larger totals alone.\nDownside growth: revenue 36, variable 21.6 at 60 percent, same fixed 11/capex 3, collections 25.2. Total operating 3.4, incremental versus baseline 0.4, receivables 10.8, project cash movement minus 10.4 and company end cash minus 2.4. No dated downside schedule is supplied, so do not invent its exact peak draw. With 60 percent variable cost/fixed 11, total operating breakeven revenue 27.5. To match existing operating 3 requires revenue 35; to deliver incremental 2.5 requires growth operating 5.5 and revenue 41.25. The 41.25 threshold is not a sales forecast, funding plan or quality guarantee. A staged redesign needs its own capacity, cost, price, collection and quality model; do not cut capex or shift payables without a supported changed plan.\nNumerical defence lab: derive existing 3 operating/3 receivables/0 cash movement, growth 5.8/8.4/minus 5.6, incremental 2.8/5.4/minus 5.6 and dated balances 5/1.5/0.6/2.4 from opening 8. Reconcile collections 33.6, variable 25.2 and fixed 11 across events; show precisely when the 2 floor fails. Explain why end cash 2.4 cannot substitute for minimum 0.6 or a full intraperiod liquidity model. Reconcile downside 3.4 total operating/0.4 increment/10.8 receivables/minus 10.4 movement/minus 2.4 company end cash. Derive 27.5 total operating breakeven,35 baseline-matching and 41.25 incremental target revenues under fixed unit economics, not validated demand. Preserve quality denominators 19/20 vs 27/30 and funding/authority gaps even if a revenue target passes. Deliver a formula workbook, eight-slide claim/evidence storyboard and action register; rehearse a timed defence with actual measured duration only if performed. Show the strongest objection and revised continue/redesign/defer recommendation without assuming a staged plan has been costed or approved.\n<section aria-label=\"Native D82 D83 board lab\"><h3>Defend the growth choice without hiding cash or quality</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d82-d83-board-lab.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Editable seven-sheet board lab (XLSX)</a> | <a href=\"/practice/top1-d82-d83-board-lab.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">seven-page print view (PDF)</a> | <a href=\"/practice/top1-d82-d83-board-practice.pptx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">editable eight-slide practice deck (PPTX)</a> | <a href=\"/practice/top1-d82-d83-board-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">deck print view (PDF)</a>. The original fictional D82-D83 case and rubric remain unchanged. This is solo synthetic practice, not an actual board decision, company disclosure, permission or completed timed defence.</p><p>One 90-day basis, lakh INR: existing revenue 30, variable 18, fixed 9, collections 27, capex 0 gives operating 3, receivables 3, cash movement 0. Growth totals 42, 25.2, 11, 33.6 and 3 give operating 5.8, receivables 8.4, cash movement minus 5.6. The increment is only 2.8 operating, less extra receivables 5.4 and capex 3. Operating remainder is before depreciation, interest and tax, not net income. This case assumes zero opening working capital, inventory, payables, taxes and financing; stated operating costs are paid within the horizon.</p><p>Opening company cash 8 becomes 5 at day 0, 1.5 at day 30, .6 at day 60 and 2.4 at day 90. Minimum .6 breaches proposed floor 2; maximum reduction 7.4 is not endpoint movement minus 5.6. Dated ledger totals must reconcile with growth inputs before the gate appears. Transfer: opening 10 makes minimum 2.6, yet quality still fails. Restore 8, then shift collections 2 from day 90 to day 30 while preserving total 33.6: minimum and ending cash 2.4, yet quality still fails. These are hypothetical changes, not authorized financing or negotiated receipts. Only simultaneous net events on specified dates are modeled; intraday ordering and unlisted obligations remain unknown.</p><p>Quality is separate: existing 19/20 is 95 percent; growth 27/30 is 90 percent and fails proposed 95 percent. Zero eligible deliveries is undefined, not 100 percent. Raising growth on-time count to 29/30 clears that sample screen, not the failed original cash floor or population-quality proof. Downside revenue 36, costs 21.6/11, collections 25.2 and capex 3 gives operating 3.4, incremental .4, receivables 10.8, movement minus 10.4 and ending cash minus 2.4. Its dates are absent: dated downside peak remains UNKNOWN. Conditional sales thresholds 27.5/35/41.25 produce zero operating/existing operating 3/incremental target 2.5 only at 60 percent variable cost and fixed 11; algebra is not a demand or funding plan.</p><p>Eight editable slides retain counterfactual, baseline/growth inputs, operating-to-cash bridge, native dated cash chart, quality denominators, downside/thresholds, strongest objection and fictional 0/30/60/90 evidence/cash/quality/rights owners. Recommendation: do not adopt the full growth plan under supplied gates; preserve existing as counterfactual and redesign/stage/defer until a complete funded, quality-tested case and actual authority exist. Do not hide the objection that growth earns 2.8 more operating and ends with 2.4 cash. Explain the earlier floor breach and failed quality. Choose a real solo timer limit, record actual elapsed time only when performed, retain objections and log changed reasoning. No timer, spoken defence, learner/panel response or recording review was performed here.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice passed 89 exact-rational baseline comparisons, 90 opening-cash, 90 earlier-collections, 85 zero-denominator, 91 quality-only and 89 restored comparisons. Eight invalid inputs, three invalid ledger events, unreconciled totals, broken references and zero revenue/contribution were tested. Saved workbook has 89 cached formulas, zero errors, no external links/macros. Seven print pages and all eight saved-deck PDF slides inspected; slide 4 callout separation fixed. Editable chart/table/text structure and eight notes checked. Changed 320/390/1280 course states inspected. Bounded authored execution is not Excel/Sheets parity, independent delayed learner transfer, actual timed defence, full AV/source coverage, real rights/funding or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["State common 90-day/units/accounting basis and separate baseline/growth/incremental results, not forecast as fact.","Reconcile baseline 3/3/0,growth 5.8/8.4/minus 5.6 and increment 2.8/5.4/minus 5.6 operating/AR/cash under exclusions.","Reconcile dated collections 33.6/variable 25.2/fixed 11/capex 3 and company balances 5/1.5/0.6/2.4 from opening 8.","Mandatory provenance: log synthetic input/source/date/unit/formulas and assumptions, preserve uncertainty and original-versus-revised versions.","Compare minimum 0.6 with floor 2; endpoint 2.4 does not prove funding pass, and monthly events do not verify actual intraperiod cash.","Compute 19/20 and 27/30 on-time against 95 percent; distinguish recognized sales, collections, receivables and capital outlay.","Mandatory downside/reversal: derive downside 0.4 increment/10.8 AR/minus 10.4 movement and 27.5/35/41.25 revenue thresholds; do not claim feasible demand/financing.","Explain model sensitivity, missing evidence and strongest objection; keep actual permission/governance separate from economic calculation.","Mandatory owner/gate: assign fictional day 0/30/60/90 finance/quality/evidence/authority checks; no board resolution or new financing inferred.","Deliver formulas,eight-slide claim storyboard/action register and revised choice; timing is measured only if actually rehearsed."]}},"84":{"objectives":["Test fresh mixed transfer after contradictory evidence using manually observed errors and changed assumptions, not an invented measured weakest-skill profile or repeated familiar questions."],"videos":[{"id":"h5qWn0SfTXA","title":"What the Best Pitch Decks Have in Common with Mike Vernal (Sequoia Capital)","by":"NFX","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - preparing a board deck","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/preparing-a-board-deck","note":"Read decision-focused calibration, actual-versus-plan metrics/challenges, working discussion and preparation using operating reports. A practitioner template, not Indian legal governance requirements, a fixed meeting length or authority to circulate board materials."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - writing a business plan","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"Clear purpose/problem/solution/market/alternatives/business model. Fundraising structure is distinct from a board decision, cash forecast or verified evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NFX - how VCs judge a startup (2020)","url":"https://www.nfx.com/post/how-vcs-judge-your-startup-on-zoom","note":"Historical publisher interview article on clarity, problem/solution/results, metric relevance and conversation. Multiple investor preferences differ; warm-introduction-only or meeting-timing advice is not a universal rule. Not a verbatim transcript of the assigned short clip."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - valuation/FCF (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Scenario and explicit cash/working-capital/capex reasoning; historical rate/tax examples are not current Indian rules. Workbook states its own simplified accounting and timing basis."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - retrieval and interleaving","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Supports closed-notes recall and mixed practice, not measured product learning gains or automatic question-level diagnosis."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GHG Protocol - inventory versus project accounting","url":"https://ghgprotocol.org/blog/inventory-and-project-accounting","note":"Planned counterfactual effects are not corporate scope deductions or issued credits. Use matching service/unit/boundary and visible baseline sensitivity."}],"exercise":"Private review-day transfer. All company, role, cash and emissions records are synthetic. Solve without notes, then check formulas and log attempted answer, error cause, correction, evidence and a delayed changed-context test. Pick two remediation areas from actual mistakes. Sparse or no errors means provisional choices. The app's day score/timestamp does not provide question-level weakness measurements. Confidence or an 8/10 self-check is not independent mastery.\nFresh 90-day SME case, lakh rupees. No opening working capital, inventory, payables, taxes or financing; stated operating costs are paid within the window. Existing revenue 20 - variable 12 - fixed 6 = operating 2. Collections 18 give receivables 2 and cash movement 0. Growth totals: revenue 30 - variable 18 - fixed 8 = operating 4; collections 24 give receivables 6; capex 2 and cash movement 24-18-8-2 = minus 4. Incremental operating 2, receivables 4 and capex 2 also yield minus 4, not 4 new cash. Operating remainder is before depreciation/interest/tax, not net income.\nOpening company cash 7, proposed floor 2. Dated growth events: day 0 capex 2; day 30 collect 4/pay variable 6/pay fixed 2; day 60 collect 8/pay variable 6/pay fixed 3; day 90 collect 12/pay variable 6/pay fixed 3. Checkpoint cash 5, 1, 0, 3; minimum 0 fails floor 2 even though ending 3 passes. With only these simultaneous net events, maximum reduction is 7. Unlisted intraday ordering remains unknown. Invented quality 22/25 = 88 percent fails a proposed 95 percent target; revenue alone cannot cure this. A new 1 rework charge paid day 60, outside the original variable 18, reduces operating to 3/increment to 1, ending company cash to 2 and minimum to minus 1. Negative cash means a funding gap, not negative receivables or automatically permitted borrowing. Show old and new models and why final floor equality does not fix earlier deficits.\nIndependent climate transfer: invented equal-service baseline 0.70 and solution 0.55 tCO2e per unit, 2,000 annual units gives 300 tCO2e/year planned avoided emissions. Changed baseline 0.50 gives minus 100. Not realized savings, carbon credits, revenue or scope inventory netting. State matching lifecycle boundary, significant effects and uncertainty before using the factors. Independent common-equity deal: cheque 2/pre-money 18 gives 10 percent entry ownership; retained fraction 0.60 gives 6 percent exit ownership; distributable equity exit 100 gives cash 6/2 = 3x gross deal MOIC. A proposed 4x needs equity 133.333333 holding other inputs fixed. Adding 1 follow-on needs a new ownership/total-cost model, not keeping the original denominator 2. Assume no interim distributions, no added preferences and common pro-rata payout; no real company valuation or investment clearance is claimed.\nDeliver a corrected workbook, source/input/change/error logs, strongest counterevidence and two delayed transfer prompts. Distinguish company cash from project movement, totals from increments, planned climate effects from actual inventory and single-deal MOIC from fund net returns. Record fictional day 0/30/60/90 review owners and stop/review gates. No actual spending, external approval, data disclosure or board resolution.\n<section aria-label=\"Native D84 mixed transfer\"><h3>Fresh transfer requires your own attempt, not an invented diagnosis</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d84-mixed-transfer.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Editable ten-sheet mixed-transfer workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d84-mixed-transfer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">ten-page print view (PDF)</a>. Original D84 synthetic case and rubric remain unchanged. Solve without notes before looking at formulas. Attempts is blank by design: no learner errors, dates, delays, weakest-skill profile or mastery are invented. Pick two areas from actual errors only; with sparse/no errors, label choices provisional. Confidence, day score and an 8/10 self-check are not independent learning evidence.</p><p>Separate 90-day SME, planned climate and common-equity cases. Lakh INR: existing operating 2, receivables 2 and movement 0; growth original operating 4, receivables 6 and movement minus 4. Incremental operating 2 minus extra receivables 4 minus capex 2 also gives minus 4, not 4 new cash. Operating remainder is before depreciation/interest/tax. Zero opening working capital/inventory/payables/taxes/financing and all stated operating costs paid are explicit simplifications, not full real accounts.</p><p>Opening company cash 7 yields original checkpoint cash 5/1/0/3. Minimum 0 fails proposed floor 2 despite ending 3. Extra rework 1, outside variable 18 and paid once day 60, gives revised operating 3/increment 1, movement minus 5, cash 5/1/minus 1/2: minimum minus 1 still fails even though ending equals floor. Original/revised maximum reductions 7/8 differ from endpoint project movements minus 4/minus 5. Ledger totals must reconcile before funding gates appear. Moving the same rework to day 30 changes revised checkpoint cash to 5/0/minus 1/2, but not its minimum or endpoint in this case. Opening 10 gives revised minimum 2/end 5, yet revised operating increment 1 and quality 22/25=88 percent still fail proposed targets 2 and 95 percent. No actual financing, supplier delay or borrowing authorized.</p><p>Independent equal-service climate arithmetic: (.70 minus .55) times 2000 = 300 planned tCO2e/year; changed baseline .50 gives minus 100, not zero. State matching service/lifecycle boundary, significant effects and uncertainty. These are not actual savings, inventory deductions, credits or cash. Separate common-equity cheque 2/pre-money 18 gives 10 percent entry, retained .60 gives 6 percent exit, distributable equity 100 gives cash 6 and 3x gross deal MOIC. Proposed 4x needs equity 133.333333 conditionally. Retention .50 gives 5 percent exit/cash 5/2.5x/target equity 160. Extra follow-on 1 needs a new ownership and total-cost model, not the old denominator 2. Gross single-deal MOIC is not fund net-LP return or investment clearance.</p><p>Keep attempted answer/reasoning, observed error/cause, correction/evidence, strongest counterevidence and actual delayed retest in the blank ruled Attempts sheet. Two future prompts vary rework timing/opening cash and climate baseline/deal retention; no delayed test was performed here. Fictional evidence/cash/quality/rights owners at day 0/30/60/90 preserve stop/review gates, never real obligations. Do not use planned climate effects or gross deal returns to erase SME cash/quality failures. Preserve actual permission gaps and mandatory provenance/downside/owner checks.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice passed 104 baseline, 104 earlier-rework, 105 opening-cash, 104 each zero/2/.5 rework, 98 zero-quality, 104 changed-climate, 104 zero-units, 104 retention, 103 zero-retention, 102 zero-cheque and 104 restored exact-rational comparisons. Twelve invalid financial/day/count/climate/deal inputs, three invalid events, unreconciled ledger and broken company-cash/ownership references tested. Zero eligible count makes quality undefined; zero cheque makes MOIC undefined; zero retention makes target equity undefined. Saved 104 cached formulas, zero errors, no external links/macros. All ten print pages inspected; Attempts header spacing/ruled blanks and prose readability improved and rechecked, with blank cells verified. Changed 320/390/1280 course views inspected. This closes authored native practice only, not independent learner/delayed transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, full AV/source reading, actual rights/funding or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Attempt fresh mixed cases without notes; keep the 90-day SME, climate and ownership cases separate and synthetic.","Reconcile existing 2/2/0, growth 4/6/minus 4 and incremental 2/4/minus 4 operating/receivables/cash; totals are not increments.","Build cash 5/1/0/3 from opening 7. Minimum 0 fails floor 2; ending 3 is not the peak funding test.","Mandatory provenance: record actual attempted errors, synthetic inputs, original source dates/units and the new 1 rework event; no invented weak-skill profile.","After rework compute operating 3/increment 1/ending company cash 2/minimum minus 1; quality 22/25 = 88 percent fails 95 percent.","Compute climate 300 then minus 100 planned tCO2e/year and deal 10 percent entry/6 percent exit/3x gross MOIC; avoid credit/net-LP/netting claims.","Mandatory downside/reversal: show 133.333333 equity needed for 4x, follow-on cost needs a new model and changed cash/baseline can reverse the recommendation.","Pick two remediation areas from actual mistakes, provisional when sparse. Day score, confidence and self-check are not independent mastery.","Mandatory owner/gate: fictional day 0/30/60/90 review owners and delayed changed-context tests; no spending, borrowing, disclosure or real approval.","Deliver corrected workbook/error log, strongest objection and two delayed transfer prompts; retain old/new reasoning and remaining unknowns."]}},"85":{"objectives":["Build a private 15-slide board exercise in which each claim matches its evidence, time basis and modeled counterfactual; distinguish clarity from causal proof and reconcile total revenue through incremental economics and company cash."],"videos":[{"id":"elMPIxLYtoc","title":"Consultant Explains the Pyramid Principle","by":"Analyst Academy","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the specific written synthetic workbook for counterfactual, cash and permissions; a communication framework or business-plan recording does not itself validate this invented case."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Analyst Academy - assigned Pyramid Principle recording","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=elMPIxLYtoc","note":"Put the answer first, support it logically and make each parent claim summarize its children. Adjust storyboard depth to the audience; structure alone does not prove the facts or causality."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Analyst Academy - slide structure (2021)","url":"https://www.theanalystacademy.com/consulting-slide-structure/","note":"Written publisher explanation of main message, supporting arguments and evidence. Logical presentation does not establish causality, current historical example facts or truthful numerical assumptions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Analyst Academy - consulting slides versus regular slides (2021)","url":"https://www.theanalystacademy.com/consulting-slides-vs-regular-slides/","note":"Structure, visuals, insight headlines and consistent formatting. A chart or polished visual is not evidence of accuracy. Historical consulting examples are illustrations, not current empirical findings."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - preparing a board deck","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/preparing-a-board-deck","note":"Decision-focused calibration, metrics/challenges and working discussion; boards may use memos. Practitioner guide, not Indian legal requirements or approval to share documents."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Wharton - effective business plans, edited interview (2010)","url":"https://knowledge.wharton.upenn.edu/podcast/knowledge-at-wharton-podcast/how-entrepreneurs-can-create-effective-business-plans/","note":"Ian MacMillan edited interview: needs, resources, alternatives, evidence, milestones, risk, cash and revising with learning. Different interview from assigned Patrick FitzGerald 2015 recording. Historical page-count/failure-rate/funding-tranche claims are not universal rules or current financial authority."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - explicit cash/working-capital reasoning (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Use a declared accounting/timing basis and distinguish modeled cash from earnings. Historical taxes/rates not adopted as current Indian rules."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic Indian SME case, not real company evidence or a board authorization. All periods, roles, demand, cash and records are invented. Compare no new growth bet, launch the bet, redesign/stage or defer. Do not circulate the exercise to a real board or use real participant data, contracts or spending.\nTwo equal twelve-month windows A and B, lakh rupees. A is a fictional completed observation: revenue 24. B is a forward-looking model, not observed actual performance. Without the bet B revenue 30, variable 18, avoidable fixed 8, collections 27, no capex. With the bet B revenue 36, variable 21.6, fixed 10, collections 25.2 and capex 2.5. All operating costs paid within B; zero opening receivables, inventory or payables, no taxes, interest, depreciation, financing or disposals. Existing-plan B operating 4, receivables 3 and cash movement 1. Growth B operating 4.4, receivables 10.8 and cash movement minus 8.9. Incremental growth versus no bet in B: revenue 6, variable 3.6, fixed 2, operating 0.4, receivables 7.8, capex 2.5 and cash movement minus 9.9. Check 0.4-7.8-2.5=minus 9.9, also minus 8.9 minus baseline cash 1. Never call revenue 36 or operating 4.4 incremental.\nA before/after chart 24 to 36 shows 12 modeled change, not 12 caused by the bet. The B counterfactual 30 implies only 6 modeled incremental revenue; even that is conditional, not a measured causal effect. Time trends, prices, selection, capacity and competing changes could confound a real before/after comparison. Show A observed versus B forecast visibly and label B without/with bet on matching units/windows. Do not draw a solid actual line through the forecast or move between revenue, contribution and cash without a bridge. The pyramid's headline summarizes supporting logic; a clear title does not make a hypothesis true.\nOpening unrestricted company cash 10 and proposed minimum floor 2. No-bet B ends 11; growth ends 1.1, so it fails the floor even at the endpoint. Without dated cash events, exact minimum/peak funding is unknown; endpoint failure suffices to reject an unconditional pass, not to invent the full draw. Proposed review gate: incremental operating at least 2 plus cash never below 2 and delivery quality at least 95 percent. Quality evidence is absent; mark unknown, not zero or 100 percent. Larger revenue alone cannot pass these screens. Under 40 percent contribution/fixed 10, growth revenue 40 is required for operating 6 and thus incremental 2 above baseline 4. This is algebra, not a validated forecast of demand, funding or delivery quality.\nCoherent sensitivity on the same B basis, holding baseline 4 operating/1 cash movement unchanged. Downside growth revenue 30,var 18,fixed 10,collections 21,capex 2.5: operating 2,increment minus 2,receivables 9,cash movement minus 9.5 and end company cash 0.5. Upside growth revenue 42,var 25.2,fixed 10,collections 33.6,capex 2.5: operating 6.8,increment 2.8,receivables 8.4,cash movement minus 4.1,end company cash 5.9. Upside endpoint cash and operating gates may pass, but peak timing and quality remain unknown. Scenarios are not probabilities or measured outcomes. A redesign needs its own price/volume/cost/collection/quality model and actual authority, not a cosmetic new title or assumed deferral of supplier costs.\nDeck task: retain 15 slides as this course's practice format, not a universal consulting or legal board rule. Use sentence headlines whose certainty matches the body. Suggested storyboard:1 conditional recommendation/decision;2 fictional scope/window/alternatives;3 A-observed/B-forecast status;4 B without/with-bet revenue;5 incremental revenue 6 bridge;6 variable/fixed/operating 0.4 bridge;7 receivables/collections;8 capex/incremental cash minus 9.9;9 company cash 10 to 1.1 and floor 2;10 quality unknown/needed test;11 downside/upside;12 revenue 40 threshold and what does not follow;13 strongest contrary explanation;14 fictional day 0/30/60/90 action register;15 exact remaining evidence/authority and revise/defer conditions. Details can go in an appendix, not disappear.\nEach slide needs claim ID, evidence/input ID, source/date/unit/window, synthetic flag, assumption-versus-observation status and decision consequence. Use matched scales, explicit denominators, labeled missing values and readable source notes. Bar/column comparisons use a truthful common zero baseline unless a different scale is clearly justified; do not truncate to manufacture a larger effect. Avoid double axes or incomparable time windows that imply a false relationship. Before treating a learner's deck as ready, inspect all actual rendered slides for readability, clipped charts/footnotes and whether the headline is supported by the displayed data; this curriculum text itself is not a completed or visually verified deck. Log the strongest objection, a corrected headline and whether the provisional recommendation changed. No actual board distribution or capital release. Operating remainder is before depreciation, interest and tax, not net income. The A observation is fictional within the case, not a real observed company.<section aria-label=\"Native D85 board deck\"><h3>Editable 15-slide evidence-led board practice</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d85-board-practice.pptx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Editable PowerPoint (PPTX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d85-board-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">15-slide print view (PDF)</a>. 15 native editable slides, two editable zero-baseline charts, tables and notes with claim/input/status metadata. Separates fictional-observed A from forecast B and categorical cash endpoints from a dated cash timeline. All 15 rendered slides inspected; repaired negative-sign wrapping and preserved chart labels/scales.</p><p>Original synthetic case/rubric remain unchanged. INR lakh, equal 12-month A/B windows; A 24 is fictional-observed within case, B 30 without bet and 36 with bet are forecasts. Before/after 12 is not modeled increment 6 or measured causality. No-bet operating 4, cash movement 1, ending cash 11 versus growth operating 4.4, cash movement -8.9, ending cash 1.1. Incremental operating 0.4, receivables 7.8, capex 2.5 and cash -9.9 reconcile independently. Endpoint 1.1 fails proposed floor 2; the 0.9 endpoint gap is not peak funding. Dated minimum, quality and rights remain UNKNOWN. Downside increment -2 / ending cash 0.5 and upside 2.8 / ending cash 5.9 use coherent collections/costs; upside does not clear all gates. Revenue 40 meets only the conditional operating target under 60% variable costs and fixed 10.</p><p>Keep no-bet, launch, redesign/stage and defer under the same evidence standards. Fictional day 0/30/60/90 reviewers are simulated dependencies, not real appointments or authority. The strongest objection is authored solo practice, not a participant quote. No actual board distribution, funding, consent, learner defence or independent signoff is claimed. Source/input/status details stay in the artifacts; existing Analyst Academy/Sequoia/Wharton/MIT method framing is not evidence for invented numbers.</p><p>Separate exact-rational arithmetic and saved chart/native/PDF readback passed 83 combined checks for D85/D86; this is authored artifact QA, not independent human/learner assessment. Original cases/rubrics preserved. This closes the authored native-format example gap, not actual learner delivery/transfer, PowerPoint/Word/Slides parity, full source/audiovisual validation, account flow or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Keep 15 slides as private practice, not actual board approval or a universal rule; name the fictional decision, scope, window and audience.","Separate A observed 24 from B forecast 30 without bet/36 with bet; before/after 12 is not modeled increment 6 or measured causality.","Compare no-bet, launch, redesign/stage and defer using the same units/window and evidence standards.","Mandatory provenance: each claim has input/source/date/unit/window and synthetic/observed/forecast/assumed status; unknown values stay visible.","Reconcile incremental operating 0.4/AR 7.8/capex 2.5/cash minus 9.9 and company 10 to 1.1, not larger total revenue as cash benefit.","Use truthful comparable chart scales/denominators and check actual rendered headlines, data, sources and footnotes rather than trust polish.","Mandatory downside/reversal: show downside increment minus 2/cash end 0.5 and upside increment 2.8/end 5.9; threshold 40 does not validate quality/peak cash.","Retain strongest contrary explanation and distinguish clear logic, conditional estimates, causal proof and real authority.","Mandatory owner/gate: fictional day 0/30/60/90 demand/cost/cash/quality/rights reviewers, stop/review conditions, no capital release.","Deliver 15 slides with source footnotes, claim/evidence register, corrected headline/strongest objection and changed recommendation; no actual board distribution."]}},"86":{"objectives":["Write a private 10-page answer-first board memo with a precise conditional decision, consistent economic alternatives, falsifying evidence and owned review gates, without burying the ask or turning a mock vote into real approval."],"videos":[{"id":"zlrb_X6fYZ0","title":"Business Plan Writing 101: Wharton Entrepreneurship Series","by":"Wharton School","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the specific written synthetic workbook for counterfactual, cash and permissions; a communication framework or business-plan recording does not itself validate this invented case."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Wharton - assigned Business Plan Writing101 (2015)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zlrb_X6fYZ0","note":"Supports clear company purpose, team, product process, market, roadmap, competition, revenue/cost drivers and ask. Historical specific team/hiring/affiliation, survey-count, page-count, legal/option/equity and fundraising advice is not adopted as universal or current Indian rules. Never invent customer conversations or disclose their contacts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Analyst Academy - slide structure (2021)","url":"https://www.theanalystacademy.com/consulting-slide-structure/","note":"Written publisher explanation of main message, supporting arguments and evidence. Logical presentation does not establish causality, current historical example facts or truthful numerical assumptions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Analyst Academy - consulting slides versus regular slides (2021)","url":"https://www.theanalystacademy.com/consulting-slides-vs-regular-slides/","note":"Structure, visuals, insight headlines and consistent formatting. A chart or polished visual is not evidence of accuracy. Historical consulting examples are illustrations, not current empirical findings."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - preparing a board deck","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/preparing-a-board-deck","note":"Decision-focused calibration, metrics/challenges and working discussion; boards may use memos. Practitioner guide, not Indian legal requirements or approval to share documents."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Wharton - effective business plans, edited interview (2010)","url":"https://knowledge.wharton.upenn.edu/podcast/knowledge-at-wharton-podcast/how-entrepreneurs-can-create-effective-business-plans/","note":"Ian MacMillan edited interview: needs, resources, alternatives, evidence, milestones, risk, cash and revising with learning. Different interview from assigned Patrick FitzGerald 2015 recording. Historical page-count/failure-rate/funding-tranche claims are not universal rules or current financial authority."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - explicit cash/working-capital reasoning (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Use a declared accounting/timing basis and distinguish modeled cash from earnings. Historical taxes/rates not adopted as current Indian rules."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic Indian SME case, not real company evidence or a board authorization. All periods, roles, demand, cash and records are invented. Compare no new growth bet, launch the bet, redesign/stage or defer. Do not circulate the exercise to a real board or use real participant data, contracts or spending.\nTwo equal twelve-month windows A and B, lakh rupees. A is a fictional completed observation: revenue 24. B is a forward-looking model, not observed actual performance. Without the bet B revenue 30, variable 18, avoidable fixed 8, collections 27, no capex. With the bet B revenue 36, variable 21.6, fixed 10, collections 25.2 and capex 2.5. All operating costs paid within B; zero opening receivables, inventory or payables, no taxes, interest, depreciation, financing or disposals. Existing-plan B operating 4, receivables 3 and cash movement 1. Growth B operating 4.4, receivables 10.8 and cash movement minus 8.9. Incremental growth versus no bet in B: revenue 6, variable 3.6, fixed 2, operating 0.4, receivables 7.8, capex 2.5 and cash movement minus 9.9. Check 0.4-7.8-2.5=minus 9.9, also minus 8.9 minus baseline cash 1. Never call revenue 36 or operating 4.4 incremental.\nA before/after chart 24 to 36 shows 12 modeled change, not 12 caused by the bet. The B counterfactual 30 implies only 6 modeled incremental revenue; even that is conditional, not a measured causal effect. Time trends, prices, selection, capacity and competing changes could confound a real before/after comparison. Show A observed versus B forecast visibly and label B without/with bet on matching units/windows. Do not draw a solid actual line through the forecast or move between revenue, contribution and cash without a bridge. The pyramid's headline summarizes supporting logic; a clear title does not make a hypothesis true.\nOpening unrestricted company cash 10 and proposed minimum floor 2. No-bet B ends 11; growth ends 1.1, so it fails the floor even at the endpoint. Without dated cash events, exact minimum/peak funding is unknown; endpoint failure suffices to reject an unconditional pass, not to invent the full draw. Proposed review gate: incremental operating at least 2 plus cash never below 2 and delivery quality at least 95 percent. Quality evidence is absent; mark unknown, not zero or 100 percent. Larger revenue alone cannot pass these screens. Under 40 percent contribution/fixed 10, growth revenue 40 is required for operating 6 and thus incremental 2 above baseline 4. This is algebra, not a validated forecast of demand, funding or delivery quality.\nCoherent sensitivity on the same B basis, holding baseline 4 operating/1 cash movement unchanged. Downside growth revenue 30,var 18,fixed 10,collections 21,capex 2.5: operating 2,increment minus 2,receivables 9,cash movement minus 9.5 and end company cash 0.5. Upside growth revenue 42,var 25.2,fixed 10,collections 33.6,capex 2.5: operating 6.8,increment 2.8,receivables 8.4,cash movement minus 4.1,end company cash 5.9. Upside endpoint cash and operating gates may pass, but peak timing and quality remain unknown. Scenarios are not probabilities or measured outcomes. A redesign needs its own price/volume/cost/collection/quality model and actual authority, not a cosmetic new title or assumed deferral of supplier costs.\nMemo task: retain 10 pages as a practice constraint, not a mandate that investors or boards read exactly ten. Page 1 states the private conditional recommendation, amount/time basis, alternatives, evidence gaps and decision requested in the simulation. Do not hide a funding/quality failure behind an optimistic ask. Pages 2-3 scope, no-bet counterfactual and demand/evidence status;4-5 incremental operating/receivables/capex/company cash and financing constraints;6 coherent sensitivities/revenue 40 threshold;7 quality/rights/execution risks and contrary explanations;8 alternatives including defer/staging, each with the evidence needed;9 fictional day 0/30/60/90 owners/stop-review gates;10 strongest objection, change log, original-source register and remaining actual authority. Keep footnotes beside the supported claims, with detailed calculations in a labeled appendix if necessary rather than shrink unreadably.\nThe first-page answer may be defer or redesign pending evidence. A statement of modeled benefit is not a request already approved, a legal resolution or commitment to financing. Present the same facts and standards when defending the no-bet case or the favored plan. Distinguish a stakeholder question, tentative preference, conditional recommendation and actual owner authorization. A signed-looking fictional sponsor box is not real signoff. Deliver the memo, source/input register, eight-row decision/action/change log and strongest objection. Independently review formulas and inspect actual rendered pages before calling a learner's memo ready; an outline is not the finished memo. No real sending, recordings, hiring, contracts or spend. Operating remainder is before depreciation, interest and tax, not net income. The A observation is fictional within the case, not a real observed company.<section aria-label=\"Native D86 board memo\"><h3>Editable 10-page answer-first board-memo practice</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d86-board-memo.docx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Editable Word memo (DOCX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d86-board-memo.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Ten editable pages with decision on page 1, source/input register, eight-row decision/action/change trail, strongest authored objection and fictional dependency gates. Actual review/change row explicitly NOT PERFORMED. All 10 final rendered pages inspected; source URLs remain readable. Ten pages is this practice constraint, not a universal board rule.</p><p>Original synthetic case/rubric remain unchanged. INR lakh, equal 12-month A/B windows; A 24 is fictional-observed within case, B 30 without bet and 36 with bet are forecasts. Before/after 12 is not modeled increment 6 or measured causality. No-bet operating 4, cash movement 1, ending cash 11 versus growth operating 4.4, cash movement -8.9, ending cash 1.1. Incremental operating 0.4, receivables 7.8, capex 2.5 and cash -9.9 reconcile independently. Endpoint 1.1 fails proposed floor 2; the 0.9 endpoint gap is not peak funding. Dated minimum, quality and rights remain UNKNOWN. Downside increment -2 / ending cash 0.5 and upside 2.8 / ending cash 5.9 use coherent collections/costs; upside does not clear all gates. Revenue 40 meets only the conditional operating target under 60% variable costs and fixed 10.</p><p>Keep no-bet, launch, redesign/stage and defer under the same evidence standards. Fictional day 0/30/60/90 reviewers are simulated dependencies, not real appointments or authority. The strongest objection is authored solo practice, not a participant quote. No actual board distribution, funding, consent, learner defence or independent signoff is claimed. Source/input/status details stay in the artifacts; existing Analyst Academy/Sequoia/Wharton/MIT method framing is not evidence for invented numbers.</p><p>Separate exact-rational arithmetic and saved chart/native/PDF readback passed 83 combined checks for D85/D86; this is authored artifact QA, not independent human/learner assessment. Original cases/rubrics preserved. This closes the authored native-format example gap, not actual learner delivery/transfer, PowerPoint/Word/Slides parity, full source/audiovisual validation, account flow or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Use 10-page private practice format and put the conditional recommendation/decision request on page 1, not a real approval or fixed board rule.","Define B no-bet counterfactual versus growth totals and separate observed A from forecast B; do not sell before/after change as caused benefit.","Present no-bet/launch/redesign/defer under consistent costs, evidence and actual authority limits; a staged plan needs a new model.","Mandatory provenance: original source/input/date/unit/status register, synthetic case flag and explicit unresolved evidence on the claims they support.","Reconcile incremental 0.4 operating and minus 9.9 cash/company end 1.1; missing quality/peak dates/financing cannot be cleared by title wording.","Explain assumptions, stakeholder perspectives and which question/choice/approval is actually simulated; no invented conversations or consent.","Mandatory downside/reversal: compare coherent downside/upside, revenue 40 target and cash floor/quality gaps; permit a defer/redesign recommendation.","Keep strongest objection, alternative explanation and version/change trail; readability/answer-first is not factual correctness or external signoff.","Mandatory owner/gate: fictional day 0/30/60/90 owners, evidence/artifact dependencies and measurable stop/review conditions, no real execution.","Deliver 10-page memo, source/input registry and eight-row action/change log; inspect actual pages and formulas before claiming learner completion."]}},"87":{"objectives":["Write matched private invest/pass IC memos for two fictional Indian startups using identical evidence standards, explicit dilution and single-deal versus fund-return denominators."],"videos":[{"id":"7ss7yOFgY94","title":"How Venture Capital Firms Create and Use Investment Memos | Slauson & Co. ♻️","by":"Slauson & Co.","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the written synthetic exercise and its sources for the exact math, consent and decision gates; a watched indicator is not evidence of a completed workbook, rehearsal or real approval."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Slauson - investment memo process","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7ss7yOFgY94","note":"Historical US firm check sizes, ownership, timing and geography are not current Indian requirements or fund-return formulas."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - entrepreneurial finance lecture","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Historical teaching on explicit investor valuation/cash assumptions, not current tax rules or automatic investment authority."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA - performance definitions, granular v1.1 (2025)","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Distinguish contribution/distribution/NAV and performance denominators. Use the written definitions and the explicit case figures for the calculation. A single gross deal multiple or proceeds/commitment contribution is not whole-fund net LP performance."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic fund case. Both Indian startup names, teams, metrics, references, terms, roles and outcomes are invented. Do not research or contact real founders, issue a term sheet, send a memo or invest. Compare invest in A, invest in B, pass both or defer; if considering both, build a separate combined allocation and reserve model. A course recommendation is not an actual IC decision.\nAll monetary amounts in this fund case are crore rupees. Fund commitment 100 crore. Simplified lifetime fee/cost allowance 10 leaves 90 for investments, of which 30 is a follow-on reserve and 60 is initial-investment capacity. This is a fictional budget, not a universal fund structure. Candidate A cheque 2 at pre-money 18 gives post-money 20 and 10 percent entry ownership. Candidate B cheque 2 at pre-money 8 gives post-money 10 and 20 percent entry ownership. Assume common equity, no interim distributions, no preference waterfall and no extra money from this fund after entry. External future issuances reduce the fund's ownership by retained fractions 0.60 for A and 0.50 for B, giving exit ownership 6 percent and 10 percent. These dilution assumptions are not guaranteed future financing.\nBase distributable equity exits A 150/B 60 give proceeds 9/6 and gross deal MOIC 4.5x/3x. Failure exits 0/0 give zero proceeds; upside equity exits 300/120 give proceeds 18/12 and deal MOIC 9x/6x. No probabilities are supplied, so do not call these expected returns. A proposed 5x gross-deal target needs proceeds 10: at unchanged ownership A needs equity exit 166.666667, B 100. This is a hurdle calculation, not evidence that either exit is plausible. Follow-on spending requires new cost, ownership, reserve and payout calculations; do not keep denominator 2 automatically.\nA's base proceeds 9 are 0.09x of committed fund capital 100; B's 6 are 0.06x. A 4.5x deal is not a 4.5x fund. These contributions are not whole-fund TVPI, DPI, IRR, net LP returns or realized results. Other portfolio values, capital-call/distribution timing, fees, carry and terms are missing. Even upside proceeds 18/12 do not alone return the whole 100 commitment. Initial check 2 fits the stated 60 capacity, but cannot establish that the full portfolio/follow-on budget or legal process is satisfied. Preserve the no-investment/defer alternative and flag unverified product, customer, cash, rights, governance and founder evidence.\nDeliver paired invest and pass IC arguments for EACH candidate using the same headings, evidence requirements, source/status register, scenario table and kill/reversal criteria. A stronger modeled A multiple must not excuse missing retention or demand evidence that would disqualify B. Treat unknown customer retention as unknown for both, not zero for the disliked candidate and a positive estimate for the favorite. Swap the labels A/B and recheck whether the logic follows facts rather than preference. Separate founder narrative, investor-authored diligence and an actual authorized IC decision. Build a fictional day 0/30/60/90 evidence/owner register with customer, unit economics, cash, legal-rights and financing gates; log the strongest objection and whether each conditional invest/pass/defer view changed. No actual reference calls or data-room disclosure.\n<section aria-label=\"Native D87 paired IC\"><h3>Equal evidence standards, separate deal and fund denominators</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d87-paired-ic.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Editable nine-sheet paired IC practice workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d87-paired-ic.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">nine-page print view (PDF)</a>. Original D87 synthetic case/rubric remain unchanged. Worked invest AND pass/defer arguments for EACH candidate use the same headings and evidence gaps; blank actual practice register is not a completed learner memo or independent diligence. No real founder outreach, reference calls, disclosure, term sheet or investment.</p><p>Crore INR. No interim distributions/preferences/follow-on. A cheque 2/pre-money 18 gives post 20/entry 10%; retained .60 gives exit 6%; base distributable equity 150 gives cash 9/gross deal 4.5x. B cheque 2/pre 8 gives post 10/entry 20%; retained .50 gives exit 10%; base equity 60 gives cash 6/3x. Failure equity 0 gives zero cash; upside equity 300/120 gives cash 18/12 and 9x/6x. No probabilities or dates are supplied, so none is expected or realized return. Proposed 5x requires cash 10 and equity 166.666667/100 conditionally, not proof of exit plausibility. Distributable equity is not enterprise value.</p><p>Fund commitment 100 minus fee/cost allowance 10 leaves 90; reserve 30 leaves initial capacity 60. Each cheque 2 separately fits with 58 capacity remaining; that is not combined portfolio/reserve/rights approval. Base proceeds/COMMITTED capital 9/100=.09x and 6/100=.06x are contribution ratios, not whole-fund DPI/TVPI/IRR or net LP return. Paid-in capital, portfolio values, actual calls/distributions/timing/carry remain UNKNOWN. No customer retention evidence for either candidate: keep UNKNOWN for both, never invent favorable evidence for the preferred company.</p><p>Native transfer tests swap ALL A/B inputs so numerical outputs move with the facts, without claiming a learner bias test. Reducing A retained fraction to .40 gives exit 4%, base cash 6/deal 3x and hurdle equity 250. Changing commitment to 80, with allowance 10/reserve 30 unchanged, gives capacity 40 and A/B contribution .1125x/.075x, while deal 4.5x/3x stays unchanged. Commitment 41 gives capacity 1 so both cheque 2 screens fail. Follow-on requires a fresh ownership, total-cost, reserve and payout model; do not silently keep denominator 2.</p><p>Keep conditional invest/pass/defer and strongest objections for BOTH candidates; use same source/date/unit/status requirements. Fictional day 0/30/60/90 evidence/economics/portfolio/rights reviewers preserve stop gates. Attempts has blank actual dates, reasoning, errors/corrections, strongest objections, changed view and delayed-retest fields. Future closed-notes prompts vary dilution/commitment and swap candidates; no actual delay, learner completion, field research or IC approval was observed.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice passed 38 exact-rational checks each baseline/A-retention/full-swap/commitment 80/restored,3 capacity-fail checks,34 each zero-cheque,37 zero-retention,2 zero-commitment checks plus undefined-ratio assertions. Eight invalid negative/excess-retention/text/missing/budget inputs and broken exit-ownership/capacity references suppress affected conclusions. Zero cheque leaves deal MOIC/hurdle undefined; zero retention leaves hurdle undefined; zero commitment leaves contribution ratios undefined. Saved 38 cached formulas,zero errors,no external links/macros. All nine print pages inspected, prose readability improved/rechecked and actual-attempt cells verified blank; changed 320/390/1280 course views inspected. This closes authored native practice, not completed paired learner memos, independent delayed transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, full audiovisual/source validation, real diligence/rights or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Keep both startups, fund, roles and outcomes fictional; compare A, B, pass and defer without actual investment or outreach.","Reconcile commitment 100, fee allowance 10, investable 90, reserve 30 and initial capacity 60; a single affordable cheque is not portfolio approval.","Calculate A 10 percent/B 20 percent entry and A 6 percent/B 10 percent diluted exit ownership under declared common-equity assumptions.","Mandatory provenance: same source/date/unit/status/unknown-evidence requirements for both candidates; no favored-company exceptions.","Reconcile base proceeds 9/6 and gross deal 4.5x/3x, not 0.09x/0.06x fund contributions as total fund performance.","Distinguish founder story, investor diligence and real IC authority; retain missing product/customer/cash/rights evidence equally.","Mandatory downside/reversal: failure zero, upside 18/12 proceeds and 5x hurdle exits 166.666667/100; no probabilities, expected return or automatic follow-on.","Write matched invest/pass arguments for EACH candidate and swap labels to test bias, preserving the strongest objection.","Mandatory owner/gate: fictional day 0/30/60/90 customer, economics, cash, rights and financing review with stop conditions, no actual capital release.","Deliver paired memos, scenario/denominator/reserve checks, source register and revised conditional decision; no fund net performance claim."]}},"88":{"objectives":["Audit a private synthetic growth workbook through linked income statement, balance sheet and cash flow checks, formula lineage and consistent nominal/real discounting, not hardcoded outputs or a balanced sheet alone."],"videos":[{"id":"f3T0tCjw1k8","title":"How the 3 Financial Statements Connect Together","by":"Josh Aharonoff (Your CFO Guy)","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the written synthetic exercise and its sources for the exact math, consent and decision gates; a watched indicator is not evidence of a completed workbook, rehearsal or real approval."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ICAEW - 20 principles for good spreadsheet practice (2024)","url":"https://www.icaew.com/technical/technology/excel-community/20-principles-for-good-spreadsheet-practice-2024-edition","note":"Written principles 1-20: input/process/output flow, no embedded constants, checks, testing, independent review, versions and access. Protection is not a guarantee of confidentiality, nor permission to change real account access."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA - inflation and investment appraisal","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f9/technical-articles/inflation-investment-appraisal.html","note":"Nominal/real consistency, general versus specific inflation and Fisher relationship. Historical worked tax assumptions and approximate after-tax discount-rate shortcut are not adopted as universal valuation rules or current Indian tax law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Your CFO Guy - linked-statements worksheet page","url":"https://www.yourcfoguy.com/templates/how-the-3-financial-statements-connect","note":"Publisher page describes a practical worksheet. The written synthetic exercise supplies the explicit statement bridge."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic workbook, not an external submission. Use the D85/86 twelve-month B case, lakh rupees and exclusions. Opening company balance sheet: cash 10 plus PPE 20 = equity 30; no receivables, inventory, liabilities, taxes or financing. Add depreciation 2 in BOTH B plans, excluded from their previously stated operating remainders. No dividends, disposals, impairments, interest or other changes. Baseline income: revenue 30 - variable 18 - fixed 8 - depreciation 2 = net income 2. CFO 2 + depreciation 2 - AR increase 3 = 1; CFI 0, CFF 0. Ending cash 11, receivables 3 and net PPE 18 sum to assets 32 = closing equity 30+2. Growth income: 36-21.6-10-2 = net income 2.4. CFO 2.4+2-10.8 = minus 6.4; CFI minus 2.5, CFF 0, net cash movement minus 8.9. Ending cash 1.1, AR 10.8 and PPE 20+2.5-2 = 20.5 sum to assets 32.4 = closing equity 32.4. Incremental net income 0.4 and cash movement minus 9.9 versus baseline are unchanged by equal depreciation. Do not double-count capex as an operating expense or add depreciation twice to cash.\nCreate separate inputs, calculations, output, source registry, audit and sensitivity tabs. A registry row needs original source/input ID, date, unit, period, owner, assumed/observed/forecast status and limitations. The invented opening ledger is not real company accounting. Derive outputs by references to inputs, not pasted answers. Audit checks: opening/closing assets minus liabilities minus equity = 0; closing minus opening cash minus cash-flow movement = 0; revenue minus collections minus AR increase = 0 under these exclusions; opening PPE plus capex minus depreciation minus closing PPE = 0; opening equity plus net income minus dividends minus closing equity = 0. Check formulas, signs, ranges, units and scenario dependency, not just balances. A balancing plug or stale copied number can force zero without economic correctness.\nControlled tests: if collections rise by 1 with revenue/cost unchanged, AR falls 1 and cash rises 1; net income and total assets stay unchanged. If capex rises 1 paid in B with depreciation fixed by the case, cash falls 1 and PPE rises 1; earnings unchanged. If variable cost rises 1 paid in B, earnings, cash and equity each fall 1. Test negative/non-numeric/impossible inputs, broken references, inserted rows and zero denominators; reject or label invalid inputs instead of treating blanks as evidence of zero. Record observed test outcomes, not an assertion that a learner's file has been independently audited.\nIndependent nominal/real lab, not an extra benefit to add to the SME. Today cost 150 and real cash receipts 100 at each year-end 1 and 2; real annual discount rate 10 percent and general inflation 5 percent. Assume all these cash flows inflate at the same rate, no tax, fees or other effects. Nominal receipts 105 and 110.25; exact nominal discount rate (1.10*1.05)-1 = 15.5 percent, not 15 percent. Real NPV -150+100/1.10+100/1.10^2 = 23.553719. Nominal NPV -150+105/1.155+110.25/1.155^2 is the same before rounding. Discounting nominal receipts at the real rate inflates NPV; discounting real receipts at the nominal rate understates it. Label rate, price-date, cash-flow date, currency, inflation and tax basis. Different specific revenue/cost inflation or taxes needs a new model, not blind general inflation or a universal tax adjustment of the discount rate.\nDeliver the private workbook, audit/source registry, baseline/downside/upside sensitivity and failure log. Preserve cash-floor failure, missing dated liquidity and quality evidence from D85/86. Give fictional day 0/30/60/90 formula/data/permission reviewers and stop gates. External review is only a simulated audience: no actual share, account protection change, credential export or outside data disclosure. Inspect actual sheets and formulas before reporting a learner workbook complete; these expected answers are not a rendered or independently audited workbook.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Native D88 statements lab\"><h3>Link the statements, then try to break them</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d88-statements-practice.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable D88 linked-statements workbook (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d88-statements-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">inspect its nine-sheet print view (PDF)</a>. Rebuild your own model first, then use the authored formulas as a checking aid. Separate Inputs, Calculations, Output, Audit, Sensitivity, Registry, Valuation and Failure log sheets preserve the original case and rubric. The Start sheet explains the scope.</p><p>Four coherent scenarios retain the no-bet counterfactual and growth base/downside/upside. Income, AR, PPE, cash and retained equity are linked, not balanced with a plug. The base growth case gives net income 2.4, CFO minus 6.4, investing cash minus 2.5, closing cash 1.1 and assets/equity 32.4. It fails the endpoint cash floor. Upside ending cash 5.9 does not establish cash-never-below-floor or delivery quality: dated liquidity and quality evidence remain visibly unknown. The independent real/nominal NPV lab gives 23.553719 on both matched bases and is not added to SME benefits.</p><p>Native LibreOffice recalculation matched an independent exact-rational evaluator on 113 baseline statement/scenario/NPV checks. Thirty-two controlled-change checks cover collections +1, capex +1 and variable cost +1. A row inserted in Inputs preserved all 113 checks; removing it and restoring inputs passed another 113. Five invalid-input cases, a broken formula reference, a zero revenue denominator and a minus-100-percent rate were tested: invalid or undefined results are flagged, not silently treated as zero. The Failure log records observed authored-file outcomes; it does not certify your own file.</p><p>All nine PDF sheets and mobile/desktop course links were visually inspected; saved XLSX formula caches were checked. This is authored native execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, independent review of a learner workbook, actual accounting or real company evidence. The registry labels invented inputs and source limitations. No macros, external links, account access, protection changes, outside disclosure or learner-progress changes are created.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Use the same fictional B case and opening cash 10/PPE 20/equity 30; depreciation 2 in both plans, no actual external share.","Derive baseline NI 2/CFO 1/closing assets 32 and growth NI 2.4/CFO minus 6.4/CFI minus 2.5/closing assets 32.4 with explicit exclusions.","Link cash 11 versus 1.1, AR 3 versus 10.8, PPE 18 versus 20.5 and retained earnings, not capex as expense or balanced plugs as proof.","Mandatory provenance: source/input/date/unit/period/owner/status registry; separate input, calculation, output, audit and sensitivity tabs.","Run cash, AR, PPE, equity and balance checks plus formula/range/unit tests; a zero check cannot prove all assumptions true.","Test collection/capex/variable cost changes 1 and invalid inputs with observed results; no pasted outputs or asserted independent audit.","Mandatory downside/reversal: carry base/downside/upside and cash/quality gaps; real 10 percent/inflation 5 percent implies nominal 15.5 percent and matching NPV 23.553719.","Keep independent valuation separate from SME benefits; specific inflation/taxes need a new model, not mismatched flows/rates or universal tax shortcut.","Mandatory owner/gate: fictional day 0/30/60/90 data/formula/permission reviewers; no real access changes, secrets or external disclosure.","Deliver and inspect actual workbook/audit tab/source registry/failure log; reference answers are not an independently audited learner file."]}},"89":{"objectives":["Use a private thirty-minute mock IC challenge and corrected answer bank to test falsifying evidence and measured response quality/time, not friendly rehearsal or an unconsented recording."],"videos":[{"id":"h5qWn0SfTXA","title":"What the Best Pitch Decks Have in Common with Mike Vernal (Sequoia Capital)","by":"NFX","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the written synthetic exercise and its sources for the exact math, consent and decision gates; a watched indicator is not evidence of a completed workbook, rehearsal or real approval."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - preparing a board deck","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/preparing-a-board-deck","note":"Decision-focused calibration, metrics/challenges and working discussion; boards may use memos. Practitioner guide, not Indian legal requirements or approval to share documents."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NFX - how VCs judge a startup (2020)","url":"https://www.nfx.com/post/how-vcs-judge-your-startup-on-zoom","note":"Historical publisher article on clarity, questions, metrics and investor perspectives. Preferences differ. Not a verbatim transcript of the assigned short recording or universal panel timing/permission rules."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GOV.UK - notes and recording user research","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/taking-notes-and-recording-user-research-sessions","note":"Consent, careful recording and useful notes inform this private practice. Not blanket consent to record a real IC, disclose private data or make public uploads."}],"exercise":"Private 30-minute mock IC exercise using either the D85/86 SME case or D87 fund case. All evidence and roles remain fictional; do not recruit real panelists, share private data or claim an actual IC decision. A solo role-play is sufficient. Preserve a recording-review task only for a recording you are allowed to make/use; if any other person participates, obtain their permission for recording, storage and review. Otherwise use timestamped notes. Do not create a public recording or upload someone else's voice by default. No practice duration or response speed has been measured by the course.\nPlan 5 minutes decision/evidence summary, 15 minutes adversarial challenge and 10 minutes correction/review, totaling 30 minutes. Use an actual timer if performed and log actual duration, pauses and missing sections, not the planned 30 as elapsed time. For each challenge record when the question ends, when the first substantive answer begins and when a supported answer finishes; separate latency, answer duration, correctness, evidence quality and admitted unknowns. No universal speed threshold is set and fast confident error is not success.\nAsk at least six material challenges, including two not rehearsed in advance. SME examples: why 24 to 36 is not 12 caused by growth; why 4.4 is not incremental operating; why 0.4 increment becomes minus 9.9 incremental cash; whether end cash 1.1 clears floor 2; why upside cash 5.9 cannot prove peak liquidity or quality; what data would favor defer/redesign. Fund examples: why A 4.5x is not fund 4.5x; why dilution and follow-on cost need new ownership/denominators; why unknown retention must be treated the same for A/B; why no probabilities means no expected-return forecast. Read the corresponding case for units, exclusions and counterfactual before answering, not the bare numbers in isolation.\nInject new counterevidence during the challenge, not merely friendlier wording. If an alleged reference or signed approval is unverified, say so and keep the real authority gate closed. A question is not proof its premise is true. The answer bank records original answer, claim/input/source, strongest objection, correction, conditional decision change and a delayed changed-context retest. Review only permitted recordings/notes and retain no unnecessary identifying data. Assign fictional day 0/30/60/90 owners for evidence, cash, rights and decision review with explicit stop conditions. Deliver permitted recording review OR timestamped notes, corrected bank, actual time records if performed and remaining unknowns; a mock sponsor vote or 8/10 self-check is not real approval or independently measured mastery.<section aria-label=\"Native D89 practice\"><h3>Timed mock and preserved-answer challenge bank</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d89-timed-bank.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">9-sheet editable lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d89-timed-bank.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">9-page print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own before checking the reference. Original synthetic case and rubric remain intact.</p><p>Proposed 5+15+10=30 minutes is not an observed session. Actual section and question elapsed timestamps stay blank; 20 actual formula outputs intentionally remain #N/A until supported inputs exist. Latency differs from answer duration and neither proves correctness. Six authored rehearsable challenges do not count as two actual unrehearsed questions; those slots stay blank. Matching original D85/D86 equal 12-month SME counterfactual gives growth operating 4.4 versus no-bet 4, incremental operating .4, extra AR 7.8, capex 2.5 and incremental cash -9.9. Opening 10 gives growth ending 1.1 below floor 2; full dated minimum and quality remain UNKNOWN. Separate collections-24 injection gives cash -10.1/end -.1/increment -11.1; separate extra paid rework 1 gives cash -9.9/end .1/increment -10.9. They are not silently combined. Native synthetic timing 12/15/35 seconds gives latency 3/answer 20 and was cleared; invalid timestamp/order/plan cases fail closed. Notes suffice, no recording or participant permission is implied; actual corrected answers and delayed review remain blank.</p><p>Authored LibreOffice execution plus independent arithmetic only. Static prose is Case-v1 and must be revised after changed inputs. No actual learner, reviewer, sponsor approval, empirical-source reading, full AV, Excel/Sheets parity, account flow, business commitment or overall 10/10 is established. Private solo classroom work requires no real records, contacts, spending or recording.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Keep 30 minute mock IC private and fictional; solo role-play is valid, not a real committee or investment decision.","Plan 5+15+10 minutes, then measure actual practice and incomplete sections only if performed; do not claim planned time as elapsed.","Use the selected SME or fund case with its units/exclusions and baseline, not bare numbers divorced from the question.","Mandatory provenance: per-answer claim/input/source and actual observed timings; unknown evidence/premises remain unverified.","Record question end, substantive answer start and supported answer finish; separate latency/duration, accuracy and evidence quality.","Ask six material questions including two unrehearsed; missing evidence and fastest confident errors are not success.","Mandatory downside/reversal: inject changed cash/collection/retention/ownership evidence, test the conditional recommendation and preserve strongest objection.","Review only consented permitted recordings or timestamped notes; record original answer, correction and delayed changed-context retest.","Mandatory owner/gate: fictional day 0/30/60/90 evidence/cash/rights/decision reviews; mock votes do not authorize spending or disclosure.","Deliver permitted recording review OR timed notes/corrected answer bank and actual time when performed; no invented mastery or speed score."]}},"90":{"objectives":["Defend and revise a private SME capstone under new collection and rework evidence, keeping decision history, dated liquidity gaps, ninety-day owners and actual authorization separate from deck polish."],"videos":[{"id":"h5qWn0SfTXA","title":"What the Best Pitch Decks Have in Common with Mike Vernal (Sequoia Capital)","by":"NFX","note":"The self-check unlocks at 90% watched. Use the written synthetic exercise and its sources for the exact math, consent and decision gates; a watched indicator is not evidence of a completed workbook, rehearsal or real approval."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Sequoia - preparing a board deck","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/preparing-a-board-deck","note":"Decision-focused calibration, metrics/challenges and working discussion; boards may use memos. Practitioner guide, not Indian legal requirements or approval to share documents."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT15.431 - explicit cash/working-capital reasoning (2011)","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/4a2e49e2bb9d4dbfe0bb7b89cccb1016_MIT15_431S11_lec01.pdf","note":"Use a declared accounting/timing basis and distinguish modeled cash from earnings. Historical taxes/rates not adopted as current Indian rules."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ICAEW - 20 principles for good spreadsheet practice (2024)","url":"https://www.icaew.com/technical/technology/excel-community/20-principles-for-good-spreadsheet-practice-2024-edition","note":"Written principles 1-20: input/process/output flow, no embedded constants, checks, testing, independent review, versions and access. Protection is not a guarantee of confidentiality, nor permission to change real account access."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NFX - how VCs judge a startup (2020)","url":"https://www.nfx.com/post/how-vcs-judge-your-startup-on-zoom","note":"Historical publisher article on clarity, questions, metrics and investor perspectives. Preferences differ. Not a verbatim transcript of the assigned short recording or universal panel timing/permission rules."}],"exercise":"Private capstone continuation of the D85/86 twelve-month B SME case, not a duplicate generic slide task. Reuse that case's baseline, units, exclusions, opening cash 10, floor 2 and quality unknown. Hand over a deck, a timed defence and a 90-day action register with old/new decision trail. The 90-day review schedule is distinct from the twelve-month model and the product's 90 percent-watched unlock. Do not imply these different periods are the same.\nNew fictional evidence packet: a claimed additional 4 of the growth collections 25.2 will arrive in B+1, not B. Confirm for the simulation that the revenue remains recognized in B and no write-off is assumed; collections become 21.2, receivables 14.8, growth operating remainder stays 4.4, cash movement becomes minus 12.9 and ending company cash minus 2.9. Versus unchanged no-bet B operating 4/cash movement 1, incremental operating stays 0.4 but incremental cash falls to minus 13.9. Next, a separate 1.2 rework cost, outside original variable 21.6/fixed 10, must be paid in B. Growth operating becomes 3.2, incremental operating minus 0.8, cash movement minus 14.1/end company cash minus 4.1 and incremental cash minus 15.1. Do not count the delayed 4 twice as both a revenue loss and a collection change, or call receivables 14.8 negative cash. Do not use B+1 collections as funding inside B without dated supported financing and actual permission. Negative modeled cash flags infeasibility under no financing, not an automatically approved loan.\nCompare original growth, delayed collection and delayed-plus-rework cases side by side against the same no-bet counterfactual. No dates for these cash events are supplied, so exact peak deficit and when each bill fails are unresolved. Endpoint failure is sufficient to withhold an unconditional pass. Quality remains unknown, not cured by a cosmetic revised deck or an operating target. The baseline alternative also needs real diligence before any real decision. Redesign/stage needs its own evidence and cost/timing model; a fictional sponsor's enthusiasm cannot authorize funds or data disclosure.\nPrivately rehearse with a chosen timer limit and record actual elapsed time only if performed, then leave a separate challenge round. Defend why economics changed, what stayed unchanged, what evidence reverses the recommendation and why source confidence/rights/authority gates remain. Maintain a claim/evidence/change register, strongest objection, and owner/evidence/dependency/day 0/30/60/90 review/stop criterion for each demand, cash, cost, quality, rights and decision gap. Render and inspect the actual learner deck for numbers, readability and source notes before calling it complete. No real board resolution, external distribution, spending, hiring or financing. Hand over the corrected deck, permitted notes, formulas and remaining unknowns, not a manufactured completion score.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Editable D90 decision lab\"><h3>Show how new evidence changes the decision</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d90-decision-practice.pptx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the editable ten-slide D90 worked example (PPTX)</a>, <a href=\"/practice/top1-d90-decision-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">inspect the ten-slide PDF</a>, or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d90-scenario-checks.csv\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">check the four-case calculation table (CSV)</a>. Build your own deck before reading the example. Original case, exercise and rubric remain in force; the example is a checking aid, not an actual learner defence.</p><p>The twelve-month B case keeps revenue at 36 lakh when collection of 4 moves to B+1. Accounts receivable rises from 10.8 to 14.8, and ending cash falls from 1.1 to -2.9. Separate rework of 1.2 paid in B then takes operating result before depreciation from 4.4 to 3.2 and ending cash to -4.1. Incremental operating result versus no bet is -0.8; incremental cash is -15.1. Do not double-count delayed cash as lost revenue or silently treat negative modeled cash as approved borrowing.</p><p>The editable deck includes four-case comparison, separate accounting paths, linked-statement checks, a native endpoint-cash chart, evidence gates, a fictional 90-day owner/dependency register, the old/new decision trail, the strongest objection and solo-practice instructions. Endpoint floor gaps are not peak-funding estimates: cash-event dates and quality evidence remain unknown. Day 0/30/60/90 are simulated review checkpoints, not bookings or assigned people.</p><p>Forty-three independent rational-value, identity and delay/rework checks passed. All ten saved slides were rendered and visually inspected; native table/chart values, shape bounds and speaker notes checked. Full Sequoia board-deck guidance and ICAEW 2024 spreadsheet principles were read. This is authored numerical/visual QA only: no elapsed-time result, spoken delivery, actual learner or panel defence, recording review, PowerPoint/Slides parity, real approvals or overall 10/10 certification is claimed.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"Self-check: at least 8 of 10. Checks 4 (provenance), 7 (downside/reversal) and 9 (owner/gate) are required. Log a material objection. This is not independent verification or sponsor approval.","checks":["Use D 85/86 same 12-month B baseline and distinct 90-day review cadence; private capstone, not real board approval.","Retain original growth 4.4 operating/10.8 AR/minus 8.9 cash movement/end 1.1 and baseline 4 operating/1 cash movement.","Delay 4 collections: operating 4.4/AR 14.8/cash movement minus 12.9/end minus 2.9/incremental cash minus 13.9; no revenue write-off assumed.","Mandatory provenance: original/new inputs, date/unit/window/source/status and change trail; no invented confirmation from a real debtor.","Add 1.2 rework once: operating 3.2/increment minus 0.8/cash movement minus 14.1/end minus 4.1/incremental cash minus 15.1.","Keep quality unknown, exact peak timing unknown and endpoint failure visible; no automatic loan or B+1 collections as B cash.","Mandatory downside/reversal: compare original/delayed/delayed-plus-rework with unchanged baseline and explain redesign/defer evidence, not deck polish.","Rehearse only privately with actual measured time if performed; retain strongest objection and conditional recommendation change.","Mandatory owner/gate: fictional day 0/30/60/90 demand/cash/cost/quality/rights/decision dependencies and stop criteria; no capital release.","Deliver corrected deck, formula bridges, permitted notes and action/change register; actual render inspection before learner completion, no real distribution."]}},"91":{"objectives":["Use a fresh mixed decision case and actual attempted errors to choose two provisional remediation areas; test changed-context transfer rather than repeated familiar questions or an invented measured weakest-skill profile."],"videos":[{"id":"elMPIxLYtoc","title":"Consultant Explains the Pyramid Principle","by":"Analyst Academy","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Solve the written synthetic case and verify the stated period, units and exclusions; a watched indicator or day score is not question-level diagnosis or independent mastery."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - retrieval and interleaving","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Full written summary supports closed-notes recall and mixed strategy practice, not an app-measured weakest-skill profile or measured learning gain."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Bain - case interview preparation","url":"https://www.bain.com/careers/hiring-process/case-interview/","note":"Clarify, structure, explain calculations and adapt to evidence. Full written overview, not linked sample-case contents, real board authority or one universal case answer."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax16.3 - indirect cash-flow reconstruction","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"Use the NI/noncash/operating-working-capital bridge and cash reconciliation. Use the fictional case figures rather than mixing inconsistent worked examples. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."}],"exercise":"Private review-day transfer. All companies, roles, records and decisions below are fictional. Solve without notes, then check calculations and record attempted answer, error cause, corrected reasoning, source/input, decision consequence and a delayed changed-context retest. Pick two remediation areas from actual mistakes; if evidence is sparse, call them provisional. The app's day score and completion timestamp do not identify question-level weaknesses or independently measured mastery. Do not infer skill improvement from confidence or a watched indicator.\nFresh 90-day SME case, lakh rupees. No opening receivables, inventory, payables, depreciation, taxes, financing or other flows. All stated operating costs paid within the window. No-bet revenue 50-variable 30-fixed 14=operating 6, collections 45 give AR 5 and cash movement 1. Growth totals revenue 60-variable 36-fixed 17=operating 7, collections 48 give AR 12, capex 4 and cash movement minus 9. Incremental operating 1, AR increase 7 and capex 4 yield incremental cash minus 10, not 7 new cash or minus 9 increment. Opening company cash 12, proposed floor 4: baseline ends 13/growth ends 3. Endpoint failure is enough to withhold an unconditional cash pass; exact peak is unknown without dated flows, and quality evidence is absent. Operating remainder is before interest/tax/depreciation, not net income.\nContradictory fictional packet:3 of growth collections 48 arrive after the 90-day window; revenue remains recognized and no write-off is assumed. A new 2 rework expense is paid within the window, outside original costs. Revised growth operating 5, AR 15, cash movement minus 14, company end minus 2; versus unchanged baseline incremental operating minus 1/AR 10/capex 4/incremental cash minus 15. Do not turn late collections into an immediate revenue reversal or automatically authorized borrowing. Defer/redesign needs its own evidence and budget, not assumed permission.\nIndependent common-equity case, crore rupees: cheque 2/pre 18=>post 20/10 percent entry; retained fraction 0.50=>5 percent exit ownership; equity exit 120=>cash 6/2=3x gross deal MOIC. No interim distributions/preferences/follow-on by this investor. Single deal MOIC is not fund net return, IRR or verified exit value. A new follow-on or payout terms needs a new cost/ownership/waterfall model. Independent uniform SaaS contract check:12 lakh upfront for 12 equal months, after 3 months earned 3/contract liability 9 before costs, not 12 earned on receipt. Do not add these separate cases into the SME cash bridge.\nDeliver corrected workbook, error/change/source logs, strongest counterevidence, two remediation areas and delayed transfer prompts. Assign fictional evidence/cash/rights/decision review owners and day 0/30/60/90 stop gates; no real board vote, external approval, refunds, contacts, data disclosure, spending or financing. A mock sponsor's acceptance is not real execution authority.","quiz":[{"q":"In the new 90-day case, which pair is growth incremental operating and incremental cash movement versus the same-period no-bet counterfactual?","o":["7 and minus 9 lakh","1 and minus 9 lakh","1 and minus 10 lakh","7 and minus 10 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"Revenue rises from an earlier observed-in-fiction 50 to a new forecast 60. The same-period no-bet forecast is 55. Which conclusion follows from these figures alone?","o":["Modeled increment 5; causal effect unproven","Modeled increment 10; causal effect unproven","Modeled increment 5; causal effect proven","Modeled increment 10; causal effect proven"],"a":0},{"q":"The contradictory packet delays 3 collections and adds 2 paid rework, leaving recognized revenue unchanged. What are revised growth operating and AR?","o":["7 and 15 lakh","5 and 9 lakh","2 and 12 lakh","5 and 15 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"After those changes, opening company cash 12 and revised cash movement minus 14 imply which company endpoint and cash increment?","o":["Minus 2 and minus 14 lakh","Minus 2 and minus 15 lakh","Plus 3 and minus 15 lakh","Minus 14 and minus 15 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"The base growth case ends company cash 3 with floor 4, but no dated events exist. Which statement is supported?","o":["Endpoint passes; exact peak unknown","Endpoint fails; exact peak is 9","Endpoint fails; exact peak unknown","Endpoint passes; exact peak is 4"],"a":2},{"q":"In the separate common deal, cheque 2/pre 18, retained fraction 0.50 and equity exit 120 give which gross deal MOIC?","o":["6x","5x","2x","3x"],"a":3},{"q":"A separate uniform 12-month SaaS contract receives 12 upfront. After 3 completed months, what are earned revenue and unearned liability before costs?","o":["3 and 9 lakh","12 and 0 lakh","9 and 3 lakh","3 and 12 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"A learner has a day-level score and timestamp, but no per-item answers. What is justified for choosing remediation?","o":["Infer item weaknesses from day score alone","Use actual new errors; choices provisional","Treat watched time as item error records","Treat all unattempted items as wrong"],"a":1},{"q":"A favorite candidate has unknown retention; the other has unknown retention. Which comparison uses the same evidence standard?","o":["Use zero for other and target for favorite","Use favorite funding as retention proof","Use other lower price as retention proof","Mark both unknown and test both needs"],"a":3},{"q":"After correcting a familiar cash bridge error, which next test best checks transfer rather than recall?","o":["Delayed new case with changed timing","Immediate identical case with same values","Immediate answer-key reread and rating","Delayed identical question with key visible"],"a":0}]},"92":{"objectives":["Post and reconcile a fictional one-crore credit sale, paid inventory cost, later collection and explicit allowance change across accrual profit, receivables, company cash and the balance sheet."],"videos":[{"id":"C8UuX75ZarU","title":"Accrual Accounting: How it Works & Why it's #1!","by":"Accounting Stuff","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Solve the written synthetic case and verify the stated period, units and exclusions; a watched indicator or day score is not question-level diagnosis or independent mastery."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax4.2 - accruals and deferrals","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/4-2-discuss-the-adjustment-process-and-illustrate-common-types-of-adjusting-entries","note":"Read written explanations of earned versus collected, prepayments, accruals and balance effects. US teaching, not current Indian standards/tax advice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax4.3 - journal and ledger adjustments","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/4-3-record-and-post-the-common-types-of-adjusting-entries","note":"Read worked prose and posted-entry explanations. Cash correcting entries are distinct from the chapter adjusting-entry convention."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax16.3 - indirect cash-flow reconstruction","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"Use the NI/noncash/operating-working-capital bridge and cash reconciliation. Use the fictional case figures rather than mixing inconsistent worked examples. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."}],"exercise":"Private fictional distributor, not actual accounting, tax advice or an instruction to alter a real ledger. All amounts lakh rupees; 1 crore=100 lakh. Opening cash 80/equity 80, no other assets/liabilities. Within days 0-60 buy inventory 65 for cash and deliver all goods for a 100 credit sale due day 90. Assume the contract and delivery satisfy this simplified exercise's revenue-recognition conditions; no returns, tax, discount, finance component, impairment, other costs or financing in the base case. Invoice creation alone is not universally enough for recognition. No customer cash by day 60 does not mean default: payment is not yet due under these terms.\nEntries: purchase Dr Inventory 65/Cr Cash 65; delivery Dr AR 100/Cr Revenue 100 and Dr COGS 65/Cr Inventory 65. Day 60 income 100-65=35, CFO minus 65 from supplier payment, closing cash 15/AR 100/inventory 0/assets 115/equity 115. Indirect CFO 35-minus AR increase 100=minus 65; beginning cash 80 minus 65=15. Neither profit 35 nor receivable 100 is cash available. Confirm cost was actually paid within the measurement window; opening inventory paid in a prior period would change this period's cash bridge.\nWhen the full 100 is collected on day 90, Dr Cash 100/Cr AR 100, no second revenue. Cumulative cash 115/AR 0/equity 115, cumulative CFO 35; collection-only subperiod CFO 100/profit 0. A day 90 due date is a contractual input, not proof payment actually arrives. Use delivery/contract/invoice/bank records and receivables aging/collection evidence in a real review; an aging total or DSO is an indicator, not proof of credit quality or consent for collection outreach.\nIndependent downside at day 60 before collection: management stipulates a supported 10 allowance loss for the exercise, no write-off yet. This is not a measured 10 percent default rate or a prescription of any jurisdiction's expected credit loss method. Dr Loss Expense 10/Cr Allowance Against AR 10. Gross AR 100/net AR 90, profit 25, cash 15, assets 105/equity 105. CFO 25+noncash allowance loss 10-minus gross AR increase 100=minus 65, equivalently 25-minus net AR increase 90=minus 65 under these exclusions; do not both add 10 and subtract net 90. Actual applicable standards, contract/collectibility, impairment and tax effects need separate current review. Treat this allowance case independently from the full-collection base; any later estimate change needs its own reconciliation.\nDeliver dated journal/ledger, income/balance/cash flow bridge, base versus allowance comparison, source/input/assumption register and strongest objection. Retain prior/revised versions, fictional review owners and day 0/30/60/90 evidence/cash/rights stop gates. No actual ledger edit, collection message, credit agreement, borrowing, data disclosure or spending.","quiz":[{"q":"The delivered 100 credit-sale meets the stated base recognition conditions, before collection. Which revenue entry fits?","o":["Dr Cash 100; Cr Revenue 100","Dr AR 100; Cr Revenue 100","Dr Revenue 100; Cr AR 100","Dr AR 100; Cr Cash 100"],"a":1},{"q":"Inventory 65 was bought for cash within the window and all goods are delivered. Which inventory/cost entry is needed at delivery?","o":["Dr Inventory 65; Cr COGS 65","Dr Cash 65; Cr Inventory 65","Dr COGS 65; Cr Cash 65","Dr COGS 65; Cr Inventory 65"],"a":3},{"q":"By day 60 the 100 sale is uncollected and goods 65 were paid for inside the period. Base profit and CFO are what?","o":["35 and minus 65 lakh","35 and plus 35 lakh","100 and minus 65 lakh","Minus 65 and zero"],"a":0},{"q":"With opening cash 80/equity 80, what does the day 60 base balance sheet contain?","o":["Cash 115/AR 0/equity 115","Cash 15/AR 100/equity 35","Cash 15/AR 100/equity 115","Cash 80/AR 35/equity 115"],"a":2},{"q":"On day 90 the base 100 is collected in full. What is the collection entry?","o":["Dr Cash 100; Cr Revenue 100","Dr AR 100; Cr Cash 100","Dr Revenue 100; Cr Cash 100","Dr Cash 100; Cr AR 100"],"a":3},{"q":"Considering only the collection subperiod, with no other transactions, which profit/CFO pair is correct?","o":["100 and 100 lakh","0 and 100 lakh","35 and 35 lakh","0 and 35 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"At day 60 the payment is contractually due on day 90 and no other credit facts are provided. What can be concluded from nonpayment alone?","o":["Thirty days past due; default proven","Sixty days past due; default proven","Not yet due; default unproven","Not yet due; collection guaranteed"],"a":2},{"q":"Independent allowance case: gross AR 100 and supported allowance 10, no write-off. Net AR and profit after the loss are what?","o":["90 and 25 lakh","100 and 25 lakh","90 and 35 lakh","10 and 25 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Which indirect CFO calculation avoids double-counting the independent allowance loss?","o":["25+10-90=minus 55","25-100=minus 75","25+10-100=minus 65","35+10-90=minus 45"],"a":2},{"q":"An earlier inventory 65 purchase was paid before this measurement window, unlike the base case. No new purchase or collection occurs now. What are current profit/CFO after delivery?","o":["35 and minus 65 lakh","35 and 0 lakh","0 and minus 65 lakh","100 and 0 lakh"],"a":1}]},"93":{"objectives":["Build a twelve-month uniform-service contract-liability roll-forward that separates upfront cash, earned revenue, delivery costs and an explicit cancellation/refund scenario without treating receipt as month-one revenue."],"videos":[{"id":"37PPccwsYJI","title":"Revenue Recognition Principle in TWO MINUTES!","by":"Accounting Stuff","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Solve the written synthetic case and verify the stated period, units and exclusions; a watched indicator or day score is not question-level diagnosis or independent mastery."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax4.2 - accruals and deferrals","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/4-2-discuss-the-adjustment-process-and-illustrate-common-types-of-adjusting-entries","note":"Read written explanations of earned versus collected, prepayments, accruals and balance effects. US teaching, not current Indian standards/tax advice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax4.3 - journal and ledger adjustments","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/4-3-record-and-post-the-common-types-of-adjusting-entries","note":"Read worked prose and posted-entry explanations. Cash correcting entries are distinct from the chapter adjusting-entry convention."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax16.3 - indirect cash-flow reconstruction","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"Use the NI/noncash/operating-working-capital bridge and cash reconciliation. Use the fictional case figures rather than mixing inconsistent worked examples. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Indian SaaS exercise, all amounts lakh rupees. Opening company cash/equity 0, no other balances. At day 0 collect 12 for 12 months of uniform stand-ready service starting immediately. Assume equal monthly service value, enforceable contract, no separate setup/licence or other performance obligation, variable consideration, discounts, financing component, tax, receivable, refund right or impairment in the base case. This is simplified teaching, not a conclusion about an actual firm's Ind AS/AS/GST applicability or all software contracts. Recognition follows the supplied service pattern, not the fact of cash receipt, an invoice or an arbitrary division when obligations differ.\nReceipt Dr Cash 12/Cr Contract Liability 12. For each completed month of equal service, Dr Contract Liability 1/Cr Revenue 1. Each month also incur and pay 0.4 service cost, Dr Expense 0.4/Cr Cash 0.4. End month m from 1 to 12: cumulative earned revenue m, contract liability 12-m, cumulative cost 0.4 m/net income 0.6 m, company cash 12-0.4 m/equity 0.6 m. With no other flows, cumulative CFO 12-0.4 m; net income plus increase in contract liability gives the same cash. End month 3: revenue 3/liability 9/cost 1.2/profit 1.8/cash 10.8/equity 1.8. Month 4 alone: revenue 1/cost 0.4/profit 0.6/CFO minus 0.4, because the 12 was collected in an earlier period. End month 12: revenue 12/liability 0/cost 4.8/profit 7.2/cash 7.2. Liability 12 at receipt is not debt borrowed, cash 12 at receipt is not 12 profit and month 3 cash 10.8 is not 3 revenue. No unpaid operating cost is assumed.\nIndependent changed-contract scenario: at the end of month 3 the service is terminated by a valid fictional amendment; refund exactly 9 for the unperformed 9 months, no penalty/other refunds/remaining service costs. This is a stipulated new agreement, not an assumed universal cancellation right. Dr Contract Liability 9/Cr Cash 9 leaves cash 1.8/liability 0/equity 1.8; earned revenue 3/profit 1.8 stay unchanged. No fourth month service/revenue/cost after termination. A refund is not automatically a reversal of the 3 already earned in this defined case; a refund of already earned amounts would need a new model. Actual refund needs owner authority and current contract/payment rules, never inferred from a classroom liability.\nBuild opening/additions/release/refund/closing rows for all 12 months and a separate cancellation branch; reconcile assets=cash to liabilities+equity. Distinguish monthly from cumulative and base from changed contract; no recognizing 12 upfront or another 12 on month 12. Log source/input/date/period/assumption, missing contract facts, strongest objection, fictional service/finance/rights review owners and day 0/30/60/90 stop gates. Deliver roll-forward, journal entries, three-statement bridge and sensitivity/change log; no actual refund, ledger edit, contract change, customer contact or external disclosure.","quiz":[{"q":"At day 0 the 12 lakh advance arrives before any service. Which entry fits the stated uniform-service case?","o":["Dr Cash 12; Cr Revenue 12","Dr AR 12; Cr Revenue 12","Dr Contract Liability 12; Cr Cash 12","Dr Cash 12; Cr Contract Liability 12"],"a":3},{"q":"After one full month of equal service, which recognition entry is correct?","o":["Dr Contract Liability 12; Cr Revenue 12","Dr Contract Liability 1; Cr Revenue 1","Dr Cash 1; Cr Revenue 1","Dr Revenue 1; Cr Contract Liability 1"],"a":1},{"q":"At end month 3, before any cancellation, cumulative earned revenue and contract liability are what?","o":["12 and 0 lakh","9 and 3 lakh","3 and 9 lakh","3 and 12 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"Monthly service cost 0.4 is paid each month. At end month 3, cumulative profit and company cash are what?","o":["1.8 and 10.8 lakh","3 and 12 lakh","1.8 and 1.8 lakh","10.8 and 1.8 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"For month 4 alone in the uninterrupted base case, which revenue/profit/CFO bundle applies?","o":["1/0.6/plus 0.6 lakh","12/11.6/plus 11.6 lakh","1/0.6/minus 0.4 lakh","0/minus 0.4/minus 0.4 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"At end month 12 with all base service/cost performed, which liability/cash/profit bundle applies?","o":["0/7.2/7.2 lakh","0/12/12 lakh","12/7.2/7.2 lakh","0/7.2/12 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Independent valid amendment cancels after month 3 and refunds 9 for unperformed service. Which entry fits?","o":["Dr Revenue 9; Cr Cash 9","Dr Expense 9; Cr Cash 9","Dr Cash 9; Cr Contract Liability 9","Dr Contract Liability 9; Cr Cash 9"],"a":3},{"q":"After that refund and no further obligations, what are company cash/liability/equity?","o":["10.8/9/1.8 lakh","1.8/0/1.8 lakh","1.8/9/minus 7.2 lakh","0/0/0 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"The base contract instead contains a separate upfront licence and unequal service obligations. What follows about the 1 per month rule?","o":["Reassess obligations and allocation first","Keep 1 per month because cash was 12","Recognize 12 because a licence exists","Recognize zero except month 12"],"a":0},{"q":"A current month releases 1 from last period's customer advance, with 0.4 paid cost. Which indirect CFO bridge matches?","o":["Profit 0.6 plus liability release 1","Revenue 1 minus liability release 0.4","Profit 0.6 minus cash receipt 12","Profit 0.6 minus liability release 1"],"a":3}]},"94":{"objectives":["Reconstruct retailer operating cash flow through receivables, inventory purchases and supplier payables, then distinguish EBITDA, capex, company cash, inventory-day conventions and coherent funding downside."],"videos":[{"id":"5lgjnd9d8AY","title":"Cash Flow Statement Explained for Beginners!","by":"Accounting Basics","note":"The quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Solve the written synthetic case and verify the stated period, units and exclusions; a watched indicator or day score is not question-level diagnosis or independent mastery."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax4.2 - accruals and deferrals","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/4-2-discuss-the-adjustment-process-and-illustrate-common-types-of-adjusting-entries","note":"Read written explanations of earned versus collected, prepayments, accruals and balance effects. US teaching, not current Indian standards/tax advice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax4.3 - journal and ledger adjustments","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/4-3-record-and-post-the-common-types-of-adjusting-entries","note":"Read worked prose and posted-entry explanations. Cash correcting entries are distinct from the chapter adjusting-entry convention."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax16.3 - indirect cash-flow reconstruction","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"Use the NI/noncash/operating-working-capital bridge and cash reconciliation. Use the fictional case figures rather than mixing inconsistent worked examples. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."}],"exercise":"Private fictional annual retailer model, lakh rupees. Opening cash 40/AR 20/inventory 30/net PPE 50 gives assets 140; operating trade AP 25 and equity 115. No other accounts. Annual sales 200, COGS 120, cash operating expense 50 and depreciation 10 give EBITDA 30/net income 20. No interest/tax, bad debts/write-offs, disposals, financing, dividends, acquisitions, noncash inventory changes or other flows. Closing AR 35/inventory 55/trade AP 40; cash capex 20 and net PPE 50+20-10=60. These are operating noncash balances, not all current accounts blindly. Growth of sales versus a previous year is not quantified because prior-year sales are absent.\nIndirect CFO 20+10-(35-20)-(55-30)+(40-25)=5, not EBITDA 30. Direct check: customer collections 200+20-35=185; inventory purchases 120+55-30=145; suppliers paid 145+25-40=130; overhead paid 50; CFO 185-130-50=5. CFI minus 20/CFF 0, net cash movement minus 15 and company end cash 25. Closing assets 25+35+55+60=175=trade AP 40+equity 135; retained earnings increase 20. Capex is not another COGS deduction, depreciation is not current cash payment and rising payables postpone cash outflow rather than erase a supplier obligation.\nDefined 365-day convention: average inventory(30+55)/2=42.5, inventory days 42.5/120*365=129.270833. A matched planning comparator with average inventory 30/same COGS 120/same 365 days gives 91.25 days. Increase 38.020833 days is against that invented comparator, not observed prior-year performance. Ending inventory 55 alone gives 167.291667 days, a different convention; do not silently compare it to average-based days. A single average/turnover ratio hides seasonal peaks, obsolescence and product mix. Actual reorder/cash decision needs dated stock/collection/payment records and quality/service evidence.\nCoherent downside: same annual sales/costs/depreciation/capex/opening balances, but closing AR 45/inventory 65/AP 30. CFO 20+10-25-35+5=minus 25, net cash movement minus 45, company end cash minus 5. Collections 175/purchases 155/supplier payments 150/overhead 50 also yield minus 25. Negative company cash is an unfunded modeled gap, not proof of an approved loan or literal observed negative cash. Exact minimum/peak funding is unknown without dated events. Base ending cash 25 does not prove all bills were payable intraperiod. A proposed floor 10 passes at base endpoint/fails downside endpoint, not an automatic peak pass.\nIndependent supplier payment sensitivity on base: close AP 55 rather than 40 with no price/cost/quantity change and confirmed hypothetical timing only; CFO increases 15 to 20, cash end 40 and liabilities increase 15. This is payment timing, not 15 extra operating profit or free permanent funding. Actual terms, supplier relationship/service, penalties and owner authority need review; do not defer payments unilaterally. Deliver formula/direct-indirect/statement bridges, inventory day definitions, source/assumption/change registry, coherent downside and strongest objection. Assign fictional day 0/30/60/90 evidence/stock/collections/supplier/rights/cash owners and stop gates. No real ledger changes, orders, supplier messages, payment deferrals, borrowing or data shares.","quiz":[{"q":"For the annual retailer base, which CFO follows from NI 20, depreciation 10, AR increase 15, inventory increase 25 and AP increase 15?","o":["5 lakh","35 lakh","30 lakh","Minus 5 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Sales 200, opening AR 20 and closing AR 35, with no write-offs or other AR changes. What are customer collections?","o":["215 lakh","165 lakh","185 lakh","200 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"COGS 120, opening inventory 30, closing inventory 55 and no other inventory changes. What are inventory purchases?","o":["95 lakh","145 lakh","175 lakh","120 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"Purchases 145, opening trade AP 25 and closing trade AP 40. What are supplier cash payments?","o":["160 lakh","105 lakh","145 lakh","130 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Base CFO 5, cash capex 20, CFF 0 and opening cash 40 imply which net cash movement/end cash?","o":["Plus 5 and 45 lakh","Minus 15 and 25 lakh","Minus 25 and 15 lakh","Minus 15 and minus 15 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"With closing cash 25/AR 35/inventory 55/PPE 60/AP 40, what is closing equity under the base exclusions?","o":["175 lakh","115 lakh","135 lakh","155 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"Using average inventory(30+55)/2, annual COGS 120 and 365 days, which inventory days is correct?","o":["129.270833 days","167.291667 days","91.25 days","77.5625 days"],"a":0},{"q":"Why can EBITDA 30 coexist with base CFO 5 without an accounting error?","o":["Capex 25 reduces operating CFO alone","Depreciation 25 reduces cash alone","Supplier AP increase 25 consumes cash","Working-capital net use 25 reduces cash"],"a":3},{"q":"Downside closes AR 45/inventory 65/AP 30, other base inputs unchanged. Which CFO/end company cash pair results after 20 capex?","o":["Minus 25 and 15 lakh","Minus 25 and minus 5 lakh","Plus 5 and 25 lakh","Minus 45 and minus 25 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"Independent base sensitivity raises closing AP 40 to 55 with no other changes and stipulated timing. What changes?","o":["CFO plus 15; profit plus 15","CFO minus 15; profit unchanged","CFO plus 15; profit unchanged","CFO unchanged; profit plus 15"],"a":2}]},"95":{"objectives":["Reconcile lease-accounting EBITDA, recurring share compensation, lease cash payments and consistently defined enterprise-value multiples in a fictional store chain without double-adding rent or calling a non-GAAP measure cash."],"videos":[{"id":"VRZ17M8dLEk","title":"IFRS 16 Lease Accounting Explained: Journal Entries + Example","by":"Farhat Lectures. The # 1 CPA & Accounting Courses","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Its blanket all-leases/signing language and rough 75%/90% criteria are not adopted as current IFRS/Indian rules. The written exercise supplies explicit limited capitalization assumptions; read the IFRS investor caveats and do not infer exemptions or cash-flow classifications from this video."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"IFRS Foundation - lessee statements and non-GAAP measures (2019)","url":"https://www.ifrs.org/content/dam/ifrs/resources-for/investors/the-essentials/the-essentials-feb-2019.pdf","note":"Staff education, not part of IFRS Standards or Indian applicability advice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"SEC - non-GAAP measures, selected interpretations","url":"https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/staff-guidance/corporation-finance-interpretations/non-gaap-financial-measures","note":"US staff guidance, not Indian law or a conclusion all SBC exclusions are prohibited."}],"exercise":"Private fictional annual store-chain case, all monetary amounts crore rupees. Not a real valuation, accounting compliance opinion or permission to change accounts, leases, compensation or investor disclosures. Assume the stated post-capitalized-lease presentation solely for this exercise; do not infer current Indian Ind AS/AS applicability, exemptions, tax or legal rules from IFRS/US teaching.\nRevenue 100 less nonlease cash operating costs 67 and recurring equity-settled share-compensation expense 3 gives stated post-lease EBITDA 30. This EBITDA already excludes lease depreciation and interest; no separate rent expense is in the 67 or 3. Other depreciation 4, right-of-use depreciation 6, lease interest 2, bank interest 1 and tax 0 give net income 17. Reconcile 17+4+6+2+1=30. The share charge is included in earnings and EBITDA; noncash settlement does not establish no economic cost or no future dilution. A promoter adds share compensation 3 to reach explicitly adjusted EBITDA 33, then adds the total lease cash payment 8 again and advertises 41. The extra rent addback duplicates an amount not deducted from EBITDA in this stated model. Trace definitions rather than subtract rent twice or assume all reported adjustments are valid.\nThe annual lease payment 8 consists of principal 6 and interest 2, not 8 principal plus 2 interest. Opening lease liability 36 accrues interest 2 and pays cash 8, ending 30; no new lease, modification, termination, FX or other change. Right-of-use depreciation 6 is noncash and is not another current lease payment. No actual lease present-value/rate or schedule is claimed; the supplied liability roll-forward does not establish a complete IFRS lease calculation. New leases can create noncash asset/liability additions, so capex and debt bridges need their own definitions.\nFor this course's analyst comparison, define after-rent, share-cost-retained operating measure = EBITDA 30 minus total lease cash 8 = 22. This is a labeled analytical convention, not GAAP EBITDA, a universal normalized profit or cash flow. With the same inputs, pre-lease cash operating surplus is 33 because the share charge 3 is noncash; deduct lease 8 to get 25. Then deduct bank interest 1, operating working-capital increase 4 and paid cash capex 5, giving total company cash movement 15. No taxes, other flows, distributions or financing; beginning unrestricted cash 10 ends 25. This is total cash movement under the exclusions, not reported CFO or free cash flow without a declared definition. Both lease principal and interest are counted once. Company cash and debt figures are synthetic, not actual bank evidence.\nAt the same year-end cash endpoint, equity value 100, bank debt 40, closing lease liability 30 and unrestricted cash 25. Excluding leases from the numerator gives enterprise value 115; including lease debt gives 145. Proposed comparison conventions: lease-inclusive EV 145/post-lease EBITDA 30 = 4.833333 x; lease-exclusive EV 115/after-rent measure 22 = 5.227273 x. Label both definitions and compare peers on the same basis; these ratios need not be equal. Do not combine lease-excluding EV with pre-rent inflated EBITDA and present 115/41 = 2.804878 x as an automatically comparable bargain. The closing liability is a discounted stock, not the same as annual lease payment or every future renewal obligation. Include cash restrictions, maturity, exemptions, new leases and SBC award/dilution terms as unresolved real-world checks.\nIndependent next-year sensitivity, not a liability remeasurement: a hypothetical new lease/renewal changes the total annual lease cash payment from 8 to 10, holding pre-lease operations and other cash inputs constant. The after-rent measure falls to 20 and company cash movement to 13, but do not invent the new liability, EBITDA depreciation/interest, enterprise value or precise multiple without a new lease/accounting model. Deliver the EBITDA/net-income/adjustment/lease/cash/net-debt bridges, source/input register and paired definition table. Keep strongest objection, fictional finance/lease/compensation/evidence owners and day 0/30/60/90 stop/review gates; no actual lease change, payment, capital commitment, data share or board approval.","quiz":[{"q":"Under the stated post-lease model, revenue 100, nonlease cash costs 67 and recurring share expense 3 give which EBITDA?","o":["33 crore","30 crore","22 crore","41 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Net income 17, other depreciation 4, ROU depreciation 6, lease interest 2 and bank interest 1 reconcile to which EBITDA?","o":["27 crore","33 crore","22 crore","30 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"The promoter adds share expense 3 to EBITDA 30, then lease payment 8 to reach 41. Which diagnosis fits?","o":["Rent addback duplicates an undeducted amount","Rent addback reverses an expense in the 67","Rent addback removes another liability 30","Rent addback proves cash generated equals 41"],"a":0},{"q":"Lease opening liability 36 plus accrued interest 2 less cash payment 8 leaves what closing liability?","o":["28 crore","34 crore","30 crore","38 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"In the stated annual lease cash payment 8, which principal/interest split applies?","o":["8 and 2 crore","6 and 2 crore","6 and 8 crore","8 and 0 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the course-defined after-rent measure retaining share cost?","o":["22 crore","25 crore","30 crore","33 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Pre-lease cash operating surplus 33 less lease 8, bank interest 1, WC increase 4 and capex 5 gives which total cash movement?","o":["16 crore","22 crore","25 crore","15 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Equity 100, bank debt 40, lease liability 30 and unrestricted cash 25 give which lease-inclusive EV?","o":["115 crore","170 crore","145 crore","140 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Which paired definition is internally consistent with the proposed course convention?","o":["EV 145/EBITDA 30, leases included","EV 115/EBITDA 41, leases excluded","EV 100/EBITDA 30, leases included","EV 145/after-rent 41, leases excluded"],"a":0},{"q":"A hypothetical new renewal raises annual cash lease payment 8 to 10, other cash inputs unchanged. What can be concluded without a new liability model?","o":["After-rent 22 and cash movement 15","After-rent 20 and lease liability 32","After-rent 20 and cash movement 13","After-rent 30 and lease liability 40"],"a":2}]},"96":{"objectives":["Compare keeping, dropping and replacing a fictional kitchen combo through avoidable contribution and scarce capacity, without treating allocated unavoidable rent, sunk setup or a fixed-cost label as cash savings."],"videos":[{"id":"nFjEz5alwtY","title":"Basic Cost Concepts...with a touch of humor | Managerial Accounting","by":"Edspira","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Covers opportunity/direct/indirect/product/period/sunk costs; does not supply a complete avoidable-contribution/capacity decision. Use the written ACCA-based exercise. Jokes and specific personal/career claims are not factual course inputs."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA - relevant costs","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f5/technical-articles/relevant-costs.html","note":"Depreciation is not itself cash, but tax effects or other cash changes need separate treatment. Ignore source employment language as execution authority; no real staff action."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA - relevant cash flows","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/foundation-level-study-resources/ffm/ffm-technical-articles/relevant-cash-flows.html","note":"Explicit future/incremental/opportunity-cost basis; unchanged committed costs excluded only within the stated comparison horizon. Not a current Indian tax or employment rule."}],"exercise":"Private fictional cloud-kitchen decision, one month, rupees unless labeled lakh. No actual food order, menu change, customer contact, layoffs, lease action or spend. Base combo price 200, paid variable cost 140 per order including ingredients, packaging, commission and variable delivery. Expected 5,000 orders gives revenue 10 lakh, variable cost 7 lakh and contribution 3 lakh. No unlisted variable costs, taxes, receivables or inventory changes. Demand and cost figures are assumptions, not measured sales or customer evidence.\nThe monthly allocated kitchen rent 4 lakh remains payable in full whether the combo is kept or dropped; no sublet or exit saving. A separate dedicated monthly support contract 1 lakh can actually be canceled with no penalty or residual payment if dropped, by a stipulated cost assumption, not a real cancellation authorization. A historical 0.5 lakh setup cost was already paid and is not recoverable. An allocation report shows revenue 10 minus variable 7 minus support 1 minus allocated rent 4 = segment loss 2 lakh. This is not evidence that dropping saves 2 lakh. Against drop with no alternative use, keep generates contribution 3 less avoidable support 1 = positive incremental operating/cash 2 lakh. Dropping loses that 2 lakh while rent 4 persists. Sunk setup is irrelevant to this forward incremental comparison but remains part of history and possible financial statements. The incremental 2 is not the whole kitchen's total profit or an unconditional cash-solvency pass; opening cash and other kitchen flows are absent.\nAt price 200 and variable 140, contribution 60/order covers avoidable support 1 lakh at 1,666.666667 orders; with whole orders at least 1,667 gives nonnegative incremental benefit. It is not the full kitchen rent-inclusive breakeven, demand forecast or funding plan. Independent downside: commission increases by 15 per order, included in a new variable 155; same price/orders/support. Contribution 45/order times 5,000 = 2.25 lakh, less support 1 = positive keep-versus-drop 1.25 lakh. Do not subtract that extra commission twice or assume the rent became avoidable.\nSeparate capacity-constrained replacement choice, not an extra benefit to add to spare-capacity keep. Existing combo uses 4 kitchen minutes/order and 20,000 minutes for 5,000 orders. A new premium item would use 5 minutes/order and earn 90 contribution/order before a new avoidable 0.4 lakh monthly support cost. If and only if dropping frees those same 20,000 usable bottleneck minutes, all 4,000 premium orders can actually be sold and no other spillovers occur, premium contribution 3.6 lakh minus support 0.4 = 3.2 lakh versus existing combo net 2 lakh, improvement 1.2 lakh. Contribution per scarce minute is existing 60/4=15 and premium 90/5=18, but setup/support, demand and usable time still matter. Do not count 5,000 premium orders that require 25,000 minutes or add displaced combo revenue back. At only 2,000 saleable premium orders, contribution 1.8 minus support 0.4 = 1.4 lakh, below existing 2. A premium replacement must sell at least 2,667 whole orders to exceed 2 lakh under these assumptions; 2,666.666667 is the equality threshold. Do not confuse a contribution-per-minute ranking with an approved rollout or observed demand.\nA changed horizon, lease termination rights, supplier penalties, fixed staff commitments, service quality, cannibalization or working-capital timing can change relevant costs and recommendation. Costs are relevant because the choice changes them, not simply because they are called fixed or variable. Verify future unavoidable versus avoidable facts before actual action. Deliver keep/drop/replacement and downside tables, arithmetic and units, strongest objection and source/input/change log. Assign fictional demand, cost, capacity, quality, cash and rights owners with day 0/30/60/90 stop/review gates. Private recommendations only; no actual cancellation, staff action, order, supplier message, financing or disclosure.","quiz":[{"q":"At price 200 and variable cost 140, what is contribution per combo order?","o":["200 rupees","140 rupees","40 rupees","60 rupees"],"a":3},{"q":"At 5,000 orders, contribution 3 lakh and avoidable support 1 lakh, what is keep-minus-drop benefit with no alternative use?","o":["2 lakh","Minus 2 lakh","3 lakh","6 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Which item is NOT a saved future cash cost from dropping in the stated month?","o":["Variable costs 7 lakh on dropped orders","Cancelable support contract 1 lakh","Allocated unavoidable rent 4 lakh","Commission included in variable cost 140"],"a":2},{"q":"A 0.5 lakh setup was paid earlier and is unrecoverable. How should it affect this forward keep/drop increment?","o":["Subtract 0.5 only from keeping","Exclude from the forward increment","Add 0.5 to savings from dropping","Subtract 0.5 again from both cash budgets"],"a":1},{"q":"What minimum whole order count covers 1 lakh avoidable support at 60 contribution/order?","o":["1666 orders","5000 orders","6667 orders","1667 orders"],"a":3},{"q":"Commission rises 15/order, already included in new variable 155. At 5,000 orders, what is keep-minus-drop benefit after 1 lakh support?","o":["2 lakh","0.50 lakh","1.25 lakh","2.25 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"Existing combo takes 4 minutes/order at 5,000 orders. How many 5-minute premium orders fit the same freed 20,000 minutes?","o":["4000 orders","5000 orders","2500 orders","6250 orders"],"a":0},{"q":"At 4,000 premium orders with 90 contribution and 0.4 lakh support, what is improvement versus existing combo net 2 lakh?","o":["3.2 lakh","1.2 lakh","1.6 lakh","0.4 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"At only 2,000 saleable premium orders, what is premium net benefit versus existing combo net 2 lakh?","o":["0.6 lakh better","1.4 lakh better","0.6 lakh worse","2.2 lakh worse"],"a":2},{"q":"What minimum whole premium orders exceed existing net 2 lakh after 0.4 lakh support at 90 contribution/order?","o":["2666 orders","2223 orders","4000 orders","2667 orders"],"a":3}]},"97":{"objectives":["Build a fictional 50-crore machine investment with dated incremental unlevered after-tax cash flows, a declared discount basis, working-capital recovery and salvage; separate positive modeled NPV from financing, authority and measured execution."],"videos":[{"id":"Fw5-wccViOM","title":"NPV and IRR explained","by":"The Finance Storyteller","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. The project example uses rounded numbers; the complete written synthetic exercise supplies its own tax, WC, timing and authority assumptions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA - advanced investment appraisal","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f9/technical-articles/advanced-investment-appraisal.html","note":"Tax rates/depreciation/timing are case assumptions, not current Indian law. Unlevered financing exclusion requires a matched flow/rate framework."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Finance Storyteller - introductory NPV and IRR","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fw5-wccViOM","note":"Broad positive-NPV language needs budget/risk/authority qualification. Rounded 20% WACC/IRR example is not an estimated project rate or general proof of unique IRR."}],"exercise":"Private fictional textile mill, crore rupees. No actual capital order, borrowing, payment, tax filing or board authority. At time 0 pay machine cost 50 plus operating working capital 5. An earlier unrecoverable feasibility study 2 is already paid; it is not a new time 0 outflow. No resale/alternative-use opportunity cost for that study. Operating horizon four years. At each year-end 1-4 the machine saves 20 of incremental cash operating costs before tax, net of all incremental operating cash costs; no extra sales or other cash effects. These fixed nominal amounts and a stipulated nominal, after-tax project-risk-matched unlevered discount rate 10 percent are teaching assumptions, not a current market WACC estimate. Do not inflate an already nominal input again or discount these nominal flows at an unmatched real rate.\nFictional tax convention only, not current Indian income-tax/IndAS law: tax allowable depreciation 10 at each year-end for four years, same-year cash tax 25 percent of incremental operating taxable income, all tax effects usable immediately, no losses/credits/other taxes or financing. Tax book basis 50 at start declines to 10 after four 10 allowances. Annual taxable saving 20-10=10; tax 2.5; unlevered operating cash flow 20-2.5=17.5. Equivalently (20-10)*(1-0.25)+10=17.5. Depreciation is not an extra cash outflow; its specified tax shield 2.5 is counted once. With the same depreciation basis for this illustration, annual after-tax accounting operating profit 7.5 differs from operating cash 17.5. No bank interest/principal deducted in these unlevered project flows because the stated discount framework already handles financing cost; an equity cash-flow model would require a different flow/rate pair.\nAt time 4 sell the machine for cash 10, equal to remaining tax basis 10: under the stipulated simplified disposal rule no taxable gain/loss, no other disposal cost. Fully release working capital 5 as cash at time 4, no write-offs, collection delays or other inventory/AR/AP balances. Neither recovery 5 nor sale 10 is annual operating saving. Working capital is invested once at time 0 and recovered once at time 4; yearly changes are zero. Do not deduct the same 5 every year. Actual collection, tax-basis/disposal and decommissioning terms could change the result.\nTimeline cash flows: time 0 minus 55; time 1/2/3 each 17.5; time 4 operating 17.5+salvage 10+WC 5=32.5. NPV=-55+17.5/1.1+17.5/1.1^2+17.5/1.1^3+32.5/1.1^4=10.717847 crore. Four-year operating present-value factor 3.1698654463. Undiscounted receipts 85 minus 55=30 is not NPV; ignoring time 0 in a spreadsheet NPV range can shift timing. Show time 0 separately when a function assumes end-of-period inputs. Positive modeled NPV is not available funding, measured savings, permission to buy or proof all safety/quality/contract checks pass. Initial gross project outflow 55 needs an authorized funding plan, and exact company minimum cash requires dated company flows, opening cash and funding sources absent here.\nCoherent independent downside: annual pretax incremental operating cash saving 14 instead of 20, same machine, WC, horizon, tax convention and salvage. Annual tax(14-10)*25 percent=1, operating cash 13; timeline minus 55/13/13/13/28; NPV minus 3.546547 crore. Recompute from changed mechanism, not a uniform haircut of mixed stocks/flows. Under the base exclusions and unchanged tax regime, constant annual pretax saving about 15.491776 is the NPV-zero threshold, not a guaranteed forecast. A delay, tax arrears, unusable tax shield, different disposal value, WC not recovered or other risk needs its own dated model. Do not assert every positive-NPV project can be chosen under capital rationing or conflicting alternatives.\nDeliver separate base/downside dated cash and tax-basis tables, formula and discounted checks, source/input/assumption register, threshold and strongest objection. Assign fictional operating/tax/cash/evidence/rights owners and day 0/30/60/90 gates. Retain prior/revised versions; no actual order, loan, hedge, vendor message, tax opinion, public disclosure or capital commitment.","quiz":[{"q":"Machine 50 plus working capital 5 are paid now; study 2 was previously paid and unrecoverable. What is new time 0 project cash flow?","o":["Minus 57 crore","Minus 50 crore","Minus 55 crore","Minus 52 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Annual pretax incremental cash saving 20, allowable depreciation 10, same-year tax 25 percent. What is annual cash tax?","o":["2.5 crore","5 crore","7.5 crore","10 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Under that tax convention, what is annual unlevered operating cash flow?","o":["7.5 crore","10 crore","20 crore","17.5 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Cost 50 depreciates for tax 10/year for four years, then sells 10. Under the stipulated disposal rule, what is taxable disposal gain?","o":["10 crore","0 crore","40 crore","50 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Time 4 operating cash 17.5, salvage 10 and full WC recovery 5 give which terminal total?","o":["17.5 crore","27.5 crore","32.5 crore","22.5 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Which timeline is correct under the base project assumptions?","o":["Minus 55/12.5/12.5/12.5/27.5","Minus 57/17.5/17.5/17.5/32.5","Minus 55/17.5/17.5/17.5/17.5","Minus 55/17.5/17.5/17.5/32.5"],"a":3},{"q":"Discounting that base timeline at 10 percent, NPV to six decimals is what?","o":["30.000000 crore","10.717847 crore","8.717847 crore","55.000000 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Why exclude loan interest and principal from these stated unlevered project cash flows?","o":["Financing handled by matched project discount framework","Financing paid by owner is never a cash transaction","Loan principal is part of tax allowable depreciation","Project NPV is defined as operating accounting profit"],"a":0},{"q":"Downside pretax saving 14, depreciation 10 and immediate 25 percent tax imply which annual cash flow?","o":["10.5 crore","14 crore","13 crore","3 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"The base NPV is positive but initial outflow 55 needs funding and no cash plan is supplied. What conclusion is supported?","o":["Modeled value positive; funding and authority unresolved","Modeled value positive; no initial financing needed","Modeled value positive; investment already authorized","Modeled value positive; company minimum cash proved"],"a":0}]},"98":{"objectives":["Use new mixed lease, kitchen and machine cases to diagnose actual attempted errors and choose provisional remediation areas; test delayed transfer without inventing measured weakest skills from a day score."],"videos":[{"id":"Fw5-wccViOM","title":"NPV and IRR explained","by":"The Finance Storyteller","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. The project example uses rounded numbers; the complete written synthetic exercise supplies its own tax, WC, timing and authority assumptions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA - advanced investment appraisal","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f9/technical-articles/advanced-investment-appraisal.html","note":"Tax rates/depreciation/timing are case assumptions, not current Indian law. Unlevered financing exclusion requires a matched flow/rate framework."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA - relevant costs and scarce resources","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f5/technical-articles/relevant-costs.html","note":"Compare future incremental, avoidable and opportunity costs. Allocated overhead is not automatically a saving; account for the relevant time horizon and capacity constraint."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - effective study and delayed retrieval","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Practice retrieval, correct actual errors and revisit the ideas after a delay. These study principles do not establish an effect size for this app or replace per-question reasoning."}],"exercise":"Private review-day transfer with new values. All records and choices fictional; no actual contracts, payments, staffing, fundraising, tax, customer contact or data disclosure. Attempt without notes, then record each answer, source/input, calculation, error cause, corrected decision and delayed changed-context retest. Select two remediation areas from actual attempted errors; with sparse evidence label provisional. A day score, completion timestamp or watched percentage does not identify question-level errors, measured weakest skills or independent mastery.\nFresh lease case, crore: revenue 80 minus nonlease cash costs 54 minus recurring equity-settled share expense 2 gives stipulated post-lease EBITDA 24. No separate rent deducted. Other depreciation 3, ROU depreciation 5, lease interest 1, bank interest 1, tax 0=>net income 14. Annual total lease payment 6=principal 5+interest 1; opening liability 25+interest 1-payment 6=closing 20, no other changes. Course-defined after-rent/share-cost-retained operating measure 24-6=18, not CFO/GAAP EBITDA. Noncash-share-adjusted pre-lease cash operating surplus 26 less lease 6, bank interest 1, operating WC increase 3 and paid capex 4=>total cash movement 12. Opening unrestricted cash 8 ends 20, no other flows. At the same year-end snapshot equity 80+bank debt 30+lease 20-cash 20 gives lease-inclusive EV 110; excluding leases gives 90. State definition pairs 110/24 and 90/18, not an automatically equal multiple. A promoter's 24+2+6=32 duplicates an undeducted lease amount and treats noncash share cost as harmless. Independent delayed-customer-collection packet adds 2 to closing operating receivables, cash movement falls 12 to 10/end cash 20 to 18, no revenue or expense change. Do not quietly reuse old snapshot cash 20 for a revised valuation; changed closing cash 18 would change lease-inclusive EV 112/lease-exclusive 92 if other snapshot values truly held.\nFresh kitchen, one month, rupees: price 180/variable 130/3,000 orders yields contribution 1.5 lakh. Support 0.4 lakh avoidable if dropped; allocated rent 2 lakh unchanged. Keep-minus-drop with no alternative use=1.1 lakh despite allocated segment loss 0.9 lakh. Same 3,000 orders use 4 minutes each, freeing 12,000 bottleneck minutes only if dropped. Premium replacement uses 6 minutes/order, contribution 75/order, new avoidable support 0.2 lakh. At fully saleable 2,000 orders, replacement net 1.3 lakh beats current 1.1 by 0.2 lakh. Equal contribution per minute 12.5 does not determine the choice before support/demand. If only 1,500 premium orders can be sold, net 1.125-0.2=0.925 lakh, below current 1.1 by 0.175 lakh. Do not invent demand sales or rent savings.\nIndependent two-year machine, crore: cost 12+WC 2 at time 0, annual pretax operating cash savings 9 each year-end. Fictional immediate 25 percent tax on savings minus allowable depreciation 5 in each of two years; same basis ends 2. Annual tax 1/CFO 8. Salvage 2 equal to tax basis(no disposal tax under stipulated rule), full WC 2 recovery at time 2. Discount 10 percent nominal matching nominal flows. Timeline minus 14/8/12; NPV=-14+8/1.1+12/1.1^2=3.190083. Earlier irrecoverable study 1 excluded. Contradictory packet: annual pretax saving 6 rather than 9 with all other inputs unchanged; tax 0.25/CFO 5.75, terminal 9.75, NPV=-14+5.75/1.1+9.75/1.1^2=minus 0.714876. Not current Indian tax law or authorization for loan/order. A recommendation must change with the mechanism, not preserve the old answer or pretend exact peak funding is proven.\nDeliver attempted-answer/error/change logs and separate corrected lease/kitchen/machine bridges. Do not sum separate case cash flows. Assign fictional evidence/cash/quality/tax/rights owners and dated day 0/30/60/90 stop gates. A delayed new case tests transfer, not just recall from immediately repeating identical numbers or a confidence rating. No actual sponsor approval, lease amendment, cancellation, staff action, order, tax filing, disclosure or financing.","quiz":[{"q":"New lease case revenue 80, nonlease cash costs 54 and share expense 2: stated post-lease EBITDA is what?","o":["24 crore","26 crore","18 crore","32 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Fresh lease case opening liability 25, interest 1 and total payment 6: closing liability is what?","o":["19 crore","24 crore","20 crore","30 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Fresh lease case pre-lease cash surplus 26 less lease 6, bank interest 1, WC 3 and capex 4 gives total cash movement?","o":["18 crore","12 crore","14 crore","26 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"An independent packet delays 2 customer cash beyond this window with recognized revenue unchanged. What changes?","o":["Cash movement 12 and end cash 20","Cash movement 10 and revenue 78","Cash movement 14 and end cash 22","Cash movement 10 and end cash 18"],"a":3},{"q":"Fresh kitchen contribution 1.5 lakh, avoidable support 0.4 and common rent 2 unchanged. Keep-minus-drop is what?","o":["1.1 lakh","Minus 0.9 lakh","1.5 lakh","3.1 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Fresh kitchen frees 12,000 minutes; premium takes 6 minutes/order and earns 75 contribution less 0.2 lakh support. At full demand, net replacement benefit is what?","o":["1.5 lakh","1.3 lakh","1.1 lakh","0.2 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"Only 1,500 premium orders sell. What is replacement net versus existing 1.1 lakh?","o":["0.175 lakh better","0.925 lakh better","0.175 lakh worse","0.4 lakh worse"],"a":2},{"q":"Fresh two-year machine time 0 minus 14, year 1 cash 8, year 2 cash 12 at 10 percent: NPV rounded six decimals?","o":["6.000000 crore","2.190083 crore","17.190083 crore","3.190083 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Fresh machine downside pretax saving 6, depreciation 5 and immediate 25 percent tax: annual operating cash is what?","o":["4.5 crore","5.75 crore","0.75 crore","6.25 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Only a day score and watched percentage exist before this new attempt. How should two remediation areas be selected?","o":["Infer measured weakest items from aggregate day score","Treat all watched lessons as corrected item-level errors","Choose identical familiar questions and count confidence","Use actual new errors, label sparse evidence provisional"],"a":3}]},"99":{"objectives":["Separate a fictional exporter's annual EBIT interest cover from dated liquidity, debt principal maturity and uncertain refinancing; test three coherent operating cases without treating a coverage ratio as available cash."],"videos":[{"id":"AOoZOVL8e1M","title":"Capital Structure (Traditional View of Gearing and the WACC) Modigliani and Miller Theory - M & M","by":"EZIKAN ACADEMY ","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use the written cash/maturity scenarios."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ACCA - financing alternatives and maturity","url":"https://www.accaglobal.com/gb/en/student/exam-support-resources/fundamentals-exams-study-resources/f9/technical-articles/suitability-financing-alternatives.html","note":"Financing maturity, EBIT interest cover, cash/retained earnings and scenario evidence. Asset book values/cash holdings alone do not prove collateral value, future generation or lender willingness. Generic debt/equity cost/control/legal statements are qualified case teaching, not current Indian rules."}],"exercise":"Private fictional auto-parts exporter, crore rupees, one coming year. No actual loan, drawdown, hedge, collateral pledge, lender message or funding authority. At time 0 opening unrestricted cash 15 plus proposed new short-term debt 20 pays long-lived expansion capex 30, leaving 5. The 20 is a hypothetical fully disbursed facility, not a real commitment or assumed lender approval. Existing debt 40 remains outstanding through the year, no principal due within this window; annual interest 4. New debt 20 interest 2 paid at year end and principal 20 all due at the same year end, no automatic renewal or new refinancing. No fees, tax, lease, dividends, disposal or other cash flow. Interest total 6 is included once, and initial expansion capex 30 must not be paid twice.\nThree independent annual scenarios, not probabilities or measured results: base EBIT 18/depreciation 4/operating noncash working-capital increase 2; cyclical downside EBIT 10/depreciation 4/WC increase 6; severe EBIT 4/depreciation 4/WC increase 10. EBIT is after depreciation but before all interest/tax. EBIT interest cover EBIT/6 is 3 x/1.666667 x/0.666667 x. EBITDA 22/14/8 is not EBIT and the coverage ratio does not include the 20 bullet principal. Use no claim of a universal lender covenant or safe ratio; actual covenants have their own definitions and dates.\nFor each scenario, cash available before financing and expansion = EBIT+depreciation-WC increase-interest 6-maintenance capex 3. This is 11/minus 1/minus 11, not CFO under a universal interest-classification policy. Add post-expansion time 0 cash 5, then repay new principal 20 at year-end: modeled endpoints minus 4/minus 16/minus 26. A modeled negative endpoint identifies an unfunded gap, not an approved overdraft or observed negative bank balance. A floor 5 needs additional incremental cash 9/21/31 at the respective endpoints, if no other inputs change. Those are endpoint needs, not the exact maximum funding requirement because dated collections/costs/capex/interest timing within the year is absent. Even base EBIT cover 3 x does not fund the bullet. Do not borrow again implicitly to force cash to the floor, call term extension a cash receipt or assume debt principal is a P&L expense.\nSeparate runway illustration only, not to be added to the annual scenarios: unrestricted cash 5 and constant month-end net operating burn 2 would suggest 5/2=2.5 months as a simple continuous-rate approximation, ignoring lumpy obligations. With strictly month-end payments and no partial-month spend it does not identify an exact failure date. A debt bullet or restricted cash would invalidate treating this as full liquidity runway. Use dated balances to make an actual runway claim.\nCompare reducing/staging capex, committed longer-term finance with separately modeled fees/rate/maturity, or new equity as conditional alternatives. None is secured, free, a new approval or proven cheaper; export FX, customer default, collateral/covenants, refinancing concentration and shareholder rights remain missing facts. Deliver operating/interest/principal/timing bridges, scenario/end-floor table, dated sources/assumptions, strongest objection and fictional finance/operations/rights owners with day 0/30/60/90 review-stop gates. No real financial commitment, staff action, data disclosure or investor outreach.","quiz":[{"q":"Opening cash 15, new debt 20 and expansion capex 30 at time 0 leave what cash?","o":["35 crore","15 crore","5 crore","Minus 15 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Existing interest 4 plus new interest 2, with base EBIT 18: EBIT interest cover is what?","o":["3 x","3.666667 x","4.5 x","0.3 x"],"a":0},{"q":"Downside EBIT 10 and total interest 6 give which EBIT cover?","o":["2.333333 x","3 x","0.666667 x","1.666667 x"],"a":3},{"q":"Severe EBIT 4, depreciation 4, WC increase 10, interest 6 and maintenance capex 3 give which pre-principal cash movement?","o":["Minus 15 crore","Minus 11 crore","Minus 7 crore","Plus 3 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Base pre-principal movement 11, post-expansion cash 5 and bullet principal 20 give what endpoint?","o":["16 crore","Minus 9 crore","Minus 4 crore","6 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Downside movement minus 1, starting post-expansion cash 5 and bullet 20: what is endpoint need to reach floor 5?","o":["16 crore","20 crore","26 crore","21 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Which cash bridge correctly treats principal 20 due this year?","o":["Deduct 20 after operating/interest cash","Deduct 20 from EBIT then addback","Ignore 20 because interest cover exceeds 1","Count 20 as refinancing cash without agreement"],"a":0},{"q":"Why does term extension alone not create a cash receipt?","o":["It reduces operating revenue by the principal","It changes maturity unless new cash is disbursed","It adds the old principal to net income","It guarantees the next lender will refinance"],"a":1},{"q":"Separate cash 5 divided by constant monthly burn 2 gives what simplified approximation?","o":["2.5 months including every debt bullet","5 months excluding lumpy obligations","10 months including every debt bullet","2.5 months excluding lumpy obligations"],"a":3},{"q":"Annual endpoints are known, intrayear dates absent. Which funding claim is supported?","o":["Endpoint gaps known; exact peak unknown","Endpoint gaps equal exact peak by definition","Positive EBIT proves every payment payable","Any bullet gap is already lender-approved"],"a":0}]},"100":{"objectives":["Link a fictional retailer's income, working capital, PPE, debt, equity and cash forecasts; expose an unfunded gap, then separately model conditional financing costs or a stated capex deferral rather than plug cash."],"videos":[{"id":"f3T0tCjw1k8","title":"How the 3 Financial Statements Connect Together","by":"Josh Aharonoff (Your CFO Guy)","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Current companion listing supplies only description, not worksheet content."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - indirect cash and statement links","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"Compare direct and indirect cash-flow bridges. The source's worked totals contain inconsistencies; use the explicitly reconciled fictional case. Its interest classification is a classroom convention, not universal Indian policy."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Your CFO Guy - three-statement worksheet listing","url":"https://www.yourcfoguy.com/templates/how-the-3-financial-statements-connect","note":"Worksheet listing, not the worksheet itself. Use the complete written synthetic exercise for the three-statement model; a download is optional."}],"exercise":"Private fictional annual retailer, crore rupees. No real listed-company facts, disclosure, ledger edit, share issue, capital order or loan. Opening cash 20/AR 10/inventory 20/net PPE 50 gives assets 100=AP 10+bank debt 30+equity 60. Forecast revenue 150/COGS 90/cash operating overhead 35/depreciation 10 gives EBITDA 25/EBIT 15. Existing cash interest 3, no tax, dividends, lease, impairment, disposals, acquisitions, FX or other changes. Net income 12. Closing AR 15/inventory 25/operating trade AP 15. All overhead paid; receivables/inventory/payables are operating balances, not all current accounts blindly. Capex 40 paid and existing debt principal 5 repaid within year; no new financing in the base.\nIndirect operating cash, using this exercise's interest-in-operating convention, =NI 12+depreciation 10-AR increase 5-inventory increase 5+AP increase 5=17. Direct check: collections 150+10-15=145; purchases 90+25-20=95; suppliers paid 95+10-15=90; overhead 35/interest 3=>145-90-35-3=17. Capex 40 and principal 5 give cash movement minus 28; opening 20 ends minus 8. PPE 50+40-10=80; ending debt 25/equity 72/AP 15. Signed algebraic assets minus 8+15+25+80=112=25+15+72. The equation balances even with negative modeled cash, which is an unfunded gap, not a valid unconstrained cash asset or bank evidence. Setting cash 10 without the other entries invents 18 of assets and breaks the balance sheet. A balanced model alone is not proof it is fundable or accurate.\nConditional funding branch, not actual authorization or secured availability: assume an incremental 20 loan fully drawn at time 0, interest 10 percent=2 for the full year, paid within year; no fees, principal due or other change on this new loan until after the modeled year. Additional interest reduces NI 12 to 10 and CFO 17 to 15. Ending cash 20+15-40-5+20=10, meeting proposed floor 10 at endpoint only. Closing debt 30-5+20=45; equity 60+10=70; assets 10+15+25+80=130=AP 15+debt 45+equity 70. Raising only 18 is not sufficient if full-year 10 percent interest must also be paid: endpoint minus 8+0.9*18=8.2, not 10. Solve minus 8+0.9*draw>=10=>draw>=20 under these narrow assumptions. Do not call interest proceeds or capitalized loan principal income. Later/different draws need dated interest calculations and maturity evidence, not a repeated full-year formula.\nIndependent capex-staging branch: no new debt, capex 22 instead of 40, explicitly assume the deferral has no current-year operating/depreciation effect solely for this comparison. NI 12/CFO 17/debt 25/equity 72 stay; PPE 50+22-10=62, ending cash 20+17-22-5=10. Assets 10+15+25+62=112=AP 15+debt 25+equity 72. That no-operating-impact assumption must be tested before an actual store-opening deferral; deferred stores may lose sales or change depreciation. Do not add this 18 saving to the funded branch unless making a new combined model. No new shares/loan drawn or actual capex canceled.\nDeliver three linked statements per branch, explicit opening/changes/closing debt/PPE/equity, formula/direct-indirect and gap checks, dated assumption/source/change log and strongest objection. Actual collection/payment/opening timing, restricted cash, tax/accounting applicability, store economics and financing covenants/authority need verification. Floor at annual endpoint is not proof no intrayear deficit. Assign fictional cash/operations/accounting/rights owners and day 0/30/60/90 review-stop gates. Private analysis only, no real lender/vendor contact, trade, public disclosure or spend.","quiz":[{"q":"Revenue 150/COGS 90/cash overhead 35/depreciation 10/interest 3/tax 0 give NI?","o":["22 crore","12 crore","15 crore","25 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"NI 12+depreciation 10-AR increase 5-inventory increase 5+AP increase 5 gives CFO?","o":["27 crore","12 crore","7 crore","17 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Opening cash 20/CFO 17/capex 40/principal 5/no new debt yield endpoint?","o":["12 crore","Minus 28 crore","Minus 8 crore","32 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Does algebraically balanced assets 112/liabilities-equity 112 with negative cash 8 prove a fundable plan?","o":["No; funding and timing remain unresolved","Yes; balance removes any cash shortfall","Yes; negative cash proves approved overdraft","No; depreciation must be another cash use"],"a":0},{"q":"Opening PPE 50 plus capex 40 minus depreciation 10 leaves net PPE?","o":["90 crore","80 crore","60 crore","50 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Conditional 20 new loan at time 0/10 percent full-year paid interest changes NI and CFO to what?","o":["12 and 17 crore","14 and 19 crore","10 and 15 crore","10 and 17 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Conditional draw 20 branch: opening 20+CFO 15-capex 40-old principal 5+draw 20 yields end cash?","o":["12 crore","8 crore","30 crore","10 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Why does draw 18 not reach floor 10 under full-year 10 percent interest?","o":["Net cash lift 16.2 leaves 8.2","Net cash lift 18 leaves 10","Net cash lift 19.8 leaves 11.8","Interest 1.8 increases operating cash"],"a":0},{"q":"With draw 20, closing debt 45/AP 15/equity 70, what assets total reconciles?","o":["112 crore","132 crore","130 crore","150 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Independent no-new-loan capex 22 branch with unchanged NI 12/CFO 17 yields cash 10 and net PPE what?","o":["80 crore","62 crore","72 crore","40 crore"],"a":1}]},"101":{"objectives":["Link a fictional logistics terminal growth assumption to reinvestment and sustainable return on capital, discount terminal value from the right date and test an independently stated valuation claim."],"videos":[{"id":"83yR6EFEl5Y","title":"Session 9: Terminal Value","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Historical percentages/growth-duration claims not treated as current measured facts."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran - terminal value slides","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/valonlineslides/session9.pdf","note":"Links growth/reinvestment/sustainable return and terminal timing. Risk-free cap, ratings/beta and long-run assumptions are analyst heuristics, not universal economic laws or current observed inputs. No current market rate adopted."}],"exercise":"Private fictional logistics enterprise valuation, crore rupees, not an investment, real market quote or trading instruction. All nominal cash flows/discount/growth in the same currency; unlevered enterprise cash flows exclude debt flows. Forecast FCFF 8/10/12 at year-ends 1/2/3 is already net of the respective operating reinvestment and taxes. Do not subtract reinvestment from these supplied FCFF figures a second time. A separate terminal operating schedule supplies year 3 after-tax operating earnings(NOPAT)20. Both schedules are synthetic consistent inputs; historical earnings, cash or quality are not observed.\nStable period begins year 4. Nominal WACC 10 percent, perpetual growth 3 percent and sustainable marginal return on capital 12 percent are stipulated, not current market estimates or claims competition actually permits these returns. Next-year NOPAT 20*1.03=20.6. Required reinvestment rate g/ROC=.03/.12=25 percent; reinvestment 5.15; year 4 FCFF 20.6-5.15=15.45. Terminal value at end-year 3=15.45/(.10-.03)=220.714286. Present terminal at time 0=220.714286/1.1^3=165.825910; present explicit FCFF 8/1.1+10/1.1^2+12/1.1^3=24.552968; enterprise value 190.378877. Present terminal share 165.825910/190.378877=87.103103 percent. High terminal share makes stable inputs material, not proof the DCF is automatically invalid or that years 1-3 do not matter. Terminal year 3 value is not current EV and is additional to year 3 FCFF 12, not a second year 4 operating flow.\nAt time 0 nonoperating unrestricted cash 10 and debt 40, no other debt-like obligations/minority/preference/holdings: indicated equity 190.378877+10-40=160.378877. Do not subtract debt in FCFF and again in this bridge. Lease, minority interest, cash restrictions and other claims need facts in a real valuation. This is not a guaranteed sale price or actual financing capacity.\nCoherent sensitivity, keep terminal year 3 NOPAT 20 and ROC 12 percent/WACC 10 percent but change growth to 5 percent: next NOPAT 21, reinvestment rate 5/12=41.666667 percent, reinvestment 8.75, FCFF 12.25 and terminal 245. Not 21/(.10-.05)=420 because growth needs reinvestment. The alternative has its own EV after discounting; do not hold FCFF 15.45 constant and claim a complete growth sensitivity. Pure denominator stress with fixed FCFF can be shown only as such, not a full economic case. A claim of terminal growth>=WACC does not produce a valid finite growing-perpetuity value under these assumptions. Whether any 3 or 5 percent perpetual growth is sustainable needs same-currency nominal economic/risk/competitive evidence, absent here.\nIndependent reverse check of a quoted end-year 3 terminal value 300, not the full EV: holding NOPAT 3=20/WACC 10 percent/ROC 12 percent fixed, solve 300=20*(1+g)*(1-g/.12)/(.10-g), with g<.10 and nonnegative reinvestment/cash assumptions. Test roots algebraically or use transparent numerical solving; do not silently choose a growth rate without checking the same reinvestment relation. This quote is a fictional hypothesis, not a verified comparable. The algebra gives roots approximately g=0.0706415533 and 0.8493584467; only the first,7.064155 percent, satisfies g<10 percent. It implies reinvestment 58.867961 percent, not zero. The second fails the convergence/cash restrictions and is rejected. Even the permitted mathematical root does not prove sustainable economic growth or competitive returns. Retain formulas/units/timing, source/input/change register, strongest objection and fictional valuation/evidence/rights owners with day 0/30/60/90 review gates. No actual trade, acquisition, financing, publication or data disclosure.","quiz":[{"q":"Terminal year 3 NOPAT 20 grows 3 percent. Year 4 NOPAT equals?","o":["20.6 crore","20 crore","15.45 crore","21 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"At g 3 percent and sustainable ROC 12 percent, reinvestment rate equals?","o":["3 percent","12 percent","25 percent","40 percent"],"a":2},{"q":"Year 4 NOPAT 20.6 and reinvestment 25 percent imply FCFF?","o":["20.6 crore","15.45 crore","5.15 crore","20 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"At WACC 10 percent/g 3 percent/year 4 FCFF 15.45, terminal at end-year 3 equals?","o":["154.5 crore","515 crore","165.825910 crore","220.714286 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Which discount timing converts that time 3 terminal value into time 0 present value?","o":["Divide by 1.1 cubed","Divide by 1.1 fourth power","Multiply by 1.1 cubed","Leave undiscounted because perpetual"],"a":0},{"q":"Present terminal 165.825910 plus explicit PV 24.552968 gives EV about 190.378877. Terminal share is about?","o":["100 percent","87.103103 percent","12.896897 percent","115 percent"],"a":1},{"q":"Current EV 190.378877, nonoperating cash 10 and debt 40 give indicated equity?","o":["220.378877 crore","190.378877 crore","160.378877 crore","150.378877 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Growth 5 percent, ROC 12 percent and next NOPAT 21: required reinvestment and FCFF are?","o":["0 and 21 crore","5.15 and 15.85 crore","12.25 and 8.75 crore","8.75 and 12.25 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Which statement about g>=WACC fits this finite growing-perpetuity model?","o":["Use absolute value of denominator for finite value","No valid finite value under these assumptions","Use g=WACC to make value equal NOPAT","Ignore growth because cash has positive earnings"],"a":1},{"q":"A market-peer EV/revenue multiple estimates terminal value. How should that component be characterized?","o":["Pure intrinsic DCF regardless of multiple origin","Liquidation cash already realized today","Independent proof of terminal reinvestment","Forward relative pricing component"],"a":3}]},"102":{"objectives":["Build a fictional SaaS peer table with matched enterprise value, period, revenue recognition and profitability definitions; separate relative pricing from intrinsic value and trading multiples from control transactions."],"videos":[{"id":"Tv0WoZVabrQ","title":"How to Value a Company - Comparable Analysis [4 Basic Steps]","by":"Stephen Spicer, CFP®, CLU, AEP, CLTC, CRCS™","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Introductory peer/metric validation only; industry EBITDA/EBIT preference and one-off addbacks are not universal rules or automatic stock-buy evidence. Use matched-period definitions and recurring cost checks."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran - revenue multiples, selected sections","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/papers/revmult.pdf","note":"Historical July 2000 examples are not current market data; use the exercise's explicit formulas and assumptions. Forward-margin formula notation is ambiguous if denominator period is not shifted; our table computes explicit denominators instead. Margin/growth/risk/reinvestment comparisons, not revenue immune to recognition errors."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic SaaS peer snapshot at time 0, crore rupees. No live share prices, company claims, broker advice or permission to trade. No leases, minorities, preference claims, pensions or other debt-like items by assumption; debt/cash are contemporaneous and unrestricted. EV=equity value+debt-cash. All revenue for same completed twelve months, same gross/net recognition and recurring-service scope; all stated growth is next-twelve-month revenue versus that historical period, not a measured forecast success. EBITDA includes recurring stock compensation and excludes the same depreciation/interest/tax definitions across peers; it is not cash flow. No discretionary removal of costs to favor the target.\nPeer A equity 300/debt 20/cash 40=>EV 280, historical revenue 100, forward revenue 120, growth 20 percent, EBITDA 20/margin 20 percent. Peer B equity 500/debt 40/cash 20=>EV 520, revenue 130/forward 169/growth 30 percent, EBITDA minus 13/margin minus 10 percent. Peer C equity 220/debt 0/cash 40=>EV 180, revenue 90/forward 99/growth 10 percent, EBITDA 18/margin 20 percent. Target historical revenue 80/forward 96/growth 20 percent/EBITDA 16/margin 20 percent, net cash 10. The target has no market equity value supplied; derive conditional values rather than invent its observed trading multiple.\nHistorical EV/revenue:A 2.8 x/B 4.0 x/C 2.0 x. Forward EV/revenue using the same current EV but matched forward revenue:A 2.333333 x/B 3.076923 x/C 1.818182 x. Historical EV/EBITDA:A 14 x/C 10 x; B negative EBITDA means its algebraic minus 40 is not a comparable positive earnings multiple or evidence it is cheaper. Faster growth B also loses money; there is no unique valid growth-adjusted multiple from dividing EV/revenue by a growth percentage. Margin, durability, risk, reinvestment, retention, definitions and survival differ. Percentage 20 versus decimal.20 can change a naive growth ratio by 100 x without adding economic evidence. Do not claim a higher-growth multiple must be deserved or peers' prices prove intrinsic value.\nIllustrative matched profitable-peer historical range 2.0-2.8 x revenue times target 80=>EV 160-224; add target net cash 10 once=>equity 170-234. This is an assumption-led conditional pricing range, not a confidence interval, fair value certification or measured market range. A/C differ in growth 10/20 percent and other unknowns; no claim their multiples perfectly bracket the target. A forward-basis crosscheck uses C 1.818182 to A 2.333333 times target forward 96=>EV 174.545455-224/equity 184.545455-234; do not apply those forward multiples to target historical 80. Different period conventions can change a range legitimately when all calculations are explicit. B may be retained as an unmatched high-growth/loss sensitivity, not silently averaged into a claimed matched cohort.\nAn extra packet reports a hypothetical control transaction EV/revenue 5 x including buyer-specific synergies/premium and a marketplace peer that reports gross customer billings as revenue unlike this cohort. Do not blend these into trading peer averages without reconciling economics, timing, control, synergy and recognition. A transaction 5 x does not imply target-equity 400 because 5*80 is enterprise value 400 before net cash bridge, and the comparable/control basis is unresolved. No universal control-premium percentage or accounting rule is asserted. One-off adjustments must be documented both gains and losses, with their economic recurrence checked; a convenient label does not make SBC free.\nDeliver peer/numerator/denominator/date/definition table, included/excluded rationale, both period ranges, target net cash bridge, coherent margin/growth/risk objection, source/assumption/change log and fictional evidence/valuation/rights owners with day 0/30/60/90 stop gates. Missing real inputs stay unknown, not filled by favorable headlines. No real stock order, fundraising, buyer message, valuation disclosure, data sharing or financial commitment.","quiz":[{"q":"Peer A equity 300/debt 20/cash 40 gives EV?","o":["300 crore","360 crore","240 crore","280 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Peer A current EV 280 divided by historical revenue 100 gives which multiple?","o":["2.333333 x","2.8 x","3.0 x","14 x"],"a":1},{"q":"Peer B EV 520/revenue 130/EBITDA minus 13: what is correct?","o":["EV/revenue 4 x; positive EV/EBITDA not meaningful","EV/revenue 4 x; negative 40 x proves cheaper","EV/revenue minus 4 x; EBITDA multiple 40 x","EV/revenue 3.076923 x using historical 130"],"a":0},{"q":"Peer C historical revenue 90 and forward 99 imply what forecast growth?","o":["9 percent","11.111111 percent","10 percent","20 percent"],"a":2},{"q":"Historical matched range 2-2.8 x times target historical 80 gives EV range?","o":["170-234 crore","192-268.8 crore","80-112 crore","160-224 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Target net cash 10 added once to that EV range gives equity range?","o":["170-234 crore","150-214 crore","160-224 crore","180-244 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Forward peer A 280/120=2.333333 x should be applied to which target amount for a matched forecast basis?","o":["Historical revenue 80","Forward revenue 96","Historical EBITDA 16","Target net cash 10"],"a":1},{"q":"Two peers have growth 20 percent and 30 percent but different loss/margin/risk. Does dividing multiples by growth give a unique justified fair price?","o":["Yes; higher growth always deserves linear value","Yes; percent versus decimal does not change quotient","No; fundamentals and units remain material","No; revenue can never be used for loss firms"],"a":2},{"q":"A 5 x control transaction includes buyer-specific synergy. What is the clean comparison?","o":["Separate until control/synergy/period reconciled","Average 5 x with trading multiples unadjusted","Apply 5 x to target equity not revenue","Treat 5 x as universal control premium"],"a":0},{"q":"A marketplace reports gross billings unlike matched net-service-revenue peers. What follows?","o":["Include because all currency units match","Divide by users instead to avoid reconciliation","Reconcile recognition before including","Treat gross billings as automatically twice EV"],"a":2}]},"103":{"objectives":["Build a fictional sum-of-parts equity bridge that distinguishes an industrial enterprise value from a bank equity value, proportionate ownership, noncontrolling interests and parent-only debt/costs without double deductions.","Build a finite-horizon APV bridge with explicit tax capacity, shield-risk discounting and financing costs; test when financing reduces value.","Reconcile fictional LBO sources and uses, annual interest/cash-tax/debt sweeps, equity exit proceeds and holding-period returns under a lower exit multiple."],"videos":[{"id":"LVMLs2z1JYg","title":"Basic leveraged buyout (LBO) | Stocks and bonds | Finance & Capital Markets | Khan Academy","by":"Khan Academy","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Illustrates leveraged acquisition, not sum-of-parts bank/consumer valuation. Its tax-deductibility, stability and net-income-as-return simplifications are not current Indian law or freecash/repayment proof; use the written sourced SOTP case."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran - financial-service equity valuation, selected sections","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/%7Eadamodar/pdfiles/papers/finfirm.pdf","note":"Focus on equity versus enterprise value, financial-service funding as an operating input, and capital/reinvestment constraints. Historical sector and regulatory examples are not current Indian rules."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran - APV mechanics and financing-cost warning","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/New_Home_Page/valquestions/apv.htm","note":"Read the APV structure: unlevered operating value plus financing benefits minus financing costs. Perpetual tax shields are conditional; historical rates, default tables and company examples are not current inputs. The finite two-year case is separately authored."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"BIWS - simple LBO sources, cash and equity exit","url":"https://breakingintowallstreet.com/kb/leveraged-buyouts-and-lbo-models/simple-lbo-model-excel/","note":"Read the paper-model structure and EV-to-equity exit bridge. Warning: the printed 140 subtotal after subtracting depreciation and interest is EBT, not EBIT. Our three-year repayment case has different authored assumptions; no linked workbook execution is claimed."}],"exercise":"Private fictional conglomerate snapshot, crore rupees, no real company or trade. Parent owns 80 percent of consumer business and 60 percent of a bank. All values same date/currency and supplied as analytical assumptions, not live market evidence. No leases, preference shares, pension deficits, intercompany loans, double-counted cross-holdings, taxes, transaction fees or other claims by assumption. Consumer annual normalized EBITDA 30 includes recurring share costs, excludes depreciation/interest/tax; assumed matched industrial EV/EBITDA 10 x gives 100 percent enterprise value 300. Consumer external debt 60 and unrestricted nonoperating cash 10 are entirely subsidiary-level; consumer 100 percent equity 300-60+10=250. Parent's 80 percent share=200, not 80 percent of EV without a debt bridge. The 20 percent noncontrolling consumer equity share is 50.\nBank 100 percent common-equity book value 50, stipulated comparable P/B 1.8 x=>common-equity value 90. Parent's 60 percent stake 54, other shareholders 36. The bank's fictional operating deposit/other financial funding liabilities 500 are already reflected in its net assets/equity and equity-multiple valuation, not another deduction from 90 or parent debt. Do not interpret this as permission to ignore asset quality, regulatory capital, deposit obligations or bank debt in a real review. Equity book value is not available distributable cash. Bank loan book quality/capital adequacy/franchise/ROE/current applicable rules need evidence; neither 1.8 x nor book value 50 is verified fair value. No one industrial EBITDA multiple is imposed on bank net interest operations.\nParent-only corporate overhead has stated present value 20 already including any stipulated tax effect; it has not been included in either subsidiary EBITDA/bank earnings/value. Parent-only external debt 40 and excess unrestricted cash 6, no subsidiary balances included. Sum parent interests 200+54=254, less overhead PV 20, parent debt 40, plus parent cash 6 gives parent-equity estimate 200. Do not deduct subsidiary debt 60 again after consumer equity 250 is derived, or subtract bank liabilities 500 again, or apply ownership percentages twice. No holding-company discount unless supported and explicitly modeled. Parent-equity 200 is a valuation stock, not cash available to acquire shares, pay dividends or repay debt.\nIndependent full-consolidation crosscheck: full consumer equity 250+full bank equity 90=340; subtract noncontrolling equity 50+36=86=>254; then overhead 20/debt 40/cash 6=>200. Alternatively full operating EV 300 plus bank equity 90 less consumer net debt 50 gives 340 before minorities. Do not mix full operating figures with proportionate minorities or subtract minorities again from already proportionate interests. Consolidated statement minority book values are not automatically the same as these hypothetical minority market values; this exercise supplies its own values. An actual group with intercompany loans/cross-holdings/guarantees requires a reconciled claim register.\nCoherent downside, independent from base: consumer EBITDA 25/multiple 8 x=>EV 200; same consumer debt 60/cash 10=>equity 150/parent stake 120. Bank book equity declines 50 to 40 after a stipulated 10 asset-quality loss, alternative P/B 1.2 x=>equity 48/parent stake 28.8. Hold overhead PV 20/parent debt 40/cash 6 unchanged=>parent-equity 94.8. The loss 10 is not subtracted again from 48 or from 148.8 sum of interests. A changed multiple or book value needs source/date/definitions; no source example or historical banking law is adopted as current Indian rule. Deliver base/downside segment EV/equity/ownership/claim tables, full-consolidation crosscheck, source/assumption/change log and strongest objection. Assign fictional evidence/bank/consumer/cash/rights review owners with day 0/30/60/90 stop gates. No real acquisition, buyout, pledge, bank transfer, public valuation, contact, disclosure or spending.\n\n\n<section aria-label=\"Additional written practice\"><h3 style=\"font-size:17px;margin:22px 0 10px\">APV extension: finite tax shields, separate from the SOTP case</h3><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\">APV separates the operating value without debt from the present value of financing benefits and costs. Do not add debt proceeds as free operating value or use both an after-tax WACC benefit and the same tax shield again. This authored example uses crore rupees and stipulated classroom assumptions, not Indian tax advice. Unlevered operating value is 100 at time 0. Debt is 60 at fair value, pays 10% annual interest at the end of years 1 and 2, and repays all 60 at year 2. Tax deductions are fully usable at a stipulated 25% rate in those two years only. The tax-shield discount rate is stipulated as 10%, reflecting this example&#x27;s assumed shield risk. There are no tax benefits after year 2. Expected financing/distress costs already have a time-0 present value of 2; do not multiply that amount by a default probability again. No other financing effects or nonoperating cash exist.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\"><strong>Try first:</strong> (1) What is the annual tax shield and its two-year present value? (2) What are levered operating value and common equity? (3) Why is tax rate times debt not the answer here? (4) If only year 1&#x27;s deduction is usable, does the modeled financing benefit still exceed its cost?</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\"><strong>Worked check:</strong> annual interest is 60 x 10% = 6; annual shield is 6 x 25% = 1.5. Shield PV is 1.5/1.10 + 1.5/1.10^2 = 2.603306. Levered operating value is 100 + 2.603306 - 2 = 100.603306. Common equity is 100.603306 - 60 = 40.603306. Subtract the debt claim once; do not add its issuance proceeds to enterprise value. The 25% x 60 = 15 shortcut would assume a qualifying perpetual shield, which this explicitly finite case excludes. If only year 1 is usable, APV is 100 + 1.5/1.10 - 2 = 99.363636: the financing effects now reduce value by 0.636364. A different tax-capacity, repayment or shield-risk assumption requires a new schedule, not a cosmetic rate change. The linked Damodaran reading supplies the APV structure and warns against counting debt benefits without its costs; its historical rates and default table are not current inputs.</p><h3 style=\"font-size:17px;margin:22px 0 10px\">LBO extension: sources, cash repayment and equity exit</h3><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\">This independent fictional nonfinancial buyout is not the consumer/bank SOTP and does not reuse its claims. Entry enterprise value is 80, target debt and target cash are zero, fees are 2 and a minimum cash balance of 3 must be funded at closing. New debt provides 50, so sponsor equity is 35: sources 50 + 35 = uses 80 + 2 + 3 = 85. A purchase multiple is not sponsor equity. No real acquisition, borrowing or spending is authorized.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\">There are three complete operating years. Annual EBITDA stays 14, depreciation is 2, capital spending is 3 and the increase in working capital is 1. Cash interest is 10% of opening-year debt, paid at year end. Cash tax is 25% of positive EBT with no deferred tax, loss carryforward or other tax item in this toy. EBT equals EBITDA minus depreciation minus interest. Cash available for debt repayment equals net income plus depreciation minus capital spending minus the working-capital increase. All of it repays principal at year end, capped by outstanding debt. The minimum cash of 3 remains untouched, with no dividends, PIK, additional borrowing, amortization outside this sweep or exit fees.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\"><strong>Try first:</strong> prepare opening debt, interest, EBT, cash tax, net income, repayment cash and closing debt for each year. Then compute equity proceeds, MOIC and annual IRR for an end-year-3 exit at 6x EBITDA. Repeat only the exit multiple at 5x, leaving the operating/debt schedule unchanged. Explain why debt repayment does not guarantee a profitable equity exit.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\"><strong>Worked check:</strong> year 1 debt opens at 50; interest 5; EBT 7; cash tax 1.75; net income 5.25; repayment cash 5.25 + 2 - 3 - 1 = 3.25; debt closes at 46.75. Year 2 interest is 4.675, EBT 7.325, cash tax 1.83125, net income 5.49375, repayment 3.49375 and closing debt 43.25625. Year 3 interest is 4.325625, EBT 7.674375, cash tax 1.91859375, net income 5.75578125, repayment 3.75578125 and closing debt 39.50046875. Interest is an expense, principal repayment is not a second income-statement expense, and EBITDA is not cash available for repayment.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\">At 6x, exit EV is 14 x 6 = 84. Equity proceeds are 84 - 39.50046875 + 3 = 47.49953125. MOIC is 47.49953125/35 = 1.357129x. With only entry equity and the exit receipt, annual IRR is MOIC^(1/3) - 1 = 10.715160%. At 5x, EV is 70 and equity proceeds are 33.49953125, MOIC 0.957129x and annual IRR -1.449939%. Operating cash paid down debt in both cases, yet the lower exit price loses sponsor money. A full LBO also needs downside liquidity, covenants, refinancing, fees and tax evidence. The linked BIWS page supports the sources/debt/cash/exit bridge. Its printed subtotal after subtracting depreciation and interest is EBT, not EBIT; preserve that distinction in your own schedule.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\">These additional APV/LBO practice checks are self-assessed written work. They do not change the existing ten-item SOTP quiz, grant an account certification or prove that a learner can build an independent spreadsheet.</p></section>\n<p><b>Spreadsheet practice:</b> <a href=\"/practice/top1-d103-d117-practice.xlsx\" download><u>Download the editable APV, LBO and regression workbook (XLSX)</u></a>, or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d103-d117-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><u>open the five-sheet print view (PDF)</u></a>. Rebuild the case in your own blank file first, then use the worked formulas to check it. Yellow inputs support changed-case tests; the Practice sheet has blank response cells. Baseline, changed-input, zero-variance and funding-gap behavior were recalculated in LibreOffice. This is authored model execution, not observed learner transfer or proof of Excel/Google Sheets parity.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Consumer EBITDA 30 at industrial EV/EBITDA 10 x gives full enterprise value?","o":["250 crore","300 crore","240 crore","200 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Consumer full EV 300, debt 60 and cash 10 imply full equity?","o":["350 crore","230 crore","300 crore","250 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Parent owns 80 percent of consumer equity 250. What is parent stake value?","o":["200 crore","240 crore","250 crore","50 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Bank equity book value 50 at P/B 1.8 x gives what kind of value?","o":["90 crore enterprise value","550 crore full equity","90 crore full equity","40 crore full equity"],"a":2},{"q":"Parent 60 percent of bank common-equity value 90 gives stake?","o":["90 crore","54 crore","36 crore","30 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Should bank operating funding liabilities 500 be subtracted again from bank equity 90 in this defined model?","o":["No; already reflected in bank equity basis","Yes; equity multiple omits all bank liabilities","Yes; bank deposits are parent-only net debt","No; deposits never impose repayment obligations"],"a":0},{"q":"Parent interests 200+54, overhead PV 20, parent debt 40 and cash 6 imply parent equity?","o":["254 crore","260 crore","140 crore","200 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Full consumer equity 250+bank equity 90 less minority 50+36 produces what parent interests before parent-only adjustments?","o":["340 crore","168 crore","254 crore","200 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Downside consumer EV 200/debt 60/cash 10/parent 80 percent gives consumer stake?","o":["120 crore","160 crore","150 crore","200 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Downside interests 120+28.8, overhead 20, parent debt 40/cash 6 give parent equity?","o":["148.8 crore","104.8 crore","94.8 crore","84.8 crore"],"a":2}]},"104":{"objectives":["Model a fictional battery startup with conditional milestone and commercial success probabilities, external-round dilution and an optional investor follow-on; discount dated expected investor cash without treating an option or expected proceeds as certainty."],"videos":[{"id":"hkcoyEsQtEk","title":"Aswath Damodaran on Valuing Young Companies","by":"CFA UK","note":"Use the written Damodaran young-company readings and the explicit fictional decision tree for the calculations."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran - young-company claims and survival, selected sections","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/papers/younggrowth09.pdf","note":"Historical survival/targetreturn tables are not probabilities or market hurdles for this startup. Differences in claims, staged financing and failure risk require explicit modeling; no arbitrary extra failure penalty in both branches and discount rate."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic common-equity battery startup, crore rupees, no actual investment commitment, guarantee, term sheet or fundraising. Investor pays 2 at time 0 at pre-money 8/post 10, buys 20 percent of common equity. All 2 enters the company for future operations, no secondary cash-out. This is a stipulated price, not a derived fair valuation. No preference, liquidation waterfall, options, debt, fees, tax, distributions, extra dilution or salvage except as stated. Historical startup failure tables are not measured probabilities for this venture.\nAt time 1 a technical qualification succeeds with probability 40 percent; if it fails, the business closes and this investor receives 0, with no follow-on. Conditional on technical success, commercial success at time 3 has probability 50 percent; commercial failure pays 0 at time 3. Therefore unconditional probability of a positive exit is 0.40*0.50=20 percent, not 40,50 or 90 percent. Joint branches: technical fail 60 percent; technical pass/commercial fail 20 percent; technical pass/commercial pass 20 percent. No independent second technical trial or repeated double count of failure. Conditional success and exit values are assumptions, not measured forecasts, offtake agreements or proof technology can scale.\nOn technical success a time 1 primary common round raises 4 at pre 12/post 16. In the no-follow-on policy all 4 comes from other investors, so the original 20 percent retains 12/16=75 percent and becomes 15 percent, not 20 percent plus 25 percent. At time 3 commercial-success company-equity exit 80 gives this investor 0.15*80=12; every failed branch pays 0. The entire company's 80 is equity after all obligations and operating/funding uses in the model, not EV requiring another debt subtraction. The new 4 is company funding, not another cash outflow for the original investor under no-follow-on. Expected time 3 investor proceeds=.20*12=2.4. Expected gross proceeds divided by original cost 2=1.2 x, not realized MOIC, IRR, fund net return or certainty; failure probability remains 80 percent.\nUse a declared 20 percent annual effective discount rate for expected investor equity cash flows as an analytical hurdle, not a current market cost estimate. For this exercise the rate does not add a second explicit technical/commercial-failure penalty: failures already appear in branch cash. No claim all risk is removed or current probabilities are objective. No-follow-on NPV=-2+2.4/1.2^3=minus 0.611111. A positive expected undiscounted 1.2 x does not imply value above this stipulated time/risk hurdle, funding availability or permission to invest. Current price 2 can still be too high under these assumptions.\nAlternative optional investor follow-on, not a commitment: only after technical pass at time 1 the same investor supplies 1 of total round 4 and other investors 3, same pre 12/post 16. Prior 20 percent dilutes to 15 percent, new 1 buys 1/16=6.25 percent post, total 21.25 percent. Do not add 25 percent of round funding directly as company ownership or treat this as 25 percent of existing 15. Commercial success proceeds 80*.2125=17 at time 3; unconditional expected 3.4. Expected additional investor outflow time 1=.40*1=.4, not 1 in all branches and not a cash inflow. Time 0 policy NPV=-2-.4/1.2+3.4/1.2^3=minus 0.365741. Difference versus no-follow-on is plus 0.245370 in expected present value, but both policies remain negative from time 0 at price 2. The follow-on option alone after technical success may be attractive while the original investment is still unattractive. At time 1 conditional increment: pay 1 now for extra 5 at time 3 with 50 percent probability=>-1+(.5*5)/1.2^2=plus 0.736111. Do not confuse this conditional value with the unconditional time 0 increment, which is.4/1.2 times it.\nNo real obligation to exercise that option, and new evidence, consent, price/round terms, capital availability and actual risk can change it. A milestone certificate alone is not commercial success or owner approval. Private decision tree/timeline, ownership roll-forward, expected versus realized/conditional versus unconditional tables, sensitivity and strongest objection are required. Assign fictional technical/demand/finance/evidence/rights owners with day 0/30/60/90 stop gates; mark probability estimates as assumptions pending actual testing. Retain original/revised records. No real term sheet, order, financing, broker contact, data disclosure, share purchase or payment.<section aria-label=\"Native D104 practice\"><h3>Conditional milestones and optional follow-on</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d104-d105-stage-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable staged and mixed review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d104-d105-stage-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Time 0 cheque 2 at pre-money 8 buys 20%. Technical pass at year 1 is .4; conditional commercial pass at year 3 is .5, so positive exit is joint .2. Exhaustive branches are .6/.2/.2. Technical pass triggers total round 4 at pre-money 12: no follow-on leaves 15%; fund 1 WITHIN total 4 gives 21.25%, outside 3. Success-only common-equity exit 80 gives investor 12 or 17. At stipulated annual hurdle 20%, original no-follow-on NPV is -.611111 and conditional-follow-on policy NPV is -.365741. Positive conditional year-1 incremental value .736111 does not make either original investment positive; time-0 increment is .245370 after reach probability and timing. No claim that the option will be reached or funded.</p><p>Seventy-one baseline formula cells were independently recalculated. Controlled probability, follow-on, zero-entry, collection-delay, zero-interest, terminal-growth and startup changes were tested and restored; twelve invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and broken stage/terminal references were checked. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision when inputs change. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, empirical probabilities, full audiovisual review, actual financial authority, learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real investment, loan, records, contacts, spending or recording is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Investor 2 at pre 8/post 10 buys which initial common stake?","o":["25 percent","16.666667 percent","80 percent","20 percent"],"a":3},{"q":"Technical pass 40 percent, then conditional commercial pass 50 percent. Joint positive exit probability?","o":["20 percent","40 percent","50 percent","90 percent"],"a":0},{"q":"On success, external primary round 4 at pre 12/post 16 with no original-investor follow-on leaves prior 20 percent stake at?","o":["20 percent","25 percent","15 percent","45 percent"],"a":2},{"q":"At exit equity 80 and retained stake 15 percent, investor success-branch proceeds?","o":["16 crore","12 crore","80 crore","2.4 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Joint success 20 percent and success proceeds 12, all failures 0: expected time 3 proceeds?","o":["12 crore","4.8 crore","6 crore","2.4 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"What is no-follow-on NPV at declared 20 percent annual rate, cost 2 now and expected 2.4 at year 3?","o":["Plus 0.4 crore","Plus 0.611111 crore","Minus 0.611111 crore","Minus 2 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Conditional follow-on 1 of total primary round 4 at post 16 adds 6.25 percent to diluted prior 15 percent. Total ownership?","o":["21.25 percent","40 percent","16 percent","18.75 percent"],"a":0},{"q":"Follow-on 1 is paid at time 1 only when technical pass probability 40 percent. Expected time 1 investor outflow?","o":["1 crore","0.4 crore","4 crore","Minus 0.4 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Under optional policy cost 2 now/expected 0.4 at year 1/expected 3.4 proceeds year 3, NPV?","o":["Plus 0.245370 crore","Minus 0.611111 crore","Minus 0.365741 crore","Plus 0.736111 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"At time 1 after qualification, incremental pay 1 for extra 5 at year 3 with conditional 50 percent commercial success has value?","o":["Minus 0.365741 crore","Plus 0.245370 crore","Plus 1.5 crore","Plus 0.736111 crore"],"a":3}]},"105":{"objectives":["Diagnose actual attempted errors with fresh debt-maturity, terminal-reinvestment and staged-ownership cases; record provisional remediation and test delayed changed-context transfer rather than infer weakest skills from a day score."],"videos":[{"id":"hkcoyEsQtEk","title":"Aswath Damodaran on Valuing Young Companies","by":"CFA UK","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use the explicit written mixed cases and selected primary teaching."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran - staged claims and survival, selected sections","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/papers/younggrowth09.pdf","note":"Focus on the opening valuation sections and the venture-method critique. Historical percentages not startup probabilities; explicit synthetic tree and timing supplied."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran - terminal value slides","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/valonlineslides/session9.pdf","note":"Equal-return/growth statements need consistent next-period earnings; our case changes prior-year earnings basis explicitly. No market growth/WACC adopted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - retrieval and study","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Supports actual attempted retrieval/error review, not app mastery claims or item analytics inferred from a day aggregate."}],"exercise":"Private mixed review, all companies, prices, probabilities and roles fictional. Attempt without notes; log answer, error cause, corrected calculation, exact source/input, decision consequence and delayed new-context retest. Pick two remediation areas only from actual attempted errors and label sparse evidence provisional. Day score/watched percentage/completion timestamp does not identify item-level errors or prove measured weakest skills, mastery or product improvement. Do not sum the separate cases below.\nFresh debt case, crore, one year: opening cash 8 plus hypothetical new debt 12 pays long-lived expansion 16 at time 0, leaves 4. Annual EBIT 9/depreciation 2/WC increase 3/total paid interest 3/maintenance capex 1 gives pre-principal cash 9+2-3-3-1=4. EBIT cover 9/3=3 x, but the 12 new principal due at year-end gives endpoint 4+4-12=minus 4. A floor 2 needs 6 endpoint funding, not an exact intrayear maximum and not an authorized loan. Existing interest is already in total 3, do not subtract again; no tax/other flows. A contradictory collection delay 2 raises operating AR 2 and lowers cash 2 without changing revenue or EBIT; revised movement 2/endpoint minus 6/floor need 8, EBIT cover 3 x unchanged. Not a measured customer default or guaranteed rollover.\nFresh terminal case, crore: year 2 NOPAT 12, terminal begins year 3, matched nominal WACC 9 percent/growth 3 percent/ROC 9 percent, not market estimates. Next NOPAT 12.36/reinvestment rate one-third/reinvestment 4.12/FCFF 8.24. End-year 2 terminal 8.24/(.09-.03)=137.333333; time 0 PV 137.333333/1.09^2=115.590719. A separate g 5 percent sensitivity, same year 2 NOPAT 12/ROC 9/WACC 9, gives next NOPAT 12.6/reinvestment 7/FCFF 5.6/end-year 2 TV 140. Different terminal NOPAT growth can change terminal value slightly even with ROC=WACC when holding previous-period NOPAT fixed; the equal-value statement holds when next-period NOPAT is fixed consistently. Do not mechanically claim every g change is exactly irrelevant regardless of numerator timing. A quote 206 at time 2 that uses next NOPAT 12.36 without required reinvestment overstated the base case under these assumptions. Neither terminal PV nor terminal TV is the whole EV unless explicit-period cash flows and other claims are supplied.\nFresh startup common equity, crore: cheque 1/pre 4/post 5=>20 percent initial stake. At time 1 technical pass probability 50 percent, fail pays 0. On pass an outside primary round 3 at pre 9/post 12 dilutes old stake by 9/12, leaving 15 percent. Conditional on technical pass, year 3 commercial-success probability 40 percent; joint positive exit 20 percent. Equity exit 60 on success gives original investor 9; expected year 3 proceeds 1.8. No original-investor follow-on, extra claims, preferences, debt, tax or other flows. At stipulated 20 percent annual expected-cash hurdle, NPV=-1+1.8/1.2^3=plus 0.041667. This narrow synthetic positive NPV does not authorize buying or prove tech/demand; actual probabilities, illiquidity, rights and losses remain material. Contradictory packet lowers conditional commercial pass from 40 to 30 percent with all else unchanged=>joint 15 percent/expected 1.35/NPV minus 0.218750. Do not keep the old positive answer, add probabilities or treat 20 percent ownership as unchanged after dilution.\nDeliver separate debt/timing, terminal/reinvestment and probability/ownership/error-change tables, strongest objections and source/input registry. Use actual new mistakes to choose delayed changed-context tests, not reread identical answers with visible keys. Assign fictional evidence/cash/technical/demand/rights owners with day 0/30/60/90 review-stop gates. No real borrowing, share issue, trade, investment, collection outreach, capital order, pledge, public disclosure or spending. A mock sponsor accepting the analysis is not actual execution authority.<section aria-label=\"Native D105 practice\"><h3>Independent maturity, terminal and startup review</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d104-d105-stage-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable staged and mixed review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d104-d105-stage-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Separate debt case: opening 8 plus debt 12 less expansion 16 leaves 4; annual pre-principal cash is 4, interest cover 3x, maturity ending cash -4 and endpoint gap to floor 2 is 6. Separate collection delay 2 gives movement 2/end -6/gap 8, not an exact intrayear maximum. Terminal begins year 3: prior-year NOPAT 12, WACC 9%, growth 3%, ROC 9% gives next NOPAT 12.36, reinvestment 4.12, FCFF 8.24, end-year-2 TV 137.333333, time-0 PV 115.590719. Growth 5% holding prior NOPAT fixed gives TV 140; ROC=WACC invariance needs the same NEXT-period earnings basis. Separate startup cheque 1 at pre 4, technical .5, outside round 3 at pre 9 and conditional commercial .4 gives final ownership 15%, joint success .2, expected investor proceeds 1.8 and NPV +.041667. Lower conditional commercial .3 reverses NPV to -.218750. The cases are never added together.</p><p>Seventy-one baseline formula cells were independently recalculated. Controlled probability, follow-on, zero-entry, collection-delay, zero-interest, terminal-growth and startup changes were tested and restored; twelve invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and broken stage/terminal references were checked. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision when inputs change. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, empirical probabilities, full audiovisual review, actual financial authority, learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real investment, loan, records, contacts, spending or recording is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Fresh debt EBIT 9 and interest 3 give EBIT cover?","o":["3.666667 x","1.333333 x","3 x","0.333333 x"],"a":2},{"q":"Fresh debt post-expansion cash 4 plus movement 4 less bullet 12 yields endpoint?","o":["Minus 4 crore","Plus 8 crore","Minus 8 crore","Plus 4 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Collection delay 2 with EBIT unchanged affects endpoint minus 4 and EBIT cover 3 x how?","o":["Endpoint minus 6; cover 2.333333 x","Endpoint minus 2; cover 3 x","Endpoint minus 4; cover 3 x","Endpoint minus 6; cover 3 x"],"a":3},{"q":"Terminalnext NOPAT 12.36, g 3 percent/ROC 9 percent: reinvestment and FCFF?","o":["0 and 12.36 crore","4.12 and 8.24 crore","3 and 9.36 crore","8.24 and 4.12 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Terminal 8.24 at year 3, WACC 9 percent/g 3 percent, gives TV at end-year 2?","o":["206 crore","91.555556 crore","137.333333 crore","115.590719 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Technical pass 50 percent and conditional commercial pass 40 percent imply joint success?","o":["50 percent","40 percent","90 percent","20 percent"],"a":3},{"q":"Original 20 percent stake is diluted by outside primary round 3 at pre 9/post 12. New stake?","o":["20 percent","15 percent","25 percent","45 percent"],"a":1},{"q":"At joint success 20 percent/retained 15 percent/equity exit 60, expected year 3 original investorproceeds?","o":["1.8 crore","9 crore","12 crore","3 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Conditional commercial success falls 40 to 30 percent, technical pass 50 percent, same 9 success proceeds. Expected proceeds?","o":["1.8 crore","2.7 crore","1.35 crore","4.5 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Only day score/timestamp exists before new attempt. How should remediation areas be chosen?","o":["From actual new errors, provisional when sparse","Infer measured weakest items from aggregate score","Choose identical numbers then count confidence","Treat watched percentage as per-item answer history"],"a":0}]},"106":{"objectives":["Solve a temporary transport-cost supply shift using explicit inverse curves; separate consumer and producer surplus, changed resource costs, and rationing losses at a binding price ceiling."],"videos":[{"id":"kIFBaaPJUO0","title":"Supply and Demand in 8 Minutes","by":"Jacob Clifford","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Shifts and equilibrium fit; numerical welfare and rationing need written sources. Do not assume every real unregulated market clears instantly."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - supply and demand shifts","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-microeconomics-3e/pages/3-2-shifts-in-demand-and-supply-for-goods-and-services","note":"Input-cost shock shifts supply; own-price movement does not shift demand. Cost-plus wording is not proof of pricing power."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - surplus and efficiency","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/3-5-demand-supply-and-efficiency","note":"Competitive private-surplus model requires stated assumptions; ceiling consumer surplus depends on rationing. No current Indian rule or market input adopted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT OCW - Lecture 9 welfare summary","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/14-01-principles-of-microeconomics-fall-2023/mit14_01_f23_lec9.pdf","note":"Use surplus areas and a matched feasible-cost benchmark; do not adopt unconditional elasticity-surplus or universal efficiency claims."}],"exercise":"Private fictional onion market for one day, not actual Indian prices, data, transport contract or policy. Let P be rupees/kg and Q be thousand kg/day. Inverse demand P=80-Q; original delivered marginal-cost supply P=20+Q. Assume competitive price-taking, no external costs, tax, market power, inventory, income change or other demand shift; curves apply only to the relevant positive range. P times Q and surplus areas are thousand rupees/day, not crore. Original equality gives Q 30/P 50; CS=0.5*(80-50)*30=450, PS=0.5*(50-20)*30=450, total 900.\nA temporary real transport resource cost of 20 rupees/kg raises delivered supply to P=40+Q, with demand unchanged. New Q 20/P 60; CS 200, PS 200, total 400. Consumers move along demand, not shift demand merely because price rose. The 500 decline in total surplus versus the old technology includes 400 extra resource cost on the 20 still traded plus 100 forgone old-cost gains on units 20 to 30. It is not all a policy deadweight-loss triangle: with the new cost, Q 20 remains efficient under the stipulated model. No carrier/tax recipient captures a transfer in this real-resource-cost example.\nSeparate hypothetical ceiling P 50 under shocked supply: Qd 30/Qs 10, shortage 20, actual traded 10 only if sellers supply that amount. Assume for one benchmark that the 10 units reach the highest willingness-to-pay buyers without queues, evasion or rationing cost. Then CS=integral from 0 to 10 of(80-Q-50)=250; PS=0.5*(50-40)*10=50; total 300, deadweight loss 100 relative to shocked efficient total 400. Random rationing or costly queues can change consumer surplus; quantity/price alone do not identify who gets the units. No claim about actual law or welfare of any current control.\nIndependent partial-recovery scenario, no ceiling: transport increment falls 20 to 10, so supply P=30+Q, Q 25/P 55; CS 312.5/PS 312.5/total 625. Improvement 225 versus shock 400 includes 200 resource saving on former 20 units and 25 restored gains on extra 5. Do not use original 900 as the remaining feasible welfare benchmark or treat all surplus as producer accounting profit/cash.\nDeliver before/shock/ceiling/recovery equations, quantities, price, units and separate surplus tables; draw labeled inverse curves with quantity horizontal/price vertical and distinguish source text from your own synthetic diagram. Explain which facts would reject transport-only causality: income, harvest, expectations or demand may also change. Identify fictional evidence/transport owners and day 0/day 1/day 7 review-stop gates, then test changed inputs without notes. No real price control, shipment, purchase, payment, contact or public claim is authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D106 practice\"><h3>Inverse curves, surplus and conditional ceiling allocation</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d106-d107-market-response.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable market and response review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d106-d107-market-response.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>One-day fictional onion market: demand P=80-Q, original supply P=20+Q; Q thousand kg/day and P rupees/kg, areas thousand rupees/day. Old Q 30/P 50 gives CS 450/PS 450/total 900. A real resource transport increment 20 shifts supply to 40+Q, Q 20/P 60/CS 200/PS 200/total 400. Loss 500 is 400 resource cost on surviving units plus 100 lost old-cost gains, not all policy deadweight loss. Ceiling 50 under shocked supply gives Qd 30/Qs 10/shortage 20. Only under highest-WTP allocation and no rationing costs, traded 10 gives CS 250/PS 50/total 300, loss 100 versus shocked efficient 400. Independent recovery increment 10 gives Q 25/P 55/CS 312.5/PS 312.5/total 625; gain 225=200 savings+25 restored gains. The editable inverse-curve chart is synthetic, not a market observation or source image.</p><p>All 156 baseline formula cells, including curve coordinates, were independently recalculated. Coherent shock/ceiling, recovery, segment-response and cost scenarios were tested and restored. Twelve invalid inputs, independent-case isolation and a broken welfare reference were checked. Unchanged fare leaves elasticity undefined while contribution still computes; nonpositive new margin blocks the stated positive-margin threshold. Nonbinding/out-of-domain ceilings and reversed recovery decomposition are not silently labeled valid. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision when inputs change. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, measured causal demand, real allocation/law/authority, full audiovisual or learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real fare, price control, shipment, participant, data disclosure, contact or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Original P=80-Q and P=20+Q. Equilibrium quantity and price?","o":["Q 50; P 30","Q 20; P 60","Q 30; P 50","Q 40; P 40"],"a":2},{"q":"Transport adds 20 rupees/kg to supply P=20+Q, demand unchanged. New equilibrium?","o":["Q 20; P 60","Q 30; P 50","Q 40; P 40","Q 60; P 20"],"a":0},{"q":"At shocked Q 20/P 60, demand intercept 80. Consumer surplus in thousand rupees/day?","o":["400","600","1200","200"],"a":3},{"q":"At shocked P 60/Q 20 and supply intercept 40, producer surplus?","o":["400 thousand rupees/day","200 thousand rupees/day","1200 thousand rupees/day","800 thousand rupees/day"],"a":1},{"q":"Old total 900 becomes 400 after a real transport-cost shock. Which decomposition matches?","o":["500 transfer to a tax collector plus 0 cost","100 extra resource cost plus 400 forgone gains","400 extra resource cost plus 100 forgone gains","500 policy deadweight loss plus 0 resource cost"],"a":2},{"q":"With shocked supply P=40+Q and ceiling P 50, shortage is?","o":["20 thousand kg/day","10 thousand kg/day","30 thousand kg/day","40 thousand kg/day"],"a":0},{"q":"Ceiling trades 10 units to highest-WTP buyers without queues. CS and PS in thousand rupees/day?","o":["CS 200; PS 200","CS 250; PS 50","CS 450; PS 50","CS 300; PS 0"],"a":1},{"q":"Ceiling total 300 versus shocked efficient 400 gives incremental deadweight loss?","o":["600 thousand rupees/day","500 thousand rupees/day","200 thousand rupees/day","100 thousand rupees/day"],"a":3},{"q":"Recovery supply P=30+Q against demand 80-Q, with no ceiling. Equilibrium and total surplus?","o":["Q 25/P 55; total 625","Q 20/P 60; total 400","Q 30/P 50; total 900","Q 25/P 55; total 312.5"],"a":0},{"q":"Only ceiling price 50 and traded quantity 10 are known; allocation and queue costs are not. Is CS 250 identified?","o":["Yes; any allocation gives identical CS 250","Yes; shortage 20 fixes CS 250 uniquely","No;250 requires efficient costless rationing","No; CS must be exactly 0 under any ceiling"],"a":2}]},"107":{"objectives":["Compute midpoint elasticities by rider segment and reconcile a fare change to total contribution after quantity response and policy cost; distinguish a descriptive arc from causal or constant-elasticity evidence."],"videos":[{"id":"B91INrZpAoU","title":"Elasticity and Consumer Choice","by":"Pacific States University","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Midpoint and nonconstant straight-line elasticity fit. Necessity/medical elasticities, exact textbook estimates, universal utility and full tax pass-through claims not adopted."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - price elasticity and midpoint method","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-microeconomics-3e/pages/5-1-price-elasticity-of-demand-and-price-elasticity-of-supply","note":"Signed ratio versus reported magnitude explicit. Arc elasticity not a universal forecasting constant or causal proof."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - total, average and marginal cost","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-microeconomics-3e/pages/7-3-costs-in-the-short-run","note":"Contribution after stipulated variable/policy cost differs from revenue. Source first-unit MC/table wording not adopted unqualified."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT OCW - welfare and market shifts","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/14-01-principles-of-microeconomics-fall-2023/mit14_01_f23_lec9.pdf","note":"Demand and supply can both shift; observed fares and completed rides need causal identification and matched service conditions."}],"exercise":"Private fictional one-hour ride-service simulation, rupees and completed trips, not actual platform take rates or permission to change a fare. To avoid confusing gross bookings with app revenue, assume this toy operator keeps the full customer fare and pays all driver/service costs as variable cost; this is NOT a statement about a real platform accounting model. At fare 200, segment A 1000 andB 1000 completed trips, total 2000. Stipulated cost 150 per trip includes driver/service payout and all other quantity-dependent costs. Unchanged fixed cost cancels in incremental comparison. Baseline revenue 400000, variable cost 300000, contribution 100000.\nAt fare 220, matched stated responses A 900/B 600, total 1500; same cost 150 per trip plus avoidable one-hour policy cost 8000. Revenue 330000, variable cost 225000, contribution before policy 105000 and after policy 97000. Revenue falls 70000 but contribution before policy rises 5000; after policy change is minus 3000. Higher margin 70 per ride is not enough by itself to approve the policy. No taxes, financing or working-capital effects in this toy comparison; this is not complete company net profit.\nCompute signed midpoint arcs using change/average quantity divided by change/average fare: price change 20/210=2/21. A quantity change-100/950 gives elasticity-21/19=-1.105263; B change-400/800 gives-21/4=-5.25; total change-500/1750 gives-3. Report magnitudes separately if desired. Total magnitude 3 is not the simple mean of 1.105263 and 5.25; midpoint quantity weights 950/1750 and 800/1750 reconcile it. Do not substitute old-base percentage responses, mix baseline and midpoint denominators, or treat an arc estimate as invariant beyond these two stipulated points.\nAt 220/cost 150/policy 8000, preserving baseline 100000 needs total Q=(100000+8000)/70=1542.857143 expected trips, or at least 1543 whole trips. If A stays 900, B needs 642.857143 expected trips or 643 whole. This threshold does not prove demand can be acquired at no extra cost. Separate downside: cost rises to 160 only for the new policy, with same 1500 trips/8000 policy cost. New contribution 82000, minus 18000 versus the original 100000 baseline. Do not quietly reprice baseline costs or count policy cost twice.\nDeliver segmented response, midpoint and total-margin bridge with units, sensitivity and a falsifiable demand/supply account. Observed peak rides can be supply constrained or affected by wait times, weather and rider mix; two observed before/after points alone do not establish causal rider elasticity. State matched service conditions/randomized or other credible identification required before applying to actual choices, without claiming such a test has occurred. Fictional demand/cost/safety owners and day 0/day 7 review-stop gates; actual action needs owner approval, not synthetic board acceptance. No real fares, payout, dispatch, participant contact, experiment, data disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D107 practice\"><h3>Segmented midpoint response and contribution bridge</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d106-d107-market-response.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable market and response review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d106-d107-market-response.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Separate one-hour ride-service toy operator keeps full fare and pays all variable costs, not an actual platform accounting model. Fare 200->220, A 1000->900/B 1000->600: signed midpoint arcs A -21/19, B -21/4, total -3. Midpoint weights 950/1750 and 800/1750 reconcile total; simple mean is wrong. Old fare 200/cost 150/2000 trips gives contribution 100000. New 220/cost 150/1500 trips gives 105000 before policy 8000 and 97000 after, minus 3000. Required trips 1542.857143 expected or 1543 whole, B 642.857143/643 if A 900. Separate NEW-policy-only cost 160 gives 82000, minus 18000 versus unchanged old baseline. Higher unit margin alone does not justify a fare change; arc estimates are not causal or invariant forecasting parameters.</p><p>All 156 baseline formula cells, including curve coordinates, were independently recalculated. Coherent shock/ceiling, recovery, segment-response and cost scenarios were tested and restored. Twelve invalid inputs, independent-case isolation and a broken welfare reference were checked. Unchanged fare leaves elasticity undefined while contribution still computes; nonpositive new margin blocks the stated positive-margin threshold. Nonbinding/out-of-domain ceilings and reversed recovery decomposition are not silently labeled valid. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision when inputs change. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, measured causal demand, real allocation/law/authority, full audiovisual or learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real fare, price control, shipment, participant, data disclosure, contact or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Toy service fare 200,2000 trips,cost 150 per trip. Baseline contribution before unchanged fixed costs?","o":["400000 rupees","100000 rupees","300000 rupees","50000 rupees"],"a":1},{"q":"New fare 220,1500 trips,cost 150 and incremental policy cost 8000. Contribution?","o":["105000 rupees","330000 rupees","82000 rupees","97000 rupees"],"a":3},{"q":"Original contribution 100000; new before policy 105000 and policy cost 8000. Incremental contribution?","o":["Minus 3000 rupees","Plus 5000 rupees","Minus 70000 rupees","Plus 97000 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"Fare 200 to 220, A 1000 to 900. Signed midpoint elasticity?","o":["-1.000000","-0.904762","-1.105263","-5.250000"],"a":2},{"q":"Fare 200 to 220, B 1000 to 600. Signed midpoint elasticity?","o":["-4.00","-5.25","-3.00","-1.105263"],"a":1},{"q":"Fare 200 to 220,total 2000 to 1500. Signed aggregate midpoint arc?","o":["-3.00","-2.50","-3.177632","-5.25"],"a":0},{"q":"At 220,cost 150,policy 8000, baseline target 100000. Minimum whole trips to preserve contribution?","o":["1429 trips","1500 trips","1662 trips","1543 trips"],"a":3},{"q":"Total threshold 1542.857143 expected trips, A 900. Minimum whole B trips?","o":["600 trips","642 trips","643 trips","1000 trips"],"a":2},{"q":"Only new-policy cost rises 150 to 160,1500 trips/fare 220/policy 8000; baseline remains 100000. New contribution/change?","o":["82000; minus 18000 rupees","90000; minus 10000 rupees","97000; minus 3000 rupees","82000; minus 8000 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"Observed peak fares and completed rides changed; waiting time and driver availability also changed. What is supported?","o":["Causal demand elasticity if two totals are exact","Descriptive arc, not isolated causal rider elasticity","Constant elasticity for all rider segments","Unchanged supply whenever fares rise"],"a":1}]},"108":{"objectives":["Compare cement-plant capacity plans using feasible output, marginal versus average cost and annual contribution thresholds; reject pricing power or investment approval inferred only from lower unit cost."],"videos":[{"id":"JdCgu1sOPDo","title":"Economies of Scale and Long-Run Costs- Micro Topic 3.3","by":"Jacob Clifford","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - short-run costs","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-microeconomics-3e/pages/7-3-costs-in-the-short-run","note":"Total versus average versus marginal cost, fixed costs not automatically sunk."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - long-run costs and scale","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-microeconomics-3e/pages/7-5-costs-in-the-long-run","note":"Capacity choice differs from spreading fixed costs in one existing plant. Historical market examples not current Indian evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - quantity responses","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-microeconomics-3e/pages/5-1-price-elasticity-of-demand-and-price-elasticity-of-supply","note":"Cost reduction alone does not fix market price; separate price/volume response scenario required."}],"exercise":"Private fictional annual cement-plant planning case, rupees/tonne and annual tonnes. Use million rupees for total money. No actual Indian cement prices, demand, cartel behavior, regulations, tax, capital order or funding terms. Current capacity 100000 tonnes, output 80000, price 5000/tonne, variable cost 3000/tonne and annual fixed operating cost 120 million. Baseline utilization 80 percent, revenue 400 million, variable 240 million, total 360 million, operating contribution after these fixed costs 40 million; average cost 4500/tonne and marginal production cost 3000 within this constant-cost range. Fixed 120 is not assumed recoverable or sunk outside the stated comparison.\nAlternative expanded configuration, if privately hypothetically chosen: capacity 150000, fixed annual operating cost 180 million and variable cost 2800/tonne. At unchanged 80000 sales, utilization 53.333333 percent, total cost 404 million/average 5050; at price 5000 revenue 400 million/operating result minus 4 million, change minus 44 million. Lower marginal 2800 does not mean lower average cost at that output.\nSeparate demand case sells 120000 at unchanged 5000: utilization 80 percent, variable 336 million/total 516 million/average 4300, revenue 600 million/operating result 84 million, improvement 44 million versus baseline 40. These are different outputs, not same-output cost savings. For outputs feasible in both configurations, alternative-minus-current total cost=60 million-200 Q. Cost equality would require 300000 tonnes, above both capacities; expanded is not cheaper at any common feasible Q under these assumptions. Additional volume beyond old capacity may still create value, but volume is not guaranteed.\nAt price 5000 expanded annual operating result 2200 Q-180 million equals baseline 40 million atQ 100000, or 66.666667 percent expanded utilization. Zero-result threshold 180 million/2200=81818.181818 tonnes or 54.545455 percent utilization is a different hurdle. Whole-tonne minimums 100000 and 81819 respectively. Neither is full project NPV because initial capex, taxes, working capital, funding, duration and residual value are absent; do not treat the annual fixed cost as the initial investment or execute any spend.\nIndependent competitor-response scenario price 4600 at 120000 tonnes, same expanded costs: revenue 552 million/operating result 36 million, below baseline 40 by 4 million despite average 4300. Break-even versus baseline at this price requiresQ 220 million/1800=122222.222222 tonnes, or 81.481481 percent expanded utilization, minimum 122223 whole tonnes. This is a stipulated sensitivity, not evidence competitors will quote 4600 or permission to coordinate prices. Lower cost and concentration alone do not prove pricing power or a safe real investment.\nDeliver configuration/output/price/cost/utilization and threshold tables; separate one-plant fixed-cost absorption from a fully optimized long-run scale claim. Supply actual capex/timing and demand evidence before investment recommendation, with fictional engineering/demand/cash/legal owners and day 0/day 30 review-stop gates. No plant order, finance, real customer/competitor contact, disclosure, collusion or payment authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D108 practice\"><h3>Capacity, annual cost and feasible output thresholds</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d108-d109-capacity-selection.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable capacity and winner-selection review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d108-d109-capacity-selection.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Fictional annual cement configuration: current capacity 100000/output 80000/price 5000/variable 3000/fixed 120 million gives result 40 million. Expanded capacity 150000/variable 2800/fixed 180 million gives minus 4 million at the same 80000 sales, 84 million at hypothetical 120000 sales, or 36 million at response price 4600. Average 4500 at 80000 versus 4300 at 120000 is not a same-output saving. Same-output cost equality 300000 is outside common capacity 100000; expanded cost is higher throughout the feasible common range. Expanded matches current result at 100000 tonnes at base price, 122222.222222 expected or 122223 whole tonnes at response price; these requirements do not establish demand. Zero-output average cost and nonpositive-unit-margin thresholds are undefined. This annual operating model is not project NPV: capex, tax, working capital, financing, lifetime and residual are absent.</p><p>All 145 baseline formula cells were independently recalculated. Thirteen changed scenario sets plus restoration cover response price, capacity equality/infeasibility, equal costs, zero margin/output, changed synergy/bids/prior/correlation, high-only wins and no middle-interval wins. Twelve invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and broken plant-result/joint-probability references were checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after input changes. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, real demand/capex/valuation/legal evidence, full audiovisual or learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real plant expansion, auction application, deposit, financing, competitor contact, coordination, disclosure or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Current output 80000/capacity 100000, annual fixed 120 million, variable 3000/tonne. Utilization/average cost?","o":["80%;3000 rupees/tonne","53.333333%;4500 rupees/tonne","80%;360000000 rupees/tonne","80%;4500 rupees/tonne"],"a":3},{"q":"Expanded capacity 150000/fixed 180 million/variable 2800. At 80000 sales average cost?","o":["4300 rupees/tonne","5050 rupees/tonne","2800 rupees/tonne","4500 rupees/tonne"],"a":1},{"q":"Expanded 80000 sales at 5000, total cost 404 million. Operating result/change versus baseline 40 million?","o":["Minus 4 million; minus 44 million","Plus 40 million; zero change","Minus 4 million; minus 4 million","Plus 56 million; plus 16 million"],"a":0},{"q":"Expanded 120000 sales,150000 capacity, fixed 180 million/variable 2800,price 5000. Utilization/result?","o":["80%;264 million rupees","120%;84 million rupees","80%;84 million rupees","66.666667%;40 million rupees"],"a":2},{"q":"Expanded 120000 sales has average cost 4300 versus old 80000 sales average 4500. Is 200/tonne a same-output saving?","o":["Yes; multiply 200 by every possible output","Yes; fixed costs can be omitted at any output","No; average cost cannot fall as output rises","No; the averages use different output levels"],"a":3},{"q":"Expanded-minus-current total cost=60 million-200 Q. At what Q are costs equal?","o":["100000 tonnes, within both capacities","300000 tonnes, outside common capacity","120000 tonnes, within both capacities","150000 tonnes, within both capacities"],"a":1},{"q":"Expanded price 5000/variable 2800/fixed 180 million. Q to match original 40 million annual result?","o":["81818.181818 tonnes","80000 tonnes","100000 tonnes","150000 tonnes"],"a":2},{"q":"Expanded price 5000/variable 2800/fixed 180 million. Minimum whole tonnes for nonnegative annual operating result?","o":["81819 tonnes","81818 tonnes","100000 tonnes","120000 tonnes"],"a":0},{"q":"Response price 4600, expanded 120000 sales, variable 2800/fixed 180 million. Result versus original 40 million?","o":["84 million;44 million higher","36 million;4 million lower","552 million;512 million higher","40 million;unchanged"],"a":1},{"q":"At response price 4600,variable 2800/fixed 180 million, output needed to match baseline 40 million?","o":["100000 tonnes,66.666667%utilization","81818.181818 tonnes,54.545455%utilization","120000 tonnes,80%utilization","122222.222222 tonnes,81.481481%utilization"],"a":3}]},"109":{"objectives":["Condition common-value estimates on winning using an explicit joint state/rival-bid distribution; distinguish a break-even bid bound, downside limit and best supplied candidate from an optimal strategy or actual bid authority."],"videos":[{"id":"4UEweGvfCNA","title":"The Winners Curse in Game Theory / Economics","by":"Ashley Hodgson","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use the selected Yale transcript and explicit synthetic distribution."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Yale - common versus private values and winner selection","url":"https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-159/lecture-24","note":"Common-value selection updates value conditional on winning. Historical oil/baseball/IPO claims not adopted; no Indian spectrum auction rule verified."}],"exercise":"Private fictional one-shot first-price spectrum-style auction, all money crore measured at the same valuation date. Not an actual Indian spectrum lot, eligibility, legal rule, payment, commitment or instruction to bid. Two common-value states: low 80/high 160, each prior probability 0.5. Rival maximum bid R is 90 or 140: conditional on low value, P(R 90)=0.75/P(R 140)=0.25; on high value, P(R 90)=0.25/P(R 140)=0.75. Your only supplied candidate choices are bid 105,bid 120,or abstain. Winner pays own bid; winning requires strictly above R, no ties at supplied candidates. Thus either bid 105 or 120 wins exactly when R 90. For these two candidates P(win)=0.5; P(high AND win)=0.125; P(high|win)=0.25, not 0.5. Conditional common value 100 versus unconditional 120. Bid-independent private operating synergy 20 minus incremental integration 10 gives net private 10, realized only if won; these are present values, not annual cash flows added to un-discounted future amounts. Conditional total net value 110 versus unconditional 130. No losing bidder cost.\nAt bid 120, expected surplus conditional on win 110-120=minus 10; unconditional expected surplus 0.5*(-10)=minus 5. At bid 105, conditional surplus 5/unconditional 2.5; realized low state net value 90 gives loss 15, high state net value 170 gives profit 65. Low-state conditional probability 75 percent =>0.75*(-15)+0.25*65=5. Abstain gives 0. Among only supplied candidates,105 is better in expected surplus; this is not a globally optimal bid and not a guarantee against loss. The full distribution permits other prices, but they are not included in this candidate exercise.\nFor bids strictly between 90 and 140, the win event is unchanged under the supplied rival distribution. Conditional expected break-even bound 110; a separate fictional minimum expected winning surplus 5 requires bid at most 105. A separate worst-realized-loss limit 20 requires bid at most 110 since low state net value 90. Both tests together bound 105 within that interval. Outside the interval, recompute win probability and conditional value; do not carry 110 or 0.5 universally, use a blanket haircut, or call bid-shading a guaranteed profit rule.\nContradictory engineering evidence reduces synergy 20 to 10 only, integration stays 10; new conditional net value 100. At candidate 105 conditional surplus minus 5/unconditional minus 2.5; candidate 120 even worse, so abstain beats both supplied bids. New minimum-winning-surplus 5 bound 95 lies in the same interval but is not an additional approved candidate or actual instruction. Any speculative change to rival distribution needs a new joint model, not silent adjustment. Pure known private value would not acquire this common-value inference from rival estimates, although first-price strategic shading/uncertainty can remain.\nDeliver joint-probability, conditional-value, candidate-payoff and downside tables, named fictional engineering/valuation/auction evidence owners with day 0/day 7 stop-review gates. Private mock acceptance is not authority to bid, contact competitors, collude, apply, deposit funds, finance, sign or disclose. All actual action remains outside this exercise.<section aria-label=\"Native D109 practice\"><h3>Joint-state winner selection, candidates and conditional bounds</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d108-d109-capacity-selection.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable capacity and winner-selection review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d108-d109-capacity-selection.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Separate same-date fictional spectrum-style common-value auction, crore PV. Low/high values 80/160 with equal priors and conditional rival 90/140 probabilities .75/.25 and .25/.75 give joint masses .375/.125/.125/.375. Strict own bid greater than rival wins; ties lose. Both bids 105/120 win only rival 90 states: win probability .5, high-and-win .125, posterior high .25, conditional common value 100 rather than prior 120. Private synergy 20 minus integration 10 gives conditional net 110. Bid 105 has conditional surplus 5, expected 2.5 and worst winning loss 15; bid 120 gives minus 10/minus 5/loss 30. Abstain is explicit zero. Required conditional surplus 5 and maximum winning loss 20 yield combined upper bound 105, only in strict interval 90&lt;bid&lt;140. Recompute joint selection outside that interval; no-win conditional values are undefined, not zero. Positive-probability winning states alone determine worst loss. A bound is not a new supplied candidate or global optimum; expected gain does not remove possible loss or supply auction authority.</p><p>All 145 baseline formula cells were independently recalculated. Thirteen changed scenario sets plus restoration cover response price, capacity equality/infeasibility, equal costs, zero margin/output, changed synergy/bids/prior/correlation, high-only wins and no middle-interval wins. Twelve invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and broken plant-result/joint-probability references were checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after input changes. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, real demand/capex/valuation/legal evidence, full audiovisual or learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real plant expansion, auction application, deposit, financing, competitor contact, coordination, disclosure or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Prior low/high each 0.5; win conditional low 0.75/high 0.25. Overall winning probability for supplied 105/120 bids?","o":["0.25","0.50","0.75","1.00"],"a":1},{"q":"P(high AND win)=0.125 and P(win)=0.5. Posterior high probability?","o":["0.125","0.50","0.75","0.25"],"a":3},{"q":"Common values 80/160, posterior high 0.25. Conditional common value?","o":["100 crore","120 crore","140 crore","80 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Conditional common 100 plus private synergy 20 less integration 10. Conditional net value?","o":["130 crore","120 crore","110 crore","90 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"At bid 120, conditional net value 110/P(win)0.5/no losing cost. Unconditional expected surplus?","o":["Minus 10 crore","Minus 5 crore","Plus 5 crore","Plus 10 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"At bid 105, low realized net value 90/high 170. Low-state winning loss?","o":["5 crore","25 crore","65 crore","15 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Candidate 105 gives conditional surplus 5 and Pwin 0.5; abstain 0. Expected surplus of 105?","o":["5 crore","52.5 crore","2.5 crore","Minus 2.5 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"For 90<bid<140 conditional net value 110. Required conditional surplus 5 and worst loss at most 20 with low net value 90. Joint bid ceiling?","o":["105 crore","110 crore","120 crore","130 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Synergy falls 20 to 10, integration 10 unchanged. Candidate 105 expected surplus with P(win) 0.5?","o":["Plus 2.5 crore","Minus 5 crore","Plus 7.5 crore","Minus 2.5 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Does unchanged P(win) 0.5/conditional value 110 apply to every possible bid?","o":["Yes; both were calculated exactly","No; outside 90 to 140 the win event changes","Yes; private synergy fixes the win event","No; every bid must have zero chance to win"],"a":1}]},"110":{"objectives":["Use exact nominal-to-real and population growth ratios, then build retailer demand from customers, transactions and basket economics; do not infer cohort sales, distribution or investment approval from GDP growth."],"videos":[{"id":"RDvMzWDzZkc","title":"Class 1, Part 1: Economic Growth Theory and the Direct Elements in Innovation","by":"MIT OpenCourseWare","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Broad innovation/growth not direct GDP deflation or retail demand. Historical biographies, deterministic bubbles/learning-time claims not adopted."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - nominal and real GDP","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-macroeconomics-3e/pages/6-2-adjusting-nominal-values-to-real-values","note":"Use an exact multiplicative growth ratio with a consistent base year. The source contains mixed dates and apparent 2005/2012 and 19/100 typos; use the written case rather than copying these. Subtracting inflation is only a small-change approximation."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - GDP per capita","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-macroeconomics-3e/pages/6-4-comparing-gdp-among-countries","note":"Population denominator and PPP versus market FX must match. Historical country rankings/9 x claim not current facts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - GDP and well-being","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-macroeconomics-3e/pages/6-5-how-well-gdp-measures-the-well-being-of-society","note":"GDP per capita is an average, not every household demand, inequality measure or store forecast. Historical US generalizations not adopted."}],"exercise":"Private fictional two-year annual comparison, no actual Indian GDP, CPI, population, retailer or policy. Economy nominal GDP 200 to 224 in consistent arbitrary currency units; GDP deflator 100 to 106 on one fixed base; population 100 to 102 in consistent units. Real GDP 200 to 224/1.06=211.320755, growth(1.12/1.06)-1=5.660377 percent. Exact real per capita growth 1.12/(1.06*1.02)-1=3.588605 percent. Nominal 12 minus deflator 6 minus population 2=4 percent is only rough subtraction, not exact; GDP deflator is not the retailer's basket inflation or household CPI. Real GDP per person is not disposable income, each household gain or addressable store demand. Do not mix PPP with market exchange rates or revisions/base years.\nSeparate retailer annual operational case uses actual synthetic unit drivers, NOT GDP as an elasticity. Old 10000 customers*4 transactions/customer=40000 baskets, average price 500/cost 350 per basket=>revenue 20000000 rupees/contribution 6000000. Proposed new 10200 customers*3.9 transactions=39780 baskets, price 520/cost 370=>revenue 20685600/contribution 5967000. Basket price rises 4 percent while units fall 0.55 percent, so nominal revenue grows 3.428 percent; contribution before new cost falls 33000. Add avoidable expansion operating cost 400000 per year, unchanged old fixed cost excluded from BOTH cases: new contribution 5567000, decline 433000 versus old 6000000. This is contribution after specified costs, not full net profit/project NPV/cash funding proof; capex, tax, timing and working capital absent.\nAt new margin 150 and expansion cost 400000, preserving old 6000000 needs 42666.666667 expected baskets or 42667 whole; with 10200 customers requires 4.183006536 expected transactions per customer, not 3.9. Extra volume may require cost/demand changes, not automatically free. Separate downside holds 3.9 transactions and 520/370 basket economics but only 9800 customers=>38220 baskets/revenue 19874400/contribution 5733000 before cost/5333000 after cost, decline 667000 versus baseline. Do not revise aggregate GDP to force this store case to pass.\nDeliver economy index/real/per capita reconciliation separately from customers-frequency-units-price-cost contribution bridge, sensitivity and exact denominators/time windows. Source registry labels invented inputs, GDP releases/revisions versus own cohort sales, employment, incomes, footfall and conversion evidence still needed. Fictional macro/cohort/cash owners with day 0/day 30 review-stop gate; a synthetic dashboard or mock sponsor is not actual expansion, vendor contact, data sharing, lease, financing or spending authority.<section aria-label=\"Native D110 practice\"><h3>Exact real growth and separate retailer demand bridge</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d110-d111-macro-lender.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable exact-growth and repricing review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d110-d111-macro-lender.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Economy nominal GDP 200 to 224, matched deflator 100 to 106, population 100 to 102: real GDP 211.320755, growth 5.660377%, exact real per capita growth 3.588605%, not approximate subtraction 4%. A common deflator-base change preserves growth, not level units. Separately, retailer customers 10000 to 10200, frequency 4 to 3.9, basket price 500 to 520/cost 350 to 370, new avoidable cost 400000: baskets 40000 to 39780, revenue 20000000 to 20685600, after-cost contribution 6000000 to 5567000. Downside customers 9800 gives 5333000. New positive margin 150 needs 42666.666667 expected or 42667 whole baskets to match old contribution, not evidence of available demand. Zero/negative margins block the positive-margin threshold; zero customers makes required frequency undefined. GDP average output is not each household income, customer forecast or store elasticity. Capex, tax, working capital and cash timing absent, not full profit/funding/NPV.</p><p>All 169 baseline formula cells were independently recalculated. Nineteen changed scenario sets plus restoration cover matched deflator base/population, zero nominal output, store frequency/price/cost/customer/margin, loan/funding timing, pass-through, signed/zero shock, contract balance and credit expense. Fifteen invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and three broken result references were checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after input changes. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, real macro/cohort/contracts/credit/legal evidence, full audiovisual or learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real store expansion, lease, lending, deposits, repricing, derivatives, borrower/vendor contact, disclosure or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Nominal GDP 200 to 224,deflator 100 to 106. New real GDP on original base?","o":["224.000000","237.440000","211.320755","218.000000"],"a":2},{"q":"Nominal growth 12%,deflator growth 6%. Exact real GDP growth?","o":["5.660377%","6.000000%","18.000000%","12.000000%"],"a":0},{"q":"Nominal growth 12%,deflator 6%,population 2%. Exact real per capita growth?","o":["4.000000%","5.660377%","9.803922%","3.588605%"],"a":3},{"q":"Retail customers 10200*3.9 transactions per year. New baskets?","o":["40800","39780","40000","38220"],"a":1},{"q":"New 39780 baskets at price 520. Revenue?","o":["20000000 rupees","21216000 rupees","20685600 rupees","19874400 rupees"],"a":2},{"q":"New 39780 baskets,price 520,cost 370; expansion cost 400000. Contribution after these costs?","o":["5567000 rupees","5967000 rupees","6000000 rupees","5333000 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"Old contribution 6000000, new after expansion cost 5567000. Incremental contribution?","o":["Minus 33000 rupees","Plus 685600 rupees","Minus 400000 rupees","Minus 433000 rupees"],"a":3},{"q":"Margin 150/basket,expansion cost 400000,target old 6000000. Minimum whole baskets?","o":["40000","42667","42666","39780"],"a":1},{"q":"Downside 9800 customers*3.9 transactions,margin 150,expansion cost 400000. Contribution?","o":["5333000 rupees","5733000 rupees","5567000 rupees","5967000 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"Exact real GDP per capita grew 3.588605%. What follows about this retailer?","o":["Every customer buys 3.588605%more baskets","Basket price must match GDP deflator 6%","No unique sales forecast without cohort evidence","Store contribution must grow 3.588605%"],"a":2}]},"111":{"objectives":["Time-weight asset and funding repricing in a static lender stress, calculate NII and NIM on average earning assets, and keep incremental credit-loss expense separate from interest margin, liquidity and economic value."],"videos":[{"id":"IMkLAP_aj3I","title":"Monetary Policy explained","by":"Sim Institute","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. General monetarypolicy title is not detailed lender repricing or credit loss teaching; use explicit written sources."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"FDIC - interest-rate risk methods, selected sections","url":"https://www.fdic.gov/risk-management-manual-examination-policies/section-7-1.pdf","note":"Read the introduction and types of interest-rate risk, then the gap, earnings-simulation and economic-value sections. Match time horizon and balance-sheet assumptions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"FDIC2024 - NIM glossary","url":"https://www.fdic.gov/analysis/risk-review/2024-risk-review/2024-risk-review-glossary.pdf","note":"NIM uses average earning assets, here no tax-equivalent adjustments or dividends. US nonaccrual/default definitions not adopted as Indian rules."}],"exercise":"Private fictional lender, crore, exactly one year split into two equal half-years with simple annual interest. No actual loan, rate, regulation, hedge or borrower contact. Static constant earning assets 1000 all loans at 10 percent, liabilities 800 at 6 percent, equity 200; no new growth, repayments, capitalization, fees, tax, nonaccrual, compounding or dividend. Earning asset average 1000; total assets also 1000 in this simplified case but NIM definition remains earning assets, not funding 800. Baseline interest income 100/funding expense 48/NII 52/NIM 5.2 percent. Separate baseline credit loss expense 5 and operating expense 20 give pretax modeled result 27; this case stipulates expense, not regulatory ECL, cash charge-off, provision stock or collateral valuation.\nA 200 basis point market shock occurs at start-year. Funding 400 reprices immediately by full 200 bp; other 400 fixed all year. Loans 600 reprice after exactly six months by only 150 bp, a 75 percent pass-through; other 400 fixed. First half interest income 50/funding expense 28/NII 22. Second half income 54.5/funding expense 28/NII 26.5. Annual income 104.5/expense 56/NII 48.5/NIM 4.85 percent, decline 3.5 crore or 35 bp, not 200 bp decline. Asset income change 600*.015*.5=4.5; liability expense change 400*.02*1=8; change in NII minus 3.5. No assumption loans/funding reprice at same time or beta.\nNaive total-year-repricing gap 600-400=+200 multiplied by 2 percent gives plus 4 NII; it is misleading here because asset timing and 150 bp versus 200 bp differ. Equal-year fully repriced run rate, assuming same balances/rates continue another fully specified year, income 109/expense 56/NII 53/NIM 5.3 percent; this is NOT first-year earned NII or a forecast including customer behavior. Short-term NII does not measure economic value of equity, refinancing cash requirement or complete solvency risk.\nSeparate credit downside raises annual stipulated credit loss expense 5 to 12, no additional nonaccrual/revenue change in this toy model. Shocked pretax result 48.5-20-12=16.5, decline 10.5 from 27:3.5 interest margin plus 7 credit expense. Credit loss expense is not deducted again inside NII/NIM; it is not automatically cash paid or a regulatory expected-loss model. If delinquency/nonaccrual affected interest income in reality, it needs an explicit separate bridge rather than hidden double counting.\nIndependent contract-evidence correction: only 300 rather than 600 loans reset at six months by 150 bp. Same 400 funding immediate+200 bp =>asset income increment 2.25/funding increment 8/NII 46.25/NIM 4.625 percent. With same stress loss 12/opex 20 pretax 14.25. Record exactly which contractual reset evidence changed, not a generic all-lines haircut. Deliver half-year/rate/balance/beta/time/NII/NIM/credit expense tables, alternative no-growth versus behavior assumptions, and fictional treasury/contracts/credit/cash owners with day 0/day 30 review-stop gates. No lending, deposits, repricing, refinance, derivatives, borrowing, actual financial disclosures or spending authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D111 practice\"><h3>Time-weighted repricing, NII and separate credit expense</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d110-d111-macro-lender.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable exact-growth and repricing review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d110-d111-macro-lender.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Separate one-year fictional lender, crore and simple annual interest: constant average earning assets 1000 at 10%, funding 800 at 6%, baseline NII 52/NIM 5.2%/pretax 27 after opex 20 and credit expense 5. Funding 400 resets+200 bp immediately; loans 600 reset+150 bp after half-year. Half-year income 50/54.5, expense 28/28, NII 22/26.5; first-year NII 48.5/NIM 4.85%, down 35 bp. Equal-year fully repriced NII 53 is not first-year earned income. Separate credit expense 12 gives pretax 16.5, down 10.5:3.5 margin and 7 credit, without double subtracting credit inside NII/NIM. Contract correction changes reset loans 600 to 300 only: asset increment 2.25, NII 46.25/NIM 4.625%, pretax 14.25. Arbitrary delays use active fractions separately in each half-year; signed shocks are permitted only when resulting rates stay nonnegative. Static balance gap times market shock misses differing timing/betas. No economic value of equity, liquidity, regulatory ECL, cash charge-off, behavior or real solvency inference.</p><p>All 169 baseline formula cells were independently recalculated. Nineteen changed scenario sets plus restoration cover matched deflator base/population, zero nominal output, store frequency/price/cost/customer/margin, loan/funding timing, pass-through, signed/zero shock, contract balance and credit expense. Fifteen invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and three broken result references were checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after input changes. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, real macro/cohort/contracts/credit/legal evidence, full audiovisual or learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real store expansion, lease, lending, deposits, repricing, derivatives, borrower/vendor contact, disclosure or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Earning assets 1000 at 10%,funding 800 at 6%. Baseline annual NII?","o":["100 crore","48 crore","6.5 crore","52 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Baseline NII 52/average earning assets 1000. NIM?","o":["6.5%","5.2%","10.0%","4.8%"],"a":1},{"q":"Loans 600 reset+150 bp only for second half. Annual incremental interest income?","o":["4.5 crore","9 crore","6 crore","12 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Funding 400 reset+200 bp immediately for full year. Incremental expense?","o":["4 crore","16 crore","8 crore","6 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Baseline NII 52,asset interest increment 4.5,funding increment 8. Shocked annual NII/NIM?","o":["56 crore;5.6%","53 crore;5.3%","44 crore;4.4%","48.5 crore;4.85%"],"a":3},{"q":"Shocked first half NII 22 and second half 26.5. Annual NII?","o":["44 crore","48.5 crore","53 crore","97 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"NIM 5.2%to 4.85%. Change expressed as basis points?","o":["Minus 35 bp","Minus 350 bp","Minus 3.5 bp","Minus 200 bp"],"a":0},{"q":"NII 48.5,opex 20,credit loss expense 12. Pretax result?","o":["23.5 crore","28.5 crore","16.5 crore","4.5 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Only 300 loans reset+150 bp for half-year instead of 600;funding increment 8/baseline NII 52. Corrected NII?","o":["48.5 crore","46.25 crore","53 crore","44 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Simple gap(600-400)*2%=+4 predicts NII gain. Why does it fail here?","o":["Credit lossexpense must be added to NII","Average earning assets must be replaced by funding","Full-year NII always equals second half annualized","Reset timing and pass-through differ by side"],"a":3}]},"112":{"objectives":["Attempt fresh conditional-auction, exact real per capita and time-weighted lender cases; select provisional remediation from actual errors and test delayed changed-context transfer rather than infer measured weakest skills from a day score."],"videos":[{"id":"IMkLAP_aj3I","title":"Monetary Policy explained","by":"Sim Institute","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Yale - selected common-value teaching","url":"https://oyc.yale.edu/economics/econ-159/lecture-24","note":"Fresh probability tree is synthetic, not real auction evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - exact nominal/real conversion","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-macroeconomics-3e/pages/6-2-adjusting-nominal-values-to-real-values","note":"Fresh exact multiplicative growth model, no current India estimate."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"FDIC - selected repricing methods","url":"https://www.fdic.gov/risk-management-manual-examination-policies/section-7-1.pdf","note":"Read the gap, earnings-simulation and economic-value sections. Compare the assumptions behind each exposure measure."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - retrieval and study","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Actual attempted errors can guide provisional review, not measured mastery or app effect size inferred from aggregate scores."}],"exercise":"Private mixed retrieval review; all numbers, actors and decisions fictional. Attempt unseen questions without notes, then log actual response, error cause, exact corrected calculation, source/input, decision consequence and a delayed changed-context test. Pick two remediation areas from observed new errors; sparse evidence stays provisional. A day aggregate, timestamp or watched percent does not identify item-level errors or measured weakest skills. Keep the three cases separate, not a summed investment.\nFresh auction: low common value 60/high 140, priors 0.5 each; for one supplied bid policy P(win|low)=0.6/P(win|high)=0.2. Pwin 0.4/posterior high 0.25/conditional common 80. Private net operating benefit 8 already after integration costs gives conditional net value 88. Bid 90=>conditional surplus minus 2/unconditional minus 0.8; don't bid the unconditional common 100 plus 8. Separate downside lowers private net 8 to 4=>conditional 84/bid 90 conditional minus 6/unconditional minus 2.4. These supplied winning probabilities are policy-specific, not a claim about every bid or optimal strategy. No real bid/deposit/coordination authorized.\nFresh macro: nominal GDP index 100 to 118,matched deflator 100 to 110,population 100 to 101. Exact real growth 1.18/1.10-1=7.272727 percent; real per capita 1.18/(1.10*1.01)-1=6.210621 percent. Approximate 118 growth 18 minus 10 minus 1=7 percent is not exact. Even exact aggregate outputper person does not forecast each store or household demand; no current country data.\nFresh lender one year, simple interest, static earning assets 500 at 12 percent/funding 400 at 8 percent/equity 100. Baseline income 60/expense 32/NII 28/NIM 5.6 percent. Funding 200 resets+200 bp immediately/full year=>expense increment 4. Loans 300 reset+100 bp only for last quarter=>income increment 0.75. New NII 24.75/NIM 4.95 percent, decline 65 bp, not the+2 predicted by naive(300-200)*2 percent. Separate opex 8/credit expense 3 gives pretax 13.75; not regulatory ECL or cash charge-off. Contract correction moves loan reset to last HALF year, not quarter; same 300/+100 bp=>income increment 1.5/NII 25.5/NIM 5.1 percent. Do not revise funding timing or double subtract credit expense inside NIM.\nDeliver separate probability/real per capita/repricing and changed-evidence tables with units, actual error log and delayed new-context retest. Fictional evidence/credit/cash owners with day 0/day 7 review-stop gate. No public advice, real auction, loan, experiment, vendor message, deposit, derivative, disclosure or spend. Mock sponsor approval is not actual authority.<section aria-label=\"Native D112 practice\"><h3>Fresh mixed retrieval with separate reference calculations</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d112-mixed-retrieval.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable mixed retrieval review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d112-mixed-retrieval.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before using the reference. Original written cases and quiz remain intact.</p><p>Three separate fictional cases, not a combined investment or measured skill score. Auction: joint low/high win probabilities 0.3/0.1 give winning probability 0.4 and conditional common value 80. Net private benefit 8 versus bid 90 gives conditional surplus -2 and unconditional -0.8; downside benefit 4 gives -6 and -2.4. Supplied win chances belong to this policy, not an optimal bid curve. Zero winning probability makes conditional values undefined, while direct unconditional surplus is zero under the no-fee/no-loss-on-losing convention. Macro: nominal 100 to 118, matched deflator 100 to 110, population 100 to 101 gives exact real growth 7.272727% and real per capita growth 6.210621%, not subtraction 7%. Lender: assets 500 at 12%, funding 400 at 8%; funding 200 resets +200 bp for a full year, loans 300 reset +100 bp for only the last quarter. NII 24.75/NIM 4.95%, down 65 bp. Correcting only loan timing to last half-year gives NII 25.5/NIM 5.1%. Operating expense 8 and credit expense 3 stay outside NII/NIM, giving pretax 13.75 or 14.5. Equal-year fully repriced NII 27 is not first-year earned income.</p><p>119 baseline formula cells independently recalculated; nineteen changed scenario sets plus restoration cover priors/win evidence/private benefit/bid sensitivity, zero winning event, exact denominators/common index base, separate reset timing/rate changes/expenses and proposed day-7 changed-context inputs. Sixteen invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and three broken result references were checked. Day-7 inputs were recalculated by the author, not attempted by a learner. Actual response, confidence before feedback, observed error, reviewer objection and delayed retest remain blank. Pick two provisional review areas only after actual item-level errors, never from an aggregate day score or watched percent. Static reference prose needs revision after input edits.</p><p>Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, new source reading, real auction/macro/contracts/credit evidence, full audiovisual, learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No real bid, deposit, loan, repricing, derivative, borrower/vendor contact, disclosure or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Fresh prior low/high 0.5,policy win probabilities 0.6/0.2. Pwin?","o":["0.4","0.2","0.6","0.8"],"a":0},{"q":"Fresh P(high AND win)=0.1/Pwin 0.4. Posterior high?","o":["0.1","0.5","0.25","0.75"],"a":2},{"q":"Fresh common 60/140,posterior high 0.25,net private 8,bid 90,Pwin 0.4. Unconditional expected surplus?","o":["Minus 2 crore","Minus 0.8 crore","Plus 7.2 crore","Plus 0.8 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Fresh nominal growth 18%,deflator 10%,population 1%. Exact real per capita growth?","o":["7.000000%","7.272727%","16.831683%","6.210621%"],"a":3},{"q":"Fresh lender assets 500 at 12%,funding 400 at 8%. Baseline NII?","o":["28 crore","60 crore","32 crore","7 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Loans 300 reset+100 bp onlylast quarter. First yearincome increment?","o":["3 crore","1.5 crore","0.75 crore","6 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Fresh baseline NII 28,asset increment 0.75,funding 200*2%full year. New NII?","o":["26.5 crore","30 crore","21.5 crore","24.75 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Fresh NII 24.75/average earning assets 500. NIM?","o":["6.1875%","4.95%","5.6%","4.15%"],"a":1},{"q":"Contract correction:300 loans reset+100 bp last HALF year,not last quarter;baseline NII 28/funding increment 4. New NII?","o":["24.75 crore","27 crore","25.5 crore","30 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Only day aggregate exists before fresh attempt. Which remediation claim is defensible?","o":["Use actual new errors; label sparse evidence provisional","Recover measured weakest questions from day aggregate","Use watched percent as item-level answer history","Claim mastery once familiar answers are repeated"],"a":0}]},"113":{"objectives":["Trace an explicit INR-per-USD quote through a fictional tariff base, price pass-through and quantity response; separate reciprocal FX percentage changes, per-unit margin and total contribution."],"videos":[{"id":"fxvsb373URw","title":"Introduction to currency exchange and trade | AP Macroeconomics | Khan Academy","by":"Khan Academy","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use specific written quote/tariff teaching and explicit invented cost basis."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - exchange quotes and currency effects","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-macroeconomics-3e/pages/16-1-how-the-foreign-exchange-market-works","note":"Quote orientation matters; profit effects require currency-specific costs and quantities. Historical FX volumes/euro membership/FDI shorthand not current facts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - tariff forms and trade barriers","url":"https://openstax.org/books/introduction-business-2e/pages/3-3-barriers-to-trade","note":"Percentage versus unit tariffs distinguished; our tax base/rates invented, not Indian customs/GST/exemption advice. Current-seeming US/Canada policy examples not independently verified or adopted."}],"exercise":"Private fictional importer, one annual sales window, rupees per unit and total rupees. Not actual INR/USD, Indian customs valuation, tariff schedule, GST, credit, exemption, shipping contract or recommendation to order or hedge. All goods purchased equal goods sold in each scenario; no unsold stock or other working-capital timing. Supplier USD 100 per unit, quote 80 INR/USD, synthetic tariff 10 percent applied ONLY to converted supplier invoice, domestic distribution 400 rupees/unit outside tariff base. Original converted invoice 8000/tariff 800/landed 9200; price 11000/margin 1800,1000 units=>revenue 11000000/total contribution 1800000 before unchanged fixed cost, tax and capex.\nShock quote 88 INR/USD and synthetic tariff 20 percent on same invoice base, supplier price 100 USD/domestic cost 400 unchanged. Converted 8800/tariff 1760/landed 10960, rise 1760=19.130435 percent. The INR-per-USD quote rises 10 percent; reciprocal USD-per-INR falls 9.090909 percent, not 10 percent on that denominator. At unchanged 10 percent tariff FX-only landed 10080, increase 880. Additional tariff 10 percentage points on NEW 8800 invoice adds 880; FX and tariff effects depend on sequence and include interaction. Do not add 10 percent FX+10 percentage point tariff to the whole landed 9200 or apply tariff to domestic 400 unless explicitly stipulated.\nNew price 12000/units 900=>margin 1040/total contribution 936000, decline 864000 or 48 percent versus 1800000. Revenue 10800000 falls 200000, a different metric. Price increase 1000 recovers 56.818182 percent of 1760 per-unit cost increase, not full pass-through and not elasticity. At fixed 900 units, price 12960 would preserve old total 1800000 because 10960+1800000/900=12960. That price is a conditional arithmetic threshold, not evidence customers will buy 900 at it. Price 12760 would preserve only old per-unit margin 1800, giving 1620000 total at 900 units, below baseline.\nIndependent downside at price 12000 and new costs 10960 but only 750 units=>contribution 780000, decline 1020000 versus old 1800000. Recompute volume/price feedback rather than claim profit moves one-for-one with FX. A real tariff's assessment base, effective date, recovery of indirect tax, payment currency, inventory and seller price concession would need original documents; none supplied here. Deliver unit cost/quote/tariff/price-volume/contribution bridges and coherent sensitivity, source/input registry, fictional sourcing/demand/cash owners with day 0/day 30 review-stop gate. No actual shipment, supplier contact, FX trade, tariff declaration, price change, disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D113 practice\"><h3>Quote direction, tariff base and contribution bridge</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d113-d114-fx-branches.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable quote and dated-equity review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d113-d114-fx-branches.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Fictional importer, INR per unit and annual INR, not crore or equity million. USD 100 supplier invoice at INR 80/USD with 10% synthetic tariff on converted invoice plus domestic 400 gives landed 9200. New quote 88 and tariff 20% gives 10960. Quote rises 10%, reciprocal USD/INR falls 9.090909%, not 10%. FX-first attribution 880 plus 880 and tariff-first 800 plus 960 both equal 1760; interaction 80 is not added twice. Old price 11000 and 1000 units gives contribution 1800000; new price 12000 and 900 units gives 936000, down 48%; downside 750 gives 780000. At fixed 900 units, price 12960 preserves old total contribution; 12760 only preserves old per-unit margin. Those price thresholds do not establish demand. Cost change of zero makes incremental pass-through undefined, old contribution zero makes percentage change undefined, and zero volume blocks total-preserving price. No actual tariff classification, landed-cost evidence, market FX or demand forecast supplied.</p><p>All 129 baseline formula cells were independently recalculated. Nineteen changed scenario sets plus restoration cover invoice, quotes, tariffs, price, quantities, zero denominators, delay probabilities/dates, FX growth/appreciation, hurdle, zero payoff and duplicate-loss diagnostics. Fifteen invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and four broken result references were checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after input changes. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, current FX/customs/country-premium/investment/legal evidence, full audiovisual or learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No shipment, supplier contact, hedge trade, investment, transfer, borrowing, disclosure or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Supplier USD 100, quote 80 INR/USD, synthetic tariff 10% on converted invoice only, domestic 400. Landed cost?","o":["8800 rupees/unit","9240 rupees/unit","9200 rupees/unit","8400 rupees/unit"],"a":2},{"q":"New quote 88 INR/USD, USD 100 invoice, tariff 20% on invoice, domestic 400. New landed?","o":["10960 rupees/unit","10560 rupees/unit","11040 rupees/unit","10120 rupees/unit"],"a":0},{"q":"Quote 80 to 88 INR/USD. Percentage change in reciprocal USD/INR?","o":["Minus 10%","Plus 10%","Plus 9.090909%","Minus 9.090909%"],"a":3},{"q":"With FX already 88, moving tariff 10% to 20% on supplier invoice 8800 adds?","o":["800 rupees/unit","880 rupees/unit","920 rupees/unit","1760 rupees/unit"],"a":1},{"q":"New price 12000/landed 10960/units 900. Total contribution?","o":["1040000 rupees","1620000 rupees","936000 rupees","1800000 rupees"],"a":2},{"q":"Old contribution 1800000, new 936000. Percentage change?","o":["Minus 10%","Minus 19.130435%","Minus 44%","Minus 48%"],"a":3},{"q":"Price increase 1000 and per-unit landed-cost increase 1760. What is incremental-cost pass-through, rounded to two decimal places?","o":["56.82%","100.00%","19.13%","10.00%"],"a":0},{"q":"At new landed 10960 and fixed 900 units, price needed for old total contribution 1800000?","o":["12760 rupees/unit","12960 rupees/unit","12000 rupees/unit","13000 rupees/unit"],"a":1},{"q":"Price 12760 preserves margin 1800 per unit, but units 900. Total contribution?","o":["1800000 rupees","936000 rupees","1980000 rupees","1620000 rupees"],"a":3},{"q":"Downside price 12000/landed 10960/units 750. Total contribution?","o":["936000 rupees","1350000 rupees","780000 rupees","1040000 rupees"],"a":2}]},"114":{"objectives":["Convert dated INR equity payoffs into USD before discounting, reconcile currency-consistent rates, and separate expected delay scenarios from risk adjustment instead of assuming every cash-flow scenario plus premium double counts risk."],"videos":[{"id":"U3D9a_H_Vrs","title":"Session 4: Equity Risk Premiums","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. ERP methods and business-exposure distinctions fit; historical 2013 US/Brazil estimates not current Indian ERP or a delay-probability estimate."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran - emerging-market valuation, selected opening sections","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/papers/emergmkts.pdf","note":"Read opening through currency mismatch, expected versus risk-adjusted cash flows, duplicate penalties and cost-of-debt discussion; not whole 2009 paper or diagrams. Expected loss probability and priced risk premium can coexist; no current country ERP/rules adopted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - FX quotes and investor returns","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-macroeconomics-3e/pages/16-1-how-the-foreign-exchange-market-works","note":"Dated quote orientation and local-versus-homecurrency returns; no current FX forecast or guaranteed hedge."}],"exercise":"Private fictional one-equity-claim infrastructure exercise. Money in million INR or million USD, not crore; specified nominal cash flows/rates and no debt, intermediate distributions, fees, taxes, withholding, default or extra capital call. At time 0 pay 800 million INR, FX 80 INR/USD=>10 million USD. On-time time 1 payoff 960 million INR all-inclusive terminal equity proceeds at 88 INR/USD=>10.909091 million USD. Local one-year total return 20 percent; USD return(960/88)/(800/80)-1=9.090909 percent, not 20 minus 10=10 percent. USD nominal stipulated expected-equity-cash hurdle 8 percent, not observed risk-free/current required return. On-time PV 960/88/1.08=10.101010 USD million/NPV+0.101010.\nFor an explicit algebraic currency-consistency check only, assume the future FX path deterministic 80 at 0,88 at 1,96.8 at 2, a 10 percent per year rise in INR/USD. Corresponding INR discount factor per year(1.08*1.10)=1.188, rate 18.8 percent, not 18 percent. On-time local PV 960/1.188=808.080808 INR million divided by spot 80=10.101010 USD million. This identity depends on the stipulated currency/rate/path convention; it is not covered interest parity, a traded forward quote, a claim FX risk-free or a real-market price forecast.\nDelay branch, probability 25 percent, pushes same 960 million INR payoff to time 2, no time 1 cash or compensation, FX 96.8=>9.917355 million USD. Delay PV 9.917355/1.08^2=8.502534 USD million/NPV minus 1.497466. With 75 percent on-time/25 percent delay gives expected PV 0.75*10.101010+0.25*8.502534=9.701391/NPV minus 0.298609 USD million. Do not divide expected INR payoff by average FX or discount every branch for one year. Delay-only local payoff two-year CAGR sqrt(960/800)-1=9.544512 percent; USD two-year CAGR sqrt((960/96.8)/10)-1=minus 0.414081 percent. These branch CAGRs are not the portfolio expected IRR or expected CF discount rate.\nThe delay scenarios estimate expected cash flows/timing; probability weighting does NOT automatically convert them to certainty-equivalent cash flows or remove systematic country/business risk. Appropriate priced risk compensation may still belong in expected cash discount rates. Conversely, mechanically haircutting each branch by another 25 percent labelled SAME delay already modeled, while changing nothing else, duplicates that specific expected loss/timing penalty without evidence. Audit exactly which risk is in scenario probabilities, expected amounts, certainty-equivalent adjustment or discount premium; do not assume every combination double counts. Switching to USD does not remove operating/regulatory/country exposure. No actual permit law, probability, ERP, FX forecast or investment recommendation verified.\nDeliver dated branch currency/return/PV tables, matched local-USD identity, risk-location registry and objections, fictional legal/evidence/FX/valuation owners with day 0/day 30 review-stop gates. Mock fund approval or positive one-branch NPV is not authority to subscribe, buy, transfer funds, hedge, borrow, contact officials, disclose or sign. No real transaction or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D114 practice\"><h3>Convert dated equity cash at date, then discount</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d113-d114-fx-branches.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable quote and dated-equity review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d113-d114-fx-branches.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Separate fictional no-debt equity case, million INR/USD. Initial 800 million INR at spot 80 gives 10 million USD. Same 960 million INR payoff at year 1/FX 88 gives USD 10.909091 and PV 10.101010 at stipulated USD hurdle 8%; delayed to year 2/FX 96.8 gives USD 9.917355 and PV 8.502534. Delay probability 25% gives expected PV 9.701391 and NPV -0.298609 despite positive on-time NPV. Delayed local CAGR 9.544512% and USD CAGR -0.414081% use the two-year horizon, not total-return labels. Matched nominal INR rate 18.8% makes local PV divided by spot equal USD PV under the stipulated deterministic FX path, not covered interest parity or a forward forecast. Expected cash is not certainty-equivalent; systematic risk can coexist with modeled delay. The diagnostic second haircut is explicitly the SAME already modeled delay loss, not a blanket claim that all scenarios plus risk premia double count. No current country premium or investment/legal permission supplied.</p><p>All 129 baseline formula cells were independently recalculated. Nineteen changed scenario sets plus restoration cover invoice, quotes, tariffs, price, quantities, zero denominators, delay probabilities/dates, FX growth/appreciation, hurdle, zero payoff and duplicate-loss diagnostics. Fifteen invalid-input cases, independent-case isolation and four broken result references were checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after input changes. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, current FX/customs/country-premium/investment/legal evidence, full audiovisual or learner/account validation or overall 10/10. No shipment, supplier contact, hedge trade, investment, transfer, borrowing, disclosure or spending is required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Time 0 equity 800 million INR at 80 INR/USD. USD outlay?","o":["10 million USD","64000 million USD","800 million USD","0.1 million USD"],"a":0},{"q":"Time 1 proceeds 960 million INR at 88 INR/USD, initial 10 million USD. USD total return?","o":["20%","10%","9.090909%","Minus 9.090909%"],"a":2},{"q":"Stipulated USD hurdle 8%, deterministic INR/USD growth 10%. Matching INR annual rate under this convention?","o":["18.0%","18.8%","8.0%","2.0%"],"a":1},{"q":"On-time USD proceeds 10.909091 at time 1, discount 8%. PV?","o":["10.909091 million USD","11.781818 million USD","8.502534 million USD","10.101010 million USD"],"a":3},{"q":"Delay same 960 million INR to time 2, FX 96.8. USD proceeds before discount?","o":["9.917355 million USD","10.909091 million USD","12 million USD","8.502534 million USD"],"a":0},{"q":"Delay proceeds 9.917355 at time 2/USD hurdle 8%. PV?","o":["9.182736 million USD","9.917355 million USD","8.502534 million USD","10.101010 million USD"],"a":2},{"q":"On-time PV 10.101010 with 75% probability/delay PV 8.502534 with 25%. Expected PV?","o":["10.101010 million USD","9.301772 million USD","8.502534 million USD","9.701391 million USD"],"a":3},{"q":"Expected PV 9.701391 minus initial 10. Expected-cash NPV at stipulated hurdle?","o":["Plus 0.101010 million USD","Minus 0.298609 million USD","Minus 1.497466 million USD","Plus 9.701391 million USD"],"a":1},{"q":"Does probability weighting delay branches automatically make cash flows certainty-equivalent?","o":["Yes; any probabilities remove all risk premiums","Yes; USD conversion removes country risk","No; expected losses and priced risk are different","No; scenario weighting must never be used"],"a":2},{"q":"Which local PV check matches on-time USD value under stipulated deterministic FX/rate convention?","o":["960/1.188 then divide by 80","960/1.08 then divide by 80","960/1.188 then multiply by 80","960/1.18 then divide by 88"],"a":0}]},"115":{"objectives":["Build a rare-event confusion matrix and Bayes posterior with explicit denominators, then compare a fictional flag-review policy using false-positive and missed-fraud costs rather than accuracy alone."],"videos":[{"id":"HZGCoVF3YvM","title":"Bayes theorem, the geometry of changing beliefs","by":"3Blue1Brown","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Conditional probability and explicit prior context fit. Librarian/farmer prevalence and psychology-effect examples are illustrative historical claims, not fraud data or verified current statistics."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT 509, section 17.3 - predictive values","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat509/lesson/17/17.3","note":"PPV depends on prevalence/sensitivity/specificity; case-control prevalence need not match deployment."}],"exercise":"Private fictional, fully labelled batch of 10000 transactions, not actual customer data, validation or enforcement. Exactly 100 fraud/9900 legitimate, synthetic screen sensitivity 90 percent/specificity 95 percent. Matrix: true positive 90/false negative 10/false positive 495/true negative 9405; actual fraud denominator 100, actual legitimate 9900, flag positive 585, flag negative 9415. PPV=P(fraud|flag)=90/(90+495)=15.384615 percent, not 90 percent sensitivity or 94.95 percent overall accuracy. False discovery share among flags 495/585=84.615385 percent. Negative predictive value 9405/9415=99.893786 percent. False positive rate 5 percent has denominator 9900 legitimate, not 10000 all transactions or 585 flags. Always-negative classifier accuracy 99 percent here but misses all 100 frauds; accuracy cannot choose action without costs.\nBayes check: prior odds.01/.99=1/99; positive likelihood ratio.9/.05=18; posterior odds 18/99=2/11; probability 2/13=15.384615 percent. If same synthetic conditional rates transported to a different 10000 transaction batch at 5 percent prevalence, TP 450/FN 50/FP 475/TN 9025, PPV 450/925=48.648649 percent, NOT 90 percent or unchanged 15.384615. This is a hypothetical transport assumption, not evidence sensitivity/specificity stay fixed under changing fraud mix, season, thresholds or selection. Case-control balanced sample proportions cannot supply production prevalence unaided.\nToy one-batch cost comparison, currency rupees: each missed fraud costs 1000, each flagged transaction gets perfect manual review costing 20, and each legitimate flag causes additional 30 friction cost even if cleared. Assume reviewer catches every flagged fraud BEFORE loss and makes no additional mistakes; fraud loss on FN only, no further true positive monetary cost/benefit. Policy total 585*20+495*30+10*1000=11700+14850+10000=36550. No-screen baseline misses 100 frauds=>100000. Model net cost reduction 63450, before fixed system/setup/appeals/delay/privacy or other costs, not measured saving. With fraud loss per missed case L, policy cost 26550+10 L versus 100 L, breaks even at L=295. At L 200 policy 28550 versus baseline 20000, worse 8550. Positive PPV or high accuracy alone does not establish economic benefit.\nDeliver labelled matrix/Bayes/denominator table/cost equation/shift sensitivity, source registry, fictional validation/review/privacy owners and day 0/day 30 hold gate. Real deployment needs independently measured out-of-time labels, uncertainty, representativeness and human-policy/legal review. No real screening, review, accusation, customer contact, data export, blocking, disclosure, refund or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D115 practice\"><h3>Rare-event denominators and consequence costs</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d115-d116-bayes-experiment.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable Bayes and fixed-horizon review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d115-d116-bayes-experiment.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Fictional labelled baseline batch of 10000 has 100 fraud and 9900 legitimate. Sensitivity 90% and specificity 95% give TP 90 / FN 10 / FP 495 / TN 9405. Positive flag denominator 585 makes PPV 15.384615% and false discovery 84.615385%, not sensitivity 90%. Prior odds 1/99 times LR 18 gives posterior odds 2/11 and probability 2/13. Perfect review at INR 20 per flag, legitimate friction 30 and missed loss 1000 yields policy cost 36550 versus no-screen 100000. Loss threshold 295 holds only for this stipulated policy; loss 200 makes policy worse by 8550. Changed-case fractional counts are expectations, not observed transaction labels. Zero flags or nonflags make affected predictive values undefined; specificity 1 makes LR undefined even when direct PPV can be defined. At prevalence 5% with unchanged conditional rates PPV becomes 48.648649%, a hypothetical transport assumption, not deployment evidence.</p><p>All 66 baseline formulas independently recomputed. Twenty-five changed scenario sets plus restoration cover prevalence, conditional rates, policy costs, zero/all fraud and flags, group sizes/counts, imprecise and low-count inference, target direction/size, zero deltas and attrition. Seventeen invalid inputs, independent-case isolation and four broken result references checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after edits. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, new empirical source reading, full audiovisual, learner/account/legal validation or overall 10/10. No real screening, user assignment, experiment, data collection, analytics/product/price change, customer contact, accusation, data export, blocking, disclosure or spend required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"100 fraud/9900 legitimate, sensitivity 90%, specificity 95%. False positive count?","o":["500 transactions","495 transactions","90 transactions","585 transactions"],"a":1},{"q":"True positives 90 and false positives 495. What is fraud probability given a flag, rounded to two decimal places?","o":["90.00%","95.00%","94.95%","15.38%"],"a":3},{"q":"True positive 90/true negative 9405 out of 10000. Accuracy?","o":["94.95%","99%","15.384615%","90%"],"a":0},{"q":"Flag positive 585 of which 495 are legitimate. False-discovery share?","o":["5%","4.95%","84.615385%","15.384615%"],"a":2},{"q":"Prevalence 1%, positive likelihood ratio 18. Posterior odds fraud given flag?","o":["18 to 1","2 to 11","1 to 99","2 to 13"],"a":1},{"q":"Different batch 10000, prevalence 5%, sensitivity 90%, specificity 95%. What is PPV, rounded to two decimal places?","o":["48.65%","90.00%","15.38%","95.00%"],"a":0},{"q":"Always-negative classifier with 100 fraud/9900 legitimate. Accuracy and missed frauds?","o":["99% accuracy;1 missed fraud","1% accuracy;100 missed frauds","90% accuracy;10 missed frauds","99% accuracy;100 missed frauds"],"a":3},{"q":"Toy policy 585 reviews at 20,495 legitimate flags at 30 friction,10 misses at 1000. Total cost?","o":["21700 rupees","26550 rupees","36550 rupees","100000 rupees"],"a":2},{"q":"Policy cost 26550+10 L versus no-screen 100 L. Break-even missed fraud loss L?","o":["295 rupees","265.5 rupees","365.5 rupees","2655 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"Balanced case-control training sample, deployment prevalence unknown. Which estimate is justified?","o":["Sample 50% fraud directly proves production prevalence","Conditional rates need target prevalence for deployment PPV","Specificity alone determines all flag posteriors","High training accuracy fixes production PPV unchanged"],"a":1}]},"116":{"objectives":["Distinguish 2 percent relative conversion lift from 2 percentage points, compute a fixed-horizon difference-of-proportions interval and approximate sample size per arm, and avoid treating nonsignificance or retrospective power as proof of no useful effect."],"videos":[{"id":"Rsc5znwR5FA","title":"Statistical Power, Clearly Explained!!!","by":"StatQuest with Josh Starmer","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use Penn State written formulas; do not rely on unqualified claims larger samples ALWAYS increase power or that highp proves sameness."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT 200, section 9.1 - two independent proportions","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat200/book/export/html/193","note":"Unpooled SE for Wald CI, pooled SE for null test; independence and sufficient counts. Displayed p=0.000 means rounded, not exact zero. Not automatic validity for repeated looks, clustered users or bias."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT 509, section 6a.7 - two-proportion sample size","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat509/lesson/6a/6a.7","note":"Equal-allocation normal approximation n is PER ARM. Here use specified 1.96 and 0.84 z values, not exact power or guaranteed precision."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT 509, lesson 6a - power and sample size","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat509/lesson/6a","note":"Power conditional on specified alternative/model/test; confidence, direction and attrition distinct. Large samples do not fix measurement or assignment bias."}],"exercise":"Private fictional fixed-horizon randomized conversion test, independent user unit, one binary outcome/user over same window, equal allocation/no attrition, no interference or crossovers, one primary metric, no early stopping and no multiple variants/looks. Synthetic planning baseline p A 0.10. Target 2 percent RELATIVE lift means p B 0.102, delta 0.002=0.2 percentage points, NOT p B 0.12 which is 2 percentage points/20 percent relative lift. Define metric, effect size, two-sided alpha 0.05, power 80 percent under specified alternative, randomization, eligibility and end rule before data; these are assumptions not a live experiment grant.\nToy completed batch A 100/1000 conversions/B 102/1000=>10 percent/10.2 percent/observed difference 0.002, relative 2 percent. Counts 100/900 and 102/898 satisfy the course's 10 success/10 failure approximation check; does not verify random assignment/independence. Unpooled Wald SE=sqrt(.10*.90/1000+.102*.898/1000)=.013475756. Approx 95 percent CI for p B-p A=.002 plus or minus 1.96*.013475756=[-.024412482,.028412482], or[-2.441248,2.841248]percentage points. Not a 95 percent probability the fixed true parameter lies in this realized frequentist interval. Includes zero AND both harmful/useful positive effects; failing to reject zero does not demonstrate no meaningful effect or equivalence. Equivalence/noninferiority need prespecified margins/design and their own test, not appropriating this nonsignificance.\nPooled null proportion 202/2000=.101, SE 0=sqrt(.101*.899*(1/1000+1/1000))=.013475830; two-sided z=.148413862 and p about .8820. Do not swap p value with probability null true, probability of replication or posterior probability treatment works. Wald CI and pooled null test are distinct approximations; not exact matching tests for every dataset. No observed power from same estimate used as new evidence; report estimate/CI and prospectively choose effect size.\nApprox equal-arm planning formula from course: n PER ARM=2*(zalpha/2+zbeta)^2*pbar*(1-pbar)/delta^2. Use specified z 1.96/.84, pbar=.101, delta.002=>355932.08=>355933 per arm/711866 total, not 355933 total. This is a course normal approximation, not exact binomial power or guaranteed required N. With 2 PERCENTAGE POINT target, p B.12, pbar.11, delta.02=>3837.68=>3838 per arm/7676 total. Different pbar means ratio 92.74668, not exact 100 though delta-squared scaling is dominant. At 10 percent ATTRITION retain 90 percent, target 355933 complete users requires ceil(355933/.90)=395482 randomized per arm under stipulated independent noninformative loss; inflating N does not fix differential attrition/bias. Actual protocol needs analysis of missing outcomes and design, not just divide.\nDeliver preregistration-style fictional specification, count table/CI/pooled null test/planning per arm-total comparison/attrition check, source-and-assumption registry and fictional experiment/statistics/privacy owners with day 0/day 30 hold gate. No real experiment, user assignment, data collection, production analytics change, price/product change, contact, disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D116 practice\"><h3>Fixed-horizon inference versus prospective per-arm planning</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d115-d116-bayes-experiment.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable Bayes and fixed-horizon review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d115-d116-bayes-experiment.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Separate fictional independent-user fixed-horizon batch, A 100/1000 and B 102/1000: difference 0.002 = 0.2 percentage points, relative lift 2%. Unpooled SE 0.013475756 gives Wald 95% interval [-2.441248,2.841248] percentage points. Pooled null SE 0.013475830 gives z 0.148413862 and two-sided approximate p 0.882016, not probability the null is true. Low counts hold the approximate interval/test summary; the count screen alone cannot verify random assignment, independence or valid stopping. Prospective baseline 10% and relative target 2% means alternative 10.2%, not 12%. Specified z 1.96/0.84 approximation gives 355932.08 then 355933 complete PER ARM / 711866 total. At 10% stipulated noninformative attrition, 395482 randomized per arm / 790964 total. Separate 2 percentage-point target gives 3838 complete per arm, not the same plan. Raw n ratio 92.746680 is not exactly 100 because pbar changes. Zero target delta is undefined required n; attrition inflation cannot repair biased missing outcomes. No retrospective observed power, equivalence, exact binomial power or real experimentation authority inferred.</p><p>All 66 baseline formulas independently recomputed. Twenty-five changed scenario sets plus restoration cover prevalence, conditional rates, policy costs, zero/all fraud and flags, group sizes/counts, imprecise and low-count inference, target direction/size, zero deltas and attrition. Seventeen invalid inputs, independent-case isolation and four broken result references checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after edits. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, new empirical source reading, full audiovisual, learner/account/legal validation or overall 10/10. No real screening, user assignment, experiment, data collection, analytics/product/price change, customer contact, accusation, data export, blocking, disclosure or spend required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Baseline conversion 10%, target 2% RELATIVE lift. Alternative conversion?","o":["12%","10.02%","20%","10.2%"],"a":3},{"q":"A 100/1000/B 102/1000. Observed absolute p B-p A?","o":["2 percentage points","0.2 percentage points","2 percent of all users","0.02 percentage points"],"a":1},{"q":"Observed proportions.10/.102, both n 1000. Unpooled CI standard error?","o":["0.001347576","0.000181596","0.013475756","0.134757560"],"a":2},{"q":"Delta.002, unpooled SE.013475756,95% z 1.96. Approx CI in percentage points?","o":["[-2.441248,2.841248]","[-0.244125,0.284125]","[0.173588,0.226412]","[-24.412482,28.412482]"],"a":0},{"q":"CI includes zero and harmful/useful positive effects. Supported conclusion?","o":["Zero is proved, so all useful effects are excluded","Equivalence is established without a margin","Treatment has exactly 95% probability of benefit","Effect imprecise; meaningful benefit and harm remain compatible"],"a":3},{"q":"A 100/B 102 conversions, both 1000 users. Pooled proportion under H 0 p A=p B?","o":["0.100","0.101","0.102","0.202"],"a":1},{"q":"Specified approximate planning:2*(1.96+.84)^2*.101*.899/.002^2=355932.08. Round up and allocate?","o":["355933 total;177967 per arm","355932 per arm;711864 total","355933 per arm;711866 total","711866 per arm;1423732 total"],"a":2},{"q":"Instead target 10% to 12%, same approx formula/pbar.11/delta.02 gives 3837.68. Required rounded per arm?","o":["3838 per arm","355933 per arm","1919 per arm","7676 per arm"],"a":0},{"q":"Need 355933 complete users per arm, stipulated 10% noninformative attrition. Randomized per arm rounded up?","o":["391527 users","395482 users","320340 users","711866 users"],"a":1},{"q":"Why not claim exact 100 fold n ratio when delta.02 falls to.002 in these two plans?","o":["Sample allocation switches from equal to unequal","Two-sided alpha changes from.05 to.5","Desired power changes from 80% to 8%","pbar(1-pbar) also changes between plans"],"a":3}]},"117":{"objectives":["Separate logistic odds from probabilities, evaluate a fictional untouched loan-default holdout by calibration, Brier score and threshold costs, and avoid inferring an intervention effect from predictive associations.","Fit an ordinary least-squares line by hand, reconcile residuals and squared errors, and distinguish training fit from a frozen future-batch comparison and from logistic probabilities."],"videos":[{"id":"yIYKR4sgzI8","title":"StatQuest: Logistic Regression","by":"StatQuest with Josh Starmer","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Probability/MLE introduction fits. Claims that nonsignificant variables are useless, logistic models have no residuals/R-squared or cannot compare nested models are overbroad and not adopted; 0.5 is not a universal cost-optimal threshold."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT504 - selected logistic regression introduction","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat504/Lesson06","note":"Read opening through GLM assumptions, binary model, grouped-data example and parameter interpretation, not remaining software/diagnostic sections. Odds multiplier is not a risk multiplier or causal effect; no borrower-policy result inferred."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"scikit-learn - probability calibration","url":"http://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html","note":"Brier score mixes reliability/resolution/uncertainty; lower score alone does not prove better calibration. Logistic calibration not guaranteed. Calibration fitting must be separate from classifier fitting and final evaluation; isotonic ties can change AUC."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT 415 - least-squares coefficients","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat415/lesson/7/7.3","note":"Read the centered slope formula and conversion to an ordinary intercept. These formulas support the authored four-point linear fit, not a validated operating model."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT 200 - fitted values and residuals","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat200/lesson/12/12.3/12.3.1","note":"Read fitted value, observed-minus-predicted residual and SSE definitions. In this exercise empirical prediction MSE uses divisor n, stated separately from residual-variance estimation."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT 462 - common error variance","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat462/node/94/","note":"Read the n-2 denominator for residual-variance estimation in simple regression. Do not confuse it with an explicitly defined average squared prediction error using n. Other model assumptions must be checked before inference."}],"exercise":"Private fictional evaluation, no real borrower data, model training, lending, rejection or pricing. Outcome default=1 within one fixed future window; all 10000 held-out loans have complete matured labels and scores locked before outcomes. Development, calibration and final chronological/group-separated holdout are distinct; using future repayment information, tuning thresholds on final labels or allowing the same borrower across splits would leak information. We stipulate disjointness rather than claim it was measured on real data.\nHoldout scores have only two values: 9000 loans each p=0.01, of which 90 default/8910 do not; 1000 loans each p=0.20, of which 200 default/800 do not. Expected defaults 9000*.01+1000*.20=290, observed 290, prevalence 2.9%. Each of these two bins matches its empirical rate exactly. That finite-sample equality is not proof of population calibration, uncertainty bounds, subgroup fairness or future stability. Threshold p>=0.10 flags the high bin: TP 200/FP 800/FN 90/TN 8910, PPV 20%, sensitivity 200/290=68.965517%, accuracy 91.1%. Always-negative accuracy 97.1% but misses all 290 defaults. Accuracy alone rewards the majority class.\nBinary Brier score is average (p-y)^2 with this explicit binary scaling, not sum of both class-coordinate errors. Here [90*.99^2+8910*.01^2+200*.80^2+800*.20^2]/10000=.02491. Constant p=.029 predicts the observed holdout prevalence and has retrospective Brier .029*.971=.028159. It is a descriptive comparator using holdout prevalence, NOT a deployable baseline estimated without leakage. A deployable constant would be fixed from earlier data. Lower Brier reflects probabilistic performance including resolution, not calibration alone; both this two-bin model and the retrospective constant match their respective aggregate empirical rates.\nToy classification costs only: false positive 10 rupees, false negative 100, true decisions 0; no actual loan rejection or claim an intervention prevents defaults. Flagged-policy cost 800*10+90*100=17000 versus always-negative 29000, difference 12000 before all omitted costs. With a genuinely calibrated p and these stipulated costs, flag when 10*(1-p)<100*p, so p>1/11=9.090909%; at equality either action ties. This expected-cost threshold is conditional on calibration and cost definitions, not a real credit rule. Threshold 0.5 flags none in this holdout. Shifting prevalence alone does not authorize transporting score calibration or conditional error rates.\nSeparate illustrative logistic equation, NOT the fitted two-bin model: logit(p)=ln(1/99)+ln(2)*x, x in {0,1}. At x 0 p=.01; x 1 odds 2/99 => p 2/101=1.980198%, not 2% exactly and not +2 percentage points. The x coefficient doubles odds conditional on this model, not probability or the effect of changing x. Observational predictors can encode treatment selection or omitted causes; predictive validation does not identify policy benefit.\nNew independent same-size future batch keeps original scores/bin counts but observes 180 defaults in the low bin and 300 in the high bin: empirical 2%/30%, total 480 versus expected 290, observed/expected 1.655172. Freeze decisions for review and investigate drift/labels/selection, using fresh calibration and another untouched evaluation if changes are made; do not silently recalibrate on final test data and call it validated. Deliver score-bin/count/cost/Brier and shifted-batch tables, source registry and fictional model/credit/data owners with day 0/day 30 hold gates. No actual data export, borrower contact, loan, rate change, scoring deployment, disclosure or spend authorized.\n\n\n<section aria-label=\"Additional written practice\"><h3 style=\"font-size:17px;margin:22px 0 10px\">Linear regression extension: fit, residuals and a changed batch</h3><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\">Use a separate fictional process-response dataset, not the default classifier above. x is a measured load index in arbitrary units; y is observed completion time in hours. Development pairs are (0,1), (1,2), (2,2), (3,5). No real customer or operational decision is involved. Fit y-hat = intercept + slope x by ordinary least squares with an intercept. The method chooses coefficients to minimize the sum of squared residuals, where each residual is observed y minus predicted y. A continuous-hours prediction is not a default probability; a slope measured in hours per load unit is not a logistic odds multiplier.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\"><strong>Try first:</strong> (1) Find x-bar and y-bar. Use slope = sum[(x-x-bar)(y-y-bar)] / sum[(x-x-bar)^2], then intercept = y-bar - slope x-bar. (2) List all fitted values and signed residuals. Compute SSE and training MSE using divisor n = 4; distinguish this from the residual-variance estimate using n-2. (3) Compute training R-squared = 1 - SSE/SST with SST = sum[(y-y-bar)^2]. (4) Freeze this fitted line and compare it with the development-mean baseline on a new chronological batch (4,4), (5,4.5). Which has lower mean squared prediction error in that new batch? Do not refit on the new labels before comparing them.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\"><strong>Worked check:</strong> x-bar = 1.5, y-bar = 2.5, centered cross-product sum = 6 and centered x-square sum = 5. Slope is 1.2 and intercept is 0.7, so y-hat = 0.7 + 1.2x. Development fitted values are 0.7, 1.9, 3.1 and 4.3; residuals are 0.3, 0.1, -1.1 and 0.7. Their signed sum is zero here because the fitted model includes an intercept, but their squared sum is 1.8, not zero. Training MSE is 1.8/4 = 0.45 hours squared. The residual-variance estimate under the usual simple-regression model would use 1.8/(4-2) = 0.9; it answers a different question. SST is 9, giving training R-squared 0.8. Neither 80% causal impact nor 80% future prediction accuracy follows from that figure.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\">On the new batch the frozen line predicts 5.5 and 6.7 hours. The observed-minus-predicted errors are -1.5 and -2.2, so new-batch MSE is (2.25 + 4.84)/2 = 3.545. The frozen development-mean baseline predicts 2.5 for both, with MSE (2.25 + 4)/2 = 3.125. The fitted line therefore performs worse than this baseline on this stated new batch despite its training R-squared of 0.8. This is a two-point authored illustration of validation risk, not a measured population result or proof of a particular drift cause. The new x values also lie outside the original 0-to-3 range, making extrapolation an explicit concern. Inspect process changes, label quality and the response shape before deciding what to investigate; do not quietly remove inconvenient observations.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\"><strong>Changed-input check:</strong> replace only the final development y value 5 with 4, and refit from the four development pairs. Do not edit the earlier fit&#x27;s coefficients independently. The revised y-bar is 2.25, centered cross-product sum 4.5 and x-square sum 5; slope is 0.9 and intercept 0.9. Fitted values are 0.9, 1.8, 2.7 and 3.6; residuals 0.1, 0.2, -0.7 and 0.4 give SSE 0.7. A single changed observation affects both coefficients. If all x values were identical, this slope denominator would be zero and these data would not identify a unique slope. Do not return a guessed zero coefficient as a verified fit.</p><p style=\"margin:10px 0;line-height:1.65\">For an actual model, inspect the sampling process, independence, response shape, residual pattern, influential observations and variance assumptions appropriate to the inference. Computing a least-squares line alone does not validate confidence intervals, establish causal effects or justify automatic credit decisions. The Penn State readings support the fit/residual definitions; all numbers here are newly authored toy data. Deliver both fit tables, the frozen-batch comparison and a short explanation of why linear and logistic outputs need different interpretations. These are self-assessed written checks, separate from the existing ten-item logistic quiz and not observed learner-transfer evidence.</p></section>\n<p><b>Spreadsheet practice:</b> <a href=\"/practice/top1-d103-d117-practice.xlsx\" download><u>Download the editable APV, LBO and regression workbook (XLSX)</u></a>, or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d103-d117-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><u>open the five-sheet print view (PDF)</u></a>. Rebuild the case in your own blank file first, then use the worked formulas to check it. Yellow inputs support changed-case tests; the Practice sheet has blank response cells. Baseline, changed-input, zero-variance and funding-gap behavior were recalculated in LibreOffice. This is authored model execution, not observed learner transfer or proof of Excel/Google Sheets parity.</p><section aria-label=\"Native D117 practice\"><h3>Frozen logistic scores, calibration and classification costs</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d117-d118-validation.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">13-sheet editable score and assignment review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d117-d118-validation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">13-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Fictional matured labelled holdout: low bin 9000 at p=.01 with 90 defaults, high bin 1000 at p=.20 with 200. Expected/observed 290; binary Brier .02491 versus retrospective constant .028159. The latter uses final prevalence, not a deployable earlier-data baseline. Threshold .10 gives TP 200/FP 800/FN 90/TN 8910; FP cost 10/FN 100 gives 17000 versus always-negative 29000. These are classification costs, not identified intervention savings. Cost indifference at p=1/11 only under genuine calibration and stipulated costs; exact bin-rate equality is not population/subgroup calibration. Frozen later scores face 180/300 defaults, O/E 480/290=1.655172 and Brier .03973; classification cost 25000. Investigate labels/selection/drift, not silent recalibration on final test data. Separate illustrative logit doubles odds 1/99 to 2/99, probability 2/101, not doubled risk or causal effect. Existing separate linear-regression extension and its workbook remain intact.</p><p>All 75 baseline formulas independently recomputed. Twenty-seven changed scenario sets plus restoration cover thresholds, class costs, zero/all defaults, changed scores and later labels, assignment/exposure/activation/failures, guardrail equality, low counts, independent-test family size and alpha. Nineteen invalid inputs, independent-case isolation and five broken result references checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after edits. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, new empirical source reading, full audiovisual, learner/account/legal validation or overall 10/10. No real screening, user assignment, experiment, data collection, analytics/product/price change, customer contact, accusation, data export, blocking, disclosure or spend required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Two-bin holdout: 9000 scores 0.01 and 1000 scores 0.20. Expected defaults?","o":["100","200","290","480"],"a":2},{"q":"At threshold 0.10 only 1000 high-score loans flagged;200 default. PPV?","o":["20%","68.965517%","91.1%","2.9%"],"a":0},{"q":"TP 200/FP 800/FN 90/TN 8910. Accuracy?","o":["97.1%","91.1%","20%","68.965517%"],"a":1},{"q":"False-positive cost 10/false-negative 100. Counts FP 800/FN 90. Toy total cost?","o":["8000 rupees","9000 rupees","29000 rupees","17000 rupees"],"a":3},{"q":"Calibrated probability p; flag costs 10 on nondefault, no flag costs 100 on default. Strict preference to flag when?","o":["p>1/2","p>10/100","p>1/11","p>10/10000"],"a":2},{"q":"For binary Brier average squared error, supplied two-bin counts give?","o":["0.02491","0.028159","0.04982","249.1"],"a":0},{"q":"Does lower Brier score alone establish better calibration?","o":["Yes; it measures only calibration error","Yes; low Brier proves causal policy benefit","No; Brier applies only to hard class labels","No; resolution and outcome uncertainty also enter"],"a":3},{"q":"Separate logit equation: baseline odds 1/99, coefficient ln 2, x rises 0 to 1. New probability?","o":["2/100=2%","2/101=1.980198%","3/100=3%","2/99=2.020202%"],"a":1},{"q":"Future same bins observe 180+300=480 defaults; locked expected 290. Observed/expected?","o":["1.655172","0.604167","1.000000","0.029000"],"a":0},{"q":"Predictive logit coefficient doubles odds. What causal conclusion follows without identification evidence?","o":["Changing predictor must double default probability","Changing predictor must halve all credit losses","No identified effect of intervening on that predictor","Random assignment is established by holdout accuracy"],"a":2}]},"118":{"objectives":["Analyze a fictional onboarding test by randomized assignment, distinguish exposure-conditioned rates from intent-to-treat, and apply prespecified safety and stopping rules without turning repeated nominal p-value checks into valid evidence."],"videos":[{"id":"BElijgjOgSQ","title":"Lecture 3.1 Experiment Design Concepts in a Simple A B Test","by":"IxD Online: UCSD & Coursera","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT50910.2 - intention-to-treat","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat509/lesson/10/10.2","note":"Analyze by assignment; clinical ICH/FDA rules are not UPI law or permission. ITT does not itself solve missing outcomes or a poorly chosen endpoint."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Microsoft Research - optional stopping, publication abstract","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/continuous-monitoring-of-a-b-tests-without-pain-optional-stopping-in-bayesian-testing/","note":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case. Continuous monitoring requires a valid design/stopping rule; not a blanket ban on safety review or blanket approval for Bayesian peeking."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State STAT200 - independent-proportion interval","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat200/book/export/html/193","note":"Unpooled normal-approximation CI on independent users with sufficient counts; not a sequentially valid interval."}],"exercise":"Private fictional UPI-style onboarding experiment, no actual UPI rules, payment processing, bank permissions, production analytics change or customer experiment. One user randomly assigned once, one prespecified binary activation within 7 days, no crossover or interference. Exactly 1000 assigned A/1000 B; complete activation and failure outcomes tracked for every assigned user, including those who never load the new flow. No missing-data imputation hidden in denominators. A 800 exposed/280 activated; B 900 exposed/320 activated, and all activations arise among exposed users by stipulation.\nAssignment/ITT rates 280/1000=28% versus 320/1000=32%, difference+4 percentage points/relative 14.285714%. Exposure-conditioned rates 280/800=35% versus 320/900=35.555556%, difference 0.555556 percentage points. Exposure occurs after assignment and differs by treatment; this contrast is descriptive and does not automatically identify the same causal estimand or a treatment-on-treated effect. Do not discard nonexposed assigned users to improve a rate. If outcomes were genuinely missing, ITT would still be the target but assignment labels alone would not recover unobserved outcomes; a prespecified missing-data strategy/sensitivity would be needed.\nAt one fixed terminal analysis, unpooled SE sqrt(.28*.72/1000+.32*.68/1000)=.020474374, approximate 95% CI for B-A=[-.000129773,.080129773], or[-.012977,8.012977] percentage points. Under stipulated independent random assignment, no selection/missingness/interference and fixed-horizon analysis, estimate concerns the assignment policy for this eligible population/window. CI includes zero; not proven no effect or equivalence. No clinical/financial causal claim about other populations.\nPrespecified toy guardrail: severe onboarding failures must not rise by more than 1 percentage point; operational rule holds rollout if observed assigned-user difference exceeds that limit, while statistical safety uncertainty is reviewed separately. A 20/1000=2%, B 45/1000=4.5%, difference+2.5 percentage points, so this hold rule fails despite positive activation point estimate. This is an explicit operational threshold on observations, not a confidence-bound noninferiority test or proof of actual causal harm. Do not call safety established by a nonsignificant guardrail test, or drop the guardrail after viewing results.\nFixed target and terminal test were specified before data. Repeatedly viewing a standard fixed-horizon p-value and stopping on the first p<.05 changes its operating properties; the terminal p-value is not automatically a valid sequential test. Looking for safety/data-integrity issues is different from opportunistic efficacy stopping, but any action and inference must follow the protocol. Valid sequential designs, alpha spending or suitable always-valid methods can allow planned monitoring; they are not implemented in this exercise. Bayesian labels alone do not remove stopping/prior requirements.\nSeparate numerical illustration:20 INDEPENDENT null tests, each type-I error.05, have P(at least one false positive)=1-.95^20=64.151408%. This independence is an illustration and is NOT the false-positive rate of 20 correlated cumulative looks at one experiment. For a defined family of 20 valid tests, Bonferroni uses.05/20=.0025 per test and controls familywise error by union bound without independence, but does not automatically validate arbitrary unplanned continuing looks or multiple objectives. No universal 64% claim for this experiment.\nDeliver assignment/exposure/outcome table, interval, guardrail and stopping/multiplicity specification; fictional experiment/statistics/safety owners with day 0/day 30 hold gate. Real rollout also needs legal/privacy/payment review and explicit authority. No actual randomization, user contact, payment, production change, data export, production analytics change, disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D118 practice\"><h3>Assigned-user inference, safety hold and multiplicity</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d117-d118-validation.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">13-sheet editable score and assignment review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d117-d118-validation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">13-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Separate fictional complete-outcome fixed-horizon onboarding experiment: A 1000 assigned/800 exposed/280 activated; B 1000/900/320. ITT 28%/32%, difference 4 percentage points and relative 14.285714%, versus exposure-conditioned 35%/35.555556%, difference .555556 pp. Exposure is postassignment; not automatic treatment-on-treated identification. Unpooled standard error .020474374, nominal 95% CI [-.012977,8.012977] percentage points includes zero, not equivalence. Failures 20/45 per 1000 rise 2.5 pp; explicit observed hold threshold 1 pp fails, so HOLD despite positive activation. Equality does not exceed this particular rule, but passing is not proof of safety/noninferiority. Low counts hold approximate CI; zero exposure makes affected descriptive rates undefined without changing ITT denominator. 20 independent valid null tests at .05 give 64.151408% at least one false positive; not 20 correlated cumulative looks. Defined-family Bonferroni .0025 per test does not require independence, but valid tests/design and stopping rules still matter. No actual user assignment or production analytics change.</p><p>All 75 baseline formulas independently recomputed. Twenty-seven changed scenario sets plus restoration cover thresholds, class costs, zero/all defaults, changed scores and later labels, assignment/exposure/activation/failures, guardrail equality, low counts, independent-test family size and alpha. Nineteen invalid inputs, independent-case isolation and five broken result references checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after edits. Actual learner attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, new empirical source reading, full audiovisual, learner/account/legal validation or overall 10/10. No real screening, user assignment, experiment, data collection, analytics/product/price change, customer contact, accusation, data export, blocking, disclosure or spend required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"A 280 activations/1000 assigned; B 320/1000 assigned. ITT B-A?","o":["0.555556 percentage points","4 percentage points","14.285714 percentage points","40 percentage points"],"a":1},{"q":"A 280/800 exposed; B 320/900 exposed. Exposure-conditioned B-A?","o":["4 percentage points","5.555556 percentage points","Minus 4 percentage points","0.555556 percentage points"],"a":3},{"q":"Why is comparing only exposed users not automatically the ITT effect?","o":["Exposure is postassignment and treatment-dependent","Randomization always balances exposure-conditioned groups","Exposure removes all missing-outcome uncertainty","ITT requires dropping users who did not comply"],"a":0},{"q":"Observed B-A.04, unpooled SE.020474374, z 1.96. Approx 95% CI in percentage points?","o":["[1.952563,6.047437]","[-0.129773,0.801298]","[-0.012977,8.012977]","[0.027023,0.052977]"],"a":2},{"q":"Guardrail A 20/1000/B 45/1000, hold if observed increase>1 pp. Decision under this rule?","o":["Pass: positive activation overrides safety","Hold: observed increase 2.5 pp exceeds 1 pp","Pass: compare 45 only with 1000 total activations","Hold: increase is exactly 25 pp"],"a":1},{"q":"A standard fixed-horizon p-value is checked repeatedly until first p<.05. What is missing?","o":["Only a larger effect label on the chart","A rule to discard all interim nonsignificant results","Proof that every interim p-value is independent","A valid prespecified sequential inference/stopping design"],"a":3},{"q":"20 independent null tests at 0.05 each. What is the chance of at least one false positive, rounded to two decimal places?","o":["5.00%","100.00%","64.15%","2.50%"],"a":2},{"q":"Defined family 20 valid tests, familywise alpha.05. Bonferroni per-test alpha?","o":["0.0025","0.025","0.05","0.25"],"a":0},{"q":"Does the independent-test 64.15% illustration equal error rate for 20 cumulative correlated looks?","o":["Yes; every pair of p-values is independent by definition","Yes; same number of looks fixes all dependence","No; correlated looks can never inflate any error","No; dependence/stopping design differ"],"a":3},{"q":"If assigned-user outcomes were genuinely missing, is naming an analysis ITT enough to recover them?","o":["Yes; randomization fills every missing outcome exactly","No; missing-outcome strategy and sensitivity remain necessary","Yes; all missing outcomes can be silently dropped","No; ITT must always switch to exposed-user analysis"],"a":1}]},"119":{"objectives":["Attempt fresh prediction-calibration, assignment-denominator and subsidy-counterfactual cases without notes; choose provisional remediation from actual new errors and test delayed changed-context transfer rather than infer measured weakest skills from a day score."],"videos":[{"id":"BElijgjOgSQ","title":"Lecture 3.1 Experiment Design Concepts in a Simple A B Test","by":"IxD Online: UCSD & Coursera","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use fresh written cases and actual attempted errors, not repetition of familiar answer positions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"scikit-learn - calibration and probabilistic scoring","url":"http://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html","note":"Fresh shifted bins require new denominator and calibration checks; aggregate matching or Brier alone is not measured mastery/calibration proof."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State - assignment-based analysis","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat509/lesson/10/10.2","note":"Fresh exposure contrast differs from assignment estimand."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"World Bank DIME - differences-in-differences","url":"https://dimewiki.worldbank.org/Difference-in-Differences","note":"Overstrong wording that DiD removes ALL time-varying factors or comparison checks confirm assumptions is not adopted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IES - retrieval and effective study","url":"https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/resolve-study-effectively-2018","note":"Actual error correction and changed-context retrieval, not a measured app effect or weakest-skill inference from aggregate completion."}],"exercise":"Private mixed retrieval exercise; all numbers, labels, actors and decisions fictional. Attempt without notes before reading solutions. Log actual response, reasoning, confidence, error cause, corrected equation, source/input and decision consequence; select two provisional remediation areas from these observed errors, not a day total or watch timestamp. If evidence is sparse, label it provisional. Delay a retest and change base rates, denominator or timing instead of repeating identical items. These are learning checks, not psychometric or clinical validation.\nFresh prediction:800 loans each score.05 and 200 each score.30=>expected 40+60=100 defaults. New complete labels show 80/800=10% and 80/200=40%, total 160/1000=16%; expected average 10%, observed/expected 1.6. Scores underpredict in both supplied bins. Aggregate disagreement is not a complete explanation of drift, and retrospective refitting on these same labels cannot serve as an untouched test. No actual borrower dataset or scoring action.\nFresh onboarding:500 users assigned each arm, A 300 exposed/150 activated, B 450 exposed/165 activated, complete outcomes for all assigned users; activations all among exposed by stipulation. ITT rates 30%/33%, +3 percentage points. Exposure-conditioned 50%/36.666667%, difference minus 13.333333 percentage points, opposite sign. Postassignment selection means it is not automatically a causal treatment-on-treated effect; do not replace assignment contrast with it. If assigned B outcome count is corrected 165 to 145, ITT becomes 29%-30%=minus 1 percentage point; change only this input and label the correction, not every denominator.\nFresh seller case,50 treated and 50 comparison sellers, fixed membership and equal monthly windows. Per-seller GMV treated 120 to 170, control 100 to 130. Under untreated parallel changes/no anticipation/no spillover/no composition or concurrent differential shock, treatment counterfactual 150, arithmetic DiD(170-120)-(130-100)=20 rupees/seller;50 sellers=>1000 incremental GMV. Marketplace net take after variable cost 8% yields 80; fixed one-month subsidy 100 rupees with no other costs gives minus 20 incremental contribution, not 1000 profit. Not an identified causal estimate from arithmetic alone, uncertainty cannot be inferred from these four aggregate means. Different pre-trend/control evidence could reverse the estimate and requires explicit sensitivity.\nKeep cases separate, no summed investment. Deliver actual error log and corrected tables, fictional evidence/model/experiment/cash owners with day 0/day 7 hold-review gate and delayed fresh retest. No real scoring, loan, user experiment, subsidy, production analytics change, contact, data export, actual permission/signoff, disclosure or spend.<section aria-label=\"Native D119 practice\"><h3>Fresh mixed retrieval, not measured weakest skills</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d119-d120-counterfactual.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable retrieval and counterfactual review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d119-d120-counterfactual.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Three separate fictional fresh cases. Scores 800 at .05/200 at .30 predict 100 defaults; labels 80/80 give 10%/40%, observed 160 and O/E 1.6. No refitting on final labels and calling them untouched test. Assigned A150/500 and B165/500 give ITT +3 pp, versus exposed A150/300 and B165/450 giving -13.333333 pp, not automatic causal treatment-on-treated. Correct only B activations 165 to 145: ITT -1 pp. Seller case 120 to 170 versus 100 to 130, 50 treated, conditional DiD 20/GMV 1000/net 8% minus subsidy 100 => contribution -20. No summed cross-case decision. Choose provisional remediation from actual new errors, not day totals or invented attempts.</p><p>All 64 baseline formulas independently recomputed. Twenty-four changed input sets plus restoration cover corrected activation, zero scores/exposure, changed labels/assignment, seller count/GMV, positive/zero/negative net margin, subsidy/setup, asymmetric gamma and promotion boundaries. Sixteen invalid inputs with independent-case isolation and five broken result references checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after edits. Actual attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, new empirical source reading, full audiovisual, learner/account/legal validation or overall10. No real scoring, user experiment, seller subsidy, rollout, contact, data export, contract, disclosure or spend required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Fresh 800 scores.05/200 scores.30. Expected defaults?","o":["160","80","300","100"],"a":3},{"q":"Fresh labels 80 defaults in 800 low bin/80 in 200 high bin. Empirical rates?","o":["5% low;30% high","10% low;40% high","8% low;8% high","16% low;16% high"],"a":1},{"q":"Locked expected 100 defaults, actual 160. Observed/expected?","o":["0.625","0.16","1.6","1.0"],"a":2},{"q":"Onboarding A 150/500 assigned/B 165/500 assigned. ITT difference?","o":["Plus 3 percentage points","Minus 13.333333 percentage points","Plus 10 percentage points","Plus 0.3 percentage points"],"a":0},{"q":"A 150/300 exposed/B 165/450 exposed. Exposure-conditioned B-A?","o":["Plus 3 percentage points","Plus 13.333333 percentage points","Minus 3 percentage points","Minus 13.333333 percentage points"],"a":3},{"q":"Correction B 145 activations not 165, both assigned 500/A 150 unchanged. New ITT B-A?","o":["Plus 3 percentage points","Minus 4 percentage points","Minus 1 percentage point","Plus 1 percentage point"],"a":2},{"q":"Seller GMV treated 120 to 170/control 100 to 130. Arithmetic DiD per treated seller?","o":["20 rupees","50 rupees","40 rupees","30 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"DiD 20/seller,50 treated,8% net take, subsidy 100 total. Incremental contribution?","o":["Plus 1000 rupees","Minus 20 rupees","Plus 80 rupees","Minus 100 rupees"],"a":1},{"q":"Can four aggregate means alone prove untreated parallel trends and a statistical confidence interval?","o":["Yes; DiD subtraction proves both automatically","Yes; positive treated growth makes trends irrelevant","No; identification and uncertainty need more evidence","No; only if pre means were exactly equal can DiD exist"],"a":2},{"q":"Which basis supports provisional remediation after this review?","o":["A day score proves two measured weakest skills","Repeating the same keyed questions proves transfer","Watching 90% establishes question-level mastery","Actual new errors plus delayed changed-context retest"],"a":3}]},"120":{"objectives":["Separate a marketplace subsidy’s arithmetic difference-in-differences GMV from net platform contribution, state the untreated parallel-trend and no-spillover assumptions, and show sensitivity rather than declare causality from matched-looking controls."],"videos":[{"id":"gRkUhg9Wb-I","title":"14. Causal Inference, Part 1","by":"MIT OpenCourseWare","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. This is broader causal teaching, not direct evidence subsidy controls satisfy DiD assumptions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"World Bank DIME - difference-in-differences","url":"https://dimewiki.worldbank.org/Difference-in-Differences","note":"Two-group/two-period difference formula and comparison logic; not a guarantee all time-varying confounding is removed, not proof of parallel trends from placebo nonrejection."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"World Bank - sensitivity to parallel-trend violations","url":"https://blogs.worldbank.org/en/impactevaluations/revisiting-difference-differences-parallel-trends-assumption-part-ii-what-happens","note":"Pretrend tests can lack power; sensitivity to plausible violations matters. Toy bias range below is not Honest DiD, a confidence set or a universal linear-trend fix."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT - selected predictive versus causal teaching","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gRkUhg9Wb-I","note":"Predictive coefficients/observed group differences do not alone identify intervention benefit; medical examples not marketplace findings."}],"exercise":"Private fictional marketplace, exactly 100 treated/100 comparison sellers with fixed membership across equal one-month pre/post windows, same outcome definition and no missing sales. All rupees, GMV is seller sales volume, NOT platform revenue. Mean monthly GMV per seller: treated 1000 before/1500 after; control 800 before/1040 after. Raw after gap 460 and treated before-after increase 500 are not the subsidy effect. Under stipulated untreated parallel changes, no anticipation, no spillovers, no changing composition or concurrent differential shock, treatment counterfactual 1000+(1040-800)=1240. Arithmetic DiD 1500-1240=260 rupees/seller,100 treated=>26000 incremental GMV. These assumptions are not proven by four means or by unlike seller baselines being stable.\nAn additional older preperiod treated 900/control 700 would give +100/+100 into the immediate preperiod, consistent with parallel pre-changes but not proof of the unobserved post counterfactual. A nonsignificant pre-trend test can have low power; significant small differences can also require substantive size assessment. Need mechanism, comparable definitions, seller selection, shocks, spillovers, composition, repeated preperiods and suitable design/inference. Four aggregate means and 100 sellers per group do not supply standard errors; actual dependence/assignment/cluster structure and seller-level variation matter. No fake p-value or confidence interval here.\nEconomics, incremental basis for this one month: platform take 10% of GMV and variable service cost 4% of GMV =>net 6% of incremental GMV before subsidy. Treat the subsidy as a fixed 2000 total for the treated cohort, plus incremental setup 300 total, both outside the 4% variable cost and paid only in this proposal. Incremental contribution 26000*.06-2000-300=1560-2300=minus 740 rupees. Raw additional treated GMV 500*100=50000 is not all caused by subsidy under this counterfactual; multiplying it by 6% would overstate incremental contribution. Platform total treatment GMV 150000, commission 15000 and variable cost 6000 are levels, not the incremental policy benefit. No recurring or sunk cost is silently added twice.\nAt net 6%, incremental GMV needed to cover 2300 is 38333.333333 expected rupees, or 38334 whole rupees if GMV restricted to integer rupees. With conditional increment 26000 and setup 300, maximum total subsidy for arithmetic break-even is 1260, or 12.60 per treated seller. This is a zero-margin arithmetic bound under supplied assumptions, not an authorized offer, feasible seller response or a robust recommendation; changing subsidy may change behavior.\nTransparent sensitivity, not an estimated statistical interval: let gamma be the treated sellers’ untreated post change MINUS the control group’s 240 change. Parallel changes means gamma 0. A plausible assumed range[-100,+100]rupees/seller gives causal-effect candidate 260-gamma, range[160,360]. Under fixed net 6% and total cost 2300, contribution range[minus 1340, minus 140], still below zero. Bounds are assumption-driven and not a measured confidence set. Do not extrapolate them beyond the specified range, claim they identify a universal effect, or infer every marketplace spillover is covered.\nSpecific contradictory evidence: control-only promotion adds 80 to observed control post 1040; no treated effect of that promotion, pre-values unchanged. If that 80 is credibly separately attributable, control untreated without promotion post 960, growth 160, conditional counterfactual treated 1160, adjusted DiD 340/seller, total 34000, net 2040-2300=minus 260. This adjustment needs independent evidence; not subtracting inconvenient observed data just to improve fit. If promotion interacted with subsidy or affected treated sellers, this simple correction would fail. Document competing explanations rather than silently certify causality.\nDeliver pre/post/counterfactual/GMV-platform-cost and sensitivity tables, source/input registry, fictional causal/data/finance owners with day 0/day 30 hold-review gate. No actual seller subsidy, rollout, merchant contact, data export, experiment, contract, financing, disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D120 practice\"><h3>Marketplace counterfactual, contribution and sensitivity</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d119-d120-counterfactual.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable retrieval and counterfactual review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d119-d120-counterfactual.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written cases remain intact.</p><p>Fictional fixed one-month treated 1000 to 1500/control 800 to 1040: counterfactual 1240, arithmetic DiD 260 per seller, 100 sellers => 26000 incremental GMV. Commission 10% less variable 4% yields 1560 before cohort subsidy 2000/setup 300: contribution -740. Levels 150000 GMV / 15000 commission/6000 variable cost are not incremental benefit. Positive net 6% requires 38333.333333 expected or 38334 whole-rupee incremental GMV; maximum conditional subsidy 1260 total/12.60 per seller, not authority or a behavior-invariant recommendation. Gamma is treated-minus-control untreated post change: effect 260-gamma. Assumed gamma [-100,100] gives effect [160,360] and contribution [-1340,-140], not a statistical confidence interval. Economic endpoints sort correctly even if net margin reverses. Credibly separately attributable control-only promotion 80 gives corrected control 960, conditional effect 340 and contribution -260; not permission to remove inconvenient controls. Equal older prechanges 100/100 do not prove missing post counterfactual. Four means/counts cannot supply statistical uncertainty.</p><p>All 64 baseline formulas independently recomputed. Twenty-four changed input sets plus restoration cover corrected activation, zero scores/exposure, changed labels/assignment, seller count/GMV, positive/zero/negative net margin, subsidy/setup, asymmetric gamma and promotion boundaries. Sixteen invalid inputs with independent-case isolation and five broken result references checked. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after edits. Actual attempts, confidence, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, new empirical source reading, full audiovisual, learner/account/legal validation or overall10. No real scoring, user experiment, seller subsidy, rollout, contact, data export, contract, disclosure or spend required or authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Treated GMV 1000 to 1500/control 800 to 1040. Arithmetic DiD per treated seller?","o":["260 rupees","500 rupees","460 rupees","240 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"Under parallel untreated changes, treated counterfactual post GMV?","o":["1040 rupees","1500 rupees","1240 rupees","1260 rupees"],"a":2},{"q":"Conditional DiD 260/seller,100 treated. Incremental GMV?","o":["50000 rupees","150000 rupees","46000 rupees","26000 rupees"],"a":3},{"q":"Platform take 10%, variable cost 4% of incremental GMV 26000, subsidy 2000/setup 300. Incremental contribution?","o":["Plus 1560 rupees","Minus 740 rupees","Plus 700 rupees","Plus 26000 rupees"],"a":1},{"q":"At net 6% and 2300 total incremental cost, minimum WHOLE-rupee incremental GMV to break even?","o":["38334 rupees","38333 rupees","26000 rupees","2300 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"Conditional incremental GMV 26000/net 6%, setup 300. Max total subsidy for arithmetic zero contribution?","o":["1560 rupees","2000 rupees","1260 rupees","2300 rupees"],"a":2},{"q":"Does older preperiod 900/700 to immediate pre 1000/800 prove post-parallel counterfactual?","o":["Yes; equal 100 changes eliminate every later confounder","No; matching pre-changes support but do not prove it","Yes; DiD always removes any time-varying shock","No; different baseline levels always make DiD invalid"],"a":1},{"q":"gamma is treated-minus-control untreated post change, assumed[-100,+100]. Candidate effect 260-gamma range?","o":["[260,360]rupees/seller","[Minus 100, Plus 100]rupees/seller","[360,460]rupees/seller","[160,360]rupees/seller"],"a":3},{"q":"Effect range 160 to 360/seller,100 treated/net 6%/cost 2300. Contribution range?","o":["[Plus 160, Plus 360]rupees","[Minus 1340, Minus 140]rupees","[Minus 740, Plus 740]rupees","[Plus 960, Plus 2160]rupees"],"a":1},{"q":"Control promotion adds 80 to 1040 post, no treated spillover/pre-change. After credible removal ofonlythat 80, conditional adjusted DiD?","o":["340 rupees/seller","180 rupees/seller","260 rupees/seller","420 rupees/seller"],"a":0}]},"121":{"objectives":["Evaluate frozen, horizon-matched rolling-origin forecasts against a seasonal baseline; separate forecast errors, asymmetric inventory costs and stockout-censored sales instead of treating random time splits or RMSE alone as operational proof."],"videos":[{"id":"Aw77aMLj9uM","title":"Time Series Forecasting Theory | AR, MA, ARMA, ARIMA | Data Science","by":"AnalyticsU","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Written forecasting sources fill the specific rolling-origin, baseline and censoring teaching."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Forecasting: Principles and Practice - rolling-origin evaluation","url":"https://otexts.com/fpptr/tscv.html","note":"Training uses only prior information; match the operational forecast horizon. Lowest RMSE is not a universal inventory-cost decision rule."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Hyndman - selected forecast-evaluation slides","url":"https://robjhyndman.com/files/1-ForecastEvaluation.pdf","note":"Selected seasonal-naive definitions, test-set metrics/MASE and rolling-origin formula read, not whole slides or diagram images. MAE/RMSE units and denominators matter; historical stock examples not current forecasts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Mersereau2015 - censored demand, publisher abstract","url":"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/msom.2015.0520","note":"Inventory limits observed sales. Use stockout records and inventory uncertainty to distinguish sales from demand; do not assume an unspecified demand-recovery algorithm."}],"exercise":"Private fictional grocery forecasting exercise, one SKU, units per festival-relative week. No actual festival dates, retailer sales, replenishment, supplier contact or order. Four rolling forecast origins each issue a one-week-ahead forecast using only information available at that origin. The fixed one-week lead time, SKU, eligibility and outcome definition match across methods. Forecasts are frozen before each outcome. We stipulate that all four realized demands are fully observed with adequate inventory; a separate censored-sales example follows. Seasonal baseline A repeats the same festival-relative week from the previous synthetic season, not the same civil-calendar date. Candidate B and C rules were locked on earlier development data, not chosen after seeing these four outcomes. This tiny descriptive backtest does not establish deployment performance or statistical superiority.\nRealized demands [100,120,80,140]. Seasonal baseline A forecasts [90,110,100,130], errors actual-minus-forecast [10,10,-20,10]. MAE 50/4=12.5 units; MSE 700/4=175 units squared; RMSE sqrt 175=13.228757 units; mean signed error 10/4=2.5 units, positive means underforecast on average. Candidate B [100,120,100,140], errors [0,0,-20,0], MAE 5/RMSE 10/mean error-5. Its 20 excess units and zero shortage units differ from A's 20 excess/30 shortage. Zero signed average error alone would not imply small absolute errors.\nToy operational loss, no actual procurement: shortage 4 rupees/unit plus excess 1 rupee/unit, additive over these four separate windows with no carryover, salvage, substitution, backorders or lost-customer effects. A costs 30*4+20*1=140; B costs 0*4+20*1=20. Candidate C [100,120,80,130] has errors [0,0,0,10], RMSE 5/MAE 2.5 but shortage 10, cost 40. Thus C has lower RMSE than B yet higher specified inventory loss; metric ranking and operational cost ranking can differ. These point forecasts are not order quantities automatically. A real inventory policy needs lead-time demand distributions, service goals, unit costs, capacities, order timing and actual authority.\nSeparate censored observation: available 90 units, observed sales 90 and a known stockout. In a lost-sales/no-backorder model sales=min(demand, availability), so demand>=90, not known to equal 90 or some invented lost-sales number. Forecast 110 therefore does NOT have a verified demand error of-20 just because sales were 90. With adequate availability 150 and sales 90, demand 90 is observable under the model. Inventory-record inaccuracy can also make the stockout indicator unreliable. Log inventory, availability, returns, substitutions and promotions rather than silently using capped sales as unconstrained demand.\nRandomly mixing later weeks into training for earlier forecasts leaks time information. Fit transformations, imputation, feature engineering and tuning within the past-only development folds; exogenous features must have been genuinely available at issue time, not realized weather or future promotion outcomes. If procurement needs four-week lead time, evaluate four-step forecasts and appropriate lead-time aggregation, not one-step scores as if they were equivalent. Reserve a final untouched period after model selection; a rolling backtest used to select models is not also an untouched final test. Calendar/festival alignment and structural shifts need explicit review, not automatic transfer of last year's pattern.\nDeliver origin/as-of/features/horizon ledger, demand/forecast/error/cost table and censored-data exception log; fictional forecasting/inventory/cash owners with day 0/day 30 hold-review gate. No actual data export, order, inventory change, rollout, purchase, disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D121 practice\"><h3>Frozen rolling forecasts, cost ranks and censored sales</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d121-forecast-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable forecast and censoring review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d121-forecast-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written case remains intact.</p><p>Four fictional past-only one-week forecasts with a synthetic issue/target/feature-as-of ledger, not real timestamps. Seasonal A gives MAE 12.5/MSE 175/RMSE 13.228757/mean error +2.5 units; B RMSE 10 and toy loss 20; C RMSE 5 but loss 40 at shortage/excess costs 4/1 INR per unit. Current forecast deviations recompute the B/C shortage-cost tie, baseline 2 INR/unit. Forecast-error ranking is not automatically an inventory policy. Metadata checks cannot prove unseen training or feature availability. Separate availability 90/sales 90/known-stockout case gives demand at least 90, exact demand/error UNKNOWN and error lower bound -20 with no finite upper bound for forecast 110. Adequate availability 150/sales 90/no stockout gives exact demand 90 and error -20 only under the stated lost-sales/no-backorder model. Missing stockout flags do not mean zero. No invented lost demand or order quantity.</p><p>All 112 baseline formulas independently recomputed, including unknown-status text. Twenty changed input sets plus restoration cover demand, zero and fractional forecasts, cost ties/reversals, zero costs, past as-of and censored versus adequate-stock observations. Eighteen invalid cases/774 checks preserve the independent case; six broken result references/348 checks hold the affected Audit. All 12 native PDF pages inspected, source-row clipping fixed. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after edits. Actual attempts, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not native Excel/Sheets parity, actual model training, empirical forecast, full audiovisual, independent learner/account/legal validation or overall 10/10. Selected rolling-origin text, seasonal-naive/error slides and publisher abstract rechecked, not the full slide deck or demand paper. No real data export, supplier contact, replenishment, inventory change, rollout, order, disclosure or spend authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Actual [100,120,80,140], A forecasts [90,110,100,130]. MAE?","o":["2.5 units","12.5 units","13.228757 units","50 units"],"a":1},{"q":"A errors [10,10,-20,10]. RMSE?","o":["12.5 units","175 units","2.5 units","13.228757 units"],"a":3},{"q":"A errors actual-minus-forecast [10,10,-20,10]. Mean signed error?","o":["Plus 2.5 units","Plus 12.5 units","Minus 2.5 units","Zero units"],"a":0},{"q":"B errors [0,0,-20,0]. RMSE?","o":["5 units","20 units","10 units","100 units"],"a":2},{"q":"Shortage 4/excess 1 per unit. A shortage 30/excess 20. Toy total loss?","o":["50 rupees","140 rupees","120 rupees","80 rupees"],"a":1},{"q":"B shortage 0/excess 20; C shortage 10/excess 0. At shortage 4/excess 1, cost ranking?","o":["C 10 rupees beats B 20 rupees","B 80 rupees loses to C 40 rupees","Both cost 20 rupees","B 20 rupees beats C 40 rupees"],"a":3},{"q":"Availability 90, sales 90, known stockout, lost-sales model. Verified latent demand?","o":["Exactly 90 because sales equal demand always","Exactly 110 because that was the forecast","At least 90; exact value unknown","Exactly 180 because every stockout doubles demand"],"a":2},{"q":"Forecast 110, stockout-capped sales 90. Can demand forecast error be certified as-20?","o":["No; demand is censored, not known 90","Yes; every sales error is a demand error","Yes; no inventory record is needed","No; demand error is always zero at stockout"],"a":0},{"q":"Operation needs four-week-ahead forecasts. Which evaluation fits?","o":["Randomly split all dates and use one-step fit error","Use full-series transformations before splitting dates","Rank four-week policy solely by one-week residuals","Past-only rolling origins at matching four-step horizon"],"a":3},{"q":"What is the supplied seasonal-naive baseline matching?","o":["Latest future week after the forecast origin","Prior synthetic season’s same festival-relative week","Average of all later realized festival sales","The civil-calendar date regardless of festival shift"],"a":1}]},"122":{"objectives":["Fold back a private signal-before-commitment decision tree with Bayes updates, distinguish EVSI/EVPI from net gain after test and delay costs, and preserve the ability to change action instead of buying information after irreversible scale-up."],"videos":[{"id":"ydvnVw80I_8","title":"Decision Analysis 3: Decision Trees","by":"Joshua Emmanuel","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Expected-value foldback fits, but its stock/mutual-fund example is not investment advice or a signal/Bayes EVSI lesson; written source supplies that part."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Dartmouth ENGS41 - value-of-information teaching","url":"https://cushman.host.dartmouth.edu/courses/engs41/Value-of-info.pdf","note":"EVSI excludes acquisition cost; net gain subtracts cost and delay. Source says EVSI>0 justifies purchase, but that alone is insufficient; EVSI<=EVPI can include equality. Rounded source probabilities not model inputs."}],"exercise":"Private fictional deeptech choice, all payoffs in lakh rupees at one common valuation date. Risk-neutral expected net monetary payoff criterion, not risk-adjusted utility, financing feasibility or an actual pilot/scale-up approval. Two fixed states: technically/commercially successful with prior 0.4, failure 0.6. Scale gives+100 if successful or-40 if failure; these are NET terminal present values including all scale investment and operating cash flows, no additional capex subtraction. Stop gives 0 in either state. No-test scale EV 0.4*100+0.6*(-40)=16, better than stop 0 but still downside-40. Risk capacity may reject the EV-best choice; no guarantee or permission follows.\nOptional test occurs BEFORE irrevocable scale decision, does not itself change the state or action payoffs, and preserves both scale/stop choices. Conditional signal rates P(+|success)=0.8, P(+|failure)=0.2; negative complements 0.2/0.8. Joint probabilities success+0.32/failure+0.12/success-0.08/failure-0.48, sum 1. P(+)=0.44/P(-)=0.56. Posterior success given+=0.32/.44=8/11=72.727273%; given-=.08/.56=1/7=14.285714%. Do not mistake signal sensitivity 80% for posterior success or use the prior again after observing signal.\nScale conditional EV=140 p-40, break-even p=2/7=28.571429%, stop 0. After+ scale EV 680/11=61.818182; after- scale EV-20 so stop. Gross optimal expected payoff with signal .44*61.818182+.56*0=27.2. Gross EVSI 27.2-16=11.2, not 27.2 and not net of test cost. Test acquisition cost 5 plus separate present-valued delay/opportunity cost 2, each paid once on every test path and not in terminal scale payoffs, gives test-policy EV 27.2-5-2=20.2. Net gain versus best no-test 16 is 4.2. Under fixed signal/payoff assumptions, maximum acquisition cost with delay 2 is 9.2 at break-even; actual cost 10 gives net policy 15.2, inferior to no-test 16 despite positive gross EVSI.\nPerfect information before choice allows scale only in success: EV with PI.4*100+.6*0=40, EVPI 40-16=24. Under same decision/state/payoff assumptions, 0<=EVSI 11.2<=EVPI 24. Sample information can equal perfect information in decision value, and uninformative tests may have 0 gross EVSI; positive gross EVSI is not proof a costly test is worthwhile. No test perfects the underlying state or guarantees safety.\nTiming counterexample: if irrevocable scale choice already made and neither payoff nor later action can change, this test has zero decision value for this specified choice, though it may alter beliefs. Paying 5+2 then reduces expected payoff 16 to 9. Other possible uses of information require new defined decisions, not an invented benefit. If the pilot actually changes product performance, scale cost, delay or salvage, it is not a pure information-only test and the model must include those causal effects rather than call all gains EVSI. Deliver joint tree, posterior/action/payoff table, acquisition/delay boundary and explicit ability-to-stop check, source registry and fictional technical/cash/governance owners with day 0/day 30 hold gate. No actual research purchase, pilot, factory commitment, financing, order, contact, disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D122 practice\"><h3>Signal before commitment, not purchase approval</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d122-information-value.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable signal and foldback review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d122-information-value.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written case remains intact.</p><p>Fictional prior .4, scale net terminal payoffs +100/-40 lakh INR, stop0. Positive likelihoods .8/.2 yield joint .32/.12/.08/.48 and posteriors8/11 and1/7. After positive scale; after negative stop. Best no-test16, gross with-signal27.2, EVSI11.2, perfect-info gross40/EVPI24. Acquisition5 and separate PV delay2 each paid once give policy20.2 and net gain4.2; conditional acquisition ceiling9.2. Cost10 makes policy15.2 inferior despite positive gross EVSI. Once irrevocable scale cannot change any action/payoff, free information decision value0; paying5+2 lowers committed EV16 to9. A pilot changing state/performance/payoffs is not a pure information-only test. Zero-probability signals have UNDEFINED posterior and UNREACHED action, weighted value0.</p><p>All 40 baseline formulas independently recomputed. Twenty-four changed sets plus restoration cover prior0/1, payoff boundaries, informative, inverted, perfect, uninformative and impossible signals, cost tie/high costs/negative cost ceiling. Fourteen invalid cases/258 checks preserve valid gross arithmetic when only costs fail; seven broken references/112 checks hold affected summaries. All 10 PDF pages inspected. Static prose is Case-v1 and needs revision after edits; actual attempts, errors, reviewer words and delayed transfer remain blank. Selected Dartmouth teaching text rechecked, not all diagrams/full AV. Source purchase inference corrected: EVSI>0 is insufficient when costs apply, and EVSI<=EVPI can include equality. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not native Excel/Sheets parity, empirical signal calibration, risk/financing feasibility, independent learner/account/legal validation or overall10. No real research purchase, pilot, financing, scale-up, contact, disclosure or spend authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Prior success 0.4; scale payoffs+100/-40, stop 0. Best no-test EV?","o":["40 lakh","60 lakh","16 lakh","Minus 24 lakh"],"a":2},{"q":"P(success)=.4/P(+|success)=.8/P(+|failure)=.2. P(+)?","o":["0.44","0.80","0.32","0.20"],"a":0},{"q":"Joint success and positive signal probability 0.32; P(positive)=0.44. What is P(success | positive), rounded to two decimal places?","o":["80.00%","40.00%","32.00%","72.73%"],"a":3},{"q":"Joint success and negative signal probability 0.08; P(negative)=0.56. What is P(success | negative), rounded to two decimal places?","o":["20.00%","14.29%","40.00%","56.00%"],"a":1},{"q":"Scale expected value = 140p-40. What probability gives arithmetic break-even, to two decimal places?","o":["40.00%","50.00%","28.57%","72.73%"],"a":2},{"q":"After+ scale EV 61.818182/after- stop 0, P(+).44. Gross with-signal EV?","o":["27.2 lakh","11.2 lakh","20.2 lakh","16 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Gross with-signal 27.2/best no-test 16. EVSI?","o":["27.2 lakh","11.2 lakh","4.2 lakh","24 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"Test cost 5/delay 2, gross with-signal 27.2/no-test 16. Net gain from test?","o":["11.2 lakh","20.2 lakh","Minus 7 lakh","4.2 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Perfect info lets scale only in success 0.4, payoff 100. EVPI versus no-test 16?","o":["24 lakh","40 lakh","11.2 lakh","16 lakh"],"a":0},{"q":"Irrevocable scale already committed; test cannot change any later action/payoff. Decision value for this choice before its costs?","o":["11.2 lakh","24 lakh","Zero","27.2 lakh"],"a":2}]},"123":{"objectives":["Start a fictional growth forecast from an explicit reference-class prior, update odds only with defined conditional evidence, and score frozen forecasts without confusing hindsight fit, binary Brier scaling or one small ledger with proven calibration."],"videos":[{"id":"pedNak4S9IE","title":"Superforecasting | Philip Tetlock","by":"The Long Now Foundation","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Long Now written talk summary is also not the full talk."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Long Now - written Tetlock talk summary","url":"https://longnow.org/talks/02015-tetlock/","note":"Outside-in reference classes, numerical forecasts and keeping score; historical 30% tournament advantage, calibration/resolution shorthand and automatic extremizing claims not independently validated or adopted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State - conditional probabilities and predictive values","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat509/lesson/17/17.3","note":"Use Bayes' theorem with matching denominators and likelihood ratios. Medical examples are not venture-growth base rates or evidence of the startup's success probability."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"scikit-learn - calibration and proper scores","url":"http://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html","note":"Explicit binary squared-error convention; Brier combines calibration/resolution/uncertainty, not calibration-only proof."}],"exercise":"Private fictional investment-committee forecast, not an actual company, security, fundraising decision or investment recommendation. Define event before evidence: an eligible fictional firm grows audited same-definition annual recurring revenue by at least 30% over the next 12 months from a fixed baseline. Specify currency, acquisition/price effects, source, cutoff and resolution rule in a ledger; ambiguous/incomplete outcomes remain unresolved under the rule, not silently counted as failures or removed to improve scores. No current Indian company/base-rate data supplied.\nSynthetic reference class of 100 comparable completed firm-windows has 20 successes/80 failures. Treat 20% as an assumed prior for this exercise, not a precise universal startup rate, a causal model or evidence reference-class selection was valid. A defined independent audit signal has P(signal|success)=.60 and P(signal|failure)=.30, giving likelihood ratio 2. Prior odds.2/.8=1/4, posterior odds 1/2, posterior probability 1/3=33.333333%. Frequency check: among 100 imagined prior cases 12 success-signals and 24 failure-signals, so 12/36=1/3. A vivid founder story is not an extra quantified signal without conditional-rate evidence.\nA second newsletter repeats the exact same audit report. It is not conditionally independent new evidence, so counting another LR 2 would wrongly change odds 1/2 to 1 and probability 50%. Correct probability remains 1/3 if there is literally no new information. Distinct truly conditionally independent signals can multiply likelihood ratios under their model; do not assume independence from two URLs, two analysts or correlated market data. If genuinely revised relevant prior is 10% instead of 20%, the same LR 2 gives posterior odds 2/9/probability 2/11=18.181818%, not 33.333333; log which prior and evidence changed.\nSeparate locked binary forecast ledger of four DIFFERENT resolved events uses p=[.8,.7,.3,.2], outcomes y=[1,0,1,0], same forecast horizon and equal weight. Binary Brier=(1/4)sum(p-y)^2=(.04+.49+.49+.04)/4=.265, range 0 to 1 under THIS convention, lower better. Constant precommitted p=.5 yields.25 on these outcomes; our tiny ledger has worse average squared error by.015. It does not establish that .5 is generally better, every confident forecast is wrong or the team is calibrated. Sum over both binary class coordinates would be twice these scores; do not compare unlabelled conventions. Rewriting probabilities after outcomes creates hindsight fit, not improved forecasting.\nFor a calibration thought check,10 forecasts all.8 with 8 events occurring match that bin's empirical 80%; this is small-sample descriptive agreement, not proven population calibration or resolution. Two realized outcomes cannot certify a 2/3 forecast was wrong just because one failed; probability forecasts are assessed over many predeclared comparable events with uncertainty and discrimination considered. Do not automatically push group averages farther from.5 without justified model/evidence. Record forecast versions, update times and reasons before resolution, not a retrospective edited final number. Deliver prior/joint/odds/duplicate-evidence table and frozen forecast ledger/Brier/error analysis; fictional evidence/forecast/review owners with day 0/day 30 hold-review gate. No actual investment, trade, new research purchase, founder contact, data sharing, public prediction, disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D123 practice\"><h3>Base rates, duplicate evidence and frozen scores</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d123-forecast-ledger.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable Bayes and forecast ledger (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d123-forecast-ledger.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written case remains intact.</p><p>Fictional reference class 20/100, signal rates .6/.3 gives joint .12/.24 and posterior 1/3. Exact same-audit newsletter adds no information: posterior stays 1/3; naive independent-repeat assumption wrongly gives .5. Separate revised prior .1 gives2/11. Boundary priors, infinite odds and impossible-signal UNDEFINED handled. Four different frozen event forecasts .8, .7, .3, .2 versus outcomes 1, 0, 1, 0 give binary Brier .265 against precommitted .5 benchmark .25: higher loss by .015 on this ledger only. Two-coordinate convention doubles both scores. Ten same-bin forecasts .8 with eight successes is tiny-sample descriptive agreement, not population calibration or resolution proof.</p><p>All 35 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 24 changed sets plus restoration; 17 invalid cases/465 isolation checks; seven broken references/11 checks. Missing outcomes hold aggregate rather than become failures or dropped rows. Synthetic lock copies and issue/resolve day offsets check internal consistency, not immutable timestamp proof; editing both copies can evade equality. All 10 PDF pages inspected. Static prose is Case-v1 and requires revision after edits; actual attempts, errors, reviewer words and transfer fields remain blank. Selected Penn State, scikit-learn and Long Now written text checked, not full talk, audiovisual continuity, source diagrams or empirical venture-base-rate validation. Authored LibreOffice execution only, not Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal or overall 10. No actual investment, trade, research purchase, founder contact, public prediction, disclosure or spend authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Synthetic reference class 20 successes/80 failures. Prior odds of success?","o":["1 to 5","4 to 1","20 to 1","1 to 4"],"a":3},{"q":"Prior success 0.2; positive-signal probabilities 0.6 if success and 0.3 if failure. What is the positive-signal posterior, rounded to two decimal places?","o":["60.00%","33.33%","20.00%","50.00%"],"a":1},{"q":"Among 100 imagined cases,12 successful signals and 24 failed signals. P(success|signal)?","o":["12/20","24/80","12/36","36/100"],"a":2},{"q":"Newsletter repeats exactly the same audit report, no new information. Updated probability from 1/3?","o":["1/3","1/2","2/3","1/5"],"a":0},{"q":"Revised relevant prior 10%, same LR 2. Posterior success probability?","o":["1/3=33.333333%","20%","2/9=22.222222%","2/11=18.181818%"],"a":3},{"q":"Frozen p[.8,.7,.3,.2], outcomes[1,0,1,0]. Binary average squared-error Brier?","o":["0.53","0.265","0.25","1.06"],"a":1},{"q":"Precommitted constant.5 gives Brier.25 versus ledger.265. Supported conclusion?","o":["This four-event ledger has higher squared error","The team is proved uncalibrated in every population","Every forecast should forever be.5","Post-outcome editing would validate improvement"],"a":0},{"q":"Why not compare binary Brier.265 directly with two-coordinate score.53 as if methods differ?","o":["The outcomes must have changed automatically","One is necessarily a causal treatment estimate","They use different stated scaling conventions","Brier cannot be used for binary events"],"a":2},{"q":"Ten forecasts all.8, eight events occur. What is established?","o":["Perfect population calibration is proven","That bin matches empirical 80% in a tiny sample","All future.8 forecasts must succeed","Resolution is proven maximal across events"],"a":1},{"q":"Which forecast ledger practice preserves honest evaluation?","o":["Replace old forecasts after outcomes without retaining history","Drop every unresolved failure to improve the score","Treat two articles quoting one audit as independent evidence","Timestamp versions before resolution with stated update reasons"],"a":3}]},"124":{"objectives":["Correct a fictional churn-dependent valuation, compare a same-date BATNA to an all-in negotiated price, and distinguish truthful diligence through authorized channels from bluffing or indiscriminate disclosure of confidential customer facts."],"videos":[{"id":"RfTalFEeKKE","title":"The Harvard Principles of Negotiation","by":"Erich Pommer Institut","note":"Quiz unlocks at 90% watched. Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Broad people/issues/interests/criteria/options principles, not a guarantee of win-win or a legal duty to broadcast confidential churn."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Harvard PON - compare a deal with BATNA","url":"https://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/batna/negotiation-and-bargaining-with-your-batna-in-mind/","note":"Avoid agreeing to a worse deal merely to avoid impasse; historical experiment percentages not independently verified or universal."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Harvard PON - facts, predictions and private preferences","url":"https://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/business-negotiations/how-much-should-you-share/","note":"Relevant factual corrections differ from valuation opinions and reservation preferences; evaluate confidentiality and authorized scope. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Harvard PON - BATNA disclosure and no bluffing","url":"https://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/batna/negotiation-research-you-can-use-should-you-brandish-your-batna-nb/","note":"Do not fabricate alternatives; private BATNA need not automatically be revealed. Source conflates weak BATNA with WATNA, not adopted: best weak alternative is not the worst alternative."}],"exercise":"Private fictional investor diligence and negotiation memo, no actual counterparty, churn records, transaction or outbound message. Amounts in crore rupees, all values at one common valuation date. Buyer seeks one specified asset claim; payoffs include all future cash flows once through a stipulated 4 x annual contribution valuation shorthand, not a DCF, market multiple evidence or independent fairness opinion. No debt, tax, capex or working-capital adjustments in this toy case; actual valuation would need them.\nSeller pack originally assumed annual recurring revenue 10, variable service costs 20% of revenue, fixed annual cost 2: contribution 10*.8-2=6, toy asset value 24. During pre-signing diligence an already-effective customer departure reduces same-definition annual recurring revenue to 8, verified within the fictional facts; no new customer offsets/price effects. New contribution 8*.8-2=4.4, toy value 17.6, decline 6.4 from 24. Revenue down 20% does not make value down 20% with fixed cost 2. Do not continue circulating the old 10/6/24 as current after verifying this correction. In a real case distinguish signed departure notice, cohort/date/contract basis, revenue recognition and unverified rumor; not every clue is confirmed material fact.\nSame-date incremental buyer utility comparison, not alternative project gross value: available BATNA produces net surplus 1.2 after all its costs/risk convention already reflected; must remain genuinely available on comparable timing/capital/conditions. Proposed transaction yields toy value 17.6 minus price P minus incremental transaction costs.4. Therefore buyer walk-away price bound P<=17.6-.4-1.2=16. At P 17 transaction surplus.2 is below BATNA 1.2 by 1; at P 16 surplus 1.2 ties BATNA, no strictly positive advantage. Price 16 is an arithmetic reservation ceiling, not an opening offer, approved price, seller acceptance or authority to sign. Old valuation would have implied 22.4 ceiling under same conventions, now stale. Historical diligence spending.3 already paid and unrecoverable is sunk, not subtracted from only one future choice or used to justify signing. If BATNA expires or needs new costs, recompute rather than bluff that it exists.\nEthical process in this fictional memo: stop relying on stale pack/representations, separate verified facts from estimates, preserve source/date and correction history, and route the specific churn/value discrepancy to the designated authorized internal diligence/legal owners before signing or making a factual assertion. A representation known to be false must not be repeated as current merely to get favorable terms. Do not fabricate another offer, conceal a verified correction while affirming the old claim, or label the resulting price as seller-approved. A private reservation price or genuine BATNA is not the same as a material customer fact; keeping it private does not require a lie. A truthful response can decline to discuss alternatives rather than invent them.\nNo jurisdiction, contract, NDA, party role or legal disclosure rule has been verified; these sources teach negotiation, not Indian securities/company-law duties. Do not infer that an investor must disclose every private diligence finding to the seller, other bidders or the public, or that an NDA permits false statements. Determine who is entitled/authorized to receive which facts and obtain case-specific legal advice through the actual owner before any disclosure; if authority or legal duty is unresolved, hold the affected assertion/signing/disclosure while internal authorized review proceeds. No real customer names or contract attachments belong in this fictional deliverable.\nDeliver before/after contribution and value bridge, BATNA-versus-deal utility table, reservation ceiling/sunk-cost check, fact/opinion/preference and recipient-authority registry, plus truthful internal-review draft FOR REVIEW ONLY, not a sent message. Fictional evidence/legal/cash owners with day 0/day 7 hold-review gates. No actual seller contact, offer, public accusation, confidential-data transfer, contract, financing, purchase, signature, disclosure or spend authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D124 practice\"><h3>Corrected value, comparable BATNA and disclosure scope</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d124-batna-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable value and BATNA review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d124-batna-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written case remains intact.</p><p>Fictional old ARR 10, variable costs 20%, fixed 2 yields contribution 6/toy value 24 at 4x. Verified case departure gives ARR 8, contribution 4.4/value 17.6: value decline 6.4 or 26.666667%, not revenue decline 20%. Net comparable BATNA 1.2 and incremental deal fees .4 give conditional reservation ceiling 16. Proposed price 17 gives deal surplus .2, below BATNA by 1; price 16 ties. Old ceiling 22.4 is stale. Common sunk diligence .3 excluded from both forward alternatives; lifetime columns subtract it from both without changing their difference. Unavailable or unknown BATNA holds conditional comparison while preserving hypothetical arithmetic. Zero old-value percentage UNDEFINED; negative reservation ceiling remains visible.</p><p>All 32 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 24 changed sets plus restoration; 15 invalid cases/220 isolation checks; seven broken references/35 checks. All 10 PDF pages inspected. Fictional fact, estimate and private-preference registry separates the ARR correction from reservation/BATNA preferences and unresolved recipient rights. Fictional internal-review text is FOR REVIEW ONLY, not addressed to any real recipient or sent as the user. No customer names or attachments. Selected PON written text distinguishes information categories and no bluffing; weak best alternative is not WATNA. Fresh third article fetch was title/date only, not full text or empirical-percentage validation. Static prose is Case-v 1, revise after edits; actual attempts, errors, reviewer words and transfer remain blank. Authored LibreOffice only, not Excel/Sheets parity, full AV, independent learner, actual Indian legal/account clearance or overall 10/10. No real seller contact, offer, signing, data transfer, transaction, disclosure or spend authorized.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Old revenue 10, variable cost 20%, fixed 2. Annual contribution?","o":["6 crore","8 crore","10 crore","4.4 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Verified churn reduces same-definition revenue 10 to 8, costs 20% variable/fixed 2. New contribution?","o":["4.8 crore","6.4 crore","4.4 crore","6 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Toy value 4 times annual contribution 4.4. Updated value?","o":["24 crore","17.6 crore","19.2 crore","16 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Updated value 17.6, incremental fees.4, comparable available BATNA net 1.2. Reservation price ceiling?","o":["17.2 crore","18.4 crore","22.4 crore","16 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"At price 17, updated value 17.6/fees.4/BATNA 1.2. Proposed deal net surplus?","o":["0.2 crore, below BATNA by 1 crore","1.2 crore, equal BATNA","1.6 crore, above BATNA by.4 crore","6.6 crore, above BATNA by 5.4 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"At price 16 under same assumptions, deal surplus versus BATNA 1.2?","o":["Higher 2.2 crore; gain 1 crore","Lower.2 crore; loss 1 crore","Equal 1.2 crore; no strict advantage","Equal 16 crore because price is payoff"],"a":2},{"q":"Earlier diligence.3 crore already paid/unrecoverable. How enter forward choice comparison?","o":["Subtract it only from BATNA to force deal acceptance","Add it to the value to avoid booking a loss","Treat signing as mandatory to recover it","Exclude same sunk cost from both future alternatives"],"a":3},{"q":"Seller pack 10 revenue now verified 8. What is an ethical internal next step before signing?","o":["Affirm 10 is current while hiding correction to lower price","Preserve correction and route to authorized diligence/legal owners","Broadcast customer records to all competing bidders","Delete departure evidence so the old pack looks consistent"],"a":1},{"q":"Can one keep a private BATNA/reservation price without inventing another offer?","o":["No; ethics always requires publishing the full BATNA","Yes; fabricate a stronger offer if never challenged","Yes; decline to discuss it truthfully","No; every private preference is fraudulent concealment"],"a":2},{"q":"Does this generic negotiation teaching establish Indian legal duties to disclose all churn findings to every bidder?","o":["No; role, contract, law and recipient authority are unresolved","Yes; PON articles are binding Indian transaction law","Yes; any NDA authorizes public customer disclosure","No; therefore known false representations may be repeated"],"a":0}]},"125":{"objectives":["Build a portfolio map from explicit market boundaries, growth periods and relative-share denominators; treat quadrants as hypotheses, and refuse unsupported Titan placements or double-counted CaratLane revenue."],"videos":[{"id":"gNR49lk5dS0","title":"BCG Matrix (Growth-Share Matrix) EXPLAINED — Business To You","by":"Business To You","note":"Use the written BCG readings and explicit fictional portfolio for the calculations. Interpret historical examples critically rather than treating a translated or unclear media passage as a fact."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"BCG - growth-share framework","url":"https://www.bcg.com/about/overview/our-history/growth-share-matrix","note":"Classical allocation logic, not proof that every high-share unit has superior returns or that all pets are worthless."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"BCG - revisiting the matrix","url":"https://www.bcg.com/publications/2014/growth-share-matrix-bcg-classics-revisited","note":"Historical study uses a third-ranked competitor and relative growth, unlike the classroom convention. Historical statistics and Google examples are not current facts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"McKinsey - nine-box written introduction","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/enduring-ideas-the-ge-and-mckinsey-nine-box-matrix","note":"Industry attractiveness/competitive strength with judgment and trade-offs; nine boxes, not 2 x 2."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Titan - dated Q4FY26 company release","url":"https://www.titancompany.in/news/titan-FY26-results","note":"Headline/body jewellery conflict preserved; company growth not external market growth or independent market-share proof."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Titan - filing cover and press release","url":"https://www.titancompany.in/sites/default/files/2026-05/SEoutcomeBMQ4202526.pdf","note":"Dated reporting reference; obtain a legible filing and independent market denominators before plotting real portfolio positions."}],"exercise":"Private classroom portfolio memo, not a capital-allocation recommendation to Titan. Two tracks must remain separate: real-company evidence registry and a fully fictional numeric portfolio. Real registry: use Titan's Q4FY26 release dated May 8, 2026, not a claim about latest quarter. The company website gives conflicting jewellery figures: 18,195 crore in the headline and 8,195 in the body; the filing copy also has a damaged leading digit. Resolve the amount from a legible dated filing before using it. Record the website inconsistency, do not use 8,195 or silently repair it as a clean observation. The filing's press release is not a substitute for the full financial statements and notes. Reported India jewellery includes CaratLane; do not plot both as additive standalone revenue without an aligned carve-out. Domestic rounded parts 16,047+1,066=17,113 versus reported 17,114: flag 1 crore rounding difference, not a hidden new business. Jewellery excludes bullion/digital-gold; Q4 includes Damas consolidation, so geography/acquisition scope matters. Watch total-income growth 8% is not analog-watch growth 15%, and neither is industry growth. Fragrance is inside a combined emerging-business disclosure with bags and Indian dresswear, not an isolated measured segment. No external market-size/largest-rival denominator has been established here: actual Titan quadrant placements remain UNRESOLVED, not guessed from brand, company growth or profit.\nFictional same-year, same-geography, nonoverlapping markets, revenue in crore: A current own 30/prior own 25, largest rival 20, total market current 100/prior 90; B own 20/rival 40, market 200/prior 190; C own 15/rival 10, market 100/prior 100; D own 10/rival 20, market 60/prior 50. Revenue share for each divides by its own market, not portfolio total. Relative share = own share / largest rival's share = own revenue/rival revenue only because boundaries, period and revenue units match. For a leader, denominator is its largest OTHER rival, not itself. Predeclared classroom high-market-growth cutoff>=10%, high-relative-share>=1, including ties. A growth 11.111111%, relative 1.5 => star; B5.263158%,0.5 => low-growth/low-share pet; C0%,1.5 => cash cow; D20%,0.5 => question mark. A's own growth 20% is not its market growth 11.111111%. These thresholds are assumptions, not universal industry cutoffs. Largest-rival convention differs from BCG2014 historical study's third-ranked-company denominator and relative-growth convention; do not mix their classifications.\nBoundary check independent of Titan: fictional jewellery total 200 already includes subsidiary 40. Total plus subsidiary 240 double-counts 40; valid same-basis decomposition 160+40=200 requires consolidated inclusion and no other intercompany/definition differences. Real carve-outs require a reconciliation, not automatic subtraction of inconsistent standalone figures.\nPortfolio economics at one common date, lakh present values: a pet loses 2 on its own but enables incremental 6 in a star that would disappear without it. Keeping the pair has incremental 4 versus removing both effects; closing the pet alone improves standalone 2 but loses 6, portfolio change minus 4. If an available substitute costing 1 preserves all 6, closing plus substitute instead improves portfolio 1 versus current, conditional on transition/quality and no extra costs. Do not keep every pet forever or sell every pet on its label.\nOptional GE/McKinsey comparison uses a 3 x 3, not 2 x 2: explicitly subjective attractiveness scores growth 4/margin 2/risk 3 on 1-5 scale, weights 0.5/0.3/0.2 give 3.2. Define score direction (higher risk-resilience is better), cutoffs and sensitivity before assigning boxes; weighted judgments are not independently measured truth. A SWOT list can organize evidence but must lead to a diagnosis, a choice, accountable actions and disconfirming checks. Do not attribute an unsourced quotation to Rumelt or mechanically classify a real company's Ansoff move without defining product and customer-market baselines.\nDeliver a denominator/evidence ledger, fictional growth-share table, overlap reconciliation, externality/substitute bridge, and an uncertainty register for real Titan placements. Day 0/day 7 fictional review owners only. No actual investment, divestiture, offer, customer data, contact, disclosure or spend.<section aria-label=\"Native D125 practice\"><h3>Portfolio boundaries, overlap and substitute economics</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d125-portfolio-map.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable portfolio denominator review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d125-portfolio-map.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original written case remains intact.</p><p>Fictional A market 90 to 100 grows 11.111111%, while own revenue 25 to 30 grows 20%; largest OTHER rival 20 gives relative share 1.5. At predeclared high-growth cutoff 10% and share cutoff 1, including ties, A is star, B pet, C cash cow and D question mark. Missing B/C/D prior own revenues remain UNDEFINED, not invented. Largest-other-rival and absolute market-growth convention differs from BCG2014 study. Included subsidiary 40 inside total 200 is not additive: wrong 240, compatible residual 160 plus 40 reconciles 200. Pet loss 2 enables benefit 6: no-substitute closure changes portfolio by minus 4. Available full-retention substitute cost 1 changes it by plus 1; cost 2 ties, zero retention gives minus 5. Subjective GE score 3.2 is one axis only, not a complete nine-box placement.</p><p>All 43 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 24 changed sets plus restoration; 16 invalid cases/508 isolation checks; six broken references/six guarded HOLD checks. All 10 PDF pages inspected; actual learner/reviewer/transfer fields blank. Invalid unit holds its row, invalid cutoff holds classifications, unknown substitute availability holds decision result, not hypothetical economics. Fresh Titan Q4FY26 website still conflicts on jewellery headline/body; included CaratLane, Damas, geography, exclusions and watch metric scope remain explicit. Real Titan quadrants UNRESOLVED without legible dated filing and comparable external market denominators. Selected BCG/McKinsey/Titan written context checked, not full diagrams, interactive media or video, full statements or empirical study replication. Authored LibreOffice only, not Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner, account or legal clearance or overall 10/10. No real allocation, divestiture, offer, contact, disclosure or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Fictional A revenue 30, largest rival 20, same market 100. Relative share under largest-other-rival rule?","o":["30%","0.666667","1.5","3.0"],"a":2},{"q":"Fictional A market 90 to 100; own revenue 25 to 30. What is market growth, rounded to two decimal places?","o":["11.11%","20.00%","10.00%","25.00%"],"a":0},{"q":"Cutoffs high growth>=10%, high relative share>=1. A market growth 11.111111%/relative 1.5 belongs where?","o":["Cash cow under these stated conventions","Question mark under these stated conventions","Pet under these stated conventions","Star under these stated conventions"],"a":3},{"q":"Same cutoffs: B market 190 to 200, own 20/rival 40. Classification?","o":["High-growth/high-share star","Low-growth/low-share pet","Low-growth/high-share cash cow","High-growth/low-share question mark"],"a":1},{"q":"C market 100 to 100, own 15/rival 10. Classification?","o":["Low-growth/high-share cash cow","High-growth/high-share star","Low-growth/low-share pet","High-growth/low-share question mark"],"a":0},{"q":"D market 50 to 60, own 10/rival 20. Classification?","o":["Low-growth/high-share cash cow","High-growth/high-share star","High-growth/low-share question mark","Low-growth/low-share pet"],"a":2},{"q":"Fictional total 200 includes subsidiary 40. Adding both as standalone units gives what error?","o":["No amount double-counted; compatible split is 200+40","40 double-counted; compatible carve-out is 160+40","200 double-counted; compatible split is 0+40","40 omitted; compatible total is 280"],"a":1},{"q":"Pet standalone loss 2 enables star benefit 6, both present-value lakh. Close pet, no substitute: portfolio change?","o":["Plus 2 lakh","Plus 4 lakh","Minus 6 lakh","Minus 4 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Same pet; available substitute costs 1 and preserves all 6. Close+substitute versus current changes portfolio by?","o":["Minus 1 lakh, conditional on assumptions","Plus 5 lakh, conditional on assumptions","Plus 1 lakh, conditional on assumptions","Minus 4 lakh, conditional on assumptions"],"a":2},{"q":"Titan company growth known, but same-market industry growth/largest-rival share not verified. Correct quadrant result?","o":["High-growth star because own income rose","Unresolved until comparable external denominators are established","Cash cow because the brand is familiar","Pet because one small division loses money"],"a":1}]},"126":{"objectives":["Review the revised decision cases with 25 aligned questions: update a fresh pilot tree after a negative signal, price information before commitment, and carry forecast, base-rate, BATNA and portfolio caveats into the decision."],"videos":[],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Dartmouth - sample and perfect information","url":"https://cushman.host.dartmouth.edu/courses/engs41/Value-of-info.pdf","note":"EVSI minus costs governs purchase;0<=EVSI<=EVPI permits equality and zero."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Penn State - conditional probabilities","url":"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat509/lesson/17/17.3","note":"Use the denominator of all matching signals, not all failures or all prior cases. The lesson does not supply a venture-success base rate."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Harvard PON - BATNA discipline","url":"https://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/batna/negotiation-and-bargaining-with-your-batna-in-mind/","note":"Compare net alternatives, not an instruction or legal permission to share confidential information."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"BCG - dated matrix reassessment","url":"https://www.bcg.com/publications/2014/growth-share-matrix-bcg-classics-revisited","note":"Share/growth proxies need definitions and economic checks; source study denominator differs from this curriculum convention."}],"exercise":"Private fictional cumulative review, 25 scored questions, not a duplicate of Day 122's payoff inputs and not a new actual pilot. All fresh pilot amounts in crore at one common present valuation date. Success prior 40%. Scaling costs 80 now and later pays gross 300 if good, 20 if bad; stipulated payoffs already discounted to that date. Net scale payoffs+220/-60; stop 0. Risk-neutral expected monetary value, no financing constraints or omitted outcome-dependent costs. No-pilot EV 0.4*220+0.6*(-60)=52; threshold scale if p>3/14, indifferent at equality. Pilot acquisition cost 6 at same date, P(+|good)=0.9 and P(-|bad)=0.9, information only, available before irreversible scaling. No separate delay or effect on performance in this fresh case.\nConstruct joints positive-good 0.36/positive-bad 0.06/negative-good 0.04/negative-bad 0.54, summing 1. P(+)=0.42/P(-)=0.58. After negative posterior 0.04/0.58=2/29=6.896552%; scale EV 220*(2/29)-60*(27/29)=-1180/29=-40.689655, so stop. After positive posterior 6/7=85.714286%, scale EV 180, so scale. Gross policy EV 0.42*180+0.58*0=75.6. Gross EVSI 75.6-52=23.6, net pilot-policy EV 69.6, net improvement 17.6. Maximum total information acquisition cost 23.6 makes the pilot policy tie no-pilot 52, not a cost-free recommendation. Perfect information gross EV 0.4*220=88, EVPI 88-52=36, so EVSI 23.6<=EVPI 36. At the negative-result decision node, already-paid pilot 6 is sunk, not subtracted again from just scaling or used to force scale-up.\nSensitivity is not just a story: change both sensitivity and specificity to 0.6, keep prior/payoffs fixed. P(+)=0.48, posterior+=0.5; P(-)=0.52, posterior-=4/13=30.769231%; both exceed 3/14. Scale after either signal, gross policy EV stays 52, EVSI 0, net after acquisition 6 becomes 46. Informative signals can have zero decision value when no optimal action changes. If pilot itself changes quality, finances, timing or demand, this pure-information tree is no longer enough.\nCumulative challenge, each uses its OWN stated inputs rather than mixing currencies or cases: Day 120 conditional DiD/economic contribution, not automatic causal proof; Day 121 horizon-matched past-only forecast evaluation and stockout censoring; Day 122 information value net of costs before commitment; Day 123 reference-class odds, duplicate evidence and frozen proper-score convention; Day 124 corrected valuation versus comparable net BATNA and truthful authorized review; Day 125 market denominators, overlap and portfolio externalities. An actual wrong answer supports a targeted retry on that question's skill, not an invented question-level diagnosis from an aggregate day score. Pre-outcome forecasts and source versions remain frozen; do not edit old answers after resolution.\nIf a fictional co-investor affirms old claims while suppressing a verified confidential negative result, do not reflexively forward it to another fund. Preserve evidence, stop relying on known false assertions, identify entitled recipients and use authorized internal diligence/legal review; law/NDA/roles are not established by this generic source. Do not convert a favorable classroom EV into a real pilot, scale-up, purchase, investment, accusation, contact, confidential disclosure or spend. Deliver tree/joint table, negative-result memo, acquisition/signal-quality sensitivity, independent cumulative corrections and a FOR REVIEW ONLY action/evidence/permission checklist with fictional day 0/day 7 owners.<section aria-label=\"Native D126 practice\"><h3>Fresh pilot tree and response-based cumulative review</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d126-cumulative-pilot.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable cumulative pilot review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d126-cumulative-pilot.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before the reference; original 25-question review remains intact.</p><p>Fresh fictional prior good 40%, scale gross 300/20 less 80 gives net 220/-60, not net minus capex twice. Sensitivity and specificity 90% yield positive joints .36/.06 and negative .04/.54. Positive marginal .42, posterior 6/7 and scale EV 180; negative marginal .58, posterior 2/29 and scale EV minus 40.689655, so stop. Best no-pilot 52; gross policy 75.6; EVSI 23.6; cost 6 gives net policy 69.6 and improvement 17.6. Perfect-info gross 88, EVPI 36. Already-paid 6 belongs in both negative-node lifetime alternatives: scale minus 46.689655 versus stop minus 6, leaving forward difference minus 40.689655. At sensitivity and specificity 60%, both posteriors exceed threshold 3/14; both scale, EVSI 0, net 46. Informative does not guarantee decision value.</p><p>All 45 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 24 changed sets plus restoration; 16 invalid cases with 336 isolation checks; seven broken references with 118 checks. All 12 PDF pages inspected. Impossible signal has undefined posterior and unreached action, not zero success probability. Bad costs preserve valid gross arithmetic. Cumulative prompts keep earlier cases and units separate; actual question response is needed for targeted retry, not an aggregate score diagnosis. Actual learner, reviewer and delayed transfer fields blank. Selected Dartmouth and Penn State written text refreshed, earlier case source evidence reused within recorded limits. Not full diagrams, interactive media or video, Excel or Sheets parity, independent learner, account or legal clearance or overall 10/10. No actual pilot, investment, purchase, contact, confidential forwarding or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Fresh review pilot: scale pays gross 300/20 and costs 80. Prior good 40%. No-pilot scale EV?","o":["128 crore","52 crore","75.6 crore","46 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Sensitivity 0.9/specificity 0.9/prior 0.4. P(negative)?","o":["0.10","0.54","0.42","0.58"],"a":3},{"q":"Same tree. What is P(good | negative), rounded to two decimal places?","o":["6.90%","10.00%","40.00%","93.10%"],"a":0},{"q":"Net payoffs+220/-60, negative posterior 2/29. EV of scaling?","o":["Minus 46.689655 crore","Plus 40.689655 crore","Minus 40.689655 crore","Plus 52 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"After negative, stop 0 versus scale EV minus 40.689655. Decision under stated risk-neutral model?","o":["Scale to recover the already-paid pilot cost","Scale because gross success payout exceeds 80","Stop because success probability must equal zero","Stop; already-paid pilot cost does not force scaling"],"a":3},{"q":"Same tree. What is P(good | positive), rounded to two decimal places?","o":["90.00%","85.71%","40.00%","14.29%"],"a":1},{"q":"Optimal pilot policy scales after+, stops after-. Gross policy EV before pilot 6?","o":["75.6 crore","69.6 crore","23.6 crore","180 crore"],"a":0},{"q":"Gross with-signal EV 75.6, best no-signal EV 52. Gross EVSI?","o":["75.6 crore","17.6 crore","36 crore","23.6 crore"],"a":3},{"q":"Pilot cost 6, gross policy 75.6, no-pilot 52. Net improvement?","o":["69.6 crore","23.6 crore","17.6 crore","11.6 crore"],"a":2},{"q":"Same-date no delay. Highest pilot acquisition cost for weak preference versus no-pilot?","o":["23.6 crore, with indifference at that cost","75.6 crore, with indifference at that cost","17.6 crore, with indifference at that cost","36 crore, with indifference at that cost"],"a":0},{"q":"Perfect signal good 40%, success nets 220, bad stop 0. EVPI above no-pilot 52?","o":["88 crore","36 crore","23.6 crore","52 crore"],"a":1},{"q":"Net scale 220 p-60(1-p). Indifference probability?","o":["2/7=28.571429%","1/5=20%","3/14=21.428571%","4/5=80%"],"a":2},{"q":"Sensitivity/specificity both 0.6, same prior/payoffs. Both posteriors 0.5 and 4/13 exceed threshold. Gross EVSI?","o":["0; both signals still lead to scaling","6; because acquisition cost creates value","17.6; signal quality never changes value","36; every informative signal is perfect"],"a":0},{"q":"Same 0.6-quality pilot has zero EVSI and cost 6. Net strategy EV?","o":["52 crore, equal to no-pilot 52","58 crore, above no-pilot 52","46 crore, below no-pilot 52","69.6 crore, above no-pilot 52"],"a":2},{"q":"New evidence says the pilot itself improves launch quality. Which model response is sound?","o":["Keep all payoffs fixed and call improvement information value","Drop pilot cost so the action becomes attractive","Treat the negative signal as proof of failure","Rebuild outcome/payoff assumptions; pure-information tree no longer suffices"],"a":3},{"q":"Day 121 availability 90/sales 90 during a stockout. Demand for scoring forecast 110?","o":["Exactly 90; error actual-minus-forecast is minus 20","At least 90; exact forecast error is not identified","Exactly 110; forecast itself recovers lost sales","Exactly zero; the shelf later became empty"],"a":1},{"q":"Day 121 B RMSE 10/loss 20; C RMSE 5/loss 40, same cost objective. Which lower modeled inventory loss?","o":["C, because lower RMSE overrides loss definition","Both equal because both are forecasts","B, despite higher RMSE","Neither because RMSE is not defined for units"],"a":2},{"q":"Day 123 one audit report appears in two newsletters with no new information. Bayes update?","o":["Multiply twice because there are two publishers","Reset prior to 50% regardless of evidence","Use the larger headline as the denominator","Do not multiply the same likelihood ratio twice"],"a":3},{"q":"Day 123 binary Brier 0.265 versus two-coordinate 0.53 on same forecasts. Why different?","o":["The stated scaling convention doubles both-coordinate loss","The outcomes must have changed","The larger score proves a worse forecast model","The difference is a causal treatment effect"],"a":0},{"q":"Day 124 updated value 17.6/fees 0.4/BATNA 1.2. Price 16 gives what?","o":["Net 16, strictly better than BATNA","Net 1.2, equal BATNA, no strictly better deal","Net 1.6, strictly better than BATNA","Net 0.2, worse than BATNA"],"a":1},{"q":"Day 125 market growth is known, but largest-rival share is missing. Correct action?","o":["Use total company revenue as rival share","Treat brand familiarity as relative share above 1","Assume profit margin equals market share","Leave relative share/quadrant unresolved and seek comparable evidence"],"a":3},{"q":"Day 125 total 200 already includes subsidiary 40, compatible boundaries. Additive disjoint decomposition?","o":["160 remaining business plus 40 subsidiary","200 remaining business plus 40 subsidiary","240 remaining business plus 40 subsidiary","200 remaining business minus 40 subsidiary"],"a":0},{"q":"Day 120 historical pre-trend differences happen to match. Does that prove the untreated post-period counterfactual?","o":["Yes; one earlier matching change proves every later shock equal","Yes; arithmetic difference-in-differences guarantees causality","No; parallel-trends remains a substantive identifying assumption","No; therefore no conditional arithmetic may be computed"],"a":2},{"q":"An aggregate day score is 60%. Can it identify exactly which questions/skills were missed?","o":["Yes; every probability question must be wrong","No; inspect actual responses before targeted remediation","Yes; the lowest numbered six items were correct","No; therefore question-level review is never useful"],"a":1},{"q":"Confidential negative pilot contradicts an old claim being affirmed. Permitted next step in this fictional exercise?","o":["Preserve evidence and seek authorized diligence/legal review, no automatic broadcast","Forward every attachment to all rival funds","Repeat the old claim until the co-investor permits correction","Publish named customer data to create negotiation pressure"],"a":0}]},"127":{"objectives":["Test a precisely defined logistics resource against value, rarity, imitation/substitution and organization using comparative evidence; keep Delhivery management claims separate from independent advantage proof."],"videos":[{"id":"SD8XJw_qeNI","title":"VRIO Framework EXPLAINED — Business To You","by":"Business To You","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Ownership alone is not organization; VRIO judgments require comparative evidence, not guarantees."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"OpenStax - internal analysis and VRIO","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-management/pages/8-5-the-internal-environment","note":"Resources/capabilities and organization to capture value; Starbucks judgments are classroom examples, not verified current comparisons. Historical rideshare fares/autonomy status not adopted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Oregon State - VRIO comparative tests","url":"https://open.oregonstate.education/strategicmanagement2e/chapter/4-vrio-analysis/","note":"I includes economically viable substitutes and useful-life horizon; O requires capture, not ownership alone. Source 17-year patent statement and dated corporate examples not adopted as current facts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Delhivery - dated FY26 shareholder letter","url":"https://www.delhivery.com/uploads/2026/04/LetterToShareholders_Q4FY26.pdf","note":"May 16, 2026 company letter and FAQs. Separate management's forecasts and adjusted ROIC definitions from independent evidence of rarity, causality and durable advantage."}],"exercise":"Private resource-evidence memo, not an investment view or access to Delhivery's proprietary systems. Begin with a dated claim register from Delhivery's May 16,2026 shareholder letter for Q4FY26/FY26. Use the letter and its twelve FAQs as a dated management account, not a substitute for the full annual report. Management describes its integrated Express/PTL network, investment in automation/AI and proprietary shipment/telemetry data. These are company statements about capabilities and plans, not independent proof of resource rarity, non-substitutability, causal profit contribution or perpetual advantage. Current state beyond that report is not asserted. Management's reported Transport ROIC 16.0% uses adjusted EBIT 308/tangible allocable capital 1925 with exclusions including ROU assets/cash/intangibles; service EBITDA is before corporate overhead allocation while adjusted EBITDA allocates overheads. Do not equate 16% with return on all acquisition capital or compare unmatched rival definitions. Company projections of future margins/growth are not realized facts.\nDefine the resource at useful granularity: not 'many warehouses' or 'AI', but a specific integrated scheduling/data/operations capability with geographic/service scope and comparator set. V asks whether it improves economically relevant customer outcomes or net cost versus a credible alternative; R needs evidence of which rivals possess a comparable capability at the decision date, not that another company also owns trucks; I asks replication/substitution cost and time over the useful advantage horizon, not that duplication is literally impossible; O asks incentives, rights, access, process reliability, staff and customer execution that capture value, not legal ownership alone. Unknown evidence stays UNKNOWN, not automatic yes or no. A network's physical size does not prove parity or sustained advantage; messy-address data do not automatically establish path dependence, causal ambiguity, data-use rights or an algorithmic moat. Do not assert Ekart/XpressBees weakness or privileged access to rival systems without grounded evidence.\nFictional economics, not Delhivery metrics: 100,000 shipments in one month, an operationally usable routing tool reduces cost by 2 rupees per shipment with service quality unchanged. Gross avoided cost 2 lakh, incremental support 0.6 lakh and licence 0.4 lakh leave net 1 lakh. One-period investment variant pays 0.8 lakh now for the sole net 1 lakh cash saving at month-end; stipulated discount 10% for THIS one-month period, no terminal/residual value or other cash flows. NPV 1/1.10-0.8=6/55=0.109091 lakh. Positive NPV can support value relative to stated alternative, not prove rarity, isolate causality or authorize purchase. If all matched rivals can buy the same tool and organize it equally, the stipulated tool alone gives competitive parity, even with positive own NPV. If rival adoption takes 6 months while the resource's useful advantage lasts 18 months, six-month replication is not infinite protection or a guaranteed 18 month moat; check interim advantage and substitutes. No statement about real competitors' dates is implied.\nSeparate fictional one-month bundle test in lakh, all effects counted once: data/route-optimization/shift-incentive bundle yields gross 7, incremental operating 2 and coordination 1, net 4 versus its stated baseline. Removing an element may reduce benefits, but 'copying one is useless' requires a measured complementarity interaction, not an attractive activity map. Define the alternative, same shipment mix/volumes, customer concentration, costs and service failures; label estimates versus identified effects. A staff departure is one possible imitation/capture risk, not universally the fastest cause of decay, and no invitation to poach people or access confidential data. Historical causes, social complexity and causal ambiguity are hypotheses with evidence, not interchangeable labels or an excuse to stop learning.\nDeliver V/R/I/O evidence table with yes/no/unknown plus source/date/comparator/reversal tests; own NPV/net-saving bridge; activity complementarity hypothesis; authorized data-access and customer-quality constraints. Fictional analyst/operations/finance/legal day 0/day 7 review owners, no actual validation experiment, system access, staff contact, confidential data transfer, procurement, investment or spend.<section aria-label=\"Native D127 practice\"><h3>Routing economics and comparative VRIO evidence</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d127-vrio-evidence.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable VRIO evidence lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d127-vrio-evidence.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original quiz and exercise retained.</p><p>Fictional 100,000 shipments times INR 2 saves 2 lakh; incremental support 0.6 and licence 0.4 leave 1 lakh. Pay 0.8 now for ONLY 1 lakh one month later at stipulated 10% ONE-MONTH discount: PV 10/11 = 0.909091, NPV 6/55 = 0.109091 lakh. No perpetuity or annual-rate shortcut. Positive modeled NPV supports narrow value versus the stated alternative, not R/I/O. Matched rivals using identical tools equally and capturing benefits imply parity for the tool alone. Replication 6 months / useful life 18 months is a timing comparison, not a permanent moat or benefit stream. Separate bundle 7 - 2 - 1 = 4 lakh does not identify complementarity without component/no-bundle comparisons.</p><p>All 24 baseline formulas independently computed; 24 changed sets plus restoration; 16 invalid cases / 380 isolation checks; six broken references / six checks. All 11 PDF pages inspected;replication output wrapping fixed and rechecked. Actual V/R/I/O evidence statuses default UNKNOWN, separate from cash-flow NPV; ownership alone does not establish capture. Company-reported 16 May 2026 Transport ROIC 308/1925 = 16% excludes ROU, cash, intangibles/goodwill. Displayed 561 - 252 = 309, not 308; presentation rounding warning retained and unrounded bridge unresolved. Newer annual report selected performance text differs from earlier letter; definitions/restatements not reconciled,not merged. Same issuer documents are not independent moat proof. Actual learner/reviewer/transfer blank. Selected written text only, not full diagrams/video, Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal/company moat proof or overall10/10. No actual system access, staff contact, confidential data, procurement, investment or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Fictional 100,000 shipments save 2 rupees each; incremental support 0.6 lakh/licence 0.4 lakh. Net monthly saving?","o":["2 lakh","0.4 lakh","3 lakh","1 lakh"],"a":3},{"q":"Pay 0.8 lakh now, receive only 1 lakh one month later; stipulated one-month discount 10%. NPV?","o":["0.2 lakh","0.109091 lakh","0.3 lakh","0.909091 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"Positive modeled NPV for a tool proves which narrow result?","o":["Rarity against every current rival","Inimitability throughout all future markets","Value versus stated cash-flow alternative, not all VRIO tests","Organization-wide capture without execution evidence"],"a":2},{"q":"All matched rivals buy/use identical routing tool equally. The stipulated tool alone yields?","o":["Competitive parity despite positive own NPV","Sustained advantage because software is intangible","Temporary advantage because the brand is large","Competitive disadvantage solely because rivals adopt"],"a":0},{"q":"Rival replication takes 6 months, useful advantage horizon 18 months. Supported statement?","o":["The capability is impossible to imitate for 18 months","Imitation may erode advantage after 6 months; test substitutes and remaining economics","The ratio 3 proves permanent competitive advantage","All net economic benefits necessarily disappear today"],"a":1},{"q":"Firm legally owns valuable/rare/hard-to-copy data but lacks access rights for intended use, staff process and incentives. O test?","o":["Passed automatically because ownership is legal","Irrelevant once a resource is rare","Failed only if a rival has more revenue","Not demonstrated; ownership alone does not establish capture"],"a":3},{"q":"Delhivery says its integrated network/data create a moat. Evidence status of rarity from this letter alone?","o":["Management claim; matched competitor evidence still needed","Independent proof no rival has a substitute","Proof every algorithm was caused by historical ambiguity","Proof nationwide network ownership is automatically rare"],"a":0},{"q":"No reliable matched-rival resource data is available in the dossier. Mark R as?","o":["Yes, because the firm is large","No, because every firm has employees","Unknown, with specific evidence needed","Yes, because collection took many years"],"a":2},{"q":"Fictional bundle gross 7/operating 2/coordination 1 lakh, same one-month basis. Net benefit?","o":["7 lakh","4 lakh","5 lakh","6 lakh"],"a":1},{"q":"Company Transport ROIC excludes ROU/cash/goodwill and uses adjusted EBIT. May it establish return on all acquisition capital?","o":["Yes; every measure called ROIC has identical capital scope","Yes; highROIC independently proves data inimitability","No; therefore no company-reported metric may be discussed","No; denominator/adjustments differ and need reconciliation"],"a":3}]},"128":{"objectives":["Use a strategy canvas and ERRC to form a testable buyer-utility/price/cost proposal; check noncustomer demand and cannibalization without inventing historical IndiGo or stockbroker conversion proof."],"videos":[{"id":"sYdaa02CS5E","title":"The Explainer: Blue Ocean Strategy — Harvard Business Review","by":"Harvard Business Review","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Historical Cirque comparisons and 10-15 year protection are not verified universal results."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Blue Ocean Strategy - originator overview","url":"https://www.blueoceanstrategy.com/what-is-blue-ocean-strategy/","note":"Framework definition, not universal profit or permanent competition-free guarantee."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Blue Ocean Strategy - strategy canvas","url":"https://www.blueoceanstrategy.com/tools/strategy-canvas/","note":"Buyer factors/relative offering levels; targets need units/anchors and empirical validation."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Blue Ocean Strategy - ERRC","url":"https://www.blueoceanstrategy.com/blog/errc-grid-template-examples/","note":"Flattened example grids lose category labels; historical CitizenM/other-case statistics not independently verified/current or adopted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Blue Ocean Strategy - utility, price and cost","url":"https://www.blueoceanstrategy.com/tools/value-innovation/","note":"Alignment is a proposal criterion, not causal noncustomer conversion or NPV guarantee."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Blue Ocean Strategy - three tiers","url":"https://www.blueoceanstrategy.com/tools/three-tiers-of-noncustomers/","note":"Tiers are hypotheses about defined-category alternatives, not automatic labels for real Indian cohorts."}],"exercise":"Private fictional proposal for a monthly job-skills learning service, no actual users/payments/data. A blue-ocean claim requires a tested path to buyer value, sustainable provider economics and genuinely new demand, not a different-looking curve, low price, press coverage or no visible rivals. Originators describe differentiation and low cost together, but the framework does not guarantee permanent uncontested markets or automatic win-win outcomes. Historical claims that IndiGo converted train/bus travelers or that early stockbrokers won FD savers are not established by these generic sources. Do not score those stories as verified facts. A real 2006/2024 air-travel canvas would require dated comparable fares, schedules/service, scope and revealed noncustomer conversion evidence; leave unverified values blank rather than reconstructing from reputation.\nFictional current industry: passive long video libraries and learners who instead use free tutorials or peer help. Define buyer-facing factors with explicit units before charting: monthly total price (rupees, lower is cheaper), scheduled feedback turnaround (hours, lower is faster), weekly practical tasks reviewed (count, higher is more), scheduling choice (documented windows, not arbitrary prestige). Separate observed as-is evidence from proposed target levels. Never average hours, rupees and counts into an unlabelled offering score; if an illustrative 1-5 index is used, declare orientation/anchors/assumptions and distinguish target from customer-perceived measurement. A curve is a diagnostic proposal, not proof of causal demand or a measured utility index. Noncustomer interviews and actual willingness-to-pay/retention data would be future authorized research, not evidence already collected.\nERRC proposal, all effects stipulated and none real: eliminate expensive studio production for nonessential intro videos; reduce paid acquisition dependence; raise timely practical feedback; create flexible task clinics. Do not eliminate accessibility, privacy, learner safety, required teaching support or legal obligations to make costs look good. Net unit-cost bridge in rupees per paid learner-month: old variable 400 minus studio 120 minus acquisition 50 plus feedback 40 plus clinic 30 = new variable 300. Stipulated total price 500 each month, no discounts/taxes/payment fees/refunds beyond those already assumed in variable cost; must revisit these in a real case. New contribution 200 versus old 100. Bridge is incremental cost at constant service assumptions, not fixed-cost absorption passed off as cash saving.\nMonthly economics: existing 2000 paying learners remain baseline; proposed service attracts 500 paid learners, of whom 100 are diverted from the old service, NOT500 plus 100. Additional fixed cash cost 60,000 per month, no other setup or terminal cash flows. New-service contribution 500*200=100,000; lost old contribution 100*100=10,000; fixed 60,000 => incremental monthly contribution 30,000 before any omitted costs. Baseline old contribution 200,000; post total 1900*100+500*200-60,000=230,000, gain 30,000. Break-even proposed paid learners given fixed 100 diversions is(60,000+10,000)/200=350; equality is zero incremental gain, not profit above baseline. If diversion scales with enrollment, recompute using a diversion rate instead of freezing 100. Countercase: if variable cost 330, same 500/100/fixed inputs yield 15,000, not 30,000. If monthly fixed rises above 90,000 with other inputs fixed, incremental result becomes negative.\nFictional classification exercise only: of the 500 proposed paid learners, 100 are current own-service switchers, 220 previously bought rival paid courses, 180 previously used only free tutorials/peer help. Sum 500. Only 180/500=36% are described as new to this DEFINED paid-course category; rival switching is not new-category demand, and own diversion is cannibalization. Self-reported alternatives do not prove the proposal CAUSED incremental adoption: need valid comparison/assignment/measurement, retention, outcomes, refund/complaint and accessibility checks. Originators' tiers: edge/minimal buyers considering exit, deliberate refusers, and distant people who never considered the category. Do not automatically assign every low-income person, FD saver or bus traveler to one tier without evidence.\nDeliver an as-is/target factor table with units and source status, an ERRC grid with dependency constraints, unit-cost and baseline-versus-proposal bridges, conditional break-even/sensitivity, and a noncustomer/research registry. Fictional day 0/day 7 buyer-outcome/finance/quality/permission owners. No actual experiment, learner contact, enrollment offer, price change, data collection, platform change, subsidy, purchase or spend.<section aria-label=\"Native D128 practice\"><h3>Cost bridge, cannibalization and new-category demand</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d128-new-demand.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable new-demand proposal lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d128-new-demand.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original quiz and exercise retained.</p><p>Fictional learner-month cost bridge: 400 - 120 studio - 50 acquisition + 40 feedback + 30 clinics = 300 rupees. Price 500 gives new contribution 200 versus old 100. Existing 2000 learners, proposed 500 INCLUDING 100 own diversions: incremental 500*200 - 100*100 - 60000 = 30000 rupees monthly. Baseline 200000, post 1900*100 + 500*200 - 60000 = 230000. Fixed 100 diversions gives continuous break-even 350; alternative fixed 20% diversion rate gives marginal 180, continuous 333.333333, whole 334 and expected gain 120. These are different assumptions, with capacity limits and no enrollment authority. Nonpositive slopes remain undefined, not negative learner targets.</p><p>Of the stipulated 500, 100 own switchers, 220 rival switchers and 180 formerly free-only imply 180/500 = 36% new to the DEFINED paid-course category. Descriptive classification is not causal conversion, retention or learning proof. Canvas keeps rupees, hours, task counts and documented windows separate; actual as-is and target values blank, no invented utility index. ERRC dependencies preserve teaching, accessibility, privacy and safety; reduced acquisition may reduce demand. All 30 baseline formulas independently computed; 24 changed sets plus restoration, 16 invalid cases / 460 isolation checks, six broken references / six checks. All 12 PDF pages inspected. Actual learner, reviewer and delayed transfer blank. Five originator written sources refreshed, not independent efficacy, historical IndiGo/train-bus or FD-saver conversion proof. Not full diagrams, interactive media/video, Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal clearance or overall 10/10. No actual learner experiment, contact, enrollment, price change, data collection, subsidy, purchase or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Fictional learner-month variable 400 minus 120 minus 50 plus 40 plus 30. New variable cost?","o":["300 rupees","230 rupees","330 rupees","400 rupees"],"a":0},{"q":"New price 500/variable 300, old price 500/variable 400. New versus old unit contribution?","o":["300 versus 400 rupees","500 versus 500 rupees","200 versus 100 rupees","100 versus 200 rupees"],"a":2},{"q":"500 new-service paid learners, 100 old-service diversions, new contribution 200/old 100/fixed 60,000. Incremental month?","o":["40,000 rupees","30,000 rupees","100,000 rupees","Minus 10,000 rupees"],"a":1},{"q":"Same economics, hold old-service diversions 100 fixed. Break-even new-service paid count?","o":["300; exactly zero incremental gain at that count","500; exactly zero incremental gain at that count","700; exactly zero incremental gain at that count","350; exactly zero incremental gain at that count"],"a":3},{"q":"Variable becomes 330, price 500/count 500/diversion 100*old 100/fixed 60,000 unchanged. Incremental result?","o":["30,000 rupees","25,000 rupees","15,000 rupees","85,000 rupees"],"a":2},{"q":"Of 500 paid learners, 100 own switchers/220 rival switchers/180 formerly free-only. Share new to defined paid category?","o":["36%, descriptive classification not causal conversion proof","80%, descriptive classification not causal conversion proof","44%, descriptive classification not causal conversion proof","100%, descriptive classification not causal conversion proof"],"a":0},{"q":"Proposal has lower price and a differently shaped value curve. Does that establish a blue ocean?","o":["Yes; any cheaper product creates new demand by definition","Yes; different curves guarantee at least 15 years protection","No; the framework requires every old feature to increase","No; utility, economic viability and incremental demand still need evidence"],"a":3},{"q":"Canvas mixes price rupees, turnaround hours and tasks count. Sound presentation?","o":["Add raw values because all high numbers mean better utility","Keep units/direction or declare indexed anchors; label targets versus observations","Convert every factor to revenue without assumptions","Label proposed targets as measured customer perceptions"],"a":1},{"q":"Learner deliberately rejects paid courses after considering them. Originator noncustomer tier?","o":["Refusing tier, within the defined category and evidence","Soon-to-be tier solely because income is unknown","Unexplored tier because the learner assessed the category","Existing loyal buyer tier because tutorials are available"],"a":0},{"q":"What is a responsible ERRC cost reduction here?","o":["Remove all learner support regardless of harm to learning","Count fixed-cost absorption as certain cash savings","Remove nonessential studio expense while preserving teaching/accessibility and required protections","Treat privacy and consent as optional if margins improve"],"a":2}]},"129":{"objectives":["Distinguish repeated-game models from real rivalry evidence; calculate incentives and test monitoring, credibility and alternative explanations."],"videos":[{"id":"UkXI-zPcDIM","title":"Prisoners' Dilemma and Nash Equilibrium — Khan Academy","by":"Khan Academy","note":"Use the fictional payoff table below. A prisoner's-dilemma example does not establish real firms' payoffs or the equilibrium of a repeated market."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Repeated Games I - Jonathan Levin, Stanford","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/~jdlevin/Econ%20286/Repeated%20Games%20I.pdf","note":"Compare the model's monitoring and continuation assumptions before using a grim-trigger argument. Its payoff example differs from the fictional case."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Evolution of Trust - optional interactive","url":"https://ncase.me/trust/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Optional simulation; use the written payoff table and do not infer tournament results from the resource landing page."}],"exercise":"Private fictional model, not competitor coordination advice. Two players choose C or D each period; own payoff: mutual C=3, unilateral D=5, unilateral C=0, mutual D=1. No actual firm payoffs or intent supplied. (a) Identify stage best responses, Nash and Pareto comparison. Infinite play, common discount delta, perfect public observation, grim punishment at mutual D: derive 3/(1-delta)>=5+delta/(1-delta); test delta=.4/.6 and the weak boundary. Contrast a known finite horizon with no terminal rewards. (b) Independent 2% false alarms each cooperative period: calculate at least one in 10 periods. Explain why forgiveness can reduce punishment errors without being universally optimal. (c) Separate signal toy: acceptance benefit 10, cost 4 strong type/14 weak, outside 0; acceptance follows signal. Compare sending incentives, not a complete equilibrium. (d) Optional Ola/Uber memo: leave payoff entries, common-owner causal effect and exit intent unknown pending dated primary evidence; test financing, demand and regulation alternatives. Trust is optional exploration, not verified tournament evidence. No competitor contact, pricing/subsidy coordination or real signal authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D129 practice\"><h3>Repeated-game incentives, horizons and false alarms</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d129-repeated-incentives.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable repeated-incentive lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d129-repeated-incentives.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original quiz and exercise retained.</p><p>Fictional symmetric stage own payoffs T = 5, R = 3, P = 1, S = 0. D gains 2 against C and 1 against D, so mutual D is unique stage Nash while mutual C gives each 2 more. Infinite perfect observation, common delta .6 and grim punishment yield cooperation 7.5 versus one deviation 6.5, margin 1 and weak threshold .5. At delta .4 IC fails; .5 equality is weak, not strict or unique predicted play. Normalized 3 versus 2.6 differs from unnormalized sums. Known 10 periods, complete information and no terminal reward imply all D by backward induction; finite value 2.484883456, cooperative comparator 7.454650368 is not equilibrium.</p><p>Independent 2% single PUBLIC false alarm each cooperative period: 1 - .98^10 = 18.292719311% at least one. Separate policy model pays R now and switches to perpetual P NEXT period on alarm: V = (R + delta*p*P/(1-delta))/(1 - delta*(1-p)) = 7.354369, not a noisy equilibrium test or optimal forgiveness claim. Deviation signal law missing. Signal benefit 10, strong cost 4, weak 14, outside 0 yield 6 and -4 conditional sending incentives, not complete equilibrium without receiver payoffs, beliefs and prior. All 33 baseline formulas independently computed; 24 changed sets plus restoration, 16 invalid cases / 498 isolation checks, six broken references / six checks. All 10 PDF pages inspected; caches clean, no externals/macros. Actual work blank. Selected Stanford/MIT written text refreshed; Stanford payoff matrix differs. Optional Trust landing/repo not executed tournament. Actual Ola/Uber payoffs, investor causal effects and exit motives unknown. No competitor contact, price/subsidy coordination, real signal or spend. Not full diagrams, interactive media/video, Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal or overall 10/10.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"In this one-period game, what is a best response against either opponent action?","o":["C:3 rather than 1 when the opponent also chooses C.","D:5 rather than 3 against C, and 1 rather than 0 against D.","C against D, to raise own payoff from 0 to 1.","D against C but C against D, to match the opponent."],"a":1},{"q":"What does mutual D establish here?","o":["Pareto efficiency, because neither can gain by changing alone.","Not Nash, because moving jointly to C raises both payoffs.","The only feasible outcome, because D is dominant.","Nash equilibrium, although mutual C gives both higher payoffs."],"a":3},{"q":"Minimum delta for C to be weakly incentive-compatible under the stated infinite grim model?","o":["1/2, from 3 >= 5(1-delta)+delta.","1/3, from 3 >= 4(1-delta)+delta.","2/5, from 3 >= 5(1-delta)+0delta.","3/5, from 2 >= 5(1-delta)+0delta."],"a":0},{"q":"At delta=.6, compare unnormalized cooperation and one-deviation values.","o":["Cooperation 5; deviation 5.67, so deviation is preferred.","Cooperation 3; deviation 5, so repetition changes nothing.","Cooperation 7.5; deviation 6.5, so the constraint holds.","Cooperation 7.5; deviation 8.5, so deviation is preferred."],"a":2},{"q":"Known final period, no terminal reward, same unique stage Nash: backward induction predicts?","o":["Mutual C until final-period mutual D.","Mutual D in every period under these assumptions.","Mutual C whenever more than two periods remain.","Alternating C and D because the horizon is known."],"a":1},{"q":"Independent 2% false alarms: probability of at least one in ten cooperative periods?","o":["1-.98^10, approximately 18.29%.",".02^10, approximately zero percent.","10*.02/(1-.02), approximately 20.41%.",".98^10, approximately 81.71%."],"a":0},{"q":"Why is mutual-D punishment credible in this specific game?","o":["Punishment lowers both below cooperative payoffs.","Punishment lasts forever and is publicly visible.","The initial promise cannot be changed after deviation.","D remains a best response when the other chooses D."],"a":3},{"q":"Signal benefit 10, costs 4 strong/14 weak, outside 0: if sending induces acceptance, what follows?","o":["Both send because benefit 10 is positive for either type.","Neither sends because costs precede acceptance.","Strong nets 6; weak nets-4, giving different sending incentives.","Weak sends and strong abstains to demonstrate strength."],"a":2},{"q":"Common investor stakes in two rivals: what is needed to claim this ended a subsidy war?","o":["Changed firm decisions and a causal design addressing other changes.","Stake percentages alone, translating portfolio motives to firms.","A later price increase alone, identifying the investor mechanism.","A joint-profit model alone, establishing firms maximize portfolio value."],"a":0},{"q":"Exit transaction observed, motives/counterfactual undocumented: what may the memo conclude?","o":["Highest deal price proves commitment to narrower business scope.","Record the exit and test motive hypotheses without keying one as fact.","Retained equity proves rivalry stopped after the transaction.","Costly exit proves weak firms could not send the same signal."],"a":1}]},"130":{"objectives":["Separate payment-network roles, app differentiation and causal effects; test economics without assuming free multihoming or verified current fees."],"videos":[{"id":"pipf0O0IytA","title":"Marshall Van Alstyne — Leveraging a Platform Business Model to Compete in the New Economy","by":"Stern Strategy Group: Speakers & Advisors","note":"Use the written platform-economics case. Do not assume universal platform dominance, zero marginal cost or that historical market shares remain current."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"UPI roles and responsibilities - NPCI","url":"https://www.npci.org.in/product/upi/roles-responsibilities","note":"Official roles, not a substitute for dated fee/cap instruments."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Dynamics of Network Effects - a 16 z","url":"https://a16z.com/the-dynamics-of-network-effects/","note":"Mechanisms, saturation and multihoming caveats; historical cutoffs are not universal laws."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"UPI circulars and notifications - NPCI","url":"https://www.npci.org.in/circulars/upi","note":"Starting point for dated policy research. OC 237 listing directs readers to their bank; its listing alone does not establish operative pricing terms."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Network Effects Manual - NFX","url":"https://www.nfx.com/post/network-effects-manual","note":"Selected opening/taxonomy reading; labels alone do not prove benefit, defensibility or ownership."}],"exercise":"Private fictional analysis, not UPI fees or data-use permission. (a) Use NPCI roles to distinguish NPCI, PSP bank, TPAP, remitter/beneficiary banks, payer/payee. Separate interoperability from app service/distribution. For each same/cross-side effect identify beneficiary, mechanism, congestion/fraud possibility and metric. Volume alone is not causation. (b) Toy randomized merchant-availability test: comparable payer cohorts, treatment completion 60% to 66%, control 61% to 63%, same window. Compute difference-in-differences; test spillovers, mix and precision before generalizing. (c) Unrelated fictional monthly economics:10,000 transactions, revenueRs. 0.30/costRs. 0.50 each;100 subscribers, revenueRs. 30/costRs. 5 each;fixedRs. 1,000. Compute contributions and net. No data monetization entitlement assumed. Second app costs 30 minutes/month atRs. 200/hour plusRs. 20 administration: compute multihoming cost, distinct from switching. (d) Debate a hypothetical concentration cap, not operative law. For current UPI pricing/caps, reconcile dated official summaries with operative instruments, products, customer/merchant scope, exceptions and dates. Neither blanket zero-MDR nor reported selective rates is established as a current-law answer in this exercise. No account change, financial product, data use or disclosure authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D130 practice\"><h3>Interoperability, measured completion and unit economics</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d130-platform-mechanisms.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable platform-mechanism lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d130-platform-mechanisms.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original quiz and exercise retained.</p><p>Comparable fictional payer cohorts, treatment 60% to 66% and control 61% to 63%, yield DiD 6 - 2 = 4 percentage points. This is not the 3-point final gap. The control-trend counterfactual is 62%; relative-to-treatment-baseline lift is 6.6667%, not 4%. Random assignment is stipulated, not observed. Sample sizes, clustered inference, spillovers, mix and compliance are not supplied. Algebra alone proves neither a causal network effect nor statistical significance.</p><p>Unrelated monthly toy: 10,000 transactions times (0.30 - 0.50) = -2,000 INR contribution; 100 subscribers times (30 - 5) = 2,500; fixed 1,000 gives net -500. With subscriber contribution fixed, break-even transaction revenue is 0.35. With transaction volume/margin fixed, positive 25 subscription margin needs 120 subscribers. Nonpositive margin has no positive-margin threshold in this model. Second app: 30 minutes at 200/hour plus 20 administration and zero provider fee costs 120/month. That is multihoming, not switching or zero cost.</p><p>NPCI selected consolidated scope/roles dated 18 September 2026 distinguishes shared rails, PSP/app/bank responsibilities and retains pricing circulars, with consolidation cutoff 31 July 2026. A 15 September PIB summary states a scoped MDR framework; it is not verified operative pricing here. OC 237/238 directory entries refer readers to their bank, not rate terms. Selected clause 7.5 convenience disclosure versus PIB app-fee prohibition needs entity/product/fee scope; we do not merge blanket advice. Current pricing/cap applicability remains open. No fee calculator or UPI rate is used in this toy.</p><p>All 21 baseline formulas independently computed; 24 changed sets plus restoration, 16 invalid cases / 336 isolation checks, six broken references / six checks. All 11 PDF pages inspected; clean caches, no external links/macros, actual work blank. a16z written article / NFX opening selected concepts are not empirical efficacy or current company-share evidence. No payment-data use, data monetization entitlement, real experiment, contact, account change, financial product or spend. Not full source/diagrams/AV, Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal or overall 10/10.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Why is higher payment volume alone insufficient network-effect evidence?","o":["Network effects require volume to fall as user value rises.","Network effects require owning payer and merchant businesses.","Marketing or demand can raise volume without participation improving others' value.","Volume is a network effect only when app market share is unchanged."],"a":2},{"q":"More accepting merchants improve payer usability. Which direction is this?","o":["Cross-side: merchants benefit payers, subject to actual usability.","Same-side: merchants benefit other merchants with payer numbers fixed.","Cross-side: more payers directly increase the value to other payers.","Same-side: processing cost falls as transaction volume increases."],"a":0},{"q":"Treatment 60% to 66%, control 61% to 63%: difference-in-differences?","o":["5 percentage points: final 66% minus starting control 61%.","4 percentage points:6-point change minus 2-point change.","6 percentage points: treatment change without control adjustment.","10 percentage points: treatment change relative to baseline 60%."],"a":1},{"q":"What most directly weakens that merchant test's causal interpretation?","o":["It measures completion rather than total annual payment value.","Treatment starts at 60% while control starts at 61%.","It reports percentage-point changes rather than relative changes.","Control payers also use treatment merchants, contaminating comparison."],"a":3},{"q":"Fictional 10,000 transactions, revenue.30/cost.50 each: monthly transaction contribution?","o":["MinusRs. 2,000 before subscriptions and fixed costs.","PlusRs. 3,000 before subscriptions and fixed costs.","MinusRs. 5,000 before subscriptions and fixed costs.","PlusRs. 2,000 before subscriptions and fixed costs."],"a":0},{"q":"Add 100 subscribers with 30 revenue/5 cost each and fixed 1,000. Monthly net result?","o":["PlusRs. 500 afterRs. 2,500 subscription contribution.","PlusRs. 1,000 afterRs. 3,000 subscription revenue.","MinusRs. 500 afterRs. 2,500 subscription contribution.","MinusRs. 3,000 after subtracting subscription contribution."],"a":2},{"q":"Second app:30 minutes/month at 200/hour plus 20 administration. Multihoming cost?","o":["Rs. 20 monthly because time is not a provider charge.","Rs. 220 monthly by charging the full hourly rate.","Rs. 100 monthly because administration is in time cost.","Rs. 120 monthly even with no provider app fee."],"a":3},{"q":"What can interoperability imply about app defensibility?","o":["Shared reach makes all apps equally defensible through the same merchants.","Shared reach reduces exclusivity; service, trust or distribution may still differentiate.","Shared reach transfers all customer relationships to the network operator.","Shared reach prevents differentiation in reliability and customer support."],"a":1},{"q":"Dated policy summaries conflict and the operative instrument is unavailable. What is sound analysis?","o":["Prefer the older summary because a familiar framework is more reliable.","Prefer the newer summary without checking operative scope and exceptions.","Leave applicability open and identify missing instruments, dates and exceptions.","Choose the simplest fee rule to keep the subsidy explanation consistent."],"a":2},{"q":"Payment activity creates an adjacent data-service opportunity. What does that establish about permission?","o":["Nothing by itself: purpose, consent, law and agreements still need checking.","Permission follows from app operation if the new service has positive margin.","Permission follows from interoperability if payment processing is free.","Permission follows from participation if users can switch payment apps."],"a":0}]},"131":{"objectives":["Use foothold, trajectory and resource-allocation tests without treating low price, digital delivery or success alone as disruption."],"videos":[{"id":"qDrMAzCHFUU","title":"Disruptive Innovation Explained — Harvard Business Review","by":"Harvard Business Review","note":"Treat disruption theory as a conditional explanation, not a way to predict the future with certainty. Test the written case's mechanism and alternatives."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Disruptive Innovation Theory - Christensen Institute","url":"https://www.christenseninstitute.org/theory/disruptive-innovation/","note":"Theory page: process and boundary conditions, not a synonym for cheap/successful."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Assessing Your Organization's Innovation Capabilities - Christensen","url":"https://personal.utdallas.edu/~chasteen/Christensen%20-%202nd%20article.htm","note":"Focus on the resources, processes and values framework and the conditions for separating a new venture in this 2001 article."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"What Is Disruptive Innovation? - HBR","url":"https://hbr.org/2015/12/what-is-disruptive-innovation","note":"Summary/landing only; accessible Institute theory is the core reading."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Uber, Disruptive Innovation, and Regulated Markets - Horn","url":"https://www.christenseninstitute.org/blog/uber-disruptive-innovation-and-regulated-markets/","note":"2016 interpretive disagreement with uncertainties, not a fixed Uber key or current financial evidence."}],"exercise":"Private fictional cases. A: mainstream needs performance 70, incumbent supplies 80; entrant begins 40 for low-end buyers needing 30, operates profitably at 20% gross margin where incumbent approval requires 35%; improves 40->65->75. This suggests a candidate low-end trajectory, not certain displacement. B:80 of 100 first customers previously could not access comparable service; investigate access mechanism, viable economics, incumbent response and later mainstream improvement before declaring new-market disruption. C: cheaper digital premium product serves incumbents' demanding best customers with better performance; distinguish sustaining competition from a foothold. (a) Evidence table: known, unknown, disconfirming facts. (b) Apply resources-processes-values (RPV); values are priority criteria, not just ethics. Choose structure from actual process/priority mismatch, not a separate unit for every innovation. (c) Optional UPI/card, Jio/telco or PhysicsWallah/coaching memo: define incumbent, geography, entry date, cohort and metric; seek dated primary customer/model evidence. Unsupported prices, classifications and intent remain unknown, not fixed keys. Expert interpretations can differ. (d) Five sentences: claim, foothold evidence, trajectory, alternative explanation, refuting metric. No real investment, restructuring or market action authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D131 practice\"><h3>Foothold evidence, thresholds and organizational fit</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d131-disruption-evidence.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable disruption-evidence lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d131-disruption-evidence.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Case A index: mainstream need 70, low-end 30, incumbent 80; entrant 40/65/75 first meets fixed mainstream need at observation 3. Ordered observations are not equal years or predicted displacement. Baseline gaps are 10 above low-end at entry and -30/-5/+5 versus mainstream. Equality meets the stipulated threshold; zero crossing flag means none of these observations, not never.</p><p>Case B: 80 of 100 first customers previously lacked comparable access, share 80% of that cohort only. Not market share, retention, incremental demand or disruption probability. Added monthly toy revenue 1000, COGS 800 and operating fixed 300 gives gross profit 200, gross margin 20%, operating result -100 and a 15 percentage-point shortfall versus incumbent hurdle 35%. At fixed 20% gross margin, break-even revenue 1500; at fixed COGS 800 instead it would be 1100. Nonpositive margin has no positive-margin threshold here. Priority failure differs from profit and disruption classification.</p><p>RPV: resources are assets, processes are ways of working, values are priority criteria. Process mismatch with priority fit suggests a heavyweight team within core; priority mismatch raises allocation autonomy, not simply a separate office. Both fit may use existing/lightweight structure. Binary teaching codes are not company recommendations, a disruption score, or proof resources exist. Case C initially superior for demanding incumbent customers is sustaining competition under the stated foothold account.</p><p>23 baseline formulas independently computed; 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid controls/368 isolation checks, six broken references/six checks. All 10 PDF pages inspected; clean saved caches, no external links/macros, actual work blank. Refreshed Institute written theory, full returned archived 2001 RPV, selected HBS-hosted 2015 article text (HBR page summary only), full returned Horn 2016 interpretation, Tuck critique report and selected Sloan introduction/method footnote. Historical taxi versus black-car arguments and financial uncertainty are not current Uber facts, inevitable predictions or fixed company keys. Not full diagrams/AV, native cross-viewer, independent learner/account/legal or overall 10/10. No real investment, restructuring, acquisition, hiring, customer contact or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Which observation gives the weakest evidence of a strict low-end/new-market foothold?","o":["Low price and fast revenue growth among existing customers.","Viable low-end model serving buyers overserved on the old metric.","New access enabling people who previously could not consume.","Entry cohort needing less performance than mainstream customers."],"a":0},{"q":"A performs 40; mainstream needs 70 and low-end needs 30. What follows?","o":["It cannot serve anyone until performance exceeds incumbent 80.","It meets mainstream need because 40 exceeds low-end 30.","It meets the stated low-end need but initially falls short of mainstream need.","It must be new-market disruption because lower performance creates users."],"a":2},{"q":"A improves 40->65->75; mainstream need stays 70. When first clear it?","o":["First observation 40, because lower price closes any performance gap.","Second observation 65, because its improvement rate closes the gap.","Never, because entrants must exceed incumbent 80 rather than requirement 70.","Third observation 75, without proving customer displacement."],"a":3},{"q":"Entrant works at 20% margin; incumbent approval requires 35%. Which RPV element most directly blocks priority?","o":["Resources: its engineers necessarily lack the required skills.","Values: the incumbent's margin-based investment criterion.","Processes: it necessarily has no product-development routine.","Values: its staff necessarily hold unethical personal beliefs."],"a":1},{"q":"B has 80 prior nonconsumers among 100 first customers. What is supported?","o":["80% market share, establishing eventual mainstream displacement.","80% retention, establishing profitable recurring customer demand.","80% prior-nonconsumer share, relevant to a possible new-market foothold.","80% disruption probability, establishing likely market success."],"a":2},{"q":"C targets demanding incumbent buyers with superior performance. Best starting description?","o":["Sustaining competition, rather than the specified foothold pattern.","Low-end disruption established by its lower price.","New-market disruption established by digital delivery.","Disruption established by incumbent customers switching."],"a":0},{"q":"When is a separate unit most defensible under RPV diagnosis?","o":["Any new technology is used even when core processes and priorities fit.","Needed processes/priorities conflict with the core and need allocation independence.","A separate office is available while core approval criteria remain mandatory.","Core resources are plentiful so organizational fit becomes irrelevant."],"a":1},{"q":"What most directly tests a proposed low-end foothold?","o":["Current valuation and founder popularity compared with incumbent valuation.","Present audience and advertising reach compared with total industry volume.","One low advertised price compared with the incumbent's premium offer.","Entry-cohort needs, willingness to pay, viable economics and incumbent priorities."],"a":3},{"q":"Authors disagree on a real company's disruption classification. Sound exercise treatment?","o":["Specify case boundary/evidence or leave the actual-company verdict open.","Choose the later publication without comparing case boundaries.","Choose the better-known author without inspecting evidence.","Treat disagreement as proof foothold tests cannot be used."],"a":0},{"q":"What most weakens A's proposed low-end account?","o":["Later performance rises 65 to 75 while mainstream need remains 70.","Incumbent hurdle stays 35% while entrant model operates at 20%.","Dated entry data show demanding mainstream buyers, not the claimed low-end cohort.","Initial performance 40 exceeds low-end requirement 30."],"a":2}]},"132":{"objectives":["Build unit-consistent resilience stress tests with closed loops, cash bridges and bounded options; a paper trigger is not real authority."],"videos":[{"id":"3G4zLbQSMEc","title":"Creating Strategy in Uncertainty: Scenario Planning — SME Strategy","by":"SME Strategy","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use written scenario/continuity sources and this explicit fictional model."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"The use and abuse of scenarios - McKinsey","url":"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/the-use-and-abuse-of-scenarios","note":"2009 written article including base-case/contingency and revision caveats; no universal scenario count/probability rule."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Business Continuity Planning - Ready.gov","url":"https://www.ready.gov/business-continuity-plan","note":"Organize continuity work around a team, a plan and testing."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"LOOPY - optional systems sketchpad","url":"https://ncase.me/loopy/","note":"Landing text, not tested quantitative model. Draw signed links/delays; toy animation is not a causal estimate."}],"exercise":"Private fictional D 2 C tabletop, monthly cash plus a separate one-horizon option model. No real budget/contract/vendor onboarding authorized. (a)100,000 impressions, CPM Rs. 200, CTR 2%,purchase conversion 5% of clicks, all purchases distinct new customers. Calculate spend/customers/ CAC . CPM rises 60% to 320: calculate CAC with unchanged rates, then CTR 1.6%/click conversion 4%. CPM is not CAC . (b) Customer weights 70% Meta/30% other; baseline CAC 200/300, then 320/300. Compute blended CAC ; spend weights or changing customer mix need recomputation. (c) Monthly cash in lakh: opening 10; collected sales less paid variable product/fulfilment costs before marketing 5;marketing 2.3;fixed costs 3;additional working-capital build 1;principal.5; no other flows/tax. Reconcile cash without deducting fulfilment twice; working-capital build is incremental, not already in the 5. (d) Close a feedback loop: service failures -> refunds/backlog -> cash pressure -> capacity cuts -> service failures. State link signs, delays and assumptions. Fixed-cost absorption is not an extra cash flow. Scenarios are stress narratives, not exhaustive probabilities. (e) Separate option toy inRs. 1,000, single horizon/no discount/tax: unprotected failure loss 200 with probability p; option standby 20 plus exercise 60 and residual loss 40 on failure; full redundancy 80 avoids all failure loss. Expected costs 200 p,20+100 p,80: calculate indifference points/capacity limits, not an always-cheaper option rule. (f) Draft trigger, window, owner, fallback and approval boundary. A paper trigger never grants real spend authority. LOOPY can sketch hypotheses, not estimate a forecast.<section aria-label=\"Native D132 practice\"><h3>Acquisition, cash, closed loops and bounded resilience options</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d132-resilience-stress.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable resilience-stress lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d132-resilience-stress.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">13-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Campaign toy 100000 impressions, CPM 200, CTR 2%, click conversion 5% yields spend 20000, clicks 2000, 100 distinct new customers, CAC 200. CPM 320 alone gives CAC 320; combined CTR 1.6% and conversion 4% yields 64 customers and CAC 500. Modeled expectations may be fractional after edits; zero customers makes CAC undefined, not zero. Separate CUSTOMER weights 70% Meta/30% other give blended CAC 230 then 314 at fixed mix, not 368 or a spend-weighted average.</p><p>Monthly cash in lakh INR: opening 10 + collected sales less paid variable product/fulfilment 5 - marketing 2.3 - fixed 3 - additional working-capital build 1 - principal .5 = 8.2, net change -1.8. The 5 excludes these later deductions. Do not double-count fulfilment or working capital; fixed-cost absorption is not extra cash. Endpoint is not profit, runway or intramonth minimum.</p><p>Separate single-horizon thousand-INR option toy: unprotected 200p, standby option 20+100p, redundancy 80. Global minimum: unprotected below .2, option between .2/.6, redundancy above .6, ties at boundaries. At p=.4 costs 80/60/80: option wins despite the other pair tying. Edited crossings outside 0..1, parallel lines or reversed rankings require all-cost comparison, not reuse of baseline regions. Toy probability is assumed, not a frequency inferred from scenario labels. Added alternative capacity 100 required/100 available passes quantity only; 80 available leaves 20 uncovered and requires revisiting residual/full-avoidance assumptions. Lead time, quality, shared failure and contracts remain open.</p><p>Four positive hypothesized links close service failures to refunds/backlog to cash pressure to capacity cuts back to failures. Delays and other loops matter; polarity is not calibrated forecast or guaranteed exponential collapse. Paper seven-day failure trigger above 5% with at least 100 eligible orders, review window, fictional owner and fallback are planning examples, not actual monitoring or budget/vendor/account authority.</p><p>30 baseline formulas independently computed; 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid/480 isolation checks, six broken references/six checks; 12 sheets, 13 PDF pages after splitting input print for readability. All pages inspected; saved caches clean, no external links/macros, actual work blank. Full returned McKinsey 2009 written article, Ready.gov written landing updated 25 March 2026 (Planning Suite unsupported/unavailable), LOOPY written landing only, OECD risk-report abstract/executive summary and selected foresight nonprediction passage, selected MIT glossary definitions. Not supporting manuals/videos, full OECD/MIT PDFs/diagrams, executed LOOPY, empirical business proof, native cross-viewer, independent learner/account/legal or overall 10/10. No real campaign, vendor onboarding, budget, participant test, contract or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"100,000 impressions, CPM 200, CTR 2%,click conversion 5%: spend, new customers, CAC ?","o":["Rs. 20,000;2,000 customers;Rs. 10 CAC .","Rs. 200;100 customers;Rs. 2 CAC .","Rs. 20,000;5,000 customers;Rs. 4 CAC .","Rs. 20,000;100 customers;Rs. 200 CAC ."],"a":3},{"q":"CPM 320 with unchanged CTR /conversion: CAC ?","o":["Rs. 200: impressions remain 100,000.","Rs. 320: spend rises 60% with customers still 100.","Rs. 500: a CPM shock necessarily lowers conversion.","Rs. 125: higher CPM necessarily improves quality."],"a":1},{"q":"CPM 320, CTR 1.6%,conversion 4% on 100,000 impressions: CAC ?","o":["Rs. 500 fromRs. 32,000 and 64 customers.","Rs. 320 by using only the CPM change.","Rs. 400 fromRs. 32,000 and 80 customers.","Rs. 200 by preserving baseline CAC ."],"a":0},{"q":"70% of new customers cost 200,30% cost 300. Blended CAC ?","o":["Rs. 250 using an unweighted average.","Rs. 210 using 70% as a discount.","Rs. 230 using customer shares.","Rs. 270 weighting the high-cost channel 70%."],"a":2},{"q":"Keep customer weights; Meta CAC 320,other 300. Blended CAC ?","o":["Rs. 368 by applying 60% to the whole baseline.","Rs. 320 because the larger channel sets the blend.","Rs. 306 by weighting Meta 30% after the shock.","Rs. 314; a changed acquisition mix requires recomputation."],"a":3},{"q":"Under the stated monthly cash bridge, closing cash in lakh?","o":["9.7 from 10+5-2.3-3.","8.2 from 10+5-2.3-3-1-.5.","9.2 from 10+5-2.3-3-.5.","7.2 from 10+5-2.3-3-1-1-.5."],"a":1},{"q":"What distinguishes the stated feedback loop from a one-way chain?","o":["Every sequence ending in lower cash is a loop.","Every reinforcing loop must act without delay.","Capacity cuts feed back into service failures, closing the cycle.","Every balanced accounting statement creates a causal loop."],"a":2},{"q":"Option 20+100 p versus unprotected 200 p, in thousands: option strictly cheaper when?","o":["p>.2; equal at p=.2 under these assumptions.","p>.1 after ignoring exercise and residual costs.","p<.2 because lower failure risk recovers standby cost.","Every p>0 because flexibility always saves money."],"a":0},{"q":"Redundancy 80 versus option 20+100 p: redundancy strictly cheaper when?","o":["p>.4 after comparing residual loss 40 to cost 80.","p>.6; equal at p=.6 under these assumptions.","p<.6 because a lower failure rate favors full provision.","Every p because eliminating loss guarantees lowest expected cost."],"a":1},{"q":"Tabletop plan says switch vendors after a backlog trigger. What may be executed?","o":["Any named switch because a trigger grants pre-authorization.","Any spend below expected failure loss because the model grants permission.","Any onboarding assigned to an owner because accountability grants authority.","Only actions within verified approvals, contracts and account authority."],"a":3}]},"133":{"objectives":["Recompute changed repeated-game thresholds and review VRIO, new-demand, platform, disruption and resilience evidence without unsupported real-company verdicts."],"videos":[],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Repeated Games I - Stanford","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/~jdlevin/Econ%20286/Repeated%20Games%20I.pdf","note":"Selected earlier model/grim-proof reading; this game has different payoffs."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"UPI roles - NPCI","url":"https://www.npci.org.in/product/upi/roles-responsibilities","note":"Roles do not establish current fees, cap applicability or app value capture."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Disruptive Innovation Theory - Christensen Institute","url":"https://www.christenseninstitute.org/theory/disruptive-innovation/","note":"Apply foothold and trajectory evidence, not winner labels."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Business Continuity Planning - Ready.gov","url":"https://www.ready.gov/business-continuity-plan","note":"Team, plan and testing. A paper trigger is not real authority."}],"exercise":"Private fictional review of Days 127-132, not actual competitor coordination or operational authority. New game own payoffs in crore per quarter: mutual C 40; unilateral D 70; unilateral C 10; mutual D 15. Infinite common discount d, perfect public observation and credible grim punishment: derive 40/(1-d)>=70+15 d/(1-d), then change only temptation 70 to 50 and derive again. This is a parameter shock, not an assertion about real regulation. At d=.5 compare cooperation 80 with deviation 85 before and 65 after. Explain equality and finite-horizon boundaries. For a claimed driver-network advantage leave unsupported rarity/imitability unknown; organization means capture, not ownership. Review new-demand evidence against rival/own-service switching, network effects against volume, and disruption against cheapness alone. Fresh acquisition-channel toy: 100, 000 impressions, CPM 250, CTR 2%, purchase conversion 5% of clicks, each sale a distinct new customer. Then CPM 300 with rates unchanged; calculate CAC. A different cohort has 60% of customers at CAC 300 and 40% at CAC 200; calculate blended CAC. Fresh cash bridge in lakh: opening 12, collected sales less paid product/fulfilment costs before marketing 4, marketing 2, fixed 3, additional working-capital build 1, principal 0.5, no other flows/tax. Working capital is not already deducted from 4. Close a service-failure/cash/capacity loop and write a hypothetical trigger with data window, owner and approval boundary. No real pricing, subsidy, data use, budget, vendor or account action is authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D133 practice\"><h3>Changed games, noisy policies and integrated evidence review</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d133-integrated-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">14-sheet editable integrated-review lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d133-integrated-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">15-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Own synthetic crore/quarter payoffs T70 R40 P15 S10, common discount .5: perfect-public-observation infinite grim gives cooperation 80 against deviation 85, weak threshold 6/11. Change only T to 50: deviation 65 and threshold 2/7. At either boundary the tested values tie, not strict preference or unique equilibrium. Known finite N with unique stage Nash and no terminal rewards gives all D; different terminal games require another analysis. Strict-PD gates stop copying the verdict after payoff order changes.</p><p>Separate public false-alarm policy: independent .02 per cooperative period, N10 gives at least one alarm 1-.98^10, not 20%. Reward arrives before alarm and punishment starts next period. Cooperative policy value 79.019607843, punishment value 30, loss .980392157 against the perfect always-C path. No true-deviation signal law, noisy IC, equilibrium or optimal-forgiveness claim follows.</p><p>Fresh campaign 100000 impressions, CPM250, CTR2%, purchases/click5% gives 25000 spend, 100 distinct new customers, CAC250; CPM300 alone gives CAC300. Separate customer shares 60% at300 and40% at200 give weighted CAC260, not250 or240. Monthly lakh cash 12+4-2-3-1-.5=9.5, change -2.5. The4 is collected after paid variable product/fulfilment costs, before later deductions; endpoint is not profit or intramonth minimum.</p><p>Integrated evidence review separates VRIO benefit capture from ownership/unknown rarity; new category demand from own/rival switching; network effects from volume/scale and UPI roles from unresolved fee applicability; foothold/performance from adoption/displacement; closed causal-loop hypothesis from calibrated prediction and paper trigger from actual authority. Source disagreement remains attached to claims.</p><p>14 sheets and 15 PDF pages; 46 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid controls/668 isolation checks and six broken references/six checks. Saved caches clean, no external links/macros, actual work blank. Selected Stanford/NYU game setup, grim and finite passages plus MIT monitoring/tolerant-grim remarks revisited; not entire proofs/figures/course. Prior strategy/resilience evidence reused, not newly expanded. No actual competition/campaign/vendor/account/budget action, full source/AV, native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal or overall10/10 claim.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"New stage game C 40, unilateralD 70/C 10, mutualD 15: what dominates?","o":["C: 40>15 when both choose the same action.","C against D, because 10 is less costly than 15.","D: 70>40 against C and 15>10 against D.","Neither, because mutual C gives both a higher payoff."],"a":2},{"q":"What is the one-shot Nash outcome of the new game?","o":["Mutual D at 15 each, despite mutual C giving 40 each.","Mutual C at 40 each, because total payoff is larger.","One chooses D and one C at 70 and 10.","No Nash outcome because both prefer mutual C."],"a":0},{"q":"Original infinite grim threshold with R 40, T 70, P 15?","o":["d>=3/7, from 40>=70-70 d.","d>=2/3, by using punishment 25 instead of 15.","d>=2/7, from 40>=50-35 d.","d>=6/11, from 40>=70-55 d."],"a":3},{"q":"Change only T from 70 to 50. New threshold?","o":["d>=6/11, because punishment remains 15.","d>=2/7, from 40>=50-35 d.","d>=1/5, by dividing temptation gain 10 by 50.","d>=4/5, by dividing cooperative payoff 40 by 50."],"a":1},{"q":"At d=.5 in the original game, cooperation versus one-deviation value?","o":["80 versus 65, so cooperation satisfies it.","40 versus 70, so discounting has no role.","80 versus 85, so cooperation fails this constraint.","80 versus 100, because punishment is added undiscounted."],"a":2},{"q":"At d=.5 after T becomes 50, cooperation versus deviation?","o":["80 versus 85, so cooperation still fails it.","40 versus 50, so repetition cannot alter incentives.","80 versus 50, because future punishment is omitted.","80 versus 65, so cooperation satisfies this constraint."],"a":3},{"q":"At exactly d=6/11 in the original model, what is established?","o":["Indifference between following and the tested one-shot deviation.","Cooperation is uniquely optimal after every possible history.","Deviation gives strictly more than following cooperation.","All possible repeated-game equilibria give the same payoff."],"a":0},{"q":"Which change requires re-deriving rather than copying the grim threshold?","o":["All payoffs are renamed from rupees to crore consistently.","Defection is observed imperfectly and punishment can follow false alarms.","The matrix is drawn with the other player as rows.","Cooperation and defection labels are replaced by letters."],"a":1},{"q":"Known finite horizon, unique stage Nash and no terminal rewards: prediction?","o":["Mutual C until the final period, then D.","Mutual C if more than eleven periods remain.","Alternating actions to share the temptation payoff.","Mutual D each period by backward induction."],"a":3},{"q":"Network may be valuable, but matched-rival evidence is missing. VRIO verdict?","o":["Sustained advantage follows because the network is large.","Temporary advantage follows because incentives can always copy networks.","Rarity/imitability remain unknown; no sustained-advantage verdict yet.","Parity follows because every competitor operates some network."],"a":2},{"q":"What best tests VRIO organization?","o":["Rights, processes and incentives enable capture of the resource benefit.","The company owns the resource outright on its balance sheet.","The resource has a large historical acquisition price.","The resource cannot be named by rival management teams."],"a":0},{"q":"One-month 100 k shipments save 2 each; support.6 lakh/licence.4 lakh. Net saving?","o":["2 lakh before deducting operating support.","1.4 lakh after support but before licence.","1 lakh after 2 lakh gross minus 0.6 minus 0.4.",".6 lakh after treating licence as another support charge."],"a":2},{"q":"What separates proposed paid-category new demand from own-service diversion?","o":["Every customer buying the new product counts as new demand.","Prior category participation and own-product switching are recorded separately.","Every low-price buyer counts as a prior nonconsumer.","Every customer leaving a rival counts as new to the category."],"a":1},{"q":"A target value curve differs from rivals. What has been proved about willingness to pay?","o":["Nothing yet; a target curve needs customer and economic validation.","Difference proves customers will pay a premium for every raised factor.","Difference proves noncustomers lacked only product awareness.","Difference proves delivery costs will fall enough to fund creation."],"a":0},{"q":"Higher platform volume occurs during a subsidy campaign. What is still unknown?","o":["Whether volume rose, because subsidies make counting impossible.","Whether the platform has any users at all.","Whether higher volume necessarily lowers processing costs.","Whether participation itself improves value to other participants."],"a":3},{"q":"More merchants improve payer usability. Direction?","o":["Same-side benefit from merchants to other merchants.","Cross-side benefit from merchant participation to payers.","A pure supply-side economy of scale in processing.","A direct benefit from payers to other payers."],"a":1},{"q":"Interoperable rail: what follows for apps?","o":["Every app must have identical value and profit margins.","All customer relationships belong to the rail operator.","Reduced reach exclusivity may coexist with differentiated service or trust.","No app can retain users because app switching is free."],"a":2},{"q":"Conflicting fee summaries without operative text: defensible research treatment?","o":["Choose the older framework because it was familiar.","Choose the newer summary without scope checks.","Average reported rates into a stable planning assumption.","Keep dated applicability open and identify instruments/exceptions needed."],"a":3},{"q":"A cheap successful entrant immediately serves the best incumbent customers. Best starting hypothesis?","o":["Sustaining competition, not a demonstrated low-end/new-market foothold.","Low-end disruption because the price is lower.","New-market disruption because delivery is digital.","Disruption because revenue and customer count rose."],"a":0},{"q":"Entrant reaches required performance. What remains needed to establish actual displacement?","o":["Nothing: performance equality guarantees customer switching.","Evidence of adoption and competitive outcomes, not threshold attainment alone.","Only a higher valuation than the incumbent company.","Only confirmation that the founder intended disruption."],"a":1},{"q":"Fresh 100 k impressions, CPM 250, CTR 2%, click conversion 5%: CAC ?","o":["Rs. 12.50 from Rs. 25, 000 and 2, 000 clicks.","Rs. 25 from Rs. 2, 500 and 100 customers.","Rs. 500 from Rs. 25, 000 and 50 customers.","Rs. 250 from Rs. 25, 000 and 100 new customers."],"a":3},{"q":"Then CPM 300, same impression count/ CTR /conversion: CAC ?","o":["Rs. 300, since customers remain 100 and spend is 30, 000.","Rs. 250, since impression count is unchanged.","Rs. 600, because conversion necessarily halves.","Rs. 150, because higher spending improves reach quality."],"a":0},{"q":"60% of customers at CAC 300, 40% at CAC 200: blended CAC ?","o":["Rs. 250 using an unweighted average.","Rs. 240 using the opposite customer weights.","Rs. 260 using customer-share weights.","Rs. 300 using the larger acquisition channel."],"a":2},{"q":"Fresh monthly cash 12+4-2-3-1-.5, lakh: closing?","o":["11 lakh after omitting working capital and principal.","9.5 lakh after every specified cash flow.","10.5 lakh after omitting working capital only.","8.5 lakh after subtracting working capital twice."],"a":1},{"q":"Hypothetical vendor-switch tripwire is reached. What may happen in practice?","o":["Only actions within verified owner approvals and contract/account limits.","Every named response is authorized by writing the plan.","Any spend less than modeled expected loss is authorized.","Any action assigned to an accountable owner is authorized."],"a":0}]},"134":{"objectives":["Compare net parenting value with standalone and alternative-owner outcomes; apply diversification tests without forced actual-company pass/fail verdicts."],"videos":[{"id":"ZDExLnS9IC0","title":"Diversification Strategy (With Real World Examples) — From A Business Professor","by":"Business School 101","note":"Separate diversification from disruption. Treat historical company examples as dated illustrations, not proof a strategy causes success."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Corporate Strategy: The Quest for Parenting Advantage - Campbell, Goold and Alexander","url":"https://hbr.org/1995/03/corporate-strategy-the-quest-for-parenting-advantage","note":"March-April 1995 opening/landing only, not full article. Owner fit differs from industry attractiveness."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Diversification Strategy - Grant textbook chapter","url":"https://www.blackwellpublishing.com/content/GrantContemporaryStrategyAnalysis/6th_Edition/CSAC15.pdf","note":"Selected three-tests/better-off sections, not full chapter. Historical premium ranges/examples are not current facts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"From Competitive Advantage to Corporate Strategy - Porter, university mirror","url":"https://management.web.uniroma1.it/sites/default/files/optional%20reading%201%20porter_0.pdf","note":"Selected opening/portfolio/restructuring text, not full article. Dated country/conglomerate claims are not universal conclusions."}],"exercise":"Private fictional owner comparison. All values below are present values in crore on one consistent horizon/risk basis, not annual profit. Unit base 100 excludes all parenting benefits/costs. Parent A adds 10 gross, costs 3 central resources and 2 operating disruption: net 5. Parent B adds 9, costs 2 central resources and 1 disruption: net 6. Separate standalone enhancement option adds 4 net, attainable without either parent. Compare alternatives without adding mutually exclusive benefits. Intercompany management fees transfer value inside the consolidated group; count actual resources, outside taxes/costs and incentives separately rather than deducting the fee twice. An optional sale offers 106 for the unit, with 2 exit costs; compare 104 cash value to 105 retained value under A. This is not a real divestiture approval. Apply Porter: structural attractiveness/capable of improvement; entry cost must not consume all future profits; either linked unit or parent gains competitive advantage, not necessarily both. Test why ownership beats contracting, considering implementation, bargaining and control costs. For Tata Sons/Reliance/Aditya Birla/Campa optional research, specify legal entity, date, unit, parent capability, counterfactual owner and source. Do not force a negative unit or presume brand/distribution causes net advantage. Missing primary comparative evidence stays unknown. A conglomerate discount or relatedness alone does not establish causality. No actual offer, purchase, disclosure or corporate action authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D134 practice\"><h3>Net parenting, alternative owners and entry price</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d134-parenting-value.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable parenting-value lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d134-parenting-value.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Common-basis crore present values, not annual profit: bare base 100; A 10-3-2=5 net, value 105; B 9-2-1=6 net, value 106; standalone enhancement 4 net, value 104. A adds 5 over bare base, only 1 over enhanced standalone and trails B by 1. Paths mutually exclusive. Positive value added is not best-owner advantage; B is best only among the specified modeled candidates.</p><p>Optional gross offer 106-exit 2=104 versus A retained 105. Financial indifference gross 107, not 106. Buyer B modeled value 106 need not be the attainable offered price. Internal fee 8 leaves unit 100, parent 5, consolidated 105; fee 20 changes allocation to 88/17, not group 105. Count actual central resources and outside effects once; no tax/minority/incentive or real offer evidence supplied.</p><p>Separate accessible contract 8 gross-3 provider-1 coordination=4 net, value 104; A ahead 1. Contract gross 10 instead gives 106, tying B and exceeding A. Do not add standalone enhancement or parent resource costs twice. Separate purchase intrinsic 110-price 108-execution 2=0 NPV headroom; price 107 gives 1, 109 gives-1. Zero is not strictly positive value creation, affordability or authority.</p><p>Source questions differ: Porter attractiveness/cost-of-entry/better-off tests do not establish comparative best ownership. HBR opening and CMR abstract only; Grant tests/better-off and Porter opening/portfolio selected; BCG adding/subtracting discussion includes Campbell flexible 10% heuristic and Krell 2-5% objection; selected corporate-advantage/ownership text compares contracts. No current company or universal threshold claim; historical premium/share-price/Disney examples not current evidence.</p><p>10 sheets and 11 PDF pages; 31 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 24 changed sets + restore, 16 invalid controls/496 isolation checks, six broken references / six checks. Saved caches clean, no external links/macros, actual work blank. Full 11 PDF pages inspected after Start clipping fix. Not full source PDFs/figures/video, native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal or overall 10. No real corporate offer, sale, acquisition, contract, account, budget or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Parent A gross benefit 10, central resources 3, disruption 2: net parenting uplift?","o":["10 crore, making retained value 110 before parent costs.","5 crore, making total retained value 105 on base 100.","7 crore, making retained value 107 after central resources only.","3 crore, making retained value 103 after double-counting disruption."],"a":1},{"q":"Parent B gross 9, central 2, disruption 1 versus A net 5: which has greater modeled unit value?","o":["A at 110 versusB 109, comparing gross benefits only.","A at 105 versusB 104, treating disruption as three deductions.","B at 109 versus A 105, omitting B central/disruption costs.","B at 106 versus A 105, subject to the stated common basis."],"a":3},{"q":"Standalone enhancement adds 4 net versus A net 5. A's incremental uplift over that alternative?","o":["1 crore, not the entire 5 crore uplift over base.","5 crore, because parent benefit replaces all counterfactual analysis.","9 crore, by adding parent and standalone enhancements.","4 crore, because standalone enhancement is the whole parent benefit."],"a":0},{"q":"Sale proceeds 106 less exit cost 2 versus retention 105: modeled financial choice?","o":["Sell at 106 because headline price exceeds retained value 105.","Sell at 108 because exit costs are added to proceeds.","Retain at 105 rather than sell for 104, before omitted risks/constraints.","Indifferent at 105 because exit costs are irrelevant to value."],"a":2},{"q":"A unit pays its parent a management fee. Consolidated valuation should ordinarily do what?","o":["Deduct the fee as a group expense and also count all underlying resources.","Add the parent fee revenue without recognizing the unit payment.","Ignore actual central resources because the fee is internal.","Eliminate the internal transfer and count actual resources/external effects once."],"a":3},{"q":"Which correctly states Porter's better-off test?","o":["Both must gain exactly equal profit in the first year of ownership.","Either the linked unit or parent gains competitive advantage from the link.","The new unit must be impossible for any competitor to operate alone.","Group revenue must increase even if acquisition returns are negative."],"a":1},{"q":"An attractive industry but entry price absorbs all future excess returns: which test is at risk?","o":["Cost-of-entry, even if structural attractiveness is favorable.","Attractiveness only, because every expensive industry is structurally weak.","Better-off only, because ownership never improves high-priced assets.","No test, because attractive industries justify any entry premium."],"a":0},{"q":"Why compare ownership with a supply/distribution contract?","o":["Any feasible contract makes ownership automatically value-destroying.","Ownership always eliminates bargaining and implementation costs.","Similar benefits may be available without ownership, with different coordination costs.","Contracting is irrelevant whenever businesses share a category label."],"a":2},{"q":"A parent has strong brand recognition but no unit-level counterfactual data. What is supported?","o":["A sustained unit advantage, because brand awareness reduces every cost.","A possible parenting mechanism, not a proven net advantage or best-owner verdict.","A negative parenting verdict, because lack of data proves no benefit.","A best-owner verdict, because familiar brands cannot be replicated."],"a":1},{"q":"What makes a parenting-fit diagnosis more credible than a forced negative-unit example?","o":["Finding any underperforming unit and attributing all losses to its parent.","Selecting the least familiar industry and classifying it as alien territory.","Using portfolio size as the measure of value destroyed by the parent.","Evidence of unit success factors, parent capabilities, net effects and rival explanations."],"a":3}]},"135":{"objectives":["Separate standalone cash-flow value, control improvements, combination synergy and purchase price; match risk/basis and avoid ungrounded tax or competition-law rules."],"videos":[{"id":"bgKNMDWyaI8","title":"Session 24: Acquisition Valuation — Aswath Damodaran","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the written merger case for its explicit assumptions. Historical tax and accounting anecdotes are not current Indian rules, and concealment is not a recommended negotiation tactic."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Acquisition Valuation - Aswath Damodaran","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/AcqValn.pdf","note":"Selected valuation components/sequence only. Dated pooling, goodwill and tax examples are not current rules."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Value of Synergy - Damodaran, October 2005","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/papers/synergy.pdf","note":"Selected opening/sources/valuation/control separation, not full paper. Timing, incremental cash flow and risk matter."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Session 24: Acquisition Valuation - optional lecture","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bgKNMDWyaI8","note":"Garbled historical tax/country claims are not adopted."}],"exercise":"Private fictional cash acquisition, no actual Swiggy transaction. Revenue 120/EBITDA minus 5% and growth/WACC alone do not determine FCFF: first identify missing tax, cash conversion, D&A, capex, working capital, horizon, terminal and debt/cash inputs. Then use this COMPLETE independent toy, all crore: debt 20, excess cash 5; operating FCFF year 1=8/year 2=10, already after operating taxes/reinvestment; year 3=10.3, perpetual growth 3% thereafter; operating discount 13%, end-year flows. Terminal value at end of year 2=10.3/(.13-.03), no other nonoperating assets/liabilities. Compute EV and equity. CONTROL incremental after-tax FCFF 2 only at end of year 2, no terminal continuation, no overlap with base. COMBINATION synergy expected after-tax incremental FCFF 3 at end of year 1 and 5 at end of year 2, already probability-weighted and net of cannibalization/ongoing costs/reinvestment; upfront integration cost 2 at time 0; no terminal synergy. Stipulate 13% risk-matched discount for these incremental cash flows, not a claim that acquirer or combined WACC always applies. Compute control value/net synergy, maximum break-even EQUITY cash price to target holders and premium over standalone equity. Fixed target debt/cash assumption stays consistent with EV-to-equity bridge. A bid 20% above the WHOLE maximum price, not merely above its premium: compute price and acquirer NPV; paying the ceiling leaves no modeled acquirer upside. Keep control/synergy disjoint; no data benefit unless lawful access and incremental cash flow supported. Create an integration dependency/owner/evidence/stop-condition table, not an always-three/100 days rule. Indian competition/tax/accounting applicability stays an unscored primary-instrument research task, not a high-share-only rule. No bid, payment, securities trade, agreement, data access or disclosure authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D135 practice\"><h3>Acquisition value, overlap and price ceiling</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d135-acquisition-value.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable acquisition-value lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d135-acquisition-value.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>FCFF 8 in year 1, 10 in year 2, year 3=10.3 grows 3% at 13% operating discount: terminal 103 at end of year 2, EV 95.575221; debt 20/cash 5 give equity 80.575221. Revenue 120 and negative EBITDA alone leave FCFF/tax/reinvestment/terminal/bridge inputs missing. No actual Swiggy transaction.</p><p>Disjoint control 2 only year 2 gives 1.566293 PV. Expected combination 3 in year 1 and 5 in year 2 gives 6.570601 gross PV, less upfront integration 2=4.570601 net. No terminal increments or extra probability haircut. Separate stipulated 13% risk-matched rates do not establish universal acquirer/combined-WACC rules.</p><p>Zero-NPV equity ceiling 86.712115, modeled premium 6.136894 above standalone equity.20% above WHOLE ceiling gives 104.054538 bid and minus 17.342423 acquirer NPV. Paying ceiling leaves 0 upside; not an observed market premium, affordability proof, funding or authority.</p><p>Separate diagnostics, not original case revisions: delay combination benefits 1 year with integration still now gives net 3.814691, ceiling 85.956205. Find duplicate 1 in year 2 synergy already in control: remove.783147 PV once, net 3.787454 and ceiling 85.928969. Delay 0/overlap 0 restore. Actual dependency/owner/evidence/stop-condition work stays blank.</p><p>Selected Damodaran acquisition sequence/control and 2005 synergy opening/valuation/timing/wrong-rate/control passages refreshed, not full papers/charts/video. Combined-rate language has new-business-risk exceptions; match flow risk. Selected lecture slides support positive NPV/target-risk, not independent replications; prior Grant/BCG context reused without expanded coverage. Historical tax/pooling/goodwill/deal figures are not current Indian rules or prices.</p><p>11 sheets and 12 PDF pages; 35 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 24 changed sets + restore, 16 invalid controls/560 isolation checks, six broken references/six checks. Saved caches clean, no external links/macros, actual work blank. All 12 final PDF pages inspected after Input print sizing fix. No full source/AV, native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal or overall 10. No real bid, trade, agreement, funding, data access, account, budget or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Why do revenue 120, negative EBITDA margin, growth and WACC not uniquely determine standalone DCF ?","o":["Revenue growth itself supplies all required reinvestment and terminal assumptions.","Negative EBITDA forces standalone equity value to exactly zero under every cash-flow path.","A country equity premium alone supplies all operating cash flow and capital-structure inputs.","Taxes, reinvestment/cash conversion, horizon/terminal and balance-sheet inputs remain unspecified."],"a":3},{"q":"Toy year 3 FCFF 10.3, g 3%, discount 13%: terminal value at end of year 2?","o":["79.230769 crore, using 10.3/.13.","103 crore, using 10.3/(.13-.03).","100 crore, using 10/(.13-.03).","343.333333 crore, using 10.3/.03."],"a":1},{"q":"Year 1 FCFF 8, year 2 FCFF 10, terminal 103, discount 13%: standalone EV ?","o":["103 crore, using the year-2 terminal value without discounting.","107.079646 crore, using (8+113)/1.13 for both years.","95.575221 crore, using 8/1.13+113/1.13^2.","110.575221 crore, adding net debt15 to operating value."],"a":2},{"q":"Standalone EV 95.575221, debt 20, excess cash 5: equity?","o":["80.575221 crore after subtracting debt and adding excess cash.","110.575221 crore after adding debt and subtracting excess cash.","70.575221 crore after subtracting both debt and cash.","95.575221 crore because EV already equals equity regardless of financing."],"a":0},{"q":"Control increment 2 only at end of year 2, discount 13%: present control value?","o":["2 crore because control benefits need no discount.","15.384615 crore by treating one payment as a perpetuity.","1.566293 crore, with no automatic percentage control premium.","20 crore by applying an arbitrary 25% premium to equity 80."],"a":2},{"q":"Synergy 3 end of year 1, 5 end of year 2, upfrontintegration 2: net synergy value?","o":["6.570601 crore, using 3/1.13+5/1.13^2.","6 crore, using 3+5-2 without discounting.","8.136894 crore, by adding separate control value to gross synergy while omitting integration.","4.570601 crore, using 3/1.13+5/1.13^2-2."],"a":3},{"q":"What is maximum break-even EQUITY price with these nonoverlapping components?","o":["101.712115 crore by adding net debt 15 to the equity ceiling.","86.712115 crore: 80.575221+1.566293+4.570601.","80.575221 crore because all control and synergy must be ignored.","93.282716 crore by adding gross synergy before integration twice."],"a":1},{"q":"Bid 20% above WHOLE maximum 86.712115: bid and acquirer NPV ?","o":["104.054538 crore bid; minus 17.342423 crore NPV.","87.939494 crore bid; minus 1.227379 crore NPV.","104.054538 crore bid; plus 17.342423 crore NPV.","86.712115 crore bid; zero NPV because premium is already included."],"a":0},{"q":"Why not value risky target FCFF using a safer acquirer's cheaper hurdle automatically?","o":["Any acquisition uses the buyer's rate because its shareholders provide funds.","A larger acquirer eliminates target business risk through ownership alone.","The cheapest available borrowing rate is always the correct FCFF discount.","Discount risk must match the target cash flows, not merely who raises the money."],"a":3},{"q":"A proposed synergy duplicates a cost saving already in control FCFF. What is required?","o":["Retain both entries because control and synergy have different labels.","Double the risk discount but keep both copies of the same cash saving.","Remove the overlap or rebuild a consistent counterfactual before adding components.","Move one entry into the premium so the duplication is less visible."],"a":2}]},"136":{"objectives":["Design bounded decision rights and change support with evidence of adoption and outcomes, without inventing actual airline authority or employee incentives."],"videos":[{"id":"22coAwMD9M0","title":"Kotter's 8-Step Change Model Explained — EPM","by":"EPM","note":"Compare the change model's assumptions and sequence with the written case. A 75% management-support threshold is a heuristic, not a universal requirement."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"RAPID Decision Making - Bain","url":"https://www.bain.com/insights/rapid-decision-making/","note":"Whole 2023 text, not diagram. Group decider exception matters; agreement roles are limited to specified mandatory constraints."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Making good decisions, and making them happen - Bain","url":"https://www.bain.com/insights/making-good-decisions-and-making-them-happen/","note":"Whole 2007 written article; roles not lockstep or a panacea. Historical survey not independent current evidence."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The 8 Steps for Leading Change - Kotter","url":"https://www.kotterinc.com/methodology/8-steps/","note":"Whole current written page, not embedded video; distinguishes earlier steps from accelerators."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Prosci ADKAR Model","url":"https://www.prosci.com/methodology/adkar","note":"Whole written page/FAQs: individual model, not whole methodology; sequential but not linear; reassess as needs change."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Air India completes merger with Vistara -12 November 2024","url":"https://www.airindia.com/in/en/newsroom/press-release/vistara-merger-completed-second-airline.html","note":"Whole historical company release, not current fleet/metrics or actual decision rights. Future facility plans not verified as realized."}],"exercise":"Private fictional service-operation design, not an Air India instruction or current org chart. Historical evidence register: Air India 12 November 2024 press release reports completed Vistara merger and the five-year Vihaan.AI program; its dated fleet/routes/future plans are not present-day verified data, and the release does not reveal current individual KPIs or decision authority. (a) Design five hypothetical recurring decisions. For each specify scope, recommender, required agreement constraints, input, decider/rule, performer, deadline, escalation and execution check. RAPID is not the chronological order of its acronym. Bain 2023 says ideally one decider; a group can decide if resolution rules are explicit. A role label cannot override law, budget, safety, contracts or the actual owner's approval. (b) Kotter's earlier linear steps evolved into accelerators; urgency may concern an opportunity, not a fabricated crisis. Use visible wins and reinforcement as hypotheses, not guaranteed transformation success. (c) ADKAR: awareness, desire, knowledge, ability, reinforcement at individual level; a model, not the whole organizational method. Fictional 100 employees: 90 attend training, 60 demonstrate the required task independently; assess performance/support rather than infer ability from attendance. (d) Separate fictional call cohorts, comparable case mix/period/definition: before 100 calls at 10 minutes each, 80 resolved first contact; after 120 calls at 8 minutes each, 84 resolved first contact. Compute total time, resolution rate and change; volume/mix/precision and repeat-contact costs still need checks. Do not claim shorter calls improve customer outcomes. Track safety/quality and accessibility safeguards with speed/cost, and distinguish learning pilot from authorization to change systems. No actual roster, route, fare, vendor, account, budget or employee action authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D136 practice\"><h3>Decision boundaries, adoption and service tradeoffs</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d136-decision-change.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable decision/change lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d136-decision-change.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>RAPID names roles, not chronological steps. One recommender; ideally one decider, or a group with explicit disagreement rule. Agree is limited to mandatory constraints. Escalation to D does not waive required law, safety, budget or owner approval. Toy Gate checks listed fictional signoffs, scope, roles, performer/deadline and execution evidence; pass is not actual authority.</p><p>100 eligible people, 90 attend, 60 demonstrate independently: 90% attendance and 60% demonstrated ability. No linked roster, so 60/90 is not automatically attendee conversion. No inferred desire, knowledge, sustained use or employee diagnosis.</p><p>Before 100 calls at 10 min/80 resolved; after 120 at 8 min/84 resolved: total 1000 to 960 min (-4%); resolution 80% to 70% (-10 percentage points, -12.5% relative). Volume +20%, mean duration-20%, resolved count +5%. Speed/count alone do not prove better customer outcomes or causality.</p><p>Separate explicit assumption only: one extra 6 minute contact per unresolved case. 20/36 unresolved =>120/216 extra minutes, total 1120/1176 (+5%). Unresolved does not establish actual repeats or cost. Zero-baseline relative changes stay undefined, not zero. Compare quality/accessibility/safety and matched follow-up, case mix/period/definition before any incentive judgment.</p><p>Five blank fictional decision designs retain scope, R/A/I/D/P, group rule/deadline, signoff source, execution evidence and stop/reviewer conditions. Kotter opportunity urgency/evolution to accelerators; ADKAR individual model, sequential but not linear, earliest unmet needs reassessed. Actual airline KPIs/authority or employee changes not inferred.</p><p>10 sheets/11 PDF pages; 34 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid controls/544 isolation checks, six broken refs/six checks. Saved caches clean, no external links/macros, actual work blank. All 11 PDF pages inspected after Failure log layout corrected. No full source/AV, native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal/overall 10; no actual airline/employee/system/account/budget/spend action.</p><p>Complete returned written Bain 2023/2007, Kotter, Prosci/FAQs, Air India 12 Nov 2024 historical release and IHI measurement page refreshed; not diagrams/embedded video. Bain 2023 group-D exception qualifies 2007 single-person wording; same firm not independent replication. Air India fleet/plans are dated, not current verified facts. IHI healthcare measurement transferred as design aid, not airline causal validation; vendor success claims not independent efficacy evidence.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Under Bain 2023 RAPID, what is a defensible decider rule?","o":["Ideally one decider; a group requires a clear rule to resolve disagreement.","Exactly one human in every case, with no committee exception.","Every input provider must agree before any decision can be final.","The performer decides automatically because implementation follows the choice."],"a":0},{"q":"What distinguishes Agree from Input in a bounded RAPID design?","o":["Agree is anyone affected; Input is reserved only for legal approval.","Agree is optional advice; Input always holds final veto power.","Agree checks mandatory constraints; Input informs without a general veto.","Agree andInput are identical titles for the same unanimous vote."],"a":2},{"q":"A decider wants to skip a mandatory safety signoff. What follows from the RAPID label?","o":["TheDcan override every agreement because final accountability is singular.","No override: required approval must be obtained or the plan changed/stopped.","TheRcan record the disagreement and treat silence as safety approval.","ThePcan execute early because a decision role establishes legal authority."],"a":1},{"q":"What is wrong with treatingR-A-P-I-Das chronological steps?","o":["TheDmust always act before any recommendation is prepared.","ThePmust never supply input before implementation begins.","TheArole replaces all fact gathering once a proposal exists.","It names roles; input and execution planning need not follow that order."],"a":3},{"q":"How does currentKotter framing qualify a compulsory burning-platform story?","o":["Urgency can mobilize an opportunity; a fabricated crisis is not required.","Urgency requires an existential crisis in every transformation.","Urgency means repeating alarming messages without changing barriers.","Urgency guarantees success once 75% ofmanagers publicly agree."],"a":0},{"q":"90 of 100 attend training; 60 demonstrate independent task ability. What can be reported?","o":["90% ability because attendance proves skill transfer.","60% attendance because only task performers count as trainees.","90% attendance and 60% demonstrated ability, not 90% ability.","150% adoption by adding attendance and performance shares."],"a":2},{"q":"Before 80 resolved of 100 calls; after 84 of 120: first-contact resolution rates?","o":["80% before, 84% after, a 4 percentage-point improvement.","80% before, 70% after, a 10% relative decline.","80% before, 84% after, a 5% relative improvement.","80% before, 70% after, a 10 percentage-point decline."],"a":3},{"q":"Before 100 calls*10 minutes; after 120*8: aggregate time change?","o":["1, 000 to 800 minutes, a 20% decline in aggregate time.","1, 000 to 960 minutes, a 4% decline, despite 20% lower average duration.","1, 000 to 1, 200 minutes, a 20% increase in aggregate time.","1, 000 to 960 minutes, a 40% decline in aggregate time."],"a":1},{"q":"What do shorter calls but lower resolution suggest for an incentive redesign?","o":["Reward average duration alone because total minutes fell.","Reward resolved-count alone because 84 exceeds 80 regardless of volume.","Review repeat contacts and quality; speed alone does not prove customer benefit.","Declare causality proven because both metrics changed in the same period."],"a":2},{"q":"What can the datedAir India merger release establish for this exercise?","o":["Dated merger/program facts, not current individual KPIs or our authority map.","The actual current incentive scorecard for every airline manager.","Current fleet size because a historical press release remains online.","Authority to assign airline decisions using our hypothetical RAPID table."],"a":0}]},"137":{"objectives":["Separate verified evidence, allegations and unresolved gaps when tracing board oversight; test a fictional control chain without assigning unverified real-company blame."],"videos":[{"id":"KGs4n4kV-qQ","title":"Corporate Governance Explained — J.P. Morgan Insights","by":"jpmorgan","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use the OECD reading and fictional controls; no real-company failure verdict is sourced to this video."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"G20/OECD2023 Principles - board responsibilities","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/g20-oecd-principles-of-corporate-governance-2023_ed750b30-en/full-report/component-8.html","note":"Read the board chapter and annotations. Good practice across jurisdictions, not binding Indian law or a verdict on any named company."}],"exercise":"Private fictional governance case, not an investigation or legal opinion. The OECD2023 board chapter is a good-practice reference, not a statement of current Indian statutory requirements. At reporting date, three non-overlapping entities have independently confirmed cash7,5,4 crore, while a draft consolidation reports20. Receivables12 and8 include a3 crore internal balance counted once in those amounts; eliminate it to compute external receivables. Identify differences without treating either as proof of fraud. A material vendor relationship was disclosed on day1; the committee received it on day3; the board approved the transaction on day5; an internal memo alleges influence but supplies no minutes. Build a timeline separating each record, inference, unresolved question and next evidence. Explain conflicts, recusal, disinterested review and documented escalation as controls, not a guarantee or unverified legal minimum. Distinguish internal audit, external audit, board oversight and credit ratings. If researching Jet Airways, Yes Bank, IL&FS, Satyam or HDFC, first obtain dated primary findings and responses; this exercise supplies no factual verdict on their causes, directors' knowledge or quality. Finish with an unsent four-sentence fictional refusal to misstate numbers: state the issue, accurate alternative, evidence needed and escalation boundary. No actual message, access request, disclosure, allegation or regulator contact is authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D137 practice\"><h3>Governance evidence without invented blame</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d137-governance-evidence.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable governance-evidence lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d137-governance-evidence.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional same-date, non-overlapping confirmed cash 7+5+4=16 crore; draft 20 differs by signed/absolute 4, relative 25%. Arithmetic equality 0. A difference is not fraud probability, legal materiality, director knowledge or a missing transaction. Unverified basis flags leave relevant totals undefined; zero cash denominator leaves relative difference undefined.</p><p>Receivables 12+8 include internal 3 ONCE: external total 17. Wrong double elimination yields 14, understating by 3. That diagnostic wrong result is not an acceptable alternative. This receivable elimination does not reduce the separate independent cash total. IFRS 10 B86 supports elimination within its scope, not an asserted Indian applicability conclusion.</p><p>Disclosure day 1, committee receipt day 3, approval record day 5: intervals 2/2/4, chronology 1. Recorded sequence is not proof of knowledge, influence or statutory delay. Reverse dates are flagged, not silently sorted. An influence memo is an allegation record, not corroborated minutes.</p><p>Toy disclosure, disinterested review, minutes, balance check, escalation and confidentiality flags are 1/1/0/1/1/1: overall 0. Changing minutes to 1 only passes listed fictional switches, not actual operating assurance or authority. Management owns decisions/risk; internal audit is independent assurance/advice; external audit has scoped assurance; board oversees. Related-party status triggers review, not a finding of abuse.</p><p>Blank evidence register distinguishes record, allegation, inference and gap; four unsent fictional refusal sentences ask for issue, accurate alternative, missing evidence and escalation boundary. Protect identities and authorized access. No real-company verdict, actual message, access request, disclosure or regulator contact.</p><p>10 sheets/11 PDF pages; 25 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid controls/400 isolation checks, six broken references/six explicit checks. Saved caches clean, no external links/macros; actual register and four unsent sentences blank. All 11 PDF pages visually inspected. No native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/legal or overall 10/10 claim.</p><p>Complete returned OECD 2023 board chapter/annotations read. Selected IIA principles/roles, PCAOB .01-.10A/.29, SEC credit-rating definition/limitations, IFRS 10 B86 and COSO 2013 definition/effectiveness/limitations passages only, not whole sources/diagrams/AV. OECD good practice is not binding Indian law; PCAOB is US context; ratings are credit opinions, not guarantees or cash confirmations. COSO offers reasonable, not absolute assurance. Six source organizations support design, not six independent efficacy tests.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Confirmed cash7+5+4; draft total20 crore. What is established?","o":["Confirmed total20; consolidation adds4 of internal funding.","Confirmed total16; the difference proves a director stole4.","Confirmed total4; entity cash must be netted against the draft.","Confirmed total16; the draft has an unreconciled difference4."],"a":3},{"q":"Receivables12+8 include an internal3 counted once. External total?","o":["20 crore because both entities recorded valid assets.","17 crore after eliminating the internal3.","14 crore after subtracting the internal3 twice.","23 crore because intercompany lending adds liquidity."],"a":1},{"q":"A memo alleges influence, but minutes are missing. Best record?","o":["Label allegations and missing minutes apart from verified facts.","Treat the memo as minutes because both describe a board decision.","Remove the memo because an allegation has no investigative value.","Attribute the influence to the directors because approval followed it."],"a":0},{"q":"A director has a material vendor tie. Best control design here?","o":["Keep the tie confidential and rely on the director's sector expertise.","Approve if the price is low, because that resolves the conflict.","Disclose the tie; use disinterested review and record its decision.","Require the conflicted director to certify the transaction alone."],"a":2},{"q":"What can be inferred from an external AAA credit rating alone?","o":["A substitute for independent verification of reported cash balances.","A credit-risk opinion, not proof that every governance control works.","A board-level assurance that no related-party transaction exists.","A conclusion that any later loss must involve deliberate fraud."],"a":1},{"q":"A transaction is with a related party. Which conclusion follows?","o":["It must transfer value away from shareholders regardless of its terms.","It is safe if the counterparty is a separate legal company.","It is arm's-length if its price matches last year's approved price.","Review conflict and terms; related-party status is not proof of abuse."],"a":3},{"q":"The board assigns audit work to a committee. OECD good-practice reading?","o":["Delegation makes the external auditor responsible for the whole company strategy.","Delegation removes the board from oversight in every jurisdiction.","Board responsibility generally remains, subject to the legal framework.","Delegation means only committee members may examine any financial information."],"a":2},{"q":"How should internal and external audit relate to board oversight?","o":["Distinct assurance roles feed oversight; neither replaces management or the board.","External audit sets operating risk appetite and authorizes every vendor payment.","Internal audit prepares all accounts and independently audits its own choices.","The board delegates strategy and all risk ownership to the auditors."],"a":0},{"q":"Which exercise output is defensible without primary case findings?","o":["A ranked list of named directors responsible for causing the collapse.","A claim that an entire named group has proven durable governance.","A fictional control map, labeling the missing real-case evidence.","A definitive first-failed control chosen from the news headline."],"a":2},{"q":"A reporting employee fears retaliation. Best governance response?","o":["Publish the employee's identity so all directors can seek corroboration.","Confidential reporting, protected review and controlled evidence access.","Ask the implicated manager to decide whether the concern may be recorded.","Suppress the concern unless the employee first proves a criminal offence."],"a":1}]},"138":{"objectives":["Test actionable segmentation hypotheses with behavioral evidence and correct relative search-interest interpretation, without inventing interviews or market size."],"videos":[{"id":"iGOw39GWDaI","title":"STP Marketing (Segmentation, Targeting, Positioning) — EPM","by":"EPM","note":"Use the written segmentation and Trends sources; the exercise does not imply completed interviews or an actual market dataset."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Effective market segmentation - OpenStax5.4","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-marketing/pages/5-4-essential-factors-in-effective-market-segmentation","note":"Written chapter and knowledge checks: ADAMS criteria. Category labels do not guarantee actionability."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GoogleTrends FAQ","url":"https://support.google.com/trends/answer/4365533","note":"Sampling, normalization, statistical noise and low-volume zero. Search interest is not a poll or a demand count."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Compare Trends search terms","url":"https://support.google.com/trends/answer/4359550?hl=en","note":"Distinguish topics from terms and use compatible filters."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Trends regional interest","url":"https://support.google.com/trends/answer/4355212?hl=en","note":"Regional relative interest is not the absolute number of searches."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"The MomTest - official teacher resources","url":"https://www.momtestbook.com/teachers","note":"Exercises on biased questions and commitments; this is not the full book or a completed interview study."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Rediscovering Market Segmentation - HBR","url":"https://hbr.org/2006/02/rediscovering-market-segmentation","note":"Excerpt or overview; the complete publication may require separate access. Treat this as a further-reading lead."}],"exercise":"Private research design for an adult packaged-snack concept, not nutrition advice or a live market study. Draft three need/occasion hypotheses, including current alternatives, exclusions and a test that could overturn each. Demographic or geographic cuts are allowed when evidence shows different responses and feasible actions. Apply accessible, differentiable, actionable, measurable and substantial criteria; do not assume perfect segment homogeneity. Build a two-interview protocol using recent actual behavior, current alternatives, constraints and concrete next steps. Do not conduct outreach or invent participants, quotes or commitments here. If real evidence is later collected with permission, preserve consent and anonymize notes. Record stated intent as weaker, qualified evidence, not zero; prior spending is a clue, not the new offer's proven willingness to pay. Fictional pilotA:100 qualified offers,12 purchases,price200,variable cost120 per purchase,attributable acquisition spend600. PilotB:200 qualified offers,18 purchases,unit contribution50,attributable acquisition spend300. Compute conversion and contribution after acquisition; do not treat unequal, unrandomized cohorts as causal proof. GoogleTrends calculation only:regionA has200 matching searches among10,000 total;B has1,000 among100,000. Under a shared maximum share2%, calculate indices100 and50. They are relative search-interest shares, not population, absolute demand or sales. No Trends dataset was downloaded. Specify identical term/topic, geography, time and category filters for a future comparison. Choose a beachhead with uncertainty and a stop/test condition, not simply the smallest or largest segment.<section aria-label=\"Native D138 practice\"><h3>Segmentation evidence and pilot economics</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d138-segment-economics.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable segmentation/economics lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d138-segment-economics.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional pilot A: 100 qualified offers, 12 purchases, price 200, variable cost 120, attributable acquisition spend 600. Conversion 12%, unit contribution 80, revenue 2400, contribution before acquisition 960, after acquisition 360, net per offer 3.6 and acquisition per purchase 50. This is contribution, not accounting profit, full CAC or LTV.</p><p>Pilot B: 200 offers, 18 purchases, unit contribution 50, attributable acquisition 300. Conversion 9%, gross contribution 900, net 600, net per offer 3 and acquisition per purchase 16.666667. B has higher cohort total; A higher conversion/net per offer. Unequal unrandomized cohorts do not prove causality or scale economics. No fixed costs, retention, refunds or capacity supplied; B revenue cannot be reconstructed.</p><p>Synthetic search A 200/10000=2%, B 1000/100000=1%; shared maximum 2% gives indices 100/50 despite B having five times matching searches. Common maximum derives from current shares; a changed maximum can rescale both indices. This is normalization arithmetic, not the full provider algorithm or actual downloaded dataset. Low-volume zero/blank regions do not establish no interest; sampling/noise and filter scope matter.</p><p>Three blank need/occasion hypotheses retain current alternatives, constraints, reach/action, disconfirmation and stop conditions. Two blank interview protocols ask about recent behavior, alternatives and tradeoffs without pitching. Prior spending is a clue, not proven willingness to pay for this offer; stated intent is qualified evidence, not a purchase or no evidence. No actual outreach, participants, quotes, consent, commitments or market-size estimate.</p><p>ADAMS means accessible, differentiable, actionable, measurable, substantial. Toy flags 1/1/0/1/1 give overall 0; all 1 only passes listed fictional switches. Need labels do not guarantee homogeneity or superiority over useful demographic/geographic cuts. No automatic largest/smallest/highest-index beachhead. No nutrition advice, actual launch, ad spend or account action.</p><p>10 sheets/11 PDF pages; 28 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid controls/448 isolation checks, six broken references/six explicit checks. Zero offers/purchases and all-zero search shares retain undefined ratios/indices; negative A unit contribution is not clipped. Saved caches clean/no externals/macros; actual hypotheses/protocols blank. All 11 PDF pages inspected; not native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/field/account/nutrition/legal or overall 10/10.</p><p>Complete returned written OpenStax 5.4 chapter/knowledge checks, three Google Trends help pages and Mom Test teacher page read. HBR 2006 opening/summary excerpt only, not full article; teacher page not full book. Terms/quoted phrases/topics and compatible time/geography/category/property filters distinguished. Six URLs from four organizations are educational/provider/author/practitioner guidance, not six independent efficacy tests. No diagrams/AV, actual Trends UI/data, interviews or customer demand verified.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Which segmentation cut can be useful?","o":["Only a need-state label, even when members respond identically to others.","Only a demographic cut, because census counts establish product demand.","An age or region cut supported by different responses and feasible action.","Any small cluster, because a narrow label establishes an unmet need."],"a":2},{"q":"A proposed segment cannot be reached through available channels. Which criterion is weak?","o":["Accessibility.","Differentiability.","Measurability.","Substantiality."],"a":0},{"q":"Two segments respond similarly to every tested offer. Main concern?","o":["Accessibility fails even though both groups can be reached.","Measurability fails even though both groups can be counted.","Substantiality fails even though both groups are profitable.","Differentiability is unproven for those marketing decisions."],"a":3},{"q":"Which evidence best tests a proposed snack occasion?","o":["A compliment about how original the product concept sounds.","A recent purchase episode, alternative used and tradeoff made.","A promise to buy if price, taste and availability all improve.","An agreement with the interviewer's suggested use case."],"a":1},{"q":"What does prior spending on an alternative establish?","o":["A guaranteed minimum price every customer will pay for the new offer.","A verified ceiling for all customers who share the same demographic.","A useful spending reference, not proven willingness to pay for this offer.","A commitment to switch if the new product has similar ingredients."],"a":2},{"q":"Which question avoids baking the desired answer into an interview?","o":["Would you love an affordable, healthier snack that saves time?","Would you buy this if everyone in your group recommended it?","Do you agree our proposed product solves your main problem?","Tell me about the last time you bought a snack for that occasion."],"a":3},{"q":"How should a participant's stated future interest be logged?","o":["As conditional intent, distinct from observed behavior and commitments.","As a completed purchase if the participant sounded enthusiastic.","As no evidence at all, regardless of specificity or context.","As a market-wide conversion forecast after two similar answers."],"a":0},{"q":"FictionalA:12 sales from100 offers;200 price,120 variable cost,600 acquisition. Result?","o":["12% conversion;960 contribution after acquisition.","12% conversion;360 contribution after acquisition.","8% conversion;360 contribution after acquisition.","12% conversion;1,800 contribution after acquisition."],"a":1},{"q":"FictionalB:18/200 offers,50 unit contribution and300 acquisition. Result?","o":["18% conversion;600 contribution after acquisition.","9% conversion;900 contribution after acquisition.","9% conversion;300 contribution after acquisition.","9% conversion;600 contribution after acquisition."],"a":3},{"q":"A has12% conversion and net360;B9% and net600 with twice as many offers. Conclusion?","o":["B proves that lower conversion causes higher profitability.","A must be better on every unit-economic measure because conversion is higher.","B has higher cohort total; A higher net per offer; neither proves causality.","B proves the same result would hold at A's size and acquisition mix."],"a":2},{"q":"RegionA200/10,000 searches;B1,000/100,000. Relative shares?","o":["A2%;B1%, despite B having more matching searches.","A1%;B2%, because B has more matching searches.","A20%;B10%, after dividing by the matching-search count.","A2%;B10%, after using A's total-search denominator for both."],"a":0},{"q":"With a shared maximum share2%, indices for A2% and B1%?","o":["A20;B100, from the raw matching counts200 and1,000.","A100;B100, because both include at least100 searches.","A100;B50, not an absolute search-count ratio.","A2;B1, because the displayed index is a percentage count."],"a":2},{"q":"A Trends cell shows0. Best interpretation?","o":["It proves nobody in that region searched for any synonym.","Low volume is possible; zero does not prove no searches or demand.","It proves market revenue was zero during the selected period.","It proves a product launch cannot work in that region."],"a":1},{"q":"Which setup makes a Trends comparison more interpretable?","o":["Compatible geography/time/category and a deliberate term-versus-topic choice.","Separate time windows chosen to maximize each term's visible peak.","One topic and one exact term treated as identical query definitions.","Independent charts whose100 peaks are compared as absolute volumes."],"a":0},{"q":"Two convenience interviews contradict a segment hypothesis. Next step?","o":["Discard the interviews because qualitative evidence cannot challenge a model.","Multiply the two responses by the city population to size the segment.","Claim the segment is impossible because both participants rejected the offer.","Revise the hypothesis and test deliberately; do not estimate population prevalence."],"a":3},{"q":"Which beachhead choice is defensible?","o":["Always the largest segment, because total population determines early fit.","A reachable, worthwhile segment with delivery fit and a testable economic case.","Always the smallest segment, because narrowness guarantees defensibility.","The highest Trends index alone, because interest proves willingness to pay."],"a":1},{"q":"No interviews or Trends download occurred. Correct exercise status?","o":["Two interviews completed because the protocol has two participant slots.","Market demand validated because the toy index is arithmetically correct.","Protocol and toy results only; actual customer and dataset evidence are missing.","Segmentation rejected because no live dataset was attached to this exercise."],"a":2}]},"139":{"objectives":["Develop positioning from alternatives, differentiated capabilities and buyer value; test contribution and conversion without assuming a frame creates a premium or a moat."],"videos":[{"id":"hdjlCLb9Hl8","title":"How to nail your product positioning — April Dunford (Obviously Awesome), Lenny's Podcast","by":"Lenny's Podcast","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. B2B examples and sponsor claims are not independent evidence for consumer-brand premiums or historical company verdicts."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"An Introduction to Positioning - April Dunford","url":"https://www.aprildunford.com/post/an-introduction-to-positioning","note":"April Dunford's May 4, 2020 article explains five positioning components, context and the limits of fill-in-the-blank statements. It is an article, not the full book."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Elements of Value - Bain","url":"https://www.bain.com/insights/the-elements-of-value-hbr/","note":"Introductory excerpt describing thirty value elements in four categories. Use it as a hypothesis checklist, not a formula guaranteeing a price premium."}],"exercise":"Private hypothetical positioning exercise, not a factual claim about PaperBoat or boAt. Follow Dunford's five components: competitive alternatives including status quo; differentiated capabilities; customer value; best-fit target segment; market category. A fill-in-the-blank statement is an output summary, not the method or proof. Choose a familiar adult beverage concept; if using a real brand, leave current category, prices, purchase motives and capabilities as evidence gaps until verified. Propose two frames and identify which attributes each makes salient, the promised experience, delivery costs and a falsification test. Use Bain's four value categories as a hypothesis checklist, not a universal premium ranking or proof that nostalgia/identity is uncopyable. Dunford's discussion focuses on B2B; transfer to consumer packaged goods needs testing. Fictional packageA:100 qualified offers,20 buyers,price80,variable cost50 per purchase,total marketing200. PackageB:100 qualified offers,15 buyers,price100,variable cost50,total marketing200. Compute conversion, contribution before marketing and after marketing. B changes both frame and price; an observed difference cannot isolate the frame effect. Design a randomized, comparable follow-up that distinguishes price from frame and measures net contribution, returns and repeat behavior. No real test, outreach, persona-tool account, brand claim or pricing change is authorized. Retain unverified claims as hypotheses, not scored company verdicts.<section aria-label=\"Native D139 practice\"><h3>Positioning, price and contribution</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d139-positioning-economics.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable positioning/economics lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d139-positioning-economics.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional original A: 100 qualified offers, 20 buyers, price 80, variable cost 50, marketing 200 =>20% conversion, revenue 1600, gross contribution 600, after marketing 400. Original B: 100/15/100/50/200=>15%, revenue 1500, gross 750, net 550. Conversion falls 5 percentage points (25% relative), net rises 150. Price and frame changed; frame effect not isolated. Contribution is not accounting profit, full CAC or LTV.</p><p>Illustrative extra cells, not actual observations: frame A/price 100 has 16 buyers; B/80 has 24. Each cell 100 offers, variable cost 50, marketing 200. Four-cell design contrasts conversion B-A +4 points at 80, -1 at 100; interaction-5 points, equal-weight average+1.5 points. Net per offer contrasts+1.2/-0.5, interaction-1.7, average+0.35. Average hides reversal; not significance, optimal price, forecast or actual causal proof. Hypothetical cells cannot repair missing actual random assignment.</p><p>Five linked positioning components: competitive alternatives including status quo, differentiated capabilities, buyer value, best-fit customers, category context. A statement summarizes choices, not method or proof. Useful common features are not differentiation by themselves. Two blank positioning cards and a blank falsification protocol retain counterevidence and stop conditions; no invented customer, quote, consent, real-brand capability, current price, premium or moat.</p><p>Bain consumer framework 30 elements/four categories supports value hypotheses, not a universal premium hierarchy. Its 2019 US survey/growth associations are not randomized causal proof; 40%rating 8+ is its operational rule, not universal threshold. Current B 2 B/B 2 C site variants must not be conflated. B 2 B-to-consumer transfer remains unverified; no historical example promoted to a current brand fact.</p><p>Plan only: comparable randomized 2 x 2 frame-price cells, justified assignment unit, stable eligibility/channel/exposure, primary net contribution per qualified offer, cost scope, window, returns/repeat/delivery costs, uncertainty, power/consent gaps and predeclared stop rules. No actual experiment, participant, price change, outreach, ad spend, persona account or deployment. Randomization does not guarantee power or representativeness.</p><p>11 sheets/12 PDF pages; 44 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 24 changed sets+restore, 16 invalid controls/704 isolation checks, 6 broken refs/6 explicit checks, 4 same-price mismatches/176 comparabilitychecks. Unequal paired prices suppress frame contrasts and interactions; zero denominators undefined, negative contribution retained. Saved caches clean/no externals/macros; actual cards/protocol blank. All 12 PDF pages inspected, not native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner/account/field or overall 10/10.</p><p>Complete returned Dunford 2020 written article, NIST three-factor example/tables and one-factor randomization reference, OpenStax chapter 3 key terms read. Bain 2016 returned introductory excerpt, not full HBR article; Bain 2019 returned written brief incl method/figure-caption text, not underlying data or figure pixels. Six URLs from four organizations author/practitioner/statistical/accounting sources, not 6 independent tests. No book, diagrams/AV, actual buyer or brand study verified.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"What role does a positioning statement play in Dunford's approach?","o":["It establishes the best category once a benefit is written in the final blank.","It can summarize choices; filling blanks is not the positioning process.","It validates customer value before alternatives or capabilities are examined.","It replaces target segmentation if the tagline is specific enough."],"a":1},{"q":"Which belongs in competitive alternatives?","o":["Only products with an identical technical specification and list price.","Every firm listed in the same stock-market sector classification.","Only the product the seller most wants to displace this quarter.","What the customer would do without the offer, including status quo."],"a":3},{"q":"Which link connects a differentiated capability to positioning?","o":["A buyer-valued outcome, supported for the target context.","A unique technical label that no buyer needs to understand.","A larger feature count than the nearest competitor's catalogue.","A category name that conceals how the capability changes outcomes."],"a":0},{"q":"A capability is common among alternatives. What follows?","o":["It cannot create any customer value under any market condition.","It proves the whole offer has no possible differentiated capability.","It may be necessary value, but not evidence of differentiation by itself.","It becomes unique when the positioning statement uses different wording."],"a":2},{"q":"How should a market category be chosen?","o":["To name the largest available market regardless of the offer's capabilities.","To imply premium pricing before purchase and delivery evidence exists.","To avoid mentioning any alternative the customer already understands.","To supply useful context for the differentiated value and best-fit buyers."],"a":3},{"q":"A new frame makes nostalgia salient. What is still unproven?","o":["Whether the seller can write a concise slogan about the new frame.","Whether buyers value it enough to cover price, costs and usage tradeoffs.","Whether nostalgia can be described as an emotional concept in language.","Whether the proposed category label differs from the previous label."],"a":1},{"q":"Which is a defensible use of Bain's value categories here?","o":["Use the four value categories to generate hypotheses and test buyer relevance.","Rank every product's pricing power solely by its highest category.","Assume every buyer values all30 elements in the same priority order.","Infer an uncopiable moat whenever an emotional benefit is mentioned."],"a":0},{"q":"A brand claims identity makes its moat uncopyable. Best response?","o":["Accept the moat because identity is not a physical product specification.","Reject any possible advantage because all slogans can be copied.","Test switching, alternatives and replication costs before claiming a moat.","Measure only social-media followers to establish sustained pricing power."],"a":2},{"q":"PackageA:20/100 buyers,price80,cost50,marketing200. Result?","o":["20% conversion;1,600 contribution before marketing and1,400 after.","20% conversion;600 contribution before marketing and400 after.","25% conversion;600 contribution before marketing and400 after.","20% conversion;600 contribution before marketing and600 after."],"a":1},{"q":"PackageB:15/100 buyers,price100,cost50,marketing200. Result?","o":["15% conversion;1,500 contribution before marketing and1,300 after.","20% conversion;750 contribution before marketing and550 after.","15% conversion;750 contribution before marketing and750 after.","15% conversion;750 contribution before marketing and550 after."],"a":3},{"q":"B versusA in this toy: conversion and after-marketing difference?","o":["Conversion falls5%; contribution rises150 percentage points.","Conversion rises5 percentage points; contribution falls150.","Conversion falls5 percentage points; contribution rises150.","Conversion falls25 percentage points; contribution rises350."],"a":2},{"q":"Can the package comparison isolate the causal frame effect?","o":["No; price also changed and assignment/comparability are not established.","Yes; both packages were shown to100 qualified people.","Yes; after-marketing contribution differs even after subtracting200.","No; conversion and contribution must always move in the same direction."],"a":0},{"q":"Which follow-up can distinguish frame and price effects?","o":["Change frame,price,channel and product simultaneously, then compare totals.","Ask only repeat buyers which slogan sounds more premium.","Keep only the higher-converting cell and discard cost and refund data.","Randomize comparable cells that vary frame and price separately."],"a":3},{"q":"More branded search appears after a campaign. What does it prove alone?","o":["A causal rise in long-term pricing power independent of paid promotion.","A correlation; ads, seasonality and measurement changes remain alternatives.","A durable moat because branded search excludes all competitor influence.","A reduction in customer acquisition cost even if spend is not measured."],"a":1},{"q":"The positioning discussion is B2B-focused. Consumer application?","o":["Invalid in every consumer category because the source discusses B2B.","Proven for all consumer categories because the components have broad names.","A hypothesis requiring category, channel and buyer-context testing.","Valid only if the consumer product is renamed as a software service."],"a":2},{"q":"What is required before claiming PaperBoat currently earns a frame-driven premium?","o":["Matched current prices and buyer evidence, with rival explanations checked.","A new slogan that mentions nostalgia and traditional ingredients.","A persona describing a buyer who says they value their childhood.","A higher shelf price without matching pack size, channel or product."],"a":0},{"q":"Which persona output is useful for this exercise?","o":["An invented biography presented as a real interviewed customer.","A labeled hypothesis linked to observed jobs, constraints and counterevidence.","A demographic portrait assumed to prove a common buying motivation.","A polished avatar treated as a substitute for purchase evidence."],"a":1},{"q":"A frame improves first purchase but raises returns and costs. Next decision?","o":["Adopt it because first-purchase conversion is the only positioning metric.","Adopt it if the brand-search index also increases during the campaign.","Recompute net economics and repeat behavior before adopting the frame.","Reject all positioning work because a frame cannot influence expectations."],"a":2}]},"140":{"objectives":["Recompute a complete fictional enterprise-to-equity acquisition model and review parenting, decision rights, governance, segmentation and positioning without treating a multiple or control checklist as proof."],"videos":[],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"G20/OECD2023 Principles - board responsibilities","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/g20-oecd-principles-of-corporate-governance-2023_ed750b30-en/full-report/component-8.html","note":"Good-practice oversight, conflicts and evidence; not a guarantee or current Indian legal threshold."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Effective market segmentation - OpenStax5.4","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-marketing/pages/5-4-essential-factors-in-effective-market-segmentation","note":"Apply actionability and response evidence, not labels alone."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"An Introduction to Positioning - April Dunford","url":"https://www.aprildunford.com/post/an-introduction-to-positioning","note":"Five linked choices; a statement is not the process."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GoogleTrends FAQ","url":"https://support.google.com/trends/answer/4365533","note":"Normalized sampled search interest is not absolute demand. The valuation uses stipulated classroom cash flows, not Trends forecasts."}],"exercise":"Private fictional review of Days134-139, not a bid or actual company valuation. All values in crore. Standalone FCFF is5 at endyear1 and6 at endyear2. Year3 FCFF is6.24, growing4% thereafter; WACC12%. Terminal value is at endyear2; no other operating assets/liabilities. Debt10 and excess cash2 at closing, no other equity bridge items. Separately, a non-overlapping control improvement adds1 at endyear1. Buyer-specific synergies add2 at endyear1 and3 at endyear2 only; integration cash cost1.5 at closing. These are already incremental after-tax cash flows; do not tax them again or extend them forever. Use12% for all stated flows in this toy, while explaining why real risks may require different treatment. Compute enterprise value, standalone equity, control PV, net synergy PV and zero-NPV maximum equity price. Current revenue15; ask6x revenue is an enterprise ask90. Convert it to equity82 with the same bridge, then compute buyer NPV. A multiple is a price reference, not proof of intrinsic value. Separate operating calculation: EBITDA20,D&A4,stipulated operating tax25% of positive EBIT,capex6,increase in working capital2. Compute EBIT16,tax4 and FCFF8 using EBIT*(1-t)+D&A-capex-dWC. This is not a statement of current tax law or loss-offset treatment. Then stress overlap, delay, demand and integration costs, keeping enterprise/equity and timing consistent. Review alternate-parent opportunity cost, RAPID Agree versus Decide, evidence versus allegations, actionable segmentation, relative search interest, and frame-versus-price confounding. Draft an evidence/owner/approval/stop plan; no one board control guarantees honest estimates and no real commitment, contact or payment is authorized.<section aria-label=\"Native D140 practice\"><h3>Integrated acquisition review</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d140-acquisition-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable acquisition review lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d140-acquisition-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional crore model: FCFF 5 at end year 1, 6 at end year 2, 6.24 at end year 3 growing 4% thereafter; WACC 12%. Terminal 78 at end year 2; EV 71.428571; equity 63.428571 after debt 10 and excess cash 2. Terminal discounted two years, not three. No other claims/assets supplied; negative equity retained.</p><p>Disjoint control 1 at year 1 gives PV 0.892857. Already after-tax synergies 2/3 at years 1/2 give gross PV 4.177296, less integration 1.5 now gives net 2.677296. Equity ceiling 66.998724. Revenue 15 times 6 gives ENTERPRISE ask 90, equity ask 82; buyer NPV -15.001276. Multiple is price reference, not intrinsic proof; ceiling not a bid or approval. No extra tax deduction or invented terminal synergy.</p><p>Separate operating model: EBITDA 20, D&A 4, positive-EBIT tax 25%, capex 6, increase in working capital 2. EBIT 16, tax 4, FCFF 8. Do not replace the standalone forecast with this separate example. D&A addback reverses expense, not cash receipt. Negative EBIT retains loss but no automatic tax refund under toy rule; actual loss utilization/current law unverified.</p><p>Independent controls: delay synergy receipts only, keep integration at closing; remove duplicated year 1 synergy once while retaining its control item; demand factor scales standalone FCFF including terminal numerator only; integration multiplier changes closing cost. Baseline stresses restore unchanged values. Risk, covariance, demand elasticity, probability and reinvestment consistency are not estimated. Real flows require supported risk matching without duplicate haircuts.</p><p>Integrate best-owner opportunity cost, defined RAPID rights, evidence versus allegations, actionable segmentation, relative search interest and frame-versus-price confounding. Positive parent value or board signoff does not prove value/honesty. Blank five-row evidence/source/model/gap/owner/approval/stop plan records no accepted owner or actual approval. No real participant, quote, private record, bid, transaction, price change, account or spend.</p><p>10 sheets/11 PDF pages; 29 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid controls/464 isolation checks, 6 broken references/6 explicit checks. Clean saved caches/no external links/macros; actual plan blank. All 11 PDF pages inspected after input pagination fix. Not native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner, real diligence, legal/tax opinion or overall 10/10.</p><p>New selected Damodaran chapter 15 opening through first stable-growth example read: FCFF versus EBITDA, discounting, growth/reinvestment and debt/cash bridge. Not rest of chapter/APV/figure pixels. Prior D 135 selected synergy, D 134 selected BCG parenting, D 137 returned OECD board chapter/annotations, D 138 OpenStax 5.4 and D 139 Dunford written article reused without expanded coverage. Six URLs from five organizations, not six independent tests; same author not independent replication. Historical rates/examples not current law/brand/deal facts. Existing Trends/factorial evidence retained, not a new study; full source/AV, native parity, independent learner/account/field/legal and overall 10 remain open.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Year3FCFF6.24,g4%,WACC12%. Terminal value at endyear2?","o":["78, using6.24/(.12-.04).","75, using6/(.12-.04).","52, using6.24/.12.","87.36, using78*1.12."],"a":0},{"q":"Standalone enterprise value from FCFF5,6 and terminal78?","o":["79.464286, using89/1.12.","64.766308, using5/1.12+6/1.12^2+78/1.12^3.","71.428571, using5/1.12+84/1.12^2.","89.000000, using5+6+78."],"a":2},{"q":"EV71.428571,debt10,excess cash2. Standalone equity?","o":["79.428571 after adding debt and subtracting cash.","63.428571 after subtracting debt and adding cash.","59.428571 after subtracting debt and cash.","73.428571 after adding cash but ignoring debt."],"a":1},{"q":"Separate control improvement1 at endyear1. PV?","o":["1.000000, using1 with no discount.","0.797194, using1/1.12^2.","8.333333, using1/.12 as a perpetuity.","0.892857, using1/1.12."],"a":3},{"q":"Synergies2 at year1,3 at year2,cost1.5 at closing. NetPV?","o":["2.677296, using2/1.12+3/1.12^2-1.5.","4.177296, using2/1.12+3/1.12^2.","3.500000, using2+3-1.5.","1.177296, using2/1.12+3/1.12^2-3."],"a":0},{"q":"Zero-NPV maximum equity price including control and synergies?","o":["74.998724, using71.428571+.892857+2.677296.","68.498724, using63.428571+.892857+4.177296.","66.998724, using63.428571+.892857+2.677296.","63.428571, using standalone equity only."],"a":2},{"q":"Revenue15; banker asks6x as enterprise price. Equity ask?","o":["98, from90 enterprise plus10 debt minus2 cash.","90, because a revenue multiple is automatically an equity price.","78, from90 enterprise minus debt and cash.","82, from90 enterprise minus10 debt plus2 cash."],"a":3},{"q":"Equity ceiling66.998724 versus equity ask82. BuyerNPV at ask?","o":["+15.001276, using82-66.998724.","-15.001276, using66.998724-82.","-23.001276, using66.998724-90.","+3.570153, using.892857+2.677296."],"a":1},{"q":"Separate EBITDA20,D&A4,tax25%positiveEBIT,capex6,dWC2. FCFF?","o":["7, from20*.75-6-2.","4, from16*.75-6-2.","8, from16*.75+4-6-2.","12, from16*.75+4-6+2."],"a":2},{"q":"Why is taxing EBITDA directly wrong in that calculation?","o":["The stipulated tax base is EBIT after D&A, not EBITDA.","D&A is always a cash payment that must be taxed a second time.","The stated tax percentage applies only to revenue multiples.","Capex replaces the tax base so no operating tax can be computed."],"a":0},{"q":"Given already after-tax incremental synergies, correct tax treatment?","o":["Tax them again because the standalone model also contains operating tax.","Gross them up by dividing by.75 before adding them to value.","Treat after-tax as before-tax whenever integration costs occur at closing.","Do not deduct another25% unless the assumptions are changed explicitly."],"a":3},{"q":"Real target has negative EBIT. Does this toy establish an immediate cash tax refund?","o":["Yes; multiply every loss by25% and collect that amount immediately.","No; loss utilization and actual cash taxes need a separate supported model.","Yes; all depreciation deductions generate the same cash refund timing.","No; negative EBIT prevents any future tax benefit in every jurisdiction."],"a":1},{"q":"A control cash flow is also included in the synergy model. What must change?","o":["Remove the overlap before adding the two values.","Add both because control and synergy have different labels.","Increase WACC until the double count becomes numerically small.","Move one duplicated flow into terminal value to avoid overlap."],"a":0},{"q":"The synergies arrive one year later, amounts otherwise unchanged. PV effect?","o":["Net synergyPV is unchanged because the nominal amounts are unchanged.","Integration cost must also be discounted one more year despite closing payment.","Their benefitPV falls; the unchanged closing integration cost does not move.","BenefitPV rises because later receipts have more time to compound before valuation."],"a":2},{"q":"What is needed before using a common discount rate for different real cash flows?","o":["A rule that every synergy has the same risk as standalone operations.","A defensible risk treatment without counting the same risk adjustment twice.","A rule that probability haircuts always eliminate all discounting needs.","A rule that changing the discount rate can repair overlapping cash flows."],"a":1},{"q":"A parent increases subsidiary value but another owner can add more net value. Conclusion?","o":["Retain automatically because any positive improvement proves parenting advantage.","Sell automatically because every alternate-owner claim is independently verified.","Compare gross synergies only and ignore recurring parenting costs.","Assess best-owner opportunity cost, not just positive improvement."],"a":3},{"q":"RAPID: how do Agree and Decide differ?","o":["Agree supplies all optional comments; Decide must follow each comment.","Agree always overrides Decide, even outside its assigned constraint scope.","Agree covers assigned constraints; Decide owns the decision within defined rights.","Decide grants access to any budget or account named in the recommendation."],"a":2},{"q":"A proposed board committee signs the synergy spreadsheet. What follows?","o":["A review step exists; evidence, conflicts, monitoring and challenge still matter.","The synergies become certain because a board committee approved the cells.","Independent cash verification is unnecessary once the spreadsheet is signed.","All post-deal accountability transfers to the external auditor."],"a":0},{"q":"An unresolved cash difference appears in a target report. Best treatment?","o":["Add the difference to synergy value because it may be hidden cash.","Assume fraud and assign blame before confirming ownership or timing.","Remove it from diligence because only audited totals can be questioned.","Reconcile and preserve evidence; neither ignore it nor announce fraud as fact."],"a":3},{"q":"An adult snack segment is large but cannot be served economically. Beachhead?","o":["Established because the largest group must be the best first customer.","Not established by size; test reach, value, capacity and contribution.","Established if its search-interest index exceeds every smaller group.","Impossible forever because one early pilot was not profitable."],"a":1},{"q":"A region has a higher Trends index but fewer matching searches. Possible?","o":["No;100 always means the greatest absolute count across all regions.","No; normalization divides by population rather than total searches.","Yes; relative share can be higher while absolute count is lower.","Yes; the index is a direct measure of product sales per resident."],"a":2},{"q":"A positioning package changes both frame and price. What can comparison alone show?","o":["A pure frame effect because price is part of the category label.","A package outcome, not an isolated causal frame effect.","A durable brand moat if total contribution rises in one cohort.","A willingness-to-pay distribution for the whole national market."],"a":1},{"q":"Pilot conversion falls while contribution rises. Correct review?","o":["Review both, with comparable scope, costs and uncertainty made explicit.","Reject the package because lower conversion always lowers contribution.","Adopt the package because contribution from one toy cohort proves causality.","Replace contribution with branded search to avoid conflicting measures."],"a":0},{"q":"Which decision memo is ready for a real approval review?","o":["A sector multiple labeled fair value without an enterprise/equity bridge.","A base-case synergy total presented as certain because management supplied it.","A signed checklist used to omit conflict and implementation evidence.","A consistent model with evidence gaps, downside, owner, limits and approvals."],"a":3},{"q":"What status does completing this fictional review establish?","o":["A checked classroom model, not a real valuation or authorized transaction.","A binding maximum bid available for use in any similar real transaction.","A legal tax opinion because the exercise specifies a25% tax assumption.","A successful integration because governance and positioning were discussed."],"a":0}]},"141":{"objectives":["Design and test pricing hypotheses with explicit price units, stated-versus-behavioral evidence and contribution economics; do not confuse three-threshold interviews with four-question PSM."],"videos":[{"id":"A6veeCbKIzw","title":"The art and science of pricing — Madhavan Ramanujam (Simon-Kucher), Lenny's Podcast","by":"Lenny's Podcast","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Three-threshold interview guidance is distinct from four-question PSM. Sponsor statistics, corporate stories and numeric purchase-probability rules are not independently validated."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Van Westendorp PSM - Conjointly","url":"https://conjointly.com/products/van-westendorp/","note":"Four perceptions, not observed demand. Vendor chart conventions are not a universal profit rule."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Gabor-Granger or Van Westendorp? - Conjointly","url":"https://conjointly.com/blog/gabor-granger-or-van-westendorp/","note":"Written comparison and FAQs; stated intention does not become observed purchasing. Do not adopt a blanket claim that all simple WTP answers have zero value."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Value-Based Pricing - HBR","url":"https://hbr.org/2016/08/a-quick-guide-to-value-based-pricing","note":"Excerpt or overview; the complete publication may require separate access."}],"exercise":"Private fictional pricing memo for a CA-firm workflow tool, not a live GST filing service, legal advice or customer study. Prepare, but do not conduct, consent-based research covering buyer roles, recent alternatives, budgets and value; do not invent CAs, quotes or purchase commitments. Ramanujam's acceptable/expensive/prohibitively-expensive conversation is a three-threshold interview approach. Standard Van Westendorp PSM asks four different price-perception thresholds: too cheap, bargain/cheap, expensive but still considered, and too expensive. Do not label three answers a complete PSM. State price unit, period, tax treatment, respondent eligibility and ordering checks. PSM cumulative perceptions are not observed demand or a profit optimum; three or ten convenience responses do not establish population validity. Compare per-seat, per-firm and per-filing metrics against measurable buyer value, predictability, delivery cost and gaming. A firm with40 times as many clients need not receive40 times the value. Tier fences need clear usable entitlements, not deliberately unusable decoys or hidden charges. Fictional monthly packageA:100 comparable offers,20 buyers at1000 each,variable service cost300 per paying buyer,acquisition spend6000. B:100 offers,15 buyers at1400,variable cost400 per buyer,spend6000. Compute revenue and contribution after acquisition; this is a package comparison, not an isolated price elasticity test or real experiment. Separate freemium toy per month:1000 free accounts cost20 each;100 paying accounts contribute300 each after all paid-user variable costs;fixed operating cost5000, no other costs. Determine net contribution and explain why nonzero free-user cost is not automatically unsustainable. Model an explicitly hypothetical competitor free bundle; no claim about current Zoho/Clear prices and no actual price, account, outreach or purchase change.<section aria-label=\"Native D141 practice\"><h3>Pricing, packages and perceptions</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d141-pricing-perceptions.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable pricing/perceptions lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d141-pricing-perceptions.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional rupees per firm per month, exclusive of tax; no tax calculated. Package A: 100 offers, 20 buyers, price 1000, paid variable cost 300, acquisition 6000 gives revenue 20000 and contribution after acquisition 8000 (80 per offer). B: 100 offers, 15 buyers, price 1400, cost 400, acquisition 6000 gives revenue 21000 and net 9000 (90 per offer). Both price and service cost differ: package outcome, not isolated elasticity or actual experiment.</p><p>Separate freemium toy: 1000 free accounts at cost 20 each, 100 paid accounts contributing 300 each after paid variable costs, fixed 5000 gives net 5000. Paid break-even 83.333333 continuous, 84 whole; maximum affordable free accounts 1250 continuous. Zero contribution/cost denominators remain undefined; negative contribution/capacity retained and explained, not negative users. No churn, conversion or lifetime value estimated.</p><p>Six entirely fictional four-threshold rows, not real respondents. Too cheap, bargain, expensive but considered, too expensive are distinct from three acceptable/expensive/prohibitive interview thresholds. Price grid 0 to 3000 in 500 steps: descending shares count thresholds strictly above p; ascending count at or below p. At p 1000, shares are 0, 1/6, 1/6, 0. Equal weights and adjacent equal thresholds allowed; exact-boundary rule explicit. No interpolation, intersection estimate, revenue curve or financial optimum.</p><p>Raw thresholds remain visible. Any nonnumeric, negative or out-of-order row, or invalid grid blocks all perception outputs under this declared teaching policy. It does not block independent economics. Invalid economics do not block valid perceptions. Do not force order, silently sort, impute or drop real replies. Eligibility, comprehension, sampling, alternatives, consent, tax/unit framing and behavioral validation are separate requirements.</p><p>10 sheets/10 PDF pages; 57 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid economics/912 isolation checks, 30 bad perception states/1710 checks, 8 invalid grids/456 checks, equal-threshold boundary/57 checks, 6 broken references/6 checks. Clean saved caches/no external links/macros. Actual five-row research/consent/owner/stop plan blank. All 10 PDF pages inspected; not native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner or actual research.</p><p>Conjointly supports exploratory PSM and adaptive stated-intent Gabor-Granger; selected MIT 2010 opening criticizes PSM predictive claims and price-ladder negotiation; Chapman supports exploratory perceptions but opposes forcing ordered answers. Different chart/cleaning conventions preserved, not averaged away. PSM perceptions and modeled/stated intentions are not actual purchasing. Blanket zero-value simple-WTP claim not adopted. Source examples not independently reproduced.</p><p>Complete returned written Conjointly PSM explanation and comparison/FAQs, Chapman article/R code and OpenStax chapter 3 key terms read. MIT selected sections 1.1-1.3 only; HBR title/byline/introductory paragraph only. Six URLs/five organizations across vendor, academic, practitioner and accounting sources, not six independent studies. No source figure pixels, package execution, full book, podcast AV or actual competitor-price verification. No CA, quote, consent, purchase commitment, real GST filing, price change, account, outreach or spend. Full source/AV, native parity, independent learner/account/field/legal and overall 10 remain open.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Which set completes standard four-question Van Westendorp PSM?","o":["Acceptable; expensive; prohibitively expensive; competitor list price.","Unit cost; target margin; competitor price; maximum discount.","Too cheap; bargain/cheap; expensive but considered; too expensive.","Budget owner; procurement process; renewal date; preferred payment term."],"a":2},{"q":"Ramanujam's acceptable/expensive/prohibitive interview is what?","o":["A three-threshold approach, not a complete four-question PSM.","A four-question PSM because the product pitch counts as a threshold.","An observed elasticity experiment because respondents name prices.","A binding price commitment once three accountants give the same answers."],"a":0},{"q":"What does a PSM curve describe without extra behavioral evidence?","o":["Actual purchase volume at each tested price in the national market.","A profit-maximizing price after all service costs and churn are accounted for.","The causal effect of price holding product and competitor offers constant.","Cumulative price perceptions, not realized demand or profit optimization."],"a":3},{"q":"Which price-metric selection is defensible for the fictional workflow tool?","o":["Use filings automatically because client counts always scale value proportionally.","Test value alignment, predictability and costs across seats, firms and filings.","Use seats automatically because software costs are independent of usage.","Copy the cheapest competitor's metric because that proves buyer acceptance."],"a":1},{"q":"A:20 buyers at1000,cost300 each,acquisition6000. Monthly result?","o":["Revenue20,000; contribution after acquisition14,000.","Revenue14,000; contribution after acquisition8,000.","Revenue20,000; contribution after acquisition8,000.","Revenue20,000; contribution after acquisition7,700."],"a":2},{"q":"B:15 buyers at1400,cost400 each,acquisition6000. Monthly result?","o":["Revenue21,000; contribution after acquisition15,000.","Revenue15,000; contribution after acquisition9,000.","Revenue21,000; contribution after acquisition8,600.","Revenue21,000; contribution after acquisition9,000."],"a":3},{"q":"B has fewer buyers but higher contribution. What follows?","o":["The stipulated package earns more; design and uncertainty limit causal claims.","The higher price caused the difference, because each group had100 offers.","The result proves every firm will prefer the higher-priced package at renewal.","The lower conversion provesB is inferior regardless of costs or retention."],"a":0},{"q":"1000 free accounts cost20 each;100 paid contribute300 each;fixed5000. Net?","o":["25,000 per month, because only fixed costs reduce paid contribution.","5,000 per month, so nonzero free costs can fit these economics.","10,000 per month, because fixed costs are already included in the stated300.","-25,000 per month, because paid contribution must be deducted from free costs."],"a":1},{"q":"A hypothetical free rival bundle appears. Best pricing review?","o":["Match free immediately because every buyer values the bundle identically.","Keep all prices unchanged because stated WTP never changes with alternatives.","Raise all tiers because a free rival necessarily signals lower quality.","Recheck buyer alternatives, paid differentiation and economics before reacting."],"a":3},{"q":"Which tiering or discount conclusion is supportable?","o":["An intentionally useless middle tier always improves long-term trust and margin.","Every discount permanently lowers every customer's renewal reference price.","Test clear usable fences and renewal effects; neither guarantees value.","Charm prices always fail in B2B because business buyers ignore price presentation."],"a":2}]},"142":{"objectives":["Separate observable creative, attribution and causal lift; compute acquisition and incremental contribution without inventing competitor performance or authorizing ad spend."],"videos":[{"id":"9mDMqYM_Img","title":"Inside Google Marketing — Attribution & Incrementality — Google Ads","by":"Google Ads","note":"Distinguish attribution from incrementality. Historical GA4 migration advice is not a current setup instruction. The exercise is offline and requires no production analytics changes."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Conversion Lift by geography - GoogleAds Help","url":"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/14097193?hl=en","note":"Whole written page: eligibility, feasibility and geographic contamination. No account access, analytics setup or experiment was performed."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Meta Ad Library tools - Transparency Center","url":"https://transparency.meta.com/en-us/researchtools/ad-library-tools","note":"Ad Library overview. Scope and disclosure differ by ad type and geography; an ad's presence is not evidence of its profitability."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Growth Hacker is the new VP Marketing - AndrewChen","url":"https://andrewchen.com/how-to-be-a-growth-hacker-an-airbnbcraigslist-case-study/","note":"Dated platform reach/reverse engineering is not current fact, permission or recommended scraping."}],"exercise":"Private fictional channel memo, not live analytics work or budget approval. If studying a named D2C brand later, record dated ad IDs, creative/offer/format and coverage limits, with interpretations separate. This exercise has no live brand ad inventory. Longevity, ad density, ratings and app ranks do not establish spend, CAC, profit or true channel mix. Meta disclosure varies by ad type and geography. A hypothetical dashboard assigns30% revenue to brand-search last click: this is an allocation rule, not proof the channel is overcredited by a known amount or causes30% of sales. Prepare an experiment with comparison units, preperiod, contamination controls, conversion delay, power/feasibility, uncertainty and decision thresholds. Two matched states/four weeks is not automatically sufficient or randomized. Google platform lift is not available to every account; do not contact account teams or change settings here. Fictional acquisition cohort:100,000 impressions,CPM60,CTR2%,purchase conversion5% of clicks,each purchase a distinct new customer. AOV300 and first-order contribution40% before acquisition, no other deductions. Compute spend6000,100 new customers,CAC60 and cohort contribution after acquisition6000; repeat revenue is not automatically profit. Separate normalized geo toy: treatment pre1000 orders/post1250;control pre800/post880. Assume counterfactual proportional trend and no other differences just for calculation: expected treatment1100,incremental150. AtAOV300,incremental spend20,000 and incremental unit contribution120 compute iROAS2.25 and net minus2000. These assumptions are not validated by the arithmetic. Recommend a research threshold, not a real10-lakh allocation, holdout shutdown, API integration or analytics action. Historical Airbnb-Craigslist discussion is not current platform permission or a workflow to reproduce.<section aria-label=\"Native D142 practice\"><h3>Acquisition and geo incrementality</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d142-channel-incrementality.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable channel/incrementality lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d142-channel-incrementality.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional rupees, one acquisition cohort and a separate geo period. 100000 impressions, CPM 60, CTR 0.02, purchase fraction 0.05 per click, every purchase stipulated a distinct new customer: spend 6000, clicks 2000, new customers 100, CAC 60. AOV 300 and contribution fraction 0.4 before acquisition give revenue 30000, contribution 12000 and net 6000. No other deductions or repeat profit assumed.</p><p>Separate proportional geo toy: treatment pre 1000/post 1250, control pre 800/post 880. Expected treatment 1100 from control trend 1.1; lift 150, incremental revenue 45000 at AOV 300, iROAS 2.25 on incremental spend 20000, contribution after spend -2000 at unit contribution 120. Revenue break-even iROAS 2.5, incremental orders 166.666667 continuous or 167 whole. Positive revenue lift can lose money.</p><p>Additive parallel-change assumption instead predicts 1080 and lift 170, net 400. It is a different identifying assumption, not a correction or license to choose the favorable result. Scenario multiplier applies only to proportional counterfactual, not measured spillover correction: 0.9 gives expected 990/lift 260/net 11200; 1.1 gives 1210/40/-15200. Neither two states/four weeks nor these arithmetic assumptions establishes randomization, comparability, power or causal lift.</p><p>Supplied illustrative lift endpoints -20 and 320 map to revenue -6000/96000, iROAS -0.3/4.8 and net -22400/18400. No confidence level, standard error, estimator or probability assigned; these are scenarios, not a computed confidence interval. Negative lift retained; zero denominators undefined. Independent cohort/geo/bounds gates isolate invalid controls without fabricating economic certainty.</p><p>A hypothetical 30% brand-search last-click share is attribution allocation, not 30% causal sales or a known overcredit amount. Ad longevity, density, ratings and ranks do not establish spend, CAC, profit or true channel mix. Google treatment labels depend on intervention: go-dark treatment is unexposed to the tested ads. Plan KPI, preperiod, feasibility, purchase cycle, conversion lag, contamination, other media and prespecified economic/precision/stop rules before any real study.</p><p>10 sheets/11 PDF pages; 38 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 24 changed sets plus restore, 16 invalid economics/608 isolation checks, 4 invalid bounds/152 checks, 6 broken references/6 explicit checks. Clean caches/no external links/macros. Actual five-row inventory/evidence/owner/approval/budget/stop plan blank. All 11 PDF pages inspected after prose-spacing correction; not native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner, actual experiment or overall 10/10.</p><p>Complete returned written Google setup, metrics and Meridian implementation guidance/table read; figure pixels and linked statistical methods not read or executed. Meta only short returned 2023 EU/branded-content overview, not full global disclosure/API rules or actual ad inventory. Complete returned written historical Andrew Chen case, not images/current reach/platform permission; no scraping/integration reproduced. Prior D 141 OpenStax key terms reused without expanded coverage. Six URLs/four organizations, official/practitioner/accounting sources, not six independent effectiveness studies. Google revenue-efficiency wording not a profit guarantee; posthoc positive slicing not adopted as confirmation. No ad account, tracking change, account-team contact, real budget, shutdown, outreach or spend. Full source/AV, native parity, independent learner/account/field/legal and overall 10 remain open.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"A dashboard assigns30% of revenue to brand-search last click. Meaning?","o":["A causal estimate that30% of sales would disappear without those ads.","An attribution allocation, not the counterfactual revenue lost without spend.","Proof that exactly30% of revenue was created by earlier discovery channels.","Proof the channel is unprofitable because it appears late in the journey."],"a":1},{"q":"An ad remains visible for90days. What is established?","o":["Profitability, because any losing campaign would be removed before90days.","Exact spend, because days active are enough to infer impressions and CPM.","Strong retention, because a long campaign requires the same customers to renew.","Observed longevity, not verified profit, budget or incremental performance."],"a":3},{"q":"CPM60 on100,000 impressions,CTR2%,click-to-purchase5%. Spend and buyers?","o":["6,000 spend;2,000 clicks;100 new buyers under the stated assumption.","60,000 spend;2,000 clicks;100 new buyers under the stated assumption.","6,000 spend;2,000 clicks;5,000 new buyers under the stated assumption.","6,000 spend;200 clicks;10 new buyers under the stated assumption."],"a":0},{"q":"Same cohort,AOV300,40% first-order contribution before acquisition. CAC and net?","o":["CAC60; contribution after acquisition24,000.","CAC120; contribution after acquisition6,000.","CAC60; contribution after acquisition6,000.","CAC60; contribution after acquisition12,000."],"a":2},{"q":"Geo toy control orders800 to880,treatment pre1000. Proportional counterfactual?","o":["1,080 orders, using1000+(880-800).","1,250 orders, using observed treatment post.","880 orders, using observed control post.","1,100 orders, using1000*(880/800)."],"a":3},{"q":"Treatment post1250 versus assumed counterfactual1100. Lift and iROAS atAOV300/spend20000?","o":["250 orders; iROAS3.75 using75,000/20,000.","150 orders; iROAS2.25 using45,000/20,000.","150 orders; iROAS0.9 using18,000/20,000.","1,250 orders; iROAS18.75 using375,000/20,000."],"a":1},{"q":"Incremental150 orders,unit contribution120,incremental spend20000. Profit effect?","o":["-2,000, using150*120-20,000.","+25,000, using150*300-20,000.","+18,000, using150*120 with no spend.","+2,000, reversing the cost-minus-benefit sign."],"a":0},{"q":"Does a two-state,four-week comparison establish causal lift by itself?","o":["Yes; geographic separation guarantees no cross-region customer exposure.","Yes; four weeks always spans every category's complete purchase cycle.","No; comparability, power, spillovers and other changes still need checking.","No; geographic experiments can never support causal inference in principle."],"a":2},{"q":"A holdout reports a wide interval including zero. Best conclusion?","o":["The channel has proven zero incremental effect in every future period.","The test is inconclusive at that precision; review power and decision risk.","All measured sales were organic because the estimate is not significant.","The point estimate is certain enough if last-click ROAS is also high."],"a":1},{"q":"What channel decision is allowed by this exercise?","o":["A10-lakh allocation because the exercise asks which channel should receive it.","A brand-search shutdown because a holdout is useful in principle.","A current Craigslist integration because an old growth case describes one.","A conditional research recommendation, not an actual budget or tracking change."],"a":3}]},"143":{"objectives":["Build a measured growth-loop hypothesis and correctly defined cohort cash model, keeping activation, engagement, customer retention and revenue retention distinct."],"videos":[{"id":"TreOkXyYKTo","title":"What's the difference between Loops and Funnels? — 20VC with Harry Stebbings","by":"20VC with Harry Stebbings","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use the written Reforge, Chen/LiJin and ChartMogul sources; no actual named-product retention, CAC or referral advantage is verified."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Growth Loops are the New Funnels - Reforge","url":"https://www.reforge.com/blog/growth-loops","note":"July 31, 2018 growth-loop article. Reinvested output needs measurable power and cost; a circular diagram does not prove self-sustaining growth."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Power User Curve - AndrewChen/LiJin","url":"https://andrewchen.com/power-user-curve/","note":"Whole written article, not image plots or current named-company metrics. Product cadence and chosen action matter; no universal smile or single best metric."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Customer Retention - ChartMogul","url":"https://help.chartmogul.com/article/159-cohort-customer-retention","note":"October 22, 2026 explanation of customer retention. Definitions of active and reactivated customers affect the calculation; no actual competitor cohort is supplied."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NetMRR Retention - ChartMogul","url":"https://help.chartmogul.com/article/160-cohort-net-mrr-retention","note":"October 22, 2026 explanation of revenue retention. Distinguish it from customer retention and check treatment of past-due and reactivated accounts."}],"exercise":"Private fictional subscription analysis, not a claim about KukuFM, Headfone or cult.fit. Public review dates, ad intensity and SaaS benchmarks cannot reconstruct actual paying-customer retention, CAC or a brand's causal referral loop. Specify the event and denominator before comparing engagement. AnL30 activity histogram normally conditions on active users and days used; it is not the retained fraction of all original subscribers. Weekly-use products need not show daily smiles. Map a hypothetical referral loop with100 initial paid subscribers,20% referring,2 invites per referrer and25% invite-to-paid conversion, no duplicate referrals:10 new paid subscribers,0.1 new per initial subscriber for that one cycle. These rates alone do not sustain successive equal-size generations; costs, retention, cycle time and external acquisition remain unknown. Synthetic12-month cohort:100 initial paying users. Month1 is the first paid service month and all100 pay; active fractions of original cohort at starts of months1-12 are1,.80,.70,.65,.60,.55,.50,.45,.40,.35,.30,.25. No reactivation, expansion, refunds or within-month churn; revenue150 and gross contribution70% per active user-month, collected at each month end, no discounting. Compute12month contribution per original customer687.75 and first3months262.50. CAC260 at time0 is just recovered by endmonth3 under these assumptions; other cash/support/fixed costs may change that conclusion. This is a finite12month gross-contribution proxy, not total lifetime profit. SeparateMRR cohort starts100,loses20 to churn/5 to contraction and adds30 expansion,with no new customers/reactivation:NRR105%,GRR75%. Define activation before outcome measurement; an association between early completion and renewal does not prove activation caused retention. Design an authorized-review experiment, not a live product intervention or contact.<section aria-label=\"Native D143 practice\"><h3>Growth loops and finite cohorts</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d143-growth-cohorts.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable growth/cohort lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d143-growth-cohorts.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional rupee subscription cohort: 100 initial paying subscribers; month 1 is first service month, all pay. Original-cohort active fractions 1, .80, .70, .65, .60, .55, .50, .45, .40, .35, .30, .25 at month starts, no reactivation/expansion/refunds/within-month churn. Monthly revenue 150, contribution fraction .70, collected at month ends. CAC 260 per original customer at time 0. Twelve-month contribution 687.75, first three months 262.50, net of CAC 427.75 and 2.50 respectively. Discrete undiscounted payback end month 3, no fractional interpolation. Finite contribution proxy is not total lifetime profit.</p><p>Editable monthly discount defaults 0. Each end-month contribution is discounted by (1+r)^month, time-0 CAC deducted once. At nonzero monthly discount PV differs from contribution, but undiscounted payback does not change. No annual/monthly rate mixing, discounted-payback claim, future extrapolation or assumed absence of excluded support/fixed/tax/working-capital flows.</p><p>Separate referral toy: seed 100, referring .20, invites 2, conversion .25, no duplicates. One-cycle new users 10, coefficient .1. Under identical rates/new generations referring once, five new generations are 10, 1, .1, .01, .001; cumulative including seed 111.111. Fractional counts are expectations, not observed people. No repeat referrals from earlier generations, cost/cycle time/installed-base retention/market saturation or infinite sustainable-growth claim.</p><p>Separate original MRR base 100, churn 20, contraction 5, expansion 30, no reactivation: GRR 75%, NRR 105%. Original-customer reactivation 10 raises NRR 115% while GRR stays 75%. No new-business revenue. Separate exact-day activity counts total 80 users/460 days/mean 5.75 days, high-frequency 16+day share 12.5%. Separate original-customer retention 55/100=55%; populations not matched, so do not turn activity sample into paid retention. Natural cadence and selection matter; no universal smile.</p><p>Eleven sheets/12 PDF pages, 96 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 24 changed sets plus restore, 30 invalid inputs/2880 independent-family isolation checks, 6 broken references/6 explicit checks. Clean saved caches/no externals/macros. No-reactivation cohort gate blocks invalid increasing/blank/out-of-range schedules; real reactivation can raise other cohorts. Zero-base ratios undefined; unrecovered-within-12-month payback undefined here, not never. Actual five-row evidence/owner/permission/stop plan blank. All 12 PDF pages inspected after input pagination fix; not native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner or overall 10.</p><p>Complete returned written Reforge 2018 loops and Chen/Li Jin power-user essay, ChartMogul customer/MRR docs updated October 22, 2026 and 2021 gross/net article read. Reforge monetization returned overview and reasons 1-3 read, not complete 19 minute/four-step program. No image plots/slide pixels/linked benchmarks/AV/current brand metrics/account implementation. ChartMogul imprecise above 100 sentence qualified: unchanged 100/100 is 100%, growth needed for>100%. Source payback preference conditional on value/cost/risk; benchmarks and activation-renewal association not causal proof. Six URLs / three organizations, not six independent studies. No actual named-brand cohorts, CAC, referral advantage, outreach, participants, product intervention, account or spend; full source/AV/native parity/independent learner/account/field/legal/overall 10 remain open.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Which feature distinguishes a growth-loop hypothesis from a one-way funnel?","o":["The diagram uses circular arrows instead of horizontal stage boxes.","Every initial user necessarily produces more than one additional user.","Acquisition has no paid cost because users perform the final step.","A specified output is reinvested into a measurable next-cycle input."],"a":3},{"q":"100 initial users,20% refer,2 invites each,25% convert,no duplicates. New users?","o":["20 new users;0.2 new per initial user in that cycle.","10 new users;0.1 new per initial user in that cycle.","40 new users;0.4 new per initial user in that cycle.","100 new users;1 new per initial user in that cycle."],"a":1},{"q":"What can anL30 histogram of monthly active users establish?","o":["The retained percentage of all original paying subscribers automatically.","Causal reasons why nonusers canceled in the previous month.","Days-of-use distribution among the included active users.","The lifetime profitability of every high-frequency customer segment."],"a":2},{"q":"A weekly workflow has few users active30days a month. Verdict?","o":["Judge against its natural cadence and value, not a universal daily-smile target.","It must fail because every sustainable subscription requires daily usage.","It has perfect retention because weekly use excludes all churn.","Its revenue model must be ads because users return more than once."],"a":0},{"q":"Synthetic cohort:150 monthly revenue,70% contribution,retention sum6.55 over12months. Value?","o":["982.50 contribution per original customer over12months.","1,260 contribution per original customer over12months.","687.75 contribution per original customer over12months.","458.50 contribution per original customer over12months."],"a":2},{"q":"Months1-3 active fractions1,.8,.7;monthly contribution105. Three-month contribution?","o":["315.00 per original customer.","210.00 per original customer.","236.25 per original customer.","262.50 per original customer."],"a":3},{"q":"CAC260 at time0 against that first3month contribution262.5. Payback?","o":["Recovered by endmonth2 because12month contribution exceeds260.","Recovered by endmonth3 in the toy, with2.5 contribution beyond CAC.","Not recovered by month3 because the retention fraction is below1.","Recovered immediately because acquisition is paid before service starts."],"a":1},{"q":"StartingMRR100,churn20,contraction5,expansion30,no new customers. Retention?","o":["NRR105%;GRR75%, on the same original revenue base.","NRR135%;GRR105%, treating expansion as new initial revenue.","NRR75%;GRR105%, swapping net and gross conventions.","NRR105%;GRR95%, subtracting only contraction for gross retention."],"a":0},{"q":"Early content completion correlates with renewal. What is established?","o":["A causal activation effect that makes every completion nudge worthwhile.","A retention failure unrelated to onboarding because timing differs.","Proof that completion is the single most diagnostic metric in all content apps.","An association; user intent and selection may explain part of it."],"a":3},{"q":"Only public review dates and SaaS benchmarks are available for a brand. Proper output?","o":["Actual month1/month3 retention inferred from the number of dated reviews.","Actual CAC inferred from how many ad creatives remain visible.","A measurement gap and synthetic model, not invented brand retention or CAC.","A verified referral advantage inferred from the product's referral button."],"a":2}]},"144":{"objectives":["Draft a fictional fund term sheet with explicit entity, fee, commitment and contract assumptions; keep Indian regulatory applicability and US-LP examples separate."],"videos":[{"id":"qPKY5mEP2_k","title":"How VC works | What's a venture capital fund, and how does it work | VC 101","by":"Carta","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. This is a US-style introductory entity illustration, not Indian regulatory guidance or an absolute liability/ring-fencing guarantee."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"SEBIAIF Regulations - amended15October2026","url":"https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/oct-2026/1792668513871.pdf","note":"Read the angel/co-investment exceptions and distinguish amendment footnotes from current operative text. The exercise is not a case-specific legal opinion."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"SEBIAIF MasterCircular - updated5November2026","url":"https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/nov-2026/1793875212889.pdf","note":"AIF master circular issued June 3, 2026 and updated November 5, 2026. Read the applicable continuing-interest, governance and custodian provisions with their exceptions. Sections 17.2 and 17.6 address different conditions; they do not require every investor to fund all capital on day one."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Limited Partnership Agreements - Carta","url":"https://carta.com/learn/private-funds/structures/limited-partner/lpa/","note":"June 25, 2026 general private-fund explanation, not Indian law. Negotiated terms and exceptions vary; survey figures and simplified liability descriptions are not legal proof."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA model documents index","url":"https://ilpa.org/industry-guidance/templates-standards-model-documents/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Models are negotiation references, not enacted law or signed terms."}],"exercise":"Private400crore fund design on paper, not formation, solicitation, investment advice or a capital commitment. Draw investors -> fund vehicle -> portfolio with sponsor/manager/trustee/custodian roles and money/authority arrows. Indian AIFs may use different permitted forms; a US limited partnership GP/LP illustration is not proof that an Indian trust has the same entities or absolute liability cap. Compare CategoryI venture-capital fit with alternatives using actual strategy and relevant restrictions, not a label or guaranteed tax benefit. Regulatory research is unscored: use SEBI's consolidated regulations amended15October2026 and the master circular issued3June2026/updated5November2026, inspect exact category/subcategory, current provisos, exemptions, transition and implementation. General10(d) ordinary continuing-interest language cannot be applied to allCategoryI/II: angel and co-investment-specific exceptions exist. Distinguish corpus minimum from pro-rata funding timing under master17.6, and the current custodian rule/manner from historical>500crore text in amendment footnotes. Read the applicable provisions and their exceptions together; a selected excerpt is not the full regulatory framework. No complete legal/tax assessment is supplied. Use two separate classroom calculations: voluntary sponsor commitment5% of400=20; a stipulated ordinary rule lower2.5%corpus/5crore gives5 at400, not20 or10. These are not commitments or a case-specific compliance certification. Negotiated toy fees:2% of400 committed annually in years1-4;1.5% of250 remaining invested cost in year5, no offsets/taxes in this toy. Calculate8 and3.75. Separate simplified whole-fund waterfall:400 contributed,total distributable650 after all costs,return400 capital then20%carry on profit250,no hurdle/catchup/recycling/clawback/taxes;carry50,investors600. Do not apply those economics as a universal market standard. Draft8terms comparing manager versus hypothetical investor preferences:fees/offsets,carry-waterfall-clawback,keyperson,investment-period exceptions,extension votes,removal,cause/notice/cure,MFN/eligibility/exclusions,excuse/default consequences. Combine related terms to keep8rows. Define who can consent and what happens on trigger rather than assert automatic rights. No named investor intent, signature, account, fund setup, solicitation, fee payment or actual commitment.<section aria-label=\"Native D144 practice\"><h3>Fund entities, fees and terms</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d144-fund-terms.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable fund terms lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d144-fund-terms.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional 400 crore paper fund design, not formation or solicitation. Actual Indian vehicle and roles must be established separately from US GP/LP illustration; no absolute liability/ring-fencing/tax/return guarantee. Voluntary sponsor 5%=20. Separate STIPULATED ordinary min(2.5%*400, 5)=5, not 20 or 10; arithmetic does not establish actual regulatory applicability. Numeric gates do not certify legal compliance.</p><p>Separate annual fee bases: years 1-4, 2%*400=8 each; year 5, 1.5%*250 remaining invested cost=3.75. Five-year sum 35.75, not full-life cost. No offsets/taxes/proration/later years implemented. Separate whole-fund endpoint 400 contributed/650 AFTER-ALL-COST distributable: capital 400, profit 250, 20%carry 50, capital-provider pool 600, multiple 1.5. Do not deduct 35.75 again. No hurdle/catch-up/recycling/clawback/datedIRR/provider-specific allocation.</p><p>Separate staged calls 25%, 30%, 20%, 25% of corpus 400: 100, 120, 80, 100, including voluntary sponsor 20 allocated 5, 6, 4, 5; other 380 providers 95, 114, 76, 95. Total fractions below 1 leaves uncalled balance, not default. Above 1/negative/text/blank invalidates this toy. Same-fraction allocation is a classroom convention, not actual notices or proof of continuing-interest timing compliance.</p><p>Eight authored negotiated term comparisons cover fees/offsets, carry/clawback, keyperson, investment-period exceptions, extensions, removal/cause/cure, MFN/conflicts, excuse/default. Identify trigger, scope, electorate, notice/vote/remedy rather than assert automatic rights. No named investor intent, accepted owner, signed terms, legal advice, bankaccount, actual fund formation/call/fee/commitment. Actual five-row evidence/permission/owner/stopplan blank.</p><p>Twelve sheets/12 PDF pages; 43 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 24 changedsets+restore, 24 invalidinputs/1032 independent-family isolationchecks, 6 brokenrefs/6 explicitchecks. Clean saved caches/no externals/macros. Corrected uncalled-sponsor cell reference caught by baseline test before first pass; improved input precision so.025/.015 not rounded away; clipped eight-term page split into two four-term pages and re-inspected. Loss-case carry 0/shortfall visible; zero-capital multiple undefined. All 12 PDF pages inspected, not native Excel-Sheets parity or independent learner.</p><p>Six URLs / four organizations, not six independent studies. Complete returned Carta June 25, 2026 exposition and ILPA index read; selected July 2020 ILPA model 6.1-6.2, 8.3-8.4.1, 11.1-11.5, selected Taxmann introduction/1-5.1, not full model/article/linked NISM book. Downloaded SEBI PDFs: selected 10(d), angel 19 B/19 G, co-investment applicability, migrated 19 W, current 20(11); master issued June 3 / updated November 5, 2026 selected 17.2.1-.2/17.6.1-.3. General 10(d)has exceptions; operative custodian rule differs from historical>500 crore footnote; 17.6.1 pro-rata does not mean full corpus dayone. No complete law/tax applicability or linked-condition assessment.</p><p>Source disagreements retained: vendor usual quarterly advance vs commentary arrears/GNAV context is not universal timing; toy annual whole periods. ILPA actual offset carryforward/conditional calls/keyperson details not implemented as this toy. Vendor liability/carry summaries and MFN threshold direction require actual governing clause; no automatic 66.6/75 vote or economic norm. Commentary only-liquidation performance-fee wording cannot override negotiated deal-by-deal mechanics. No source diagram pixels/AV/account implementation/current investor outcomes; full source/AV/native parity/independent learner/account/field/legal/tax/overall 10 remain open.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"What must be resolved before copying a USGP/LP diagram into an IndianAIF design?","o":["The actual vehicle, roles, governing law and contractual liability terms.","Only the brand names, because every investment vehicle is a limited partnership.","Only the fee percentage, because legal liability follows the same diagram worldwide.","Only investor count, because trusts necessarily have the same GP entity as USLPs."],"a":0},{"q":"Voluntary sponsor commitment5% of fictional400crore corpus equals?","o":["5crore; voluntary5% is capped automatically by every legal minimum.","10crore;5% means the same thing as the stipulated2.5% rule.","20crore; it is not established as the regulatory minimum by that choice.","40crore; commitment percentages apply twice to sponsor and manager."],"a":2},{"q":"In the stipulated ordinary rule,lower of2.5% of400 and5crore is?","o":["10crore, using2.5% of400 with no cap.","5crore, usingmin(10,5) under the stipulated rule.","20crore, using the separate voluntary5%.","15crore, adding10 and5 instead of taking the lower."],"a":1},{"q":"Why not present the general continuing-interest formula as universal for allCategoryI/II?","o":["AllCategoryI/II funds instead follow the sameUSlimited-partnership law.","The sponsor's preferred percentage overrides any applicable regulation automatically.","A voluntarily larger commitment removes the need to identify the fund category.","Special subcategory/scheme provisions and exemptions can change applicability."],"a":3},{"q":"Toy annual fee2% of400 committed in years1-4;year1fee?","o":["8crore, using400*2%.","4crore, using400*1%.","20crore, using400*5%.","3.75crore, using250*1.5%."],"a":0},{"q":"Year5fee1.5% of250 remaining invested cost;fee?","o":["6crore, using400*1.5%.","5crore, using250*2%.","3.75crore, using250*1.5%.","8crore, using400*2%."],"a":2},{"q":"Toy400contributed/650distributable,capital first,20%carry,no other waterfall layers. Result?","o":["Carry130;investors520, totaling650.","Carry50;investors650, totaling700.","Carry250;investors400, totaling650.","Carry50;investors600, totaling650."],"a":3},{"q":"What does a key-person clause do in a defensible term sheet?","o":["Guarantee replacement partners will preserve the same investment returns.","Define named triggers, suspension scope, exceptions and resolution rights.","Automatically liquidate every asset whenever any employee leaves.","Transfer all fund management to theLPAC without a contractual decision process."],"a":1},{"q":"How should anMFN right be described?","o":["An unconditional right to every better term given to any other investor.","An automatic right to withdraw committed capital on any preferred timetable.","An election subject to its negotiated eligible terms, exclusions and procedures.","A universal right for the smallest investor to set every other investor's fees."],"a":2},{"q":"Which conclusion follows from a higherGP commitment or a longer fund term alone?","o":["Neither guarantees returns, lower risk or superior legal structure.","A5% commitment proves the manager will outperform a1% manager.","An8+2term guarantees the winners mature before forced exits occur.","A foreign orIFSC structure is superior for every institutional investor."],"a":0}]},"145":{"objectives":["Reconcile committed, contributed and invested capital; compute a cash-capped waterfall with a stated fee base, preference and catch-up, and separate early carry from ultimate entitlement."],"videos":[{"id":"Kz_opneLNSk","title":"Carry Calculation & Waterfall with Preferred Return, GP LP Split, & GP Catch Up","by":"Nidhish Singh FCCA, CISI, M.IoD, Dip-IFRS","note":"The transcript has unclear numerical passages and mixes GP-investor return with carry. Use the explicit toy cash flows rather than copying its numbers or assuming a universal 8% preference or GP commitment."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Carried Interest in Venture Capital Funds - Cooley","url":"https://thefundlawyer.cooley.com/primer-carried-interest-in-venture-capital-funds/","note":"Contract bases, timing, preferred return, full/tapered catch-up and clawback limits vary. US tax claims and survey frequencies not independently validated or used as scored current-law facts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Distribution Waterfalls - Carta","url":"https://carta.com/learn/private-funds/management/distribution-waterfall/","note":"Fund waterfalls and company-equity liquidation examples are different; preferred returns are not guaranteed."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA Performance Definitions - April2025","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Investor contributions and net residual value must use a consistent scope."}],"exercise":"Private fictional waterfall workbook, not a real fund return forecast, LPA interpretation or distribution instruction. All amounts in millions. Fund commitments60; LPs contribute50 at time0,40 invested and10 spent on stipulated fees/expenses,leaving10 undrawn. No GP investment,subscription debt,recycling,taxes,interim cash flow or residual NAV. At exactly year5,the portfolio realizes100 cash,with no further fees/expenses;all100 is distributable. Toy contract returns all50 contributed first,then an8% annual compound preference on50 over5years,then100% GP catch-up until GP carry equals20% of profit distributed through preference/catch-up,then80% LP/20% GP of remaining cash. No carry on returned capital. Preference50*((1.08)^5-1)=23.46640384; full catch-up is preference*.2/.8=5.86660096; residual20.6669952; LP90,GPcarry10,netDPI1.8 on actual50 paid in. Gross portfolio multiple100/40=2.5 is a different basis and does not imply a universal gross-to-net haircut. Recompute with only75 distributable: after capital and preference only1.53359616 remains for catch-up,so GP cannot receive its full target or a residual split;LP73.46640384 andGP1.53359616. Preference is a priority,not guaranteed money. Separate deal-timing toy,not the preceding preferred-return contract:40 paid at time0 into two20-cost deals,no fees/preference;A exits for60 at year2,B returns0 at year5. Deal-by-deal term pays20% ofA's40 profit as8 early carry. Whole-fund term returns all40 first,then pays20% of20 overall profit as4 carry. Stipulate the deal-by-deal contract requires final gross true-up to that same4 ultimate entitlement:4 excess is due back before taxes/caps. Do not assume it is collectible; inspect guarantors,tax limits,escrow,solvency and timing. Recycling is allowed only under actual contract limits; do not assume110-125% invested or infer IRR without dated investor flows. No cash transfer or real fund commitment.<section aria-label=\"Native D145 practice\"><h3>Cash-capped preferred return and catch-up</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d145-waterfall-catchup.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable waterfall lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d145-waterfall-catchup.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional fund cash waterfall, not an LPA interpretation or distribution instruction. Commitments 60, LP paid-in 50 at time 0, invested 40/fees 10 inside commitments, undrawn 10. No GP investment, debt, recycling, tax, interim cash flows, NAV or further costs. Fees already funded, not deducted again from 100 available at exactly year 5.</p><p>Stipulated 8% annual compound preference on 50 for 5 whole years=>23.46640384. Return capital 50, then capped preference, then 100% GP catch-up target preference*.2/.8=5.86660096, then 80/20 residual 20.6669952=>LP 90/GP 10. At 75 available: LP 73.46640384/GP 1.53359616, residual 0, not negative or full catch-up. At 30: LP 30, capital shortfall 20, no pref/carry. Seven-row editable cash grid varies cash only.</p><p>Net DPI 90/50=1.8; NAV 0=>TVPI 1.8; portfolio 100/40=2.5 uses different denominator, not certified ILPA fund-level gross MOIC. Exactly two synthetic flows time 0/year 5 allow annual endpoint rate 1.8^(1/5)-1=12.4746%; not IRR from an undated multiple or full cash-flow model. Zero distributions/zero paid-in leave this IRR undefined, not a valid -100% root.</p><p>Separate no-fee/no-pref two-deal timing toy: 40 paid time 0; A 20 cost=>60 year 2, B 20=>0 year 5; early carry 8, ultimate whole-fund entitlement 4, stipulated gross true-up 4 due. Default assumed recovery 0 means unrecovered 4, carry retained 8/providers 52 versus ultimate 56. Recovery 100%=>retained 4/providers 56. Later winners can require additional carry, not negative clawback. No actual recovery/escrow/solvency/tax cap/enforcement guessed.</p><p>Ten sheets/10 PDF pages, 79 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 24 changed sets/1896 checks+restore, 5 boundary sets/395 checks, 24 invalid inputs/1896 family-isolation checks, 6 broken references/6 checks. Clean restored caches, no externals/macros; actual five-row source/authority/accepted owner/stop plan blank. Zero-cash IRR undefined guard added before final PASS. All 10 PDF pages and responsive/master changes inspected, not native Excel-Sheets parity or independent learner.</p><p>Six URLs / three organizations: selected Cooley June 10, 2026 terminology/carry/pref/base/timing/clawback/conclusion, not tax 1061/waiver/securities sections or verified survey; complete returned Carta May 28, 2025 written article, not linked guide/images; selected ILPA 2025 definitions calls/commitment, netIRR/netTVPI/grossIRR/grossfundMOIC, not full PDF. Downloaded ILPAJuly 2020 model selected pref definition/14.3/14.7 plus complete returned overview; selected Principles 3.0 waterfall/carry/recycling/clawback, not full PDFs/legal agreement review.</p><p>Terms differ: toy 100% GP full catch-up versus ILPA model bracketed 80% GP/20% LP tapered catch-up versus Principles 3.0 hard-hurdle preference. Principles gross-of-tax recommendation and model tax-limited clawback not silently merged. Model optional 30%escrow/guarantor provisions not actual security. Preference priority not guaranteed cash; accounting allocation not cash entitlement; European label not elimination of later-call/clawback risk. No universal 8/20 term, US/Indian legal/tax advice, current actual fund data, source AV/image pixels, account/learner/spend/transfer; full source/AV/native parity/independent learner/account/field/legal/tax/overall 10 remain open.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Commitments60,paid in50,invested40,fees/expenses10,no other use. Undrawn capital?","o":["20 undrawn;only deployed investments count as contributed.","10 undrawn;50 contributed differs from40 invested.","0 undrawn;the stated commitment is fully drawn at inception.","50 undrawn;commitment equals cash already paid byLPs."],"a":1},{"q":"Toy8% compound preference on50 over5years,capital returned separately. Preference?","o":["20.00000000,using50*.08*5 as simple interest.","73.46640384,including returned principal in preference.","4.00000000,using only one year of the stipulated term.","23.46640384,using50*((1.08)^5-1)."],"a":3},{"q":"For20%carry and100%catch-up after that preference,full catch-up target?","o":["5.86660096,using23.46640384*.2/.8.","4.693280768,using23.46640384*.2.","23.46640384,matching the entireLP preference.","10.00000000,applying carry to original capital."],"a":0},{"q":"With100 available,50 capital and23.46640384 preference returned,then full catch-up5.86660096. Residual?","o":["26.53359616,omitting the catch-up from cash used.","50.0000000,omitting both preference and catch-up.","20.6669952,split80/20 only after those layers.","15.973714432,deducting preference carry a second time."],"a":2},{"q":"Base case100 distributable,all layers satisfied. FinalLP andGPcarry?","o":["LP80;GP20,total100.","LP90;GP10,total100.","LP100;GP10,total110.","LP86;GP14,total100."],"a":1},{"q":"Only75 distributable under the same contract. Catch-up and residual?","o":["Catch-up1.53359616;residual0.","Catch-up5.86660096;residual-4.3330048.","Catch-up0;residual1.53359616 split80/20.","Catch-up5;residual-3.46640384."],"a":0},{"q":"Base case LP90,paid-in50,portfolio proceeds100 on invested40,no residualNAV. Ratios?","o":["NetDPI2.25;gross portfolio multiple2.5,same invested denominator.","NetDPI1.5;gross portfolio multiple2.5,using commitments forDPI.","NetDPI2.0;gross portfolio multiple1.8,swapping gross and net.","NetDPI1.8;gross portfolio multiple2.5,different bases."],"a":3},{"q":"SeparateA20->60,B20->0 toy,20%carry,no fees/preference. Early deal and ultimate whole-fund carry?","o":["12 early onA;4 under the stipulated whole-fund basis.","8 early onA;8 under the stipulated whole-fund basis.","8 early onA;4 under the stipulated whole-fund basis.","4 early onA;8 under the stipulated whole-fund basis."],"a":2},{"q":"The deal-by-deal toy requires4 gross clawback. What is established?","o":["A contractual amount due,not guaranteed recovery of4 in cash.","Guaranteed recovery because a clause is equivalent to funded escrow.","No liability because any early carry is permanently earned.","Automatic after-tax recovery of4 regardless of limits or solvency."],"a":0},{"q":"Can the original50commitment/40investment/110distribution figures alone determine netIRR?","o":["Yes;110/40 is an annualized investorIRR without dates.","No;investor cash amounts,dates,fees and residual basis are missing.","Yes;110/50 isIRR because committed capital includes timing.","No;private funds can never computeIRR even with dated cash flows."],"a":1}]},"146":{"objectives":["Assess a manager with scoped,permission-safe evidence requests,complete attribution and net performance reconciliation;keep signals,claims and verified investment conclusions separate."],"videos":[{"id":"CDqCOB4Ie4c","title":"What LPs Want from VC Fund Managers","by":"K Street Capital","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Named fundraising, portfolio, market-size, return-outperformance and secondary-volume claims are speakers' claims, not independently verified facts or universal LP preferences. No actual investment or contact action."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA Due Diligence Questionnaire2.0","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/ILPA-DDQ-2.0.pdf","note":"It is a private-equity diligence reference, not compulsory law or a fully tailored emerging-VC questionnaire."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA DDQ2.0 User Guide","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/ILPA-DDQ-2.0-User-Guide.pdf","note":"N/A, confidentiality/redaction and staged provision may be appropriate; yes/no alone is not a diligence verdict."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Emerging VC Managers - TheVCFactory","url":"https://thevcfactory.com/emerging-vc-fund-managers/","note":"Named performance statistics, causal superiority and stage-specific return norms not independently validated."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"LP Framework - TheSideLetter","url":"https://thesideletter.co/lp-framework/","note":"Enterprise-to-equity, debt/cash, preferences, dilution and ownership must be bridged before estimating investor proceeds."}],"exercise":"Private fictional investment-committee evidence-gap memo,not diligence on a real manager or an investment recommendation. Do not fabricate confidential track records,founder quotes,references or actualLP intentions. Public deal announcements show reported participation,not the manager's actual cost,ownership,role,decision authority,cash realization or personal attribution. Build an evidence ledger with claim,source/date,permission,verification status,contradiction and next check. Ask on paper for full predecessor-deal population including losses,manager-role attribution with employer permissions and supporting records,dated investor cash flows,fee/carry bridge,valuation policy and changes,credit-facility impact,key-person allocation and conflicts. Do not contact anyone,seek unauthorized former-employer files or imply permission from availability. Reference plan should sample supportive,neutral and difficult outcomes across founders,co-investors,former colleagues andLPs where authorized;no single universal best reference or secret backchannel entitlement. Emerging managers may lack predecessor funds;recordN/A and test relevant experience,capacity and controls rather than invent a track record. Hypothetical aggregate evidence:paid-in100,distributions30,net residual value120 givesDPI.3,RVPI1.2,TVPI1.5. If residual is marked down25% to90,TVPI1.2 butDPI stays.3;no timedIRR is supplied. Paper marks are estimates,not evidence of cash return. A strategy expansion or faster fundraise is a risk question,not proof of bad faith;test changed competence,team time,portfolio construction and disclosed mandate. Likewise strong communication can aid trust without proving or causing a re-up. Decide which checks block a hypothetical decision,which may be bounded conditions and who owns each next check. No live investment verdict,reference outreach,disclosure of private data or commitment.<section aria-label=\"Native D146 practice\"><h3>Manager evidence and investor reconciliation</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d146-manager-evidence.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable manager evidence lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d146-manager-evidence.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional manager-evidence lab, not actual fund diligence or investment advice. Four invented portfolio deals: costs 20/30/25/15, proceeds 60/0/0/0 and residual marks 0/0/45/15. Complete cost 90, proceeds 60, residual 60, total value 120 and gross portfolio multiple 1.333333. Excluding the 30-cost total loss gives selected cost 60 and selected multiple 2.0, not the complete record. Editable selection flags demonstrate cherry-picking, not verified evidence or personal attribution.</p><p>Investor paid 100 includes portfolio cost 90 and fees 10. Stipulated paid carry 10 reduces proceeds 60 to investor distributions 50; accrued carry 5 reduces residual 60 to net NAV 55. Net DPI .5, RVPI .55, TVPI 1.05. Markdown 25% applies only to portfolio residual, giving 45; fixed accrued carry 5 leaves net NAV 40 and TVPI .9. DPI .5 stays unchanged. Carry is an assumed amount, not recomputed LPA entitlement. Net residual toy floors at zero; no carry reversal or liability estimated.</p><p>This new portfolio-before-carry dataset is separate from the original exercise aggregate: paid 100/distributions 30/net NAV 120 gives DPI .3/RVPI 1.2/TVPI 1.5; a 25% net NAV markdown gives TVPI 1.2 with DPI unchanged. No dated cash-flow IRR, facility adjustment, currency conversion or certification inferred from either multiple.</p><p>Separate ordinary-equity toy: EV 100 minus debt 20 plus cash 5 gives equity 85; 10% ownership gives 8.5. A 20% relative ownership dilution leaves 8% and proceeds 6.8, not EV times ownership. No security preferences, debt-like adjustments, tax, costs, actual cap table, exit forecast or fund-return prediction. Negative equity has a limited-liability zero floor only in this toy.</p><p>Claims and reference sheets separate reported participation from actual cost/ownership/lead/sourcing/diligence/decision role, prior-employer permission, full population, cash and marks. Paper reference plan includes varied roles/outcomes, direct observation, nomination/conflicts, minimum disclosure and conflicting accounts. No names, contacts, secret backchannels, outreach, private records or owner acceptance invented. Arithmetic is not a diligence verdict; N/A, staged/redacted access and yes/no require context.</p><p>Ten sheets/ten PDF pages; 31 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 21 changed sets/651 checks plus restore, five boundary sets/155 checks, 20 invalid inputs/620 family-isolation checks and six broken references/six checks. Clean saved caches, no external links/macros; five-row actual evidence/permission/accepted-owner plan blank. All ten PDF pages and changed responsive/master pages inspected. LibreOffice authored tests, not native Excel/Sheets parity or independent learner evidence.</p><p>Six URLs/three organizations: downloaded ILPA November 2021 DDQ selected 14.3-14.7, 15.1-15.7 and Appendices C 1-C 8/E 1-E 2/F 1-F 3, plus overview, not full questionnaire/appendices; complete returned November 9, 2021 User Guide and current DDQ listing; selected ILPA 2025 investor cash-flow/net IRR/net TVPI definitions, not full PDF. VCFactory July 22, 2024 selected evaluation/track-record sections, not independently validated performance, charts or linked studies/podcasts; complete returned Side Letter written framework, not universal LP rule.</p><p>ILPA questionnaire is a PE reference, not compulsory law or fully tailored emerging-VC diligence. Source return alone does not verify a manager. No universal return targets, causal outperformance, current legal clearance, actual records, source image/AV, account, learner, outreach, spend, investment or commitment. Full source/AV/native cross-viewer/independent learner/account/field/legal/tax and actual overall 10 remain open.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"A public announcement lists a manager in a successful round. What is verified?","o":["The manager sourced,led and owned the investment decision personally.","The fund has distributed the headline valuation gain as cash toLPs.","Reported participation,not personal attribution or realized net returns.","The manager held its original ownership through exit without dilution."],"a":2},{"q":"A deal list shows only winners. Best next evidence request?","o":["The full relevant deal population,losses,exclusions and attribution basis.","Only the largest winner,to maximize the signal-to-noise ratio.","A newer press release,to confirm the strongest company is still growing.","A single blended gross multiple,to replace the missing deal population."],"a":0},{"q":"Paid-in100,distributions30,netNAV120. DPI,RVPI,TVPI?","o":["0.3;1.5;1.2,interchanging residual and total value.","0.3;1.2;1.5,using the same investor basis.","0.25;1.0;1.25,using residual value as denominator.","0.2;0.8;1.0,using total value as denominator."],"a":1},{"q":"Residual120 is marked down25% to90,other inputs unchanged. DPI andTVPI?","o":["DPI0.225;TVPI1.125.","DPI0.3;TVPI1.125.","DPI0.225;TVPI1.2.","DPI0.3;TVPI1.2."],"a":3},{"q":"A manager citesTVPI1.5 as proof1.5 cash returned. Correct response?","o":["Accept because everyTVPI numerator contains only bank-settled cash.","Reject everyTVPI because unrealized values can never be useful.","Separate net residual marks from distributions and verify both.","ConvertTVPI to annualIRR by multiplying1.5 by the fund age."],"a":2},{"q":"Which reference plan is defensible before authorized outreach?","o":["Only theGP-selected most enthusiastic founder,as a universal best predictor.","A permission-safe cross-section covering supportive and difficult outcomes.","Any former employee approached secretly with requests for confidential files.","Only currentLPs,because founders can never describe investor behavior."],"a":1},{"q":"An emerging manager has no predecessor fund. Best treatment?","o":["RecordN/A and test attributable experience,capacity and controls.","Reconstruct a predecessor record by assigning all prior employer wins to theGP.","Reject automatically because no first-time manager can have relevant experience.","Treat an incomplete fund table as proof of deliberately hidden losses."],"a":0},{"q":"A fund expands into a new sector while raising faster. What follows?","o":["Fraud is established because every expansion contradicts every original mandate.","Future outperformance is established because faster fundraising validates strategy.","No diligence is needed if the manager explains the change confidently.","Investigate mandate,expertise,time and construction;bad faith is not established."],"a":3},{"q":"Exit enterprise value100,debt20,cash5,ordinary equity ownership10%,no other adjustments. Illustrative proceeds?","o":["10,from enterprise value100 times10%,ignoring debt and cash.","8.5,from equity85 times10%,before any unmodeled preferences/taxes.","12.5,from adding debt20 and cash5 to enterprise value100.","7.5,from subtracting both debt20 and cash5 from enterprise value100."],"a":1},{"q":"A manager sends frequent honest updates. What can diligence conclude alone?","o":["A causal increase in retention because updates precede commitments.","A complete replacement for valuation,track-record and legal diligence.","Communication behavior,not proven return quality or a guaranteedLP re-up.","A guaranteed re-up because transparency removes liquidity constraints."],"a":2}]},"147":{"objectives":["Review a fund case using complete cash reconciliation,contract-sensitive hurdle bases,partial catch-up and net investor denominators rather than memorized market rules."],"videos":[],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Carried Interest Primer - Cooley","url":"https://thefundlawyer.cooley.com/primer-carried-interest-in-venture-capital-funds/","note":"Contracts A and B are explicit classroom assumptions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA Performance Definitions","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Selected fund net metrics and investor-cash-flow definitions, not all pages. The actual contributed denominator does not change when a carry contract excludes fees."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA Reporting Guidance2025","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/ILPA-Reporting-Template-v.-2.0-Suggested-Guidance.pdf","note":"It reports negotiated treatment rather than mandating which expenses management fees cover."}],"exercise":"Private fictional cumulative review of pricing,growth and fund economics. No current-law quiz,real investor judgment or actual fund instructions. All fund amounts in millions:commitments60;paid-in54 at time0 consisting45 investments plus9 fully spent fees/expenses;6 remains undrawn. No GPinvestment,debt,recycling,tax,interim distribution or residualNAV. At exactly year4,70 distributable. ContractA:all54 capital returned first,6% annually compounded preference on54,100% GPcatch-up toward20% of distributed profit,then80/20 residual split. Preference14.17375584;catch-up target3.54343896 but only1.82624416 cash remains,so partial catch-up,LP68.17375584/GP1.82624416,total70;netmultiple68.17375584/54=1.26247696. ContractB counterfactual deliberately excludes9 fees from both capital-return and preference/carry bases:45 returned,preference11.8114632,fullcatchup2.9528658,residual10.235671 split80/20;GP5,LP65,total70. Actual investor paid-in remains54,so netmultiple65/54=1.203703704,not65/45. This is a stipulated alternative,not a claim all contracts exclude fees. UnderA with90 proceeds instead,all layers fit:LP82.8/GP7.2,netmultiple1.533333333. Show every cash layer before rounding and cap every layer by available cash;never add carry outside the proceeds. Contrast metric definitions and evidence levels fromD141-146:PSM four perceptions is not observed demand;attribution is not causal lift;finite cohort contribution is not lifetime profit;netTVPI is not cashDPI;legal applicability remains separate unscored research. Identify one unresolved input that could change each recommendation. No blanket recycling range,manager bad-faith diagnosis or communication-causes-re-up claim.<section aria-label=\"Native D147 practice\"><h3>Contract bases and investor reconciliation</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d147-contract-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable contract review lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d147-contract-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional contract-review workbook. Commitments 60; actual investor contributions 54 at time 0 include investments 45 and fully spent fees/expenses 9; undrawn 6. At exactly year 4, cash 70 after all costs, no interim flows, NAV, GP investment, debt, recycling or tax. Fees are not deducted a second time. Original exercise and all quiz questions/keys retained.</p><p>Contract A returns 54, then 6% annual compound preference on 54 for four years: 14.17375584. Full 100% GP catch-up target 3.54343896 exceeds cash left 1.82624416, so catch-up is partial. LP 68.17375584, GP 1.82624416, net investor multiple 1.26247696. At cash 90, LP 82.8 and GP 7.2. Every layer is capped by cash.</p><p>Contract B deliberately excludes the 9 fees from return, preference and carry bases. Return 45, preference 11.8114632, catch-up 2.9528658, residual 10.235671: LP 65, GP 5. Actual investor paid-in remains 54, so net multiple 65/54=1.203703704, not 65/45=1.444444444. It is a stipulated counterfactual, not universal expense coverage or a claim that ILPA mandates this clause.</p><p>Editable cash grid compares both contracts. At cash 30 both distribute LP 30/GP 0; actual investor shortfall is 24 even though B contract-return-base shortfall is only 15. Contract bases do not erase losses. Zero denominators remain undefined; no dated IRR inferred from the multiples.</p><p>Separate simplified investor net NAV roll: beginning 40 + contributions 10 - distributions 8 - gross management fee 2 + applied offset .5 - external expenses 1 - internal chargebacks .5 + net gains 12 - incremental accrued carry 2 = ending net NAV 49. Earlier carry is already reflected in beginning net NAV; do not deduct its full historic balance again. Carry is a supplied expense, not calculated entitlement. Not a completed or modified official ILPA template, financial audit or full accounting implementation.</p><p>Separate geo review in rupees: supplied incremental orders 150 times unit contribution 120 less spend 20000 = -2000, not revenue profit. Break-even 166.666667 orders. Supplied lift is not causally estimated here. PSM perceptions are not observed demand; attribution is not causal lift; finite cohort contribution is not lifetime profit; net TVPI is not cash DPI; reported participation is not verified individual deal attribution. Actual evidence, permission and accepted-owner plan remains blank.</p><p>Eleven sheets/twelve PDF pages; 99 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 26 changed sets/2574 checks plus 99 restored, seven boundary sets/693 checks, 26 invalid inputs/2574 family-isolation checks and six broken references/six checks. Clean saved caches, no external links/macros. Inputs print view split for readability and clipped Review title shortened before final inspection. All PDF pages and changed responsive/master pages inspected. Authored LibreOffice tests, not native Excel/Sheets parity or independent learner evidence.</p><p>Seven URLs/three organizations: new ILPA January 2025 reporting guidance selected overview/objectives/conformity/estimates/optionality/footnotes/fee-allocation/FOF plus downloaded XII verification-limits paragraph, not full PDF; returned FOF ending truncated. Selected reporting definitions for investor NAV/contributions/distributions/management fee/internal-external expenses/carry, not full PDF; complete returned template listing. Guidance reports negotiated fee treatment, not which costs management fees must cover, and does not verify GP calculations. No exhaustive latest-version or legal applicability search.</p><p>Reused D 145 selected Cooley June 10, 2026 terminology/base/timing/preference/clawback/conclusion, not tax 1061/waiver/securities or independently verified survey; complete returned Carta written article, not linked guide/images; selected ILPA granular measurement definitions and model preference/14.3/14.7, not full PDFs. ILPA model brackets 80% GP/20% LP catch-up, different from our stipulated 100% GP catch-up. No universal fee, hurdle, carry, recycling, manager bad-faith or communication-causes-re-up rule. Full source/AV/native cross-viewer/independent learner/account/field/legal/tax and actual overall 10 remain open. No payout, outreach, real investor judgment, learner testing, commitment, new account or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Review case60commitments/54paid-in/45invested/9costs. Undrawn and reconciliation?","o":["15 undrawn;only45 invested counts as contributed.","6 undrawn;54+9=63 contributed.","0 undrawn;60commitments equals54paid-in automatically.","6 undrawn;45+9=54 contributed."],"a":3},{"q":"ContractA6% compound preference on54 for4years,principal separate. Preference?","o":["12.96,using54*.06*4 as simple interest.","14.17375584,using54*((1.06)^4-1).","68.17375584,including54principal in preference.","11.8114632,using the alternative45 fee-excluded base."],"a":1},{"q":"A has70 proceeds,54capital,14.17375584 preference. Actual catch-up?","o":["3.54343896,the full target despite insufficient proceeds.","2.834751168,simply20% of preference.","1.82624416,limited by remaining cash.","0.365248832,20% of remaining cash without catch-up."],"a":2},{"q":"UnderA at70,finalLP andGPcarry?","o":["LP68.17375584;GP1.82624416,total70.","LP66.45656104;GP3.54343896,total70.","LP70;GP1.82624416,total71.82624416.","LP66.8;GP3.2,total70."],"a":0},{"q":"ContractB excludes9costs from return/preference/carry bases. At70,LP65/GP5. Netmultiple?","o":["65/45=1.444444444,on the fee-excluded waterfall base.","65/60=1.083333333,on total committed capital.","70/54=1.296296296,before deductingGPcarry.","65/54=1.203703704,on actual investor contributions."],"a":3},{"q":"UnderB45capital,preference11.8114632,catch-up2.9528658. Residual andGPcarry?","o":["Residual13.1885368;GPcarry5.59057316 including residual2.63770736.","Residual10.235671;GPcarry2.0471342 excluding catch-up.","Residual10.235671;GPcarry5 including residual2.0471342.","Residual10.235671;GPcarry7 including an extra fee allocation."],"a":2},{"q":"UnderA with90 instead of70proceeds,all catch-up paid. FinalLP andGP?","o":["LP72;GP18,total90.","LP82.8;GP7.2,total90.","LP90;GP7.2,total97.2.","LP80.8;GP9.2,total90."],"a":1},{"q":"What does a four-questionPSM result establish by itself?","o":["Price perceptions,not realized demand or a profit-maximizing price.","Actual paid conversion because every threshold is a purchase observation.","The causal revenue effect of switching the market to that price.","A complete four-question result from only three thresholds."],"a":0},{"q":"A geo toy has150incremental orders,contribution120 each,spend20000. Net effect?","o":["+25000;use revenue45000 instead of contribution18000.","+18000;exclude the stated incremental spend20000.","-2000;positive revenueROAS need not mean positive contribution.","+2000;reverse the cost-minus-benefit sign."],"a":2},{"q":"Which conclusion is defensible across the review?","o":["All funds recycle110-125%,so missing recycling terms can be assumed.","A faster fundraise proves bad faith and frequent updates guarantee re-ups.","A12month cohort contribution model establishes full lifetime profit.","Stated bases and evidence gaps matter;netTVPI is not cashDPI."],"a":3}]},"148":{"objectives":["Prepare fictional capital-call and quarterly reporting exercises with commitment,NAV and investor-cash-flow reconciliations;verify authority and payment details separately from arithmetic."],"videos":[{"id":"5nHw7LFCnBA","title":"Fund Administration Explained | Key Functions, NAV, Reporting & Compliance","by":"Imarticus Learning","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Generic fund-administration roles and regulatory examples are not universal legal mandates; do not import garbled Dodd-Frank/EMIR references, redemption mechanics or per-unit NAV into every closed-endVC fund."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA Capital Call & Distribution Guidance2025","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/ILPA-Suggested-Guidance-2025-Final.pdf","note":"Supplemental notices remain aligned with the LPA; updated main-template fields differ from optional editable supplemental calculations."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA Capital Call Definitions2025","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Capital-Call-Template-Definitions.pdf","note":"Inside/outside fund differs from commitment impact; negative contributions, distributions and recallability must not be conflated."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA Reporting Template2.0 Guidance","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/ILPA-Reporting-Template-v.-2.0-Suggested-Guidance.pdf","note":"Closed-end private-equity focus; other assets may require adaptation. The LPA, jurisdiction and accounting govern actual deadlines."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NAV - Carta","url":"https://carta.com/learn/private-funds/management/fund-performance/nav/","note":"Page presents per-share NAV; the exercise explicitly separates aggregate assets-minus-liabilities from per-unit NAV. Its opening cost-subtraction wording is not adopted as a fund NAV formula."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA Performance Definitions","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ILPA-Performance-Template-Definitions-Granular-Methodology-v1.1.pdf","note":"Dated investor cash flows and proper net valuation basis are required for IRR."}],"exercise":"Private fictional administrator packet only. Do not issue a real call,send anLP letter,create bank details or instruct a transfer. All amounts in millions. Toyfund commitments400,prior paid-in100,no recallable distributions/transfers/outside-commitment calls. New call12 comprises8 investment,3 stipulated management fees and1 approved-under-toy-contract expense;all reduce unfunded commitment. Fund unfunded300->288. LP has10% same-basis allocation:commitment40,priorpaid10,call1.2 comprising.8/.3/.1,unfunded30->28.8. State the fee period,base,rate and approval evidence as unresolved if not supplied;3 is a stipulated allocated fee,not a verified calculation or legally permitted charge. Draft fields fund/legalentity,LP,noticeID,purpose,transaction amounts,prior/current unfunded,reconciliation,reviewer and due-date basis. Use [verified bank details not supplied] and [due date to be verified against governing documents and applicable business-day calendar]. No invented business-day deadline,account number,customer data or transfer. Bank-change verification requires an independently established route,not a reply address in an unverified notice;no security system is infallible. Separate quarterly NAVtoy,not the preceding call ledger:closing portfolio fairvalue150,cash10,receivables2,liabilities12=>aggregateNAV150,not162 and not a per-unit price. Independent same-scopeNAVbridge:opening130+contributions20-distributions10+fairvaluegain15-fees3-otherexpenses2=150,with no other effects. No double-counting already-accrued liabilities as another deduction. Cumulative same-investor-scope paid-in120,distributions30,netNAV150=>DPI.25,RVPI1.25,TVPI1.5;undated aggregates cannot supplyIRR. Explain valuations,material events,fees/offsets,capital activity,risk and comparables without inventing real fund figures. ILPA reporting guidance2025 is supplemental,industry-driven and contract/accounting-sensitive;its Q12026 transition and direct-fund60/120day framework are not universal statutory deadlines. Updated capital-call guidanceSeptember2025 has separate Q12026/Q12027 cohorts and firstdeliveryQ12027 rules;do not collapse them into one immediate2026mandate. Verify current relevant law,LPA,sideletters,accounting basis and reporting entity before actual application. No actual report issuance,filing,default notice,payment or bank verification is performed.<section aria-label=\"Native D148 practice\"><h3>Administrator reconciliation and notice review</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d148-administrator-reconciliation.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable administrator lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d148-administrator-reconciliation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">13-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional administrator workbook. Original all-inside-commitment call: commitments 400, prior paid 100, call 8 investment + 3 stipulated fee + 1 expense = 12; unfunded 300 to 288. Same-basis LP 10% has commitment 40, prior paid 10, call .8/.3/.1 = 1.2, unfunded 30 to 28.8. Fee period/base/rate/approval unresolved. Original exercise and all questions/keys retained.</p><p>Separate supplemental LP transaction case: opening paid 10, distributions 2, unfunded 30. Investment call .8 reduces unfunded .8; fee call .3 is inside fund but outside commitment; negative contribution -.2 returns excess investment capital and increases unfunded .2, not a distribution; recallable distribution .4 increases distributions and unfunded .4; outside-fund subsequent-close conduit interest .05 is excluded from inside-fund aggregate. Terms/classifications stipulated, not universal or verified.</p><p>Baseline inside-fund contributions .9, distributions .4, net called .5; outside-fund net .05; all included net called .55. Explicit unfunded impact gives 29.8; inside-fund cumulative paid 10.9/distributions 2.4. Editable signed contributions, distributions, commitment decreases/increases and inside/outside flag have row numeric gates. Numerical pass does not validate classification, contract treatment or payment authority. Negative resulting unfunded or paid-in stays visible for investigation.</p><p>Independent quarterly snapshot 150 portfolio + 10 cash + 2 receivables - 12 already-accrued liabilities = aggregate NAV 150, not assets 162 or a per-unit price. No unit count supplied. Same-scope bridge 130 + 20 contributions - 10 distributions + 15 fair-value gain - 3 fees - 2 expenses = 150, zero difference at baseline. No repeat deduction of already-accrued liabilities. Different dates/scopes cannot be repaired by matching totals. Original call, supplemental rows, quarterly bridge and metric case are independent.</p><p>Independent same-investor-scope cumulative paid 120, distributions 30, net NAV 150 gives DPI .25, RVPI 1.25, TVPI 1.5. Zero paid leaves ratios undefined; no dated IRR. Fees remain in actual investor contributions. Snapshot NAV is not cash returned or gross portfolio MOIC. No valuation model, tax, transfers, foreign exchange, in-kind or full accounting implementation.</p><p>Private incomplete notice checklist uses explicit fund/LP/notice/fee-basis, verified-bank-details and governing-business-day-due-date placeholders. No bank number, assumed ten-day deadline, default notice or signed instruction. Independent known-number/in-person verification is separate from authority and arithmetic, and no security control guarantees safety. Actual evidence/permission/accepted-owner rows remain blank. No actual call, investor letter, report, filing, contact, payment or transfer.</p><p>Twelve sheets/thirteen PDF pages; 41 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 28 changed sets/1148 checks plus 41 restored, seven boundary sets/287 checks, 24 invalid inputs/984 family-isolation checks, six broken references/six checks. Clean saved caches, no external links/macros. Inputs print initially orphaned a third page; blank rows compacted to two readable pages, then final changed wording and pages inspected. Authored LibreOffice checks, not native Excel/Sheets parity or independent learner evidence.</p><p>Seven URLs/three organizations: new ILPA September 2025 capital-call guidance selected implementation summary and VI-XII mapping/description/optionality/negative-contribution/verification limits, not full PDF or official workbook; returned transaction definitions read; returned written template listing read, not timeline image/downloads. First delivery Q 1 2027; performance-template adopters funds commencing Q 1 2026, non-adopters Q 1 2027. Industry disclosure guidance does not verify GP calculations or replace LPA/accounting/jurisdiction.</p><p>New Carta visible October 30, 2024 written article body/formula read, not images/linked report. Its opening cost-subtraction wording is not adopted as aggregate NAV; published formula is per-share. New complete returned FBI IC 3 April 6, 2020 PSA read for compromised legitimate email and known-number/in-person payment-change verification; historic losses not current statistics. Reused D 147 selected ILPA reporting/conformity/estimates/optionality/fees/verification limits and D 145 selected investor contribution/net TVPI/net IRR/gross definitions, not full PDFs. Not exhaustive latest-version/legal/accounting/valuation search. Full source/AV/native cross-viewer/independent learner/account/field/legal/tax/actual overall 10 remain open. No actual notice, transfer, account, learner test or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Fund400commitment,100priorpaid,12newcall,all inside commitment,no other changes. Unfunded?","o":["300 before;288 after.","300 before;312 after.","400 before;388 after.","100 before;88 after."],"a":0},{"q":"LP10% same-basis share of8investment/3fee/1expense call. Allocation?","o":["0.8/0.03/0.01,total0.84.","8/0.3/0.1,total8.4.","0.8/0.3/0.1,total1.2.","0.8/0.3/1,total2.1."],"a":2},{"q":"LPcommitment40,priorpaid10,newcall1.2,no recallability or other changes. Unfunded after?","o":["31.2,from40-10+1.2.","38.8,from40-1.2 without priorpaid.","8.8,from10-1.2 using paid-in as unfunded.","28.8,from40-10-1.2."],"a":3},{"q":"A notice asks for payment to changed bank details. Safe preparation step?","o":["Use the new email reply address because the notice names it as official.","Hold transfer;verify authority and details through an independently established route.","Trust matching logos because payment notices always use authenticated branding.","Assume account validation is complete once the amount reconciles arithmetically."],"a":1},{"q":"Portfolio150,cash10,receivables2,liabilities12. AggregateNAV?","o":["150;per-unitNAV additionally needs the unit count.","162;liabilities do not reduce fund value.","138;subtract liabilities twice to be conservative.","15;divide by10 despite no unit count being given."],"a":0},{"q":"OpeningNAV130+contributions20-distributions10+gain15-fees3-expenses2. ClosingNAV?","o":["170,adding distributions rather than subtracting them.","155,omitting fees and other expenses.","135,omitting the stated fairvalue gain.","150,with all listed flows reconciled once."],"a":3},{"q":"Cumulativepaid120,distributions30,netNAV150. DPI,RVPI,TVPI?","o":["0.25;1.5;1.25.","0.2;1.0;1.2.","0.25;1.25;1.5.","0.25;1.25;1.25."],"a":2},{"q":"Can those cumulative undated figures establish annualizedIRR?","o":["Yes;TVPI1.5 means50% annualIRR regardless of fund age.","No;dated cash flows and terminal valuation timing are missing.","Yes;DPI.25 means25% annualIRR regardless of timing.","No;IRR cannot ever include terminal residual value."],"a":1},{"q":"Does ILPA2025 guidance create one universal legal reporting deadline?","o":["Yes;all funds worldwide must report within45days by statute.","Yes;capital-call and reporting templates have identical rollout dates.","No;industry guidance cannot be useful for any negotiated reporting design.","No;scope,transition,LPA,jurisdiction and accounting still matter."],"a":3},{"q":"What can this fictional administrator packet legitimately contain?","o":["Explicit placeholders,scope assumptions,reconciliations and unresolved checks.","Invented bank numbers to make the capital call look operationally complete.","A liveLP letter signed as the manager without reviewed words or recipients.","A default notice using an assumed10business-day calendar and cure period."],"a":0}]},"149":{"objectives":["Map cross-border investment and share-swap proposals into facts, regulatory branches and open legal/tax checks, without a blanket structuring verdict."],"videos":[{"id":"XwlpAm9JnvQ","title":"SEBI's New AIF Rules Explained (2026) | 10 Major Changes Every Investor Must Know","by":"Let Me Flow","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Topic is SEBI inoperative funds, not flips, IFSC tax or direct FDI. Its legal and safety claims are not scored as current law."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"IFSCA fund regulations 2025: selected provisions","url":"https://ifsca.gov.in/CommonDirect/ViewFile?id=21626bde60601ef44a0ed022017f9e07&fileName=IFSCA__Fund_Management__Regulations__2025__Amended_up_to_July_30__2025__20250818_0105.pdf","note":"Selected definitions and regulations 18-21/30-35 from the 100-page consolidation through 30 July 2025, not the whole framework. Later amendments/circulars need checking; domestic SEBI terms do not apply automatically."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IFSCA fund amendment 2026: Gazette","url":"https://ifsca.gov.in/CommonDirect/ViewFile?id=36ff47aaeb9222f627d166fe86841979&fileName=106_IFSCA__Fund_Management___Amendment__Regulations__2026_20260202_0720.pdf","note":"Notification dated 27 January 2026; commencement follows publication wording."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"RBI overseas investment direction","url":"https://rbi.org.in/scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=12710","note":"Updated 1 April 2026. Entity, individual, fund and IFSC routes differ."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"RBI foreign investment direction","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=11200","note":"Read sections 7.14 and 8.1-8.11 with their definitions and exceptions; other applicable provisions may add requirements. Direction of transfer, listed status, swap valuation and exceptions matter."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"RBI non-debt payment/reporting regulations","url":"https://rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=11723","note":"Selected opening/payment provisions only, not all reporting rules. Updated 13 June 2026. This is separate from the substantive foreign-investment direction."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Budget 2024 memorandum: historical proposal","url":"https://www.indiabudget.gov.in/budget2024-25/doc/memo.pdf","note":"Selected Section 56 passage only, not the whole memorandum or enacted/current tax law. Proposed AY 2025-26 sunset does not settle every swap, historical or pricing consequence."}],"exercise":"Private fictional issue-map, not legal or tax advice. Draw three structures: US investor -> IFSC fund -> Indian startup; US investor -> Indian startup directly; Indian holders exchanging shares for foreign holding-company shares. Label residence, beneficial ownership, sector, instrument, dates, control and flows. Unknown facts stay unknown.\nAn IFSC scheme is not automatically governed on domestic SEBI AIF terms. Selected IFSCA 2025 provisions distinguish venture-capital and restricted schemes, with category references; later amendments and circulars matter. Neither GIFT location nor Category I/II alone establishes tax neutrality, treaty benefits or permission. Current-law application remains unscored research.\nCheck both inbound and outbound swap legs. RBI routes differ for resident individuals, entities and funds; selected overseas-investment provisions do not make every return-to-India structure illegal. Pricing depends on issue/transfer direction, listed status, swap rules and exceptions, not universal DCF. Payment/reporting provisions are separate; an identifier or filing is not approval. The 2024 Budget memorandum describes a proposed Section 56(2)(viib) sunset from AY 2025-26. This alone is not enacted/current case advice, a waiver of old exposure, or proof about swap capital gains, FEMA pricing or all taxes. Do not infer FY from AY without official grounding.\nToy: 100 issued ordinary shares, founder 60/investor 40, exchange ratio two new ordinary shares per old share, no fresh money, fees, options or CCPS. New shares: 200 total, founder 120/investor 80; percentages remain 60/40. That proves neither tax neutrality nor rights continuity. Actual CCPS, preferences, consents, ESOP replacement, IP and contracts need separate review. Prepare a two-page map with evidence gaps, owners and stop/go conditions. No formation, filing, bank contact, share transfer, advice signed as counsel, solicitation or investment.<section aria-label=\"Native D149 practice\"><h3>Cross-border issue map and exchange arithmetic</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d149-cross-border-map.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable map and share lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d149-cross-border-map.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional cross-border map and ordinary-share exchange workbook. Original three routes retained: US investor via IFSC fund to Indian startup; US investor directly to Indian startup; Indian holders exchanging Indian shares for foreign holding-company shares. Residence, beneficial ownership, sector, instrument, control, dates and flows remain open until supported. No actual transaction, investor, startup or fund supplied.</p><p>Original uniform exchange: 60 founder / 40 investor ordinary shares, total 100; two new per old -> 120 / 80, total 200, 60% / 40%. Empty receiving-company toy with no other shares, fresh money, fees, options or CCPS. Arithmetic does not establish rights, tax neutrality, residence, eligibility, pricing, control, consents, reporting or approval.</p><p>Separate existing-holder sensitivity adds 50 existing receiving-company ordinary shares: total 250, founder 48%, exchanging investor 32%, legacy holder 20%. The exchanged block remains internally 60/40, not company-wide. Separate unequal ratios founder 2 / investor 3 yield 120 / 120 = 240, 50% / 50%, without legacy shares. Neither scenario is a recommended or fair exchange ratio.</p><p>Editable old counts, common ratio, existing stake and separate ratios have four numeric gates and three isolated output families. Fractional modeled entitlements remain visible with a whole-share flag, not rounded into a legal issuance or invented cash-in-lieu rule. Zero total/ratio, negative/text/blank counts or noninteger existing counts fail the relevant gate. Zero individual holder is allowed; all outputs conditional on the explicitly separate scenario.</p><p>Two-page issue map traces money/return flows separately from the inbound/outbound swap legs. Proposed reviewer/evidence/authority/stop fields are not actual accepted ownership or a legal go decision. Citizenship/incorporation alone do not establish residence. CCPS/preferences, ESOPs, voting, consents, IP and contracts need their own review. No formation, filing, bank contact, transfer, counsel opinion, solicitation, account, investment, learner flow or spend.</p><p>RBI OI page visibly updated April 1, 2026: selected pricing, UIN-not-approval, reporting, conditional layers and entity/individual/other/IFSC branches read, not all rules/schedules/footnotes/exceptions. Foreign-investment page visibly June 15, 2026: selected 7.14 and 8.1-8.11 written provisions read directly. Issue/transfer direction, listed status, specific swap valuers, exceptions and certificate validity differ; no universal DCF or price floor/ceiling. Payment/reporting page June 13, 2026 selected opening/Schedule I payment provision read, not all reporting or case deadlines.</p><p>Selected IFSCA July 2025 regulations 18/30 distinguish venture-capital/restricted schemes and category references, not automatic domestic SEBI terms. Official index now lists September 10, 2026 consolidation and second amendment; listing is not their operative-text clearance or proof every later circular checked. Existing original sources and questions retained; current-law application remains unscored.</p><p>Budget 2024 memorandum selected Section 56(2)(viib) passage remains a proposed AY 2025-26 sunset, not enacted/current all-tax advice, old exposure waiver or swap capital-gains clearance; no FY inferred. January 2025 JSA returned written share-swap analysis read, not diagrams/linked cases; dated permission/valuation language not adopted as universal/current eligibility or transaction authority. Seven URLs/four organizations, regulator directions/text, official proposal/index and practitioner analysis, not seven independent legal opinions.</p><p>Ten sheets/ten PDF pages; 37 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 15 changed sets/555 checks plus 37 restored, six boundary sets/222 checks, 24 invalid inputs/888 family-isolation checks, six broken references/six checks. Clean saved caches/no external links/macros. Authored LibreOffice only, not native Excel/Sheets parity, independent learner, complete source/AV/current-law review, account/field testing or actual overall 10. Actual evidence/authority/accepted-owner rows stay blank.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Which facts select the cross-border review branches?","o":["Start with investor citizenship and sector, treating tax residence and flows as follow-up items.","Start with fund category and expected return, treating the share instrument as interchangeable.","Start with recipient incorporation and currency, using the same branch for all holder types.","Residence, beneficial ownership, sector, instrument, control, dates and flows."],"a":3},{"q":"Why not copy domestic SEBI AIF terms into every GIFT fund memo?","o":["IFSC funds have no regulator or legal conditions.","IFSCA has its own framework; category references need scoped reading.","Every GIFT fund is a Category II trust on domestic SEBI terms.","A foreign LP exempts the fund from all Indian reporting."],"a":1},{"q":"What does GIFT location or Category I alone establish?","o":["No complete tax-neutrality, permission or superiority conclusion.","Every portfolio return and investor distribution is tax exempt.","Indian sector caps and beneficial ownership can be ignored.","Treaty benefits apply automatically to every subscription."],"a":0},{"q":"A share swap creates inbound and outbound interests. Proper review?","o":["Check only Indian issue price; the foreign leg is automatically permitted.","Check only the foreign charter; Indian holder rules never apply.","Check both legs, holder eligibility, valuation, consents and reporting.","Check only whether it is noncash; noncash means no regulatory event."],"a":2},{"q":"How should a conditional return-to-India layered-structure rule be used?","o":["Treat every foreign investment with an Indian link as illegal.","Treat two layers as an exemption from every other requirement.","Treat holding-company nationality as proof of founder tax residence.","Read defined control, layers, conditions and exceptions, not a blanket ban."],"a":3},{"q":"What does an explanatory 2024 tax proposal establish on its own?","o":["Its proposed change and AY scope, not complete current case treatment.","Current tax clearance for every past and future funding round.","An exemption from swap capital gains and FEMA valuation.","Permission to equate assessment year and financial year without checking."],"a":0},{"q":"100 shares, founder 60/investor 40, exchange two new per old. New counts?","o":["200 total; founder 60/investor 140; ownership 30%/70%.","200 total; founder 120/investor 80; ownership 60%/40%.","100 total; founder 120/investor 80; ownership 120%/80%.","200 total; founder 100/investor 100; ownership 50%/50%."],"a":1},{"q":"Unchanged 60/40 ownership in that toy proves what?","o":["Both countries must treat the exchange as tax free.","All preferences, ESOPs and customer contracts continue automatically.","Only modeled percentages, not tax neutrality or rights continuity.","No authorization is needed because percentages are unchanged."],"a":2},{"q":"Which pricing-research approach avoids a blanket legal rule?","o":["Identify direction, issue/transfer, listed status, swap rules and exceptions.","Use DCF for every transaction regardless of instrument or route.","Use the same price ceiling for nonresident issue and exit.","Use an old 2012 PDF as proof no later carve-out applies."],"a":0},{"q":"What is the classroom output?","o":["A Delaware company and bank account because the diagram includes them.","A filed return and share transfer without verified case approval.","A signed compliance opinion based only on the exercise.","An issue-map with source bounds, open checks and owners."],"a":3}]},"150":{"objectives":["Design permission-safe sourcing with identity resolution, coverage, missingness, out-of-time testing and explicit costs, without claiming vendor alpha."],"videos":[{"id":"o3-clbrGi4Q","title":"#207 Data Driven Venture Capital | Andre Retterath, Partner at Earlybird VC","by":"DataCamp","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Unity data platform: Ensemble VC","url":"https://www.ensemble.vc/research/unity-data-driven-sourcing","note":"Whole written page dated 3 January 2025, not diagram or underlying dataset validation. Coverage and superiority claims are vendor claims."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Investment firm 2030: Data Driven VC","url":"https://www.newsletter.datadrivenvc.io/p/do-you-still-need-analysts-the-investment-firm-of-2030-fewer-people-better-decisions","note":"Whole written thought experiment dated 26 February 2026, not underlying datasets or images. Productivity, automation and inevitable learning claims are predictions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Harmonic: vendor homepage","url":"https://www.harmonic.ai","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. No account or private-network connection."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"LinkedIn prohibited software: official help","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/a1341387","note":"Whole written help page. Unauthorized scraping and automated activity are prohibited. Public visibility does not create bulk collection or redistribution permission."}],"exercise":"Private fictional sourcing architecture, not a live scraper, outreach system or purchase. Use stable company IDs, domain/person links with match confidence, deduplication, event versus collection time, provenance, refresh history and correction controls. Preserve source conflicts. Public visibility is not permission to collect or redistribute data. Verify licenses, purpose and access; company-level UPI/GST transactions are not assumed available from aggregate dashboards. LinkedIn prohibits unauthorized scraping and automation. No scripts, extensions, fake accounts, bypass or private account connections.\nVendor homepages establish advertised features, not verified India coverage, prices, refresh SLAs or investment alpha. Ensemble's coverage claims and Retterath's 2030 automation thesis are attributed advocacy, not measured guarantees. No universal best signal or three-to-six-month window is established.\nToy weights 20/20/25/20/15 and normalized signals .8/.6/.4/.9/.2 give 59/100, not 59% success probability. If the third signal is missing, covered weight is 75 and available weighted score 49: report 65.3333 normalized score with 75% coverage, not proven quality improvement. Missing is not observed zero. Test comparable observability, out-of-time features and entity-family separation. Watch leakage, selection, survivorship, proxy bias, drift and delayed labels.\nComplete-label toy: 500 eligible companies; 100 flagged contain 30 relevant/70 not; 400 unflagged contain 10 relevant/390 not. Precision 30%, recall 75%, relevance base rate 8%. Real labels and missed-universe coverage remain unverified. Use reviewer capacity, random blind-spot samples and recorded overrides; invested-deals-surfaced-first alone is not ROI.\nCost toy in lakh: build 8 one-off + annual licenses 6/run 6/security 2 = 22 year one, 14 recurring. Buy 10 annual + integration 2 one-off/operations 4 = 16 year one, 14 recurring. Analyst fully loaded annual cost 18 is stipulated. These are not quotes or equivalent roles; no automatic salary saving. No live feed, API, tracking, outreach, account change or spend.<section aria-label=\"Native D150 practice\"><h3>Sourcing score, coverage and cost lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d150-sourcing-quality.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable sourcing lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d150-sourcing-quality.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional offline sourcing workbook. Original weights 20/20/25/20/15 and normalized signals.8/.6/.4/.9/.2 produce 59/100, an unvalidated ranking score, not 59% success probability or investment alpha. Separate availability flags 1/1/0/1/1 give covered weight 75 and available weighted numerator 49 ->65.333333/100 at 75%coverage. Higher normalized score does not prove better quality; missing is not observed zero.</p><p>Editable weights, signals and availability flags have separate complete/partial row gates. Outputs normalize by actual nonnegative weight sum, not assumed 100 after edits. A blank/text signal excluded with flag 0 does not invalidate partial score but does invalidate full score. Available flag 1 requires numeric signal 0-1. All flags 0 gives coverage 0 but partial normalized score undefined; all weights 0 leaves score/coverage undefined. No ranking comparability or calibrated probability implied.</p><p>Independent complete-label screening case TP 30/FP 70/FN 10/TN 390: eligible 500, flagged 100, unflagged 400, relevant 40, precision.30, recall.75, relevance base rate.08, flagged fraction.20. Miss fraction among relevant.25, false-positive fraction among flagged.70. Count gate requires nonnegative integers. Denominator 0 remains undefined, not observed 0 performance. Unknown unflagged labels cannot be fabricated as FN/TN 0; real recall/base rate/coverage remain unestablished.</p><p>Independent cost case lakh INR: build one-off 8+annual licenses 6/run 6/security 2 ->22 year one, 14 recurring; buy annual 10+one-off integration 2+annual operations 4 ->16 year one, 14 recurring. Analyst annual 18 stipulated, not a quote/equivalent role or automatic salary saving. Three undiscounted full years at unchanged rates cost 50 build/44 buy; difference 6 is the one-off gap, not a buy recommendation. No inflation, taxes, financing, discounting, benefit, delay, contract terms or ROI.</p><p>Permission-safe architecture records stable company IDs, versioned aliases, match confidence, source conflicts, purpose/license/access/allowed audience, retention, corrections and refresh evidence. Public visibility or vendor marketing does not authorize bulk collection, redistribution or private data. Company-level UPI/GST transactions/revenue not inferred from aggregate dashboards. Actual data/source/consent/accepted-owner plan stays blank.</p><p>As-of plan separates event time from collection/availability time; later-found old events do not become known earlier. Freeze target/horizon/eligibility/label maturity/threshold/capacity before test; split before fitting transformations and never tune on reported test outcomes. TimeSeriesSplit alone does not isolate company families; GroupKFold alone does not protect chronology. Joint constraints need explicit design. TimeSeriesSplit equal-spacing condition concerns comparable fold durations; gap counts samples, not automatically calendar days or label delay. No splitter/model actually applied or trained.</p><p>Nine URLs/five organizations: scikit-learn selected precision/recall definitions and zero_division notes, selected common-pitfalls 12.2 / 12.2.1 and selected TimeSeriesSplit/GroupKFold opening/parameters, not all API/examples/plots or custom combined splitter. Library may return 0 with warning/configuration on zero denominators; this authored lab deliberately uses undefined #N/A. Complete returned LinkedIn help read: prohibited scraping/automation/fake accounts/bypass/unconsented copying/distribution. No policy violation attempted.</p><p>Returned Ensemble Unity written page(January 3, 2025) and Harmonic homepage overview read, not diagrams/images/linked resources/dataset/customer-proof/India coverage/price/terms verification. Claims are attributed vendor advertising, not measured alpha. Earlier complete returned Retterath written 2030 thought experiment(visible February 26, 2026) reused, not images/underlying datasets/advertised guide. Predictions are not guaranteed productivity, learning improvement, investment returns or job savings.</p><p>Eleven sheets/eleven PDF pages; 44 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 22 changed sets / 968 checks plus 44 restored, eight boundaries / 352 checks, 24 invalid inputs / 1056 family-isolation checks, six broken refs / six checks. Clean caches, no externals/macros. Authored LibreOffice only, not native Excel / Sheets parity, independent learner, complete source/AV/account/field/legal or actual overall 10. Actual evidence/authority/accepted-owner rows blank. No scraper, extension, API, feed export, private-network sync, real founder data, outreach, account, purchase or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Sources show different names/domains for a startup. Best graph action?","o":["Preserve IDs, provenance and confidence; review uncertain matches.","Use the latest domain as the company ID; reconcile older aliases after computing scores.","Merge on company name and city; keep source confidence only for person links.","Keep each source record as a different company until a legal identifier is found."],"a":0},{"q":"What should the design assume about company-level UPI/GST data?","o":["Public national totals reveal each private company's revenue.","A startup database grants access to all taxpayer returns.","Access, permission, granularity and purpose need verification.","A public profile waives consent for bank and tax retrieval."],"a":2},{"q":"Weights 20/20/25/20/15; signals .8/.6/.4/.9/.2. Score?","o":["61/100, an unvalidated ranking score.","59/100, an unvalidated ranking score.","75/100, an unvalidated ranking score.","80/100, an unvalidated ranking score."],"a":1},{"q":"Signal three missing: weighted available 49, covered weight 75. Report?","o":["49 score with 100% coverage; missing means observed zero.","75 score with 65.3333% coverage; reverse the inputs.","65.3333 score with 100% coverage; normalization creates data.","65.3333 score with 75% coverage; no quality improvement proven."],"a":3},{"q":"Can score 59/100 be called 59% success probability?","o":["No; probability needs a defined outcome and validated calibration.","Yes; every normalized score is calibrated by definition.","Yes; scale 100 removes selection bias and drift.","No; probabilities cannot ever be estimated from sourcing data."],"a":0},{"q":"100 flagged include 30 relevant; 400 unflagged include 10 relevant. Metrics?","o":["Precision 75%; recall 30%.","Precision 30%; recall 6%.","Precision 20%; recall 8%.","Precision 30%; recall 75%."],"a":3},{"q":"Which holdout addresses future-data leakage?","o":["Use a random row split after including each company's eventual financing outcome.","Freeze feature dates but let duplicate company aliases enter both train and test.","Freeze as-of features and separate future periods/entity families.","Separate entity families but tune weights using labels from the reported test period."],"a":2},{"q":"Build 8+annual 6/6/2; buy annual 10+one-off 2+annual 4. Year one?","o":["Build 14 lakh; buy 14 lakh; recurring 14 each.","Build 22 lakh; buy 16 lakh; recurring 14 each.","Build 22 lakh; buy 12 lakh; recurring 14 each.","Build 20 lakh; buy 16 lakh; recurring 14 each."],"a":1},{"q":"Analyst cost 18 lakh versus the toy software costs. Proper comparison?","o":["Buy 16 guarantees 2 lakh saved with identical judgment and coverage.","Build 22 guarantees 4 lakh lost regardless of future benefit.","Equal recurring costs necessarily produce equal investment returns.","Compare capacity, quality, permissions and roles, not automatic job savings."],"a":3},{"q":"Which operational boundary is supported by LinkedIn help?","o":["No unauthorized scraping, automation, fake accounts or bypass.","Public profiles can always be scraped and redistributed.","Vendor integration copy authorizes private contact access.","A classroom design authorizes paid feeds and founder outreach."],"a":0}]},"151":{"objectives":["Prepare founder/customer diligence questions and a contradiction ledger without fabricated calls, consent or behavioral verdicts."],"videos":[{"id":"IZp-Y_IZ5z8","title":"Anita Rehman - Startup Investing Masterclass: Due Diligence","by":"Republic","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Historical platform counts, runway rules and investment stories are not current availability or instructions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Founder reference checks: 1752vc","url":"https://www.1752.vc/learn/how-vcs-run-founder-reference-checks","note":"Backchannel, hesitation, elite-reference and cultural-rating heuristics are author views, not permission or validated predictors."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Assess founders: Allied VC","url":"https://www.allied.vc/guides/how-to-assess-founders-during-due-diligence","note":"Whole written page dated 9 October 2025 including disclaimer. Full-time commitment is this firm's policy; scorecards and turnover do not guarantee a verdict."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA DDQ 2.0 user guide","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/ILPA-DDQ-2.0-User-Guide.pdf","note":"Confidentiality, staged information and N/A handling are contextual, not startup-specific outreach permission."}],"exercise":"Private fictional diligence pack, not real calls. No Module 5 startup facts, contact permission or actual interviews are supplied. Use labeled fictional roles and blank note templates, never invented quotes or completed-call claims. Draft 12 open questions covering facts, observed work, decisions under pressure, feedback, failure risks, customer purchase/use, alternatives and renewal constraints. Ask for firsthand examples, relationship/time context and permission to retain and share notes. Do not request protected personal data, credentials or confidential former-employer files.\nOffered and off-list sources have different selection biases. Off-list does not mean truthful, unbiased or authorized for secret contact. There is no universal best question; pauses, dates-only refusal, cultural style, blame phrasing and turnover do not prove dishonesty or uncoachability. Refusal may reflect policy or limited knowledge. Keep both conflicting accounts with source proximity, dates, scope, counterevidence and open checks; do not invent a resolution or contact anyone.\nFictional ledger: 100 accounts include 40 paying and 60 partner-sourced free trials. Paying does not mean organic, and trial accounts need not be distinct companies. If the 40 paying accounts are independent monthly subscriptions at 5,000 each, without other adjustments, MRR is 200,000. Largest customer MRR 80,000 gives 40% concentration. These are classroom givens, not customer findings.\nToy survey: three of five selected respondents are very disappointed without the product, or 60% of that sample. It does not establish population PMF, retention or willingness to pay. Define eligible users, sampling, nonresponse and behavioral checks. Switching friction, satisfaction, habit, alternatives and renewal are distinct. No calls, outreach, private disclosure, background check or investment recommendation.<section aria-label=\"Native D151 practice\"><h3>Diligence evidence and selected-sample lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d151-diligence-evidence.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable diligence lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d151-diligence-evidence.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional offline diligence workbook. Original 100 accounts=40 paying+60 partner-sourced free trials; paying is not organic, accounts not necessarily distinct companies. Uniform 40 independent monthly subscriptions*5,000=200,000 MRR under stipulated comparable recurring terms/no adjustments. Annualized snapshot run rate 2,400,000 not forecast next-year revenue, cash collected or recognized accounting revenue.</p><p>Separate concentration case 80,000 largest-customer MRR/200,000 total=40%; other-customer 120,000/60%. This cannot be the same uniform 40*5,000 single-subscription-per-customer ledger. It uses an independent stipulated revenue mix; no reconciliation or same-customer mapping invented. Original quiz/key retained; the new lab explicitly separates those classroom cases.</p><p>Original three of five selected respondents very disappointed=60% of that sample, not population PMF, retention, renewal or willingness-to-pay proof. Separate nested case eligible 20/invited 10/responded 5/positive 3 gives invitation coverage 50%, completion among invited 50%, respondent fraction eligible 25%, answer share 60%. Five invited nonrespondents and ten uninvited eligible unknown. Extreme fractions invited 30-80%, eligible 15-90% hold observed answers fixed and assign unknowns no vs yes, not confidence intervals, estimates or fabricated responses. Simple completion ratio is not standardized survey response-rate methodology; low completion alone neither proves nor excludes bias, high completion alone does not establish representativeness.</p><p>Twelve open single-focus draft prompts cover relationship, observation window, firsthand work, pressure decision, feedback, failure risk, purchase process, actual use, alternatives, switching friction, renewal constraints, counterevidence. Optional follow-ups asked separately. Off-list does not mean unbiased or authorized; pause, dates-only refusal, style, cultural stereotype or turnover not dishonesty/coachability verdicts. Actual contradiction ledger and contact/note/recording/retention/audience/attribution/accepted-owner plan remain blank; no actual founder/customer facts, quotes, consent, calls, background check, outreach, private disclosure, account, investment or spend.</p><p>Seven URLs/five organizations, three source types. Complete returned 1752 vcwrittenpage read, not images/linked sources: backchannel weight, elite reference, cultural/hesitation heuristics and critic-probability model are author views/assumptions, not validated predictors; quoted 2016 survey/linked 2026 media not independently checked. Complete returned Allied written October 9,2025 page including disclaimer read, not images/links; full-time is firm policy, scorecard/turnover not guarantees. Complete returned ILPA November 9,2021 v 1.0 written guide read, not PDF pixels / underlying DDQ: PE-manager reference point, staged/redacted/confidential responses and N/A context, not startup outreach permission.</p><p>Selected Pew question-development/open-vs-closed/question-wording portions read, not whole page/images/video; complete returned July 21,2015 nonresponse interview read, not underlying studies/images. Historical US polling context, not India startup benchmark or weighting guarantee. Selected Stripe MRR definition/calculation plus analytics discount/active-subscriber settings read, not full API/UI/benchmarks/PMF claim validation. Vendor metric conventions configurable; no billing account used. Seven URLs are in workbook evidence sheet; full source/AV or trait/outcome validity not implied.</p><p>Eleven sheets/eleven PDF pages; 23 independently recomputed baseline formulas, 14 changed sets / 322 checks plus 23 restored, eight boundaries / 184 checks, 20 invalid inputs / 460 family-isolationchecks, six broken refs/six checks. Clean caches, no externals/macros. Authored LibreOffice only, not native Excel / Sheets parity, independent learner/account/field/legal or actual overall 10. Inputs gate whole counts, nested survey constraints, largest<=total, nonnegative INR; zero denominators undefined. Original exercise/quiz/objectives retained. No real contact, recording, participant data, private disclosure, background check or spending.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"No real calls or contact permission are supplied. Appropriate notes?","o":["Plausible anonymous quotes, because names are removed.","Labeled fictional or blank templates, not claims of completed calls.","Real off-list calls, because the exercise implies consent.","Imagined customers labeled verified if the story is coherent."],"a":1},{"q":"Which reference prompt supports a defensible method?","o":["Ask for a single rating first and use it as the main comparison across all references.","Use the same five adjectives for every founder and omit relationship context for consistency.","Ask only for negative examples to compensate for the selection of supportive references.","Ask for firsthand examples, context and limits, not a universal rating."],"a":3},{"q":"An off-list reference criticizes a founder. What is established?","o":["Unbiased truth, because off-list sources cannot be conflicted.","A deal-killer without checking scope or firsthand knowledge.","One attributed account with its own access, bias and context.","Permission to publish the source's identity and private claims."],"a":2},{"q":"A reference confirms dates only. Proper interpretation?","o":["Limited information; policy, knowledge or confidentiality may explain it.","A polite negative proving poor performance in that culture.","A positive referral because no criticism was stated.","Evidence of illegal behavior because they declined discussion."],"a":0},{"q":"100 accounts: 40 paying, 60 partner trials. What can be stated?","o":["40 organic paying companies and 60 independent trial companies.","Those cohorts; organic status and distinct-company counts remain unknown.","100 paying customers, because registration counts as payment.","Only 40 accounts, because trials cannot belong in an account ledger."],"a":1},{"q":"40 independent monthly paid subscriptions at 5,000, no adjustments; free trials are excluded. What is MRR?","o":["500,000 monthly.","300,000 monthly.","200,000 monthly.","120,000 monthly."],"a":2},{"q":"Largest customer MRR 80,000; total MRR 200,000. Concentration?","o":["20% of MRR.","60% of MRR.","80% of MRR.","40% of MRR."],"a":3},{"q":"Three of five selected respondents are very disappointed. Conclusion?","o":["60% of that sample, not population PMF or renewal proof.","60% of all customers will renew next year.","Verified PMF regardless of selection or product stage.","The answer establishes the price each user will actually pay."],"a":0},{"q":"Founder and former colleague disagree. Best contradiction entry?","o":["Retain the more recent claim as fact and move the older account to an unshown archive.","Retain the closer colleague's claim as fact without recording the founder's response.","Both claims, sources, dates, scope, counterevidence and open check.","Combine the shared elements into one account and omit the remaining disagreement."],"a":2},{"q":"What can switching-cost questions help distinguish?","o":["Guaranteed renewal by every user facing transition cost.","Workflow, data and contract friction from satisfaction, alternatives and habit.","Causal retention from one stated preference without behavioral data.","Permanent pricing power whenever a customer calls the product useful."],"a":1}]},"152":{"objectives":["Combine commercial, technical and legal evidence with coherent economics and conditional remediation, not automatic stage-based verdicts."],"videos":[{"id":"rmgHaeHMSCU","title":"143. The Truth About Due Diligence: How VCs Evaluate Startups","by":"AI Founders’ Mindset","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Speaker experience is not this startup's evidence, Indian law or a universal diligence deadline."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Technical due diligence: SeedForge","url":"https://www.seedforge.com/blog/technical-due-diligence-for-startups","note":"June 16, 2026 technical-diligence article. Stage and ten-times-load heuristics are not universal requirements; a correlation does not establish causality."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Legal diligence readiness: Consilium Law","url":"https://consilium.law/sparkpoint/startup-fundraising-legal-due-diligence-checklist/","note":"Whole written article dated 10 August 2026, US-focused rather than Indian law. US copyright, Delaware remedies, tax and fee details are not imported as Indian rules."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Nicolas Cava: consultant overview","url":"https://www.nicolascava.com/resources/technical-due-diligence-checklist","note":"Consultant overview, not a diligence checklist. Use the written diligence exercise; no engagement or paid service is required."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Indian B2B AI workflow vendor, not a named-company verdict. All financials and technical/contract claims are synthetic or unknown. No actual data room, competitor pricing, private customer data, code inspection, litigation clearance or security test. Three one-page notes map claim -> evidence/scope/date -> contradiction -> risk/impact -> owner/next check.\nCommercial toy: 40 crore qualified pipeline times stipulated comparable stage-to-close conversion 25% gives 10 crore weighted pipeline, not revenue, ARR or a guarantee. Check stage definitions, aging, duplicate/correlated accounts, sales cycles and censored outcomes before using historical conversion.\nTechnical: a foundation-model API changes dependency and model claims, but does not automatically remove every workflow, data-rights or distribution advantage. Monthly revenue 60 lakh, API variable cost 12 and other variable cost 18 give contribution 30/margin 50%. Doubling API cost to 24 leaves contribution 18/margin 30%, before fixed costs and other unknowns. Not current supplier pricing. Request authorized architecture, dependency, access, data-flow, observability, recovery, load, key-person and delivery evidence. No universal microservices, daily deploy, coverage or SOC 2 rule. Seed stage does not excuse critical security risk; certification does not prove safety.\nLegal: missing contractor assignment is a potential ownership gap, requiring governing law, actual terms and counsel review, not automatic criminality or a backdated fix. Preserve history; never manufacture approvals. Change-of-control wording may trigger consent, notice, termination or other rights depending on transaction and law. Consilium is US-focused: Delaware ratification is not an Indian IP/CCPS/ESOP cure. MCA/entity records are not a complete litigation search. Lending partnerships need current RBI, role, contract and loss-allocation review, not assumed DLG/FLDG legality. A risk table must show uncertainty, dependencies, evidence quality and blocking conditions; arbitrary scores are not measured expected losses. No investment, contract edit, pen test or compliance certification.<section aria-label=\"Native D152 practice\"><h3>Diligence pipeline, cost and dependency lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d152-diligence-dependencies.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable diligence lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d152-diligence-dependencies.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional Indian B2B AI workflow vendor. Original 40 crore pipeline * stipulated comparable .25 close conversion = 10 crore weighted pipeline, not signed bookings, revenue, ARR, cash or guarantee. Separate stage-mix case: 20 crore at .1 plus 20 at .4 gives 10; changing mix to 30/10 gives 7 despite unchanged total 40. Do not reuse aggregate .25 blindly. Correlation affects uncertainty, while linear expectation with correct marginal probabilities does not require independence. No variance or calibrated probability model estimated.</p><p>Monthly revenue 60 lakh, API variable 12 and other variable 18 give contribution 30 / margin .5 before fixed costs. API factor 2 gives 24 API / 42 total variable / 18 contribution / .3 margin. Change is -12 lakh and -20 percentage points, not -20 percent relative. Separate supplied fixed cost 15 gives operating remainder 15 baseline / 3 at factor 2 / zero at factor 2.25 / -9 at factor 3. Zero baseline API makes multiplier threshold undefined; negative threshold is not an achievable nonnegative factor. Revenue threshold 57 holds costs fixed, not a volume-based break-even forecast. No statutory gross margin, EBITDA, net profit or cash-flow claim.</p><p>Pipeline crore and monthly cost lakh have different units, periods and meanings; no unsupported mapping into monthly recognized revenue. Three one-page commercial, technical and legal draft notes separate claim, dated artifact/scope, contradiction, conditional impact, dependency and next check. Actual evidence/permission/accepted-owner rows blank. Missing contractor assignment is a potential rights gap, not automatic crime or backdated cure. Actual contract trigger, governing law and valid remediation need appropriate counsel. Proposed reviewer is not accepted owner. No actual data room, CRM, code access, dependency scan, pen test, supplier contact, contract edit, litigation clearance, account, investment or spend.</p><p>Seven URLs / six organizations / three source types. Complete returned SeedForge June 16, 2026, Consilium August 10, 2026 and Nicolas Cava written pages read, not images or linked studies/statutes. SeedForge stage, 10-times-load, AI-code and investor behavior claims are guidance/interpretations, not universal rules or causal validation; underlying GitClear/Verizon/IBM/BlackDuck/NBER studies not checked. Consilium is US-focused; Delaware 204/205 corporate-act remedies do not cure every IP/contract/employment/tax gap even there and are not imported into Indian law. Cava URL returns consultant overview, not checklist; advertised prices/track record not adopted or validated.</p><p>Selected CISA tool opening/response criteria/development/deployment portions read, not all 316 tasks/58 controls, interactive branches, exports or underlying guide. Supplier Yes/No/Partial/N/A responses require scope/support; self-report is not independent proof. Complete returned August 26, 2025 CISA announcement read, not linked guide/downloads/outcome study. US federal context is not Indian compliance law. Selected Salesforce forecasting definition/triangulation/model types read, not whole page/images/linked studies; AI accuracy and numerical bookings example not adopted. Complete returned OpenStax accounting key terms read, not entire chapter/book. Seven exact URLs in workbook Evidence sheet; no form/account used.</p><p>Twelve sheets / twelve PDF pages; 25 baseline formulas independently recomputed, 16 changed sets / 400 checks plus 25 restored, eight boundaries / 200, 20 invalid inputs / 500 family-isolation checks, six broken refs / six checks. Clean caches, no external links/macros. Negative contributions/remainders retained, zero denominators undefined, input families isolated. Original exercise/quiz/objectives retained. Authored LibreOffice only, not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner/account/field/legal/actual overall 10. No current comprehensive Indian-law, security, ownership, company or investment verdict.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"A vendor uses a third-party model API. Immediate implication?","o":["Every possible moat dies regardless of integration or data rights.","All proprietary-model claims are verified because API use is common.","Recheck model claims and workflow/data/distribution economics, not an automatic verdict.","The business is investable because its supplier is well known."],"a":2},{"q":"40 crore pipeline times stipulated 25% comparable close conversion. Amount?","o":["10 crore weighted pipeline, not booked revenue or a guarantee.","40 crore weighted pipeline, not booked revenue or a guarantee.","30 crore weighted pipeline, not booked revenue or a guarantee.","16 crore weighted pipeline, not booked revenue or a guarantee."],"a":0},{"q":"Monthly revenue 60 lakh, API 12, other variable cost 18. Contribution?","o":["48 lakh; margin 80%, before fixed and other costs.","42 lakh; margin 70%, before fixed and other costs.","18 lakh; margin 30%, before fixed and other costs.","30 lakh; margin 50%, before fixed and other costs."],"a":3},{"q":"API cost doubles to 24 lakh; other inputs unchanged. Contribution?","o":["30 lakh; margin 50%, before fixed and other costs.","18 lakh; margin 30%, before fixed and other costs.","24 lakh; margin 40%, before fixed and other costs.","6 lakh; margin 10%, before fixed and other costs."],"a":1},{"q":"A contract contains a change-of-control clause. Proper finding?","o":["The customer necessarily terminates after any new share issue.","Every acquisition transfers the contract unchanged.","Review trigger, consent, notice, remedies and transaction structure.","The clause matters only if its heading also mentions tax."],"a":2},{"q":"A contractor assignment appears missing. Proper response?","o":["Backdate a new assignment to the original work date.","Preserve history, check ownership/law, and seek valid documented correction.","Declare all code criminally obtained without reading terms.","Use Delaware ratification to cure every Indian IP defect."],"a":1},{"q":"No SOC 2, one region, monthly deploys at seed. Best review?","o":["Assess data, customer needs, critical controls, recovery and delivery trajectory.","Pass automatically; all seed security risk is immaterial.","Fail automatically; every seed company needs daily multi-region deploys.","Clear all risk once one certificate is obtained, regardless of scope."],"a":0},{"q":"Only one engineer can safely change a critical service. Risk?","o":["Scalability risk, inferred from staff count without load or architecture evidence.","IP ownership risk, inferred from critical knowledge without reading assignments.","Delivery-speed risk, inferred as actual missed commitments without a release record.","Key-person dependency; test documentation, backup skills and recovery."],"a":3},{"q":"An entity/MCA search finds no litigation entry. Conclusion?","o":["No pending, threatened or foreign claims exist.","None found in that search; broader claims and court coverage remain unverified.","All IP ownership and licenses are verified.","Any competitor allegation must be false."],"a":1},{"q":"How should a risk table handle uncertain probability?","o":["Convert severity labels into exact objective probabilities.","Use an unsupported most-common deal-killer rank as the sole rule.","Show uncertainty, evidence and blocking conditions; scores are not measured loss.","Treat an RBI partnership label as compliance proof without reading terms."],"a":2}]},"153":{"objectives":["Build an age-aligned synthetic D2C cohort triangle and contribution bridge; separate censoring, order status, hypothesis and evidence."],"videos":[{"id":"dhqB_tRaUF0","title":"Cohort analysis for startups: how to measure retention and growth","by":"The Startup CFO","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Speaker benchmarks and healthy-cohort/PMF/causal claims are not universal guarantees."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cohort analysis: GoingVC","url":"https://www.goingvc.com/post/a-guide-to-conducting-a-cohort-analysis-as-a-vc","note":"May 15, 2025 venture-capital cohort framework. Apply its definitions to the explicit fictional D2C case rather than treating it as company data, causal evidence or a current vendor quote."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Investor product metrics: Amplitude","url":"https://amplitude.com/blog/startup-fundraising-metrics","note":"Whole written 21 February 2023 article, not images, cited outcomes or current product tier. Investor views differ by model/stage. Dashboard access is optional, not a diligence entitlement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Retention calculations: Amplitude official docs","url":"https://amplitude.com/docs/analytics/charts/retention-analysis/retention-analysis-calculation","note":"Exact-interval and on-or-after events differ; incomplete intervals change eligibility. Check current prices and access terms on the provider's site; no purchase is required."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic dataset, not an actual Indian brand or accounting opinion. Construct 14 monthly acquisition cohorts T1-T14, each with 1,000 distinct first-purchase buyers, observed at the end of T14. Age 0 is acquisition month; age a occurs in calendar T(cohort+a). Future ages are N/A, not zero. Each listed monthly repeat buyer places exactly one retained, paid order at that age; a person can return in several ages, so adding monthly repeat counts does not count distinct-ever-repeat people. Cohorts 1-6 repeat counts at ages 1-6 are 250/180/150/120/100/80. Cohorts 7-14 use 200/140/100/80/60/40, except T10 uses 120/80/60/40/20/10. These are designed patterns, not causal findings. No age 7+ or full lifetime behavior is supplied.\nAll retained orders have price Rs 800 and variable cost Rs 560, except T10 initial orders at 20% off, price 640. No taxes, fixed costs, other adjustments or time discounting in this toy. T10 acquisition has 1,250 attempts: 1,000 delivered and retained first orders, 150 COD returns-to-origin, 100 cancelled before dispatch. The 1,000 successful buyers define its cohort. Each RTO costs 100 with zero revenue; each pre-dispatch cancellation costs/revenue zero. Charge the 15,000 RTO cost to T10 acquisition. All other cohorts have 1,000 retained initial orders and no extra failed attempts. Ordinary CAC 200 per acquired buyer; T10 CAC 250. These stipulated acquisition costs exclude the separately modeled fulfillment/RTO costs. No customer identities or real data requests.\nCompute M1/M3/M6 monthly repeat rates only for eligible cohorts. As of T14, 13/11/8 cohorts have matured to ages 1/3/6. T1 rates 25%/15%/8%; T7 rates 20%/10%/4%; T10 M6 is not observed. T7 is the first designed weaker cohort, but its age 1 weakness is first visible in calendar T8. This dataset does not prove an exact month where calendar topline growth masked decay; state that claim as unestablished rather than invent one.\nAt end-age 3, a baseline T1-T6 cohort contribution is 1,000*240+(250+180+150)*240=379,200, or 379.2 per original buyer; after CAC 179,200. T10 initial contribution 1,000*(640-560)-150*100=65,000; three repeat months add (120+80+60)*240=62,400; total 127,400, or 127.4/buyer; after CAC minus 122,600. These are cumulative observed-horizon contribution curves, not full LTV or dated cash flows. Baseline T1-T6 contribution clears CAC already at age 0; T10 does not by age 3, future unknown. At equal age 3, net sales per original buyer are 1,264 for baseline T1-T6 and 848 for T10. Cumulative net-sales/initial-net-sales ratios 158% and 132.5% are not SaaS NRR or repeat-person rates. No RTO or cancelled revenue is recognized in this stipulated toy; real GMV definitions and recognition require order/contract/control/refund review, not automatic fraud findings. Deliver the 14-row eligible triangle, status-to-revenue/cost bridge, horizon/CAC table and a scoped diligence paragraph. No mandatory live dashboard access, data-room connection or account change.<section aria-label=\"Native D153 practice\"><h3>Cohort age, status and contribution lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d153-cohort-horizons.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable cohort lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d153-cohort-horizons.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional D2C cohort lab. Fourteen acquisition cohorts T1-T14, each with 1,000 distinct retained first-order buyers, observed at end T14. Age 0 is acquisition; age a for cohort c occurs in calendar T(c+a). Counts are distinct monthly repeat buyers, exactly one retained paid order each. People may overlap across months, so 250+180+150=580 order events, not 580 distinct-ever-repeat people. Ages 0-6 supplied only; no age 7+ or lifetime extrapolation.</p><p>Triangle and exact-age 1/3/6 rate tables keep future intervals N/A, not zero. At end T14, eligible counts are 13/11/8; T10 age 6 is unknown. T1 rates .25/.15/.08 and T7 .2/.1/.04. T7 age 1 weakness is first visible in T8, not acquisition T7. No exact calendar month where topline masked decay is established. Changing cutoff to T12 gives eligible 11/9/6 and T10 age 3 N/A. Monthly aggregate counts cannot establish KM survival or time to second order.</p><p>Status bridge retains original T10 1,250 attempts = 1,000 retained + 150 RTO + 100 pre-dispatch cancellations. Initial sales 640,000 minus retained variable 560,000 minus RTO 15,000 = 65,000 contribution; after CAC 250,000 = -185,000. RTO cost 100 with zero revenue; cancellations zero cost/revenue, stipulated only. Ordinary price 800/cost 560/CAC 200; T10 initial price 640/CAC 250, repeat price 800. CAC excludes modeled fulfillment/RTO costs. No taxes, fixed costs, other adjustments or discounting.</p><p>Cumulative through age 3: baseline contribution 379,200, net after CAC 179,200, 379.2 per original buyer and 1,264 sales per buyer. T10: 127,400/-122,600/127.4/848. Cumulative-to-initial sales ratios 1.58/1.325 are not SaaS NRR or repeat-person rates. Baseline clears CAC at age 0; T10 does not by age 3, future unknown. RTO cost 200 separately gives T10 initial 50,000, age 3 contribution 112,400, net -137,600 while repeat rates stay unchanged. These are observed-horizon sums, not full LTV, cash flows, profit, guaranteed payback, current accounting clearance or campaign causality.</p><p>Seven URLs/six organizations/three types. Complete returned written GoingVC May 15, 2025, Amplitude February 21, 2023 investor article and current retention docs, Yelp February 20, 2020, EcomToolkit September 9, 2026, NIST censoring and OpenStax key terms read, not images/linked resources/underlying papers, replicated simulations or current product tiers. Investor views/PMF claims are contextual, not universal. Amplitude opening by-point wording conflicts with explicit on-day-X-or-later numerator; follow the explicit event definition, not within-by-X. Workbook uses exactly-age monthly activity. Yelp 12-16% speedup is context-specific, not adopted as forecast. KM not fit to this aggregate toy; NIST reliability context is conceptual, not a retail dropout model. No service contact or dashboard account.</p><p>Eleven sheets/eleven PDF pages; 174 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 16 changed sets/2,784 checks plus 174 restored; eight boundaries/1,392; 20 invalid/3,480 family-isolation; six broken references. Clean caches/no external links/macros. Future intervals N/A, zero observed denominator undefined, negative contribution retained. Actual evidence/authority/accepted owner blank. All eleven PDF pages inspected; long pattern sequence wrapping fixed and changed pages rechecked. Original objectives/exercise/quiz retained. Authored LibreOffice only, not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner/account/field/current law/actual overall 10. No actual brand/customer records, private upload, analytics invitation, accounting clearance, campaign, account or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"At end T14, which cohorts are eligible for an age 6 comparison?","o":["T1-T14; fill future ages with zero.","T1-T8; T9-T14 age 6 cells are N/A.","T1-T6; eligibility equals the requested age number.","T6-T14; compare only the newest cohorts."],"a":1},{"q":"T1 has 1,000 initial buyers and 150 age 3 repeat buyers. Monthly age 3 rate?","o":["25%.","40%.","58%.","15%."],"a":3},{"q":"T1 monthly repeat counts 250/180/150. What does their sum 580 establish?","o":["580 repeat-order events across three months, not 580 distinct people.","580 distinct repeat people because monthly counts are disjoint.","58% of buyers repeated every month.","The full lifetime repeat rate is 58%."],"a":0},{"q":"T7 is the first weaker designed cohort. When is its age 1 weakness observable?","o":["Calendar T7, before any repeat interval has elapsed.","Calendar T6, using a forecast as observed data.","Calendar T8, not at acquisition T7.","Calendar T14 only, after the complete dataset closes."],"a":2},{"q":"Baseline T1-T6 cohort end-age 3 contribution before CAC?","o":["Rs 240,000.","Rs 379,200.","Rs 139,200.","Rs 179,200."],"a":1},{"q":"T10 has 1,000 retained first orders at Rs 640, cost Rs 560 each, plus 150 RTO at Rs 100 each. Initial contribution?","o":["Rs 65,000.","Rs 80,000.","Rs 95,000.","Rs 140,000."],"a":0},{"q":"T10 end-age 3 contribution 127,400 and CAC 250,000. Net after CAC?","o":["Plus Rs 122,600; acquisition has paid back.","Minus Rs 250,000; ignore all contribution.","Plus Rs 127,400; acquisition cost is not relevant.","Minus Rs 122,600; future payback remains unknown."],"a":3},{"q":"NRR 110% and logo retention 80% on the same SaaS starting cohort establish?","o":["Only small customers churned; their original sizes are determined.","Every surviving account grew by exactly 30%.","Aggregate revenue expanded despite lost logos; sizes of lost logos remain unknown.","The business is profitable because revenue retention exceeds 100%."],"a":2},{"q":"A comparable age 3 rate improves in newer cohorts. Proper inference?","o":["An association to investigate with mix, seasonality, definitions and uncertainty.","Compare only first-month acquisition counts and attribute the rest to onboarding.","Compare unmatched ages and treat newer immature cohorts as complete.","Remove cancelled orders from one cohort only, then call the rate change causal."],"a":0},{"q":"GMV includes cancellations; a contract is signed before service. Proper review?","o":["Treat gross GMV definition alone as proof financial revenue is inflated.","Reconcile definitions and performance/refund terms before net-revenue conclusions.","Treat signing as earning all revenue under every contract.","Treat every delivery-over-time contract as a single automatic recognition rule."],"a":1}]},"154":{"objectives":["Transfer fund reporting, sourcing and diligence methods into an explicit discount-cohort stress case, without unrelated current-law memorization."],"videos":[],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cohort analysis: GoingVC","url":"https://www.goingvc.com/post/a-guide-to-conducting-a-cohort-analysis-as-a-vc","note":"May 15, 2025 venture-capital cohort framework. Apply its definitions to the explicit fictional D2C case rather than treating it as company data, causal evidence or a current vendor quote."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Retention calculations: Amplitude official docs","url":"https://amplitude.com/docs/analytics/charts/retention-analysis/retention-analysis-calculation","note":"Exact-interval and on-or-after events differ; incomplete intervals change eligibility. Check current prices and access terms on the provider's site; no purchase is required."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA reporting guidance","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/ILPA-Best-Practices-Quarterly-Reporting-Standards_Version-1.1.pdf","note":"Contractual call/default/expense/in-kind terms remain case-specific."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Unity: Ensemble VC","url":"https://www.ensemble.vc/research/unity-data-driven-sourcing","note":"Coverage/timing claims are attributed, not guaranteed."}],"exercise":"Private mixed review using fictional case facts, not real fund instructions, customer contacts or a report sent to LPs. Preserve the distinction between investment/fund cash, company revenue, customer events and model scores. Label laws, contracts and data access as unresolved case checks unless sourced within scope.\nD2C case: 1,000 original buyers per cohort. Ordinary order price 800, variable cost 560, contribution 240. Age 0 initial order; each monthly repeat buyer places exactly one order at ages 1/2/3. Baseline repeat counts 250/150/100, all full price. Festive first price 480 after 40% discount, unchanged variable 560, repeats 120/80/60 all at 800. No taxes, returns, fixed costs, collection delays or other costs in this exercise. CAC is separately 200/buyer baseline and 250 festive. End-age 3 includes age 0 plus the three repeat months, not only three purchase windows. Baseline contribution 360,000=360/buyer; after CAC 160,000. Festive contribution minus 17,600=minus 17.6/buyer; after CAC minus 267,600. Baseline clears CAC at age 0 since 240>=200. Festive does not clear by age 3; later behavior unknown. These are modeled contributions, not measured cash destruction, causal discount effects or full lifetime values. Repeat-month buyers may overlap; 26% sum does not establish 26% unique-ever-repeat.\nWrite a draft LP-summary sentence keeping revenue, discounted cohorts, full-price repeat and contribution separate. Draft a data-cut request, not a sent request: matched-age full-price repeats, order/refund/discount definitions and acquisition channels with eligible denominators and permitted access. Longer horizon adds evidence, not proof that all festive buyers become loyal. A sourcing screen based only on festive revenue may reward paid acquisition or discounting but misranking is a hypothesis needing outcomes, not inevitable model failure. A live dashboard is optional; an authorized scoped export may be enough.\nIndependent synthetic fund examples, not a single linked period: commitment 400, paid-in 100, new call 12 -> uncalled 288 if no other changes. A 10% LP call 1.2/unfunded 28.8. Separate NAV bridge: NAV 150+call 10+gain 2-distribution 12=150. Separate performance snapshot: paid-in 120/distributed 30/NAV 150 -> DPI .25/RVPI 1.25/TVPI 1.5; no dated IRR. Actual recallable distributions, fees, valuation and contractual notice/default/expense/in-kind terms need source documents. ILPA guidance is not automatic law. No real call, wire, LP letter, reference contact, commitment or share transfer. Use quiz errors actually observed to plan remediation; day score/confidence is not question-level diagnosis.<section aria-label=\"Native D154 practice\"><h3>Discount cohort and fund-compartment lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d154-mixed-cohort-review.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable mixed review (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d154-mixed-cohort-review.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional mixed review keeps company rupee cohorts separate from three independent fund snapshots in arbitrary same-scale units. End-age 3 includes initial age 0 plus repeat ages 1/2/3. Monthly repeat buyers may overlap; one retained paid order each at that age. No taxes, returns, fixed costs, collection delays or other adjustments. No real LP letter, call, wire, reference, dashboard account or spending.</p><p>Original baseline 1,000 buyers at 800 price/560 cost/200 CAC, repeats 250/150/100: initial contribution 240,000; full-price repeat 120,000; total 360,000 or 360/buyer; after CAC 160,000. Festive initial 480 after 40% discount/560 cost/250 CAC, repeats 120/80/60 at 800: initial -80,000, repeat 62,400, total -17,600 or -17.6/buyer, net -267,600. Sales 1,200,000 versus 688,000 are not contributions or fund NAV. Repeat events .50/.26 are not distinct-ever-repeat or SaaS NRR.</p><p>Age paths baseline 240,000/300,000/336,000/360,000 versus festive -80,000/-51,200/-32,000/-17,600. Baseline covers CAC at age 0; festive not through age 3, later unknown. Flags test listed endpoints only, not exact first crossing under arbitrary non-monotone edits. CAC 300 alone leaves festive age 3 contribution -17,600, changes net to -317,600. Separate repeat price 700 at original CAC 250 gives repeat contribution 36,400, total -43,600/net -293,600. No causal discount effect, full LTV, measured cash destruction, profit or guaranteed payback.</p><p>Independent call: commitment 400/prior paid 100/new call 12 gives unfunded 300 to 288; LP 10% current call 1.2/ending unfunded 28.8. Separate NAV: 150+10+2-12=150. Separate performance: paid 120/distributed 30/NAV 150 gives DPI .25/RVPI 1.25/TVPI 1.5; identity zero, no dated IRR. Call 12 is not silently linked to NAV contribution 10 or paid 120. Negative computed NAV/unfunded retained as investigation exceptions, zero paid-in ratios undefined. Classifications stipulated, not actual contract or current law.</p><p>One-page private draft LP summary and unsent data-cut request separate discounted acquisition, full-price repeats, contribution, fund cash and NAV. A scoped authorized export may suffice; no dashboard entitlement. Revenue-only sourcing may reward discounts/acquisition mix; misranking hypothesis needs outcomes, not guaranteed failure. Day score/confidence cannot diagnose measured weakest question-level skill. Actual evidence/observed errors/authority/accepted-owner plan blank. Reference nonanswer not founder verdict or permission to off-list contacts. Call/default/expense/in-kind/IFSC and contract triggers need actual governing documents, facts and current scoped review, not universal rules or backdated cure.</p><p>Seven URLs/five organizations/three types. D154 complete returned written GoingVC May 15, 2025, Amplitude February 21, 2023 investor article/current retention docs and OpenStax key terms reused without redundant fetch, not images/linked studies/current tiers/whole accounting chapter. Exact-age versus on-or-after definitions stay separate. New complete returned Ensemble January 3, 2025 written page read, not platform image/data/outcomes; world-leading/never-miss/coverage/precision are unvalidated vendor assertions. Selected ILPA 2016 v1.1 opening/disclaimer/summary-letter/PCAP/commitment/NAV/metrics/sample-label passages and January 2025 v2 guidance opening/key changes/continuity/NAV roll/conformity/implementation read, not full PDFs/external templates/visual PDF verification. Historical sample amounts not adopted. Updated guidance targets go-forward Q1 2026 adoption for specified funds, not universal legal deadline or current-fund compliance verdict. Supplemental guidance consistent with LPA/accounting, not permission or prescribed expense coverage.</p><p>Ten sheets/ten PDF pages; 54 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 20 changed sets/1,080 checks plus 54 restored; eight boundaries/432; 20 invalid/1,080 family-isolation; six broken references. Clean caches/no external links/macros. All ten PDF pages inspected; original objectives/exercise/all 25 quiz retained. Authored LibreOffice only, not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner/account/field/current-law/actual overall 10. No customer identities, real LP accounts, sent letter/call/wire/contact, private upload, account or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Baseline end-age 3 contribution before CAC?","o":["Rs 240,000.","Rs 120,000.","Rs 160,000.","Rs 360,000."],"a":3},{"q":"Festive first-order contribution per buyer, price 480/cost 560?","o":["Minus Rs 80.","Rs 144.","Rs 240.","Minus Rs 320."],"a":0},{"q":"Festive repeat contribution per original buyer through age 3?","o":["Rs 26.","Rs 124.8.","Rs 62.4.","Rs 240."],"a":2},{"q":"Festive cohort contribution before CAC through age 3?","o":["Rs 62,400.","Minus Rs 17,600.","Rs 80,000.","Minus Rs 267,600."],"a":1},{"q":"Festive contribution after CAC 250 per buyer?","o":["Minus Rs 17.6 per buyer.","Plus Rs 232.4 per buyer.","Minus Rs 250 per buyer.","Minus Rs 267.6 per buyer."],"a":3},{"q":"When does baseline modeled cumulative contribution first clear CAC 200?","o":["Age 1, because initial orders never count.","Age 3, because that is the review horizon.","Age 0, since initial contribution 240 exceeds 200.","Never, since total fixed costs are missing."],"a":2},{"q":"Festive fails payback by age 3. What follows?","o":["Future payback is unknown without later contribution evidence.","It can never pay back under any future behavior.","Age 4 payback is guaranteed by any positive repeat.","Refunding CAC would establish organic customer retention."],"a":0},{"q":"Repeat rates 12/8/6% across months establish what?","o":["260 unique people, since months cannot share buyers.","26% of buyers returned every month.","All repeat customers have the same acquisition channel.","260 order events per 1,000 initial buyers, with person overlap unknown."],"a":3},{"q":"End-age 3 includes which purchase windows?","o":["Only ages 1,2,3; exclude acquisition contribution.","Age 0 initial plus ages 1,2,3.","Calendar months 1,2,3 regardless of acquisition date.","Four repeat months and the initial order."],"a":1},{"q":"Which LP-summary wording preserves the case distinction?","o":["Festive revenue proves organic retention and positive lifetime value.","Acquisition losses are fund NAV write-downs of the same amount.","Discounted acquisition cohorts showed negative age 3 contribution after CAC; later full-price behavior is unverified.","An unobserved future repeat rate is zero by definition."],"a":2},{"q":"Which comparison tests the festive-loyalty hypothesis?","o":["Compare older baseline cumulative sales with festive acquisition-month sales.","Matched-age full-price repeat/contribution with discount, channel and refund definitions.","Compare full-price repeaters only and omit all nonrepeat buyers.","Compare channels after changing buyer identity rules in only one cohort."],"a":1},{"q":"A sourcing model ranks only festive revenue. Proper concern?","o":["It may reward discount/acquisition mix; test ranking outcomes and costs.","It necessarily misses every good company in every sector.","Revenue rankings are calibrated investment probabilities.","Public revenue claims authorize private GST retrieval."],"a":0},{"q":"Commitment 400, prior paid 100, new call 12, no other changes. Uncalled?","o":["300.","312.","388.","288."],"a":3},{"q":"A 10% LP in that toy has current call and uncalled amounts?","o":["12 and 288.","1.2 and 28.8.","1.0 and 30.0.","2.8 and 12.0."],"a":1},{"q":"NAV 150+contribution 10+gain 2-distribution 12 equals?","o":["162.","138.","150.","172."],"a":2},{"q":"Paid 120/distributed 30/NAV 150: DPI/RVPI/TVPI?","o":[".25/1.25/1.5.","1.25/.25/1.5.",".20/1.00/1.2.",".25/1.50/1.75."],"a":0},{"q":"What establishes the actual capital-call notice period and default remedy?","o":["One universal 10-15-business-day rule for every fund.","An ILPA template overrides all negotiated terms.","Governing documents, applicable law and valid notice facts.","Any LP delay automatically causes full forfeiture."],"a":2},{"q":"An unusual travel expense is charged to a fund. Proper review?","o":["Every private-flight charge is automatically illegal.","Any manager approval makes the expense permitted.","Put all expenses into NAV without examining allocation.","Check agreement, allocation, purpose, evidence and conflict process."],"a":3},{"q":"An LP receives an in-kind distribution. Proper evaluation?","o":["Review contract, liquidity, custody, valuation, tax and recipient constraints.","All LPs reject every in-kind distribution.","All in-kind transfers are tax free and immediately liquid.","The transfer creates permission to sell the assets for the LP."],"a":0},{"q":"An IFSC label and tax proposal appear in a memo. Proper treatment?","o":["GIFT location settles all domestic and foreign taxes.","Scoped primary-law and case review, not blanket tax-neutrality.","A historical proposal clears every old round.","An IFSC label removes instrument and investor eligibility checks."],"a":1},{"q":"A reference declines an investment-opinion question. What is known?","o":["A reliable negative verdict on the founder.","A reliable positive verdict because there is no criticism.","Consent to contact the reference's private colleagues.","Only a nonanswer; policy, knowledge or conflict may explain it."],"a":3},{"q":"An API-dependent vendor is under review. Best next step?","o":["Reject every API-dependent company regardless of other facts.","Accept proprietary-model claims without architecture evidence.","Check workflow/data claims, dependency, contracts and contribution stress.","Assume supplier marketing verifies this company's margins."],"a":2},{"q":"A contract heading says change of control. Proper inference?","o":["Every new financing terminates the customer contract.","Read actual triggers, consent, remedies and transaction facts.","Every acquisition leaves every contract unchanged.","A heading establishes the exact compensation payable."],"a":1},{"q":"A learner got the day score but no item-level error history. Remediation?","o":["Use observed answers or provisional practice, not a claimed measured weakest skill.","Declare calibration the weakest skill from the overall score.","Infer every conceptual error from confidence alone.","Treat passing as independent proof of applied mastery."],"a":0},{"q":"What is the authorized review output?","o":["A sent LP letter because a reporting sentence was requested.","A bank wire because the case contains a capital call.","Covert references because diligence benefits from off-list sources.","Private synthetic analysis and drafts, not real LP/client/account actions."],"a":3}]},"155":{"objectives":["Design a resolvable forecast journal and consent-based reference protocol; distinguish Brier loss, calibration, evidence and hindsight."],"videos":[{"id":"g4zzkuFyI18","title":"5 Ingredients for the Perfect Investment | Jeff Horing Interview","by":"Invest Like The Best","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Horing growth/software and sourcing views are attributed experience, not a calibration study or universal five-factor rule. Current returns, fund sizes and anecdotal statistics not independently verified."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Brier score: official scikit-learn documentation","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.brier_score_loss.html","note":"For binary events, use mean((p-y)^2), with probabilities on a 0-1 scale. Multiclass scoring conventions can differ."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Calibration and scoring: scikit-learn","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html","note":"Whole written documentation, not underlying papers/charts. Brier combines reliability, resolution and uncertainty; a lower score alone does not prove better calibration."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Reference checking investors: CRV","url":"https://www.crv.com/content/reference-check-questions-for-founders","note":"It is founders checking investors, not empirical proof of a founder-check technique. Hedging/pauses as negative signals and five-to-seven-call target are author views, not universal findings or contact permission."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"VC bias review: abstract only","url":"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4818073","note":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case. Written 10 February 2024, posted May 2024. No specific effect size, automatic overconfidence or best-VC hit-rate claim adopted."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Metaculus calibration adjustment analysis","url":"https://www.metaculus.com/notebooks/43356/calibration-adjustment-analysis/","note":"Particular 2025 bot tournament/train-test experiment, not a guarantee for 20 human forecasts or the native platform scoring rule."}],"exercise":"Private training pack, no actual investment decision, calls, quotes, public forecasts or calendar changes. Draft 20 binary prediction slots with precise yes/no event, deadline/timezone, resolver/source, treatment of amendments/cancellation/missing evidence and void criteria before outcomes are known. Select events only when they are observable within the chosen horizon; fundraising rumors or regulatory headlines need exact definitions. No universal startup base rate is supplied. Record comparable population/date/denominator and uncertainty before adjusting a prior. A 9/10 confidence label is not automatically a calibrated 90% outcome probability or a known below 90% accuracy.\nKeep original timestamped probability and rationale; append updates with their evidence rather than overwrite history. Prespecify whether scoring uses a fixed checkpoint or updates with time weights; do not cherry-pick the best update after resolution. Unresolved/void is not automatically y=0. A 20-slot exercise is not 20 scored outcomes today and does not create a future monitor. Any actual follow-up schedule would need its own authorized scope.\nFor the explicitly defined binary Brier loss, p is 0-1 and y is 0/1, score=mean((p-y)^2), lower better, range 0-1. Four synthetic resolved questions with p=.8/.6/.3/.1 and y=1/0/1/0 give losses .04/.36/.49/.01, mean .225. A fixed .5 forecast on those same questions scores .25; skill 1-.225/.25=.10 relative to that specific baseline. This small synthetic set does not establish human skill or calibration. Calibration asks whether outcome frequencies match forecast probabilities over adequate comparable observations; discrimination/resolution, base rates and sample dependence also matter. Compare scores on the same eligible questions and scoring convention. Native Metaculus platform points are not assumed to equal Brier; its historical primer explicitly points to later rules. The offline exercise requires no account or leaderboard; check current provider terms before using an online feature.\nReference protocol: eight fictional role slots across former boss/report/cofounder/customer, with relationship, time span, access limits, permission and selection bias. Draft four blank interview-note templates using shared core questions plus context-sensitive follow-ups. Ask for concrete firsthand examples and counterevidence; a bounded concern question may invite detail but cannot guarantee candor. Do not infer a negative verdict from pauses, hedging or refusing a financial opinion. Record actual quotes only if a real authorized interview occurred; otherwise leave blank. A premortem makes plausible failure paths discussable, not unbiased truth. Where decision-makers disagree, identify testable claims, cost, timing, information value and reversibility; the cheapest evidence is not always decisive. No outreach, background check, investment recommendation or fabricated completed work.<section aria-label=\"Native D155 practice\"><h3>Forecast history and comparable scoring lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d155-forecast-journal.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable forecast journal (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d155-forecast-journal.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional offline forecasting lab. Eight toy rows include four resolved, two unresolved and two void. Codes 1/0/2 respectively; unresolved/void outcomes truly blank, never no. All original/alternative/reference probabilities numeric 0-1, y binary only when resolved. Strict declared common-set policy: one invalid row blocks aggregates instead of silent exclusion. Resolved status does not authenticate a source or pre-outcome timestamp.</p><p>Original p .8/.6/.3/.1 with y 1/0/1/0 gives binary Brier losses .04/.36/.49/.01, mean .225. Same-question fixed .5 baseline .25, relative skill .10. Appended alternative .9/.4/.6/.2 gives .01/.16/.16/.04, mean .0925/skill .63. Difference .1325 descriptive, not human skill or calibration proof. Mean original p .45 versus yes fraction .50 gives gap -.05, not full calibration. Twenty blank slots are not twenty scored outcomes or a created future monitor.</p><p>Change only Q2 original p to .9: original loss .3375/skill -.35; alternative unchanged .0925. Separately mark Q4 unresolved and clear outcome: n3, original .296666667, alternative .11, reference .25. No resolved rows leaves means undefined; perfect reference zero loss leaves relative skill undefined, not infinite skill. Negative skill retained. Binary mean square on 0-1 differs from unhalved/multiclass 0-2 conventions and Metaculus notebook per-option MC normalization; not assumed current platform points.</p><p>Twenty blank design slots specify observable yes/no event, deadline/timezone, resolver/source/cutoff, amendment/cancellation/missing-evidence and void rules, original timestamp/p/rationale and appended updates. Choose checkpoint before outcomes; do not cherry-pick each best historical update. Editable workbook is not a tamper-proof log or timestamp verifier. No time-weighted scoring, source resolver, event retrieval, real calibration fit, calendar change, account or future monitoring implemented. 9/10 confidence not automatically p=.9; no universal startup base rate or sample-size threshold. Lower Brier mixes reliability/resolution/uncertainty, not calibration alone. Fit and evaluation data must be separated for a meaningful held-out test.</p><p>Eight fictional reference-role slots and four blank note templates: relationship/time span/firsthand access/selection bias, permission/audience, concrete example, counterevidence and unknowns. Shared core plus context follow-ups cannot guarantee candor. Pauses/hedges/refusing a financial opinion are not negative verdicts. CRV is founders checking investors, not empirical founder-check technique validation. No actual people, interviews, quotes, outreach/backchannel contact, permission or accepted owner invented. Premortem hypotheses are not measured failure probabilities. Actual evidence/learner-response/authority plan blank.</p><p>Six URLs/five organizations/four types. Complete returned written scikit-learn Brier API/calibration docs read, not source code, gallery/figure pixels or underlying papers. CRV April 21, 2026 complete returned written read; five-seven call target/hedged-answer heuristic are author views, not universal findings or contact permission. SSRN complete returned abstract/metadata only: written February 10, 2024, posted May 7/revised May 9, not 63-page paper or studies. Complete returned Metaculus written/table read, not figures/code/data/replication: 2025 Q1-Q2 bot tournament, 500-forecast filter heuristic, temporal 70/30 split; reported improvements not guaranteed for twenty human slots. Complete returned Forecasting Wiki written summary read; it imports Wikipedia, not independent primary validation. Its lower-loss-implies-better-calibration wording conflicts with official decomposition caution and is not adopted. Skill formula applied to fixed reference, not treating every skill ratio as strictly proper.</p><p>Ten sheets/ten PDF pages; 53 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 20 changed sets/1,060 checks plus 53 restored; eight boundaries/424; 20 invalid/1,060 checks; six broken references. Clean saved caches/no external links/macros. All ten PDF pages inspected; journal headers separated and updated page rechecked. Original objectives/exercise/all ten quiz retained. Authored LibreOffice only, not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner/account/field/actual overall 10. No public forecasts, actual outcomes, reference calls, investment recommendation, real account, calendar, monitoring or spending.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Four toy probabilities .8/.6/.3/.1, outcomes 1/0/1/0. Binary Brier loss?","o":[".25.",".45.",".225.",".10."],"a":2},{"q":"Same four questions, fixed .5 baseline loss .25, model .225. Relative skill?","o":[".10 relative to the stated .25 baseline.",".90 relative to the stated .25 baseline.",".025 relative to the stated .25 baseline.","1.10 relative to the stated .25 baseline."],"a":0},{"q":"A prediction cannot yet be resolved. Proper scoring treatment?","o":["Resolve no because no positive evidence has arrived yet.","Drop every poor-looking forecast but retain lucky unresolved ones.","Choose the outcome after seeing which produces the best score.","Keep unresolved or apply prewritten void rules; do not set y=0."],"a":3},{"q":"What does lower Brier loss alone establish?","o":["Better calibration regardless of resolution and base-rate changes.","Lower loss on the comparable scored set, not calibration improvement alone.","Greater investment profit under every decision policy.","A reliable skill ranking across unrelated question populations."],"a":1},{"q":"A partner reports confidence 9/10. Long-run accuracy?","o":["Exactly 90% because the number is normalized.","Necessarily below 90% because every expert is overconfident.","Unknown without defined probabilities, outcomes and track record.","Exactly 50% because investment decisions are binary."],"a":2},{"q":"A late forecast update is more accurate. Which journal rule is defensible?","o":["Overwrite the original so the final file looks more accurate.","Select the best historical update after the outcome.","Treat all updates as independent evidence about forecasting skill.","Retain originals and updates; score by a prespecified checkpoint/time policy."],"a":3},{"q":"What does a bounded concern question guarantee?","o":["Honest criticism from anyone asked directly.","Nothing about candor; seek examples, context and counterevidence.","A negative finding whenever the reference hesitates.","Consent to share a reference's private statements publicly."],"a":1},{"q":"Why use a common reference-question core with tailored follow-ups?","o":["Improve comparability while retaining relationship and context differences.","Ask identical questions only and omit context to avoid variation in the notes.","Use an aggregate rating without source relationship or firsthand examples.","Tailor every question to confirm the leading investment thesis."],"a":0},{"q":"A premortem lists reasons a deal could fail. Proper status?","o":["Measured probabilities because partners agreed on labels.","A complete cure for groupthink and optimism.","Scenario hypotheses to investigate, not evidence those failures will occur.","The actual cause of a future loss, known today."],"a":2},{"q":"Two partners disagree. Good next step?","o":["Buy the cheapest datum even if unrelated to the decision.","Convert seniority directly into an outcome probability.","Average opinions and call the result a verified base rate.","Identify decision-relevant facts and weigh evidence value, cost, delay and reversibility."],"a":3}]},"156":{"objectives":["Compare financing instruments and model three explicit rounds; distinguish US SAFE terms, conditional conversion and unresolved Indian legal applicability."],"videos":[{"id":"5jSH7keeLjY","title":"Early-stage Investment Instruments in Startups | SAFEs and Convertible Notes","by":"Osage University Partners","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Some spoken/transcribed numbers are inconsistent; independent arithmetic and actual forms govern this exercise, not US-to-India legal assumptions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC cap-only SAFE: actual US form","url":"https://bookface-static.ycombinator.com/assets/ycdc/Postmoney%20Safe%20-%20Valuation%20Cap%20Only%20-%20FINAL-f2a64add6d21039ab347ee2e7194141a4239e364ffed54bad0fe9cf623bf1691.docx","note":"Specific US template, not Indian permission or an executed agreement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC MFN SAFE: actual US form","url":"https://bookface-static.ycombinator.com/assets/ycdc/Postmoney%20Safe%20-%20MFN%20Only%20-%20FINAL-2bc87fa3d2ec5072a60d653aec9a885fb43879781e44341fa720a8e7d1cc42ff.docx","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Section 3 notice/election/amendment and definitions control this example; landing-page automatic-best-terms shorthand is incomplete."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"YC post-money SAFE user guide","url":"https://bookface-static.ycombinator.com/assets/ycdc/SAFE%20User%20Guide-a47c6588327d73aa2799e61ed7c2cae9f1a0ee9acfa9c43b62039dc06e715832.pdf","note":"Low/near-cap valuation, new money and pool dilution qualify ratio shortcuts; versions matter."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Foreign investment NDI Rules: RBI official text","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewContent.aspx?Id=5083","note":"For an actual Indian issuance, identify the applicable instrument, current law, tax treatment and approvals before relying on a US template."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Startup India playbook: selected convertible-note passage","url":"https://www.startupindia.gov.in/content/dam/startupindia/corporate-partner/Startup-Playbook-DPIIT-Recognised-Startups-in-India.pdf","note":"Selected note/deposit summary and adjacent benefits only, not full playbook or underlying operative deposit rules. A benefit summary is not a legal opinion."}],"exercise":"Private hypothetical Bengaluru startup financing map, not a term sheet, legal/tax opinion or fundraise. Keep issuer jurisdiction, investor residence/beneficial ownership, startup eligibility, instrument terms, sector, valuation, approvals, reporting, tax and deposit treatment as separate case checks. Neither the iSAFE label nor a US template proves Indian legality; do not call every SAFE universally banned by SEBI or assume an adapted name solves compliance. Selected official RBI NDI definitions/rule 18 distinguish foreign-investor startup convertible-note eligibility, single-tranche threshold, approval and conversion conditions; this is not the full Companies Act, deposit, securities, FEMA, tax or later circular review. Case legality remains unscored. No actual entity, investor, address, wire, filing, signature, outreach or purchase.\nEconomics-only three-round illustration, crore: start founders 100%; round 1 new 1.5 at 13.5 pre/15 post sells 10%; seed new 8 at 32 pre/40 post sells 20%; A new 40 at 160 pre/200 post sells 20%. All pre-money figures include all prior as-converted holdings. No options, convertible overhang, fees, secondary sale, participating rights, anti-dilution or pro rata purchases. Final founders 57.6%, round 1 investors 6.4%, seed 16%, A 20%. These are stipulated valuations, not realistic-market evidence or a recommended instrument. Add a separate hypothetical 10% post-A option issuance borne pro rata by all existing holders: founders 51.84/round 1 5.76/seed 14.4/A 18/pool 10. This ordering differs from an investor-protected pre-money pool top-up; model actual terms rather than reuse the same formula.\nUS-form comparison: YC cap-only SAFE is future equity, no interest or maturity, with defined financing/liquidity/dissolution rights and priority. No maturity does not mean no possible proceeds; entitlement is not guaranteed collection. The actual cap-only form uses greater share count from purchase/standard price or purchase/Safe Price, with defined capitalization. Cap ratios are not universally final ownership. Under a separate cap-binding toy with no other securities/options, checks 1 crore at 20 crore post-money cap and 1 crore at 25 crore cap imply 5%+4%=9% immediately before priced new money. A subsequent round selling 20% dilutes them to 4% and 3.2%, founders 72.8%; final ownership is not locked forever. A lower/near-cap round may yield more shares; exact five-check stack ownership cannot be computed from only total 5 crore and cap endpoints 20-50 crore.\nA 20% discount on new-money share price 100 gives 80, not a discount proportional to elapsed time unless an actual term says so. A hypothetical note 1 crore at 8% simple for 18 months has interest .12 crore and total 1.12; if both convert at 80/share, 1.4 lakh shares. Compounding, conversion of interest and maturity remedies must come from terms, not assumed punitive conversion or control concessions.\nActual YC MFN form Section 3 requires notice, investor written election within its stated window, then amendment to the later instrument, with defined exclusions; it is not automatic cherry-picking of all later benefits. Current landing-page shorthand differs from the retrieved form/guide. Read version and operative terms. Pro rata is an optional agreed right and needs new money, not free protection. US QSBS, accredited-investor and governance clauses are not imported as Indian rules. Deliver an instrument-comparison/evidence-gap table and explicit cap-table walks, not completed contracts or a live financing recommendation.<section aria-label=\"Native D156 practice\"><h3>Financing economics and instrument evidence lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d156-financing-economics.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable financing economics lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d156-financing-economics.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional offline financing economics lab, not a term sheet, fundraise or legal/tax opinion. Three stipulated priced rounds in crore: 1.5 new/13.5 pre, 8/32, 40/160. Pre includes all prior as-converted holdings; no overhang, options, fees, secondary, pro rata or preference changes. After A founders 57.6%, round 1 6.4%, seed 16%, A 20%. A separate 10% post-A pool borne pro rata gives 51.84/5.76/14.4/18/10. Reserved pool is fully diluted math, not actual issued stock or grants.</p><p>Separate investor-protected-A pool scenario: no existing pool, final target 10% carved from pre-A holders while A stays 20%. Founders 50.4%, round 1 5.6%, seed 14%, A 20%, pool 10%; founder difference -1.44 percentage points from post-A pro-rata pool. Feasible pool must leave positive pre-A holder remainder. Existing-pool topups or differing term-sheet denominators need a new model. No actual hiring pool size or market valuation inferred.</p><p>Independent conditional cap-binding toy: checks 1 crore/20 crore and 1/25 imply 5%+4%=9% before priced new money. New money 20% gives SAFE 1 4%, SAFE 2 3.2%, founders 72.8%, new 20%. Arithmetic gate requires positive caps, total cap fractions below 1, new fraction below 1. These do not prove caps bind. No recursive actual-form solver; exact unknown five-check ownership not inferred from endpoint caps or average.</p><p>Separate supplied reconciled-capitalization comparison: purchase 1 crore, cap 20 crore, capitalization 1000000 shares gives Safe Price 200 rupees and 50000 shares. Supplied standard price 250 gives 40000 shares, choose 50000. Alternative standard 150 gives 66666.666667 shares, greater than 50000. Capitalization includes this and other converting securities as defined, not founder-only shares. Supplied standard price/capitalization not derived by this calculator; fixed-denominator sensitivity is not a solved low-round company or final ownership.</p><p>Note principal 1 crore, 8% annual simple, 18 months gives.12 interest/1.12 total.20% discount on 100/share gives 80; both principal and interest converting yields 140000 shares. Flag 0 excludes interest from shares =&gt; 125000, does not erase actual repayment obligation. At 24 months interest.16/shares 145000. Compounding, day count, rounding, maturity remedies and interest conversion come from actual terms, not assumed. Change A new 40 to 80 gives A 1/3, founders 48%before pool/43.2%post pool/40.8%protected pool. Cap 1 from 20 to 10 gives 14%combinedbefore/11.2%after. Static prose is baseline; refresh before changed export.</p><p>Actual retrieved YC cap-only form Sections 1-2: greater share count from standard-price and Safe-Price alternatives, defined Company Capitalization versus Liquidity Capitalization, event proceeds/priority, not current ordinary voting shares. No maturity does not mean no proceeds or guaranteed collection. MFN Section 3: notice/copy, investor written election within 10 days of receipt, then matching amendment with defined exclusions. Current landing automatic-best-terms shorthand conflicts; operative document/version matters. No automatic cherry-picking. Pro rata needs optional agreed right and new cash.</p><p>Source coverage seven URLs/three publishers/three types. Complete returned paragraph text of actual cap-only/MFN US forms read; fresh table/textbox/pixels not rechecked, prior rendered evidence reused. Current YC guide returned QuickStart/intro/selected Q&amp; A A-F throughF 6 read, not fresh full 33 pageappendices/pixels. Complete returned current YC landing written and Carta Aug 20, 2026 option-pool article, older Carta product definitions, FounderDecisions explanation read, not images/linked benchmarks/interactive tools/underlying code. Carta US 409 A/Rule 701 and market percentages not imported into India; older product formula label/dilution definition imperfections not adopted. Generic convertible pool shorthand does not override actual form capitalization. No service execution/current product access claim.</p><p>Twelve sheets/twelve PDF pages; 59 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 23 changed sets 1357 checks + 59 restored, 12 boundaries 708, 20 invalid 1180, six broken references. Independent numeric families, undefined infeasible pools/cap sums/zero-price and clean saved caches/no macros/external links. Actual evidence/accepted legal reviewer/permission fields blank. Actual Indian issuer/investor residence/beneficial ownership/startup eligibility/sector/deposit/FEMA/valuation/approvals/reporting/tax/current amendments unresolved, legality unscored. Selected prior RBI/StartupIndia text not a full current law review. Original objectives/exercise/all ten quiz retained. Authored LibreOffice only, not native Excel/Sheets/full source AV/independent learner/account/field/actual overall 10. No actual issue, signature, wire, outreach, account, calendar, monitoring or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"What does the actual YC SAFE form provide before conversion?","o":["A contractual future-equity interest with defined event rights, not current ordinary stock or a loan.","A loan with mandatory interest and maturity under every version.","Current voting common stock immediately on signing.","No rights to any proceeds because no maturity exists."],"a":0},{"q":"Two cap-binding post-money checks 1/20 and 1/25, before priced new money. Total implied ownership?","o":["10%.","8%.","9%.","4.5%."],"a":2},{"q":"Those 9% SAFE holdings face new money buying 20%, no pool or other changes. Combined final share?","o":["9%.","7.2%.","11.25%.","29%."],"a":1},{"q":"Five checks total 5 crore, cap endpoints 20-50 crore, individual allocations unknown. Ownership?","o":["Exactly 25%, using all money at the lowest cap.","Exactly 10%, using all money at the highest cap.","Exactly 14.29%, using the arithmetic mean cap.","Not enough information for an exact sum or universal 14-18% range."],"a":3},{"q":"A cap-only SAFE enters a lower/near-cap priced round. Proper conversion check?","o":["Compare actual standard-price and Safe-Price share calculations under defined capitalization.","Use only the cap ratio before checking whether the standard-price alternative yields more shares.","Exclude all converting securities from capitalization regardless of the definition.","Include every future pool increase in the current SAFE denominator without reading the exception."],"a":0},{"q":"Actual retrieved YC MFN Section 3. Correct mechanics?","o":["Automatic copying of every later side-letter benefit without an election.","Notice, written election within the stated window, then matching amendment subject to definitions.","Unlimited cherry-picking of only favorable terms from several instruments.","An automatic board seat whenever later investors receive governance rights."],"a":1},{"q":"Three priced rounds sell 10%,20%,20%; no other dilution. Founders final?","o":["50%.","64%.","57.6%.","72%."],"a":2},{"q":"A separate 10% post-A pool is borne pro rata by all existing holders. Founders?","o":["47.6%.","57.6%.","64%.","51.84%."],"a":3},{"q":"20% discount on price 100; note 1 crore+8% simple 18 months both convert. Price/shares?","o":["80 per share; 1.25 lakh shares.","80 per share; 1.4 lakh shares.","20 per share; 5.6 lakh shares.","100 per share; 1.12 lakh shares."],"a":1},{"q":"What establishes suitability for the hypothetical Indian issuer?","o":["Actual current Indian rules and case terms reviewed with qualified advice, not the label alone.","The iSAFE name automatically establishes FEMA and deposit compliance.","YC's US popularity removes Indian issuer/investor conditions.","A US tax characterization proves Indian tax neutrality."],"a":0}]},"157":{"objectives":["Model an explicit three-round exit waterfall with seniority, capped participation and mutually consistent conversion elections."],"videos":[{"id":"aZm_KNYUWa0","title":"Liquidation Preferences and Participating Preferred Stock: How VC Terms Affect Deals","by":"Mergers & Inquisitions / Breaking Into Wall Street","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Simplified min/max thresholds and cap bases are not universal; use the explicit joint-election toy and actual terms."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Chasing Waterfalls: Use Your Term Sheet to Calculate Exit Distributions (HSBC Innovation Banking)","url":"https://www.hsbcinnovationbanking.com/en/resources/chasing-waterfalls-how-to-use-your-term-sheet-to-calculate-exit-distributions","note":"A 1x preference is not guaranteed cash when proceeds are insufficient. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Liquidation Waterfall: How Exit Proceeds Are Distributed (Equity List - India)","url":"https://www.equitylist.co/blog-post/liquidation-waterfall","note":"Generic all-convertibles-must-convert and simple single-class threshold claims require actual instrument/other-class qualification."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Eqvista","url":"https://eqvista.com","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Offline model works; no signup/upload."}],"exercise":"Private economics-only toy for a hypothetical Indian startup, not an opinion on enforceable CCPS, insolvency priority, tax or a live sale. Start founder common 100%; Seed 10 crore at 40 post buys 25%; A 30 at 150 post buys 20%; B 100 at 500 post buys 20%. No pool, warrants, convertibles, dividends, debt, fees, escrow, taxes or secondary. Final as-converted weights founder 48%, Seed 16%, A 16%, B 20%. E is distributable equity cash after any excluded liabilities, not enterprise value. Explicit preference ranking B senior to A senior to Seed, not inferred from round chronology. Seed and B 1x non-participating; A 1x participating, total preference-plus-participation capped at 60 crore (2x its 30 investment), with optional conversion that removes both preference and cap. Each series elects as a block. No forced conversion or special vote rules in this toy.\nFor any election vector, pay preferences of nonconverted series in B/A/Seed order, each limited by available cash. Converted series waive preference and join founder common. Nonconverted A joins residual sharing with its as-converted weight; nonconverted Seed/B do not. Normalize weights among eligible residual claimants. If A reaches 60 total, remove it from further participation and redistribute unused residue among the remaining eligible common. Compare all eight conversion vectors and check each series cannot improve its own proceeds by switching its election with others fixed; ties allowed. Reconcile payouts to E and never assign negative cash or guaranteed 1x proceeds.\nFor exits E=200/500/1000/2500 crore, payout rows founder/Seed/A/B are 42/14/44/100; 240/80/80/100; 480/160/160/200; 1200/400/400/500. At 200 Seed converts, A participates, B takes preference. At 500 Seed/A convert and B is indifferent; at 1000/2500 all convert. Piecewise thresholds with other elections rechecked: Seed indifferent 180; A reaches cap 280, remains capped to 400, is indifferent converting at 400 and converts above 400; B indifferent 500. Below these, insufficient-proceeds and preference tiers still apply. Solve the equalities from the appropriate normalized residual, not investment divided by final ownership in isolation.\nCounterfactual A 1x non-participating changes founder proceeds at 500 by ZERO because A already converts; do not manufacture participation harm there. Add 200 comparison: A takes 30, Seed converts, founder 52.5/Seed 17.5/A 30/B 100, so founder improves 10.5. Participation can matter in some ranges, not every exit. Explain why actual debt, dividend, option cash-out/rollover, contingent consideration and legal terms would require a different model. A generic vendor statement that all SAFEs must convert contradicts event-specific instrument rights; do not use it as a universal rule. Offline model is sufficient, no account, cap-table upload or Eqvista free-feature entitlement assumed.<section aria-label=\"Native D157 practice\"><h3>Joint-election exit waterfall lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d157-exit-waterfall.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable exit waterfall (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d157-exit-waterfall.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional offline exit-waterfall lab, not an actual sale, contract, financing or legal/tax opinion. Three priced rounds: Seed 10 crore at 40 post, A 30 at 150 post, B 100 at 500 post. Final as-converted fractions founder .48, Seed .16, A .16, B .20. E is distributable equity cash after excluded liabilities, not enterprise value. Explicit B &gt; A &gt; Seed ranking is stipulated, not inferred from chronology. Seed and B 1x non-participating; A 1x participating capped at total 2x its investment, with optional conversion removing preference and cap. Each series elects as one block.</p><p>All eight joint conversion vectors are visible. Pay nonconverted preferences B/A/Seed in order, each limited by remaining cash. Founder and converted classes share residual, with nonconverted participating A joining until its total preference plus participation hits the cap. A then leaves residual sharing and unused cash is redistributed among remaining eligible holders by normalized weights. One capped participating series only, not a general multiple-cap solver. Every vector must have nonnegative payouts summing to E, even when its elections are unstable.</p><p>For every vector, each series compares its current payout against toggling only its own election while other elections stay fixed. Signed own-switch gains are shown. Stable means no gain above .00000001 crore numerical tolerance; ties allowed. Stable count and first stable ID are shown, with all rows available. First ID is deterministic presentation, not a claim of unique payouts or an actual legal/vote outcome. No stable vector leaves selection undefined. No coalition, compulsory conversion, class-consent or individual-holder voting mechanics inferred.</p><p>Baseline E=200: Seed converts, A participates, B takes preference; founder/Seed/A/B =42/14/44/100 crore. Preferences B100/A30 leave70; eligible residual weight .48+.16+.16=.80, so A receives30+.20*70=44. E500 gives240/80/80/100, Seed/A converted and B indifferent. E1000 gives480/160/160/200; E2500 gives1200/400/400/500. E50 gives B50 and others0, not an invented guaranteed100. Zero-exit ties are visible, not fabricated unique elections.</p><p>Baseline-only thresholds, with other elections rechecked: Seed indifferent at180; A reaches60 total cap at280, remains capped through400, indifferent converting at400 and converts above; B indifferent at500. A cap equality30+.20*(E-130)=60 gives280. Conversion equality .20*(E-100)=60 gives400. Independent investment/final-ownership thresholds are not universal. Changes to ownership, investment, cap or rights require full joint recalculation.</p><p>Participation counterfactual uses input flag0 for A non-participating. At200, founder52.5/Seed17.5/A30/B100, founder gain10.5 relative to42. At500, founder240 under both terms, gain zero because A already converts. Do not manufacture participation harm at every exit. Static benchmark prose is baseline only and must be refreshed before exporting a changed scenario. No debt, fees, taxes, dividends, option exercise/cash-out, rollover, escrow, earnouts or contingent consideration modeled.</p><p>Six URLs/six publishers/three source types. Complete returned written HSBC/EquityList/Eqvista/CRV/Oakshore and Humphreys extracted PDF text read, not source chart pixels, underlying reports/data, linked studies, product UI/code or actual account execution. HSBC uses 588 UK 2024 term sheets; not an India norm. Its guaranteed-1x wording is not adopted. EquityList all-convertibles-must-convert and simple independent thresholds require actual instrument/event qualification; retrieved YC event rights provide a counterexample. Eqvista free homepage claim is not entitlement to all waterfall features, no signup/upload/migration. CRV September 2, 2026 market/negotiation and Trados claims not independently validated. Oakshore documents classwide economic elections and possible multiple stable outcomes, not legal voting; its post-money SAFE/liquidity rights are outside that version. Humphreys April 2020 has extraction/column-order limits; historical UK EIS/VCT/tax/corona commentary is not current UK/Indian legal clearance.</p><p>Eleven sheets/eleven PDF pages; 181 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 11 changed sets/1991 checks plus181 restored; 18 boundaries/3258; 12 invalid sets/2172; six explicit exit/participation comparisons/1110 checks; six broken references. Baseline selected-summary sum initially pointed at the wrong column, caught and fixed before release. Clean saved caches, no external links/macros. Actual issuer/rights/exit/permission/reviewer/evidence fields blank. Actual Indian CCPS, insolvency, tax and enforceability unscored. Original objectives, exercise and all ten quiz retained. Authored LibreOffice tests are not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner, account, field or actual overall10. No real sale, signature, wire, account, upload, outreach, calendar, monitoring or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"After all three stated rounds, final founder/Seed/A/B weights?","o":["40%/20%/20%/20%.","48%/16%/16%/20%.","35%/25%/20%/20%.","60%/16%/16%/8%."],"a":1},{"q":"Toy E200: Seed converts, A participates, B takes 100. Residual eligible denominator?","o":["Founder 48 only; no converted series participates.","All 100, because the original cap table never changes.","Founder 48+Seed 16+A 16=80; B is excluded.","Founder 48+Seed 16=64; ignore uncapped A participation."],"a":2},{"q":"Toy E200 founder/Seed/A/B payout, crore?","o":["96/32/32/40.","45/15/40/100.","52.5/17.5/30/100.","42/14/44/100."],"a":3},{"q":"A cap 2x investment 30 in this toy limits which total?","o":["Participation alone to 60 in addition to 30 preference.","Preference plus participation to 60 while remaining preferred.","All future common proceeds forever to 60 after conversion.","Preference always to 60 before any residual sharing."],"a":1},{"q":"A cap starts binding at which E under stated stable elections?","o":["280 crore.","250 crore.","375 crore.","400 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"A converted proceeds while B takes 100 and Seed converts are .2*(E-100). Indifference with 60 cap?","o":["300 crore.","375 crore.","400 crore.","500 crore."],"a":2},{"q":"At 500, changing A to non-participating changes founder cash by?","o":["Zero; A converts under both terms.","Plus 30 crore; eliminate the preference mechanically.","Plus 60 crore; eliminate the cap mechanically.","Minus 80 crore; common holders pay A twice."],"a":0},{"q":"At 200, A non-participating counterfactual founder delta?","o":["Plus 14 crore, from 42 to 56.","Plus 10.5 crore, from 42 to 52.5.","Zero, because participation never matters.","Minus 10.5 crore, from 42 to 31.5."],"a":1},{"q":"A low exit has only 50 distributable cash and B senior 100 preference. Payout?","o":["B 50; junior holders zero under this toy.","B 100; company must invent 50 additional cash.","Everyone gets final ownership times 50 regardless of ranking.","Seed 10, A 30, B 10 because earlier rounds go first."],"a":0},{"q":"A source says every SAFE must convert before any exit. Proper treatment?","o":["Read each instrument/event; some provide liquidity cash-out or other rights.","Carry the latest cap-table percentage into the sale without resolving cash-out priority.","Assume the general vendor summary overrides each signed instrument.","Use the financing conversion clause without checking the separate liquidity-event clause."],"a":0}]},"158":{"objectives":["Draft a fictional governance review that separates board votes, shareholder consents, operational thresholds and unresolved legal enforceability."],"videos":[{"id":"7Fw9coKWWYk","title":"Understanding the Key Terms in Venture Capital Financings","by":"HBS Rock Center for Entrepreneurship","note":"Use the 2023 webinar alongside the written term-sheet case. Historical negotiating examples are not current Indian legal advice; use the written exercise for its explicit economics and control assumptions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Protective Provisions - The Holloway Guide to Raising Venture Capital","url":"https://www.holloway.com/g/venture-capital/sections/protective-provisions","note":"Protective provisions can be negotiated. Treat the author's view of customary terms as guidance, not as Indian law or evidence that one clause matters most in every deal."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Protective Provisions in Term Sheets: What Requires Investor Consent (Glencoyne)","url":"https://www.glencoyne.com/guides/protective-provisions-investor-consent","note":"Compare consent thresholds and procedures with the actual financing documents. Authorizing new shares and issuing them can have different control and dilution effects; this case states its own thresholds."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Cooley GO","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. US sample resources are not Indian legal forms or proven free entitlement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"US model documents: NVCA","url":"https://nvca.org/model-legal-documents/","note":"NVCA model-document index. Compare financing-document types and their stated versions. US and Delaware models are not Indian law; the index is not the operative charter or a signed agreement."}],"exercise":"Private negotiation exercise, not representation of a real company or a legal redline for signature. Use twelve fictional proposed reserved matters: annual budget; senior/pari-passu equity; M&A; adverse charter changes; board size; option-pool increase; debt over 2 crore; related-party deals over 25 lakh; winding up; dividends; auditor replacement; new share classes. These are case inputs, not twelve universally standard/non-negotiable terms. For each mark accept/negotiate/reject with company rationale, exact scope/threshold, approval body, information package, carve-outs, response process, expiry/ownership condition and investor counterargument. Pick three investor priorities and identify conditions under which your ranking changes. No universal most-regretted clause or presumed automatic law.\nSynthetic board: 2 founders, 2 investors, 1 mutually selected independent, all 5 present, one vote each, ordinary action needs 3 affirmative votes. Founder bloc 2 alone cannot pass; 2+independent=3 can pass an ordinary action unless separate reserved rights apply. Two investor seats do not alone give a majority. Independent selection matters but does not uniquely determine every decision; attendance, conflicts, vacancies, class rights and actual duties can change outcomes. Separate preferred holdings A 1,000,000/B 500,000: ordinary pooled preferred consent requires MORE THAN 50% of 1,500,000, so minimum 750,001 whole votes. Separate-series consent needs 500,001 A AND 250,001 B. If a holder with 251,000 B votes no, the remaining 249,000 yes votes cannot reach the 250,001 B majority; pooled-majority arithmetic differs. Voting toy excludes abstention/rounding/treasury shares and mandatory law.\nPropose, do not execute, a quorum mechanism: initial meeting 3 directors including 1 investor; after proper notice and an adjournment, fallback quorum 3 without investor for ordinary matters only. Preserve reserved-matter consent and legally mandatory approvals; fallback is not a method to evade them. Give a deadlock escalation/continuity plan with response deadlines but no automatic deemed consent for actions that require actual approval. A 10% variance allowance or 2 crore debt threshold is stipulated, not market data.\nObservers have only rights in the actual agreement, ordinarily no director vote from observer status alone. Do not assert universal immunity from fiduciary/confidentiality/competition/privilege issues. Information rights need recipient eligibility, scope, frequency, confidentiality and conflict/privilege safeguards, not unrestricted daily dashboards or a real reporting calendar. A director's duties and conflicts require jurisdiction-specific analysis. Drag rights depend on actual thresholds, approvals, conditions and safeguards, not majority status alone. Indian SHA/articles/statute/case-law effect is an unresolved legal issue map; do not score the blanket claim that SHA-only rights never bind a company. US NVCA/Cooley materials are comparison examples, not Indian approvals. No document generation/account/real board or investor contact.<section aria-label=\"Native D158 practice\"><h3>Governance approvals and quorum lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d158-governance-approvals.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable governance lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d158-governance-approvals.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional governance approval calculator, not a real resolution, meeting, legal/tax opinion, signature, contact or reporting calendar. Five seats: two founders, two investors, one independent. One vote each; three affirmative director votes required even when attendance changes. No casting vote, vacancy, conflicted-director rule, written consent or majority-of-those-present rule inferred. No observer vote or quorum from observer status in this toy.</p><p>Initial quorum requires three attending directors including one investor, plus supplied proper-notice flag. Stipulated ordinary fallback requires three directors, proper notice and adjournment, ordinary action only. Founders plus independent can supply three votes but without an investor initial quorum fails. Proper ordinary fallback can change that result; reserved matters cannot use the fallback. Quorum is not affirmative vote, and silence is not deemed consent.</p><p>A eligible votes 1000000/B 500000. Strict pooled majority minimum 750001; separate majority EACH series 500001 A and 250001 B. A 1000000 yes/B 249000 yes passes pooled but fails separate. B 251000 no can block separate consent without necessarily blocking pooled consent. Minimum floor(N/2)+1 handles odd denominators; equality at half is not enough. Positive eligible denominators only. Whole affirmative votes cannot exceed eligible holdings.</p><p>Separate board, quorum, pooled/series and required-preferred flags stay visible. Selected consent mode and whether preferred consent is required are explicitly stipulated case inputs, never inferred from seat count or amount alone. Combined 1 means all modeled conditions only, not approval to act. Legal/mandatory approvals remain UNKNOWN. Changing required flag to 0 describes a different hypothetical, not permission to bypass a right.</p><p>Separate operational trigger family: debt OVER 2 crore, related-party transaction OVER 25 lakh, absolute budget variance outside inclusive 10% allowance. Exact amounts and 10% equality do not trigger; one rupee above does. Product comparison avoids division-rounding at budget boundary. No aggregation, prior outstanding debt, netting, commitments, line-item budget rules or actual approved budget inferred. Trigger flags do not substitute for board, preferred, statutory or conflict approvals.</p><p>Twelve fictional review matters: budget; senior/pari-passu equity; M&amp;A; adverse charter changes; board size; pool increase; debt; related-party transactions; winding up; dividends; auditor replacement; new classes. Record exact scope, accept/negotiate/reject rationale, approval body, information, carve-outs, response/escalation, expiry/ownership condition and investor counterargument. Information/observer eligibility, confidentiality, privilege, competition and duties need specific legal review. Authorization differs from issuance and its dilution effects. Majority ownership alone is no universal drag right. No blanket SHA-only enforceability conclusion.</p><p>Eight exact URLs/five publishers/three source types: guides/practitioner articles, resource directory/model-document index, professional-association legal commentary. Complete returned written pages read, not linked generators, model documents, cases, images or actual legal engagement. Holloway 2023/Glencoyne 2025 non-negotiable/market claims not universal or India evidence; Glencoyne authorizing-shares wording qualified because issuance differs. Its USD 250 k/5-10% examples not imported as current Indian terms. Cooley control last reviewed 2014, Asia/India 2022, meetings 2025; Singapore weighted votes not Indian law, US/Delaware quorum/notice/written-consent guidance not Indian legal proof. NVCA index lists October 2025 certificate update but operative linked text not reviewed. ABA observer 2014 fiduciary statements not blanket immunity; contractual limits and litigation risks matter. No official current Indian statute/cases or actual instruments read for a legal outcome.</p><p>Ten sheets/ten PDF pages; 27 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 19 changed sets 513 checks plus 27 restored; 108 exhaustive feasible attendance/affirmative-count combinations under fixed initial process 2916 checks; all 8 notice/adjournment/reserved combinations at the three-vote/no-investor boundary 216 checks; 50 preferred threshold/mode scenarios 1350; 18 other boundaries 486; 42 invalid sets 1134; six broken references. Raw preferred-boundary test block includes the 8 process scenarios: 1566 checks total, not 58 preferred cases. Clean saved caches/no external links/macros; actual company/documents/permission/reviewer fields blank. All original objectives, exercise and ten quiz retained. Authored LibreOffice tests not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner, account, field, current Indian law or overall 10. No real approval, disclosure, account, contact, calendar or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Toy board 2 founders/2 investors/1 independent, all present, 3 votes needed. Founder 2+independent?","o":["Two votes only; independent has no vote.","Always pass every action despite class consent or law.","Three votes pass an ordinary action unless separate rights apply.","Four votes because founder votes count twice."],"a":2},{"q":"Two investor directors on that five-person board establish?","o":["Automatic majority because investors supplied money.","No influence because founders own common shares.","Two votes, not an automatic majority or all-purpose control.","Control over every shareholder resolution."],"a":2},{"q":"Pooled preferred 1,500,000 shares, MORE THAN 50%, whole votes. Minimum?","o":["750,001.","750,000.","500,001.","250,001."],"a":0},{"q":"A 1,000,000 and B 500,000; separate majority EACH series. Minimum A/B?","o":["500,001 and 250,001.","750,001 total with no series constraints.","500,000 and 250,000.","1,000,000 and 500,000."],"a":0},{"q":"Initial investor-inclusive quorum, then stipulated fallback ordinary quorum 3. Does fallback waive reserved consent?","o":["Yes; notice alone supplies every missing consent.","Yes; any adjournment erases all shareholder rights.","No; ordinary quorum and reserved approval are separate requirements.","No meeting may ever occur without the investor under any negotiated fallback."],"a":2},{"q":"Budget consent extends to every minor reallocation. Appropriate counter?","o":["Keep every invoice subject to unanimous shareholder approval.","Define materiality, delegated bounds and approval process against the plan.","Delete all oversight and conceal material reallocations.","Assume 10% flexibility exists without negotiated wording."],"a":1},{"q":"An observer attends board meetings. What follows from observer status alone?","o":["Treat the observer agreement as identical to a director appointment without checking the role.","Infer blanket liability immunity from lack of a director vote.","Assume fund information rights remove the need for confidentiality limits.","No director vote; actual rights, confidentiality and conflicts still need review."],"a":3},{"q":"Preferred protection is proposed. Proper analytical distinction?","o":["Every veto is identical to managing all operations.","A specific consent veto differs from board management power; test the actual scope.","Any two investor directors can force all corporate actions.","Reserved matters are irrelevant when founders own most shares."],"a":1},{"q":"Indian memo says SHA-only investor rights never bind a company. Correct review?","o":["Treat RBI filing as universal enforceability clearance.","Treat the blanket rule as proven by a US template.","Treat signature alone as curing every mandatory-law issue.","Check parties, wording, articles, statute and current relevant cases before a conclusion."],"a":3},{"q":"Requested governance deliverable permits which action?","o":["Private fictional review table and proposed processes, not actual approvals or reports.","Create and send a real shareholder resolution.","Give a new observer access to private dashboards.","Calendar actual investor financial reports without scope."],"a":0}]},"159":{"objectives":["Roleplay a financing negotiation using explicit economic scenarios and labeled nonquantifiable risks, without inventing agreement or universal term rankings."],"videos":[{"id":"fb5Lm_BbR_E","title":"Term Sheet Negotiations","by":"Stanford Graduate School of Business","note":"Use the written option-pool case for its explicit assumptions; a negotiating anecdote is not evidence for the fictional company."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"How to Negotiate a Term Sheet Without a Lawyer (Xaver Lehmann)","url":"https://xaverlehmann.substack.com/p/how-to-negotiate-a-term-sheet-without","note":"Pool example is ambiguous/inconsistent; recompute explicit post-round target independently. Clause rankings and lawyer-saving claims are experience, not universal advice."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Negotiating Your Term Sheet: A Founder's Guide (Seed Legals)","url":"https://seedlegals.com/us/resources/negotiating-your-term-sheet/","note":"US-oriented guidance, not current Indian law, guaranteed nonbinding status or universally optimal no-shop."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Cooley GO Docs","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. US sample resources are not Indian legal forms or proven free entitlement."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Indian-fintech Series B roleplay, not a live term sheet or an actual agreement. Investor proposal 80 crore at 320 pre/400 post, 1x uncapped participating, full ratchet, 2 of 5 board seats, 20% pool target post-round created in pre-money, 3 year founder re-vesting, 45 day no-shop and milestone tranches. Rank five changes conditional on company runway, probabilities, alternatives and terms; record founder counter, investor counter and a final SIMULATED proposal with unresolved points. No real investment/commitment, negotiation, signature, contact or document generator. Do not invent consent or claim both sides actually agreed.\nFor the exit-cost comparison only, assume founders own all old shares before a newly created employee allocation, no prior preferred/debt/fees/taxes/convertibles/other rights. Investor owns 20% post. Stipulate 20% post employee allocation INCLUDED pre-money, all allocated shares already issued vested common for the payout illustration, not an unallocated option reserve. Founders 60%, employee common 20%, investor 20%. This requires pool 25% of pre-financing capitalization, not 20% pre then diluted. Compare the separate smaller 10% post allocation, same 20% investor => founder 70%, employee 10%. Avoid distributing proceeds to a nonexistent unallocated pool.\nAt distributable exits 400/1500 crore, uncapped participating investor gets 80+.2*(E-80)=144/364; residual founders get 60/80 of 256/1136=192/852, employee 64/284. With 1x non-participating investor gets max(80,.2*E)=80/300, founders 240/900, employee 80/300. Founder gain from removing participation 48 at BOTH exits in this toy, not a universal most-expensive-clause ranking. With 10% post employee allocation and non-participating investor 20%, founders get .7*E=280/1050 at those exits, another 40/150 improvement versus 20% post allocation. Do not add overlapping negotiated changes as if all effects were independent.\nSeparate down-round anti-dilution toy, not another hidden round in the exit case: old conversion price 10; 1,000 preferred shares originally convertible 1:1; broad-base pre-issue as-converted share count A=10,000; new cash 20,000 at price 5 gives new shares C=4,000 and B=2,000 equivalent shares at old price. Stipulated broad-weight formula CP2=10*(A+B)/(A+C)=60/7; conversion ratio 10/CP2=7/6 yields 1,166.6667 as-converted shares before rounding. Full ratchet CP2=5 gives 2,000. Actual definitions/exclusions/rounding and other holders determine real dilution; neither mechanism protects a fixed ownership percentage.\nFor board/quorum, re-vesting, no-shop and tranches, say no unique rupee cost from current data. List missing triggers, vesting credit, leaver terms, milestones, funding obligations, cure/verification, runway/cash timing, outside date, binding carve-outs, alternatives and probability assumptions. Material low-probability personal liability or regulatory risks cannot be discarded merely as edge clauses. A credible alternative may improve leverage but is not the only factor or a guarantee of price or tone. Term-sheet binding effect is document/jurisdiction-specific; diligence and a partner change create risks, not automatic cancellation. Templates/dated surveys are comparison inputs, not law, current Indian market norms or permission to skip qualified review.<section aria-label=\"Native D159 practice\"><h3>Term-sheet package comparison lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d159-term-negotiation.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-sheet editable negotiation lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d159-term-negotiation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">10-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional Indian-fintech Series B roleplay and conditional calculator, not an actual financing, term sheet, negotiation, investor consent or agreement. New money 80 crore / pre-money 320 / post-money 400 implies investor 20%. No other preferred, prior pool, options, notes, debt, fees, taxes or secondary. Employee common is explicitly issued and vested solely for payouts, not an unallocated option reserve.</p><p>Base employee allocation 20% POST created within pre-money implies founder 60% / employee 20% / investor 20%. Required allocation fraction of PRE-financing capitalization is 20%/(1-20%)=25%. Separate 10% POST allocation gives founder 70% / employee 10% / investor 20%, requiring 12.5% PRE. A 20% PRE allocation then diluted by 20% investor would end 16% POST, not 20%. Creating a pool after the round pro rata would dilute the investor too and is a different package.</p><p>Uncapped 1 x participating investor first receives min(investment, E), then 20% of residual. Non-participating compares available 1 x preference with 20% of total distributable equity cash, not residual after another payout. Common founder/employee split the remainder in their relative common fractions. At E 400, participating investor/founder/employee 144/192/64; non-participating 80/240/80. At E 1500, 364/852/284 versus 300/900/300. Removing participation gives founder 48 at BOTH endpoints in this toy, not a universal clause ranking.</p><p>Smaller 10% employee allocation plus non-participating investor 20% gives founder 280 at 400 and 1050 at 1500. Pool-only gain compared with same non-participating 20% employee allocation 40/150. Total package gain compared with BASE participating 88/198, reconciled as non-overlapping sequential changes 48+40/150. Do not add overlapping base-to-package improvements twice. At low E 50 investor receives 50, others 0; no guaranteed 80 or negative cash. All payout sums equal E.</p><p>Separate anti-dilution family, not a hidden down round in the exit case: old conversion AND original issue price 10, protected preferred 1000 initially 1: 1, supplied broad-base pre-issue A 10000, new cash 20000 at price 5. B=cash/oldprice 2000; C=cash/newprice 4000; CP 2=10*(10000+2000)/(10000+4000)=60/7, conversion ratio 7/6, as-converted shares 1166.666667. Full ratchet CP 5, ratio 2, shares 2000. Neither guarantees original ownership percentage or automatically issues shares now. Actual rounding, definitions, exclusions and post-round denominator unresolved. At equal new/old price both ratios 1; up-round regime outside calculator.</p><p>Rank five changes conditionally on runway, hiring, probabilities, alternatives, exit scenarios and legal/regulatory risk. Record founder counter, investor counter and final SIMULATED proposal with unresolved points. No unique rupee cost for 2 of 5 board seats, 3 year re-vesting, 45 day no-shop or milestone tranches without actual rights/quorum, independent selection, service credit, leaver/acceleration terms, binding scope, expiry/fees, objective milestones, cure, verification, funding obligations and cash timing. Material personal-liability/regulatory tails cannot be discarded as edge clauses. Alternatives do not guarantee price, tone or leverage. Actual binding effect is document/jurisdiction-specific.</p><p>Seven URLs/six publishers/three source types: founder anecdote, practitioner legal guides, vendor help/product/accounting guidance. Complete returned written six new pages read; prior D 158 Cooley written homepage evidence reused. Not source image pixels, linked PDF/cases, generator UI, webinar/call or anecdote verification. Xaver Apr 30, 2026 ranks valuation fifth then calls it sixth; universal ranking not adopted. Its 20%new-money/15%POST pool implies 65%founder, not 68%; 68%fits 15%PRE ending 12%POST. Automatic later-round preference seniority not universal. SeedLegals US publishedNov 2024/updatedAug 2023 metadata conflict retained; nonbinding/no-shop/confidentiality and market claims qualified. DLA formula definitions vary; strict ratchet-greater claim excludes equality. Holloway ownership-protection wording not fixed percentage. UK SeedLegals EMI/75%/Companies House procedures not Indian law; reserved shares do not exist until exercise. Beancount normative redlines/defaults not universal. No account/API/ledger/generator/resolution/filing/contact action.</p><p>Ten sheets/ten PDF pages; 41 baseline formulas independently recomputed; 10 changed sets 410 checks plus 41 restored; 14 boundaries 574; 23 invalid 943; two explicit exit endpoint comparisons 102 checks; six broken references. Clean saved caches/no macros/external links. Actual company, investor, documents, permission, reviewer, alternatives/acceptance fields blank. All original objectives/exercise/ten quiz retained. Authored LibreOffice tests not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner, account, field, current Indian law or actual overall 10. No real deal, signature, contact, account, calendar, document generation or spend.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Investor 80 at 320 pre; employee allocation 20% post included pre-money. Founder/employee/investor?","o":["64%/16%/20%.","80%/20%/20%.","64%/20%/16%.","60%/20%/20%."],"a":3},{"q":"A 20% post employee allocation created before a 20% new-money round is what fraction of pre-financing capitalization?","o":["20%.","25%.","16%.","40%."],"a":1},{"q":"Toy E400: 1x participating 80 invested/20%. Investor proceeds?","o":["64 crore.","80 crore.","160 crore.","144 crore."],"a":3},{"q":"Same E400, founder 60/80 of common residue. Participating versus nonparticipating founder cash?","o":["256 versus 320 crore.","240 versus 192 crore.","192 versus 240 crore.","192 versus 320 crore."],"a":2},{"q":"At E1500, remove participation, same ownership. Founder gain?","o":["300 crore, equal to investor ownership proceeds.","64 crore, from 852 to 916.","Zero, because participation always disappears at large exits.","48 crore, from 852 to 900."],"a":3},{"q":"Nonparticipating 20% investor, employee allocation 10% post instead of 20%. Founder share/cash at E1500?","o":["70%; 1,050 crore.","72%; 1,080 crore.","80%; 1,200 crore.","60%; 900 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"Separate toy weighted average CP2=10*(10000+2000)/(10000+4000). What is CP2 to four decimal places?","o":["8.5714","5.0000","7.5000","10.0000"],"a":0},{"q":"That toy 1,000 preferred, full ratchet old 10/new 5. As-converted shares?","o":["4,000.","1,000.","1,166.6667.","2,000."],"a":3},{"q":"45-day no-shop and 3-year re-vesting, no other terms. Exact founder rupee cost at E400?","o":["Use the 45-day exclusivity period alone as an annualized charge on exit value.","Use the 3-year reset alone to calculate forfeiture without a leaver date or vesting credit.","Not identifiable; need binding scope, vesting, timing, alternatives and outcome assumptions.","Assign zero cost to every non-price term because no cash changes hands at signing."],"a":2},{"q":"Roleplay final table and a second interested investor establish?","o":["A universally improved price with no relationship risk.","An executed financing with investor consent.","A simulated proposal and possible alternative, not a real agreement or guaranteed leverage.","Permission to sign definitive documents for both parties."],"a":2}]},"160":{"objectives":["Build an evidence-led ESOP remediation and secondary issue map; distinguish promise, grant, vesting, exercise, tax timing and actual liquidity."],"videos":[{"id":"kSMUv7SpdyU","title":"How ESOPs Are Creating India's New Millionaires","by":"Capitalmind","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Anecdotes, tax rates and valuation/maturity claims are not current law or suitable exercise advice."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Brief Guide to ESOP Taxation in India (Capitalmind)","url":"https://www.capitalmind.in/insights/guide-to-employee-stock-options-esop-taxation-india","note":"Historical eligibility/date/deferral and an inconsistent vesting-versus-allotment sentence are not scored as current law. Toy tax uses explicit assumptions."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Employee Stock Options for Startups (Startup India)","url":"https://www.startupindia.gov.in/content/sih/en/reources/startup_india_notes/regulations_and_policies/employee_stock_options_for_startups.html","note":"Government-hosted author guidance, not current consolidated statute/rules or universal exercise recommendation."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Equity List","url":"https://www.equitylist.co","note":"Optional public educational resource, not legal clearance or permission to upload a real cap table. Check current access terms, pricing and provider identity before using a real cap table."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Foreign employee-share classification: RBI direction","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=12710","note":"Read the opening definitions and paragraph 22 with adjacent qualifications. This RBI direction is updated April 1, 2026; ESOP tax and company-law analysis require their own operative sources."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Income-tax statute: ESOP research reference","url":"https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/documents/d/guest/income_tax_act_2025_as_amended_by_fa_act_2026-pdf","note":"Identify the applicable rates, sections and relief conditions from the current statute before a real exercise decision. This classroom model uses stipulated tax assumptions."}],"exercise":"Private hypothetical 60-person company with a stated 12% pool but incomplete offer-letter/grant/approval records. Do not declare all promises legally nonexistent, automatically binding shares or void: preserve each document/date/communication, affected employee, conditions, approvals, cap-table and accounting record, then have qualified counsel assess contractual/equitable rights and lawful remedies. Stop new unsupported equity representations; reconcile authorized/issued/reserved/granted/vested/exercised/lapsed categories without backdating. Draft remediation options, approvals/documents/register/accounting/tax steps and transparent employee notices for review, not actual notices or legal ratification. No automatic universal scheme-registration/FMV grant-price rule: exercise price, grant-date option valuation, tax FMV and transaction price serve different purposes. Exact Companies Act/Rule 12/private-company exemptions/eligibility/amendments/SEBI branch must be verified for the company. Retrieved 2019 Startup India/2021 Capitalmind are historical guidance, not current law; the tax statute/transition and eligible-startup relief need current primary text and case dates. Do not score old tax section numbers, rates, deferral clocks, buyback limits or salary-versus-capital classification as current law from those articles.\nSynthetic option arithmetic only: 10,000 vested options, strike 80/share, stipulated exercise-date FMV 200 and an illustrative 30% perquisite collection rate, no deferral/surcharge/cess/fees. Exercise cost 8 lakh; modeled spread 12 lakh; illustrative withholding 3.6 lakh; upfront cash 11.6 lakh. Rate/timing are assumptions, not actual Indian tax advice. Later sale 260 gives 26 lakh gross and modeled sale-minus-FMV basis 6 lakh, before separately determined capital-gains tax/costs; do not tax the entire 26 lakh as gains or assume automatic salary-tax refund on subsequent loss. Buyer withdrawal could leave exercise/tax cash exposed. A quoted valuation does not establish an executable buyer, liquidity probability or suitable exercise recommendation. Ask for actual plan/exit window, legal/tax basis, affordability, concentration and sale conditions; 90 days is not a universal Indian leaving-employee deadline.\nSeparate vesting toy: 4,800 options over 48 months, 100/month accrued, 12 month cliff releases 1,200, then 100/month; no acceleration/leaver changes. At 18 months 1,800 vested; at 11 months zero. Distinguish vesting and exercise from shares/votes or cash. Double trigger means the two specified plan events, often change of control plus qualifying termination/good reason within a stated window; not any resignation, not automatically more employee-friendly, not proof that every acquisition pays cash.\nSeparate founder-secondary toy: holder sells existing 8,000 shares at 5,000/share=4 crore gross; 1,000,000 issued shares unchanged gives buyer .8%. No primary funds reach company and no new-share dilution from this transfer alone. Four crore NET would require tax/fee/cost-basis assumptions and possibly more shares. Record sale restrictions/ROFR/co-sale/consents, share-class rights, pricing, valuation, tax, FEMA/residency, filings and settlement conditions. No universal discount or founder-exit-risk signal. A company buyback is a different transaction requiring its own eligibility/capital/tax rules, not interchangeable with investor secondary. Delaware-parent employee options require separate issuer/employee/entity/residency/OPI versus ODI/foreign-asset/tax/treaty/reporting review; selected RBI provisions show ESOP shares can be OPI rather than all grants automatically ODI. No real option exercise, buyback, share transfer, loan, filing, advice to a specific employee or sent board message. Deliver private checklist, conditional secondary outline and unresolved-tax issue map.<section aria-label=\"Native D160 practice\"><h3>ESOP cash, vesting and secondary lab</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d160-esop-cash.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable ESOP lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d160-esop-cash.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference; original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Private fictional ESOP cash, vesting and existing-share secondary lab, not an actual employee exercise, transfer, buyback, loan, notice, filing, advice or agreement. Twelve sheets and twelve PDF pages. Actual rights, company records, tax/legal reviewer and permission fields remain blank. The original objectives, exercise and ten quiz items are retained.</p><p>Exercise case: 10000 vested options, strike 80 rupees, stipulated exercise-date FMV 200 and illustrative positive-spread collection fraction 30%. Purchase cost 800000, modeled spread 1200000, collection 360000 and upfront cash 1160000 (11.6 lakh). This rate and cash timing are assumptions, not actual Indian tax liability, withholding, deferral, surcharge, cess or fees. A quote or valuation is not executable liquidity; buyer withdrawal leaves cash exposed.</p><p>Later full sale at 260 gives 2600000 gross and 600000 sale gain relative to the supplied 200 FMV basis, before separately determined capital gains tax and costs. Cash after the modeled upfront outflow is 1440000, not final after-tax wealth. At sale 20, gain is negative 1800000 and cash net negative 960000; prior illustrative collection remains 360000, not automatically refunded. The upfront-only covering price 116 is not fair value or an exercise recommendation. Zero quantity leaves its per-share covering price undefined.</p><p>Separate vesting: 4800 options over 48 months, 100 accrued per completed month, 12-month cliff releases accrued 1200 and then 100/month. Month 11 yields 0; month 18 yields 1800; month 48 or later yields 4800. Changed nondivisible grants may create fractional quantities; actual rounding, partial months, leaver, acceleration and exercise-window terms remain unresolved. Vested rights are not issued shares, votes or cash; no universal 90-day exercise deadline.</p><p>Separate secondary: seller owns 8000 existing shares at 5000 rupees each, issued denominator 1000000 unchanged. Seller gross 40000000 (4 crore), buyer fraction .008 or .8%, new shares 0 and company primary cash 0. Net proceeds need actual cost basis, tax, fees and settlement. Transfer, primary issuance and company buyback are different. Review title, restrictions, ROFR/co-sale/consents, class, pricing, residency, FEMA, filings and buyer settlement before an actual case.</p><p>Remediation case: 60 employees and a stated 12% pool do not establish individual grants, actual shareholder status or automatic nullity. Preserve promises, grant letters, approvals, plan, registers, accounting, dates, reliance and cap-table evidence. Reconcile authorized/issued/reserved/granted/vested/exercised/lapsed without double counting reserved and its subcategories. Qualified review must distinguish contractual rights and lawful prospective remedies; no backdating, automatic grant allocation, employee notice, board action or scheme registration.</p><p>Current-law issue map, not case clearance: official Income-tax Act 2025 as amended by FA 2026, section 17(1)(d) and 17(4)(h) use specified employer securities and exercise-date FMV less amount paid; section 73(1) Table item 4 links acquisition basis to FMV taken into account. Practitioner Elite cites 17(5)(h), but primary text says 17(4)(h). Its exercise versus allotment FMV wording is inconsistent. No actual valuation method, employee rates, holding period, forms, treaties or implementing rules validated.</p><p>Selected sections 140, 289(3), 391(2), 392(1)-(3) show conditional eligible-startup timing within 14 days of the earliest 60 months from relevant tax-year end, sale or ceasing employment. Section 140 conditions include April 2016 to before April 2030 incorporation, IMB certification and amended 300 crore turnover limit. Not universal DPIIT-only relief or proven employee/employer eligibility. Selected opening of section 536 preserves earlier operation/liabilities/proceedings; not a full transition review. No actual deferral deadline computed and no blanket invalidation of old citations.</p><p>RBI April 1, 2026 direction: selected opening, definitions and paragraph 22(1)-(7) with adjacent qualifications read. Specified ESOP, sweat-equity and minimum-qualification holdings up to 10% without control can qualify as OPI even if unlisted; reporting and remittance branches differ. Do not apply the generic unlisted-equals-ODI definition alone. Actual residency, issuer, instrument rights, control, LRS, reporting, foreign-asset and tax facts remain unresolved; no filing, remittance or foreign-parent exercise.</p><p>Six URLs and six publishers across primary government text, historical/current practitioner guidance and a vendor homepage. Complete returned Capitalmind 2021 and Startup India 2019 author guidance is historical, not current rates/eligibility or universal exercise advice. Complete returned EquityList homepage/product cards are marketing, not verified security, performance, customers or pricing. Complete returned Elite June 2026 text is secondary and qualified. Full official 686-page PDF recovered, but only selected sections and partial transition read; seven source pages visually checked. RBI is selected written text, not the entire direction or all linked rules. No source AV, vendor UI or actual account interaction.</p><p>Authored LibreOffice tests: 24 baseline formulas independently recomputed using a rational oracle; 12 changed sets / 288 checks plus 24 restored; 21 boundaries / 504; 38 invalid sets / 912; six broken-result references. Saved caches clean, no macros/external links. Twelve native PDF pages visually checked. This is not native Excel/Sheets parity, legal/tax clearance, full source/AV, independent learner, account, field or overall 10. No exercise, transfer, buyback, loan, filing, notice, contact, account, calendar or spend action.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Incomplete approvals and offer-letter equity promises establish?","o":["Every employee already owns voting shares.","Every promise is void and may be deleted without notice.","An evidence/rights gap requiring review, not automatic shares or automatic nullity.","A later backdated board minute automatically cures everything."],"a":2},{"q":"Toy 10,000 options at strike 80, FMV 200, illustrative 30% spread withholding. Upfront cash?","o":["20 lakh; pay full FMV to exercise.","8 lakh; no tax assumption was supplied.","6 lakh; tax applies to full FMV only.","11.6 lakh: 8 exercise+3.6 illustrative tax."],"a":3},{"q":"Same toy later sale 260/share, modeled basis 200/share. Gain before separately determined tax/costs?","o":["26 lakh.","6 lakh.","18 lakh.","12 lakh."],"a":1},{"q":"4,800 options/48 months, 100/month, 12 month cliff, then monthly. At 18 months?","o":["2,400 vested.","1,200 vested.","1,800 vested.","4,800 vested."],"a":2},{"q":"That same toy employee leaves at 11 months, no exceptions. Vested?","o":["Zero under this specific cliff schedule.","1,100 automatically vested.","1,200 because a one-year cliff rounds up.","4,800 because leaving accelerates every grant."],"a":0},{"q":"Founder sells 8,000 existing shares at 5,000; total issued 1,000,000 unchanged. Gross/buyer stake?","o":["4 crore/.8%; all other holders diluted by new issuance.","4 crore/8%; company raises 4 crore.","40 crore/.8%; company raises 40 crore.","4 crore/.8%; company gets no primary cash."],"a":3},{"q":"Founder requests 4 crore NET. Does 8,000 shares at 5,000 settle it?","o":["No; all founder proceeds are automatically salary.","Only 4 crore gross; net needs tax, fees and cost-basis facts.","Yes; company automatically pays seller taxes.","Yes; secondary proceeds are always tax free."],"a":1},{"q":"A plan calls itself double-trigger. What must be checked?","o":["Acquisition alone always triggers full cash payout.","Any resignation at any time automatically accelerates all options.","A second financing is universally the second trigger.","Exact change-of-control and qualifying-termination/good-reason conditions and time window."],"a":3},{"q":"A leaving employee asks about exercise. A universal 90-day window and known exit odds?","o":["Apply a familiar 90-day convention without reading the current grant and leaver terms.","Neither is established; inspect the actual plan and evidence, plus cash/tax risk.","Use the preferred financing valuation as the executable common-share exit price.","Count a nonbinding buyer indication as sufficient cash certainty for exercise."],"a":1},{"q":"Foreign-parent employee options for India resident: correct scope?","o":["Every option grant is automatically ODI.","Separate legal, tax, reporting and OPI/ODI classification review; no blanket ODI or tax exemption.","Foreign parent means Indian tax never applies.","A US document automatically satisfies all Indian requirements."],"a":1}]},"161":{"objectives":["Recompute a downside exit with explicit independent recap weights, joint conversion elections and conditional governance/employee-rights review."],"videos":[],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Chasing Waterfalls: Use Your Term Sheet to Calculate Exit Distributions - HSBC Innovation Banking","url":"https://www.hsbcinnovationbanking.com/en/resources/chasing-waterfalls-how-to-use-your-term-sheet-to-calculate-exit-distributions","note":"Use the explicit independent D161 recap. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Liquidation Waterfall: How Exit Proceeds Are Distributed - Equity List","url":"https://www.equitylist.co/blog-post/liquidation-waterfall","note":"Use the explicit independent D161 recap. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Protective Provisions - The Holloway Guide to Raising Venture Capital","url":"https://www.holloway.com/g/venture-capital/sections/protective-provisions","note":"Use the explicit independent D161 recap. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."}],"exercise":"Private fictional review, not an actual sale, legal opinion or permission to contact a board. The D157 round history remains background only. This D161 recap is independently stipulated: common plus employee shares 35%, Seed 25%, A 20%, B 20%, total 100%. Do not derive it from D157, whose unrecast weights differ. Distributable equity cash E=240 crore, after all liabilities excluded from this toy. B 100 preference ranks above A 30 above Seed 10; Seed/B are 1x non-participating. A 1x participating with total receipts capped at 60, and optional conversion that removes both preference and cap. All classes elect as blocks. No debt, dividends, fees, taxes, contingent consideration or mandatory conversion.\nFor each of eight conversion vectors, pay remaining preferred claims in B/A/Seed order up to available cash. Converted classes join common; nonconverted A also shares residual by its 20 weight until 60 total. Normalize weights over eligible residual claimants only. On cap binding, remove A from further participation and redistribute leftover residue. An election is stable if no series can improve its own cash by switching with other elections fixed; ties allowed. At 240 stable elections Seed converts/A participates/B takes preference, payouts common 48.125/Seed 34.375/A 57.5/B 100. Residual 110 is shared 35: 25: 20, denominator 80. If A alone converts while B stays preferred, A gets 35, so participation is better than conversion here. B receives 41.6667% of cash versus 20% ownership. Preference is not guaranteed 100 if E is insufficient.\nCounterfactual 1: change only A to 1x non-participating, then re-solve elections. A converts; common 61.25/Seed 43.75/A 35/B 100, common gain 13.125. Counterfactual 2: force B to convert by removing its preference; A remains participating. A reaches 60 cap, leaving 180 shared common 35/Seed 25/B 20 over 80: 78.75/56.25/45, common gain 30.625. This is a negotiated change, not B's voluntary choice. At the base with other elections held, B's unilateral conversion would give 45, below 100. Do not add gains as if independent or infer a universal best clause across all exits.\nThe 35 common bucket includes a stated 10 percentage-point employee sub-bucket for arithmetic, but actual employee grant/approval defects make entitlement uncertain. A proportional synthetic allocation of 48.125 would give 13.75 to that 10/35 sub-bucket; this is NOT confirmed employee proceeds, ratification or a universal void rule. Preserve promises/records and identify documents, remedy, holder eligibility, vesting/exercise, approvals, buyer conditions and legal/tax treatment needed before real allocation. No unallocated pool is a cash payee.\nName the economic term causing the modeled common shortfall. Identify the actual charter/SHA/approval evidence needed to know who could approve or block it; this case supplies no specific historical consent clause or real vote. Compare board votes, class consent, quorum and enforceability separately. Rank negotiation priorities conditional on exits, runway and alternatives. Deliver an eight-vector table, reconciled base/two counterfactuals, and a private rights/remedy issue map. Twenty-five review questions test the stated assumptions, not current-law clearance.<section aria-label=\"Native D161 practice\"><h3>Downside recap and negotiated alternatives</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d161-downside-recap.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable recap lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d161-downside-recap.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference;  original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional independent recap, not reconstructed round history or an actual sale, employee payout, legal opinion or consent. Twelve editable sheets and twelve PDF pages. Original objectives, exercise and all 25 quiz items retained. Actual company, rights, records, reviewer and permission stay blank.</p><p>Distributable equity cash E 240 crore after excluded liabilities;  common .35, Seed .25, A .20, B .20. Stated B 100 &gt;  A 30 &gt;  Seed 10 preferences;  Seed and B non-participating, A participating with total receipts cap 60. Optional conversion waives preference and cap. Common includes a synthetic employee sub-bucket, not a confirmed allocation or unallocated reserve cash payee.</p><p>All eight conversion vectors remain visible. Pay nonconverted preferences in explicit order, limited by remaining cash. Common, converted classes and nonconverted participating A share residual. When A hits its total cap, redistribute remaining cash over other eligible weights. One capped class only. Check feasible unilateral own-payout switches holding other choices fixed, not isolated preference/ownership thresholds. Multiple stable rows/ties can occur;  first stable ID is only deterministic presentation, not legal priority.</p><p>Base 240 stable payout: common 48.125 / Seed 34.375 / A 57.5 / B 100, total 240. Seed converts, A participates and B stays preferred. A conversion yields 35 instead of 57.5;  B conversion yields 45 instead of 100. B receives 41.666667% of proceeds despite 20% ownership. This is gross cash, not enterprise value, IRR, recovery or post-tax employee wealth.</p><p>A-only negotiated change: participation 0, forcedB 0. Re-solved common 61.25 / Seed 43.75 / A 35 / B 100;  common gain 13.125. B-only change: participation 1, forcedB 1. Common 78.75 / Seed 56.25 / A 60 / B 45;  gain 30.625. Forced B is an imposed feasible-choice counterfactual, not a claim B voluntarily accepts 45 instead of 100. It excludes preferred-B vectors from stability and ignores only its infeasible reversal;  other classes still must have no profitable feasible unilateral switch.</p><p>Combined participation 0 and forcedB 1 must be re-solved: common 84 / Seed 60 / A 48 / B 48;  gain 35.875, NOT 13.125+30.625=43.75. Changing rights changes elections and denominators. At exit 50 with base rights B 50 and juniors 0;  at exit 0 all payout 0 and irrelevant choices can tie. No universal preference-removal recommendation from one exit.</p><p>At base only, synthetic employee 10 percentage points within 35 common gives 10/35*48.125=13.75 crore. This is not confirmed title, grant, vesting, exercise or entitlement. Missing approvals do not automatically void all promises or grant shares. Preserve communications, plan, grants, approvals, register, reliance and dates;  qualified review must distinguish contractual/equitable rights and lawful remedies. No backdating or automatic employee cure.</p><p>Rights map separates economic terms from authority. Need actual charter/articles, SHA, instruments, amendments, grant/issue approvals, liquidation definition, ranking, conversion mechanics, cap scope, dividend, escrow, tax and buyer terms. Board majority/quorum/conflicts, class/pooled-holder votes, reserved matters and mandatory approvals differ. No historical protective provision or actual vote was established. Share sale, asset sale, insolvency and employee settlement can differ;  no automatic pro-rata pool payout.</p><p>Eight URLs/eight publishers across bank commentary, practitioner legal/finance writing, product guide and educational text. Reused D 157 complete returned HSBC/EquityList/CRV/Oakshore written evidence and extracted Humphreys PDF;  D 158 Cooley 2022 India/SE Asia text. HSBC 588 UK 2024 term sheets are not India norms;  compulsory conversion/threshold/guaranteed 1x claims qualified. CRV market/Trados anecdotes not independently validated. Oakshore UI/code not operated;  Humphreys multi-column extraction limits and historical UK scope retained. No underlying reports/charts/actual instruments established.</p><p>New complete returned Holloway protective-provisions text and TEJU cap explanation read. Holloway consent judgments are not universal law;  authorized and issued shares differ. TEJU aggregate cap includes preference;  its displayed charts explicitly defer conversion and single-investor formula cannot replace a multi-series election re-solve. Diagrams, linked course and live calculator not inspected. No current Indian statute/case clearance or actual employee/board consent. Source URLs are in the workbook Evidence sheet.</p><p>Authored LibreOffice/rational-oracle checks: 181 baseline formulas; 12 changed sets 2172; 21 boundaries 3801; 32 invalid sets 5792; 44 rights/exit combinations 7964; four explicit counterfactuals 740; 181 restored;  six broken-result references. Saved caches clean, no macros/external links. Twelve native pages visually checked. Not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner/account/field/current-law clearance or actual overall 10. No sale, signature, vote, employee notice, contact, filing, account, calendar, spend or commitment.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"D161 final weights common/Seed/A/B are 35/25/20/20. How were they obtained?","o":["By adding original round purchases without dilution.","Mechanically from D157 unchanged rounds.","By treating every percentage as a share of the remaining float.","Independently stipulated recap weights, not derived from D157 round history."],"a":3},{"q":"D161 E240, B 100 and A 30 preferred, Seed converted. Eligible residual denominator?","o":["Common 35+Seed 25+A 20=80.","All 100 including B preferred.","Common 35+Seed 25=60.","A 20+B 20=40."],"a":0},{"q":"Same base case. Cash available for residual allocation?","o":["100 crore.","110 crore.","130 crore.","140 crore."],"a":1},{"q":"Same base case. A total proceeds?","o":["60 crore, cap binds immediately.","30 crore, with no participation.","57.5 crore, below 60 cap.","72 crore, using 20% of 210."],"a":2},{"q":"Same base case. Seed proceeds after conversion?","o":["60 crore.","27.5 crore.","10 crore.","34.375 crore."],"a":3},{"q":"Same base case. Common bucket proceeds?","o":["84 crore.","48.125 crore.","61.25 crore.","38.5 crore."],"a":1},{"q":"If A alone converts with B preferred and Seed converted, A receives?","o":["57.5 crore, preference survives conversion.","48 crore, exactly 20% of 240.","60 crore, cap survives conversion.","35 crore, less than participating 57.5."],"a":3},{"q":"What is B cash share at E240?","o":["100/140=71.4286%, because junior cash is the denominator.","20%, because cash must track ownership.","100/240=41.6667%, versus 20% ownership.","40%, from rounding ownership to preference."],"a":2},{"q":"Base case payout reconciliation?","o":["84+60+48+100=240.","48.125+34.375+60+100=240.","48.125+34.375+57.5+100=240.","48.125+34.375+30+100=240."],"a":2},{"q":"B senior 100 claim and only E50 cash. Payout under toy ranking?","o":["B 100 and common minus 50.","B 50, all junior classes zero.","Seed 10, A 30, B 10 because earlier rounds come first.","Everyone receives ownership times 50 despite stated ranking."],"a":1},{"q":"A changed to 1x non-participating, E240, other terms unchanged. A elects?","o":["Take 30 plus 35 participation.","Take 30 because preference always dominates.","Convert for 35 rather than take 30.","Take 60 because old participation cap is a minimum."],"a":2},{"q":"That A non-participating counterfactual common proceeds?","o":["78.75 crore.","48.125 crore.","61.25 crore.","84 crore."],"a":2},{"q":"Common improvement from changing only A to non-participating?","o":["13.125 crore.","30.625 crore.","27.5 crore.","35 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"Force B to convert, keep participating A. At E240, A total?","o":["72 crore; cap applies only to participation.","60 crore; total-receipts cap binds.","30 crore; participation disappears without B preference.","48 crore; A automatically converts."],"a":1},{"q":"B forced-conversion counterfactual common/Seed/B cash after A 60?","o":["78.75/56.25/45 crore.","73.5/52.5/42 crore.","84/60/48 crore.","48.125/34.375/100 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"Why is B forced-conversion a counterfactual rather than base election?","o":["B gets 100 plus 45 if it converts.","B must always convert whenever E exceeds invested capital.","B would get 45 by switching, below its 100 preference.","Common can unilaterally cancel any preference."],"a":2},{"q":"Common gain from removing only B preference at E240?","o":["30.625 crore, greater than A-only gain 13.125.","13.125 crore, equal to A-only change.","45 crore, equal to B conversion cash.","100 crore, equal to eliminated preference."],"a":0},{"q":"Can the two separate common gains be added for a combined renegotiation?","o":["Yes; add them to total exit cash for every holder.","Yes; each preference has a fixed independent cash cost.","No; solve the combined rights and elections again.","No; neither individual scenario can be calculated."],"a":2},{"q":"A 10 percentage-point employee sub-bucket within 35 common: proportional toy cash at base?","o":["Zero by automatic legal voiding of every defective grant.","4.8125 crore, exactly 10% of common cash.","24 crore, exactly 10% of all proceeds regardless of preferences.","13.75 crore, conditional arithmetic, not confirmed entitlement."],"a":3},{"q":"What follows from never-board-approved employee grant records here?","o":["Use the buyer headline price as the grant settlement amount without reviewing terms.","Treat the stated employee percentage as sufficient evidence of issued shares.","Assume missing approvals establish automatic nullity without reviewing promises or remedies.","A rights/remedy gap; no automatic payout, nullity or cure is established."],"a":3},{"q":"An unallocated option reserve appears in a cap table. Is it automatically a cash recipient?","o":["Exclude every employee claim because a reserve is not itself an issued shareholder.","Allocate the full pool percentage of exit cash before identifying any issued or settled claims.","Count the reserved capacity as issued common for every legal and payout purpose.","No; identify actual claims, holders and transaction treatment."],"a":3},{"q":"Which protective provision approved these historic preferences?","o":["Infer consent from board composition without reading the charter.","Not supplied; inspect actual documents and approvals before naming it.","Infer the pooled-holder threshold from the preference payout alone.","Treat a model-document example as evidence of the historic vote."],"a":1},{"q":"A board majority approves a sale. What must still be checked?","o":["Applicable class/holder rights, quorum, conflicts and mandatory approvals.","Nothing; board majority cancels every shareholder condition.","Only headline valuation; cash ranking cannot matter.","Only whether observers attended, because they supply missing votes."],"a":0},{"q":"A term ranking from this E240 case establishes?","o":["The B preference is always the most costly term at every exit.","A conditional economic comparison, not a universal founder priority.","Price is irrelevant to all investment decisions.","All low-probability legal risks can be ignored."],"a":1},{"q":"Why compare all eight conversion vectors and unilateral switches?","o":["To find consistent elections and catch isolated max-preference shortcuts.","To guarantee there is exactly one legal equilibrium in every real deal.","To let every series collect preferred and converted cash together.","To replace the actual contract with assumed round chronology."],"a":0}]},"162":{"objectives":["Build a reproducible, budget-conserving fund simulation and distinguish assumptions, sample uncertainty and investment policy."],"videos":[{"id":"ek16GTNMDyA","title":"How to Build a Venture Capital Fund (2025)","by":"How I Invest Podcast","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Use the Kauffman/Angel List readings and explicit offline model instead."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Reserves (AVC - Fred Wilson)","url":"https://avc.com/2017/01/reserves/","note":"USV experience on reserves/recycling is not universal optimal sizing or a verified normal-distribution funding guarantee."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Strategies for Optimal Follow-On Investments (Kauffman Fellows Journal)","url":"https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/journal/strategies-for-optimal-follow-on-investments","note":"Vendor-model workflow, not independently validated strategy superiority, chart data or an emotion-free forecast."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Tactyc","url":"https://tactyc.io","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Optional model reference; use the explicit synthetic calculation below rather than assuming a paid tool is required or its outputs are correct. Offline simulator is sufficient."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Multi-stage portfolio construction with Monte Carlo","url":"https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/journal/multi-stage-portfolio-construction-with-monte-carlo","note":"Normal graduation-rate assumptions, modeled marks and illustrative results are not guaranteed fund outcomes."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Should seed investors follow on? Angel List 2019 study","url":"https://angel.co/pdf/followon.pdf","note":"Study of 1,218 filtered 2014-17 investments and 10,000 simulations, with many unrealized marks and a same-size follow-on assumption. Access, selection and fees limit generalization; mean and median results differ and do not establish one best reserve policy."}],"exercise":"Private synthetic simulator, not a real LP pitch or estimated market return. All dollars are millions. Commitment 40; one terminal distribution at year 10; investment tranches at year 0 for this simplified multiple model; no fees, carry, taxes, leverage, recycling, intermediate distributions or interest. Five-point per-company terminal initial-check multiple: 0 with probability 55%, 2 with 25%, 6 with 12%, 25 with 7%, 100 with 1%. These replace undefined within-bucket values and 50x+tail; they are chosen assumptions, not fitted venture data. Independent company draws in baseline. Exact single-deal EV=.55*0+.25*2+.12*6+.07*25+.01*100=3.97x.\nFour configurations conserve 40: A 24 initials*1=24 plus 16 uninvested cash returned at par; B 40*1=40 and no reserves/cash; C 24 initials*(20/24)=20 plus 20 follow-on into 6 selected existing companies at 20/6 each; D40 initials*.5=20 plus 20 follow-on into 10 selected existing companies at 2 each. Follow-on selection is TOP QUARTILE by a pre-exit observable score, not future realized winners or the old undefined top-decile rule. Stipulate score is independent of terminal payoff in baseline; it has no predictive edge. A score can be observed without being predictive. Follow-on shares cost twice the initial per-share price, same final per-share payoff and no intervening share-count changes in this toy; follow-on dollar multiple therefore equals initial multiple/2. No arbitrary extra free ownership or guaranteed pro-rata right. This stylization is not a full multiround ownership model.\nRun 1000 paths with Python random.Random(20261110). Per path draw 40 independent uniform outcome variables, then 40 independent score variables, then one shock uniform reserved for stress. Map outcome uniforms to cutoffs.55/.80/.92/.99/1. Use first 24 for A/C and all 40 for B/D. Sort scores descending within each eligible set, select 6 or 10, ties by index. Pair strategies on the same universe but do not call differing check counts identical portfolios. Gross terminal value/40 commitment includes idle cash; deployed-only A investment multiple excludes cash and uses 24 denominator. Do not call a changing-denominator metric a better fund outcome.\nReport mean, median, nearest-rank 5th/95th percentiles, P(multiple>3), P(multiple<1), standard deviation and mean MCSE=sample SD/sqrt 1000. Analytic gross fund means A 2.782/B 3.97/C 2.9775/D2.9775 are expectation checks, not the fixed-seed sample means. Probability MCSE=sqrt(p*(1-p)/1000); 1000 paths do not remove model error. Separate structural mean differences from sampling noise. Reference fixed-seed means 2.83695/4.0782/3.019/3.024725; medians 2.45/3.75/2.541667/2.69375; P>3=.350/.691/.381/.415. These are this toy run only, not policy superiority.\nStress 1: replace 100 tail payoff with 50 or 0 while leaving its 1% probability; single-deal EV falls to 3.47 or 2.97, not automatically 1x. Stress 2: common 20% probability zeroes all risky company payoffs; otherwise multiply them by 1/.8, leaving A idle 16 unchanged. This deliberately preserves each marginal expected payoff but changes correlation/tails. Stress 3: evaluate fees/carry with an explicitly funded budget and documented waterfall, not as free capital; e.g. reserve 4 of 40 for total fees, scale risky allocations AND idle cash to 90%, then apply 20% carry only to positive terminal profit above 40 total paid-in if stipulated. Label modified budget. Separate marked TVPI from realized cash DPI and multiples from timing-dependent IRR. Future-winner selection may be run only as a labeled hindsight upper-bound diagnostic, never an implementable strategy. Deliver code/spreadsheet, four aligned distribution charts, assumptions/checks, and a 10-line private LP-style defence with limitations and conditions that change the choice. Offline work is sufficient; no paid tool/account/LP contact.\n\n<section aria-label=\"Runnable D162 portfolio lab\"><h3>Reproduce the paths, then challenge the reserve policy</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d162-portfolio-practice.zip\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Download the runnable offline D162 lab (ZIP)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d162-portfolio-practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">inspect the four-page method and distributions (PDF)</a>. Build independently first, then use the packaged Python 3 model, six-scenario path/statistic exports, four aligned SVG charts and ten-line private defence as checking aids. Standard library only; no package install, network, paid service, account, real fund data or LP contact. Run instructions identify the output files the command overwrites.</p><p>The prior authored model core is reused, not presented as a newly devised simulator. Seed 20261110, the exact 40 outcome/40 score/one shock draw order, four 40M budgets and original no-edge follow-on pricing are retained. Baseline means are 2.836950/4.078200/3.019000/3.024725x; strict probabilities above 3x are 35.0/69.1/38.1/41.5 percent. Exact 3x is not above 3x. C and D have the same analytic mean, not identical risks or sample means.</p><p>An independent integer-sixths and rational-waterfall evaluator matched all 24,000 strategy/path outputs across baseline, two tail replacements, compensated common shock, funded fees/carry and nonimplementable hindsight. Forty-eight exact threshold comparisons, 36 prior chart bins, twelve baseline references and budget/tie/constant/boundary controls passed. A clean extracted ZIP reproduced all five generated outputs byte for byte. All four PDF pages and the four-panel SVG were visually inspected; categorical bins have unequal widths and equal visual spacing, not density scaling.</p><p>The net scenario explicitly funds 4M fees, scales risky allocations and A idle cash to 90 percent, then applies the stipulated terminal profit-only carry. The common shock preserves marginal expected payoffs analytically, not each sample mean. MCSE excludes model error. Historical source/vendor limits and the assigned fund-administration recording mismatch remain; this package adds no new empirical return claim, real reserve recommendation, learner-transfer result, full audiovisual clearance or overall 10/10 certification. Existing case, ten MCQs and feedback identities remain unchanged.</p></section>\n<section aria-label=\"D162 source-reading calculation\"><h3>Audit the source's denominator, not only its conclusion</h3><p>Open the assigned <a href=\"https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/journal/strategies-for-optimal-follow-on-investments\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">2022 Kauffman follow-on article</a>, Step 2 and Step 3 tables. In the displayed initial table, downside/big-exit/underwrite probabilities are 20/20/60 percent; reserve amounts are 0.5/1/1M; follow-on multiples are 0.49/12.14/2.43x. Its displayed 3.98x is consistent with the probability-weighted mean of case multiples: 0.2*0.49+0.2*12.14+0.6*2.43=3.984x. It is not automatically the ratio of expected follow-on proceeds to expected capital. Using those same displayed rounded inputs, expected capital is 0.9M and expected proceeds are 3.935M, so that different ratio is 4.37222x.</p><p>Repeat after the article changes probabilities to 40/0/60 percent: mean case multiple=1.654x (displayed 1.65x), expected capital=0.8M, expected proceeds=1.556M, ratio=1.945x. Both averages can answer a defined question; label the estimand and weighting before comparing deals. Do not call the calculation a reproduced vendor model or a correction of its unrounded output. State the conditions under which the two statistics coincide, then explain why neither by itself sets optimal reserves when access, concentration, future opportunity and uncertainty matter. Changing per-case investment size changes the weighting; a larger displayed ratio is not free alpha.</p><p>Next inspect the assigned <a href=\"https://www.kauffmanfellows.org/journal/multi-stage-portfolio-construction-with-monte-carlo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">2021 multistage article</a>. Its follow-on allocation graphic displays 29.750M allocated and 37.780043M needed. The Monte Carlo screenshot labels probability above 37.780043M as 43.5 percent, approximately the prose's 44 percent. That is not the displayed probability of exceeding 29.750M allocated reserves. Sketch both thresholds on the same distribution; exact exceedance of 29.750M needs the actual simulated draws or a readable cumulative statistic, not a substituted 44 percent. Source screenshots/prose were re-read and their arithmetic checked; raw vendor draws, animated input changes and full recording coverage are not thereby validated.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"A 40 M commitment, 24 checks of 1 M, no other allocation. What must be specified?","o":["The remaining 16 M, e.g. idle cash at par under this toy.","Treat all 40 M as deployed without additional investments.","Ignore 16 M and call 24 M the original commitment.","Add 16 M of proceeds without recording a cash asset."],"a":0},{"q":"Five-point toy probabilities 55/25/12/7/1%, multiples 0/2/6/25/100. Exact deal EV?","o":["1x.","2.97x.","3.97x.","5.5x."],"a":2},{"q":"Toy A 24 M deployed, 16 M cash at par. Expected terminal value/40 commitment?","o":["2.782x.","3.97x.","2.382x.","4.6367x."],"a":0},{"q":"Change to 40 checks of 1 M and 50% of 40 reserved. Feasible without other capital?","o":["No; 40 initial+20 reserve exceeds 40.","Yes; count the same20as both reserves and part of the40initial deployment.","Yes; keep40initial checks but deduct20only from reported return, not funded capital.","Yes; apply the50%reserve to a different denominator without changing the stated40commitment."],"a":0},{"q":"Follow-on price twice initial price, same terminal per-share cash. Follow-on dollar multiple?","o":["Half the initial-check multiple.","Twice the initial-check multiple.","Identical despite paying double per share.","Initial multiple plus the two-times markup."],"a":0},{"q":"Observable score independent of payoff versus realized-future top decile. Proper use?","o":["Both are equally available at investment time.","First is implementable but no-edge here; second is hindsight diagnostic only.","Observable scores necessarily predict exits perfectly.","Future-winner selection is a conservative baseline."],"a":1},{"q":"Toy C 20 M initial and 20 M follow-on, no score edge, follow-on EV 1.985. Analytic fund EV?","o":["1.985x.","3.97x.","2.9775x.","5.955x."],"a":2},{"q":"1000 runs show sample SD 2.0. Mean MCSE approximately?","o":["2x.","0.06325x.","0.002x.","0.6325x."],"a":1},{"q":"Replace 1% tail payoff 100 with 0, other probabilities/payoffs unchanged. Deal EV?","o":["0x because the largest outcome disappears.","1x by definition of a power law.","3.96x, subtracting only the probability.","2.97x, not an automatic collapse to 1x."],"a":3},{"q":"A common shock keeps expected payoffs fixed but increases simultaneous failures. What is needed?","o":["Recompute distribution/tails; unchanged marginal EV does not imply unchanged risk.","Ignore it because correlation cannot affect portfolio tails.","Report all simulated terminal values as cash already distributed.","Call multiple an IRR without modeling timing."],"a":0}]},"163":{"objectives":["Write a conditional follow-on decision and a private board-discussion outline using explicit price, dilution, reserves and cash scenarios."],"videos":[{"id":"4OJ7Dll0Mvs","title":"A Startup CEO's Guide to Board Meetings, with Dave Kellogg","by":"Run the Numbers with CJ Gustafson","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Three slides/48-hour pre-read are speaker practices; duties/observer rights require documents/law."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Follow-On Investment: How VCs Decide When to Double Down (1752 vc)","url":"https://www.1752.vc/learn/follow-on-investment","note":"Practitioner synthesis of follow-on decisions. Test the source data and portfolio assumptions; it is not a universal reserve or follow-on prescription."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Choosing Your First Investor Board Member (CRV)","url":"https://www.crv.com/content/board-member","note":"US and Delaware framing; statistics/board-practice opinions not validated empirical rules or Indian director duties."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Visible.vc","url":"https://visible.vc","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Not a universal industry format, verified entitlement, required signup or permission for real investor reporting."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IPEVvaluation guidelines 2025","url":"https://www.privateequityvaluation.com/Portals/0/Documents/Guidelines/2025%20IPEV%20Valuation%20Guidelines.pdf","note":"Read the application principles, section 3.10 recent-investment-price qualifications and section 5.8 indicative offers. Valuation judgment still needs the facts, measurement date and applicable accounting policy."}],"exercise":"Private hypothetical decision, not a real investment or board meeting. Existing 12% stake, seed post 100 crore 14 months ago. Add explicit classroom assumptions: Series A 250 pre-money plus 50 new primary cash=300 post, no pool change, debt, preferences, fees, tax, convertibles or secondary. The phrase 2.5x seed post refers here to 250 PRE, not 250 post. Stipulate 10 crore existing common-equivalent shares before the round, unchanged since seed: current new-share price 25 rupees, versus historical 10 rupees;50 crore buys 2 crore new shares, 12 crore total post. Same common-equivalent per-share price for all new money. An outside lead does not prove terms, diligence or quality. Assume the fund has an exercisable pro-rata right for this toy, 8 crore available reserves and a separate alternative requiring 5 crore. Full pro-rata=.12*50=6, partial half=3, none 0. Ownership after round full 12%, partial 11%, none 10%. Remaining reserves 2/5/8. Rights in a real deal require documents, allocation and closing conditions.\nSeparate existing holding from incremental cheque economics. At distributable exits 100/300/900 crore, full-stake proceeds 12/36/108; none 10/30/90. Incremental 6 investment adds 2/6/18 proceeds, gross incremental multiples 1/3, 1, 3. Partial 3 adds 1/3/9 proceeds versus none, same incremental multiples at same price. These do not subtract historical cost and are not whole-position profit or IRR. Original entry price remains relevant to cumulative economics/tax basis/memo review, but is not sufficient justification for spending new money. Full preserves 12%, not a guarantee of better risk-adjusted returns. Full plus 5 alternative needs 11>8; partial plus 5 uses 8, but arithmetic alone does not make partial optimal. Compare opportunity cost, concentration, mandate, actual rights, execution and downside, not signaling alone.\nOperating inputs remain ARR 2.2 crore, monthly growth 8%(slowing from 15%), NRR 95%, 10 months runway. Add current unrestricted cash 4 crore/net cash burn 0.4 crore per month; downside burn 0.6 with no new funding gives 6.6667 months. These constant-burn scenarios omit working-capital/collection variability and are not cash forecasts. New 50 primary is COMPANY cash, distinct from fund 8 reserves. NRR 95% means the measured existing cohort shrank 5% over its stated measurement period, not necessarily monthly; new logos may still grow ARR. State missing cohort period, gross retention, margins, pipeline quality, concentration and dated collection/runway evidence. Do not compound 8% forever or infer 8% MoM caused 95% NRR.\nWrite a conditional one-pager: updated thesis/evidence versus original, absolute new-share price and exit sensitivities, reserve tradeoffs, missing facts/decision owner/checkpoint, full/partial/none rationale and what changes your answer. Draft a private board agenda, not invitations or official minutes. Three slide outlines: metrics versus agreed plan with unchanged definitions; base/downside cash and financing milestones; decisions/asks with owner/options/tradeoffs. Include time for strategic discussion, conflicts and appropriately scoped closed sessions, attendance dependent on documents/law. The Kellogg interview's first-three-slides, 48 hour pre-read and observer/independent views are speaker practices, not legal duties or universal optimal rules. Ensure material information reaches authorized directors fairly; do not conceal downside to manufacture a good signal.\nValuation memo: IPEV December 2025 guidelines apply for quarterly reporting periods beginning on/after 1 April 2026, subject to applicable accounting/law. A recent investment can calibrate inputs but is not a standalone valuation technique; different share-class rights/measurement-date changes matter. An indicative offer may be supporting evidence, usually insufficient alone, with more weight as execution becomes firmer and uncertainty assessed. Neither every unsigned quote automatic markup nor only closed rounds ever valid is a correct rule. Case-specific adopted policy/accounting review remains necessary. No real fund decision, reporting, cap-table upload or platform entitlement.<section aria-label=\"Native D163 practice\"><h3>Follow-on, reserves and board evidence</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d163-follow-on.xlsx\" download>12-sheet editable follow-on lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d163-follow-on.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Attempt independently before reference;  original exercise and quiz retained.</p><p>Fictional independent follow-on lab, not an actual investment, allocation, valuation or board decision. Twelve editable sheets and twelve PDF pages;  original objectives, exercise and all ten quiz items retained. Actual rights, company data, fund mandate, owner, reviewer and permission remain blank.</p><p>Pre-money 250 crore, primary 50, old shares 10 crore, old fund 12%, partial take-up 50%, reserves 8 and alternative 5. Common-equivalent same-price arithmetic only: post-money 300, price 25 rupees/share, new shares 2 crore, post shares 12 crore. Ignores preferences, secondaries, options, convertibles, fees, tax and transaction timing. Pro-rata is document-dependent, not automatic or obligatory.</p><p>Full cheque 6 preserves 12%, partial 3 gives 11%, none gives 10%. At stipulated exits 100/300/900 crore, compare retained gross proceeds and incremental cash separately. At 900 none 90, partial 99, full 108;  incremental 9/3=3x and 18/6=3x per new rupee. At 100 the incremental multiple is 1/3x, not guaranteed recovery. No probability, expected-return recommendation, whole-position MOIC/IRR or fair-value conclusion.</p><p>Reserves 8: none leaves 8, partial leaves 5, full leaves 2. Including alternative 5, none leaves 3 and partial 0;  full+alternative needs 11 and leaves-3. Budget fit is not investment merit or approval. Compare mandate, concentration, alternative economics, evidence and execution. No concealment or invented fund constraint.</p><p>Cash 4 and monthly burn 0.4 give 10 months;  downside burn 0.6 gives 6.666667 months. Conditional full 50 company financing, if actually closed and received, gives 54 cash and constant-burn 135/90 months. This intentionally unrealistic unchanged-burn sensitivity excludes deployment, working capital and timing. Company cash differs from fund reserves;  neither is a dated weekly cash-flow forecast or evidence funding arrived.</p><p>Updated thesis, full/partial/none cases, reversal conditions and private decision gate are separate. Stipulated ARR 2.2 crore, growth 8% against 15%, NRR 95% need dated cohorts, definitions, retention, margins, pipeline, concentration and collections. Eight percent monthly growth and 95% NRR do not by themselves prove total ARR decline. No recommendation from three unweighted exits.</p><p>Board preparation separates information from actual authority. Metrics versus plan with stable definitions;  cash and financing milestones;  decisions, owner and evidence;  appropriate closed/conflict processes depend on documents and law. No actual vote, observer entitlement, notice, signature or consent. Kellogg first-three-hours preread is recording-specific, not universal duty and not newly heard here.</p><p>Six URLs/six publishers across primary guidance, practitioner finance/governance, vendor educational writing and a product homepage. Complete returned written 1752 follow-on and CRV July 2026 board article read. Their market statistics, control percentages, exit study, firm claims and causal interpretations were not independently verified. Delaware patterns are not Indian-law clearance. Visible homepage only: no product UI, price, security, performance, entitlement, reporting, upload, account or signup established.</p><p>Complete returned Carta follow-on article read: pro-rata right not obligation, reserve opportunity cost, senior preference not guaranteed recovery, pay-to-play and anti-dilution document-dependent;  secondary does not issue new shares. Capital-call, reporting and onboarding content remains outside execution. No real capital call, recipient, reporting schedule or account action.</p><p>IPEV December 2025 public 92-page PDF recovered;  selected pages 5,8,17-18,38-41,59-60 text read and selected source pixels checked, not full 92 pages. Primary guidance effective quarterly periods beginning 1 April 2026, subject to prevailing law/accounting. Recent transaction price calibrates inputs, not a standalone method or default markup;  complexity is not automatically better. Indicative offers can support valuation but usually not alone. Later transactions can inform conditions known/knowable at measurement date, not import later new conditions. Lincoln January 2026 Q&amp; A read, its broad post-date summary qualified against primary 18/60. No actual valuation reviewer, applicable-policy or case clearance.</p><p>Authored LibreOffice/rational-oracle checks:65 baseline formulas; 11 changed sets 715 checks; 13 boundaries 845; 31 invalid sets 2015; 81 price/ownership/take-up combinations 5265; 6 broken references; 65 restored. Saved caches clean, no macros/external links;  twelve native pages visually checked. Not native Excel/Sheets parity, full source/AV, independent learner/account/field/current-law validation or actual overall 10. No investment, trade, wire, capital call, board action, LP message, account, calendar, spend or commitment.</p></section>","quiz":[{"q":"Toy seed post 100, Series A 250 PRE plus 50 new cash. Series A post?","o":["250 crore.","300 crore.","200 crore.","350 crore."],"a":1},{"q":"Existing 12%, round 50 primary at same price, stipulated pro-rata. Full check?","o":["12 crore.","30 crore.","3 crore.","6 crore."],"a":3},{"q":"Full 6/partial 3/none 0. Post-round ownership?","o":["14%/13%/12%.","12%/6%/0%.","12%/11%/10%.","10%/11%/12%."],"a":2},{"q":"E900, full versus none. Incremental 6 check adds what proceeds/multiple?","o":["6 crore/1x.","108 crore/18x.","90 crore/15x.","18 crore/3x."],"a":3},{"q":"Fund 8 reserves, alternative 5. Full 6 plus alternative feasible?","o":["Yes; all rounds create free fund reserves.","No; 11 needed. Partial 3 plus 5 fits 8 but still needs investment judgment.","Yes; use company 50 new cash as fund money.","No; partial 3 plus 5 also exceeds 8."],"a":1},{"q":"Current cash 4, net burn 0.4 monthly; downside 0.6. Constant-burn runway?","o":["4 months base and 6 months downside.","10 months base and 10 months downside.","1.6 months base and 2.4 months downside.","10 months base, about 6.67 months downside."],"a":3},{"q":"NRR 95% and new-logo growth coexist. Correct reading?","o":["Every customer shrank 5% each month.","Existing cohort net revenue fell 5% over its specified period; total ARR may still grow.","Total ARR must have fallen 5% regardless of new customers.","Logo churn is exactly 5% and expansion zero."],"a":1},{"q":"A 2.5x round markup and outside lead establish full follow-on is best?","o":["Preserve 12% ownership without comparing the new check return to alternatives.","No; evaluate absolute price, terms, expected outcomes, reserves and new evidence.","Use the seed entry price alone to justify the new check.","Treat the outside lead as a substitute for reviewing terms and operating evidence."],"a":1},{"q":"IPEV 2025 recent-investment guidance implies?","o":["Use transaction information to calibrate, then assess current facts and rights at each measurement date.","Copy the latest round price to every share class indefinitely.","Only completed rounds may ever support valuation evidence.","Any unsigned offer proves fair value without uncertainty."],"a":0},{"q":"Authorized deliverable for board work in this exercise?","o":["A real board commitment to approve full 6 crore.","Official minutes asserting approvals already granted.","An invitation to every LP including conflicted recipients.","Private agenda/slide outlines with conditional asks, not actual votes, invitations or sent reports."],"a":3}]},"164":{"objectives":["Compare a fictional co-invest funding gap and fee waterfalls while separating India regulatory routes from US SPV examples."],"videos":[{"id":"r4KGcwwwqOM","title":"How Investors Can Use SPVs to Build an Investment Track Record, with Assure","by":"VC Lab","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Historical 2021 USprovider discussion, not current Assure availability, Indian structure eligibility or platform clearance."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"How SPVs Work in Angel Investing: A Practical Guide (Hustle Fund)","url":"https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/angel-squad-how-spvs-work-in-angel-investing-a-practical-guide","note":"US-oriented commercial guide; K-1/pass-through/vehicle/fee examples not Indian law, precise modeled tax or verified provider entitlement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"A Guide to Co-Syndicating Venture Capital SPVs (Last Money In)","url":"https://lastmoneyin.co/p/guide-cosyndicating-venture-capital-spvs","note":"March 27, 2024 co-syndication article. Carry and coordination are negotiated; practitioner opinions and sponsor examples do not guarantee execution or establish live deal terms."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Lets Venture","url":"https://www.letsventure.com","note":"Optional provider reference; no signup or deal access is needed. Compare the written model without assuming a platform ranking."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"SEBIAIF amendment September 2025","url":"https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/sep-2025/1757502393731.pdf","note":"Read the co-investment amendment, including 17A and 19A-19G and their provisos. Match the manager, vehicle and investor conditions before drawing a case-specific conclusion."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"SEBIcurrent AIFmaster, updated 5 November 2026","url":"https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/nov-2026/1793875212889.pdf","note":"Full Chapter 8 and relevant 2.5.2 launch/first-close provisions read, not all 156 pages.200 non-accredited transition scope and 31 March 2027 deadline; first-close rules separate."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"SEBIaccredited-investor framework 5 November 2026","url":"https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/nov-2026/1793874966416.pdf","note":"Optional manager route, validity, safeguards, securities-exposure/LLP/nonresident conditions. No personalized accreditation or vehicle/tax clearance."}],"exercise":"Private hypothetical structure review and 200 word LP-style draft, not an entity, solicitation, actual allocation or investment recommendation. Headline allocation 6 crore, fund reserves 2, funding gap 4. This arithmetic does not confirm any legal right or permission to use reserves or solicit 4. Identify prospective investor count, accreditation, residency, beneficial ownership, eligibility, fund mandate/PPM, manager conflicts, investee status/rights, required consents, cash deadlines and domestic/foreign structure before choosing a route. Compare domestic registered Angel Fund, other Cat I/II AIF co-invest framework, direct investors/LLP proposal and GIFT proposal as separate legal questions, not interchangeable wrappers or a loophole to avoid securities regulation. No structure is cleared by this case. One cap-table line can reduce administration but not underlying concentration, look-through, reporting, tax or compliance obligations.\nSource-dated law reading, not case clearance: September 2025 AIF amendment replaced the old angel-investor/minimum-ticket framing with accredited-investor provisions and removed the minimum investor commitment in 19D. It also created 17A co-invest schemes for eligible Cat I/II AIF investors with conditions; Angel Funds cannot launch that co-invest scheme. Read all provisos, including only accredited investors of the underlying fund, single investee, relative terms/exit timing and scheme lifecycle. Do not reuse the old universal 25 lakh ticket or 200 all-investors cap. Current SEBI master issued 3 June 2026, updated 5 November 2026, Chapter 8: post 10 September 2025 registrations accredited-only; on/before 10 September 2025 transition to 31 March 2027 with no more than 200 NON-accredited investors offered opportunities during transition, no new such contributions after deadline, existing holdings continue. Transition does NOT extend every requirement. Chapter 8.2.2 still states 8 September 2026 for then-existing funds without first close; other first-close clock now ties to eligibility to launch, with refiling on failure. These dates are document-comprehension examples; determine actual registration/first-close facts before applying.\nChapter 8.1.3 currently allows valid agency certificate, manager confirmation or deemed accreditation criteria. 5 November 2026 circular makes manager-led accreditation an optional additional route, not elimination of eligibility, records, controls, agency route or due diligence. Accreditation recognition is not wholesale permission for a specific investment. Full current consolidated regulation/amendment-chain, FEMA/tax/LLP/GIFT and case documents are outside this bounded review. Keep personalized vehicle/eligibility/tax conclusions unscored and open. No universal India fee, carry, minimum investment or tax treatment is established by US commercial guides.\nFee toy, rupees lakh: A collects 400 total; stipulated one-time setup 4 plus total one-year management fee 8(2% of 400), both funded inside 400; no other costs/taxes. Invest 388. Gross terminal company proceeds assumed 3x invested=1164. Stipulate whole-vehicle waterfall: return 400 total contributed first, 20% carry on positive profit 764=152.8, LP distribution 1011.2, net multiple 2.528x. B collects 400, setup 4, no management fee, invest 396; same 3x=>1188 gross, profit 788, carry 157.6, LP 1030.4=2.576x. These are fictional fees, one-year total not recurring forever, gross outcomes not forecasts, carry base includes all contributed fees by stipulation. At zero company proceeds LP zero/no carry, expenses lost. A request to INVEST 400 instead of raise 400 requires 412 raised for fixed 4+8 costs under the separately fixed-fee assumption; if 2% is of increased commitments solve the percentage equation instead, do not reuse 412 blindly. Multiple co-leads sharing one 20% carry pool 60/40 get 12%/8% of defined profit, not 20% each. Sequential 20% then 20% of remaining profit instead totals 36% of original profit, distinct from a shared pool.\nPrepare a structure issue-map, gross-to-net table and private 200 word draft using fictional/de-identified inputs. Explain fees, capital basis, carry/waterfall, allocation/conflicts, dilution/concentration, illiquidity/total loss, reporting and unresolved legal eligibility without guaranteed returns, live deal details or real solicitation. Draft ROFR/information/confidentiality/transfer/access questions, not executed rights or reporting calendar. Lets Venture public fetch timed out once; no evidence of disconnection, unavailable service, deals or fee entitlement. US Hustle Fund/Last Money In and 2021 Assure talk are comparative mechanics/history, not current India rules or present provider availability. Offline exercise sufficient, no signup, live-deal access, account creation, funds, contracts or member contact.","quiz":[{"q":"6 crore headline allocation minus 2 fund reserves establishes?","o":["A 4 crore funding gap, not authority or legal structure clearance.","An automatically approved 4 crore LLP solicitation.","A confirmed 6 crore company subscription already signed.","Permission to spend fund reserves irrespective of mandate."],"a":0},{"q":"400 lakh total raised, setup 4, total one-year management 8 inside capital. Investable?","o":["400 lakh.","388 lakh.","396 lakh.","412 lakh."],"a":1},{"q":"A toy 388 invested returns 3x=1164, return 400 before 20% carry. Carry amount?","o":["232.8 lakh.","155.2 lakh.","152.8 lakh.","80 lakh."],"a":2},{"q":"Same A toy LP cash after carry/net multiple on 400?","o":["931.2 lakh/2.328x.","1164 lakh/2.91x.","1011.2 lakh/2.528x.","1011.2 lakh/3x."],"a":2},{"q":"No-management-fee B: setup 4 inside 400, invest 396, 3x gross, 20% carry above 400. LP cash?","o":["950.4 lakh.","1011.2 lakh.","1188 lakh.","1030.4 lakh."],"a":3},{"q":"Two leads share one 20% carry pool 60/40. Defined profit split to leads?","o":["20% each, 40% total.","12% and 8% of profit.","60% and 40% of all principal.","20% then 20%, equivalent to 12% plus 8%."],"a":1},{"q":"Sequential 20% carry then 20% of remaining profit: total effective carry?","o":["16% of original profit.","40% of original profit.","20% of original profit.","36% of original profit."],"a":3},{"q":"SEBI master Chapter 8 transition 200 limit applies as described to?","o":["Non-accredited investors offered opportunities by qualifying existing Angel Funds during the stated transition.","Every investor in every AIF, LLP/GIFT wrapper forever.","Only accredited investors in every new fund.","Every startup shareholder under all company laws."],"a":0},{"q":"5 November 2026 manager-led accreditation route implies?","o":["Treat the new optional route as replacing all recognized agency certificates.","Treat a manager confirmation as sufficient without retaining eligibility evidence.","Optional route with eligibility, verification, records and controls; not automatic deal approval.","Treat accredited status alone as permission for every proposed vehicle and transaction."],"a":2},{"q":"17A co-investscheme, Angel Fund, LLP, GIFT and US SPV guide can be treated as?","o":["Copy US pass-through tax treatment into the Indian memo without a local analysis.","Choose the wrapper by cap-table line count before checking investor eligibility.","Distinct frameworks requiring applicable documents/law; no interchangeability or cleared vehicle here.","Use the old ticket-size rule without checking registration date and current text."],"a":2}]},"165":{"objectives":["Compare an aging position exit using explicit cash assumptions, transfer rights and continuation-fund conflicts, without treating a quote or rollover as liquidity."],"videos":[{"id":"C6ksDu4wR5c","title":"VC Secondaries - How to Buy & Sell Private Company Shares w/o Getting Blocked (ROFRs, SPVs, Fees)","by":"Andrew Romans","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Layered-SPV permission bypass, insider-trading legality, direct ownership pricing and liquidity claims are not universally valid or authority to evade actual restrictions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"The Rise of Venture Capital Secondaries (IMD)","url":"https://www.imd.org/ibyimd/finance/the-rise-of-venture-capital-secondaries/","note":"Practitioner/Reference Capital collaboration; market/SEC legal statements not independently validated as current law or universal discounts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Best Practices for Venture Capital GP-Led Secondaries (Industry Ventures)","url":"https://www.industryventures.com/insight/best-practices-gp-led-venture-secondaries/","note":"Some examples have no rollover; author 3-9 month/RIA claims are not current Indian rules or a guaranteed timeline."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Forge Global","url":"https://forgeglobal.com","note":"Forge Price is an informational mark based on limited inputs, not necessarily an executable bid. Check current access terms and instrument eligibility separately."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ILPA continuation-fund considerations, May 2023","url":"https://ilpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Continuation-Funds-Considerations-for-Limited-Partners-and-General-Partners.pdf","note":"Industry guidance, not law, universal deal terms or actual approval."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"ROFR versus ROFO: US contract comparisons","url":"https://www.bedellapc.com/blog/2014/11/right-of-first-refusal-versus-right-of-first-offer-whats-the-difference/","note":"Historical November 19, 2014 US real-estate comparison of ROFR and ROFO. Notice, process and transfer rights depend on the actual contract and jurisdiction; do not copy it as an Indian share-transfer procedure."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Share-transfer first-refusal versus first-offer provisions","url":"https://www.cummings.law/how-to-implement-a-first-refusal-vs-first-offer-provision-in-share-transfers-2/","note":"US lawyer comparison, not Indian enforceability, verified typical timing, tax advice or universal drafting instruction."}],"exercise":"Private hypothetical Indian-fund case, not a real exit process, LP notice or legal clearance. A 2017-vintage fund holds 9% of a profitable SaaS company, revenue 120 crore and growth 35%; no IPO is assumed for three years. Fund life ends in 18 months and its permitted extension has been used. Those facts do not establish a realizable price, buyer, permission for another extension, liquidation route or timeline. Verify fund/issuer jurisdiction, fund documents, actual term/end date, LP interests, company share-class rights, shareholder agreements, articles, tax/residency and required approvals before a real proposal.\nFor arithmetic only, stipulate three unrelated equity-value offers with identical cash rights, no debt/preferences/tax/escrow: strategic whole-company sale 1200 crore, sale costs 5% of the fund's gross proceeds; direct stake secondary at company-equivalent 800 crore, selling costs 2%; continuation transfer at 900 crore before its separately disclosed costs/elections. Fund gross/net strategic 108/102.6, secondary 72/70.56, continuation 81 before costs. A 9% holder cannot promise a whole-company sale or drag without actual rights. Assume current mark values the company at 1000 so stake NAV 90: secondary 800 is 20% below that reference, not a verified market-wide discount or proof the mark is fair. Compare likelihood, timing, execution, consideration quality, costs and consent risks; no unique expected-value ranking without probabilities and timing assumptions. The highest indication can be unexecutable.\nFund arithmetic toy: total LP paid-in 200 crore, prior cash distributions 80, this marked stake 90, other unrealized NAV 30. Before sale DPI=.4, TVPI=1.0. If the direct secondary closes and 70.56 net is immediately distributed to LPs with no additional tax/carry/reserves, DPI=.7528, TVPI=.9028 with other NAV 30 unchanged. Selling below mark increases DPI but can reduce TVPI. A paper rollover is not cash DPI; cash received at the fund and cash actually distributed to LPs are different events. New vehicle fees/carry and old-fund crystallization depend on actual waterfall, not automatic double collection or universal exemption.\nDistinguish direct-company share sale from an LP-interest sale and a GP-led transfer. ROFR generally allows matching a qualifying third-party offer under actual notice/terms; ROFO generally requires an initial offering/negotiation opportunity before third-party sale. Do not merge them into one mandatory same-price procedure. Inspect triggers, indirect transfers/change of control, permitted transfers, notice, response periods, matching scope, lapse, tag/drag, consents and information/confidentiality. A layered SPV does not automatically avoid restrictions, duties, look-through, securities or tax rules. A strip sale may reduce discretionary asset selection but does not eliminate adverse selection, quality variation or buyer exclusions. No universal Indian discount, buyer shortage, market volume or enforceability timeline is established here.\nFor continuation, document why it serves existing LPs versus alternatives, GP seller/buyer roles, old/new carry/fees, price validation/competitive bids, advisor and LPAC conflicts/recusals, expense allocation, information parity with confidentiality, rollover terms/side letters, follow-on dilution, fund duration and actual approval thresholds. ILPA May 2023 guidance recommends an informed genuine election, status-quo economics for rolling LPs and at least 30 calendar/20 business days, subject to the guidance's context; it is not Indian law or proof every transaction offers identical sell/roll rights. Do not deem nonresponse or an LPAC conflict waiver to be all necessary transaction approval. A fairness opinion is one safeguard, not proof of price or universal legal requirement. Draft a private election notice with placeholders for approved price, economics, cash/roll choices, deadline/timezone, documents, defaults, questions and conditions; no actual recipients, sending or funds. Timeline is a proposed workplan with contingencies, not a promised close. Offline analysis only. Proposed workplan only: months 0-2 verify documents, fund authority and cash runway; months 2-5 run permission-aware buyer diligence/bids and compare alternatives; months 5-9 target conditional documentation/closing, with a checkpoint by month 9 and fallback planning by month 12 to protect the 18 month horizon. These are invented planning windows, not measured execution times, another permitted extension or a promise to close. A probability-weighted comparison requires explicit probabilities and closing/failure cash cases; if those are not grounded, leave expected values as scenario ranges rather than invented estimates.","quiz":[{"q":"Toy 9% stake, strategic equity offer 1200,5% costs on fund gross. Net proceeds?","o":["114 crore.","108 crore.","97.2 crore.","102.6 crore."],"a":3},{"q":"Toy secondary company-equivalent 800,9% stake,2% selling costs. Net?","o":["64.8 crore.","72 crore.","78.4 crore.","70.56 crore."],"a":3},{"q":"Stake NAV 90 at company reference 1000; secondary price 72 gross at 800. Discount?","o":["25% because 90/72-1 is the discount denominator.","20% of the reference mark, not a market-wide discount estimate.","2% because transaction costs determine the mark discount.","A guaranteed 20% investment gain for the buyer."],"a":1},{"q":"LP paid-in 200, prior cash distributions 80;70.56 net sale immediately distributed, no other deductions. DPI?","o":["1.0x.","0.7528x.","0.9028x.","0.3528x."],"a":1},{"q":"Same toy, other NAV 30 unchanged. Post-sale TVPI?","o":["1.3528x because keep the sold 90 NAV as well.","0.9028x, below original 1.0x despite higher DPI.","0.7528x because all unrealized assets must be excluded.","1.0x because a sale can never change TVPI."],"a":1},{"q":"What best distinguishes ROFR and ROFO?","o":["Both automatically waive all indirect-transfer restrictions.","Both always require identical notices at the same stage.","Matching a qualifying external offer versus an initial offer opportunity, with details set by actual terms.","Both give every LP the right to buy the whole company."],"a":2},{"q":"A GP-led continuation transaction creates which conflict to examine?","o":["Only a company board conflict; fund economics need no review.","Only a price conflict if the same bank account is used.","No conflict if all parties have invested with the GP before.","GP influence over seller/buyer process, valuation and old/new economics."],"a":3},{"q":"An ILPA 30 calendar/20 business-day election recommendation establishes?","o":["Treat the LPAC conflict vote as approval of every transaction amendment.","Treat 30 days as sufficient regardless of holidays, required disclosures or actual LPA terms.","Dated industry guidance to consider alongside actual law/documents, not universal Indian mandate.","Treat nonresponse as consent to a rollover without checking the governing process."],"a":2},{"q":"A continuation transfer is priced and an LP rolls a paper interest. Does that alone increase cash DPI?","o":["No; no secondary can ever create a cash distribution.","Yes; any new vehicle resets all NAV to DPI.","Yes; the whole transfer price counts as cash to every LP.","No; identify actual cash distributed and avoid counting paper rollover as cash."],"a":3},{"q":"A layered SPV or strip sale provides what assurance?","o":["A layered SPV always bypasses every company consent.","Neither automatically removes transfer restrictions or all adverse selection.","A proportional strip necessarily includes every asset with identical quality.","Either structure guarantees buyer liquidity at the last primary mark."],"a":1}]},"166":{"objectives":["Model debt service, draw conditions, covenants and warrant economics; keep distressed Indian enforcement and duties as a case-specific legal issue map."],"videos":[{"id":"kEz625_ixSg","title":"The Venture Debt Masterclass | Marshall Hawks, Author, Venture Debt Deals","by":"Carta","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. US practitioner stage/rate/draw-frequency views not current Indian terms, universal rules or enforcement law."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"What Is Venture Debt? A Complete Guide for Startup Founders (Razorpay Rize)","url":"https://razorpay.com/rize/blogs/venture-debt-guide-for-startups/","note":"Generic 12-18%/0.5-2% equity/tenure and nonbinding-term-sheet statements are not verified current quotes or universal law; no-significant-dilution framing still needs full warrant/cash modeling."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Venture Debt Explained: How It Works, Providers, and When (The VC Wire)","url":"https://thevcwire.com/venture-debt-explained/","note":"Generic ranges, market-size/default/provider superlatives not validated. Its 1% warrant example uses pre-money shortcut and needs post-issuance/strike/settlement qualification; debt is not literally nondilutive."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Trifecta Capital","url":"https://www.trifectacapital.in","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Advertises venture debt, growth equity and advisory, not executable terms, India-largest ranking or a facility quote."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO: negotiating venture debt term sheet","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/negotiating-the-venture-debt-term-sheet/","note":"Loan/tranche/negative-pledge/warrant-base questions are useful; US/UK comparison not Indian security clearance or guaranteed leverage."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cooley GO: warrant terms","url":"https://www.cooleygo.com/what-you-should-know-about-warrants/","note":"Count/class/strike/exercise/term/corporate-event details matter; not current Indian instrument/tax authorization."}],"exercise":"Private fictional EV-logistics case, not a facility offer, live lender contact, guarantee or recommendation for a real issuer. ARR 60 crore is not cash collection or debt capacity. Add explicit toy inputs: cash 18 crore, constant operating net burn 2 per month before financing costs, no revenue cash surprises, fees/tax/working-capital changes except those specified. A nominal 25 crore facility is not 25 available cash at all times. Stipulate draw 15 at time 0, another 10 at start of month 7 only if all draw conditions remain met;2% of 25 upfront fee=.5 at time 0. Fixed annual 13% interest, monthly simple cash interest on opening drawn principal after any draw. No undrawn fee, compounding, default-rate uplift, prepayment fee or other costs in this toy. Months 1-12 interest-only; months 13-36 repay 25/24 principal monthly at month end. Do not apply 13% to all 25 for 36 months or confuse equal-principal amortization with level EMI.\nCompute the 36 monthly balances, interest, principal, operating cash and covenant headroom. First six months interest 0.1625/month; months 7-12 interest 0.2708333/month. Total interest 5.9854167 crore if all scheduled payments/draws occur; total principal 25, fee 0.5, net financing cash 24.5 before debt service. Cash at end month 12 is 15.9. Stipulate minimum unrestricted month-end cash 6; end month 14 is 9.2862847, month 15 is 5.9963542, hence covenant first breaches at 15 even though cash is positive. A monthly cash model assuming future default-free payment after 15 is a contractual arithmetic schedule, not a feasible approved forecast. A delayed/denied second draw or failed Series C would change survival earlier. Add downside lost draw, increased burn, collection delay and refinancing failure; no guaranteed new round or automatic waiver. A negative pledge is not itself an asset security interest. Definitions of cash, testing dates, permitted debt, draw conditions, cure, cross-default, material-adverse-change and acceleration must come from the actual documents.\nSeparate warrant toy:10% coverage of full 25 facility means 2.5 crore subscription notional, not 10% company equity or 10% interest. At strike 100 rupees this is 250,000 shares. With 25,000,000 existing shares, no other changes, full cash exercise creates 25,250,000 shares and lender owns 0.990099%, contributing 2.5 crore exercise cash. At a stipulated per-share settlement value 300, net intrinsic value is 5 crore before tax/costs, not 7.5 gross share value or an immediately known fair value at issue. A cashless-exercise clause, expiration, class, corporate action and share-value definitions can change outcomes. Coverage may use drawn or committed amount, so the stated base is a toy term, not market law.\nDebt versus an equity alternative requires matched net funding, timing, cash service/repayment, dilution, warrant settlement and exit basis. Do not compare only interest plus warrants against 8% of exit while silently ignoring principal repayment, fee-funded shortfall, timing and different funding amounts. Principal is a cash outflow and remains essential in survival/IRR analysis; distinguish financing charge from total debt service. No universally cheaper instrument follows from the headline percentage. Coupon 11-14% and coverage 5-15% are not verified current Indian quotes; commercial guides report different ranges and they are not market data. A nominally covenant-light 17% offer may be costly, but not proof every reduced covenant is always exactly repriced or every such deal is predatory.\nDistressed memo: compare bridge equity, lender standstill/restructure, going-concern sale and orderly wind-down using dated liquidity, actual security/charge/perfection/priority/intercreditor documents, credible bids, consent, management forecasts and alternative funding. For India, verify lender/entity eligibility and applicable Companies Act/IBC/SARFAESI/contract/IP/guarantee regimes before asserting remedies, priority, transferability, personal liability, creditor duties or an enforcement timeline. A security document does not automatically let a lender immediately seize and operate all IP; an IP negative pledge and perfected security differ. No universal months-to-enforce or blanket company/creditor fiduciary rule is scored here. Preserve records, avoid preferential/conflicted conduct, seek qualified jurisdiction-specific review, and draft conditional options/owners/deadlines, not official board advice, actual insolvency action or lender negotiation. No signed term sheet, bridge funding or permission to pledge assets.","quiz":[{"q":"25 facility,15 drawn initially,13% annual monthly simple interest. First-month interest?","o":["0.1625 crore.","0.2708333 crore.","1.95 crore.","0.325 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"2% fee on 25 at time 0 and full 25 eventually drawn. Net cash before debt service?","o":["24.5 crore.","25 crore.","25.5 crore.","23 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"Months 13-36 equal-principal repayment of 25. Monthly principal?","o":["25/36=.6944444 crore.","25/24=1.0416667 crore.","25/12=2.0833333 crore.","13% of 25=3.25 crore."],"a":1},{"q":"Toy total interest with 15 draw 0/10 draw month 7,12 interest-only then 24 equal-principal months?","o":["6.5 crore.","9.75 crore.","5.9854167 crore.","3.3854167 crore."],"a":2},{"q":"Minimum month-end cash 6. Month 14 cash 9.2863, month 15 cash 5.9964. First covenant breach?","o":["Never if the facility was called covenant-light.","Month 14 because next month may breach.","Only when cash reaches zero.","Month 15, despite positive cash."],"a":3},{"q":"10% warrant coverage of 25 facility at 100 rupees/share. Notional/share count?","o":["0.25 crore/25,000 shares.","25 crore/2,500,000 shares.","10% of company/2,500,000 shares regardless of strike.","2.5 crore/250,000 shares."],"a":3},{"q":"Those 250,000 shares exercised against 25,000,000 existing, no other changes. Lender post stake?","o":["Exactly 10% from coverage.","Exactly 1% using old-share denominator.","About 0.990099%, not 10%.","About 9.0909% from 10/110."],"a":2},{"q":"250,000 warrants, strike 100, stipulated settlement/share 300. Net intrinsic?","o":["7.5 crore, ignoring strike payment.","5 crore, before taxes/costs.","2.5 crore, equal to original coverage notional.","A guaranteed 5 crore fair value at issue."],"a":1},{"q":"A term says IP negative pledge. Does that establish a perfected security interest and immediate Indian seizure right?","o":["Use a familiar US foreclosure process without checking the Indian lender, assets and regime.","Treat a promise not to grant competing liens as equivalent to a perfected security charge.","Infer all asset remedies from the coupon and lender brand without reviewing security documents.","No; inspect actual security, perfection, eligibility, remedies and process."],"a":3},{"q":"Debt is said cheaper than 8% equity by comparing only interest+warrants. Missing check?","o":["Only whether coupon is under 14%, a universal cheaper-than-equity rule.","Only the company headline valuation, since cash service is irrelevant.","Matched net funding, principal repayment, timing, downside and actual warrant/exit basis.","Only lender brand; it removes covenant and refinancing risk."],"a":2}]},"167":{"objectives":["Reconcile a reported 160% revenue-retention claim, separating same-cohort revenue from new logos/pilots and gross-profit economics from NRR."],"videos":[{"id":"nlbX3vfNzcI","title":"Why Your Low Margin AI Company Must Be 6x Larger Than SaaS Peers | The SaaS CFO | AI Tips","by":"The SaaS CFO","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. The 6x headline is not evidence for a universal gross-margin revenue-scaling rule."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Growth for startups : YC Startup Library","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/library/6k-growth-for-startups","note":"Read the specific growth concepts used in the written case; do not infer that one article supplies a complete growth curriculum."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"How to Measure Product-Market Fit","url":"https://review.firstround.com/how-to-measure-product-market-fit/","note":"The 40% survey heuristic and interview/sample advice are practitioner tools, not universal product-market-fit, retention or mastery thresholds."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Screener.in","url":"https://www.screener.in/","note":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case. Short public landing description only. Offline exercise sufficient."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"SaaS Capital: GRR versus NRR","url":"https://www.saas-capital.com/blog-posts/examining-the-gap-between-gross-revenue-retention-and-net-revenue-retention/","note":"Same-period retention, expansion gap and concentration/measurement cautions; survey benchmarks not adopted as case facts or universal thresholds."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Bessemer AI pricing and monetization playbook","url":"https://www.bvp.com/atlas/the-ai-pricing-and-monetization-playbook","note":"Cost/quality/pricing comparison; margin ranges, vendor prices and agent ROI claims not verified benchmarks or guaranteed rules."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IFRS 15 software reseller: principal versus agent","url":"https://www.ifrs.org/content/dam/ifrs/supporting-implementation/agenda-decisions/2022/principal-versus-agent-software-reseller-may-2022.pdf","note":"Fact-pattern comparison, not full IFRS/Ind ASregime or this AI vendor accounting clearance."}],"exercise":"Private fictional AI customer-support diligence, not a verdict on a real vendor. Correct the original metric error explicitly: NRR is a revenue-cohort measure under a disclosed consistent definition, not revenue net of inference costs. Do not relabel cost-subtracted revenue as \"true NRR.\" Report revenue retention and gross-profit/contribution retention separately. Define recurring scope, cohort inclusion, annual/monthly observation basis, currency, price/usage effects, credits/refunds and pilot conversion before comparing periods. A paid recurring pilot can belong under a defensible disclosed policy; do not exclude every pilot reflexively, especially from only one side.\nToy uses matched annual observation dates and annual recurring revenue run-rates, crores, constant currency, no acquisitions or new products requiring accounting reclassification. Initial established paid recurring cohort revenue 10. Between dates cohort churn 1, contraction 1, expansion 4, so ending same cohort 12; separately new-logo recurring 3 and new nonrecurring pilots 1 give reported total 16. Reported 16/10=160% is not same-cohort NRR. Correct NRR=(10-1-1+4)/10=120%; GRR=(10-1-1)/10=80%, excluding expansion and new logos. Reconcile all 16 without deleting inconvenient customers or changing cohort to survivors. All figures are fictional assumptions;160% is an allegation to test, not verified financial performance.\nFor the same established cohort, initial inference cost 2 plus other delivery COGS 1=>gross profit 7, gross margin 70%. Ending inference 5 plus other delivery COGS 1=>gross profit 6, gross margin 50%. Explicitly named same-cohort gross-profit retention=6/7=85.7143%, not NRR 60%, NRR 85.7% or a universal accounting metric. State the numerator/denominator and cost-allocation policy. Human review/support, hosting, retries, failed queries, observability and credits may affect unit costs; sales/R&D and customer acquisition are separate categories unless an applicable accounting policy says otherwise. Retention growth can coexist with lower absolute gross profit. Include customer concentration, renewal obligations, service outcomes, quality/security risks, collection/working capital and sustainable pricing evidence.\nSeparate presentation toy, not a formal accounting conclusion: customer billings 12, model-provider charge 5, no other costs. If the vendor is principal and gross revenue 12 is appropriate, COGS 5 gives GP 7 and GM 58.3333%. If the contract instead supports agent net revenue 7 and no corresponding provider COGS, GP still 7 and displayed GM 100%. Neither identical GP nor a lower/higher margin label alone proves a superior business. Gross 12 while omitting 5 COGS incorrectly shows GP 12/GM 100%. IFRS 15 principal-versus-agent analysis first identifies promised goods/services and control before transfer; pricing discretion alone is not decisive. The 2022 software-reseller agenda decision is a fact-pattern comparison, not Indian AI case accounting clearance. Verify actual reporting framework, contracts, obligations and control; do not call all third-party inference spending pass-through or all gross presentation improper.\nCreate a private reconciliation memo, cohort table, cost bridge and decision gates. Stress inference cost 30% higher at unchanged cohort revenue 12/other COGS 1: new inference 6.5=>GP 4.5/GM 37.5%, GP-retention 4.5/7=64.2857%. This changes cost economics, not revenue NRR 120% by itself. No 6x revenue-size rule follows from the video title; equal-GP scale depends on actual margins, timing and quality. A 20% GM business needs 4x revenue versus 80% GM to match GP, not universally 6x. A PMF survey threshold is an author heuristic with selection limitations, not proof of retention, profitability or investment readiness. Screener public-market ratios do not supply a private AI cohort ledger. No account, customer data upload, outreach, company allegation or actual investment action.","quiz":[{"q":"Toy initial cohort 10; churn 1, contraction 1, expansion 4; new logos 3/pilots 1 outside cohort. NRR?","o":["120%.","160%.","80%.","85.7143%."],"a":0},{"q":"Same toy. GRR excluding expansion?","o":["80%.","120%.","160%.","90%."],"a":0},{"q":"Why is 16/10=160% not the toy NRR?","o":["Numerator includes new logos and nonrecurring pilots absent from the original cohort.","Treat total revenue growth as retention without holding the customer cohort fixed.","Remove churned customers from both numerator and denominator to compare only survivors.","Exclude new customers from the numerator but retain their revenue in the original cohort denominator."],"a":0},{"q":"Cohort initial GP 7, ending GP 6. Explicit same-cohort gross-profit retention?","o":["160%, since all company revenue is recurring cohort cash.","60%, since ending GP 6 is divided by start revenue 10.","120%, since gross profit must grow with revenue NRR.","85.7143%; a separately labeled economic ratio, not revenue NRR."],"a":3},{"q":"Ending cohort revenue 12, inference 5, other delivery COGS 1. GP/GM?","o":["6 crore/50%.","7 crore/58.3333%.","12 crore/100%.","6 crore/60%."],"a":0},{"q":"Increase inference 5 by 30%, revenue 12/other COGS 1 unchanged. GP/GM?","o":["4.5 crore/37.5%.","6.5 crore/54.1667%.","5.1 crore/42.5%.","4.5 crore/64.2857%."],"a":0},{"q":"Does that cost-only stress change revenue NRR 120% by itself?","o":["Yes; subtract the 30% cost increase from 120% NRR.","No; it changes gross-profit economics, not the stated revenue numerator.","Yes; replace NRR with the 37.5% gross margin.","No; cost increases cannot affect any economic decision."],"a":1},{"q":"Billings 12/provider 5, principal gross revenue 12 appropriate. Omit 5 COGS. Result?","o":["Equivalent accounting to agent net 7 with GP 7.","Correct 100% margin because provider charges are never COGS.","Overstates GP from 7 to 12;100% margin is not supported.","Proof all gross presentation is prohibited."],"a":2},{"q":"IFRS 15 software-reseller comparison. Is pricing discretion alone decisive for principal status?","o":["Yes; no inventory always establishes agent status.","Yes; any price discretion automatically establishes principal status.","No; identify specified goods/services and assess control with relevant facts/indicators.","No; every AI vendor must be an agent by definition."],"a":2},{"q":"Equal gross profit at GM 20% versus 80%, same revenue basis. Revenue scale needed for low-margin firm?","o":["4x, conditional on those margins, not a universal 6x rule.","6x for every AI business.","0.25x, because lower margin lowers required revenue.","1x, because revenue NRR alone sets gross profit."],"a":0}]},"168":{"objectives":["Use a fresh transfer case and item-level error evidence to separate revenue-cohort, cost and cash mistakes without inventing learner weaknesses."],"videos":[{"id":"nlbX3vfNzcI","title":"Why Your Low Margin AI Company Must Be 6x Larger Than SaaS Peers | The SaaS CFO | AI Tips","by":"The SaaS CFO","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. The 6x headline is not evidence for a universal gross-margin revenue-scaling rule."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Growth for startups : YC Startup Library","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/library/6k-growth-for-startups","note":"Read the specific growth concepts used in the written case; do not infer that one article supplies a complete growth curriculum."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"How to Measure Product-Market Fit","url":"https://review.firstround.com/how-to-measure-product-market-fit/","note":"Use this reference to study How to Measure Product-Market Fit."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Screener.in","url":"https://www.screener.in/","note":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case. Short public landing description only. Offline exercise sufficient."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Retrieval with varied examples and transfer","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29265856/","note":"Summary or description; use the written exercise for the worked case. Four geology-learning experiments and two-day novel tests support varied-example rationale; no financial-domain effect size or universal timing/mastery rule claimed."}],"exercise":"Private review of the week, using a fresh fictional variant, not a claim about this user's measured weaknesses or an actual board decision. Replace generic \"weakest skill areas as an economic metric\" with a tagged application test. First solve without notes; then use answer rationales, classify errors and correct calculations. Keep original answer, confidence, time if actually measured, evidence/source scope, error cause and revised reasoning; do not backfill a speed metric or mark a guess as mastery. A day total does not identify which skills failed. Select two remediation areas from actual item-level evidence with sufficient items; if evidence is missing/tied, state that and assign an exploratory diagnostic rather than inventing weaknesses.\nFresh same-currency annual AI cohort: starting recurring revenue 8 crore, churn 1, contraction 0.5, expansion 2.5. Established-cohort ending 9; new-logo recurring 2 plus new nonrecurring pilot 1 give total 12. Reported 12/8=150% is not cohort NRR. Correct NRR 9/8=112.5%, GRR(8-1-.5)/8=81.25%. Established cohort initial delivery COGS 2/GP 6; ending COGS 4.5/GP 4.5. Separately labeled gross-profit retention 75%, ending GM 50%, not NRR 75%. Revised fact: expansion is actually 1.5 not 2.5, no other changes. Ending cohort 8 and total 11; NRR 100%, GRR 81.25% unchanged, GP 3.5 and GP-retention 58.3333%, GM 43.75%. Show every changed numerator and unchanged denominator. Revenue correction alone does not establish when cash arrives.\nCash transfer case: fund LP paid-in 100, prior cash distributions 30, position marked 40, other NAV 20. A direct secondary closes for 36 gross, selling costs 1, all 35 net immediately distributed with no further deductions. Before DPI0.3/TVPI0.9; after DPI0.65/TVPI0.85. Remove 40 sold NAV rather than counting sale and mark together. A continuation paper roll of 40 instead of distribution would not by itself create 40 cash DPI. A nonbinding 36 quote alone is neither a close nor a distribution.\nDebt transfer case: independent loan draw 12 crore,12% annual simple interest paid monthly on drawn balance, first 3 months interest-only, then equal principal over 12 months, no fees/extra draws/default/capitalization. Initial monthly interest 0.12; first amortizing month principal 1 plus interest 0.12=1.12 debt service, before operating burn. After that principal balance 11, next monthly interest 0.11. Stipulated covenant month-end unrestricted cash 4 and forecast cash 3.9 means a test breach, not automatic zero cash, waiver or immediate legal seizure. Actual enforcement remains a legal/document question. These transfer numbers differ from D166, so copying its answer is wrong.\nSuggested item tags: cohort/denominator, cost-vs-revenue, cash-vs-mark, debt-timing, source/authority. Include counterexamples and novel numbers, not reordered familiar questions as proof of transfer. If a fictional sample log has cohort 2/4 correct and cash 1/4 correct, rates 50%/25% describe that sample, not this learner or calibrated domain ability. With only those data, cash is lower but both have tiny samples; do not infer every unmeasured area is stronger. Use attempted/scheduled/missing denominators explicitly. Set a future retest with changed numbers, the same conceptual tags, feedback and a clear success criterion. Any 8/10 criterion is a classroom gate, not validated mastery or proof that each required skill is sound. The Butler et al.2017/2018 abstract describes four geology-learning experiments with varied examples and a two-day test, not a guaranteed effect size or universal best spacing for financial diligence. No actual reminder, calendar, report or learning-profile mutation.","quiz":[{"q":"Variant start 8, churn 1, contraction 0.5, expansion 2.5; new logos 2/pilot 1 outside cohort. NRR?","o":["81.25%.","150%.","112.5%.","75%."],"a":2},{"q":"Same variant. GRR?","o":["68.75%.","112.5%.","87.5%.","81.25%."],"a":3},{"q":"Initial cohort GP 6, ending GP 4.5. GP-retention and revenue NRR?","o":["75% NRR and 112.5% GP-retention.","75% GP-retention and 112.5% NRR, distinct ratios.","150% for both ratios.","50% for both ratios."],"a":1},{"q":"Expansion corrected from 2.5 to 1.5, other inputs unchanged. Revised NRR/GRR?","o":["100%/81.25%.","100%/68.75%.","112.5%/81.25%.","87.5%/87.5%."],"a":0},{"q":"That corrected cohort 8, ending COGS 4.5, original GP 6. New GP/GP-retention?","o":["3.5 crore/43.75%.","4.5 crore/75%.","3.5 crore/58.3333%.","8 crore/133.3333%."],"a":2},{"q":"Fund paid-in 100, prior distributions 30; secondary 36 gross/1 cost=>35 net immediately distributed. After DPI?","o":["1.05x.","0.66x.","0.85x.","0.65x."],"a":3},{"q":"Same fund: sold mark 40 removed, other NAV 20. Post TVPI?","o":["0.65x, excluding remaining 20 NAV.","1.25x, retaining sold 40 NAV.","0.85x, compared with 0.9x before.","0.9x, since sales cannot reduce marks."],"a":2},{"q":"Fresh loan 12,12% annual,3 interest-only months, then 12 equal-principal months. First amortizing debt service?","o":["0.12 crore because principal never starts.","1.12 crore before operating burn.","1 crore because interest-only means interest ends.","1.0416667 crore copied from D166."],"a":1},{"q":"Day total 6/10 with no item tags. Can it identify weakest two areas?","o":["Rank domain weaknesses from day totals without observing which items were missed.","No; recover item-level evidence or run a tagged diagnostic.","Treat every missed item as equally diagnostic despite missing skill tags.","Infer untested skills are stronger merely because they contributed no recorded wrong answers."],"a":1},{"q":"A retest repeats the same ten questions with shuffled option order. Evidence of transfer?","o":["Limited; use new examples/numbers with matched concepts and inspect reasoning.","Complete proof of novel application and durable mastery.","Equivalent to a blind novel case in every setting.","No learning is possible from repeated retrieval of any kind."],"a":0}]},"169":{"objectives":["Reconcile city-level marketplace GMV, platform fees, subsidies, retention and cash using matched cohorts and explicit accounting assumptions."],"videos":[{"id":"KKyBuzMWSeE","title":"Overview of Two-Sided Marketplace Metrics","by":"Eric Andrews Clips","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Marketplace metrics: definitions and local cohorts","url":"https://a16z.com/13-metrics-for-marketplace-companies/","note":"Use this reference to study Marketplace metrics: definitions and local cohorts."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"GMV retention versus user retention","url":"https://a16z.com/gmv-retention-the-marketplace-metric-most-ignore/","note":"Dated benchmarks are not universal gates."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cold-start approaches and network density","url":"https://andrewchen.com/how-to-solve-the-cold-start-problem-for-social-products/","note":"Social-product examples are not guaranteed marketplace outcomes."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Principles of Microeconomics 3 e","url":"https://openstax.org/details/books/principles-microeconomics-3e","note":"Publisher summary/authors/edition landing read, not full textbook or specific chapter. Offline toy does not need Screener account access."}],"exercise":"Private fictional two-city marketplace exercise, not investment advice or a live experiment. Use one completed month, rupees, no GST, tips, refunds, cancellations, advertising, bad debt or other fees. Assume an agent presentation is appropriate under the actual contract/control analysis for this toy: platform revenue is the stipulated commission, not all GMV. Principal-versus-agent presentation is a separate accounting judgment; a marketplace label alone does not settle it. Define gross/net GMV, fulfilled orders, payer, subsidy funding and fee base before calculating any take rate.\nCity A: 10,000 fulfilled orders at 500 average order value; 2,000 subsidized first orders and 8,000 unsubsidized repeat orders. Commission 20% of GMV. Variable platform delivery/payment/support cost 60 per fulfilled order, excludes seller payout. Platform-funded first-order promotion 100 per first order, treated as separate analytical acquisition cost for this exercise, not a universal accounting classification. No repeat promotions. City B: 8,000 fulfilled orders at 500; 4,000 first and 4,000 repeat. Commission 20%, variable platform cost 90/order and first-order promotion 100. Seller payout is 80% of GMV and is not deducted a second time from commission revenue.\nA GMV 50 lakh, commission 10 lakh, variable cost 6 lakh, first promotions 2 lakh, contribution 2 lakh. Repeat contribution 8,000*(100-60)=3.2 lakh. First-order contribution 2,000*(100-60-100)=-1.2 lakh. B GMV 40 lakh, commission 8 lakh, variable cost 7.2 lakh, promotions 4 lakh, contribution -3.2 lakh. Repeat contribution 0.4 lakh and first-order contribution -3.6 lakh. Combined GMV 90 lakh, commission 18 lakh, contribution -1.2 lakh. Incremental avoidable central fixed cost 1 lakh in this same month gives -2.2 lakh after that cost, not EBITDA or cash flow for an unspecified corporate scope. Positive repeat contribution in both cities does not establish acquisition payback, city profitability or product-market fit.\nCohort check independent of monthly city totals: City A January first-time cohort 2,000 users/GMV 10 lakh; of exactly those users, 600 transact in February with GMV 6 lakh. One-month user retention 30%, GMV retention 60%. Do not put new February users in either matched-cohort numerator or use all 8,000 repeat orders as retained users. Order count, unique buyers and GMV retention are different denominators. A returning user can place multiple orders. Align age, geography, supply-side versus demand-side, refunds and observation windows; concentration or promotion changes can explain apparently improved curves.\nLiquidity of matching is not balance-sheet cash: proposed City A request log has 12,500 valid unique purchase requests in the same month, 10,000 fulfilled, so fulfillment 80% under that definition, not a universal market match-rate rule. Separately quantify failed searches, unavailable supply, cancellations and wait-time distribution without dropping failures. A larger seller count alone does not guarantee useful depth or faster matches. Compare categories and neighborhoods, not only city averages.\nSeparate cash timing toy: day 0 platform pays merchants 8 lakh for a completed settlement batch; customer collections 10 lakh arrive on day 3. Before operating costs, the temporary cash requirement is 8 lakh; final net cash is 2 lakh. Customer/merchant funds, contractual settlement and applicable payment rules must be mapped; this arithmetic is not permission to use escrow balances as operating cash. Reconcile promotions, fees, refunds, payables and restricted cash to actual statements before using real data.\nPropose a permission-reviewed matched city/cohort test of subsidy changes with service quality, repeat contribution, retention and cash guardrails. Stopping first-order discounts does not prove acquired users retain, and a naive before/after test is confounded by seasonality, mix and selection. Predefine gate, owner, observation window and stop conditions; no actual customer experiment, outreach, uploads or subscription required.","quiz":[{"q":"Toy City A: 10,000 orders at 500 and 20% commission, agent presentation stipulated. Platform commission revenue?","o":["8 lakh.","50 lakh.","40 lakh.","10 lakh."],"a":3},{"q":"City A: fee 100/order, variable cost 60, 8,000 unsubsidized repeat orders. Repeat contribution?","o":["2 lakh.","8 lakh.","4.8 lakh.","3.2 lakh."],"a":3},{"q":"City A first orders 2,000, fee 100, variable cost 60, first promotion 100. First contribution?","o":["2 lakh.","0.8 lakh.","-2 lakh.","-1.2 lakh."],"a":3},{"q":"City B 8,000 orders, fee 100, variable cost 90, 4,000 first promotions of 100. Contribution?","o":["-3.2 lakh.","0.8 lakh.","-4 lakh.","0.4 lakh."],"a":0},{"q":"Combined A contribution 2 lakh/B -3.2 lakh, incremental central fixed 1 lakh in the same month. After that cost?","o":["-2.2 lakh.","-1.2 lakh.","-0.2 lakh.","17 lakh."],"a":0},{"q":"Original cohort 2,000 users/10 lakh GMV; next month 600 of those users/6 lakh GMV. User/GMV retention?","o":["30%/60%.","60%/30%.","40%/60%.","30%/100%."],"a":0},{"q":"Request log 12,500 valid requests, 10,000 fulfilled. Defined fulfillment rate?","o":["30%, substituting cohort retention.","100%, dividing fulfilled by fulfilled.","125%, dividing requests by fulfilled.","80%, retaining failed valid requests in the denominator."],"a":3},{"q":"Merchant payment 8 lakh on day 0; customer collection 10 lakh on day 3, no other flows. Peak temporary funding need before collection?","o":["8 lakh, distinct from the final 2 lakh net cash.","2 lakh, because only final margin needs funding.","10 lakh, the eventual customer receipt.","Zero, because the batch is profitable."],"a":0},{"q":"Agent fee revenue 18 lakh was computed from 90 lakh GMV net of 72 lakh seller payout. Deduct seller payout again?","o":["Yes; subtracting both the fee-basis seller payout and GMV is a reconciliation adjustment.","No; that double-counts seller economics under the stipulated fee basis.","Yes; subtract 72 lakh again to turn platform fee contribution into merchant gross profit.","No; therefore merchant settlement and payment timing need no separate cash reconciliation."],"a":1},{"q":"Repeat contribution is positive in both cities. What remains unresolved?","o":["The larger city must be healthier because its GMV is higher, without matching age or costs.","Repeat-order margin alone proves that each newly acquired buyer repays acquisition cost.","First-order acquisition payback, matched retention, fixed costs and cash timing.","Removing first-order promotion can be assumed to leave conversion and repeat behavior unchanged."],"a":2}]},"170":{"objectives":["Separate a health-credit app's LSP role from its lender, model same-horizon expected-loss economics and trace funds, consent and document-specific duties without assuming a healthcare exemption."],"videos":[{"id":"f_pi8eoBRFU","title":"Fintech Body FACE Drafts Self Regulatory Rules For Digital Lending May Apply For SRO Status With RBI","by":"CNBC-TV18","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. A draft SRO-news headline is not current lender authorization or operative directions."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"NBFC Credit Facilities: current displayed consolidation","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=12957","note":"28 November 2025, displayed updated 15 July 2026. Read applicability/definitions/Board, whole digital Chapter III and Chapter X/savings/footnotes; not whole regime or separate IRACP."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NBFC credit-facility amendment","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=13293&Mode=0","note":"February 13, 2026 amendment: paragraph 25(1) points to separate NBFC IRACP Directions for asset classification and provisioning. Apply the relevant current provisions before calculating a real provision."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NBFC registration-framework amendment","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=13407&Mode=0","note":"April 29, 2026 registration amendment, effective July 1, 2026. Read Type I, exemption and aggregation conditions; it is not permission to launch a public health-lending app."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"NBFC credit-facility second amendment","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=13561&Mode=0","note":"July 15, 2026 amendment concerning project-finance explanations, not blanket lending clearance."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Earlier digital-lending consolidation","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=12848","note":"Do not treat as sole current rule for every regulated lender."}],"exercise":"Private fictional health-financing diligence, not lending, patient-data processing or regulatory clearance. A health brand, hospital partnership, app-store listing or RBI DLA-directory entry does not itself establish authority to lend. Map the actual lender legal entity and category, app/LSP, hospitals, processors, underwriting, collections, liability, agreements and funds. Use the appropriate current lender-specific instruments. The RBI NBFC Credit Facilities Directions issued 28 November 2025, displayed updated 15 July 2026, have a digital-lending chapter; the earlier 8 May 2025 consolidated directions are historical context, not automatically the sole current rule for every lender.\nSource reading covers NBFC applicability/exclusions, definitions, Chapter III digital lending and Chapter X repeal/savings, plus February/April/July 2026 amendments, not the full prudential/registration/accounting regime. Do not silently fix the displayed LSP-definition cross-reference inconsistency or claim this exercise grants an NBFC license. For a real entity, resolve exact category, applicable amended instruments and governing documents with qualified review.\nAccording to the read NBFC digital-lending chapter, the NBFC remains responsible for its outsourced LSP; the agreement defines roles and enhanced diligence/monitoring. Trace end-to-end flow. Standard disbursement is to the borrower account, with specified statutory/co-lending/specific-end-use exceptions; direct payment to the actual end-beneficiary for a specific end use is not the same as routing it to the LSP's general pool. A direct hospital payment needs documented end-use and actual conditions, not a healthcare exemption. Borrower servicing/repayment is directly to the NBFC account without an LSP pass-through; delinquent cash collection has a qualified same-day reflection exception. LSP charges payable are paid by the NBFC, not separately collected from borrowers by the LSP. Do not treat these limited excerpts as all payment/accounting permissions.\nNeed-based data collection requires prior explicit consent with audit trail; mobile contacts/call logs/files are not made acceptable just by a broad permission dialog. The chapter permits necessary one-time onboarding/KYC access such as camera/microphone/location with explicit consent, not ongoing unrelated surveillance. Document purpose, access, storage, sharing, revocation, deletion/retention duties and exceptions under the actual applicable rules. Health treatment consent is not blanket credit scoring, marketing or data-sale consent. Never upload real patient data for the exercise. KFS/APR, lender documents, complaint roles and cooling-off terms require source-specific review; the read chapter's minimum cooling-off is one day and disclosed reasonable one-time processing fee can be retained, not universally cost-free cancellation. No scored current-law deadline or personalized approval is inferred from a 2023 SRO-news video.\nEconomics toy, explicitly not statutory provisioning/ECL or a borrower APR calculation: one 12-month bullet loan cohort, 100 crore drawn at time 0 and outstanding until maturity; no interim principal, prepayments, capitalized fees, timing discount or recoveries except in the specified LGD. Assume contractual interest 18 crore but expected collected interest 16 crore, funding cost 8 crore for the same full year, acquisition 2 crore, servicing/collections 1 crore, and expected principal-loss approximation EAD*PD*LGD with EAD 100, 12-month PD 5%, LGD 60% inclusive of recoveries/costs. Expected loss 3 crore. Risk-adjusted analytical contribution 16-8-2-1-3=2 crore, before tax, overhead, capital charge and other omitted costs. Do not use 18 while also assuming no collection shortfall. PD is obligor default probability over a named horizon, LGD loss conditional on default, EAD exposure at default; this simplified single-segment toy is not a calibrated accounting model or guarantee of realized loss. Loss realization and provisions need not equal this expected amount or cash in any given year.\nDownside: PD 10%, same EAD/LGD and all other inputs unchanged, gives expected loss 6 and contribution -1 crore. Break-even PD under this exact toy=(16-8-2-1)/(100*.60)=8.3333%, not a legal lending cap or investment hurdle. The lender contribution is not automatically the LSP's revenue/profit. Separate LSP contract toy: lender pays fee 2 crore, LSP operating cost 1.2 crore, analytical contribution 0.8 before tax/overhead/guarantee costs. This fee is an incremental lender cost unless already included in an identified line; do not subtract it twice or silently add it to the first toy.\nA DLG is not a replacement for underwriting or automatic borrower debt cancellation. Read current lender-specific eligibility, permissible forms, fixed portfolio, cap, invocation/recoveries, disclosure and prudential treatment before a real arrangement. The February 2026 amendment refers asset classification and provisioning to separate NBFC IRACP Directions. Consult the applicable current provisions and exceptions before concluding that a particular loan receives relief or no relief. No real guarantee, license application, hospital/lender contact, credit offer or payment action.","quiz":[{"q":"Toy EAD 100 crore, 12-month PD 5%, LGD 60% inclusive of recoveries. Expected principal loss?","o":["3 crore.","5 crore.","60 crore.","0.3 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"Expected collected interest 16, funding 8, acquisition 2, servicing 1, expected loss 3 crore. Contribution?","o":["-1 crore using downside loss.","4 crore using contractual interest 18.","5 crore excluding expected loss.","2 crore before omitted costs."],"a":3},{"q":"PD doubles to 10%, EAD 100/LGD 60% and other inputs unchanged. Loss/contribution?","o":["6 crore/1 crore.","3 crore/2 crore.","10 crore/-5 crore.","6 crore/-1 crore."],"a":3},{"q":"Under that toy, contribution before principal loss is 5 on EAD 100/LGD 60%. Break-even PD?","o":["3%, copying baseline expected loss.","5%, copying pre-loss contribution as PD.","8.3333%, not a legal lending threshold.","60%, copying LGD as PD."],"a":2},{"q":"Separate LSP fee 2 crore/cost 1.2, no other costs. LSP contribution?","o":["-1.2 crore, treating fee as borrower principal.","2 crore, ignoring operating costs.","3.2 crore, adding fee and cost.","0.8 crore; it is not the lender's 2 crore contribution."],"a":3},{"q":"A projected expected principal loss is 3 crore. Does it establish actual cash loss or statutory provisioning?","o":["Yes; every cohort realizes its expected loss exactly.","No; it is a disclosed scenario approximation requiring separate cash/accounting reconciliation.","Yes; it is the full current NBFC IRACP requirement.","No; expected losses must never enter lending analysis."],"a":1},{"q":"A health app appears in a DLA directory. What can that alone establish?","o":["That its hospital partner is the regulated lender.","Its own unrestricted lending license.","A waiver of borrower disclosure and data requirements.","Directory inclusion, not lending authorization or RBI endorsement."],"a":3},{"q":"Hospital is the documented end-beneficiary of a specific-use loan. What must analysis avoid?","o":["Distinguishing the borrower and hospital accounts.","Treating the exception as permission for an LSP general collection pool or a healthcare exemption.","Checking lender category and documented conditions.","Separating disbursement from servicing/repayment flows."],"a":1},{"q":"App asks for contacts and call logs using a broad consent box. Best review conclusion?","o":["Treatment consent automatically covers all credit marketing.","Consent makes every phone resource acceptable for underwriting.","Broad consent is not enough; check need-based limits and prohibited resource access under applicable lender rules.","A privacy-policy link alone proves compliant data collection."],"a":2},{"q":"Original May 2025 reading and newer NBFC consolidation differ in provisioning references. What is safe?","o":["Treat the older wording as current for every lender.","Use the applicable updated lender-specific text and inspect referenced IRACP rules before scoring a prudential claim.","Infer a DLG eliminates all default risk and loan liability.","Apply a news video instead of operative amendments."],"a":1}]},"171":{"objectives":["Test a factory thermal-storage proposal using explicit heat/power boundaries, field availability, discounted savings and warranty/working-capital exposure."],"videos":[{"id":"p9PNTohyrY8","title":"Is Your Deep-Tech Startup Actually Ready for Investors? Here's How to Know.","by":"SEMSCIO","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Practitioner readiness/risk framing, not measured case readiness or universal fundraise timing."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"DOE thermal-energy-storage assessment","url":"https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2023-07/Technology%20Strategy%20Assessment%20-%20Thermal%20Energy%20Storage.pdf","note":"July 2023 draft label; specific modeled 2030 LCOS is not this field case."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Renewable thermal systems for industrial process heat","url":"https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy22osti/81146.pdf","note":"Historical US price/site models, not current India quotes."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"MIT Technology Strategy lecture index","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-912-technology-strategy-fall-2008/pages/lecture-notes/","note":"Use this reference to study MIT Technology Strategy lecture index."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IPIndia","url":"https://ipindia.gov.in/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Google Patents","url":"https://patents.google.com/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms."}],"exercise":"Private fictional industrial heat-storage case, not engineering certification, patent clearance or a capital commitment. Specify useful delivered heat at required process temperature/pressure/flow, input electricity or heat, charging window, holding duration, parasitics, losses, duty cycle and site integration. MWth is thermal power; MWhth is thermal energy. Do not compare heat-output efficiency directly with electricity-to-electricity RTE, heat-pump COP or an unrelated bidirectional LCOS forecast. DOE July 2023 report is a draft-labeled assessment using specific technologies and expert/model estimates; NREL 2021 conference slides use US-site models and historical prices. Neither validates this startup's 2026 field performance or Indian tariff.\nToy same annual operating horizon: eligible process demand 3,000 MWhth, system scheduled for 1,000 operating hours at useful delivered power 3 MWth. Field availability 90% yields 2,700 MWhth delivered, with remaining 300 MWhth supplied by unchanged backup. Assume constant efficiency 80% from billed electric charging input to useful delivered process heat, including holding/auxiliary losses under the defined duty cycle; no heat-pump ambient-heat gain. Required billed input=2700/0.8=3375 MWhe. This is not a universal technology efficiency. Do not multiply availability a second time if measured output already includes downtime.\nStipulated electricity price 3000 rupees/MWhe, no demand charge/GST/export credit; counterfactual useful process heat cost 6500 rupees/MWhth and enough backup capacity. Baseline displaced cost 2700*6500=1.755 crore, charging cost 3375*3000=1.0125 crore, annual net operating saving before maintenance=0.7425 crore. Maintenance 0.1 crore/year leaves 0.6425 crore. Installed capital 4 crore paid time 0, no subsidy, tax, depreciation, residual value, degradation, salvage, financing or replacement beyond maintenance; ten equal year-end savings at 10% annual discount. Simple payback 4/0.6425=6.22568 years is not discounted payback or guaranteed recovery. NPV=-4+.6425*sum(t 1 to 10)(1.1^-t)=-0.0521156 crore approximately, so positive annual savings do not alone give positive NPV at this discount/horizon. Show units, sensitivity and omitted project/site risks. Compute from full precision, not rounded cash flows.\nDownside independent from baseline: field availability falls 80%, scheduled 1,000 hours/3 MWth unchanged; output 2400 MWhth, efficiency 70%, input 3428.5714 MWhe, price still 3000 and displaced heat 6500, maintenance 0.1 crore. Net annual saving=0.4314286 crore; simple payback 9.27152 years, ten-year 10% NPV approximately -1.3490582 crore. Backup energy increases 600 MWhth, not a zero-output plant. No claim that availability alone equals efficiency or uptime of the whole factory. Delivering low-temperature heat below required process quality may not qualify as useful output even if an energy meter records it.\nManufacturer economics is a separate toy from customer project savings: unit selling price 1 crore, build/install cost 0.70 crore, expected lifetime warranty cost 0.05 crore per unit, no other costs. Analytical contribution 0.25 crore, not 0.30 crore. Warranty estimate is not necessarily same-period cash or a complete accounting provision. With ten identical units, stated contribution 2.5 crore before central overhead/tax/finance and omitted liabilities. If customers pay 30% upfront,60% on acceptance and 10% after 12 months, and all 0.70 crore build/install cash is paid before acceptance, peak pre-acceptance funding gap per unit=0.40 crore; post-acceptance net cash 0.20 crore before warranty/other costs and retained receivable 0.10 crore. No automatic capital savings merely from fixed-cost allocation.\nStage gates: reproducible lab tests with calibrated boundaries; representative integrated field pilot across real duty cycles/seasonality and failure modes; independent safety/site acceptance and permitted installation; contractual warranty/service/parts plan and cash buffer; commercial scale only after conditional technical/economic/customer gates. Proposed gates are a workplan, not measured timing, signed customer acceptance or real permission to run equipment. A letter of intent is not contracted cash. Patent search results can help identify claims/families/jurisdictions/status but not prove ownership, validity, patentability or freedom to operate. Google Patents/IPIndia landings are not a legal search opinion. No real site data upload, customer contact, order or operation.","quiz":[{"q":"Scheduled 1,000 hours at 3 MWth, field availability 90%, no other output loss. Useful annual heat?","o":["3,333.333 MWhth.","3,000 MWhth.","2,700 MWth.","2,700 MWhth."],"a":3},{"q":"Useful heat 2,700 MWhth at 80% defined electric-to-useful-heat efficiency. Billed input?","o":["2,700 MWhe.","2,160 MWhe.","3,375 MWhe.","3,000 MWhe."],"a":2},{"q":"Input 3,375 MWhe at 3000 rupees/MWhe. Charging cost?","o":["1.755 crore.","10.125 crore.","0.8100 crore.","1.0125 crore."],"a":3},{"q":"Displaced cost 1.755 crore, charging 1.0125, maintenance 0.1 annually. Net saving?","o":["0.6425 crore.","0.7425 crore.","0.8425 crore.","1.655 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"Capital 4 crore and equal annual savings 0.6425 crore. Simple payback?","o":["Exactly 10 years, copied from model horizon.","About 0.161 years, reversing the ratio.","About 6.226 years, before discounting and omitted risks.","About 5.387 years, omitting maintenance."],"a":2},{"q":"Ten year-end savings 0.6425, 10% discount, capital 4. Positive savings imply positive NPV?","o":["Yes; a positive margin sets discount rate to zero.","Yes; undiscounted ten-year savings exceed capital.","No; the toy NPV is slightly negative, about -0.052 crore.","No; all energy-storage projects have negative NPV."],"a":2},{"q":"Output 2400 MWhth, efficiency 70%, price 3000/MWhe. Charging input/cost?","o":["1680 MWhe/0.504 crore.","3428.5714 MWhe/about 1.0285714 crore.","3000 MWhe/0.9 crore.","3375 MWhe/1.0125 crore copied from baseline."],"a":1},{"q":"Unit price 1 crore/build-install 0.70/expected warranty 0.05. Analytical contribution?","o":["0.35 crore, adding warranty.","0.30 crore, omitting warranty.","0.25 crore, before omitted costs.","0.95 crore, omitting production."],"a":2},{"q":"Customer prepays 0.30 crore; manufacturer spends 0.70 before acceptance. Peak pre-acceptance gap?","o":["0.10 crore, substituting final retention.","0.70 crore, ignoring deposit.","0.40 crore per unit, before other costs.","Zero, because a 1 crore sale is profitable."],"a":2},{"q":"A startup shows laboratory efficiency and a patent-search hit. What is still needed?","o":["Use the lab test boundary for field efficiency without checking site parasitics or heat quality.","Representative integrated field/safety/economic evidence and separate rights/FTO review.","Treat the patent-search result as proof of valid ownership and freedom to operate.","Use positive annual savings as proof that discounted project value and warranty funding are adequate."],"a":1}]},"172":{"objectives":["Build a role-, entity-, activity- and date-specific permission map for payments, lending and health data; separate future readiness costs from actual launch authority."],"videos":[{"id":"cCngMCszXks","title":"RBI, SEBI, CPCB: Important Regulatory Bodies in India Explained | UPSC Prelims 2026 | Shyam Kaggod","by":"Unacademy IAS: English","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Verify operative law independently."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"PA Directions 2025: roles, category paths and escrow","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=12896","note":"Use the current PA Directions for the activity and fund-flow definitions. Check separate operative sources before making any MDR claim."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"DPDP Act phased commencement: GSR 843(E)","url":"https://www.meity.gov.in/static/uploads/2025/11/c56ceae6c383460ca69577428d36828b.pdf","note":"Commencement notification with immediate, one-year and eighteen-month groups. It does not put every part of the Act into force at once."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Final DPDP Rules 2025: GSR 846(E)","url":"https://www.meity.gov.in/static/uploads/2025/11/53450e6e5dc0bfa85ebd78686cadad39.pdf","note":"Read English Rules 1-23/First-Fourth Schedules/Fifth opening, not all Hindi/remaining administrative schedules. Apply phasing and corrigendum; not case clearance."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"DPDP Rules corrigendum: GSR 892(E)","url":"https://www.meity.gov.in/static/uploads/2025/12/3c7ebbae0e5456f493f486e6845df86b.pdf","note":"December 10, 2025 corrigendum containing eight text corrections. It is not a new blanket implementation date."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"DPDP 2026 wording order: S.O.5458(E)","url":"https://www.meity.gov.in/static/uploads/2026/10/daf495a7b5798a3dfa72a944b51fc176.pdf","note":"Narrow 9(1)/10(2)(c)(ii) wording changes, not launch authority or blanket commencement."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Open Government Data India","url":"https://data.gov.in/","note":"Government dataset directory, not a license registry. Confirm any permission with the issuing authority and the actual entity/activity conditions."}],"exercise":"Private fictional expansion review, not a license application, legal opinion, data disclosure or authorization to launch. Replace the original economic metric of \"entity-specific permissions and state variation\" with an evidence matrix: permissions are conditions/constraints, not revenue or a margin denominator. For each legal entity, product/activity, customer/state, money/data flow and date, record regulator/instrument/provision, applicability/exemptions, current status and evidence, conditions, owner, last check and unresolved gate. Mark unknown explicitly, not green because another group company is licensed.\nMap a payment gateway technology-only role separately from aggregation/handling/settlement of merchant funds; map lender versus LSP, healthcare provider versus health-data platform, Data Fiduciary versus processor and any consent-manager role. The RBI PA Directions dated 15 September 2025 define PA/PG and bank/non-bank rules; an existing authorized PA's expansion into another PA category has an intimation route in that source, not a universal need for a brand-new license or a right to undertake lending. A non-bank applicant, pending application, CoA holder and a bank have different paths. Inspect actual CoA/category/conditions and amendments before any real conclusion. Payment escrow is not unrestricted working capital; permitted debits have detailed conditions. Do not repurpose merchant funds to finance health loans. State clinical-establishment, professional, local, contract, cyber and other applicable regimes need their own source checks; no state-specific rule is confirmed from a regulator homepage or dataset listing.\nDPDP legal reading is explicitly staged and bounded. Act 2023 section 1(2)allows different commencement dates. Official GSR 843(E)13 November 2025 appoints immediate, one-year and eighteen-month groups; GSR 846(E)final Rules 2025 rule 1 similarly phases rules 1/2/17-21 immediately, rule 4 after one year and 3/5-16/22/23 after eighteen months from publication. GSR 892(E)December 2025 corrects wording, including publication phrasing. As of 10 November 2026, that original November 2025 one-year/18-month schedule cannot justify saying all substantive DPDP rules are already in force; check later operative changes, exact publication date and applicability before a real dated decision. Do not infer a commencement date from an upload filename or index publication year. Deferred DPDP provisions do not imply no present privacy/security/sector/contract duties. This exercise does not clear IT Act/SPDI, RBI, health, professional or other obligations.\nRead final Rules notice, consent-manager, safeguards, breach, retention, child/guardian, SDF, rights, transfer, research and schedules with their scope. The child-health exception in rule 12/Fourth Schedule is limited to specified classes and necessary health-service/treatment purposes, not an exemption for every health-financing/advertising app or all personal data. A consent-management UX is not automatically a registered statutory Consent Manager or permission to read every underlying record. The October 2026 S.O.5458(E) order published by MeitY changes two phrases in 9(1)/10(2)(c)(ii); it does not by itself activate every substantive provision or approve this business. Read the Rules and schedules alongside the complete amended Act and any applicable administrative instruments before a real legal decision. Resolve case-specific legal requirements before treating an analysis as permission to act.\nEvidence-quality test: a PDF titled Rules in a search result was a government consultation-feedback summary, not final operative rules. Use actual document text, notification number, signature, commencement, amendments, exceptions and source date. A third-party page calling its list \"Official sources\" is only a link lead; verify each operative document on its government publisher. A dated exam overview of regulator names is not entity/activity permission and may contain transcription errors. Do not count a homepage, application receipt, self-declaration, uploaded form, paid fee or provisional board discussion as a granted license or government endorsement.\nIndependent fictional readiness-cost toy, not fees/penalties prescribed by law: opening unrestricted corporate cash 5 crore, upfront control-build cost 1 crore, monthly operating burn 0.4 crore, plus compliance operations 0.1 crore/month, no revenue/funding/tax/restricted merchant balances. At six month-end periods cash=5-1-6*.5=1 crore. A further three-month delay with same costs gives -0.5 crore; threshold 2 crore first falls below at month 5(cash 1.5), not month 4(cash 2). Negative cash is a financing gap, not authority to use escrow, ignore a gate or assume extension. A compliant cheaper launch is preferable only if its full scope and permissions are established; expected penalties are not a price for illegal operation.\nPropose narrow permissible pilot/readiness alternatives, data-minimization and synthetic testing, evidence owners and stop conditions while legal gates stay unresolved. Conditional recommendation only; no actual patient-data upload, account opening, permissions change, regulator contact, capital transfer or activity launch.","quiz":[{"q":"A group company has a PA authorization. What must the expansion matrix still identify?","o":["Treat the brand as the licensed entity for all services.","Actual entity, activity/category, conditions, date and additional applicable regimes.","Treat PA status as automatic lending and health-data approval.","Use group revenue to substitute for the authorization record."],"a":1},{"q":"A service routes transaction technology without handling funds. Under the read PA/PG distinction, what matters?","o":["Actual funds handling/settlement and role, not the marketing label alone.","Any website with a pay button is automatically a PA.","Calling the service gateway defeats all applicable rules.","A service branded gateway remains outside aggregation rules even if it holds merchant funds."],"a":0},{"q":"A PA has an existing CoA and wants another PA category. Safe source-reading approach?","o":["The existing CoA covers all banking/lending without conditions.","Every new category always requires a wholly new license by definition.","Check the specific existing-CoA intimation route and conditions instead of assuming every expansion needs identical new authorization.","A pending application is equivalent to a granted CoA."],"a":2},{"q":"Rules 2025 notification phases rule 4 one year and many substantive rules 18 months after publication. On 10 November 2026, what is unjustified from that schedule alone?","o":["That exact publication and later changes must be checked.","That notification and commencement can be different events.","That all those phased substantive rules have already commenced.","That other existing privacy/sector duties may still apply."],"a":2},{"q":"A health app invokes child-health processing exceptions for loan marketing. Best response?","o":["Check specified class, necessary health purpose, exact provision and commencement; no blanket app exemption follows.","Every healthcare-adjacent commercial activity is exempt.","Any treatment consent permits unrestricted financial profiling.","A healthcare processor inherits every provider exemption for all downstream commercial uses."],"a":0},{"q":"A search hit calls a consultation-feedback PDF final Rules. What settles the distinction?","o":["Trust the search title over the PDF body.","Read the actual operative text, notification/commencement and amendments on the issuing source.","Treat requested changes in consultation comments as enacted.","Average draft and final obligations into one rule."],"a":1},{"q":"A firm is listed in a directory or has filed an application. Does that alone prove granted permission?","o":["No; inspect the actual grant, category, conditions and status from the proper authority.","Yes; a receipt and a license are the same record.","Yes; a self-declaration is a regulator endorsement.","No; every directory entry proves the business is prohibited."],"a":0},{"q":"Toy cash 5 crore, upfront 1, monthly burn 0.4 plus control operations 0.1, six months, no receipts. Closing cash?","o":["1 crore unrestricted corporate cash.","1.6 crore, omitting controls operations.","2 crore, omitting upfront cost.","4 crore, omitting all monthly costs."],"a":0},{"q":"Same toy, threshold 2 crore. Month 4 cash 2/month 5 cash 1.5. First below threshold?","o":["Month 4, because equality means below.","Month 5; equality at month 4 is not below.","Month 6 only, because projections are not actual cash.","Never, because permissions are not a financial metric."],"a":1},{"q":"Three more months of delay cost 0.5/month after month 6 cash 1. What follows?","o":["Use escrow because the funding gap is temporary.","Projected cash -0.5 crore; arrange lawful funding/scope changes without using restricted merchant funds or bypassing gates.","Launch anyway because penalties can be modeled as costs.","Assume the regulator grants a three-month extension."],"a":1}]},"173":{"objectives":["Produce an evidence-traceable, numerically reconciled strategy model and evidence index. Keep synthetic cases, proposed permissions and verified actuals distinct."],"videos":[{"id":"f3T0tCjw1k8","title":"How the 3 Financial Statements Connect Together","by":"Josh Aharonoff (Your CFO Guy)","note":"Work through the written capstone and self-check; watch time alone does not establish understanding."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Writing a Business Plan","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"A communication outline for a business plan, not an audited financial model or a formula guaranteeing funding."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cash-flow reconciliation: indirect method","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"The worked source example contains inconsistent numbers. Use the reconciled fictional case rather than copying totals. US textbook explanations are not Indian accounting clearance."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets or a local spreadsheet","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Google account creation is not required for this offline exercise; some Gemini features require paid plans. Do not upload private financial/patient data or share access."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Indian SME capstone, all amounts in lakh rupees. Every case input below is invented, not audited company data. No GST, tax, interest, depreciation, dividends, asset sales or other omitted transactions. The simplified expense recognition is stipulated for arithmetic, not an Ind AS classification opinion. Opening cash 20, receivables 10, inventory 12, net equipment 30, payables 8, debt 15 and equity 49: assets 72 equal liabilities plus equity 72. Quarter revenue 60, COGS 36, cash-type operating expense recognized and paid 18, no opening/closing expense accruals; all costs relate to that quarter; net income 6. Receivables close 16, inventory 15 and payables 10. Collections=60+10-16=54. Purchases=36+15-12=39; cash paid suppliers=39+8-10=37. Operating cash=54-37-18=-1, also 6-6-3+2=-1 by indirect bridge. Pay 8 for equipment and repay 2 debt principal. Closing cash 9; assets 9+16+15+38=78; liabilities/equity 10+13+55=78. Profit 6 is not operating cash -1 or total cash change -11. Check direct/indirect bridges and both balance sheets without plugging equity or forcing cash positive. Keep formulas, units, horizon, input version and lineage visible. This scenario is not an actual audit, investment recommendation or permission to use private data.\nDecision alternatives for the next 90-days are mutually exclusive: defer the growth program; a 2 lakh discovery-only program; or a 6 lakh staged growth program. These costs are illustrative incremental expenses, not assumed to be included in the preceding quarter. Compare relevant cash, outcome and reversibility rather than forcing three different tools to be strategic alternatives. A synthetic sponsor/owner is a role in the case, never an actual person who accepted responsibility or approved spending.\nBuild a model with Inputs, Source Register, Statements, Scenarios and Decision tabs or equivalent local files. For each input record source ID, author/publisher, document date and retrieval date, exact page/table/definition/unit, period, actual/estimate/assumption label, reviewer and downstream cells. Synthetic case inputs get a Case-v1 identifier, not a fabricated URL or audit opinion. Third-party source figures remain separate from management projections. External links need access checks before reliance, not uploading customer/payroll/bank data or granting new access. Include a change-impact map and a numbered evidence index. Reconcile every material figure in the memo to its model cell and source; no claim of evidence-room completeness from counting files. Write a conditional recommendation, strongest objection and unresolved checks.<section aria-label=\"Native versioned statements\"><h3>Editable versioned statements and correction defense</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d173-d175-d177-statements.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable statement lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d173-d175-d177-statements.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own model first; this is an authored reference, not your completed capstone. All figures and roles are fictional, in lakh INR over one fixed quarter. Original exercise and rubric remain unchanged.</p><p>Original Case-v1 reconciles opening assets and liabilities/equity 72, net income 6, collections 54, purchases 39, supplier cash 37, direct and indirect CFO -1, capex 8, principal 2, ending cash 9 and closing assets/liabilities-equity 78. No cash or equity balancing plug. D175 AR-only correction changes closing receivables 16 to 22: collections 48, CFO -7 and cash 3. D177 independent stock-only correction changes closing inventory 15 to 19: purchases 43, supplier cash 41, CFO -5 and cash 5. Do not combine these separate variants. Profit remains 6 and assets 78 under the stipulated fixed COGS; collections are not automatically impairment and stock purchases are not automatically a new expense.</p><p>Proposed floor 5: AR cash 3 fails by 2; stock cash 5 equals the floor with zero headroom, not a breach. The extra AR 12 sensitivity is explicitly authored, not observed evidence. Dated minimum/peak funding, future benefits, real financing and rights remain UNKNOWN. Next-quarter defer/discovery-only 2/staged-growth 6 are mutually exclusive incremental alternatives, not expenses already included in this quarter. Missing next-quarter operating cash/obligations cannot be invented to force an option to win.</p><p>Evidence index and cell-to-version impact map retain original versus corrected conclusions; memo and six invented skeptical-panel questions show conditional keep/revise/defer reasoning. Fictional day 0/30/60/90 roles cannot grant access or release money. Actual attempt/error/confidence/reviewer/delayed-retest cells remain blank. No invented learner weakness diagnosis, real panel, audit, owner acceptance, data upload, external sharing, loan or launch.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice execution checked 148 baseline cells and repeated independent exact-rational statement/floor checks for AR, inventory, payable and COGS changes plus restoration. Eight invalid/unbalanced inputs and a deliberate broken CFO reference blocked affected outputs; 148 saved formula caches have no errors. All 11 printed sheets inspected; initial intro clipping repaired before delivery. This closes an authored worked-model example gap, not full source/audiovisual coverage, learner transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, financial audit, account testing or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"At least 8 of 10 evidenced checks, including check 4 (provenance), check 7 (downside/reversal) and check 9 (owner/gate). Reviewer must identify one material weakness with an artifact reference. This is a learning self-check, not actual audit approval, sponsor signoff, mastery probability or permission to execute.","checks":["Define the fictional company decision, sponsor role, audience and relative review deadline; distinguish draft scope from an accepted mandate.","Reconcile opening assets 72 and the fixed-quarter baseline to closing assets 78 without an equity or cash plug.","Compare defer, discovery-only and staged growth using one next-quarter scope and explicit tradeoffs.","Trace every material input to an actual source/page/date/unit or Case-v1 assumption; preserve original and retrieval dates separately.","Show direct CFO -1, indirect CFO -1, capex 8, principal 2 and closing cash 9 with both balance sheets balanced.","Keep invented inputs, projections and externally sourced actuals separate; show a cell-to-source-to-decision impact path.","Run base, upside and downside with stated independent assumptions, a cash threshold and an explicit recommendation reversal.","Label known inputs, scenario assumptions, judgments and unresolved law; do not treat source count as confidence.","Assign fictional accountable owners, proposed 30/60/90-day evidence milestones and stop/scale gates, without claiming actual acceptance.","Deliver a reproducible model/evidence index and strongest objection; identify a material weakness and the decision it could change."]}},"174":{"objectives":["Produce an evidence-traceable, numerically reconciled sponsor memo with conditional budget gates. Keep synthetic cases, proposed permissions and verified actuals distinct."],"videos":[{"id":"zlrb_X6fYZ0","title":"Business Plan Writing 101: Wharton Entrepreneurship Series","by":"Wharton School","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Never fabricate partner contact or customer evidence."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Writing a Business Plan","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"Use this reference to study Writing a Business Plan."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"How to Present to Investors","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/how-to-present-to-investors","note":"Publisher advice on investor presentations. Slide and timing suggestions are not measured attention spans or mandatory durations."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cash-flow reconciliation: indirect method","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"US textbook, not Indian accounting clearance."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets or a local spreadsheet","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Google account creation is not required for this offline exercise; some Gemini features require paid plans. Do not upload private financial/patient data or share access."}],"exercise":"Private fictional sponsor memo and staged execution exercise, not an approved budget or actual outreach. Opening unrestricted cash 12 lakh; existing cash burn 1 lakh per month for three months, assumed no receipts or other flows. Stage-growth budget 6 lakh: discovery 1 in month 1, acquisition test 2 in month 2, scale preparation 3 in month 3. If all stages are conditionally authorized and spent, month-end cash is 10, 7, 3; a minimum cash threshold 4 is breached in month 3. Month 3 stage spend must be at most 2 to preserve the threshold under these assumptions, not automatically approved because that amount is affordable. Defer leaves cash 9 at month 3; discovery-only costs 2 total in month 1, leaving 7 at month 3; staged growth as specified leaves 3. No option dominates solely by cash because benefits and evidence differ. Model each alternative as mutually exclusive and disclose no outcome forecast was validated.\nAssign fictional case roles: sponsor S decides scope, finance F reconciles and tests cash, product P owns synthetic prototype evidence, growth G owns test design, risk R checks permissions. Named role assignment does not imply real acceptance. Relative days 0-30/31-60/61-90 are proposed checkpoints, not measured delivery times. Before stage 2 require a defined eligible-user denominator, pre-specified success/stop criteria and a permission-cleared test plan; before stage 3 require documented evidence, cash headroom including committed liabilities, risk signoff and separate budget authorization. Gate failure freezes uncommitted spending but does not cancel existing contracts or erase obligations. Track owner, evidence, budget released/spent/committed, dependencies, due window, stop conditions and decision record. Write a one-page decision memo plus tracker, not a pretend sponsor email or actual pilot launch.<section aria-label=\"Native staged execution\"><h3>Editable staged-execution memo and tracker</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d174-stage-gates.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">9-sheet editable execution lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d174-stage-gates.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">9-page print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own first. This is a synthetic Case-v1 authored reference in lakh INR, not a completed learner capstone, actual budget release, participant test or sponsor approval. Original case and rubric remain intact.</p><p>Defer, discovery-only 2 and staged-growth 1/2/3 are exclusive paths over the same three months. Existing burn 1/month excludes their extra expense. Cash ends 9, 7 or 3 respectively; staged month-ends 10/7/3 breach floor 4 only in month 3. Conditional month 3 spend ceiling 2 is not authorization and does not establish unchanged benefits or cancelable commitments. Stopping freezes uncommitted future work, not existing contracts. Intramonth minimum, other liabilities and actual benefit forecasts remain UNKNOWN.</p><p>Tracker keeps actual budget released/spent/committed cells blank, separates proposed windows, fictional S/F/P/G/R, evidence, dependencies and stop/decision record. Stage 2 needs a denominator, pre-set criteria and permission-cleared design; stage 3 needs supported outcomes, cash including commitments, risk check and separate budget approval. The one-page memo includes the strongest authored objection; actual reviewer, learner diagnosis, change of view and delayed transfer remain blank.</p><p>Native independent arithmetic tests 39 baseline formula cells plus burn 2, month 3 expense 2 equality and restoration. Burn 2 gives staged 9/5/0 and ceiling -1: even zero month 3 extra expense is insufficient. Three invalid inputs and a deliberately broken cash reference blocked affected summary outputs. All 9 printed sheets inspected, actual tracker states not filled with fake zeros. Static memo/prose stays Case-v1: revise it before presenting changed inputs. Authored execution only, not full source/AV, learner/real pilot, Excel/Sheets parity, account validation or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"At least 8 of 10 evidenced checks, including check 4 (provenance), check 7 (downside/reversal) and check 9 (owner/gate). Reviewer must identify one material weakness with an artifact reference. This is a learning self-check, not actual audit approval, sponsor signoff, mastery probability or permission to execute.","checks":["Specify scope, fictional sponsor S, intended memo audience and proposed 90-day decision window.","Calculate cash 12 to 3 for staged growth and compare defer 9/discovery-only 7 on the same horizon.","Treat defer, discovery-only 2 and staged-growth 6 as mutually exclusive alternatives with explicit expected benefits and unknowns.","Trace each budget, burn and outcome input to Case-v1 assumptions or a verified original source/date/definition/unit.","Show month-end 10/7/3, distinguish committed from uncommitted spending and avoid double-counting test costs in existing burn.","Make responsibilities, dependencies, budget releases and evidence requirements executable as a plan, not generic owner names.","Test cash threshold 4, month 3 spend ceiling 2 under fixed assumptions, benefit uncertainty and a reversal/stop condition.","Distinguish proposed targets from achieved results; a prototype or board discussion is not authorization.","Assign S/F/P/G/R role owners and 30/60/90-day gates with decision authority and separate permission/budget approval conditions.","Deliver memo/tracker with a strongest objection and material weakness; log whether it changed scope, sequence or recommendation."]}},"175":{"objectives":["Produce an evidence-traceable, numerically reconciled fresh transfer test from observed item-level evidence. Keep synthetic cases, proposed permissions and verified actuals distinct."],"videos":[{"id":"E5hkYzd2oiU","title":"Best Buy's Turnaround: The Solution No One Thought Would Work","by":"David Burkus","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. The opening 2012 claim groups later retailer failures together; do not use that chronology or asserted causal turnaround outcomes as scored facts."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Writing a Business Plan","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"Use this reference to study Writing a Business Plan."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cash-flow reconciliation: indirect method","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"US textbook, not Indian accounting clearance."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets or a local spreadsheet","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Google account creation is not required for this offline exercise; some Gemini features require paid plans. Do not upload private financial/patient data or share access."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Indian SME capstone, all amounts in lakh rupees. Every case input below is invented, not audited company data. No GST, tax, interest, depreciation, dividends, asset sales or other omitted transactions. The simplified expense recognition is stipulated for arithmetic, not an Ind AS classification opinion. Opening cash 20, receivables 10, inventory 12, net equipment 30, payables 8, debt 15 and equity 49: assets 72 equal liabilities plus equity 72. Quarter revenue 60, COGS 36, cash-type operating expense recognized and paid 18, no opening/closing expense accruals; all costs relate to that quarter; net income 6. Receivables close 16, inventory 15 and payables 10. Collections=60+10-16=54. Purchases=36+15-12=39; cash paid suppliers=39+8-10=37. Operating cash=54-37-18=-1, also 6-6-3+2=-1 by indirect bridge. Pay 8 for equipment and repay 2 debt principal. Closing cash 9; assets 9+16+15+38=78; liabilities/equity 10+13+55=78. Profit 6 is not operating cash -1 or total cash change -11. Check direct/indirect bridges and both balance sheets without plugging equity or forcing cash positive. Keep formulas, units, horizon, input version and lineage visible. This scenario is not an actual audit, investment recommendation or permission to use private data.\nFresh contradictory-evidence variant, not a retake with relabeled figures: closing receivables are corrected from 16 to 22 with all income-statement, inventory, payable and other stated transactions unchanged. Collections fall to 48, operating cash to -7 and closing cash to 3. The 6 cash reduction is offset by 6 additional receivables, so total assets remain 78; profit remains 6 absent a separately justified impairment or revenue adjustment. Correcting a collection forecast is not automatically a bad-debt write-off. A minimum cash threshold 5 is now missed by 2; identify lawful financing, spending/scope alternatives or defer without assuming a loan is available. Do not silently preserve an old cash 9 recommendation.\nSelect two skill dimensions only from the learner's actual item-level evidence: response, expected result, working, error category and confidence. A day score or rubric total cannot identify weakest skills. With no history use two explicitly chosen practice targets, not a claim about this learner. Suggested targets are accrual-to-cash reconciliation and source-change propagation. Solve the fresh case without notes first, then compare with reference calculations. Log original error, cause, corrected work, impacted outputs, why prior logic failed and a new transfer test. Do not invent wrong answers to fill an error log. If the attempt was correct, record untested risks and use a different perturbation, such as payable timing, rather than marking a fictitious failure. Retest outcome and mastery remain unknown until actual evidence exists.<section aria-label=\"Native versioned statements\"><h3>Editable versioned statements and correction defense</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d173-d175-d177-statements.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable statement lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d173-d175-d177-statements.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own model first; this is an authored reference, not your completed capstone. All figures and roles are fictional, in lakh INR over one fixed quarter. Original exercise and rubric remain unchanged.</p><p>Original Case-v1 reconciles opening assets and liabilities/equity 72, net income 6, collections 54, purchases 39, supplier cash 37, direct and indirect CFO -1, capex 8, principal 2, ending cash 9 and closing assets/liabilities-equity 78. No cash or equity balancing plug. D175 AR-only correction changes closing receivables 16 to 22: collections 48, CFO -7 and cash 3. D177 independent stock-only correction changes closing inventory 15 to 19: purchases 43, supplier cash 41, CFO -5 and cash 5. Do not combine these separate variants. Profit remains 6 and assets 78 under the stipulated fixed COGS; collections are not automatically impairment and stock purchases are not automatically a new expense.</p><p>Proposed floor 5: AR cash 3 fails by 2; stock cash 5 equals the floor with zero headroom, not a breach. The extra AR 12 sensitivity is explicitly authored, not observed evidence. Dated minimum/peak funding, future benefits, real financing and rights remain UNKNOWN. Next-quarter defer/discovery-only 2/staged-growth 6 are mutually exclusive incremental alternatives, not expenses already included in this quarter. Missing next-quarter operating cash/obligations cannot be invented to force an option to win.</p><p>Evidence index and cell-to-version impact map retain original versus corrected conclusions; memo and six invented skeptical-panel questions show conditional keep/revise/defer reasoning. Fictional day 0/30/60/90 roles cannot grant access or release money. Actual attempt/error/confidence/reviewer/delayed-retest cells remain blank. No invented learner weakness diagnosis, real panel, audit, owner acceptance, data upload, external sharing, loan or launch.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice execution checked 148 baseline cells and repeated independent exact-rational statement/floor checks for AR, inventory, payable and COGS changes plus restoration. Eight invalid/unbalanced inputs and a deliberate broken CFO reference blocked affected outputs; 148 saved formula caches have no errors. All 11 printed sheets inspected; initial intro clipping repaired before delivery. This closes an authored worked-model example gap, not full source/audiovisual coverage, learner transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, financial audit, account testing or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"At least 8 of 10 evidenced checks, including check 4 (provenance), check 7 (downside/reversal) and check 9 (owner/gate). Reviewer must identify one material weakness with an artifact reference. This is a learning self-check, not actual audit approval, sponsor signoff, mastery probability or permission to execute.","checks":["Define the Case-v2 correction decision, review audience and retest window without inventing a real sponsor.","Preserve original cash 9 and compute corrected cash 3 under the same quarter and statement definitions.","Compare lawful conditional funding, spending/scope changes and defer, without assuming approval or available debt.","Version the receivable source 16 to 22, date/unit and assumption status; trace every affected cell/output to the correction.","Show collections 48/CFO -7/cash 3, unchanged profit 6 and assets 78; distinguish collections from impairment.","Use actual item-level working to select two skills or label chosen practice targets; never infer weaknesses from a day total.","Test threshold 5, shortfall 2, recovery sensitivity and what evidence reverses the recommendation or removes the funding gap.","Record real observed errors only; distinguish a correct answer, untested skill, uncertain cause and missing evidence.","Assign fictional review owner and proposed 30/60/90-day transfer checks with evidence-based stop/advance gates.","Deliver original attempt, corrected work and a genuinely new test; identify strongest objection/material weakness without fabricating a learner history."]}},"176":{"objectives":["Produce an evidence-traceable, numerically reconciled venture cap-table and portfolio-fit memo. Keep synthetic cases, proposed permissions and verified actuals distinct."],"videos":[{"id":"7ss7yOFgY94","title":"How Venture Capital Firms Create and Use Investment Memos | Slauson & Co. ♻️","by":"Slauson & Co.","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Firm-specific historical process/check sizes/ownership/reserves and macro views are not current universal benchmarks or permission to contact investors."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"SAFE context: ownership and optional follow-on rights","url":"https://www.ycombinator.com/safe/","note":"Optional product setup instructions are not part of this exercise. Use the explicit common-equity toy, not generic investment/cap math for every contract. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Entrepreneurial Finance lecture index","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-431-entrepreneurial-finance-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/","note":"Dated educational context, not current fund mandate or law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Annotated Term Sheet: historical link post","url":"https://venturedeals.com/annotated-term-sheet/","note":"Use this reference to study Annotated Term Sheet: historical link post. Apply the source's date and jurisdiction; it is not a current Indian legal or accounting opinion."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets or a local spreadsheet","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Google account creation is not required for this offline exercise; some Gemini features require paid plans. Do not upload private financial/patient data or share access."}],"exercise":"Private fictional venture overlay; no real fund commitment, instrument issuance or legal clearance. Use common-equity arithmetic only: ignore preferences, fees, carry, tax, debt, transaction costs and option grants except the explicit pool step. Fully diluted pre-round 10 million shares. Fund invests 20 crore at 80 crore pre-money: price 80 rupees/share, 2.5 million new shares, 12.5 million post-round shares and 20% ownership. A later priced round raises 50 at 150 crore pre-money, no pool change: pre-round price 120, 4.1666667 million shares, existing fund stake 15% if it does not participate. Follow-on is conditional on actual rights/allocation; no automatic right is created by this model. If the entire round stays 50 inclusive of this fund, investing 10 of that total 50 purchases 0.8333333 million shares and keeps 20%; outside investors contribute 40, not 50 plus this 10. Total fund cost 30. If outsiders insist on investing 50 in addition, recompute the enlarged round; do not reuse the 15/20% figures.\nAlternative no-follow-on path only: after the 50 round, issue an additional unallocated pool equal to 10% of the post-pool fully diluted total, no cash and no other changes. Pool shares=16.6666667 million / 9=1.8518519 million; fund stake 13.5%. A stipulated exit distributable common-equity value 500 yields 67.5 gross, 3.375x on original 20. At 200 yields 27/1.35x. Exit threshold for gross 3x is 60/.135=444.4444. These are paper scenario payoffs, not guaranteed distributions or fund-level net return. A good company is not necessarily a good investment at a given entry price, dilution and allocation.\nSeparate fictional fund budget 100 crore of investable capital, not commitments before fees; 70 already deployed, 20 reserved for existing names, 10 truly unallocated. A new 20 initial check is 10 over that unallocated budget before its own follow-on reserve; do not double-spend existing reserves. Compare pass/defer, smaller negotiated check with recomputed ownership, or approved reallocation under actual governing documents. No assumed approval, pro-rata right or fund mandate. Evidence-index the cap table, terms, reserve policy, exit distribution waterfall and sensitivities; unresolved Indian Companies Act/FEMA/SEBI/tax/actual-document applicability is unscored legal diligence. US SAFE documentation and a dated term-sheet link post are educational context, not an India instrument form.<section aria-label=\"Native common equity\"><h3>Editable common-equity paths and reserve discipline</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d176-equity-reserves.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-sheet editable cap-table lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d176-equity-reserves.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">12-page print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own first. Private synthetic common-equity reference, crore INR/million shares/rupees per share, no actual investment, legal clearance or allocation right. Original case/rubric preserved; no preferences, fees/carry/tax/debt or grants beyond the separate stated pool.</p><p>Entry 20 at 80 pre buys 2.5 million shares at 80 rupees/share, 20% post. Later TOTAL 50 at 150 pre gives price 120 and no-follow-on stake 15%. Conditional fund 10 WITHIN total 50 maintains 20%, outside 40, total fund cost 30. Outside 50 PLUS fund 10 is a different total 60 path and gives 19.047619%, not 20%. No automatic participation right is created.</p><p>Separate no-follow-on path alone adds an unallocated pool equal to 10% POST-pool total, reducing fund stake to 13.5%, no cash. Distributable common-equity exit 500/200 gives gross 67.5/27 and 3.375x/1.35x on original 20; 3x threshold 444.444444 is algebra, not forecast or net fund return. Exit date/holding period remain UNKNOWN; no annualized return inferred.</p><p>Separate investable 100 minus deployed 70 minus existing-name reserves 20 leaves only 10. New cheque 20 is short 10 before its own reserve; including proposed follow-on reserve 10 makes the shortfall 20. Existing reserves are not free capital. Hypothetical smaller initial cheque 10 at the same entry price rebuilds entry/no-follow-on/post-pool ownership to 11.111111%/8.333333%/7.5%; gross 500 exit 37.5/3.75x, 3x threshold 400. No spare own reserve shown. Pass/defer, negotiated smaller check or actual authorized reallocation remain conditional, not investment instructions.</p><p>Independent native checks cover 58 baseline cells, pool 20%, entry 10, zero follow-on and restoration; zero initial cheque leaves MOIC/threshold undefined, not fake zeros. Eight invalid cap-table/budget cases and a broken ownership reference blocked affected summary outputs; 58 saved baseline formula caches have no errors. All 12 printed sheets inspected. Evidence index, conditional memo, fictional 30/60/90-day diligence gates and strongest authored objection are included; actual reviewer/learner/change-of-view/delayed response remains blank. Static prose stays Case-v1, not live conclusions after input edits. Real mandate/rights/waterfall/Companies Act/FEMA/SEBI/tax applicability remain unresolved; no new empirical-source reading, learner validation, cross-viewer parity, account testing or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"At least 8 of 10 evidenced checks, including check 4 (provenance), check 7 (downside/reversal) and check 9 (owner/gate). Reviewer must identify one material weakness with an artifact reference. This is a learning self-check, not actual audit approval, sponsor signoff, mastery probability or permission to execute.","checks":["Define fictional fund invest/pass decision, audience, allocation authority and investment horizon.","Reconcile 10 million initial shares/80 crore pre-money/20 new to 2.5 million new shares/20% post.","Compare pass/defer, smaller negotiated check and conditional reserve reallocation with recomputed economics.","Trace cap-table/terms/mandate inputs to exact sources or explicitly synthetic Case-v1 assumptions, dates, units and definitions.","Show no-follow-on 15%, inclusive-round follow-on 10 maintaining 20%, and separate pool dilution 13.5%; never combine paths silently.","Distinguish company quality, price, gross payoff, realized distributions and fund net results; test reserve double-spending.","Calculate 3x exit threshold 444.4444 at 13.5%, downside 200 payoff 27, plus dilution/rights sensitivities and reversal.","Keep rights, mandate, Indian law and exit assumptions unresolved unless actual applicable evidence supports them.","Assign fictional investment/finance/legal owners and proposed 30/60/90-day diligence gates; no investment without real approvals and available capital.","Deliver reproducible cap table/scenario/reserve memo and material weakness; log whether the strongest objection changes invest/pass."]}},"177":{"objectives":["Produce an evidence-traceable, numerically reconciled skeptical-review correction and decision package. Keep synthetic cases, proposed permissions and verified actuals distinct."],"videos":[{"id":"h5qWn0SfTXA","title":"What the Best Pitch Decks Have in Common with Mike Vernal (Sequoia Capital)","by":"NFX","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise. Pitch advice is not evidence that a final package is accurate or sponsor-approved."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Writing a Business Plan","url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/writing-a-business-plan","note":"Use this reference to study Writing a Business Plan."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"How to Present to Investors","url":"https://articles.sequoiacap.com/how-to-present-to-investors","note":"Use this reference to study How to Present to Investors."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Cash-flow reconciliation: indirect method","url":"https://openstax.org/books/principles-financial-accounting/pages/16-3-prepare-the-statement-of-cash-flows-using-the-indirect-method","note":"US textbook, not Indian accounting clearance."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets or a local spreadsheet","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Resource directory or overview; linked resources and optional services may have separate access terms. Google account creation is not required for this offline exercise; some Gemini features require paid plans. Do not upload private financial/patient data or share access."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Indian SME capstone, all amounts in lakh rupees. Every case input below is invented, not audited company data. No GST, tax, interest, depreciation, dividends, asset sales or other omitted transactions. The simplified expense recognition is stipulated for arithmetic, not an Ind AS classification opinion. Opening cash 20, receivables 10, inventory 12, net equipment 30, payables 8, debt 15 and equity 49: assets 72 equal liabilities plus equity 72. Quarter revenue 60, COGS 36, cash-type operating expense recognized and paid 18, no opening/closing expense accruals; all costs relate to that quarter; net income 6. Receivables close 16, inventory 15 and payables 10. Collections=60+10-16=54. Purchases=36+15-12=39; cash paid suppliers=39+8-10=37. Operating cash=54-37-18=-1, also 6-6-3+2=-1 by indirect bridge. Pay 8 for equipment and repay 2 debt principal. Closing cash 9; assets 9+16+15+38=78; liabilities/equity 10+13+55=78. Profit 6 is not operating cash -1 or total cash change -11. Check direct/indirect bridges and both balance sheets without plugging equity or forcing cash positive. Keep formulas, units, horizon, input version and lineage visible. This scenario is not an actual audit, investment recommendation or permission to use private data.\nFinal skeptical-panel simulation: a new Case-v2 correction raises closing inventory from 15 to 19, while opening inventory 12 and COGS 36 remain as stated. Purchases become 43 and supplier cash 41; CFO falls from -1 to -5, closing cash from 9 to 5 and closing inventory rises 4, keeping total assets 78. Profit remains 6 because this is a closing-stock/purchases correction under the stipulated fixed COGS, not a new expense. With the same cash threshold 5, equality is not a breach, but no surplus headroom exists. Do not carry D175's receivables correction into this independent variant; a combined variant would need explicit inputs. State what would change if the correction instead affected COGS, revenue or impairment.\nPrepare a defense transcript, versioned model, source/evidence index, decision memo and change log. Panel roles are fictional; quote simulated questions as invented, not actual sponsor feedback. For every challenge state the claim, evidence/cell/version, uncertainty, correction or reasoned disagreement, and downstream decision effect. Do not defend an old answer merely because it passed earlier checks. Ask for a strongest counterargument and compare keep/revise/defer. Every final figure must reconcile to the latest case version; quarantine superseded exports and label remaining gaps. Final means the learning package is reproducible, not a financial audit, legal clearance or approval to launch. Distinguish evidence actually available from missing information and unsupported assertions. No actual external sharing, panel meeting, spending, signoff or live activity follows from passing the rubric.<section aria-label=\"Native versioned statements\"><h3>Editable versioned statements and correction defense</h3><p><a href=\"/practice/top1-d173-d175-d177-statements.xlsx\" download style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-sheet editable statement lab (XLSX)</a> or <a href=\"/practice/top1-d173-d175-d177-statements.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"text-decoration:underline\">11-page print view (PDF)</a>. Build your own model first; this is an authored reference, not your completed capstone. All figures and roles are fictional, in lakh INR over one fixed quarter. Original exercise and rubric remain unchanged.</p><p>Original Case-v1 reconciles opening assets and liabilities/equity 72, net income 6, collections 54, purchases 39, supplier cash 37, direct and indirect CFO -1, capex 8, principal 2, ending cash 9 and closing assets/liabilities-equity 78. No cash or equity balancing plug. D175 AR-only correction changes closing receivables 16 to 22: collections 48, CFO -7 and cash 3. D177 independent stock-only correction changes closing inventory 15 to 19: purchases 43, supplier cash 41, CFO -5 and cash 5. Do not combine these separate variants. Profit remains 6 and assets 78 under the stipulated fixed COGS; collections are not automatically impairment and stock purchases are not automatically a new expense.</p><p>Proposed floor 5: AR cash 3 fails by 2; stock cash 5 equals the floor with zero headroom, not a breach. The extra AR 12 sensitivity is explicitly authored, not observed evidence. Dated minimum/peak funding, future benefits, real financing and rights remain UNKNOWN. Next-quarter defer/discovery-only 2/staged-growth 6 are mutually exclusive incremental alternatives, not expenses already included in this quarter. Missing next-quarter operating cash/obligations cannot be invented to force an option to win.</p><p>Evidence index and cell-to-version impact map retain original versus corrected conclusions; memo and six invented skeptical-panel questions show conditional keep/revise/defer reasoning. Fictional day 0/30/60/90 roles cannot grant access or release money. Actual attempt/error/confidence/reviewer/delayed-retest cells remain blank. No invented learner weakness diagnosis, real panel, audit, owner acceptance, data upload, external sharing, loan or launch.</p><p>Authored native LibreOffice execution checked 148 baseline cells and repeated independent exact-rational statement/floor checks for AR, inventory, payable and COGS changes plus restoration. Eight invalid/unbalanced inputs and a deliberate broken CFO reference blocked affected outputs; 148 saved formula caches have no errors. All 11 printed sheets inspected; initial intro clipping repaired before delivery. This closes an authored worked-model example gap, not full source/audiovisual coverage, learner transfer, Excel/Sheets parity, financial audit, account testing or overall 10/10.</p></section>","rubric":{"pass":"At least 8 of 10 evidenced checks, including check 4 (provenance), check 7 (downside/reversal) and check 9 (owner/gate). Reviewer must identify one material weakness with an artifact reference. This is a learning self-check, not actual audit approval, sponsor signoff, mastery probability or permission to execute.","checks":["Define the simulated final review, fictional panel roles, audience and version cutoff; do not claim real panel acceptance.","Show Case-v1 cash 9 and independent inventory-correction Case-v2 cash 5 without importing D175 inputs.","Compare keep, revise and defer based on corrected evidence, not performance theater.","Trace inventory 15 to 19 to original case version/date/unit, formula changes and superseded exports; preserve provenance.","Reconcile purchases 43/supplier cash 41/CFO -5/cash 5/unchanged profit 6/assets 78 without plugging.","Address disconfirming evidence openly, propagate corrections and distinguish simulation from actual audit.","Use threshold 5 with equality not breach, downside sensitivities and the evidence that would reverse the decision.","Separate facts, synthetic inputs, judgments, open legal/source/video checks and confidence limits.","Assign fictional owner and proposed 30/60/90-day remediation/closure gates, requiring real signoff before any execution.","Deliver reproducible versioned package/defense/change log; document strongest objection/material weakness and its decision effect."]}},"178":{"objectives":["Evaluate incremental plant NPV with matched cash flows and rates, not revenue size, EPS or an unexplained price move."],"videos":[{"id":"5rhild0Juf4","title":"Session 1: Class themes, structure and logistics","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Corporate Finance packet: bounded sections","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on PDF pages 1-50, 79-115, 118-139, 249-258 and 320-322 for this module's concepts. Use the written exercise's assumptions for calculations. Historical 2013/2025 examples are not November 2026 market inputs or current Indian law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Publisher post-class test: Session 1","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session1test.pdf","note":"Session 1 includes solution pages; distinguish publisher exercise from newly authored cases."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Local spreadsheet or Google Sheets","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Optional provider overview; check current access terms and functionality before relying on the tool. Offline calculations need no signup, private-data upload or shared permissions."}],"exercise":"Private fictional plant decision, not a real promoter allegation or capital approval. All amounts crore rupees, nominal annual project cash flows after operating costs/tax/reinvestment as stipulated. Time 0 capital 2000; years 1-5 incremental unlevered free cash flow 550 each year-end; additional net terminal salvage 200 at year 5, no continuation value or other cash flows. Do not add interest/debt principal to these FCFF or count financing proceeds as plant benefit. Stipulated risk-matched nominal INR WACC 12%, not a current market estimate. NPV=-2000+550*annuity factor(12%, 5)+200/1.12^5=96.1123; at 15% with cash flows fixed NPV=-56.8793. Break-even annual FCFF at 12% is 523.3375 with salvage held fixed. A revenue announcement, positive accounting profit or EPS accretion does not supply these incremental cash flows. Show cannibalization, working capital, maintenance, opportunity costs, tax and externalities if applying to real data; sunk feasibility cost is not a new time 0 outflow, while a saleable owned site has an opportunity cost. Map investment (plant), financing (funding mix/terms) and payout (cash to owners), but model their interactions. Equity-price and firm-value objectives require assumptions about protected lenders, information, market pricing and side costs; no guaranteed stock-price result. A stock fall with profit growth is underdetermined, not proof of one cause. Compare build, a separately priced smaller design or defer with consistent scope, evidence, owner and conditional gates. No actual spending, outreach or permission follows from the exercise.","quiz":[{"q":"Toy plant: 2000 now, 550 at each year-end 1-5, extra salvage 200 at 5, WACC 12%. NPV?","o":["About +96.1123 crore.","About -56.8793 crore.","About +750 crore without discounting.","About +2096.1123 crore."],"a":0},{"q":"Same plant/cash flows but WACC 15%. NPV?","o":["About +566.968 crore.","About +96.1123 crore.","About -56.8793 crore.","About -2000 crore."],"a":2},{"q":"At 12% and salvage 200, equal annual FCFF that makes NPV zero?","o":["550 crore, copying the forecast.","About 523.3375 crore.","400 crore, dividing capital by five.","566.968 crore, using 15%."],"a":1},{"q":"Plant FCFF is unlevered and discounted at WACC. What happens to loan proceeds/interest?","o":["Exclude debt proceeds and interest from FCFF; use a financing-consistent WACC.","Include debt proceeds in FCFF; retain unlevered cash flows and WACC.","Deduct interest in FCFF; retain unlevered cash flows and WACC.","Deduct loan principal in FCFF; retain the same unlevered WACC."],"a":0},{"q":"New plant, debt issue, special cash payout: primary decision labels?","o":["Investment, investment, investment because all affect value.","Financing, payout, investment, with interactions.","Payout, investment, financing, with interactions.","Investment, financing, payout, with interactions."],"a":3},{"q":"Feasibility study paid last year is unrecoverable. Owned land could be sold now. Relevant project inputs?","o":["Include the study but exclude saleable land because it is already owned.","Include both as new cash outlays regardless of recoverability.","Exclude both because neither needs a new purchase today.","Exclude sunk study cash; include the land opportunity cost under consistent tax/timing assumptions."],"a":3},{"q":"EPS rises after acquisition. What is still needed for value creation?","o":["A lower share count makes cash-flow analysis unnecessary.","EPS accretion alone establishes positive deal NPV.","Positive revenue growth establishes value at any purchase price.","Price paid and incremental risk-adjusted cash flows, including relevant integration/reinvestment costs."],"a":3},{"q":"Profit rose while the stock fell. What can those two facts establish?","o":["The entire fall proves current profits are fictitious.","The entire fall is necessarily a governance discount.","Not a unique cause; expectations, rates, risk, information and market error need separate evidence.","The entire fall proves all growth investment destroys value."],"a":2},{"q":"When is stock-price maximization a reliable proxy for firm value in the textbook framework?","o":["Whenever management predicts a higher price after an announcement.","Whenever the share trades, regardless of expropriation or misinformation.","Only when every project has positive quarterly accounting profit.","Under stated lender-protection, information and market-pricing assumptions; externalities still require constraints."],"a":3},{"q":"Positive toy NPV authorizes the real plant?","o":["Yes; the assumed positive NPV also establishes access to financing and site approval.","No; validated inputs, financing/liquidity, permissions and actual decision authority remain separate gates.","Yes; a promoter announcement is sufficient evidence that required funding and approvals exist.","No; even after all real gates pass, positive NPV can never support any investment decision."],"a":1}]},"179":{"objectives":["Trace hypothetical controller/private-party/minority economics and evidence-backed governance controls without declaring real wrongdoing or current-law clearance."],"videos":[{"id":"IPTe5Ww2kOk","title":"Session 3: Corporate Governance - Power abhors a vacuum!","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Corporate Finance packet: bounded sections","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on PDF pages 1-50, 79-115, 118-139, 249-258 and 320-322 for this module's concepts. Use the written exercise's assumptions for calculations. Historical 2013/2025 examples are not November 2026 market inputs or current Indian law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Publisher post-class test: Session 3a","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session3atest.pdf","note":"This assigned file contains questions only, not the promised solution."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Shareholder/stakeholder debate: dated author opinion","url":"https://aswathdamodaran.blogspot.com/2019/08/from-shareholder-wealth-to-stakeholder.html","note":"Normative argument, not Indian legal duties or verified current governance facts."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Local spreadsheet or Google Sheets","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Optional provider overview; check current access terms and functionality before relying on the tool. Offline calculations need no signup, private-data upload or shared permissions."}],"exercise":"Private fictional governance case, no allegation about a real group and no current Indian-law approval opinion. Listed company L pays a promoter-owned private supplier 12 crore above an independently stipulated comparable price for exactly the same service, quality, payment timing and risk. Promoter owns 60% of L and 100% of supplier; outside minorities 40%. Ignore taxes, costs, cash constraints and all other effects. Transfer reduces L value 12 and raises private value 12. Promoter loses 7.2 on L but gains 12 privately: net +4.8; outside minorities lose 4.8. The two-entity total is unchanged under this toy, not proof the transfer is fair, lawful, socially harmless or exempt. Comparative price is an assumption, not an actual independent quote. Map economic ownership, voting/control, directors, committees, agreements and conflict disclosures. Distinguish alleged, documented and unresolved facts; preserve evidence and protect confidentiality. Proposed controls include independent evaluation, recusals, appropriate approval and transparent reporting, each subject to actual entity-specific law/documents. No universal listed-company approval threshold, prohibition or exemption is scored. Lender covenants protect a creditor's claim and may not prevent minority extraction; a lender's consent does not prove fairness. Rising stock, a good brand, independent labels or institutional concentration are insufficient evidence that monitors challenge insiders. Market discount is a possible consequence needing evidence, not a guaranteed percentage or sole cause of a price move. For a short-seller allegation, verify exact evidence/conflicts, independent review and applicable disclosure duties without declaring guilt or automatically publishing confidential findings. A stakeholder tradeoff needs explicit constraints and outcomes rather than a slogan that excuses self-dealing. Submit a one-page evidence/beneficiary/control note with strongest objection and unresolved legal gates; do not contact parties or publish allegations.","quiz":[{"q":"L overpays 12 crore under the equal-service toy. Promoter owns 60% L/100% supplier. Net promoter gain?","o":["7.2 crore, the listed stake loss alone.","12 crore without counting listed stake loss.","4.8 crore.","Zero because two-entity total is unchanged."],"a":2},{"q":"Minorities own 40% L and none of supplier. Toy loss?","o":["7.2 crore.","4.8 crore.","12 crore each.","Zero because supplier gained 12."],"a":1},{"q":"Two-entity total unchanged in the toy. What follows?","o":["Every real related-party contract has zero aggregate effect.","Minority fairness follows from aggregate conservation.","Legal approval follows because no total value was destroyed.","No aggregate gain under assumptions; fairness, distribution and legal review remain unresolved."],"a":3},{"q":"Director is called independent but depends on controller for business. Best review?","o":["Rely on the director tenure and compensation growth alone.","Rely on the nomination label and the formal director classification.","Check relationships, incentives, access, recusals and evidence of challenge.","Rely on share-price performance over the director tenure."],"a":2},{"q":"Which is a proposed internal governance mechanism?","o":["An informed board/committee able to review conflicts independently.","A market-price reaction without internal review.","A takeover rumor without board evidence.","A lender trading its loan after bad news."],"a":0},{"q":"Lender consents to a group transaction. Does that settle minority fairness?","o":["No; creditor protection, minority treatment and statutory/document approvals are distinct.","Yes; creditor consent replaces all shareholder analysis.","Yes; covenants always maximize total shareholder value.","No; creditor consent proves the transaction is fraudulent."],"a":0},{"q":"A company stock rises despite a conflict allegation. What can that prove?","o":["The board is independent because market prices never err.","The allegation has been legally dismissed.","Neither guilt nor good governance by itself; inspect evidence and alternative price drivers.","A fixed governance discount is absent in every group company."],"a":2},{"q":"Institutions hold a concentrated block. Does that alone establish effective monitoring?","o":["Yes; concentrated holdings guarantee all conflicts are stopped.","No; voting behavior, incentives, access and actual engagement matter.","Yes; institutions necessarily vote independently of controllers.","No; institutions can never influence governance outcomes."],"a":1},{"q":"Short-seller alleges hidden group debt. Best first governance response?","o":["Preserve evidence; verify debt and conflicts through independent review.","Reject the evidence based solely on the short-seller financial incentive.","Publish private accounts before assessing evidence or disclosure scope.","Repurchase shares before checking debt, covenants or cash constraints."],"a":0},{"q":"A vague stakeholder slogan justifies the controller transfer. What is missing?","o":["A broad corporate-purpose statement alone replaces conflict evidence and accountable review.","A total-value-neutral transfer is enough to satisfy every stakeholder without distribution analysis.","A higher EPS forecast substitutes for identifying who receives and who loses the transfer value.","Specific beneficiaries, costs, constraints, evidence and accountable decision criteria."],"a":3}]},"180":{"objectives":["Apply a stated CAPM to matched equity flows, distinguish beta from volatility and preserve model/diversification limits."],"videos":[{"id":"_SxIBHEGhbo","title":"Session 5: Closure on the end game and first steps on risk","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Corporate Finance packet: bounded sections","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on PDF pages 1-50, 79-115, 118-139, 249-258 and 320-322 for this module's concepts. Use the written exercise's assumptions for calculations. Historical 2013/2025 examples are not November 2026 market inputs or current Indian law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Publisher post-class test: Session 5a","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session5atest.pdf","note":"This assigned file contains questions only, not the promised solution."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Local spreadsheet or Google Sheets","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Optional provider overview; check current access terms and functionality before relying on the tool. Offline calculations need no signup, private-data upload or shared permissions."}],"exercise":"Private fictional risk memo, no current market inputs or investment advice. Under a stated CAPM with diversified marginal investor, nominal INR default-free rate 6%, equity market premium 5%, utility equity beta 0.4 and app equity beta 2.0, equity required returns are 8% and 16%. Both are below an 18% uniform rule in this toy: a claim that this same 18% rule errs in opposite directions would be incorrect. For a uniform 12% rule, it is above utility 8% and below app 16%. Independent one-year all-equity projects each cost 100 now and pay expected 112 at year 1, no other flows. Utility NPV 112/1.08-100=3.7037; app 112/1.16-100=-3.4483. These are equity cash flows at equity rates, not an arbitrary project IRR compared with levered equity CAPM. Beta=covariance(asset, market)/variance(market), not total volatility, profitability or a guarantee of a move every day. Forty stocks do not prove a portfolio is diversified; exposures, correlations, weights and marginal price setter matter. Firm-specific risk can lower expected cash or create distress/operating costs even when it earns no extra CAPM premium; unpriced does not mean harmless. If a risk change affects covariance or expected cash, holding beta/cash constant is invalid. CAPM assumptions and estimated beta/rates can be wrong; no generic startup zero-beta or universal rate conclusion. A government-backed label does not eliminate contract, credit, currency, demand or implementation risks. Distinguish priced market risk, expected operating losses and binding financing/liquidity constraints. Submit matched-rate/cash-flow alternatives and sensitivities; no real trade or financing.","quiz":[{"q":"Toy CAPM rf 6%, ERP 5%, beta 0.4. Equity required return?","o":["18%.","11%.","2%.","8%."],"a":3},{"q":"Same rf 6%/ERP 5%, app beta 2.0. Equity required return?","o":["18%.","12%.","10%.","16%."],"a":3},{"q":"Uniform 18% versus toy utility 8%/app 16%. Correct comparison?","o":["18% is above both; opposite-direction error is not established here.","18% is above utility but below app 16%.","18% is below both because startup stories raise rates.","18% equals both because all rupee assets share one hurdle."],"a":0},{"q":"Uniform 12% versus utility 8%/app 16%. What distortion is possible?","o":["Equal to utility and app after diversification.","Too low for utility, too high for app.","Too high for utility, too low for app under this model.","No distortion is possible with one corporate rate."],"a":2},{"q":"Utility all-equity one-year cost 100/expected payoff 112 at 8%. NPV?","o":["About -3.4483 using 16%.","+12 without discount.","About +3.7037.","+112 without subtracting capital."],"a":2},{"q":"App same one-year 100/112 payoff at 16%. NPV?","o":["About -3.4483.","About +3.7037.","+12 because return is positive.","Zero because both projects have identical cash."],"a":0},{"q":"What does beta measure in CAPM?","o":["Probability of a stock rising on the next trading day.","Own volatility divided by own average accounting return.","Covariance with the market divided by market return variance.","Guaranteed percent price change for every market move."],"a":2},{"q":"Firm-specific operational loss is diversifiable to marginal investor. Does it disappear from valuation?","o":["Yes; diversification restores the lost company cash.","No; expected cash losses and operating/distress effects still need analysis.","Yes; no premium means no expected loss.","No; every such loss must automatically double beta."],"a":1},{"q":"Portfolio contains 40 stocks. What remains to verify?","o":["Check company count alone against a universal forty-stock cutoff.","Check weights, shared exposures, correlations and the marginal investor.","Check equal weighting alone, without shared exposures or correlations.","Check sector labels alone, without weights or cross-sector exposures."],"a":1},{"q":"Can a generic project FCFF be compared directly with a levered equity CAPM rate?","o":["No; project analysis can never use equity cash flows, even with a matching cost of equity.","Yes; a levered equity hurdle can always discount unlevered firm cash flows without adjustment.","Yes; compare whichever rate is higher even if the cash-flow claim and financing assumptions differ.","No; match cash-flow claim, leverage, currency and timing to the rate."],"a":3}]},"181":{"objectives":["Distinguish default-free proxy, debt spread, country equity premium, total ERP and exposure model; preserve currency, date and source-method consistency."],"videos":[{"id":"oeRtLInQTAM","title":"Session 6: Riskfree Rates and Equity Risk Premiums","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Corporate Finance packet: bounded sections","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on PDF pages 1-50, 79-115, 118-139, 249-258 and 320-322 for this module's concepts. Use the written exercise's assumptions for calculations. Historical 2013/2025 examples are not November 2026 market inputs or current Indian law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Publisher post-class test: Session 6a","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session6atest.pdf","note":"This assigned file contains questions only, not the promised solution."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Country premiums: dated workbook and distinct methods","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pc/datasets/ctryprem.xlsx","note":"The page labels January 1, 2026 and February 16 rating data but gives conflicting US/mature-market-premium numbers. Use the exercise's stated inputs, not a guessed reconciliation."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Country exposure methods: historical lecture extract","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/podcasts/valspr19/session5slides.pdf","note":"Separate beta and lambda country-exposure models use different assumptions."}],"exercise":"Private fictional discount-rate reconstruction. All rates below are teaching assumptions, not November 2026 India/US quotations or sovereign-rating claims. Stipulate nominal INR government bond yield 7% and compatible estimated sovereign default component 1%; default-free INR proxy 6%, recognizing tenor, liquidity, estimation and term-structure issues. A government yield is not automatically default-free merely because it is local currency. Toy mature-market ERP 5%, country default spread 1% and equity/bond return volatility ratio 1.5 gives country equity premium 1.5% and total ERP 6.5%. Under explicit exposure model A, cost of equity=rf+beta*(mature ERP+CRP), beta 1.2 =>13.8%; do not add CRP again to a total ERP. Model B independently uses rf+beta*mature ERP+lambda*CRP, with lambda 0.8=>13.2%. Different exposure assumptions, not interchangeable outputs or a reason to average. Revenue geography is one possible proxy but operations, assets, policy and risk management can matter; incorporation alone is insufficient. Under model A, market-value weights 60% equity/40% debt, pretax debt cost 9%, fully usable marginal tax 25% gives WACC 10.98%. Equity flows use 13.8%, firm flows use 10.98% under these assumptions; no simultaneous tax shields in both flows and rate.\nIndependent implied-ERP perpetuity toy: index price 1000, next-year expected distributable cash 120, perpetual growth 3%, matched default-free 4%. Implied expected return=120/1000+3%=15%; ERP 11%. At price 1200 with same 120 cash/growth, expected return 13%, ERP 9%; do not treat a price-only change as fixed-ERP. This is a highly simplified perpetuity, not a live index forecast.\nAssigned country workbook downloaded 10 November 2026 labels update 1 January 2026 with February 16 ratings note. India row distinguishes rating-based and CDS-based columns; explanation/summary/cached inputs conflict on US/mature premium arithmetic. Do not claim its label is November freshness, its country rows are directly implied market ERPs, or score one current India value. Preserve version/date/method/headers and reconcile formulas before real use; differences between methods are not a confirmed policy change. Historical packet November 2013/January 2025 figures are historical examples. FX/inflation consistency requires cash flows and discount rates in the same nominal currency or both real; changing denomination does not remove operating country risk. No live workbook edit, account registration, Bloomberg access or investment action.","quiz":[{"q":"Toy INR sovereign yield 7%, compatible default component 1%. Default-free proxy?","o":["6%, subject to the stated estimation/tenor assumptions.","7% because every local sovereign yield is risk-free.","8% adding default spread twice.","1% using only the credit component."],"a":0},{"q":"Toy default spread 1%, equity/bond volatility ratio 1.5. Country equity premium?","o":["1.5%.","1%.","0.6667%.","6.5%."],"a":0},{"q":"Mature ERP 5% plus CRP 1.5%. Total ERP?","o":["5%.","6.5%.","1.5%.","8%."],"a":1},{"q":"Model A rf 6%, beta 1.2, total ERP 6.5%. Cost of equity?","o":["13.8%.","15.3% adding CRP again.","12% excluding CRP.","10.98% substituting WACC."],"a":0},{"q":"Independent model B rf 6%, beta 1.2*mature ERP 5% plus lambda 0.8*CRP 1.5%. Cost of equity?","o":["13.8% using model A exposure.","13.2%.","15.3% adding full CRP to model A.","6.8% adding lambda as percentage."],"a":1},{"q":"Model A equity 13.8%, debt 9%, tax 25%, market weights 60/40. WACC?","o":["11.88% without debt tax adjustment.","10.98%.","13.8% ignoring debt weight.","9.60% using book-return averaging."],"a":1},{"q":"Perpetuity index 1000, next cash 120, growth 3%, rf 4%. Implied ERP?","o":["9%, using price 1200.","15%, the total return.","8%, cash yield less rf only.","11%."],"a":3},{"q":"Price rises 1000 to 1200, next cash 120/growth 3%/rf 4% fixed. Implied ERP?","o":["13%, total return.","11% unchanged by price.","9%.","6%, cash yield minus rf only."],"a":2},{"q":"Workbook country row has rating-based and CDS-based ERP columns, dates and conflicting notes. Safe next step?","o":["Ignore the dates; select the premium giving the desired value.","Average both methods; use that figure as the approved premium.","Preserve dates and methods; reconcile formulas before real use.","Add both country premiums to the workbook total-premium column."],"a":2},{"q":"Use USD discount rate with unchanged nominal INR cash flows?","o":["Yes; currency label is irrelevant to DCF.","No; convert both consistently and retain applicable operating country risk.","Yes; if company is incorporated in India.","No; conversion automatically removes all country risk."],"a":1}]},"182":{"objectives":["Test transfer across investment objectives, governance, CAPM and ERP using changed inputs and item-level reasoning, not a repeated score or fabricated weakness."],"videos":[{"id":"jH8L7cW6Yns","title":"Quiz 1 Review","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Past NYU Stern Quiz 1 papers","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfexams/prqz1.pdf","note":"Problem 2 omits an explicit equity risk premium. The historical solution uses a 5.5% course assumption, not current market data."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Solutions to past Quiz 1 papers","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfexams/prqz1sol.xlsx","note":"Historical quiz workbook, including Spring 1998 material. Match each question to its year and use the stated 5.5% equity-risk-premium assumption where appropriate; it is not a current-market input."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets (free with Google account)","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Optional provider overview; check current access terms and functionality before relying on the tool. Offline work needs no registration, private upload or sharing."}],"exercise":"Private fictional review with fresh inputs, not a real company recommendation. Keep each mini-case independent. Plant costs 100 now, pays 60 at each year-end 1 and 2, extra salvage 10 at 2, WACC 10%; compute NPV. Promoter owns 70% of listed buyer and 100% of private seller; buyer overpays 20 for identical goods/timing/risk: map promoter/private/minority effects. CAPM rf 5%, ERP 6%, logistics beta 0.5, gaming beta 1.5: rates 8%/14%; corporate 11% lies between them, while 18% exceeds both. Do not mix observed market return with expected ERP. Default-free teaching proxy: compatible nominal government yield 8% less default spread 2%=6%; mature ERP 4% plus CRP 2%=total 6%, with beta 1.25 gives equity 13.5% under beta-times-total-ERP model. These are not current India inputs. Simplified perpetuity price 200/next cash 20/growth 2%/rf 4% implies ERP 8%; at price 250 with other inputs fixed ERP 6%. Inspect assumptions before interpreting stock moves, governance, factory loss or currencies. Attempt without notes, then explain each error by question ID using actual answers and reasoning; record confidence, corrected model and one new transfer check. If no response evidence exists, leave weakness unknown. Watch time and an aggregate score do not establish concept mastery. Check your reasoning and each calculation separately. No real investment, disclosure or spending authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"Fresh plant:100 now,60 at year 1,60+10 salvage at year 2, WACC 10%. NPV?","o":["About 112.3967.","About 20.","About 30.","About 12.3967."],"a":3},{"q":"Plant positive NPV under stated inputs. What remains?","o":["Funding automatically exists because NPV is positive.","Input validation and separate funding, permissions and decision gates.","Site permission automatically follows from modeled cash flows.","A public promoter claim substitutes for independent input checks."],"a":1},{"q":"Unrecoverable study 3 paid last year; owned site sale value 9 now. Incremental time 0 cost?","o":["12 counting both as new cash.","9 for foregone site sale, not sunk 3.","3 counting study only.","0 because both already owned/paid."],"a":1},{"q":"New store, loan issue, cash dividend: primary categories?","o":["Investment, financing, payout.","Financing, investment, payout.","Payout, financing, investment.","Investment, payout, financing."],"a":0},{"q":"EPS grew and price fell. Which conclusion is supported?","o":["The full fall proves all expansion destroys value.","The full fall proves a governance discount.","The full fall proves fake accounting.","No single cause is established by those facts alone."],"a":3},{"q":"Promoter 70% buyer/100% seller; buyer overpays 20. Net promoter gain?","o":["0.","20.","14.","6."],"a":3},{"q":"Same overpay 20; outside minorities 30% buyer/0% seller. Loss?","o":["20 each.","14.","6.","0."],"a":2},{"q":"Toy aggregate value is unchanged after transfer. What follows?","o":["Distribution and legal/fairness checks remain separate.","Each shareholder is necessarily indifferent.","Every needed approval is satisfied.","No stakeholder can bear any cost."],"a":0},{"q":"Director labeled independent: strongest evidence to examine?","o":["Board size without independence or information checks.","The formal label without relationship checks.","Company price growth without conflict checks.","Relationships, incentives, access and demonstrated challenge."],"a":3},{"q":"Institutions own large blocks. Effective monitoring established?","o":["No; institutions are categorically unable to monitor.","Yes; ownership concentration proves effective challenge.","Yes; institutional ownership rules out controller influence.","No; examine incentives, votes and actual engagement."],"a":3},{"q":"rf 5%, ERP 6%, logistics beta 0.5. CAPM equity return?","o":["3%.","10%.","8%.","11%."],"a":2},{"q":"rf 5%, ERP 6%, gaming beta 1.5. CAPM equity return?","o":["14%.","11%.","9%.","16.5%."],"a":0},{"q":"Corporate 11% versus toy logistics 8%/gaming 14%. Comparison?","o":["Too high for logistics, too low for gaming.","Too low for logistics, too high for gaming.","Above both division rates.","Below both division rates."],"a":0},{"q":"Corporate 18% versus same 8%/14% rates. Comparison?","o":["Above both; opposite-direction claim fails.","Above logistics but below gaming.","Below both division rates.","Equal to both after diversification."],"a":0},{"q":"Realized market return 10% plus rf 5%, ERP 6%. Which determines expected CAPM return?","o":["Realized 10% replaces ERP 6% in every expected-return calculation.","Stated rf, beta and expected ERP, not realized 10% substituted silently.","Realized 10% is added to rf and beta times ERP.","Expected CAPM return always equals realized market 10%."],"a":1},{"q":"A diversifiable factory fire destroys expected future cash. Ignore the loss?","o":["No; every fire automatically sets beta to 2.","Yes; diversification restores the factory cash.","Yes; no CAPM premium means no expected loss.","No; expected cash losses still enter valuation."],"a":3},{"q":"Forty-stock portfolio proves full diversification?","o":["Yes; equal weighting eliminates all common risk.","Yes; stock count alone is sufficient.","No; weights, correlations and shared exposures matter.","No; no multi-stock portfolio can diversify any risk."],"a":2},{"q":"Nominal yield 8%, compatible default component 2%. Teaching risk-free proxy?","o":["8%, automatically default-free.","6%, under stated tenor/estimation assumptions.","10%, adding spread.","2%, replacing yield."],"a":1},{"q":"Mature ERP 4%, CRP 2%. Total ERP?","o":["4%.","6%.","2%.","8%."],"a":1},{"q":"rf 6%, beta 1.25, total ERP 6%. Equity cost under beta-times-total model?","o":["7.5%.","15.5%.","11%.","13.5%."],"a":3},{"q":"Perpetuity price 200, next cash 20, growth 2%, rf 4%. Implied ERP?","o":["6%.","12%.","8%.","10%."],"a":2},{"q":"Price 250, next cash 20, growth 2%, rf 4%. Implied ERP?","o":["4%.","8%.","10%.","6%."],"a":3},{"q":"Nominal INR cash flows with USD discount rate. Fix?","o":["Change rate alone to whichever produces higher value.","Change currency label alone and leave rates/flows untouched.","Convert both consistently and preserve operating risk assumptions.","Use country of incorporation as a substitute for cash currency."],"a":2},{"q":"Dated workbook gives conflicting rating/CDS methods. Safe use?","o":["Ignore version and pick favorable output.","Average methods and call it regulator-approved.","Preserve date/method and reconcile before a current-rate claim.","Add country-premium columns to a total that includes one."],"a":2},{"q":"Learner got 22/25 but no item responses are available. Weak concepts?","o":["Unknown; obtain item-level answers and reasoning.","Declare ERP weak because it came last.","Declare governance weak because it is qualitative.","Declare all topics mastered because score exceeds 80%."],"a":0}]},"183":{"objectives":["Build an assumption-bound bottom-up beta with cash and leverage adjustments; keep financial-service claims separate from industrial debt formulas."],"videos":[{"id":"awEyDWhEdhg","title":"Session 9: More on beta - bottom up betas and private company betas","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 1 (Sessions 1-15)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on the cost-of-capital and risk sections, then use the written case's explicitly stated inputs."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 9","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session9test.pdf","note":"The assigned test file contains questions, not solutions; calculate your answer and check the reasoning independently."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Bottom-up beta spreadsheet","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/webcasts/Bottomupbeta/bottomupbeta.xls","note":"Historical bottom-up beta workbook. Reconcile the company scope, peer data and formulas before using it; the exercise supplies its own explicit inputs and requires no setup or macro execution."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Financial-service valuation: separate claim treatment","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/papers/finfirm.pdf","note":"Financial-service debt/capital differs from industrial financing; historical examples not current Indian law or entity clearance."}],"exercise":"Private fictional industrial core: cables and paints only for this numeric model. Assume diversified marginal investors, negligible debt beta, usable tax 25%, matching market-value leverage and comparable operations. Cable peers equity beta 1.2 at D/E 0.5, paint peers equity beta 0.9 at D/E 0.2. Unlever each separately:0.8727273 and 0.7826087. Division operating values 300/200 produce core beta 0.8366798. Target industrial D/E 0.4 gives equity beta 1.0876838 without cash. Independent cash sensitivity: operating value 500 plus zero-beta excess cash 100 -> firm unlevered beta 0.6972332; same stipulated D/E 0.4 -> company equity beta 0.9064032. Treat leverage as a stipulated scenario, not a free unchanged ratio after arbitrary transactions. Revenue weights 60/40 coincide here only by specified value, not a general rule. Correct peer cash before calling betas pure operating and avoid subtracting it twice. A noisy regression beta 0.9 with SE 0.35 is not automatically false; compare window/index/return frequency/leverage/business mix and peer quality. Approximate normal 95% interval 0.214 to 1.586 is a model illustration, not measured predictive certainty. Original conglomerate finance arm is deliberately excluded from this industrial calculation: bank/NBFC deposits/debt and regulatory capital make generic unlever/relever and FCFF/WACC treatment inappropriate without entity-specific analysis. Consider comparable levered equity betas and equity/capital-based valuation for that arm, then reconcile claim values explicitly. Do not add operating values and finance equity as if identical claims. Private ownership alone neither proves diversified investors nor automatically authorizes total-beta pricing. No current market inputs, investment action or universal formula guarantee.","quiz":[{"q":"Cable peer equity beta 1.2, D/E 0.5, tax 25%, negligible debt beta. What is asset beta to four decimal places?","o":["0.8727","1.6500","0.8000","1.2000"],"a":0},{"q":"Paint peer equity beta 0.9, D/E 0.2, tax 25%, negligible debt beta. What is asset beta to four decimal places?","o":["0.7500","1.0350","0.7826","0.9000"],"a":2},{"q":"Operating values 300/200, asset betas 0.8727273/0.7826087. Core beta?","o":["0.8727273.","0.827668.","1.08.","0.8366798."],"a":3},{"q":"Core asset beta 0.8366798, target D/E 0.4, tax 25%, no cash. Equity beta?","o":["1.1713517.","0.8366798.","1.0876838.","0.6435998."],"a":2},{"q":"Operating value 500/core beta 0.8366798 plus zero-beta excess cash 100. Firm asset beta?","o":["0.6972332.","0.8366798.","1.0040158.","0.16733596."],"a":0},{"q":"Firm asset beta 0.6972332, same stipulated D/E 0.4, tax 25%. Company equity beta?","o":["0.6972332.","1.0876838.","0.9064032.","1.2550198."],"a":2},{"q":"Noisy regression beta 0.9/SE 0.35 versus rebuilt 1.0877. Best defense?","o":["Compare assumptions, peers, uncertainty and business/leverage changes.","Discard regression solely because rebuilt value is higher.","Treat peer averages as eliminating all estimation error.","Use the higher estimate as proven true beta."],"a":0},{"q":"Finance subsidiary includes deposits and regulatory capital. Apply industrial unlever/relever blindly?","o":["Yes; equity and operating enterprise values are interchangeable weights.","Yes; all deposits are ordinary industrial financing with the same role.","No; separate financial claims, comparable equity risk and capital needs.","No; financial subsidiaries have no measurable equity risk."],"a":2},{"q":"Peer beta includes zero-beta excess cash. To estimate operating beta?","o":["Call cash-inclusive asset beta pure operating beta without adjustment.","Remove financing effects and adjust cash once on consistent value basis.","Subtract cash both before and after weighting operating assets.","Relever at peer D/E again before unlevering it."],"a":1},{"q":"Private target has no traded stock. What still matters with peer beta?","o":["Age alone determines the missing market beta.","Business comparability, leverage basis and investor diversification.","Private status guarantees all owners are diversified.","No market price means equity opportunity cost must be zero."],"a":1}]},"184":{"objectives":["Build a project-risk-matched WACC from market values and usable tax effects; compare NPV on coherent cash flows rather than a legacy loan rate."],"videos":[{"id":"0FeHGmeqcX0","title":"Session 11: Hurdle Rates Closure and First Steps on Investment Returns","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 1 (Sessions 1-15)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on the cost-of-capital and risk sections, then use the written case's explicitly stated inputs."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 11","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session11test.pdf","note":"The assigned test file contains questions, not solutions; calculate your answer and check the reasoning independently."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Cost of debt computation (Home Depot example)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/webcasts/Debt&Cost/HDdebtcomputation.xls","note":"Lease/debt treatment needs current statements and no double counting."}],"exercise":"Private fictional nominal INR industrial export plant. All rates hypothetical, not actual credit ratings or Indian tax advice. Stipulate project-risk-matched equity cost 14%, current pretax debt cost 10%, usable marginal tax 25%, equity market value 600/debt market value 400, no preferred or other claims. WACC=.6*.14+.4*.10*.75=11.4%. No immediate usable interest tax benefit sensitivity:12.4%, not automatically tax 25% forever; timing/carryforwards require separate modeling. Book equity 200/debt 400 gives 9.6667% with these inputs, but book weights do not universally lower WACC. Plant costs 100 now/pays 111 once at year 1/no other cash. At legacy loan 8% NPV+2.7778; at WACC 11.4% NPV-0.3591, purely model verdict, not spend approval. Synthetic debt scenario independently stipulates EBIT 30/interest 6=coverage 5, a chosen model mapping supplies spread 4% over matched rf 6%=debt 10%; no claim that 5 x has a universal agency rating or current spread. Actual rating, maturity, liquidity, covenants/collateral/credit need evidence; old coupon is not always marginal debt cost. Preferred toy separately: perpetual fixed dividend 8/market price 100=8% before personal tax, no assumed corporate interest tax shield, subject to actual rights/tax. Redeemable/convertible claims require their own terms. Debt sensitivity must update equity/debt risks, weights, tax capacity and constraints rather than assume cheap debt monotonically lowers WACC. Match currency, nominal/real basis, project risk and FCFF claim; include lease/debt effects consistently without double counting an already capitalized lease. Present conditional proceed/revise/defer with downside and owner; no booking, trade, financing or spending.","quiz":[{"q":"Equity MV 600/debt MV 400, costs 14%/10%, usable tax 25%. WACC?","o":["12.4%.","11.4%.","9.6667%.","14%."],"a":1},{"q":"Same weights/rates but no immediate tax benefit in this sensitivity. WACC?","o":["12.4%.","11.4%.","7.5%.","9.3%."],"a":0},{"q":"Book equity 200/debt 400 with same 14%/10%/tax 25%. Book-weighted estimate?","o":["12.4%.","11.4%.","9.6667%.","7.5%."],"a":2},{"q":"Plant cash -100 now / +111 at year 1, no other cash. What is NPV at legacy 8%, rounded to four decimal places?","o":["+11.0000","-0.3591","+2.7778","0.0000"],"a":2},{"q":"Same plant at project WACC 11.4%. NPV?","o":["+11.","About +2.7778.","About -0.3591.","-11.4."],"a":2},{"q":"Stipulated EBIT 30/interest 6. Synthetic coverage input?","o":["5 x; mapping to spread/rating needs stated model and date.","20%; inverse ratio is the coverage convention.","6 x; interest expense alone determines coverage.","5%; ratio is a spread percentage."],"a":0},{"q":"Model-supplied spread 4% plus matched risk-free rate 6%, no double-counted spread. What is the pretax debt cost under this model?","o":["4%","6%","10%","14%"],"a":2},{"q":"Ordinary perpetual preferred dividend 8/price 100, no other features. Cost?","o":["0%, because dividend is discretionary in all claims.","6%, applying 25% debt shield.","10.6667%, grossing up personal tax silently.","8%, without an assumed interest tax shield."],"a":3},{"q":"More debt at initially lower cost. Always lower WACC?","o":["Yes; keep equity cost fixed at any debt level.","No; re-estimate risks, weights, usable tax and constraints.","Yes; default spread cannot change after borrowing.","No; debt is universally prohibited for export plants."],"a":1},{"q":"Lease already included in reported debt/operating adjustments. Safe WACC bridge?","o":["Keep interest in FCFF and apply the unmodified WACC.","Add all lease PV again regardless of existing balances.","Exclude every lease because it is never financing.","Reconcile claims and cash flows to avoid adding the same lease twice."],"a":3}]},"185":{"objectives":["Reconcile earnings, reinvestment and incremental cash flows with explicit working-capital timing and recovery; separate NPV from accounting ROIC."],"videos":[{"id":"CncUmN65lDs","title":"Session 12: Incremental, time-weighted Cash Flow Returns","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 1 (Sessions 1-15)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on the cost-of-capital and risk sections, then use the written case's explicitly stated inputs."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 12","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session12test.pdf","note":"The assigned test file contains questions, not solutions; calculate your answer and check the reasoning independently."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"ROIC calculator (Walmart example)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/webcasts/ROIC/walmartreturncalculator.xls","note":"The historical workbook appears to double-count goodwill or cash in places. Use the written case's explicit model rather than adopting the displayed adjusted returns."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Publisher Session 12 solutions: separate file","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session12soln.pdf","note":"In questions 2 and 5, the answer letters conflict with the numerical options, although the displayed 15% and 29.5 calculations are coherent. Compare the calculation, not only the letter."}],"exercise":"Private fictional Chennai retailer, amounts crore. Time 0 fit-out 100 plus operating NWC 20 gives FCFF -120. Four-year life, straight-line book/tax depreciation 25 yearly to zero, immediately usable tax 25%. Incremental EBIT years 1-4 is 10, 20, 30, 40, hence NOPAT 7.5, 15, 22.5, 30. Additional capex 10 at year 1, 10 at year 2, zero thereafter. Operating NWC balances at time 0/year 1/year 2/year 3/year 4 before closure are 20/25/30/30/30, so operating changes are 5/5/0/0. At end year 4 recover only 24 of NWC 30. That net recovery already reflects any tax consequences of the six-unit loss; do not add another shield. After-tax salvage is 12. FCFF years 1-4 is 17.5/25/47.5/91, with year 4 including recovery 24 and salvage 12 once. Matched nominal INR WACC 12% gives NPV +7.1966. EBIT already includes only incremental operating costs; no extra overhead adjustment in this main case. Negative early cash alone does not prove negative NPV.\nIndependent overhead sensitivity: reported after-tax cash 25 deducted allocated pre-tax G&A 10, of which only 2.5 is caused by the project. Usable tax 40% gives corrected cash 29.5 by adding back 7.5*(1-0.4). Do not add this separate adjustment to the main case. Exclude sunk costs but include foregone alternatives. Operating NWC excludes financing debt and cash. Recovery is not guaranteed to equal book value.\nIndependent historical ROIC toy: EBIT 40, tax 25%, opening book debt 80 plus equity 140 less excess cash 20 gives capital 200 and ROIC 15%, not market-value WACC weights or full project NPV. Assigned Walmart workbook cached goodwill/cash bridges have unresolved apparent double counting; do not copy its adjusted returns. Tax deductions are stipulated, not current Indian law. Project discounting differs from portfolio time-weighted performance. No tax filing, investment or spending is authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"Time 0 fitout 100 plus NWC 20. FCFF?","o":["-100.","-120.","-80.","+120."],"a":1},{"q":"Year 1 EBIT 10, tax 25%, depreciation 25, capex 10, NWC increase 5. FCFF?","o":["32.5.","17.5.","7.5.","42.5."],"a":1},{"q":"Year 2 EBIT 20, tax 25%, depreciation 25, capex 10, NWC increase 5. FCFF?","o":["35.","15.","40.","25."],"a":3},{"q":"Year 3 EBIT 30, tax 25%, depreciation 25, no additional capex/NWC. FCFF?","o":["22.5.","47.5.","30.","72.5."],"a":1},{"q":"Year 4 NOPAT 30+depreciation 25+net recovery 24+net salvage 12. Total FCFF?","o":["91.","97.","115.","55."],"a":0},{"q":"Operating NWC 30 but net terminal recovery 24. Add another 30?","o":["No; use 24 once and reflect recovery loss assumptions.","Yes; every book balance automatically returns as cash.","Yes; salvage 12 already includes NWC and must be repeated.","No; working capital never has terminal value."],"a":0},{"q":"FCFF -120 now, then 17.5/25/47.5/91 at year-ends 1-4. NPV at 12%?","o":["About -7.1966.","+61 without discounting.","About +127.1966.","About +7.1966."],"a":3},{"q":"Independent after-tax cash 25 includes allocated pre-tax G&A 10, only 2.5 incremental, tax 40%. Correct cash?","o":["32.5.","29.5.","27.5.","35."],"a":1},{"q":"Independent ROIC: EBIT 40, tax 25%, opening debt 80+equity 140-excess cash 20. ROIC?","o":["13.6364%.","15%.","20%.","30%."],"a":1},{"q":"When does depreciation 25 create an immediate cash tax benefit 6.25?","o":["When deductible at 25% with sufficient current tax capacity as stipulated.","Whenever book depreciation exists, regardless of tax deductibility.","Whenever EBITDA is positive, regardless of tax losses.","Never;noncash charges can have no cash tax effect."],"a":0}]},"186":{"objectives":["Build coherent uncertainty and currency comparisons with explicit cash-flow drivers; distinguish valuation consistency from actual debt-service exposure."],"videos":[{"id":"vxuuxJ68Dj8","title":"Session 13: Dealing with uncertainty and more project analyses","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 1 (Sessions 1-15)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on the investment-return, cash-flow and option-value sections. Reinvestment and project-life shortcuts need the qualifications in the written exercise; historical numbers are not current rates or law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 13","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session13test.pdf","note":"This assigned test file contains questions, not solutions."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Typical project analysis slides","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/webcasts/typicalproject.pptx","note":"Qualitative project-analysis slides. Use the explicit written cash flows for calculations; the deck is not a complete numerical model."}],"exercise":"Private fictional one-period EV-components project, all amounts million EUR unless stated. Time 0 investment 30, year 1 units 20, contribution per unit 4, fixed incremental cash cost 20; usable operating tax 25%, no depreciation deduction, NWC, further capex, salvage or other cash flows in this stipulated toy. FCFF=(units*contribution-fixed cost)*(1-tax). Base cash 45, matched EUR discount 10%, NPV 10.9091. Joint downside units 15/contribution 3 gives cash 18.75/NPV-12.9545; upside 25/5 gives 78.75/41.5909. They are coherent teaching states, not estimated probabilities. One-at-a-time sensitivity volume 16/24 at contribution 4 gives cash 33/57 and NPV 0/21.8182. Margin 3/5 at units 20 gives cash 30/60 and NPV-2.7273/24.5455. Ranking depends on selected ranges, not an intrinsic universal top-two driver claim. Break-even units 16 at contribution 4. Verify demand, unit margin and fixed costs before real application; tax losses would need explicit treatment.\nCurrency-consistency toy: spot 90 INR/EUR, stipulated year 1 conversion 94.5, EUR rate 10%, consistent INR rate 15.5%=(1.10*1.05)-1. Convert investment 2700/cash 4252.5; INR NPV 981.8182 equals EUR NPV*90. This is a stipulated internally consistent conversion, not a forecast, guaranteed hedge rate or real interest-parity proof. Separate debt-service stress: fixed EUR 45 receipts at 90 yield INR 4050 versus INR 4000 debt due, surplus 50; at 75 yield 3375, deficit 625. Accounting denomination does not eliminate this exposure. Contract terms, hedges, collateral, basis/credit/liquidity and possible distress effects need separate evidence. A hedge can affect value through costs and constraints; neither free guaranteed value nor categorically zero value. Cannibalization uses lost incremental contribution and the no-project counterfactual, not automatically all revenue or any loss beyond baseline. Side benefits/options need evidence and no double counting. No trade, financing or spending authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"Base units 20, contribution 4, fixed 20, tax 25%. Year 1 FCFF?","o":["15.","60.","45.","80."],"a":2},{"q":"Investment 30 now, FCFF 45 at year 1, EUR rate 10%. NPV?","o":["40.9091.","15.","10.9091.","-10.9091."],"a":2},{"q":"Joint downside units 15/contribution 3/fixed 20/tax 25%. NPV at 10%?","o":["-12.9545.","10.9091.","18.75.","-11.25."],"a":0},{"q":"Contribution 4, fixed 20, tax 25%, cost 30, rate 10%. Break-even units?","o":["15.","20.","16.","11."],"a":2},{"q":"Which is a joint scenario rather than one-at-a-time sensitivity?","o":["Units 16 with contribution 4 and fixed 20 held constant.","Units 15 and contribution 3 change together in a coherent downside.","Contribution 3 with units 20 and fixed 20 held constant.","Discount rate 12% with operating inputs held constant."],"a":1},{"q":"Margin±25% moves NPV more than volume±20% here. What follows?","o":["Sensitivity ranges establish the probability of each outcome.","Margin must dominate every project at every possible range.","Volume demand evidence is unnecessary after this ranking.","Focus on evidence and chosen ranges; ranking is conditional."],"a":3},{"q":"EUR rate 10%, stipulated one-year FX multiplier 1.05. Consistent INR rate?","o":["15%.","10%.","5%.","15.5%."],"a":3},{"q":"INR investment 2700/cash 4252.5, rate 15.5%. NPV?","o":["4252.5, ignoring initial investment.","1165.9091, discounting converted INR cash at unchanged 10%.","10.9091 INR, leaving denomination unchanged.","981.8182, equal to EUR NPV times spot 90."],"a":3},{"q":"EUR 45 receipts, FX 75 INR/EUR, INR 4000 debt due. Funding gap?","o":["4000 INR, ignoring export receipts.","50 INR surplus, using FX 90 instead.","625 INR, before other stipulated flows or hedge effects.","0 because the valuation model used one currency."],"a":2},{"q":"New product diverts sales from an existing line. Relevant cost?","o":["No loss whenever both products have the same owner.","All old revenue even if variable costs are saved or rivals would take it.","Lost incremental cash contribution versus the no-project alternative.","Double old sales to allow for strategic uncertainty."],"a":2}]},"187":{"objectives":["Choose feasible investments by value on matched horizons; distinguish IRR roots, terminal reinvestment strategies, MIRR and conditional life normalization."],"videos":[{"id":"k7fGl2kvq-U","title":"Session 14: Choosing between investments and Side Costs/Benefits","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 1 (Sessions 1-15)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on the investment-return, cash-flow and option-value sections. Reinvestment and project-life shortcuts need the qualifications in the written exercise; historical numbers are not current rates or law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 14","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session14test.pdf","note":"This assigned test file contains questions, not solutions."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets (free with Google account)","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Publisher landing/FAQ read earlier, not account/feature tested. Offline work requires no registration, private upload or sharing."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"IRR/NPV reinvestment distinction: Magni and Martin","url":"https://mpra.ub.uni-muenchen.de/83889/1/MPRA_paper_83889.pdf","note":"Distinguishes IRR root and NPV computation from explicit reinvestment/terminal-wealth strategies; explains the conflict with lecture shorthand."}],"exercise":"Private fictional warehouse alternatives, amounts crore, same risk and one-year horizon, mutually exclusive, no capital constraint, no further effects. A costs 40 now/pays 52 at year 1; B costs 10/pays 15. At 10% A NPV 7.2727 versus B 3.6364, though IRRs 30%/50%. Choose A under these assumptions, not because size alone is good. Incremental A-B cash -30/+37 has crossover 23.3333%; both NPVs are equal there but need not be zero. A relevant crossover need not exist or be unique for arbitrary streams. Nonconventional independent stream -100/+230/-132 at times 0/1/2 has IRRs 10%/20%; at 15% NPV+0.1890. More than one sign change permits, not guarantees, multiple admissible IRRs. NPV uses stipulated risk-matched discounting, not a solver-selected favorite root.\nAn IRR is a rate solving NPV=0 for the given stream. Computing that root does not require actual reinvestment of receipts at IRR. The assigned packet uses a reinvestment shorthand; distinguish it from terminal-wealth strategies. A specified MIRR explicitly uses finance/reinvestment rates and horizon, and does not cure scale, horizon or feasibility problems by itself. NPV does not require receipt reinvestment to compute present value. These distinctions are supported by the separate Magni/Martin pedagogical note, not copied as the packet's own claim.\nIndependent repeatable-service example at 10%: X costs 20/pays 12 at years 1 and 2, Y costs 30/pays 14 at years 1-3. Compare equivalent annual values only if repeated replacement/service, unchanged risk/technology/costs and a common need justify it; otherwise compare explicit feasible finite-horizon cash flows and residual values. Different lives alone do not force EAA or prohibit one-off NPV comparison. Independent budget 20 with indivisible projects P cost 12/NPV 8, Q cost 10/NPV 7, R cost 10/NPV 7: select Q+R for 14 if independent and feasible; greedy IRR or NPV-per-cost can fail. No real capital approval. For the stated repeatable-service toy, X NPV 0.8264/EAA 0.4762 and Y NPV 4.8159/EAA 1.9366 at 10%; these annual values do not apply automatically to one-off opportunities.","quiz":[{"q":"A-40/+52, B-10/+15 at one year. NPVs at 10%?","o":["12 and 5.","7.2727 and 3.6364.","3.6364 and 7.2727.","30% and 50%."],"a":1},{"q":"Same mutually exclusive A/B, unrestricted funding and equal risk/horizon. Choice?","o":["B, solely for higher IRR despite lower NPV.","A, for higher NPV despite lower IRR.","A, solely because every larger project is superior.","Both, ignoring mutual exclusion."],"a":1},{"q":"Incremental A-B cash -30 now / +37 at year 1. What is the crossover rate to two decimal places?","o":["10.00%","30.00%","50.00%","23.33%"],"a":3},{"q":"Stream-100/+230/-132. Which roots set NPV to zero?","o":["No real rate above-100%.","Only 15%.","Only 0%.","10% and 20%."],"a":3},{"q":"Multiple sign changes in a cash-flow stream mean?","o":["There must be exactly one positive IRR.","There must be exactly two positive IRRs.","Multiple IRRs are possible, not guaranteed.","NPV cannot be evaluated at any specified rate."],"a":2},{"q":"Does computing the root NPV(IRR)=0 require reinvesting receipts at that IRR?","o":["No; distinguish a cash-flow root from an added terminal-wealth strategy.","Yes; receipts must stay invested at IRR or the equation is invalid.","Yes; company must own infinite projects at the same IRR.","No; IRR therefore ranks all scales and horizons correctly."],"a":0},{"q":"A MIRR calculation needs what explicit choices?","o":["Finance/reinvestment rates and horizon for the modified stream.","Only the largest original IRR, regardless of cash timing.","Only the original project NPV, regardless of horizon.","No rates, because modification eliminates discounting."],"a":0},{"q":"Different project lives automatically require perpetual EAA ranking?","o":["No; first establish repeatability/service need or explicit finite horizon.","Yes; replacement is guaranteed even if demand ends.","Yes; technological change cannot affect repeatability.","No; life differences make every valuation method impossible."],"a":0},{"q":"Budget 20: P cost 12/NPV 8, Q 10/7, R 10/7, indivisible independent feasible projects. Best set?","o":["All three, cost 32/NPV 22.","P, cost 12/NPV 8.","P+Q, cost 22/NPV 15.","Q+R, cost 20/NPV 14."],"a":3},{"q":"Unverified market-entry benefit is said to rescue negative NPV. Treatment?","o":["Count the same expansion cash in base NPV and option value.","Add an arbitrary goodwill allowance until NPV turns positive.","Model supported incremental value/rights, costs and overlap separately.","Ignore all possible side benefits even after evidence arrives."],"a":2}]},"188":{"objectives":["Compare forward continuation and exit values and conditional rights; separate sunk spending, state-dependent flexibility and real execution authority."],"videos":[{"id":"pz2hF0_ygm4","title":"Session 15: Celsius Case Analysis, Real Options and Investment Post-mortems","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 1 (Sessions 1-15)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket1spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on the investment-return, cash-flow and option-value sections. Reinvestment and project-life shortcuts need the qualifications in the written exercise; historical numbers are not current rates or law."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 15","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session15test.pdf","note":"This assigned test file contains questions, not solutions."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Celsius Eats case solution (Excel)","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfexams/CelsiusEatssoln.xlsx","note":"Historical finite-life spreadsheet example, not a real-options solution or current-market quote. Check formulas and assumptions independently; no setup or macro execution is required."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Real options: rights, expiry and cost of delay","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/papers/realopt.pdf","note":"Rights/expiry/competition/cost of delay matter; uncertainty alone is not an option valuation."}],"exercise":"Private fictional grocery vertical, historical spend 300 unrecoverable, historical monthly burn 8 not assumed permanent. Net immediate exit value 20 already reflects closure costs, liabilities and taxes. Continuing requires 12 now then yields total net 40 at year 1, including all later operating/reinvestment/terminal effects with no further flows. Matched 10% gives continuation 24.3636 versus exit 20, incremental advantage 4.3636. Downside net year 1 cash 28 gives continuation 13.4545, below exit 20. Break-even year 1 total 35.2 because(20+12)*1.1. Compare alternatives on the same date and claim basis; historical spending is not a new avoidable cost. If tax deductions, asset sale, contracts or closure obligations differ across alternatives, those forward effects do matter. Original 300 is stipulated wholly unrecoverable, not all past spending inherently worthless.\nIndependent one-year pilot/right toy, not a market option quote or inferred success probability. Pilot/right fee 4 now, no other pilot cash. At year 1 expansion business value is 90 in high state and 30 in low state; exercise cost 50 at year 1 and right expires then. Holder may decline expansion, with payoffs 40/0. Stipulated time 0 state-price factors are 0.4/1.1 for high and 0.6/1.1 for low, not measured real-world probabilities or a claim that project WACC prices every nonlinear option. Gross right value 14.5455; net after fee 10.5455. Forced expansion payoffs 40/-20 gives net-0.3636. Flexibility gain 10.9091 relative to forced policy, not a second amount to add to right value. Rights must be controlled, funded and exercisable; competition, expiry, learning, operating constraints and loss of current cash while waiting can reduce value. Uncertainty alone does not guarantee a valuable option or justify rescuing negative NPV.\nAn abandonment right can resemble a put and expansion a call but contractual/operating details control the actual payoff. Preserve information available at the original decision, compare forecast and actual cash and update unbiased forward models; neither a bad outcome alone nor a simplistic symmetry test proves misconduct. Use measurable review triggers and accountable owners; do not invent actual sponsor signoff, sale authority or a monitoring responsibility. No shutdown, commitment, spending or outreach authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"Historical 300 is stipulated unrecoverable. Forward decision treatment?","o":["Continue until 300 has been earned back regardless of alternatives.","Include 300 as a new outflow in every continuation decision.","Exclude sunk 300 but include any genuinely different forward cash/tax effects.","Assume 300 can be sold for cash because it appears in accounts."],"a":2},{"q":"Continue requires 12 now and yields 40 at year 1, rate 10%. Value today?","o":["24.3636.","36.3636.","28.","-275.6364."],"a":0},{"q":"Continuation 24.3636 versus net exit 20 now. Incremental advantage?","o":["4.3636.","24.3636.","44.3636.","-4.3636."],"a":0},{"q":"Downside continuation 12 now/28 year 1, exit 20, rate 10%. Preferred model alternative?","o":["Continue to justify historical 300.","Continue because 13.4545 is positive.","Exit 20 versus continuation 13.4545.","Exit because any uncertainty makes continuation invalid."],"a":2},{"q":"12 new spending now plus forgone exit 20, rate 10%. Break-even year 1 cash?","o":["332.","22.","13.2.","35.2."],"a":3},{"q":"Pilot right high business 90/low 30, exercise 50. Optional expansion payoffs?","o":["40 and 0.","90 and 30.","40 and-20.","50 and 50."],"a":0},{"q":"State-price factors 0.4/1.1 and 0.6/1.1, payoffs 40/0, fee 4 now. Net right value?","o":["-0.3636.","14.5455.","16.","10.5455."],"a":3},{"q":"Forced expansion payoff 40/-20 with same factors and fee 4. Net value?","o":["-0.3636.","10.5455.","4.","14.5455."],"a":0},{"q":"Can high uncertainty alone justify a large expansion-option premium?","o":["No; rights, learning, funding, expiry, competition and payoff evidence matter.","Yes; volatility creates guaranteed realizable cash regardless of rights.","Yes; every future business opportunity is exclusively owned.","No; all operational flexibility always has zero value."],"a":0},{"q":"Actual cash misses forecast once. Appropriate post-mortem conclusion?","o":["A single miss proves dishonest forecasting.","Inspect original evidence, chance, bias and process; one miss alone is inconclusive.","A single miss proves the decision was irrational ex ante.","A single miss justifies repeating the old forecast unchanged."],"a":1}]},"189":{"objectives":["Test fresh beta, WACC, cash-flow and option inputs through actual item-level reasoning, with consistent horizons and no invented learner weaknesses."],"videos":[{"id":"wsSwIfvaIG4","title":"Corporate Finance Quiz 2: Review Session","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Past NYU Stern Quiz 2 papers","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfexams/prqz2.pdf","note":"Start with the Spring 1998 case facts and first subquestion. Keep historical course equity-risk-premium assumptions separate from current quotations."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Solutions to past Quiz 2 papers","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfexams/prqz2sol.xlsx","note":"Past quiz solution workbook. Match the question, year, units and assumptions before comparing an answer; an answer letter is not a substitute for reasoning."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Google Sheets (free with Google account)","url":"https://www.google.com/sheets/about/","note":"Publisher landing/FAQ read earlier, not account/feature tested. Offline work requires no registration, private upload or sharing."}],"exercise":"Private fictional review with independent mini-cases, not current quotations or law. Industrial peer equity beta 1.4, D/E 0.4, tax 25% and negligible debt beta gives asset beta 1.0769231; target D/E 0.2 gives equity beta 1.2384615. Project market-value equity/debt 300/100, costs 12%/8% and usable tax 25% give WACC 10.5%, or 11% in a no-immediate-tax-shield sensitivity. Independent one-year investment 100/payout 112 has NPV +1.3575 at 10.5%. EBIT 20, tax 25%, depreciation 5, capex 12 and NWC increase 4 give FCFF 4. Deductibility and tax capacity are stipulated, not inferred from book treatment. Independent after-tax cash 30 deducts allocated pre-tax overhead 10, only 4 incremental: corrected cash is 34.5 at tax 25%.\nMutually exclusive one-year A 20/26 and B 5/7 have IRRs 30%/40%, but NPVs 3.6364/1.3636 at 10%; choose A with equal risk and no funding constraint. Incremental cash -15/+19 has crossover 26.6667%. Stream -100/+230/-132 has IRRs 10%/20%; not every multiple-sign stream has multiple admissible roots. Different lives need explicit one-off or repeatable-service scope. Root IRR is not a mandatory reinvestment strategy; MIRR needs rates and horizon. Continue value 18 versus net exit 20 favors exit under these claims, regardless of unrecoverable 300. Distinct option gross value 9 less fee 3 gives net 6 only if rights, costs and overlap are modeled correctly. Currency conversion must match flows and rates without pretending to remove contractual FX exposure. Record actual item answers, reasoning, confidence and retest evidence; aggregate 22/25 does not locate weaknesses. A classroom score does not establish mastery, tool reliability or permission to commit real capital.","quiz":[{"q":"Peer equity beta 1.4, D/E 0.4, tax 25%, negligible debt beta. What is asset beta to four decimal places?","o":["1.4000","1.8200","0.9800","1.0769"],"a":3},{"q":"Asset beta 1.0769231, target D/E 0.2, tax 25%. Equity beta?","o":["1.0769231.","1.2384615.","1.4.","0.9364549."],"a":1},{"q":"Business beta weighting under a no-cash industrial core should use?","o":["Industrial enterprise plus bank equity as identical claims.","Headcount without a value/risk bridge.","Revenue alone regardless of margins or valuation differences.","Comparable operating values with consistent claim definitions."],"a":3},{"q":"WACC equity 300/debt 100, costs 12%/8%, tax 25%. Result?","o":["9%.","11%.","10.5%.","12%."],"a":2},{"q":"Same WACC but no immediate interest shield in this sensitivity. Result?","o":["8.25%.","10.5%.","6%.","11%."],"a":3},{"q":"One-year cost 100/payout 112, matched rate 10.5%. NPV?","o":["1.3575.","12.","101.3575.","-1.3575."],"a":0},{"q":"Old loan coupon 8% is known. Sufficient for current project WACC?","o":["No; coupon rates never contain any credit information.","Yes; financing coupon equals every project hurdle.","Yes; a positive coupon eliminates equity opportunity cost.","No; marginal debt cost, equity risk, weights and project claim still matter."],"a":3},{"q":"Interest tax benefit in an Indian-labeled hypothetical case?","o":["Use the explicit deductible/usable assumption, not universal legal inference.","Apply 25% automatically to every interest payment.","Treat all book interest as immediately deductible by definition.","Assume every tax loss expires immediately without checking."],"a":0},{"q":"EBIT 20, tax 25%, depreciation 5, capex 12, NWC increase 4. FCFF?","o":["15.","4.","9.","24."],"a":1},{"q":"Operating NWC rises 4. Effect on cash this period?","o":["Add 4 as financing proceeds.","Subtract 4 once, if not already reflected in the bridge.","Ignore 4 because balance-sheet amounts never affect cash.","Subtract 4 both from EBIT and again after NOPAT without reconciliation."],"a":1},{"q":"Depreciation 5, tax 25%. Immediate tax shield 1.25 requires?","o":["Tax deductibility and sufficient immediate tax capacity as stipulated.","Book depreciation alone, regardless of tax rules.","Positive revenue alone, regardless of taxable income.","No conditions, because depreciation is a cash payment."],"a":0},{"q":"Cash 30 after allocated overhead 10, only 4 incremental, tax 25%. Correct cash?","o":["36.","34.5.","40.","37.5."],"a":1},{"q":"Owned asset can be sold 9 today; sunk study 2 cannot be recovered. Opportunity cost?","o":["2 sunk study only.","9 foregone sale, with any relevant forward tax/timing.","11 automatically treating both as new cash.","0 because no new purchase is needed."],"a":1},{"q":"New line diverts old sales 10. Lost cash automatically 10?","o":["Yes; subtract 20 to allow for competition.","Yes; every sales unit is pure cash profit.","No; account for contribution, avoided costs and no-project counterfactual.","No; same-owner cannibalization is never relevant."],"a":2},{"q":"Scenario versus sensitivity: coherent lower volume plus margin is?","o":["A pure one-variable sensitivity with everything else fixed.","A joint downside scenario.","An estimated probability by definition.","Proof of the worst physically possible outcome."],"a":1},{"q":"One-year A 20/26 and B 5/7. IRRs?","o":["40% and 30%.","30% and 40%.","3.6364% and 1.3636%.","10% and 10%."],"a":1},{"q":"Same A/B at 10%, mutually exclusive/equal risk/no funding constraint. Best?","o":["B because 40% exceeds 30%.","A with NPV 3.6364 versus B 1.3636.","Both despite mutual exclusion.","A because size guarantees value."],"a":1},{"q":"Incremental A-B cash -15 now / +19 at year 1. What is the crossover rate to two decimal places?","o":["30.00%","26.67%","40.00%","10.00%"],"a":1},{"q":"Cash -100/+230/-132 has 10%/20% IRRs. Safe decision method?","o":["Evaluate NPV at the matched rate rather than selecting a favorite root.","Always select 20% because it is higher.","Always select 10% because it is lower.","Treat multiple IRRs as proof that NPV is undefined."],"a":0},{"q":"Every stream with multiple sign changes has multiple positive IRRs?","o":["No; all such streams have no roots.","Yes; exactly two positive roots always.","Yes; root count always equals payment count.","No; possible does not mean guaranteed."],"a":3},{"q":"Different lives, but a one-off service need ending at year 5. Compare?","o":["Higher IRR, regardless of service coverage.","Identical replacement forever, without demand evidence.","Explicit feasible five-year cash flows and residual values.","Neither; unequal lives cannot be valued."],"a":2},{"q":"Does an IRR root mathematically require reinvesting receipts at IRR?","o":["Yes; an infinite pipeline of equal-IRR projects is required.","Yes; otherwise NPV(IRR) cannot be zero.","No; a terminal-wealth strategy is a separate assumption.","No; therefore IRR always ranks mutually exclusive projects."],"a":2},{"q":"Continue forward value 18 versus net exit 20, sunk 300 irrecoverable. Model choice?","o":["Exit 20, subject to verified rights and authority.","Continue because 18 is positive.","Continue because 300 was already spent.","Exit only if historical spending can be recovered in full."],"a":0},{"q":"Distinct option gross value 9 less fee 3, no other overlap/cost. Net?","o":["9, ignoring the fee.","12, adding the fee as a benefit.","6, conditional on the stated rights and model.","18, adding the option twice."],"a":2},{"q":"Total 22/25 without item answers or work. Weak concepts?","o":["Beta is weak because it was first.","Unknown; obtain item responses and reasoning.","Options are weak because they were last.","All are mastered because the percentage is high."],"a":1}]},"190":{"objectives":["Explain financing irrelevance under explicit ideal assumptions and the actual marginal benefits/costs of debt, without inferring current Indian tax rules or safe leverage from a sector label."],"videos":[{"id":"4ZWSccnMaa4","title":"Session 16: The Debt Equity Tradeoff","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 2 (Sessions 16-26)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket2spr25.pdf","note":"Historical rates, ratings and default frequencies are not current India inputs. Use the explicit finite-shield and duration examples; the perpetual tax-times-debt shortcut is conditional."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 16","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session16AXtest.pdf","note":"These practice files do not align perfectly with the assigned topics: Session 17 repeats the 16AX trade-off test; Session 19 covers WACC, value and buybacks; Session 20 covers APV and peers. Choose the written topic checks that match this module."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Marginal tax rates by country (Damodaran dataset)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pc/datasets/countrytaxrates.xls","note":"Secondary country-tax summary dated January 5, 2026, citing the Tax Foundation and showing 0.30 for India. It does not establish the applicable regime, deduction or tax capacity of a particular entity."}],"exercise":"Private fictional financing-principles note. The no-tax MM irrelevance benchmark assumes frictionless competitive capital markets, comparable investor/firm borrowing opportunities, fixed investment/operating policy, and no tax, distress, agency, transaction or information frictions relevant to the comparison. It is not established by saying only no taxes/no bankruptcy, and not a statement that debt is always best. Financing can redistribute risk: cheaper contractual debt does not make residual equity risk or its opportunity cost disappear. Real applicability needs evidence of tax capacity, borrowing constraints, covenants, distress, information and agency effects; do not announce that a named assumption fails uniformly in India without case evidence.\nIndependent finite-debt tax toy: fixed principal 100 for three years, coupon 8 paid at each year-end, full deductible/usable tax 25%, principal repaid at end 3 and no replacement debt. Annual shield 2, stipulated shield-risk discount 8%, PV 5.1542, not perpetual tax*debt 25. Immediate shield is zero in a no-current-tax-capacity sensitivity, with any future carryforward effect unmodeled rather than assumed worthless. Independent one-year distress toy: probability 10%, conditional deadweight loss 30 at year 1, discount 10% => expected loss 3/PV 2.7273; state-dependent losses and timing generally need full expectations, not an arbitrary probability multiplied by total debt. Indirect distress includes lost customers/supplier terms, potentially already in operating cash; avoid double counting. Discipline may reduce waste but debt can also create underinvestment, risk-shifting and lost flexibility. Pecking order is a conditional behavioral theory, not universal instruction. Equity issuance is not conclusive proof of overvaluation. Sector stability, currency, maturity and covenants matter more than claiming a utility always carries most debt safely. No financing, issuance, spending or advice about an actual security is authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"No-tax MM irrelevance requires which additional basis?","o":["Only cheaper debt coupons than expected equity returns.","Only no bankruptcy, with arbitrary changing investment policies.","Fixed operations/investment and appropriate frictionless-market assumptions.","Only a management preference for retained earnings."],"a":2},{"q":"Debt costs less than equity before leverage change. What follows?","o":["Nothing about always-better leverage without residual risk and frictions.","Use the unchanged equity cost and replace equity with cheaper debt.","Infer a lower WACC without checking market-value weights or tax use.","Choose leverage by coupon alone, without modeling the residual claim."],"a":0},{"q":"Finite debt 100, coupon 8, tax 25%, fully usable. Annual shield?","o":["2.","8.","25.","100."],"a":0},{"q":"Shield 2 at ends 1-3, discount 8%, no renewal. PV?","o":["25 using perpetual tax*debt.","About 5.1542.","6 without discounting.","2 counting one year only."],"a":1},{"q":"No current tax capacity in the sensitivity. Immediate shield?","o":["Zero forever in all jurisdictions without checking carryforwards.","2 now regardless of taxable income or legal restrictions.","25 now because debt principal is 100.","Zero now; any future usable carryforward needs separate modeling."],"a":3},{"q":"One-year distress probability 10%, conditional deadweight loss 30. Expected loss?","o":["10, copying probability as money.","30 at year 1 regardless of probability.","3 at year 1 before discounting.","100, replacing loss with debt principal."],"a":2},{"q":"Customer flight is already included in expected operating cash. APV adjustment?","o":["Subtract the full forecast operating decline again as a financing adjustment.","Check overlap before subtracting the same loss again.","Omit every other distress effect because one operating loss is modeled.","Subtract contractual coupon expense as the distress adjustment."],"a":1},{"q":"Debt discipline can have what countervailing cost?","o":["Guaranteed revenue growth from fixed debt obligations.","Automatic removal of every manager-shareholder conflict.","Underinvestment, risk shifting, covenants and reduced flexibility.","Elimination of all future external financing needs."],"a":2},{"q":"New equity issue by a firm. Conclusive interpretation?","o":["Infer negative NPV from issuance dilution without valuing proceeds.","Treat the issue discount as sufficient proof of overvaluation.","None from issuance alone; inspect need, terms and information.","Assume retained earnings were cheaper without comparing opportunity costs."],"a":2},{"q":"Country-tax dataset labels 5 January 2026/India 30%. Current entity shield?","o":["Unproven; verify regime, deductibility, tax capacity and current rules.","Exactly 30% for every Indian entity and every loan.","Exactly 30% minus the coupon rate.","Zero because country data can never be useful."],"a":0}]},"191":{"objectives":["Find a schedule minimum only within its assumptions, then test operating effects, tax limits and execution frictions before calling it optimal."],"videos":[{"id":"fdEnaqWUYzM","title":"Session 17: Optimizing the Debt Mix with Cost of Capital approach","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 2 (Sessions 16-26)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket2spr25.pdf","note":"Historical rates, ratings and default frequencies are not current India inputs. Use the explicit finite-shield and duration examples; the perpetual tax-times-debt shortcut is conditional."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 17","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session17test.pdf","note":"These practice files do not align perfectly with the assigned topics: Session 17 repeats the 16AX trade-off test; Session 19 covers WACC, value and buybacks; Session 20 covers APV and peers. Choose the written topic checks that match this module."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"HP qualitative debt tradeoff worksheet, not an optimization solver","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/webcasts/tradeoffdebt/tradeoffHP.xls","note":"Historical HP/industry qualitative tradeoff worksheet, not a WACC-minimizing solver. Use dated statements, ratings and borrowing terms before applying the method to a real issuer."}],"exercise":"Private fictional nonfinancial financing schedule, not current rates/ratings. Nominal rf 5%, ERP 5%, asset beta 1, negligible debt beta approximation, usable tax 25%; leverage d=D/(D+E), D/E=d/(1-d). Supplied debt costs at d 0/20/40/60% are 8%/8%/9%/13% respectively (unused at zero debt); no rating inferred. Relevered equity betas 1/1.1875/1.5/2.125 give equity costs 10/10.9375/12.5/15.625%. WACC 0/20/40/60%=10/9.95/10.2/12.1%. Minimum over this coarse supplied grid is 20%, NOT a preordained 40% or a continuous/global optimum. High debt beta or changing tax use can invalidate this shortcut; repeat with a more suitable risk model when material.\nValue sensitivity with next-year FCFF 10, growth 2%, constant risk/cash policy: V=10/(WACC-.02). At 10%, value is 125; at 9.95%,125.7862; at 10.2%,121.9512; at 12.1%,99.0099. Cash is held fixed solely to isolate rates, not claim leverage never affects operations. Independent operational-stress sensitivity next cash 8 at 20% debt gives 100.6289, below unlevered 125 despite minimum-grid WACC. Don't subtract the same operational distress again in APV. Debt values and leverage policies must be reconciled in a full iterative model; this table does not prove a self-consistent market equilibrium or actual refinance prices. Independent perpetuity identity WACC 11% to 10%, g 2%, fixed FCFF 10 =>111.1111 to 125,+12.5%; not a universal duration multiplier. Finite cash horizons need direct repricing, with taxes/costs/fees and operating effects as applicable. Preserve covenants, liquidity, ratings/rate terms, refinancing windows, uncertain estimates and owner authority. No debt issue or buyback authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"Debt fraction 20% means D/E?","o":["0.80.","0.20.","0.25.","4.0."],"a":2},{"q":"Asset beta 1, tax 25%, D/E 0.25. Equity beta under stated approximation?","o":["0.8421053.","1.25.","1.1875.","1.0."],"a":2},{"q":"rf 5%, ERP 5%, equity beta 1.1875. Equity cost?","o":["10.9375%.","10%.","5.9375%.","11.875%."],"a":0},{"q":"20% debt, cost 8%, tax 25%, 80% equity at 10.9375%. WACC?","o":["10.9375%.","10.35%.","8%.","9.95%."],"a":3},{"q":"40% debt, cost 9%, tax 25%, equity 12.5%. WACC?","o":["10.2%.","9.95%.","11.1%.","12.5%."],"a":0},{"q":"Grid WACCs 10%,9.95%,10.2%,12.1% at debt 0/20/40/60. Minimum?","o":["40%, applying the historical Disney example to this grid.","20% on this supplied grid only.","60% because debt fraction is highest.","20% as a proven continuous global optimum."],"a":1},{"q":"Next FCFF 10, g 2%, WACC 9.95%. Perpetuity value?","o":["100.5025.","125.7862.","125.","121.9512."],"a":1},{"q":"Same rate 9.95% but operational next cash falls 10 to 8. Value?","o":["125.7862, ignoring lost cash.","100.6289, below unlevered 125.","128, adding reduced cash to old value.","No value effect because WACC is minimized."],"a":1},{"q":"Fixed next cash 10, g 2%, WACC 11% to 10%. Percentage value rise?","o":["11.1111%.","1%.","10%.","12.5%."],"a":3},{"q":"Synthetic-rating spreadsheet schedule says borrow more. Execution?","o":["Verify evidence, model limits, liquidity/covenants and actual authority.","Refinance to the grid minimum before checking covenants or fees.","Use the peer median as a substitute for sensitivity analysis.","Keep the original equity cost fixed when recomputing leverage."],"a":0}]},"192":{"objectives":["Reconcile APV with explicit finite financing and distinct project/holdco claims; treat peer ratios as descriptive evidence rather than optimal or causal rules."],"videos":[{"id":"jv-mk7lpIdg","title":"Session 19: The APV and Peer Group Approaches to Financing Mix","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 2 (Sessions 16-26)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket2spr25.pdf","note":"Historical rates, ratings and default frequencies are not current India inputs. Use the explicit finite-shield and duration examples; the perpetual tax-times-debt shortcut is conditional."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 19","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session19test.pdf","note":"These practice files do not align perfectly with the assigned topics: Session 17 repeats the 16AX trade-off test; Session 19 covers WACC, value and buybacks; Session 20 covers APV and peers. Choose the written topic checks that match this module."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Dell historical cost-of-capital workbook, not an APV implementation","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/webcasts/optdebt/dellcapstru.xls","note":"Historical WACC model, not an APV implementation or current-market quote. Use the written exercise for the APV calculation."}],"exercise":"Private fictional one-year finite-life asset, no continuation. Unlevered expected FCFF 121 at year 1, business-risk discount 10%=>unlevered value 110. Fixed debt 50 now at 8%, principal repaid year 1, no refinancing. Full deductible/usable tax 25% gives shield 1 at year 1, stipulated shield-risk discount 8%=>PV 0.925926. Distinct expected financing distress deadweight cost 2 at year 1, not already in FCFF 121, stipulated discount 10%=>PV 1.818182. Fee 0.5 today. APV=110+.925926-1.818182-.5=108.607744. Debt issued at fair value 50, no other claims/cash =>equity 58.607744 before any separate purchase consideration. If buying asset for 100 today, investment NPV 8.607744; asset value and project NPV differ. Do not add debt proceeds 50 to unlevered value 110 or count principal as an operating expense. Perpetual tax*debt 12.5 does not price this one-year shield. An independently computed WACC valuation should agree only with consistent debt policy, risk, tax cash, fees, claims and costs; a constant perpetual WACC cannot be imported blindly into changing-debt APV. Expected loss states/risk/timing need source evidence; case assumptions are not measured default probabilities.\nSeparate holdco illustration: project A equity 30, B equity 20, holdco excess cash 5, holdco debt 10, no other claims=>holdco equity 45 if equity stakes are distributable/transferable as assumed. Do not subtract project debt again from already levered equity values. Project nonrecourse debt is not free/off-balance-sheet by name; ringfencing, guarantees, cross-default, security, distribution locks and current accounting/contract rules need review. Never assume upstream cash equals consolidated EBITDA. Peer ratios 20/30/70% give median 30%, not proven optimum; explain outlier 70 rather than automatically delete it. Check definitions, date, scale, tax, asset life, risk, nonrecourse/consolidation and growth; regressions are descriptive estimates with uncertainty, not causal prescriptions. The assigned Dell workbook is a cost-of-capital model, not an APV implementation. No account changes, financing or spending authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"One-year unlevered FCFF 121, business discount 10%. Asset value?","o":["50.","121.","100.","110."],"a":3},{"q":"Fixed one-year debt 50 at 8%, usable tax 25%. Shield cash at year 1?","o":["1.","12.5.","4.","50."],"a":0},{"q":"Shield 1 year 1, stipulated shield discount 8%. PV?","o":["1.","0.925926.","12.5.","0.909091."],"a":1},{"q":"Distinct expected distress cash 2 at year 1, discount 10%. What is its PV to four decimal places?","o":["20.0000","2.0000","1.8182","0.2000"],"a":2},{"q":"Unlevered 110+shield PV 0.925926-distress PV 1.818182-fee 0.5. APV?","o":["108.607744.","158.607744.","110.925926.","98.607744."],"a":0},{"q":"APV 108.607744, fair debt 50, no other claims/cash. Equity value?","o":["158.607744.","108.607744.","58.607744.","8.607744."],"a":2},{"q":"Same asset bought for 100 today. Investment NPV?","o":["-100.","108.607744.","58.607744.","8.607744."],"a":3},{"q":"Project equity A 30/B 20, holdco cash 5, holdco debt 10, no other claims. Holdco equity?","o":["Cannot ever aggregate equity stakes, even with matched claims.","55, ignoring holdco debt.","35, subtracting holdco debt twice.","45, subject to the stated claim/distribution assumptions."],"a":3},{"q":"Nonrecourse project debt label alone establishes?","o":["Neither zero risk nor unrestricted cash upstream; inspect actual contracts.","Assume no consolidation from the nonrecourse label without accounting review.","Assume all project cash can be upstreamed before checking distribution locks.","Treat the absence of a parent guarantee as proof of zero holdco risk."],"a":0},{"q":"Peer debt ratios 20/30/70%. Median 30% means?","o":["A causal proof debt 30% raises cash flows.","Optimal debt 30% for every otherwise unexamined firm.","Outlier 70% must be deleted without explanation.","A descriptive midpoint, not a proven value-maximizing target."],"a":3}]},"193":{"objectives":["Design debt around asset cash flows, duration and liquidity, without treating concession length, cheap coupons or exporter labels as sufficient financing evidence."],"videos":[{"id":"Kk7BEn3Y4XY","title":"Session 20: Debt Design","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 2 (Sessions 16-26)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket2spr25.pdf","note":"Historical rates, ratings and default frequencies are not current India inputs. Use the explicit finite-shield and duration examples; the perpetual tax-times-debt shortcut is conditional."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Post-class test: Session 20","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session20test.pdf","note":"These practice files do not align perfectly with the assigned topics: Session 17 repeats the 16AX trade-off test; Session 19 covers WACC, value and buybacks; Session 20 covers APV and peers. Choose the written topic checks that match this module."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Debt design spreadsheet (Walmart duration example)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/webcasts/debtdesign/WMTmacrodur.xls","note":"The historical workbook treats 2012 as its latest year. A positive slope and a displayed duration of zero do not establish that the assets are risk-free."}],"exercise":"Private fictional airport, 30-year concession but this alone does not specify cash-flow duration or debt maturity. Independently consider asset cash 50 at year 1 and 50 at year 3, discount 10%. PV 83.020285; Macaulay duration=sum(t*PV cash)/PV=1.904977 years and modified duration 1.731797 for annual yield. Maturity 3 is not duration 1.904977; actual airport cash-flow profile must be obtained before debt matching. Independent principal 100 three-year interest-only loan at 7.4% versus 8% longer debt saves 0.6 per year for first 3, PV 1.546258 at 8%, excluding fees/taxes/different principal timing. A distinct refinancing stress requires an extra 10 at year 3 with stated pricing weight 20% and discount 8%, PV 1.587664; net-0.041406 under those assumptions. Not a market price of risk, exhaustive all-in cost comparison, or proof short debt always wrong. Model rollover availability, rate path, fees, amortization, covenants, hedges and liquidity rather than call 60 bps free alpha or necessarily all payment for risk.\nCFADS 40/debt service 30 gives DSCR 1.3333; stress CFADS 25 gives 0.8333. A requested 20 reserve is restricted cash, not free cash for all stakeholders; actual terms determine what it can pay. Currency design matches net operating exposure, not gross revenue or patriotism: USD receipts 100/USD cost 80 leave USD 20 before other flows; USD debt service 30 is not fully naturally hedged. Inflation-sensitive revenues do not automatically justify floating debt if costs/caps/demand prevent cash pass-through or reset dates differ. Convertibles exchange lower coupons for an option/possible dilution, not free financing; issuance terms, current tax, regulatory eligibility and call/conversion features need actual verification. A macro regression slope based on short historical sample is not automatically bond-style positive duration or causality. Assigned Walmart workbook duration cache zero alongside positive slope is not proof of risk-free assets. No actual NCD/ECB eligibility, credit line, financing or spending approval inferred.","quiz":[{"q":"30-year concession gives what financing fact by itself?","o":["Every shorter loan is automatically illegal.","Asset duration exactly 30 years regardless of cash timing.","Debt must mature exactly 30 years in every structure.","Only a legal/operating horizon, not exact cash-flow duration or optimal debt tenor."],"a":3},{"q":"Cash 50 at year 1 and 50 at year 3, rate 10%. Macaulay duration?","o":["3 years.","About 1.904977 years.","2 years exactly without discounting.","30 years."],"a":1},{"q":"Macaulay 1.904977, annual yield 10%. Modified duration?","o":["2.095475.","1.904977.","About 1.731797.","0.190498."],"a":2},{"q":"Principal 100, coupon saving 0.6 yearly for 3 years, discount 8%. PV saving?","o":["60.","1.8 without discounting.","About 1.546258.","0.6 counting one year."],"a":2},{"q":"Distinct year 3 refinancing stress 10, pricing weight 20%, discount 8%. PV cost?","o":["0 because debt has not matured now.","2 without discounting.","10 without weighting.","About 1.587664."],"a":3},{"q":"CFADS 40, debt service 30. DSCR?","o":["30%.","0.75.","10.","1.3333."],"a":3},{"q":"Stress CFADS 25, debt service 30. DSCR?","o":["1.2, inverting the coverage ratio.","0.8333, a five-unit gap before permitted reserves/other support.","1.3333, keeping base cash fixed.","0, because every cash shortfall eliminates all revenue."],"a":1},{"q":"USD receipts 100/USD cost 80/USD debt service 30. Fully naturally hedged?","o":["Yes; gross 100 receipts exceed 30 debt service.","No; net 20 operating USD leaves 10 short before other flows.","Yes; any exporter should maximize USD debt.","No; matching currency can never reduce exposure."],"a":1},{"q":"Revenue rises with inflation. Floating debt automatically best?","o":["Yes; nominal revenue indexing is sufficient even if margins fall.","No; test net cash pass-through, lags/caps, resets and downside liquidity.","Yes; annual tariff resets fully offset any monthly rate resets.","No; fixed-rate debt is preferable for every inflation-linked asset."],"a":1},{"q":"Convertible lowers stated coupon. Economic trade-off?","o":["Value of embedded conversion/call terms, dilution and all-in financing costs.","Compare coupon costs only, ignoring conversion and call value.","Treat possible conversion as eliminating debt-service risk before conversion.","Value it as straight debt without checking conversion terms."],"a":0}]},"194":{"objectives":["Compare total cash returned with a coherent FCFE bridge, liquidity needs and project evidence; distinguish payout optics from value without assuming current Indian tax treatment."],"videos":[{"id":"juhEybZYE2M","title":"Session 23: Assessing Dividend (and Cash Return) Policy","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 2 (Sessions 16-26)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket2spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on the relevant historical valuation sections; use the exercise's explicit figures rather than treating the examples as current prices. Historical growth/terminal-value labels conflict in257/259/260/262; independent examples replace them. Page261 distinguishes efficiency growth from reinvestment growth; sustainable excess returns are conditional, not prohibited."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Session23 post-class questions, not solutions","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/postclass/session23test.pdf","note":"Covers clientele, signaling, payout changes and bondholder effects, not a complete numeric payout model. Reactions are not guaranteed predictions."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"Dividend assessment spreadsheet (Intel example)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/webcasts/dividends/IntelDividends.xls","note":"Some late-year net-debt inputs differ from the analysis's zero values. Reconcile the model before drawing a conclusion; it is a historical example, not a current-market valuation."}],"exercise":"Private fictional nonfinancial payout review, all amounts in one consistent unit. Five annual net incomes 100/110/120/130/140, depreciation 10 each year, capex 20/25/30/35/40, noncash working-capital increase 5 each year and net debt issued 0/+5/0/-5/0 give FCFE 85/95/95/95/105. Net income already deducts interest; do not subtract it again. Total FCFE 475, dividends 20/25/30/35/40 total 150 and buybacks 0/10/20/10/0 total 40 give total return 190 and retained cash 285. Starting cash 50 becomes 335 only assuming no other cash movements, investment income, issuance or leakage. If 40 is contractually restricted, only 295 is unrestricted before additional verified needs, not an approved distribution. FCFE is an economic capacity estimate, not legal distributable profit, solvency clearance, covenant compliance or evidence that every positive-NPV future opportunity is already funded. Negative-year FCFE cannot be cured by taking an average blindly; debt policy and reinvestment volatility matter. Never apply the industrial bridge mechanically to a bank's regulatory-capital needs.\nIndependent fair-price buyback toy: total equity value 1000 including excess cash 100,100 shares, price 10. Buy back 10 shares for 100, remaining equity 900/90 shares=10; no automatic per-share value creation. If recurring earnings 100 exclude any cash interest and all other effects, EPS rises 1 to 1.1111 without that rise proving value creation. Pay 12.5 per share instead: buy 8 shares, remaining fundamental 900/92=9.782609, a transfer away from continuing holders under fixed-value assumptions. Alternative dividend 100 gives 1 per share and ex-dividend 9 under no-tax/friction assumptions, not guaranteed market behavior. Compare holder taxes, transaction costs, eligibility, liquidity and actual price; do not presume buybacks tax-favored in current India. Issuing equity while paying dividends is not conclusive value destruction without cash timing, total funding need and costs. Low cash yield versus risky WACC alone is not an equal-risk comparison. Assess investment quality and planned needs, not stock performance or dividend tradition alone. No payout, buyback, tax advice, issuance or actual board signoff authorized.","quiz":[{"q":"Net income 100, depreciation 10, capex 30, WC increase 5, new debt 20, repayment 15. FCFE?","o":["95, adding gross new debt but not repayment.","75, ignoring net borrowing.","80.","100, treating accounting profit as cash."],"a":2},{"q":"Five annual FCFE 85/95/95/95/105. Total?","o":["525.","95, reporting the annual mean as the total.","465.","475."],"a":3},{"q":"Dividends 150 and buybacks 40 against total FCFE 475. Cash-return gap?","o":["325, ignoring buybacks.","285, before other cash movements.","435, ignoring dividends.","665, adding payout to capacity."],"a":1},{"q":"Starting cash 50 plus the 285 retention, no other movements. End cash?","o":["525, ignoring payouts.","285, omitting starting cash.","335.","240, subtracting an extra annual 95 FCFE."],"a":2},{"q":"Of 335 cash,40 restricted. Approved payout now?","o":["190, because historical payout fixes current authority.","335, because every cash balance is distributable.","295, automatically approved by subtraction alone.","Not established;295 unrestricted still needs legal, liquidity and investment checks."],"a":3},{"q":"Equity 1000 includes 100 cash;100 shares. Buyback 100 at fair 10. Remaining fundamental per share?","o":["12.5, equating a possible offer price with value.","11.1111, retaining the spent 100 in equity.","9, ignoring the reduced share count.","10, with equity 900 and 90 shares."],"a":3},{"q":"Same toy recurring earnings 100, no lost cash interest/other effects;90 shares after buyback. EPS?","o":["1.1111; this does not itself prove value creation.","1; share count never affects EPS.","1.1111; therefore intrinsic value necessarily rises 11.11%.","0.9, dividing shares by earnings."],"a":0},{"q":"Same 1000 equity/100 shares; spend 100 buying at 12.5, underlying noncash value unchanged. Remaining value/share?","o":["10, regardless of repurchase price.","9.782609, from 900/92.","12.5, because management set that offer.","10.869565, retaining spent cash in numerator."],"a":1},{"q":"Current Indian shareholder comparison of dividend versus buyback. Assume tax preference?","o":["Use the historical US dividend tax comparison for this Indian holder.","No; verify current regime, holder status, transaction terms and total taxes/costs.","Select the lower gross cash payout without comparing holder taxes.","Apply the company marginal income-tax rate directly to both holder receipts."],"a":1},{"q":"Company pays dividends and issues equity. Necessary conclusion?","o":["None alone; reconcile funding, timing, investment benefits, constraints and costs.","Value destruction equal to every rupee of gross dividends.","Optimal policy because payout clientele overrides financing costs.","No costs because the two gross flows cancel economically in every setting."],"a":0}]},"195":{"objectives":["Rebuild a claim-consistent terminal DCF with next-period cash, reinvestment and conditional excess returns; identify uncertainty rather than label terminal concentration or a precise share value as proof."],"videos":[{"id":"CERMihGaLlA","title":"Session 25: Valuation - The Final Frontier!","by":"Aswath Damodaran","note":"Use the recording alongside the written study kit and exercise."}],"materials":[{"tag":"Reading","title":"Damodaran Lecture Note Packet 2 (Sessions 16-26)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfovhds/cfpacket2spr25.pdf","note":"Focus on the relevant historical valuation sections; use the exercise's explicit figures rather than treating the examples as current prices. Historical growth/terminal-value labels conflict in257/259/260/262; independent examples replace them. Page261 distinguishes efficiency growth from reinvestment growth; sustainable excess returns are conditional, not prohibited."},{"tag":"Reading","title":"Final exam review deck (NYU Stern)","url":"https://www.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pdfiles/cfexams/reviewFinalNew.pdf","note":"Historical exam review deck: beta, WACC, cash flows and terminal value. Its rates and accounting examples are not current India inputs."},{"tag":"Tool","title":"FCFF valuation spreadsheet (Damodaran)","url":"https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pc/fcffsimpleginzu.xlsx","note":"Almarai example from Saudi Arabia, dated February 1, 2026, not a blank model or current Indian-company valuation. Failure probability, available cash and terminal return/growth defaults are assumptions."}],"exercise":"Private fictional nonfinancial DCF, not current security advice or Indian tax clearance. Annual FCFF 10/12/14 at years 1/2/3 are after all operating tax/reinvestment and before financing, discounted using 10% each of those years: PV 29.526672. Year 3 NOPAT 20 is the starting income for terminal growth 3%; year 4 NOPAT 20.6. Stipulated sustainable marginal ROC 12%, WACC 9% from year 4 onward implies reinvestment rate 3/12=25%, terminal reinvestment 5.15 and FCFF 4=15.45. TV at end year 3=15.45/(.09-.03)=257.5; discount to today through the three explicit 10% years, not through the terminal 9% rate: PV 193.463561. Operating value 222.990233, terminal share 86.7588%. Such concentration calls for sensitivity and scenario work, not automatic rejection or evidence that a stock is cheap.\nSeparate verified-claim toy bridge: add excess cash 15 and nonoperating holdings 5, subtract fair debt 40, noncontrolling claims 10 attributable to consolidated operating cash and employee option claim 2 once; common equity 190.990233/20 shares=9.549512. Each claim must match consolidation, cash-flow scope and dilution method; do not subtract minority claims if their cash was already excluded, or double count options with a conflicting fully diluted denominator. Do not automatically add restricted/operating cash at face value.\nA 6% terminal-growth sensitivity with ROC 12% and WACC 9% gives next NOPAT 21.2, reinvestment 50%, FCFF 10.6 and TV 353.333333, not the value from holding FCFF 15.45 fixed. It is a conditional sensitivity, not a justified perpetual macro forecast. g<r is necessary for this growing-perpetuity formula but not sufficient for economic plausibility. Match currency, nominal/real basis, inflation, operating geography and claim; a local economy's nominal rate is not a universal multi-currency cap. Sustainable competitive advantage may justify excess ROC beyond WACC; equal returns are a scenario, not a mandatory truth. For fixed next-year NOPAT 21 and ROC=WACC 9%, changing g from 3% to 6% changes reinvestment from 1/3 to 2/3 and leaves terminal value 233.333333 in both cases. Holding next-year NOPAT fixed is essential to this invariance demonstration. Growth can temporarily come from better utilization/margins without proportionately new investment; g=reinvestment*ROC describes the stated steady-state new-investment mechanism, not every efficiency improvement. A source's zero-failure or fully available cash default is not evidence. No model execution, trade, actual company valuation or owner approval follows.","quiz":[{"q":"Year 3 NOPAT 20 grows 3%; terminal marginal ROC 12%. Reinvestment rate?","o":["12%.","3%.","25%.","36%."],"a":2},{"q":"Next-year NOPAT 20.6, reinvestment 25%. Terminal FCFF?","o":["20.6, omitting reinvestment.","15.45.","5.15, reporting reinvestment instead.","14, copying year 3 FCFF."],"a":1},{"q":"FCFF 4=15.45, terminal WACC 9%, g 3%. TV at end year 3?","o":["257.5.","250, applying growth to the wrong cash base.","171.6667, dividing by WACC alone.","515, counting the growth spread twice."],"a":0},{"q":"TV 257.5 at end year 3; explicit discount rates 10% in years 1-3,9% thereafter. PV today?","o":["198.837246, dividing by 1.09^3.","193.463561, dividing by 1.1^3.","257.5, leaving it at year 3.","175.875965, discounting one extra 10%year."],"a":1},{"q":"Explicit PV 29.526672 plus terminal PV 193.463561. Operating value?","o":["229.463561, adding nominal explicit cash instead of its PV.","287.026672, adding undiscounted TV.","193.463561, omitting explicit cash.","222.990233."],"a":3},{"q":"Operating 222.990233+cash 15+holdings 5-debt 40-NCI 10-options 2,20 shares. Common value/share?","o":["11.549512, omitting debt but retaining other adjustments.","11.149512, dividing operating value only.","9.549512, with matched claim scope.","13.549512, reversing debt subtraction."],"a":2},{"q":"Terminal PV is 86.76% of operating value. This proves?","o":["Strong dependence on distant assumptions, not automatic error or cheapness.","The model is invalid regardless of cash timing or business maturity.","Growth 6% is justified because the terminal share is large.","The share value is precise because the formula converges."],"a":0},{"q":"Terminal g 6%, ROC 12%, next NOPAT 21.2, WACC 9%. Reconciled FCFF/TV?","o":["21.2/706.666667, dropping reinvestment.","15.45/515, freezing cash despite higher reinvestment.","10.6/353.333333.","10.6/117.777778, ignoring growth in the denominator."],"a":2},{"q":"Fixed next NOPAT 21, ROC=WACC 9%. g 3% versus 6% with matching reinvestment. Terminal values?","o":["Both 233.333333 under these fixed-next-income assumptions.","233.333333 and 466.666667, holding free cash fixed.","Both 700, ignoring reinvestment.","No finite value whenever ROC equals WACC."],"a":0},{"q":"Higher growth without proportionate new investment is always impossible?","o":["Yes; ROC must equal WACC for every firm in every forecast year.","Yes; utilization and margin improvements can never raise income.","No; therefore stable growth can always be increased at zero reinvestment.","No; efficiency growth can occur, while perpetual new-investment growth needs consistent reinvestment/returns."],"a":3}]}});
